\nlactate IPRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system.\" \ny'y.\n- \nà stainless steel column 20 crii x 4.'6 rrim, packed with\nv octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 phi), A 1 y\n- \nmobile phase: a mixture of 90 volumes of water and \n10 volumes of a 2 per cent v/v solution of octylamine \nacetonitrile, the pH of which is adjusted to 7.0 with a\n10 per cent v/v solution of phosphoric acid,\n- \nflow rate: 2 ml per minute,  : j A MM Mr> ;\n*\n- \nspectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- \ninjection volume: 10 pl. \n;•\nInject separately the reference solution and the test solution \narid measure the responses for the major peak.7 \n(\nCalculate the content of C3H6O3, in the injection. tv \nFor dextrose—To a measured volume containing'2 g to 5 g$ \nDextrose, add 0,2 ml of 5 M ammonia and sufficient water® \nproduce 100.0; ml. .Mix well, allow to. stand \n30 minutes and measure the optical rotation in a 2-din^ \n(2.4.22). The observed rotation in degrees multipli^\n0.9477 represents the weight, in g, of dextrose, CgHnOfe® \nvolume taken for assay. :\nde ’Store in single dose containers of glass or plastic-at \nSt°mperature not exceeding 30°. On keeping, small particles \n^ay separate from the solution in glass containers."},{"name":"Tests","text":"belling- The l^el states (0 l'iat inJ action contains, in- \n'Uimolespef litre, the following approximate amounts of the\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 6.5.\n^ôdiürriJ; 1 ’ potassium, 5, calcium, 2; bicarbonate \nlactate), 29 and Chloride, 111; (2) the tdtal osmolar \nconcentration in mOsmol per litre; (3) that the inj ection should \nnot be used if it contains visible particles. ."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/compound-sodium-lactate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Compound Sodium Lactate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"COMPOUND SODIUM’ LACTATE INJECTION\nHALF STRENGTH COMPOUND SODIUM LACTATE AND DEXTROSE INJECTION\n; \\ IP 2022\nDe