e than 0.32 per cent\nw/v of sodium, Na, not less than 0.019 per cent and ndt-more \nthan 0.022 per cent w/v of potassium, K, not less than 0.37 per\ncent and not more than 0.42 percent w/v of total chloride, Cl \nnot less than 0.025 per cent and not, more than 0.029 per cent \nw/v of calcium chloride, CaCb^HjO, and not less than \n0.23 per cent ¿rid not more than 0.28 per cent w/v of lactate \ncalculated as C3H6O3. It contains no antimicrobial agent.\nCategory. Irrigation fluid.\nDescription. Aclear,.colourless,solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. -When warmed with potassium permanganate gives \nacetaldehyde, recognisable by its odour.\nB. The residue on evaporation, when moistened with \nhydrochloric acid and introduced on a platinum wire into a \nflame imparts a yellow colour to the flame. When viewed \nthrough a suitable blue glass, the flame is tinged, reddish \npurple. \n■\nC. It gives reaction (C) of calcium salts and reaction (A) of \nchlorides (2.3.1). \n■ \n.......... : \n; ."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 7.0. .. \n:\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.5 Endotoxin Unit \nperml.' \n■\" \n...\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Inj ections): ’ \n; - ■'' A ■\nAssay. For sodium:— Dilute suitably with water and detenpine \nby Method A for flame photometry (2.4.4), or by Method Afor \natomic absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.2), measuring'a! \n589 hfh and using sodium solution FP or sodium solution \nA AS respectively, suitably diluted with waterforthc standard \nsolutions.; \n' '/•\nFor potassium — Dilute suitably with water and determine \nby Method A for flame photometry (2.4.4), or by Method Afor \natomic absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.2), measuring at \n767 nm and using potassium solution FP or potassium \nsolution JglS respectively, suitably diluted with \nstandard solutions. \n. _ , \n.\nFor calcium chloride • — To 50.0 ml add 5.0 ml\n0.01 Mmagnesium sulphate and 5 ml of ammo