:"GEMFIBROZIL CAPSULES \nGEMCITABINE INJECTION\nTime \t\nMobile phase A \n( (in min.) \t\n(per cent v/v) \n0 \t\n100 \n5 \t\n100 \n20 \t\n0 \n25 \t\n0 \n28 \t\n100\nGemfibrozil \nMobile phase B\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.25 per cent, determined on \n2.0 g.\n(per cent v/v)\n0 \n0\nAssay. Dissolve 0.2 g in 25 ml of methanol, add 25 ml of water \nand 1 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid. Titrate with 0.1 Msodium \nhydroxide, determining the end point potentiometrically \n(2.4.25). Read the volume added between the 2 points of \ninflexion.\n100 \n0\nH 3C\n1 ml of 0. 1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.02503 g of \nC1 5112203 •\nRelative \t\nCorrection \nretention time \t\nfactor\nCH3\nGemfibrozil impurity A' \t0.4\nStorage. Store protected from light.\n0.5 \nGemfibrozil (Retention time: \nabout 7 minutes) \t\n1.0\nC15142203 \t\nMol. Wt. 250.3\nGemfibrozil impurity C2 \t1.3\nGemfibrozil is 5-(2,5-dimethylphenoxy)-2,2-dimethylpentanoic\n0.58 \nGemfibrozil impurity D 3 \t1.5 \t\n#0' \t\n1.8 \nGemfibrozil impurity E4 \t1.7\nGemfibrozil Capsules\nacid.\nGemfibrozil Capsules contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ngemfibrozil, C 15H2203.\nGemfibrozil contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C15112203, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\n0.5 \nGemfibrozil impurity Is \t2.0 \nGemfibrozil impurity H 6 \t2.9\n0.6\nUsual strengths. 300 mg; 600 mg.\nCategory. Hypolipidemic.\n'2,5-dimethylphenol,\nDose. 900 to 1500 mg daily."},{"name":"Identification","text":"2243-(2-ethoxyethoxy)propoxy]-1,4-dimethylbenzene,\nDescription. A white or almost white, waxy, crystalline powder.\n35 [3,6-dimethy1-2-(prop-1-enyl)phenoxy]-2,2-dimethylpentanoi c acid,\nShake a quantity of the contents of the capsules containing \n0.5 g of Gemfibrozil with 10 ml ofn-hexane for 10 minutes, filter \nthrough a filter paper previously washed with 20 ml of \nn-hexane, evaporate the filtrate to dryness on a water-bath \nand then dry over silica gel at a pressure of 2 kPa for 2 hours \nor until a waxy solid is obtained.\n45 [2,5-dimethy1-4-(prop-1-enyl)phcnoxy]-2,2-dimethylpentanoic acid,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"'methyl 5-(2,5-dimethylphenoxy)-2,2-dimethylpentanoate,\n61 ,3-bis(2 ,5-dimethylphenoxy)propane.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with gemfibrozil RS\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nor with the reference spectrum of gemfibrozil.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with gemfibrozil RS or with the reference spectrum \nof gemfibrozil."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Inject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak due to gemfibrozil impurities E and I is not more than \ntwice the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (0.2 per cent). The area of \nany peak due to gemfibrozil impurities A, D and H is not more \nthan the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (0.1 per cent). The area of \nany other secondary peak is not more than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution (0.1 per cent). The sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 5 times the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 times \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution (0.05 per cent).\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Test solution. Dissolve 40 mg of the substance under \nexamination in mobile phase A and dilute to 10.0 ml with mobile \nphase A.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1, \nMedium. 900 ml of 0.2 M phosphate buffer pH 7.5, \nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 30 minutes.\nReference solution. A 0.0004 per cent w/v solution of\ngemfibrozil RS in mobile phase A.\nChromatographic system\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtered solution immediately, suitably \ndiluted with the dissolution medium, if necessary, at the \nmaximum at about 276 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of \nCI5H2203  in the medium from the absorbance obtained from a \nsolution of known concentration of gemfibrozil RS in the \ndissolution medium, adding the minimum quantity of 0.1 M \nsodium hydroxide, if necessary, for complete dissolution.\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with \nendcapped octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica\n(5 11m), \n-\nmobile phase: A. dissolve 0.58 g of sodium acetate in\n400 ml of water, adjusted to pH 4.0 with orthophosphoric\n acid \nand add 600 ml of acetonitrile,\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nB. acetonitrile,\n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n-flow rate' 1.5 ml per minute,\nD.\n-  Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of C\nI 5H2203 . \n- \nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent, \n\t_ \n2 .0 g. Allow to stand for 1 hour after the moistening befo.re \nheating.\n-spectrophotometer set at 276 nm, \n-- injection volume: 20 pl.\n24,14 \t\n-\\\nGEMFIBROZIL CAPSULES\nDisperse a quantity of the contents of the \nTest solution. Disp  \ncapsules containing 0.4 g of Gemfibrozil with\nmethanol and\ndilute to 100.0 ml with methanol.\nDilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to \n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Further dilute 1.0 ml of this\nReference solution (a).\nsolution to 5.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nA 0.0004 per cent wlv solution of\ngemfibrozil imp urity ,4 \t\nlenol RS) in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (h).\nRS (p-xy\nA solution containing 0.001 per cent\nwlv of gemfibrozil methyl ester RS and 0.0004 per cent w/v of\nReference solution (c).\ngemfibrozil impurity A RS in reference solution (a).\nUse chromatogr\nltion (c). The test is not val\nInject reference solution\nresolution between the peaks due to gemfibrozil and gemfibrozil\nmethyl ester is not less than 4.0 and the resolution e\nemfibrozil methyl ester and gemfibrzil\nthe peaks due to g\nimpurity A is not less than 1.2.\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution. Run\nthe chromatogram twice the retention time of the principal\npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution,\nthe area of any peak corresponding to gemfibrozil impurity A \neak in the chromatogram \nis not more than the area of the p \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.1 per cent). The area of C191-124FN5S07\nany other secondary peak \n\tMesylate\npe\nthe area of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with methanesulphonate.\nreference solution (a) (0.5 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply With \t\non the anhydrous basis. \nh the tests stated under Capsules.\n14 \nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.).\nMix the contents of 20 capsules. Disperse a\nl \t\n320 mg daily. \nTest solution.\nshake on a mechanical shaker for 10 minutes,\nadd 20 ml of water, 1 ml of glacial acetic acid\n100.0 ml with methanol, filter. Dilute 1.0 ml of the filtrate to\n5.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 30 mg of g\nl of methanol, add 1 ml of glacial acetic acid and dilute\n80 m \nto 100.0 ml with water.\nReferenc solution (b). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of\nin a solution prepared by diluting\ngemfibrozil methyl ester RS\n1 volume of the test solution to 3 volumes with the mobile\nphase.\nChromatographic system\n(311m),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 1 volume\nacid, 19 volumes of Water and 80 volumes of methanol,"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gemifloxacin-mesylate-sparingly-soluble-in-water-very","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gemifloxacin Mesylate Sparingly soluble in water; very"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gemifloxacin Mesylate Sparingly soluble in water; very \nslightly soluble in acetonitrile;- practically insoluble in \nmethanol.-\nGentamicin Sulphate. Freely soluble in water; practically \ninsoluble in. chloroform, in ethanol (95 percent) and in ether.\nGlibenclamide. Sparingly soluble in dichloromethane; slightly \nsoluble in ethanol (95per cent) and in methanol; practically \ninsoluble in water and ether. \n' ■\nGliclazide. Freely soluble in dichloromethane; sparingly \nsoluble in acetone; slightly soluble in ethanol,\nGlimepiride. Soluble in dimethylformamide; sparingly soluble \nin dlchloromethane; slightly soluble in methanol; practically \ninsoluble in water.\nGlipizide. Very slightly soluble in.dlchloromethane and \nacetone; practically, insoluble in ethanol (95. per cent/and in \nwater. .\nGlucosamine Sulphate Sodium Chloride. Freely soluble in \nwater; sparingly soluble in methanol; practically insoluble in \nacetone. .\nGlutamic Acid. Freely soluble in water; practically insoluble \nin ethanol and in ether. \n.\nGlycerin. Miscible with water and withet&ano/ (95 percent); \nslightly soluble in acetone; practically insoluble in ether and \nin fixed oils and;yolatile oils. ... \n. / \n.. \n/\nGlyceryl Monostearate. Freely soluble in chloroform; soluble \nm ether, in benzene and in ethanol (95 per cent); practically \ninsoluble in water.\nGlyceryl Trinitrate Solution, Concentrated. Miscible with \nacetone and withetfeer.' .\nGlycine.-Freely soluble m. water; very slightly soluble in \nethanal (95per cent); practically insoluble in ether. w\nGlycopyrrdlate. Solublein water wadethcihbl (9 5 per cent);\npractically insoluble in chloroform and in ether. ■\nGramicidin. Soluble ih methanol; -sparingly soluble ih ethanol \n^d^ercen/y; practically insoluble mwatez; \n■\nGranisetron. Soluble to freely soluble in methylene chloride\na!id in methanol. '■ \n. ■' \n' \n.: \n'.■■\nGranisetron Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in water, sparingly \nsoluble in methylene chloride, slightly soluble in methanol;\nGris eofu Ivin. Freely soluble in dimethylformamide and in\n1,1,2,2-tetrachloroethane; soluble in acetone and in \nchloroform; slightly soluble in ethanol (95 per cent) and in \nmethanol; practically insoluble ih water.\nGuaiphenesin. Soluble in ethanol (95 per cent) and in \nchloroform; sparingly soluble in water; slightly soluble in \nether.\nGuar Gum; When stirred with 50 parts ofwater, a thick jelly is \nformed which, with further addition of 150 parts of water, \nyields a thick transparent suspension; practically insoluble in \nethanol (95 per cent).\nGnggul Resin. Sparingly soluble in ethanol (95percent), in \nmethanol and in chloroform; practically insoluble in water\nGugulipid. Soluble in ethanol (95per cent), in methanol, in \nethyl acetate, and in chloroform; insoluble in water. ■\nHaloperidol. Soluble in chloroform; sparingly soluble in \nethanol (95 per cent) and in dlchloromethane; slightly \nsoluble in ether, practically insoluble in water.\nHaloxon. Freely soluble in ethanol (95 per cent), in \ndichloromethane and in acetone; practically insoluble in \nwater.\nHeparin Sodium. Soluble in water and in saline solution \nI\nforming a clear, colourless or straw-coloured liquid. :\nHistamine Phosphate. Freely soluble in water; slightly soluble \nin ethanol (95 per cent).\nHomatropine Hydrobromide. Freely soluble in water; sparingly \nsoluble in ethanol (95per cent); very slightly soluble in ether.\nHomatropine Methylbromide. Freely soluble in water and in \ndilute ethanol (95 per cent); slightly soluble in absolute \nethanol (95 per cent); practically insoluble in ether;\nHyaluronidase. Very soluble in water; practically insoluble in \nelhhnol (95 per cent); in acetone and ih ether."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Gemifloxacin Mesylate Sparingly soluble in water; very \nslightly soluble in acetonitrile;- practically insoluble in \nmethanol.-\nGentamicin Sulphate. Freely soluble in water; practically \ninsoluble in. chloroform, in ethanol (95 percent) and in ether.\nGlibenclamide. Sparingly soluble in dichloromethane; slightly \nsoluble in ethanol (95per cent) and in methanol; practically \ninsoluble in water and ether. \n' ■\nGliclazide. Freely soluble in dichloromethane; sparingly \nsoluble in acetone; slightly soluble in ethanol,\nGlimepiride. Soluble in dimethylformamide; sparingly soluble \nin dlchloromethane; slightly soluble in methanol; practically \ninsoluble in water.\nGlipizide. Very slightly soluble in.dlchloromethane and \nacetone; practically, insoluble in ethanol (95. per cent/and in \nwater. .\nGlucosamine Sulphate Sodium Chloride. Freely soluble in \nwater; sparingly soluble in methanol; practically insoluble in \nacetone. .\nGlutamic Acid. Freely soluble in water; practically insoluble \nin ethanol and in ether. \n.\nGlycerin. Miscible with water and withet&ano/ (95 percent); \nslightly soluble in acetone; practically insoluble in ether and \nin fixed oils and;yolatile oils. ... \n. / \n.. \n/\nGlyceryl Monostearate. Freely soluble in chloroform; soluble \nm ether, in benzene and in ethanol (95 per cent); practically \ninsoluble in water.\nGlyceryl Trinitrate Solution, Concentrated. Miscible with \nacetone and withetfeer.' .\nGlycine.-Freely soluble m. water; very slightly soluble in \nethanal (95per cent); practically insoluble in ether. w\nGlycopyrrdlate. Solublein water wadethcihbl (9 5 per cent);\npractically insoluble in chloroform and in ether. ■\nGramicidin. Soluble ih methanol; -sparingly soluble ih ethanol \n^d^ercen/y; practically insoluble mwatez; \n■\nGranisetron. Soluble to freely soluble in methylene chloride\na!id in methanol. '■ \n. ■' \n' \n.: \n'.■■\nGranisetron Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in water, sparingly \nsoluble in methylene chloride, slightly soluble in methanol;\nGris eofu Ivin. Freely soluble in dimethylformamide and in\n1,1,2,2-tetrachloroethane; soluble in acetone and in \nchloroform; slightly soluble in ethanol (95 per cent) and in \nmethanol; practically insoluble ih water.\nGuaiphenesin. Soluble in ethanol (95 per cent) and in \nchloroform; sparingly soluble in water; slightly soluble in \nether.\nGuar Gum; When stirred with 50 parts ofwater, a thick jelly is \nformed which, with further addition of 150 parts of water, \nyields a thick transparent suspension; practically insoluble in \nethanol (95 per cent).\nGnggul Resin. Sparingly soluble in ethanol (95percent), in \nmethanol and in chloroform; practically insoluble in water\nGugulipid. Soluble in ethanol (95per cent), in methanol, in \nethyl acetate, and in chloroform; insoluble in water. ■\nHaloperidol. Soluble in chloroform; sparingly soluble in \nethanol (95 per cent) and in dlchloromethane; slightly \nsoluble in ether, practically insoluble in water.\nHaloxon. Freely soluble in ethanol (95 per cent), in \ndichloromethane and in acetone; practically insoluble in \nwater.\nHeparin Sodium. Soluble in water and in saline solution \nI\nforming a clear, colourless or straw-coloured liquid. :\nHistamine Phosphate. Freely soluble in water; slightly soluble \nin ethanol (95 per cent).\nHomatropine Hydrobromide. Freely soluble in water; sparingly \nsoluble in ethanol (95per cent); very slightly soluble in ether.\nHomatropine Methylbromide. Freely soluble in water and in \ndilute ethanol (95 per cent); slightly soluble in absolute \nethanol (95 per cent); practically insoluble in ether;\nHyaluronidase. Very soluble in water; practically insoluble in \nelhhnol (95 per cent); in acetone and ih ether."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gemifloxacin-mesylate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gemifloxacin