ablets. ’ \n~\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. 7"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/prothionamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Prothionamide"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Prothionamide\nNCH3\nD. It gives reaction (A) of sulphates (2.3.1).\nInject the reference solution.The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Prothionamide\nNCH3\nD. It gives reaction (A) of sulphates (2.3.1).\nInject the reference solution.The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 3.5.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nindividual impurity is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.5 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than twice the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(1.0 per cent).\nN H 2\nOptical rotation (2.4.22). -0.52° to -0.68°, determined in a \nsolution prepared by diluting the injection with 0.5 M \nhydrochloric acid so as to contain 0.8 per cent w/v of Protamine \nSulphate.\nC91-112N2S \t\nMol. wt. 180.3\nProthionamide is 2-propyl-4-pyridinecarbothioamide.\nProthionamide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C 81-1 10N2S ,calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nLight absorption. Dilute the injection, if necessary, with water \nto produce a solution containing 1 per cent w/v solution of \nProtamine Sulphate. Absorbance of the resulting solution at \n260 to 280 nm, not more than 0.1 (2.4.7).\nHeavy Metals (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy tneta... 1 s, Method B (10 ppm).\nCategory. Antitubercular.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 7.0 Endotoxin Units \nper mg of protamine sulphate.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Loss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105\" for 3 hours.\nAbnormal toxicity. Complies with the test for abnormal toxicity \n(2.2.1), using a volume containing 0.5 mg of Protamine \nSulphate.\nA.Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with prothionamide. \nRS.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as \ndescribed in the Related substances using the following \nsolutions.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nB.When examined in the range 230 nm to 350 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.002 per cent w/v solution in ethanol (95 per cent) shows an \nabsorption maximum only at about 291 nm. The absorbance at \n291 nm is about 0.78.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50.0 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 100.0 ml with the \nmobile phase. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to a 50.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nAssay. Prepare solutions of the substance under examination \nin water containing (1) 0.015 per cent w/v (2) 0.01 per cent \nw/v and (3) 0.005 per cent w/v. Titrate each of the solutions in \nduplicate with a 174 IU per ml or a suitable dilution of heparin \nsodium RS using the following procedure. Introduce a"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Reference solution. A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of \nprothionamide RS in the mobile phase. Dilute 5.0 ml of the\nAcidity. Dissolve 2.0 g in 20 ml of methanol, heating to about \n50°, and add 20 ml of water. Cool slightlyv-\"Thake \ncrystallisation occurs, if any and allow to COO to room \ntemperature. Add 60 ml of water and titrate with. 0.1 sodium\nmeasured volume of the solution to be titrated, for example\nsoluticm to 50.0.m1 with the mobile phase.\n1.5 ml, into -the cell of a suitable spectrophotometer, set the \ninstrument ata-suitable wavelength (none is critical) in the \nv,Wble tans; and add the titrant in small volumes until there Is\nInjecf.the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0, the column efficiency is not\nless than 5000 theoretical plates and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C 9H 12N2S.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/protriptyline-hydrochloride-freely-soluble-in-water-and-in","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Protriptyline Hydrochloride; Freely soluble in water and in"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Protriptyline Hydrochloride; Freely soluble in water and in \nethanol (95per cent); practically insoluble in ether: ■\nPseudoephedrine Hydrochloride. Very soluble in water; freely \nsoluble in-ethanol (95 per cent); sparingly soluble m \nchloroform. \n■ ‘\n2.4.26, SOLUBILITY'\nipsov\nPsoralen. Very soluble in chloroform; soluble in ethanol Raloxifene Hydrochloride. Freely soluble^ iffi\nspxiLgi.v.sclubk' in erter; practically insoluble dimethylsulphoxide; sparingly soluble in methanol', slightly \nin light petroleum (60a to 8(f). \nsoluble in ethanol; very slightly; soluble in water and in-\nPvrantelPamoate. Soluble indimelhylsulphoxide; practically ^opropyI alcohol, practically insoluble in. ether and in ethyl.\ninsoluble in water and in jnethanoL \nc -\nSparingly soluble in water and in cWorq^Mw; R“l,lVravk Potassium. Soluble in slightly soluble irK\nin ethanol (95 per cent); very slightly soluble \nvery slightly soluble m acetonitrile and in ethanol;\nand practically insoluble in-isopropyl'alcohol.\nineiher.\nPyridoStig^^^ \n^^^teon. Soluble in metfemoZ and insoluble in water,"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Protriptyline Hydrochloride; Freely soluble in water and in \nethanol (95per cent); practically insoluble in ether: ■\nPseudoephedrine Hydrochloride. Very soluble in water; freely \nsoluble in-ethanol (95 per cent); sparingly soluble m \nchloroform. \n■ ‘\n2.4.26, SOLUBILITY'\nipsov\nPsoralen. Very soluble in chloroform; soluble in ethanol Raloxifene Hydrochloride. Freely soluble^ iffi\nspxiLgi.v.sclubk' in erter; practically insoluble dimethylsulphoxide; sparingly soluble in methanol', slightly \nin light petroleum (60a to 8(f). \nsoluble in ethanol; very slightly; soluble in water and in-\nPvrantelPamoate. Soluble indimelhylsulphoxide; practically ^opropyI alcohol, practically insoluble in. ether and in ethyl.\ninsoluble in water and in jnethanoL \nc -\nSparingly soluble in water and in cWorq^Mw; R“l,lVravk Potassium. Soluble in slightly soluble irK\nin ethanol (95 per cent); very slightly soluble \nvery slightly soluble m acetonitrile and in ethanol;\nand practically insoluble in-isopropyl'alcohol.\nineiher.\nPyridoStig^^^ \n^^^teon. Soluble in metfemoZ and insoluble in water,"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/protriptyline-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Protriptyline Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Protriptyline Hydrochloride\nProtriptyline Hydrochloride is 3-(5//-dibenzo[«,c/|cyclohept- \n5-yl)propyi(methyl)amine hydrochloride. , \n. .? J . , .y\nProtriptyline' Hydrochloride contains hbt less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci9H21N,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis. \n7.\nCategory. Tricyclicantidepressant. ; \n7-\nDescription. A white to yellowish white powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Protriptyline Hydrochloride\nProtriptyline Hydrochloride is 3-(5//-dibenzo[«,c/|cyclohept- \n5-yl)propyi(methyl)amine hydrochloride. , \n. .? J . , .y\nProtriptyline' Hydrochloride contains hbt less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci9H21N,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis. \n7.\nCategory. Tricyclicantidepressant. ; \n7-\nDescription. A white to yellowish white powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A.: Dissolve .0.1 g in10 ml .of water, make alkaline with 7M \nsodium hydroxide, extract with 5 ml of chloroform, dry with \nanhydrous sodium:sulphate. and evaporate the solvent using \na current of nitrogen. On the oily residue/determine, by the\nIP 2022 \n< PROTRIPTYLINE ABLETS\ninfrared ^absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the \nspectrum with that obtained with protriptyline hydrochloride \nIPPS treated in the same manner or with the reference spectrum \nof protriptyline."},{"name":"Tests","text":"B- It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (?-4 .24). 5.0 to 6.5 , determined in a 1.0 per cent w/v solution.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 60° at apressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.7 g in 0.5 ml of acetic anhydride and 20 ml \nof anhydrous glacial acetic acid, add 10 ml of mercuric \nacet ate. so hit ion. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, using \ncrystal violet solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\nLml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02998 g of \nCI9H21N,HCL"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/protriptyline-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Protriptyline Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Protriptyline Tablets\nProtriptyline Hydrochloride Tablets\nProtriptyline Tablets contain not less than 90.0 percent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nprotriptyline hydrochloride, Ci9H2IN,HCl.\nUsual strength. 5 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Protriptyline Tablets\nProtriptyline Hydrochloride Tablets\nProtriptyline Tablets contain not less than 90.0 percent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nprotriptyline hydrochloride, Ci9H2IN,HCl.\nUsual strength. 5 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Ai When examined in the range 230 to 350 nm (2.4.7), a solution \nobtained in the Assay exhibits, a maximum only at 292 nm.\nShake a ,quantity of the powdered tablets containing about \n5 mg of Protriptyline Hydrochloride with 20 ml of methanol \n(^'filter. To 1 ml ofthe filtrate add 1 ml'of a 2.5 per cent w/v\n. solution of sodium hydrogen carbonate, 1 ml of a 2 per cent\nw/v solution of sodium periodate and 1 ml bf a 0.3 per cent \nw/v ^plbtioh of potassium permanganate. Allow to stand for\n15minutes, acidity with 1M sulphuric acid and extract with \n'10-ml of 2,2,4-trimethylpentane, washing the extract with\n10 ml of 0.25 M sulphuric acid. The absorbance of \nfrimethyipentanc solution does hot exhibit a maxima at 265 rim\nfrom amitriptyline and nortriptyline).\nC. Triturate a quantity of the powdered: tablets containing \nabout 0.1 g of Protriptyline Hydrochloride with 10 ml of \nchloroform, filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness. \nDissolve part of the residue in 3 ml of water and add 0.05 ml bf \na 2-5. per cent w/v solution of quinhydrone in methanol. A red \ncolour develops. : \n- \n■\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, suitably diluted with the medium, \nif necessary, at the maxima at about 290 nm (2.4.7). Calculate \nthe content of Ci9H2lN,HCl in the medium from the absorbance \nobtained from a solution of known concentration of \nprotriptyline hydrochloride IPRS in dissolution medium.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent: of the stated amount of \nC19H2]N,HC1.\nUniformity of content. Disperse one tablet in 50 ml of a solution \nprepared by mixing 1 volume of 1Mhydrochloric dcid and 9 \nvolumes of methanol and dilute to 100.0 ml with the same \nsolution, Shake well and filter, discard the first few ml of filtrate \nand dilute a volume of the filtrate containing 1 mg of \nprotriptyline hydrochloride to 100 ml with the same solution \nand measure the absorbance at the maximum at 292 nm (2.4.7). \nCalculate the content of C19H2iN,HCl taking 465 as the specific \nabsorbance at 292 nm.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14),\nBuffer solution. .Dissolve 11., g of monobasic sodium \nphosphate and 1.9 g of sodium .1-hexanesulphonate, in 1000\nml of water, adjusted to pH 2.9 with orthophosphoric acid.\nSolventmixture. Dilute 200 ml of ethanol and 40 ml of 2.5 M \nhydrochloric acid ancl dilute to 1000 ml with water.\nTest solution. Disperse 5 intact tablets in the solvent mixture \nand dilute to obtain a solution containing 0.02 per cent w/v \nprotriptyline hydrochloride in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of \nprotriptyline hydrochloride IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\na stainless-steelcolumn 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctylsilarie bonded to porous silica (5 jim), ’\n- column temperature: 30° \n1\n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 83 yolumes.of the buffer\nsolution and 17 volumes of acetonitrile,\nB. a mixture of 50 volumes of the buffer \nsolution and 50 volumes of acetonitrile ,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flowrate: 1.5 nil per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n;\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B :\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent y/v)\n0\n100\n15\n0\n. i(X)\n20\n0 . , ;\n25\n100\n0 \n- P\n^■:;y^:/:36\";\n'TOO'\nt is not valid unless the \nid the relative standard\ndeviation.for rephcate.inj ections is notmore than2.0 per cent.\nInject the referer \ntailing factor is i\nice solution,. The tes \nlot. more than , 2,5: ai\nInj ect the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of Ci9H2iN,HCl in the tablets. ,"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/pseudoephedrine-hydrochloride-120-mg","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride, 120 mg"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride, 120 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride, 120 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. In the Assay, the principal peaks in the chromatogram \nobtained with test solution (b) correspond to the principal \npeaks in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution\nCO­\nES. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2,4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel G. .\nMobile phase. A mixture of 50 volumes of toluene, 45 volumes\nof ethanol and 5 volumes of ammonium hydroxide.\nTest solution. Transfer a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 30 mg of Fexofenadine Hydrochloride to a 10-ml \nvolumetric flask, add 5.0 ml of methanol and shake vigorously \nfor 2 minutes, filter.\nReference solution. Dissolve suitable quantities of \nfexofenadine hydrochloride IPRS and pseudoephedrine\nhydrochloride IPRS in methanol to obtain a solution having \nconcentration similar to that of the test solution.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solutions. After development, \ndry the plate in air. Heat the plate at 105° until the odour of \nammonia disappears. Allow the plate to cool and examine under \nultraviolet light at 254 nm. The Rf value of the principal spots \nin the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorrespond to the spots in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution:\nNOTE —The Rfvalues of fexofenadine and pseudoephedrine \nare 0.17 and 0.39 respectively. ■ \n'."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/pseudoephedrine-hydrochloride-syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride Syrup\nPseudoephedrine Syrup is a solution of Pseudoephedrine\nHydrochloride in a suitable flavoured vehicle.\nPseudoephedrine Syrup contains not less than 90.0 percent\nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the. stated amount of\npseudoephedrine hydrochloride, CIOH1SNO, HCI.\nUsual strength. 30 mg per 5 ml."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride Syrup\nPseudoephedrine Syrup is a solution of Pseudoephedrine\nHydrochloride in a suitable flavoured vehicle.\nPseudoephedrine Syrup contains not less than 90.0 percent\nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the. stated amount of\npseudoephedrine hydrochloride, CIOH1SNO, HCI.\nUsual strength. 30 mg per 5 ml."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Shake a quantity of the syrup containing 120 mg of\nPseudoephedrine Hydrochloride with two quantifies, each of\n30 ml, of ether, and discard the ether layer. Add 4 ml of\n1 M sodium hydroxide to the aqueous layer and extract with\ntwo quantities, each of10 ml, ofether. Dry the combined ether\nextracts with anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and evaporate\nthe filtrate to dryness.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption\nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that\nobtained with pseudoephedrine hydrochloride RS treated in\nthe saame manner or with the reference spectrum of\npseudoephedrine.\nB. The residue obtained in testA melts at about U8° (2.4.21).\nC. Dissolve 50 mg ofthe residue obtained in testA in 10 ml of\n0.1 M hydrochloric acid; it is dextro-rotatory."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other tests. Complies with the tests stated under Oral Liquids.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. To an accurately measured volume ofthe syrup\ncontaining about 0.12 g of Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride,\nadd 50 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid mix, add sufficient\n0.1 M hydrochloric acid to produce 100.0 ml, filter and use\nthe filtrate.\nReference solution. A 0.12 per cent w/v solution of\npseudoephedrine hydrochloride RS in 0.1 M hydrochloric\nacid.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.2 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 /lm),\nmobile phase: a mixture of 85 volumes ofethanoland\n15 volumes ofa 0.4 per cent w/vsolution ofammonium\nacetate,\nflow rate. 1.5 mlperminute,\nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\ninjection volume. 20 /li.\nInject the reference solution and record the chromatogram.\nThe test is not valid unless the relative standard deviation is\nnot more than 2.0 per cent and the tailing factor is not more\nthan 1.5.\nInject altemately the test solution and the reference solution..\nDetermine the weightperml ofthe syrup (2.4.29), and calculate\nthe content ofCIOHlsNO, HCl weight in volume.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/pseudoephedrine-hydrochloride-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride Tablets\nPseudoephedrine Tablets contain not less than 95.0.per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \np seudoephedrine hydrochloride, C; 0H l5NO, HCI.\nUsual strength. 60 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride Tablets\nPseudoephedrine Tablets contain not less than 95.0.per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \np seudoephedrine hydrochloride, C; 0H l5NO, HCI.\nUsual strength. 60 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 60 mg \nof Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride with 10 ml of water and \nfilter. Shake the filtrate with 10 ml of ether, discard the ether \nlayer: Add 1 ml off Msodium hydroxide to the aqueous layer \nand extract with two quantities, each of 10 ml, of ether: Dry the \ncoinbined ether extracts with anhydrous sodium sulphate, \nfilter and evaporate the' filtrate to dryness.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption, \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with pseudoephedrine hydrochloride IPRS treated \nin the same manner or with the reference spectrum of \npseudoephedrine.\nB. In the test for Related substances, the principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution (b) corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes, .\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\n3395\nwww.webofpharma.com\nPSEUDOEPHEDRINE; TABLETS\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). •;\ntest solution. Use the filtrate, dilute if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium. '.f ' \n■\nReference solution. Dissolve a quantity of pseudoephedrine \nhydrochloride IPRS in the dissolution medium, to obtain a \nsolution having a known concentration similar to the test \nsolution. \n;r.. ;< / \n;\nChromatographic system ■' ■ /\na stainless steel column 25 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\n.. porous silica(5 pm), : \n..... \n>:-r< \n- ...\n. -mobile phase: a mixture of 3 volumes of 0.4 per cent w/v\nsolution of ammonium acetate and 17 volumes of \nethanol,\n- flow rale: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 214 nm, . .\n- inj ection volume: 10 p.1.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and; the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C i;)H s 5NO,I IC1, in the medium. J\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\nc10h15no,hci. :'\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel G. \n. ...\nMobile phase. A mixture of 40 volumes of butyl acetate, \n20 volumes ofacetone, 20 volumes of 1-butanol;)® volumes \nof 5 M ammoh ia and 10 volumes of methanol.\nTest solution(a). Add 25 ml of methanol to a quantity of the \npowdered tablets. containing 0.5. g .of Pseudoephedrine \nHydrochloride, shake for 5 minutes, filter,.wash the filter with \nmethanol and evaporate the. combined filtrate and washings \nto dryness. Dissolve the residue as completely as possible in \n5 ml of methanol, centrifuge and use the supernatant liquid;\nTest solution (b). Dilute 1 volume of the test solution to \n10 volumes with methanol. , \n.......\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1 volume of the test sohition to\n100 volumes with methanol.\nReference, solution, (b)A:1.0 per ■ cent w/v solution of \npseudoephedrine hydrochloride IPRS in methanol. T ;; .\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in a current of warm air, spray with a solution \ncontaining 0.3 g of ninhydrin in a mixture of 100 ml I-butanol \nand 3 ml of glacial acetic acid and heat at 120°. for 20 minutes. \nAny secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with test \nsolution (a) is not more intense than the spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). Ignore \nany yellow spot near the line of application.:\nIP 202$!\n'w: ®\n............................ ..........-...........    ■;..............   ,f |\nOther;tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets:: sy y A |\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). ' \n|\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. To a quantity of $ i\nthe powdered tablets containing about 0.12 g of J f\nPseudoephedrine Hydrochloride, add 50 ml of O.fM $ 1\nhydrochloric acid mix with the aid of ultrasound for 15 minutes,\" y |\nadd sufficient \nto produce 100.0 ml, filter and use the C |\nfiltrate. \n|\nReference solution. A 0.12 per cent w/v solution of |\npseudoephedrine hydrochloride IPRS in methanol (50 per\nc