pal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the, test solution, \nthe area of any secondary peak corresponding to pyridoxine \nimpurity B is not more than 1.5 times the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.15 per cent), the area of any other secondary peak is not \nmore than the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per cent) and the sum \nof areas of all the secondary peaks is not more than twice the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent). Ignore any peak with\nah arest less than 0.5 times the area of thè principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.05 percent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 12 ml of a 5.0 per cent w/v solution \ncomplies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method D \n(20 ppm). Use lead standard solution (1 ppm Pb) to prepare \nthe standard.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18).! Not mòre than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay, Dissolve 0.45,-g in a mixture of 5 ml . of anhydrous \nglacial acetic acid and 6 ml of mercuric acetate solution.\nTitrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, using crystal v/0/ej \nsolution as indicator, until a green colour is; produced,\nout a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02056 gOf \nC8HnNO3,HCl. \n; f\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.'"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/pyridoxine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pyridoxine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Pyridoxine Tablets\nPyridoxine Hydrochloride Tablets \n\\ L ?\nPyridoxine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of pyridoxine \nhydrochloride, CsHhNO3, MCI.\nUsual strength. 5mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Pyridoxine Tablets\nPyridoxine Hydrochloride Tablets \n\\ L ?\nPyridoxine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of pyridoxine \nhydrochloride, CsHhNO3, MCI.\nUsual strength. 5mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 20 mg \nof Pyridoxine Hydrochloride with 50 ml of0.025:Mstandard \nphosphate buffer for 15 minutes and dilute to 100 ml with the\nsame solvent. Mix, filter and dilute 5 ml of the filtrate to 100 'ml- \nwith the same solvent. When examined in the range 230 nmto \n360 nm (2.4.7), the resulting solution exhibits two maxima, at \nabout254nmand324nm. ' \n¡hi-v ooh-,\nB. Triturate a quantity of the powdered tablets containing \n20 mg of Pyridoxine Hydrochloride with 50 ini of water and \nallow to stand for 20 minutes. To 1 ml of the supernatantliquid \nadd 10 ml of a 5 per cent w/v solution of sodium acetate, 1 ml \nof water and I ml of a 0.5 per cent w/v solution of2,6-di- \nchloroquinone-4-chloroimide in ethanol (95. percent) \nshake; a blue colour is produced which fades rapidly and \nbecomes brown. Repeat the operation but adding 1 ml of a \n0.3 per cent w/v solution of boric acid in place of 1 ml of \nwater; no blue colour is produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"'■\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes. \n.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter,:. ,s\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Use the filtrate, dilute if necessary, with the\ndissolution medium. \n/; ; <;\nReference solution. Dissolve a suitable quantity Qipyridoxine \nhydrochloride IPRS in the dissolution medium to obtain a \nsolution of a known concentration similar to the expecte \nconcentration of the test solution.\nIlli,...-.-. ..-. . ...\n■ ;^^^ôgràphic system.\n/a stainless steel column 30 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\n;O^cïa3ëcÿîsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n¿cLhnibbile phase: a mixture of 73 volumes of 0.14 per cent\nw/v solution of sodium 1-hexane sulphonate in water,\n■ 27 volumes of methanol and 1 volume of glacial acetic\n: f : acid, -\n^I flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n.QiHlspectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\n■ \ninjection volume: 10 pl.\njnjèWilÎë reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 3.0 per cent.\njnjeet the reference solution and the test solution."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/pyridylazonaphthol-pan-1-2-pyridylazo-2-naphthol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pyridylazonaphthol; PAN; 1-(2-Pyridylazo)-2-naphthol:"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Pyridylazonaphthol; PAN; 1-(2-Pyridylazo)-2-naphthol:\nC1sHl1N30=249.27\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nBrick red or orange-red powder; mp, about 140\n0\n•\nlP 2010\nPyridylazonaphthol Solution: A 0.1 per cent w/v solution in\nethanol.\nComplies with the following test."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Pyridylazonaphthol; PAN; 1-(2-Pyridylazo)-2-naphthol:\nC1sHl1N30=249.27\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nBrick red or orange-red powder; mp, about 140\n0\n•\nlP 2010\nPyridylazonaphthol Solution: A 0.1 per cent w/v solution in\nethanol.\nComplies with the following test."},{"name":"SENSITIVITY -","text":"To 50 ml of water add 10 ml ofacetate buffer\npH 4.4,0.1 ml of 0.02 M disodium edetate and 0.25 ml of the\nreagent under examination; a yellow colour is produced. Add\n0.15 ml of a 0.5 per cent w/v solution of cupric sulphate; the\ncolour changes to violet.\nQninaldine Red; 2-(4-Dimethylaminostyryl) quinoline\nEthiodide: C21H23IN2 = 430.33\nWhen used for the non-aqueous titration in anhydrous glacial\nacetic acid, the colour changes from magenta (basic) to almost\ncolourless (acidic).\nQuinaldine Red Solution: A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of\nquinaldine red in methanol."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/pyridylazonaphthol-pan-l-2-pyridylazo-2-naphthol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pyridylazonaphthol; PAN; l-(2-Pyridylazo)-2-naphthol:"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Pyridylazonaphthol; PAN; l-(2-Pyridylazo)-2-naphthol: \nCi5HnN3O=249.27\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nBrick red or orange-red powder; mp, about 140°.\nPyridylazonaphthol Solution: A 0.1 per cent w/v solution in \nethanol.\nComplies with the following test.\nsensitivity — To 50 ml of water add 10 ml of acetate buffer \npH 4.4,0.1 ml of 0.02 M disodium edetate and 0.25 ml of the\n1139\nwww.webofpharma.com\n4.3. INDICATORS AND INDICATOR TEST PAPERS\nreagent under examination; a yellow colour is produced. Add \n0.15 ml of a 0.5 per cent w/v solution of cupric sulphate-, the \ncolour changes to violet.\nQuinaldine Red; 2-(4-Dimethylamino styryl) quinoline \nEthiodide: C21H23IN2=430.33\nWhen used for the non-aqueous titration in anhydrous glacial \nacetic acid, the colour changes from magenta (basic) to almost\ncolourless (acidic).\nQuinaldine Red Solution: A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \nquinaldine red in methanol."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Pyridylazonaphthol; PAN; l-(2-Pyridylazo)-2-naphthol: \nCi5HnN3O=249.27\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nBrick red or orange-red powder; mp, about 140°.\nPyridylazonaphthol Solution: A 0.1 per cent w/v solution in \nethanol.\nComplies with the following test.\nsensitivity — To 50 ml of water add 10 ml of acetate buffer \npH 4.4,0.1 ml of 0.02 M disodium edetate and 0.25 ml of the\n1139\nwww.webofpharma.com\n4.3. INDICATORS AND INDICATOR TEST PAPERS\nreagent under examination; a yellow colour is produced. Add \n0.15 ml of a 0.5 per cent w/v solution of cupric sulphate-, the \ncolour changes to violet.\nQuinaldine Red; 2-(4-Dimethylamino styryl) quinoline \nEthiodide: C21H23IN2=430.33\nWhen used for the non-aqueous titration in anhydrous glacial \nacetic acid, the colour changes from magenta (basic) to almost\ncolourless (acidic).\nQuinaldine Red Solution: A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \nquinaldine red in methanol."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/pyrilamine-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pyrilamine Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"; Pyrilamine Maleate\nTablets; Pyrilamine Tablets\nMepyrarnine Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nmepyramine maleate, C17H23N30,C,;H404'\nUsual strength. 50 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"; Pyrilamine Maleate\nTablets; Pyrilamine Tablets\nMepyrarnine Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nmepyramine maleate, C17H23N30,C,;H404'\nUsual strength. 50 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 0.1 g\nofMepyrarnine Maleate with 10 ml ofdichloromethane, filter\nand evaporate the filtrate to dryness.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption\nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that\nobtained with mepyramine maleate RS or with the reference\nspectrum ofmepyramine maleate.\nB. In the test for Related substances, the principal spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with test solution (b) corresponds to\nthat in the chromatogram obtainedwith reference solution (b).\nC. Dissolve a quantity of the powdered tablets containing\n0.2 g of MepyraInine Maleate, freed as far aspossiblefrorh\nany sugar coatiDg, in 3 ml of water, add 2 ml of 5 M sodium\nhydroxide and shake with three quantities, each of 3 ml, of\nether. Warm the aqueous layer in a water-bath for 10 minutes\nwith 2 ml of bromine solution, heat to boiling, cool and add\n0.2 ml to a solution of 10 mg of resorcinol in 3 ml ofsulphuric\nacid; a bluish black colour is produced on heating. for\n15 minutes in a water-bath."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography\n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 100 volumes of ethyl acetate and\n2 volumes of diethylamine.\nNOTE-Prepare the following solutions immediately before\nuse.\nTest solution (a). Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets\ncontaining 0.4 g of Mepyramine Maleate with 10 ml of\nchloroform and filter.\nTest solution (b). Dilute 1 rnl of test solution (a) to 10 rnl with\nchloroform.\nReference solution (a). A 4.0 per cent w/v solution of\nmepyramine maleate RS in chlorofonn.\nReference solution (b). A 0.4 per cent w/v solution of\nmepyramine maleate RS in chloroform.\nReference solution (c). A 0.008 per cent w/v solution of\nmepyramine maleate RS in chlorofonn.\n.\nReference solution (d). A 0.004 per cent w/v solution of\nmepyramine maleate RS in chlorofonn.\nApply to the plate 5 III of each solution. After development,\ndry the plate in air and examine in ultraviolet light at 254 nm.\nAny secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with test\nsolution (a) is not more intense than the spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c). The test\nis not valid unless the Rr values of the principal spots in the\nchromatograms obtained with test solutions (a) and reference\nsolution (a) are at least 0.2 and unless the spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (d) is clearly\nvisible. Ignore the spot due to maleic acid on the line of\napplication.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh accurately a\nquantity ofthe powder containing about 0.1 g ofMepyramine\nMaleate, add 75 rnl of water and 5 rnl of 2 M hydrochloric\nacid, shake vigorously for 15 minutes and dilute to 100.0 ml\nwith lfater. Centrifuge and dilute 10.0 rnl of the clear,\nsupernatant liquid to 100.0 rnl with water. To 10.0ml add 10ml\nof 0.1 M hydrochloric acid and dilute to 50.0 rnl with water.\nMeasure the absorbance of the resulting solution at the\nmaximum at about 316 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of\nC17H23N30,C4H404 taking 206 as the specific absorbance at\n316nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/pyrimethamine-and-sulphadoxine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pyrimethamine and Sulphadoxine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Pyrimethamine and Sulphadoxine ;\nTablets < ■ \n. -w\nPyrimethamine and Sulphadoxine Tablets contain not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namounts of pyrimethamine, CI2H13CIN4, and of sulphadoxine, \nC,2I114N4O4S. \n:■ \n•.\nUsual strength. Pyrimethamine 25 mg and sulphadoxine \n500 mg. \n... \n. . ;"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Pyrimethamine and Sulphadoxine ;\nTablets < ■ \n. -w\nPyrimethamine and Sulphadoxine Tablets contain not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namounts of pyrimethamine, CI2H13CIN4, and of sulphadoxine, \nC,2I114N4O4S. \n:■ \n•.\nUsual strength. Pyrimethamine 25 mg and sulphadoxine \n500 mg. \n... \n. . ;"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by th in-layer chromatography (2.4:17), coating the \nplate with silica gel GF254. ■ . \n:\nMobile phase: A mixture Of 4 volumes of chloroform^ \n4 voluriies of n-heptane, 1 volume of glacial acetic acid&ii<5 \n4 vplumesof a mixture of 1 volume of methanol and 19 volumes \nof ethanol. - ..... \n: \n. .<< . .. .\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 25 mg of Pyrimethamine with 50 ml of a 2 per cent \nw/v solution of strong ammonia solution 'll methanol. : ; p r\nPYRIMETHAMINE AND SULPHADOXINE TABLETS\nIP 2022 :\nReference solution (a). A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of \npyrimethamine IPRS in methanol.\nReference solution (b). A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of \nsulphadoxine IPRS in methanol.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm. One of the principal spots in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) and the \nother corresponds to that in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b)."},{"name":"tests","text":"Other tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Detcrmine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTestsolution. Weigh a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining about 25 mg of Pyrimethamine and 500 mg of"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/pyrimethamine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pyrimethamine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"PYRIMETHAMINE TABLETS\nAssay. Dissolve 0.2 g in 25 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic\nacid, heating gently, cooLTitrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid, \ndetermining the end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Cany \nout a blank titration. \n-c■ \n'\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02487 g of \nCI2HI3C1N4.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nPyrimethamine Tablets\nPyrimethamine Tablets contain not less than 92.5 per cent and \nnot more than 107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \npyrimethamine^ C.2H !3C1N4.\nUsual strength. 25 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"PYRIMETHAMINE TABLETS\nAssay. Dissolve 0.2 g in 25 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic\nacid, heating gently, cooLTitrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid, \ndetermining the end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Cany \nout a blank titration. \n-c■ \n'\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02487 g of \nCI2HI3C1N4.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nPyrimethamine Tablets\nPyrimethamine Tablets contain not less than 92.5 per cent and \nnot more than 107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \npyrimethamine^ C.2H !3C1N4.\nUsual strength. 25 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Shake a quantity ofthe powdered tablets containing 50 mg \nof Pyrimethamine with 50 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) for 20 \nminutes, filter and evaporate the filtrate to diyness. On the \nresidue, determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry \n(2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that obtained with \npyrimethamine IPRS or with the reference spectrum of\npyrimethamine.\n8. Extract the powdered tablets with 1 Msulphuric acid, filter \narid a.ddpotdssiumietrdiodomercurate solution to the filtrate. \nA creamy white precipitate is produced. \n' .. / ’\nC; Extract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 50 mg \nof Pyrimethamine with two TO rhl quantities of chloroform arid \nevaporate, the combined extracts to dryness. The residue melts \nat about 240c:(2.4.21)..,. \n\" \n, ....\nTests--\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid,'\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes. /\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium^ filter Measure the \nabsorbance of the filtrate, suitably diluted with the ihediiim, if \nnecessary at the maximum at about 273 mil (2.4.7). Calculate \nthe content'ofC]2Hi3CIN4 in the medium'from the absorbance\nobtained from a solution of known concentration, of \npyrimethamineJPRS in the dissolution medium........\nQ.Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of C!2Hi3ClN4.\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel GF254. \n.\nSolvent mixture. 90 volumes of chloroform and 10 volumes of \nmethanol.\nIP 2022 y\n■.............................................-......................... -■■■...................-......-.................................................................. ........-.......................................ft\nMobile phase. A mixture of 4 volumes of chloroform, 8 volumes M \nof 1-propanol, 12 volumes of glacial acetic acid \n76 volumes of toluene. \"■ ■ - ■ ■ . \n. ■■■\nTest solution. Shake.a quantity of the powdered tablets \nContaining 50 mg Pyrimethamine with 5 ml of solvent mixture W \nand filter. J . ' ? . ' \n.\nReference solution. Dilute 1 ml of the test solution to .400 ml B \nwith the solvent mixture. \nif\nApply to the plate 20|il df each solution. Allow the mobile .4 \nphase to rise 10 cm. After development, dry the plate in air and S \nexamine under ultraviolet light at 254 nm. Any secondary spot ? \nin - the chromatogram ■ obtained with the test solution is not | \nmore intense than the spot in the. chromatogram obtained | \nwith the reference solution (0.25 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity of the \npowder containing about 0.025g of Pyrimethamine, add 50 nil \nof hot 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid and heat on a water-bath for\n10 minutes, swirling occasionally. Mix with the aid of \nultrasound for 30 minutes, remove and cool to. room \ntemperature. Add sufficient 0.1 M hydrochloric acid to \nproduce 100 ml, filter and dilute 5 ml ofthe filtrate to 100 ml \nwith 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid and measure the absorbance of\nthe resulting solution at the maximum at about 272 nm (2.4.7). \nCalculate the content of C12H13ClN4taking 316 as the specific \nabsorbance at 272 nm. \n• ■"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/pyrimethamine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pyrimethamine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"PYRIMETHAMINE\nIP2022\nChromatographic system\n_ a stainless steel column 30 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\n\" octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\nmobile phase: dissolve 1.0 g of sodium I-\nin 500 ml of water, add 5.0 ml of \n^f'f'irieiiiylbmine and 100 ml of acetronitrile, dilute to"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"PYRIMETHAMINE\nIP2022\nChromatographic system\n_ a stainless steel column 30 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\n\" octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\nmobile phase: dissolve 1.0 g of sodium I-\nin 500 ml of water, add 5.0 ml of \n^f'f'irieiiiylbmine and 100 ml of acetronitrile, dilute to"},{"name":"Tests","text":"1000 ml with water, adjusted to pH 3.0 with \n: : orthophosphoric acid,\nflow rate: 2 ml per minute,\n< ^spectrophotometer set at 270 nm,. \niXjJ injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 1.5 and the relative standard \ndewtibD for replicate injections is not more than 1.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CJI jBrNXL in the tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nPyrimethamine\nNH2\n: ■ C|/M nh2\nCI2H13CIN4 \nMol. Wt. 248.7\nPyrimethamine is 5-(4-chlorophenyl)-6-ethylpyrimidine-2,4- \ndiamine. ST--.■ . ■ \n- \n.\nPyrimethamine contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C12H13C1N4, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\n^^.gd^-Antirnalarial.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or an almost white, crystalline \npowder.;"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Tests B \nand C may be omitted.if test A is carried out..\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nComparcthe spectrum with that obtained with pyrimethamine\nor with the reference spectrum of pyrimethamine.\nB-Th the test for Related substances, the principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution (b) corresponds to \nthat,in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nC. Dissolve 0.14 g in sufficient ethanol to produce 100 ml, \ndilute 10 ml of the solution to 100 ml with 0:1 Mhydrochloric\nacid and dilute 10 ml ofthe solution to 100 ml with the same \nsolvent; When examined in the range230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), \nthe resulting solution shows an absorption maximum at about\n272 nm; absorbance at about 272 nm, 0.43 to 0.46. ’\nAppearance of solution. Dissolve 0.25 g in sufficient of a mixture \nof 3 volumes of dichloromethane and 1 volume of methanol \nto produce 10 ml. The resulting solution is clear (2.4.1), and \nnot more intensely coloured than reference solution BYS6 \n(2.4.1).\nAcidity or alkalinity. Shake 1.0 g with 50 ml of water for \n2 minutes and filter (solution A). To 10 ml of solution A add \n0.05 ml ofphenolphthalein solution’, the solution is colourless \nand not more than 0.2 ml of 0.01 M sodium hydroxide is \nrequired to change the colour to pink. Add 0.4 ml of 0.01 M \nhydrochloric acid and 0.05 ml of methyl red., solution’, the \nsolution is red or orange.\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 76 volumes of toluene, 12 volumes \nof glacial acetic acid, 8 volumes of 1-propanol and 4 volumes \nof chloroform.\nPrepare the following solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. 90 volumes of chloroform and 10 volumes of \nmethanol. \n;■\nTest solution (a). Dissolve 0.1 g ofthe substance under \nexamination in 10 ml ofthe solvent mixture. \n_\nTest solution (b). Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination in 100.0 ml of the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A 0.0025 per cent w/v solution of the\nsubstance under examination in the solvent-mixture,;.....\nReference solution (b). A 0.1 per pent w/v, solution of \npyrimethamine IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nApply to the plate 20 pl of each solution: After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm. Any secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained \nwith test solution (a) is not more intense than the.spot in, the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nSulphates (2.3.17). 25 ml of Solution A complies with the limit \ntest for sulphates. Use a mixture of 5.0 ml of sulphate standard \nsolution (10 ppm SO.;) and 10 ml of distilled water to prepare \nthe standard (100 ppm). .\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19).Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 4 hours."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/quantities","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Quantities"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Quantities \nare weighed ‘or measured with an‘ accuracy. \ncommensurate: with the indicated:.degrée: \nof ‘precision. For’.’ \nweighings, the precisionis ‘plus or minus: S.units after-the last: \nfigure: stated: For example:0.25-@\"is'to’ be-interpreted \njas\n-:0:245.2 to. 0.255: g.-For the measurementof volumes; ifthe 3°\"\n“figure after the. decimal point.is.a-zero or ends 'j dina zero, epee\n10.0 mL or 0,50 mi, the volume. i is measured using.a Pipette,\na volumetric flask or a burette; as appropriate; in other.cases,’\na graduated measuring cylinder ora graduated pipette may be; \n*,\nused: ‘Volumes ‘stated: in: microlitres ‘are measured using. \nmicropipette or. microsyringe.” \n: \n:\noperation.\nPs Apparatus. Measuring \nand \nweighine'd devices. and \nother” a \napparatus are deseribed in fhe chapter entitled“ Apparatus for ” \nHees \n2\nTests: and Assays’. A specification fora definite size.or type’.\n_ of contaitier or apparatus in a test or assay is given imerely ag\na recommendation.” \noe \nee\nUnless otherwise: stated, “comparative tests are \n, Gamried cout \nusing idéntical-tubes of colourless, transparent, neutral glass\nwith @ flat base, commonly known, as Nessler, cylinders,\nReagents ‘and Solutions: The. reagenis required. for the tests\n» and-assays of the: Pharmacopoéia-are: defied ii:the WATIOUS. \nS \ni\nstrengths of the: solutions: to: be made: front them:Fhé \n“requirements set out are not intended to imply. that the materials: n : \n_ere suitable’ for use- fi: the test concerned: ‘reagents NOt)\n“covered by’ monographis i in the ae \na shall. not be \nS \nclaimed to bé of IP quality \nSo \nHee “that the article complies. w