idine-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ranitidine Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ranitidine Oral Solution\nRanitidine Hydrochloride Oral Solution.\nRanitidine Oral Solution is ;a solution of ranitidine \nhydrochloride in a suitable vehicle.\nRanitidine.Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 1 lO.O.per cent of the stated amount of the \nranitidine,CijH^^CJS.. f. ? \n.... . \n. ' r\n■Usualstrength.75mgper5ml. \n;.-i"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Ranitidine Oral Solution\nRanitidine Hydrochloride Oral Solution.\nRanitidine Oral Solution is ;a solution of ranitidine \nhydrochloride in a suitable vehicle.\nRanitidine.Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 1 lO.O.per cent of the stated amount of the \nranitidine,CijH^^CJS.. f. ? \n.... . \n. ' r\n■Usualstrength.75mgper5ml. \n;.-i"},{"name":"Identification","text":". \nc\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution. :,\npH (2.4.24). 6.7 to 7.5. \n.bzn 1 v .\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n.A-;; \n: ■ : ■\nBuffer solution. Dissolve 6.804 g of potassium clihydrogen \northophosphate in 950 ml of water, adjusted to pH 7.1 with \nsodium hydroxide solution and dilutetb 1000.0 ml with ’water\nTest solution. Prepare a solid phase extraction’cartridge \ncontaining a Cl 8 sorbent (Such as Scp-Pak C18. cartridges) by \npassing-10.0 ;fnlof methanol followed by 20.0 ml of-0.5- 'M\n1^ 2 022 S\nRANlTIDINEOKALSOLUriON\nammonia through the cartridge; attach thc tip of a7 suitable 1 \nsyringe to the cartridge. Transfer a weighed quantity of the ■ 1 \noral solution containing the equivalent of 15 mg of rarlitidihe -B\nto the barrel ofthe syringe, add 2.0 ml of 0,5, :M ammonia t0 pl| \nthe syringe,. insert,the,plunger and slowly force the mixture \nthrough the cartridge, discarding the eluant; repeat with two ■ |\n4.0 ml quantities of 0.5 M ammonia discarding the eluant. ■ ! \nPass two 5.0 ml quantities of a mixture of 25 volumes of 0.;^ : g \nhydrochloric acid arid 75 volumes of wcV/zmw/throughibe I \ncartridge and collect the eluant; add 40.0 ml of e^ahbZto tlie I \ncollected eluant, evaporate the resulting solution .to.dryness i I \nat a temperature not exceeding 30° under reduced pressure I\narid dissolve the residue in 2.0 ml of water. \n;\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1 volume ofthe test solution to \n50 volumes with water. \n‘\n■I\nf\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 volume of the test solution to\n100.volumes with water. , ' ‘ \n' \n' ,‘,7 . '\" ' j\nReference solution.(c). Dilute 1 volume ofthe testsolution to \n200 volumes with water. ■. m \n-.-vj\nReference solution (d). Dilute 1 volume of reference solution\n(b) to 5 volumes with water.\nReference solution (e). A solution containing 0.002 per cent \nw/v. of ranitidine impurity, f IPRS (1^.1 ‘rN-fmethylenebis \n(sulphanediyt-eihylene)] bis(N’-methyl-2-nitrQeiheneri,l: \ndiamine IPRS) in mobile phase A. Transfer 1.0 ml of the \nsolution 'to a l 00-ml volumetric flask containing-13 fng of \nranitidine hydrochloride IPRS; add 40 ml of water, shake to \ndissolve and dilute to volume with writer.\ni\nChromatographic system \nf;; f\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed, with\noctadecylsiiane ariiOrphous organosilich polymer \n(3.5pm) (SuchasXTerraMSCis), ' ' ’ \n’\nA - column temperature: 35°, \n-\n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 2 volumes of acetonitrile\n. and 98 volumes of buffer solution, \n/ i <\nB. a mixture of 22 volumes of apetonitrile \nand 78 volumes of buffer solution,\n- a gradient programme usirig the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at230 nm, \n>\ninjection volume: 20 pl. \n- ;\n■ |\n-■ Time -\n! Mobile phase A\nMobile phase B\nAS\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per centv/V)J\n.\n100\n■ () ■\nw \nAfi\n10\n0\n100 ' ■\n■ 0 \n■ ■'\n- . 100 ;\n.-I\n16 :\n100\n' J' X'J.1.-\n•i\n■ 20 ■ --T-....-.A 100. \n■\nIP2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ranitidine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ranitidine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RANITIDINE TABLETS\ni 'ectreferencesolution (e). The test is not valid unless the\nesolution between ■ two peaks corresponding to ranit idin e \n^purity ) and ranitidine hydrochloride is not less than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a), (b), (c), (d) and the test solution. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area \nof any secondary peak is not more than the area of principal \nneak in the chromatogram, obtained: with reference solution \n(a) (2 0 per cent),the area of not more than one secondary \npeak is more than the area; of the principal peak in the \nchrpmatb^anr obtained with reference solution (b) (1.0 per \ncent), the area of not more than two other secondary peaks is \nmore than the area ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) (0:5 per cent), the area of \nnot more than two further secondary peaks is more than the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (d) (0.2 per cent) and the; sum of the areas \nof all the secondary peaks is not more than 2.5 times the areas \nofthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (5.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an \narea less than 0.25 times the area of the: principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (d) (0.05 per \ncent). \n‘"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RANITIDINE TABLETS\ni 'ectreferencesolution (e). The test is not valid unless the\nesolution between ■ two peaks corresponding to ranit idin e \n^purity ) and ranitidine hydrochloride is not less than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a), (b), (c), (d) and the test solution. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area \nof any secondary peak is not more than the area of principal \nneak in the chromatogram, obtained: with reference solution \n(a) (2 0 per cent),the area of not more than one secondary \npeak is more than the area; of the principal peak in the \nchrpmatb^anr obtained with reference solution (b) (1.0 per \ncent), the area of not more than two other secondary peaks is \nmore than the area ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) (0:5 per cent), the area of \nnot more than two further secondary peaks is more than the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (d) (0.2 per cent) and the; sum of the areas \nof all the secondary peaks is not more than 2.5 times the areas \nofthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (5.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an \narea less than 0.25 times the area of the: principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (d) (0.05 per \ncent). \n‘"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Other tests. Comply with the tests stated under'Oral Liquids.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14)\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of oral solution, with the mobile \nphase to obtain a solution containing 0.0025 per cent w/v of \nranitidine.\nTests ■ ■\nReferencesolution. A 0.0028. per cent w/v solution of \nranitidine hydrochloride IPRS in the mobile phase.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nChromatographicsystem\nPa stainless steel cblurnn 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 85 volumes of methanol and\n15 volumes of buffer solution prepared by, dissolving\n; 7.71 gm of ammonium acetate in 200 ml of water, add\n: . ; 1 ml bf acetic acid and dilute to 1000 ml with water.\n- flow rate: 1ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 322 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl. . >1\nfaject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is hot less than 2000 theoretical plates, the \njailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard\ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent\nJnject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C13H22N4O3S. \nJ\nSt<irage. Store, protected from light, at a temperature not \nexqeeding306. \n:\nlabelling. The label states the strength in terms, of the \nequivalent amount of ranitidine.\nRanitidine Tablets\nRanitidine Hydrochloride Tablets a.; ;\nRanitidine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of the ranitidine, \nC13H22N4O3S. The tablets are coated.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 150 mg, 300 mg of ranitidine \n(1.12 g of ranitidine hydrochloride is approximately equivalent \nto 1 g of ranitidine).\nA, Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 25 mg \nof ranitidine with 5 ml of methanol for 5 minutes, filter and \nevaporate the filtrate to dryness. Add 1 ml of light petroleum \n(60° to 8(F). to the resulting residue, scratch the side of the \nvessel with a glass rod to induce crystallisation, evaporate to \ndryness and dry the residue at 60° for 10 minutes. The residue \ncomplies with the following test.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with ranitidine \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \nranitidine hydrochloride.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium: 900 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm for 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter, rejecting \nthe first few ml of filtrate. Dilute a suitable volume ofthe filtrate \nwith the, medium, if necessary.. Measure the absorbance of the \nresulting solution at the maximum at about 314 nm (2.4.7). \nCalculate the content of ranitidine, C13H22N4O3S in the medium \nfrom the absorbance obtained from a solution of known \nconcentration of Rdriitidine hydrochloride IPRS in the \ndissolution medium.\nQ. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of \nC13H^O3S. \n;\nRelated substances.: Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). - J ■ - . - . \n. . \n■•■■■ \n■■\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of powdered tablets \ncontaining about 0.13 g of Ranitidine in100.0 ml of water, \nfilter. > . i \n.\nReference, solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test , solution to \n200.0 ml with mobile phase A. \n•\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0ml of the test solution to\n20.0 ml with mobile phaseA, Dilute 3.0 ml of the solution to \n50.0 ml with mobile phaseA: \"..■hr; i •'!\nRefereitcejStoludbhtfc).^ solutions containing 0.002 per, cent\n^l^iQiAraniii&me^knpui^y/.U. IPRS (1,1 ‘~N-[methylenebis\n(sulphanediyl-ethylene)] pis(Nfmethyl-2r,mtroethene-l, I r \ndiamine IPRS) ' in mobile. phase A. Transfer 1.0 ml o f the \nsolution to a100-ml volumetric flask containing 13 nig of \nranitidine hydrochloride IPRS, add 40 ml ofwater, shake to \ndissolve and dilute to volume with water.\nChromatographic system ; \nuu: Auithft-.-u J\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n; / (3.5 pm) (SuchasXterraMSCIS), ! a -: .\noctadecylsilaneamorphous organosilica polymer\n- columntemperature:350. \n■. \n.■>\n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 2 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 98 volumes of 0.05M potassium dihydrd'gen \northophosphate, adjusted to; pH 7.1 with sodium \nhydroxide solution, . \n' >'■ :•-\ni>: B. a mixture of22 volumes of acetonitrile\n. and 78 volumes of 0.05.Mpotassium dihydrogen ortho-\nphosphate^ adjusted'to pH 7.1 with sodium hydroxide"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rappaport-vassiliadis-salmonella-enrichment-broth-medium-9","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rappaport Vassiliadis Salmonella enrichment broth (Medium 9)"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rappaport Vassiliadis Salmonella enrichment broth (Medium 9) \narid incubate at 30° to 35° for 24 to 48 hours. Subculture on a \nplate ofWilson and Blair’s BBS Agar (Medium 10) and incubate \nat 30° to 35“ for 24 to 48 hours. Green colonies with black \ncenter develop and in48 hours the colonies become uniformly \nblack. Colonies surrounded by a dark zone and metallic sheen \nindicates possibility of presence of Salmonella. If sub cultured \non plates ofXylose lysine deoxycholate agar and incubate at \n30“ to 35“ for 24 to 48 hours. Well deyolped, red colonies with \nor without black centers indicates possibility of Salmonella, \nThis should be confirmed by identification tests.\nIf there is'no growth of such type of colonics, or identification \ntests are negative it indicates absence of Salmonella and the \nproduct passes the test.\nShigella\nPrepare a sample from the product to be examined as \nmentioned under Total aerobic viable count in Microbial \ncontamination in nbnsterile products (2.2.9) and use the \nquantity corresponding to-10 g or 10 ml of the product to \ninoculate a suitable amount (deterrhiried as under Validity of \nthe Test method.) of Casein soyabean digest Broth incubate \nat 30\" to 35“ for 18 to 24hours. - -\nAfter incubation shake the growth and transfer 1 ml to f 00 ml \nof GN Broth (Medium 11) arid intubate at 30“ to 35° for 24 to \n48hours. Subculture, on a plate of Xylose lysine deoxycholate \nmedium (Medium I2),Incubate at 30“ to 35° for 24 to 48 hours. \nA red colored translucent colony without black centre indicates \npossibility of presence of Shigella. This should beconfirmed \nby identification tests.\nIf there is no growth of such colonies Or if identification tests\nare negative, it indicates absence of Shigella and the product \npasses the test. \n- :\nPseudomonas aeruginosa\nUsing Casein soyabean digest broth as a diluent make 1 in 10 \ndilution of more thanI g of the product as mentioned in Ttfaj \naerobic viable count under Microbial contamination in \nnonsterile products (2.2.9) and use 10 ml or the quantity \ncorresponding to 1 g or 1 ml of the product to inoculate a \nsuitable amount (determined as under Validity of the Test \nmethod) of Casein soyabean digest broth incubate at 30° to \n35“ for 1'8 to 24 hours?Subculture on a plate of Cetrimide agar \n(Medium 13) and incubate at 30“ to 35° for 18 to 72 hours. \nA greenish color colony indicates the possibility of presence \nof Pseudomonas aeruginosa. This should be confirmed by \nidentification tests.\nIf there is no growth of such type of colonies, or identification \ntests are negative it indicates absence ofP aeruginosa and \nthe product passes the test. \n:-"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Rappaport Vassiliadis Salmonella enrichment broth (Medium 9) \narid incubate at 30° to 35° for 24 to 48 hours. Subculture on a \nplate ofWilson and Blair’s BBS Agar (Medium 10) and incubate \nat 30° to 35“ for 24 to 48 hours. Green colonies with black \ncenter develop and in48 hours the colonies become uniformly \nblack. Colonies surrounded by a dark zone and metallic sheen \nindicates possibility of presence of Salmonella. If sub cultured \non plates ofXylose lysine deoxycholate agar and incubate at \n30“ to 35“ for 24 to 48 hours. Well deyolped, red colonies with \nor without black centers indicates possibility of Salmonella, \nThis should be confirmed by identification tests.\nIf there is'no growth of such type of colonics, or identification \ntests are negative it indicates absence of Salmonella and the \nproduct passes the test.\nShigella\nPrepare a sample from the product to be examined as \nmentioned under Total aerobic viable count in Microbial \ncontamination in nbnsterile products (2.2.9) and use the \nquantity corresponding to-10 g or 10 ml of the product to \ninoculate a suitable amount (deterrhiried as under Validity of \nthe Test method.) of Casein soyabean digest Broth incubate \nat 30\" to 35“ for 18 to 24hours. - -\nAfter incubation shake the growth and transfer 1 ml to f 00 ml \nof GN Broth (Medium 11) arid intubate at 30“ to 35° for 24 to \n48hours. Subculture, on a plate of Xylose lysine deoxycholate \nmedium (Medium I2),Incubate at 30“ to 35° for 24 to 48 hours. \nA red colored translucent colony without black centre indicates \npossibility of presence of Shigella. This should beconfirmed \nby identification tests.\nIf there is no growth of such colonies Or if identification tests\nare negative, it indicates absence of Shigella and the product \npasses the test. \n- :\nPseudomonas aeruginosa\nUsing Casein soyabean digest broth as a diluent make 1 in 10 \ndilution of more thanI g of the product as mentioned in Ttfaj \naerobic viable count under Microbial contamination in \nnonsterile products (2.2.9) and use 10 ml or the quantity \ncorresponding to 1 g or 1 ml of the product to inoculate a \nsuitable amount (determined as under Validity of the Test \nmethod) of Casein soyabean digest broth incubate at 30° to \n35“ for 1'8 to 24 hours?Subculture on a plate of Cetrimide agar \n(Medium 13) and incubate at 30“ to 35° for 18 to 72 hours. \nA greenish color colony indicates the possibility of presence \nof Pseudomonas aeruginosa. This should be confirmed by \nidentification tests.\nIf there is no growth of such type of colonies, or identification \ntests are negative it indicates absence ofP aeruginosa and \nthe product passes the test. \n:-"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rauwolfia-serpentina-powder","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rauwolfia Serpentina Powder"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rauwolfia serpentina Powder\nForeign organic matter (2.6.1). Not more than 2.0 per cent\n100.0 ml.\nSarpagandha powder is obtained by Rauwolfia serpentina \nBentham ex Kurz roots (Fam. Apocynaceae) reduced to a fine \npowder.\nEthanol-soluble extractive (2.6.2). Not less than 2.0 per cent.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.002 per cent \nw/v reserpine RS and 0.002 per cent w/v ajmalicine RS in \nethanol (95 per cent).\nWater-soluble extractive (2.6.3). Not less than 5.0 per cent.\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 8.0 per cent.\nIt contains not less than 0.15 per cent w/w and not more than \n0.2 per cent w/w of reserpine-ajmalicine alkaloid calculated on \ndried basis.\nReference solution (b). A 0.002 per cent w/v solution of \nreserpine RS in ethanol (95 per cent).\nAcid-insoluble ash (2.3.19): Not more than 2.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nChromatographic system\nCategory. Antihypertensive, antipsychotic, neurosedative, \nniddrapradaa.\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \nmobile phase: a mixture of 35 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 65 volumes of a buffer solution prepared by \ndissolving 6.80 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate \nin 1000 ml water, adjusted to pH 3.0 with dilute \northophosphoric acid, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 268 nm,\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 12.0 per cent, \ndetermined on 5 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nUsual strength. 0.175 per cent w/w.\nDescription. Taste bitter, odour indistinct.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination tests."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rauwolfia serpentina Powder\nForeign organic matter (2.6.1). Not more than 2.0 per cent\n100.0 ml.\nSarpagandha powder is obtained by Rauwolfia serpentina \nBentham ex Kurz roots (Fam. Apocynaceae) reduced to a fine \npowder.\nEthanol-soluble extractive (2.6.2). Not less than 2.0 per cent.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.002 per cent \nw/v reserpine RS and 0.002 per cent w/v ajmalicine RS in \nethanol (95 per cent).\nWater-soluble extractive (2.6.3). Not less than 5.0 per cent.\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 8.0 per cent.\nIt contains not less than 0.15 per cent w/w and not more than \n0.2 per cent w/w of reserpine-ajmalicine alkaloid calculated on \ndried basis.\nReference solution (b). A 0.002 per cent w/v solution of \nreserpine RS in ethanol (95 per cent).\nAcid-insoluble ash (2.3.19): Not more than 2.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nChromatographic system\nCategory. Antihypertensive, antipsychotic, neurosedative, \nniddrapradaa.\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \nmobile phase: a mixture of 35 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 65 volumes of a buffer solution prepared by \ndissolving 6.80 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate \nin 1000 ml water, adjusted to pH 3.0 with dilute \northophosphoric acid, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 268 nm,\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 12.0 per cent, \ndetermined on 5 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nUsual strength. 0.175 per cent w/w.\nDescription. Taste bitter, odour indistinct.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination tests."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Assay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nDetermine by thin layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating the \nplate with silica gel GF254.\nTest solution. Reflux about 2 g of the coarsely powdered \nsubstance under examination with 50 ml of ethanol (95 per \ncent), on a water bath for 15 minutes, cool and filter. Reflux \nthe residue further with ethanol (95 per cent), till the last \nextract turns colorless, cool and filter. Combine all the filterates \nand concentrate to 100.0 ml.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 70 volumes of chloroform and \n30 volumes of acetone.\ninjection volume: 10 IA.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent. Inject reference solution (b) for peak \nidentification.\nTest solution. To 0.25 g of the substance under examination, \nadd 5 nil methanol, shake for 10 minutes, and filter. Wash the \nresidue with 5 ml of methanol and add the washing to the \nfiltrate.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing a 0.002 per cent \nw/v reserpine RS and 0.002 per cent w/v ajmalicine RS in\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nReference solution. To 0.25 g of Rauwolfia serpentina powder \nRS, add 5 ml methanol, shake for 10 minutes,-7,4tindfilter. Wash \nthe residue with 5 ml of methanol and add the washing to the \nfiltrate.\nethgnyl (95 per cent).\nCalculate the content of reserpine and ajmalicine.\nof\n,ee solution (b). A 0.002 per cent w/v solution \nreserpine RS in ethanol (95 per cent) for peak identification.\nStorage. Store protected from heat, moisture and against attack \nby insects and rodents.\n, \t .-,-- \n\t - \t --z.-P \n---\na.\n3852\n1,\n-..... \n,..., \t\n•\n•4-:-----,4,-E- \n... ."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rauwolfia-serpentina-root","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rauwolfia serpentina Root"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rauwolfia serpentina Root\n1\no\n1\nSarpagandha consists of the dried roots of Rauwolfia\nserpentina Bentham ex Kurz (Fam Apocynaceae).\nSARPAGANDHA\nSarpagandha contains not less than 0.15 per cent ofreserpine\nan~ ajmalicine, calculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. Taste bitter, odour indistinct."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rauwolfia serpentina Root\n1\no\n1\nSarpagandha consists of the dried roots of Rauwolfia\nserpentina Bentham ex Kurz (Fam Apocynaceae).\nSARPAGANDHA\nSarpagandha contains not less than 0.15 per cent ofreserpine\nan~ ajmalicine, calculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. Taste bitter, odour indistinct."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Macroscopic -\nRoots are sub cylindrical to tapering,\ntortuous or curved, rarely branched.\nOccurs as segments\nusually from 5 to 15 cm in length and 3 to 20 mID in diameter.\nExternally grayish yellow to brown, wood pale yellow. Roots\ntough with longitudinal marking and slightly wrinkled surface.\nWhen scraped, bark separates readily from the wood. Fracture\nis short and irregular.\nB. Microscopic -\nIn transverse section, cork cells in 2 to 8\nalternating bands ofradically narrow and broader cells, thin,\nlignified up to 75 /lm in tangential width, broader cells up to\nabout 90 /lm in radial length, phelloderm, tangentially elongated\nto isodimetric parenchyma cells containing starch and short\nlatex cells with brown resinous matter; secondary cortex\nconsists of parenchyma cells, heavily packed with starch\ngrains secondary phloem contains phloem parenchyma and\nsieve elements, parenchyma contains starch and angular\ncrystals of calcium oxalate 3 to 20 /lm in length. Xylem is\naboiIt 4/5 ofthe diameter ofthe root, wood is transversed by\nmedullay rays 1to 5 cells in width. Xylem consists ofvessels,\ntracheids, wood parenchyma and wood fibers. Xylem vessels\nare elongated up to 350 /lm in length and 50 /lm in width and\ncontains simple or bordered pits; tracheids lignified, pitted;\nwood parenchyma with moderately thick, lignified and pitted\nwalls containing starch; wood fibres highly thickened with\npointed ends, stone cells absent.\nC. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4. I7), coating\nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 70 volumes of chloroform and\n30 volumes ofacetone.\nTest solution. To 250 mg ofthe coarsely powdered substance\nunder examination, add 5 ml methanol, shake for 10 minutes,\nand filter. Wash the residue with 5 ml ofmethanol and add the\nwashing to the filtrate.\nReference solution. To 250 mg ofsGipagandha RS, add 5 ml\nmethanol, shake for 10 minutes, and filter. Wash the residue\nwith 5 ml ofmethanol and add the washing to the filtrate.\nApply to the plate 10 /ll ofeach solution as bands 10 mID by 2\nmm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 12 cm. Dry the plate in air\nand examine in ultraviolet light at 254 urn and 365 urn, spray\nthe plate_with anisaldehYdesltlphuricaCidxeagent.Heatthe\nplllte at 100° for 5 minutesand examine the plate inday light.\nThe chromatogram obtained with test solution shows two\npinkish-violet bands corresponding to the bands in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution. Adark brown\nband may also appear at the line of application in the\nchromatogram obtained with test solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Foreign organic matter (2.6.1). Not more than 2.0 per cent\nEthanol-soluble extractive (2.6.2). Not less than 2.0 per cent.\nWater-soluble extractive (2.6.3). Not less than 5.0 per cent.\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 8.0 perceut.\nAcid-insoluble ash (2.3.19): Not more than 2.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 12.0 per cent,\ndetermined on 5 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nMicrobial contamination limits (2.2.9). Complies with the\nmicrobial contamination tests.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Reflux about 2 g of the coarsely powdered\nsubstance\nunder\nexamination\nwith\n50\nml\nof\nethanol (95 per cent), on a water bath for 15 minutes, cool\nand filter.\nReflux the residue further with ethanol (95 per\ncent), till the last extractturns colorless, cool and filter. Combine\nall the filterates and concentrate to 100.0 ml.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing a 0.002 per cent\nw/v reserpine RS and 0.002 percent w/v ajmalicine RS in\nethanol (95 per cent).\nReference solution (b). A 0.002 per cent w/v reserpine RS in\nethanol (95 per cent) for peak identification.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mID packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 /lm),\nmobile phase: a mixture of 35 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 65 volumes of a buffer solution prepared by\ndissolving 6.80 g ofpotassium dihydrogen phosphate\nin 1000 ml water, adjust the pH to 3.0 with dilute\northophosphoric acid,\n.\nflow rate. 1ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 268 urn,\ninjection volume. 10 Ill.\nInject the reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless\nthe relative standard deviation for the replicate injections is\nnot more than 2.0 per cent. Inject reference solution (b) for\npeak identification.\nInject the test solution·andTeference solution(a}\nCalcUlate the content ofreserpine and ajrnalicine.\nStorage. Store protected from heat, moisture and against attack\nby insects and rodents.\nSarpagandha Powder"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rauwolfia-serpentina-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rauwolfia Serpentina Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rauwolfia serpentina Tablets\nSarpagandha Tablets contain not less than 85.0 per cent w/w\nand not more than 115.0 per cent w/w ofstated amount of the\nalkaloids contents mainly ofreserpine togetherwith ajmalicine."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rauwolfia serpentina Tablets\nSarpagandha Tablets contain not less than 85.0 per cent w/w\nand not more than 115.0 per cent w/w ofstated amount of the\nalkaloids contents mainly ofreserpine togetherwith ajmalicine."},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the assay the principal peak in chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other tests. Comply with the tests stated under tablets.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 8.0per cent, determined\nin 1.0 gm by drying in an oven at 105°.\n.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh accurately\na quantity of the powder containing about 1 g of rauwolfia\nserpentina into a 200 ml flat bottom flask. Add 50 ml of\nethanol (95 per cent), reflux on a water bath for 30 minutes,\ncool and filter.\nReflux the residue further with ethanol\nSARPAGANDHA TABLETS\n(95per cent) till the last extract turns colorless, cool and filter.\nCombine all the filtrates and concentrate to 100.0 ml.\nReference solution. A 0.001 per cent w/v Reserpine RS and\n0.001% w/v Ajmalicin RS in ethanol(95 per cent).\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecyl silane chemically bonded to porous silica (5/l),\n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 35 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 65 volumes of a buffer (pH 3.0), prepared by\ndissolving 6.80 g ofpotassium dihydrogen phosphate\nin 1000 ml water, pH of which is adjusted to 3.0 with\ndilute orthophosphoric acid,\n-\nflow rate. Iml per minute,\n-\nspectrophotometer set at 268 nm,\n-\ninjection volume.10 Ill.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more\nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the refernce solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content ofajmalicin and reserpine.\nUsualstrengths. 0.1 mg; 0.25 mg.\nStorage. Store protected from light at a temperature not\nexceeding 30°.\nLabelling. The quantity ofactive ingredient is stated in terms\nofreserpine-ajmalicine content in labelled amount.\nSaunf\nFennel; Foeniculum vulgare\n1\no\n1\n.~~l.l~f(;Q~~!~t§ Qfthe.. d.l'ie.4fr.t1it~Qf EQ?rz.ifltlYf!I y.yIgg!:?J'4!11-\n(Fam. Apiaceae).\nSaunf contains not less than 0.60 per cent of anethole,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. The fruits are oval-oblong, greenish brown to\nyellowish brown in colour. They have an aromatic\ncharacteristic odour and the taste is sweet aromatic."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Macroscopic -\nFruit cylindrical to oval with pedicel\nattached, consists ofmericarps. Each mericap is 10 mm long\nand 4 mm broad, five sided with a wider commissural surface,\ntapered at apex and base, crowned with a conical stylepod,\nglabrous, greenish or yellowish brown with five ridges.\nB. Microscopic -\nTransverse section offruit shows pericarp\nwith outer epidermis ofquadrangular to polygonal cells with\nsmooth cuticle. Vittae 4 dorsal and 2 commissural extending\nwith length ofeach mericarp, having brown cells and volatile\noil in cavity. Mesocarp with reticulate lignified parenchyma.\nEndocarp cells thin walled arranged parallel to one another in\ngroups of5-7. Endosperm consists ofthick walled, cellulosic\nparenchyma containing fixed oil cells and rosette crystals of\ncalcium oxalate.\nC. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel GF 254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 93 volumes of toluene and 7\nvolumes of ethyl acetate.\nTest solution. To 1 g of the coarsely powdered substance\nunder examination, add 25 ml ofdichloromethane, reflux for\n15 minutes, cool and filter. Reflux the residue further for two\ntimes with 25 ml ofdichloromethane, cool and filter. Combine\nall the filtrates and evaporate under vacuo to 25 ml.\nReference solution. To 2 g of the saunfRS, add 40 ml of\nmethanol, reflux for 15 minutes, cool and filter. Reflux the\nresidue further with 25 ml ofmethanol twice, cool and filter.\nCombine all the filtrates and concentrate under vacuum to\n25ml.\nApply to the plate 10 /ll of each solution as bands 10 mm by\n2 mm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air\nand examine in ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 365 nm, spray\nwith a anisaldehyde sulphuric acid reagent. Heat at 110° for\n10 minutes and examine the plate in day light. The\nchromatographic profile ofthe test solution is similar to that\nof the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Foreign organic matter (2.6.1). Not more than 2.0 per cent.\nEthanol-soluble extractive (2.6.2). Not less than 5.0 per cent.\nWater-soluble extractive (2.6.3). Not less than 14.0 per cent\nby Method 1.\nTotalash (2-:-:r.T9rNOtmore tlianlKO percent.------·-·\nAcld=insolubie-ashC2.iT9YNot-morethan·COper'cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 10per cent, determined\non 5.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial\ncontamination tests.\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. Weigh 2.0 g ofthe coarsely powdered substance\nunder examination, add 50 ml ofmethanol, reflux on a water-\nbath for 15 minutes, cool and filter. Reflux the residue further\nwith methanol till the extract turns colourless, cool and filter.\nCombine all the filtrates and concentrate to a volume slightly\nless than 100 ml. Dilute to 100.0 ml with methanol.\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of anethole\nRS in methanol.\nChromatographic system\n-\na capillary column 30 m x 0.25 m, packed with DB1,\ntemperature:\noven 90° to 260° @10° per minute (initially and finally\nhold for 5 minutes respectively),\ninjector240°,\ndetector 280°,\nflow rate. 0.8 ml perminute,\nsplit flow 20 ml per minute.\nInject 1 fll of the reference solution.\nThe test is not valid\nunless the relative standard deviation for the replicate\ninjections is not more than 10.0 per cent.\nInject 1 fll of the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content of anethole.\nStorage. Store protected from light in well-filled containers, at\na temperature not exceeding 30°.\nSenna Leaf\nCassia leaf; Cassia angustifolia\no\nSenna leaf consists of the dried compound leaves of Cassia\nangustifolia or Cassia senna Vahal. (Fam.Leguminosae).\nSENNA LEAF\nSenna leaf contains not less than 1.0 percent w/w of\nsennosides A and B, calculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. Pale yellowish green coloured leaflets with\nmucilaginous and faint odour."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Macroscopic -Leaflets, 2.5 to 8 cm long and 5-15 mm\nwide at centre, pale yellowish green, elongated lanceolate,\nslightly asymmetric at base; margins entire, flat, apex acute\nwith a sharp spine; both surface smooth with sparce trichomes;\nodour, faint but distinctive; taste, mucilaginous and\ndisagreeable but not distinctly bitter.\nB.\nMicroscopic\nTransverse section shows outer single\nlayered mucilaginous epidermal cells. Unicellular hairs\npresents. Stomata paracytic, numerous on both surface.\nMesophyll consists of upper and lower palisade layers with\nspongy layer in between, primatic crystals ofcalcium oxalate\npresent on larger veins.\nC. In the Assay, the chromatogram obtained with test solution\ncorresponds to the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution.\nD. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of40 volumes of n- propyl alcohol,\n40 volumes ofethylacetate, 29 volumes ofwaterand 1volume\nof glacial acetic acid.\nTest solution. Take 1 g of the dried leaves powder substance\nunderexamination.Add25 ml ofmethanol, reflux for 10minutes,\ncool and filter. Reflux the residue with another 20 ml of\nmethanol, cool and filter. Combine all the filtrates and\nconcentrate to 10 ml.\nReference solution. Boil 0.5 g ofdried senna leaves RS powder\nwith 25 ml ofmethanol under reflux for 10 minutes, cool and\nfilter. Boil under reflux the residue with another 20 ml of\nmethanol, cool and filter. Combine all the filtrates and evaporate\nto 5 ml.\nApply to the plate 10 fll ofeach solution as bands 10 mm by 2\nmm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air\nand examine in ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 365 urn, spray\nthe plate with 20 per cent v/v ofnitric acidsolution. Heat the\nplate at 100° to 105° for about 10 minutes and immediately\nexamine the plate in day light. The chromatographic profile of\nthe test solution is similar to that ofthe reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Foreign organic matter (2.6.1). Not more than 1.0 per cent.\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 14.0 percent.\nAcid-insoluble ash (2.3.19). Not more than 2.5 per cent.\nSENNA LEAF\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 12.0 per cent,\ndetermined on 5 g by drying in an oven at 105° .\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial\ncontamillation tests.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh accurately 1.0 g ofthe coarsely powdered\nsubstance in a round bottom flask, add about 10 ml of 1 per\ncent v/v acetic acid and 25 ml of methanol and reflux on a\nwater bath for about 30 minutes. Cool to room temperature;\nmake up the volume up to 50.0 ml with methanol and filter.\nReference solution. A 0.004 per cent w/v solution of\nsennasides RS in methanol.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 25 em x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 Ilm),\n.\nmobile phase: a mixtures of 82 volumes of 1 per cent\nv/v acetic acidin water and 18 volumes ofacetonitrile,\nflow rate. 1ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 350 urn,\ninjection volume. 20 Ill.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injections is not\nmore than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of sennoside A and B.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reagents-and-solutions","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reagents and Solutions"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Reagents and Solutions \t\n883\n5.\nGeneral Tests \t\n973\n6.\nPrimary Packages for Pharmaceuticals \t\n1019\n7.\nTables \t\n1059\nGENERAL NOTICES\nTests and Assays\n15"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Reagents and Solutions \t\n883\n5.\nGeneral Tests \t\n973\n6.\nPrimary Packages for Pharmaceuticals \t\n1019\n7.\nTables \t\n1059\nGENERAL NOTICES\nTests and Assays\n15"},{"name":"Tests","text":"15 \nOther Tests\n15 \nLimits\n15 \nQuantities\n15 \nApparatus\n15 \nItpagents and Solutions\n15 \nIndicators\n15 \nReference Substances\n15 \nTests Animals\n16 \nCalculation of Results\n16"},{"name":"Storage","text":"16 \nStorage Containers\n16"},{"name":"Labelling","text":"IP 2018"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rebamipide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rebamipide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"After the sinking time has been recorded in test A, remove the \nbasket from the water, allow it to drain for 30 seconds with its \nlong axis in the horizontal position over the beaker, transfer it \nto a tared beaker and weigh to the nearest 10 mg. Calculate the \nweight of water retained by the sample. Repeat the procedure \non two further samples and calculate the average value.\nColouring matter. Slowly extract 10 g in a harrow percolator \nwith ethanol (95 per- cent) until 50 ml of extract is obtained. \nThe extract is not more intensely coloured than reference \nsolution YS5 or GYS6, (2.4.1) or a solution prepared in the \nfollowing manner. Tp 3.0 ml of CSS add 7.0 ml of a solution qf \nhydrochloric acid containing,! per cent w/y of hydrochloric \nacid and dilute 0.5 ml of the resulting solution to 10 mfwith \nthe same solution of hydrochloric acid.\nEther-soluble substances. Not more than 0.5 per cent, \ndetermined by the following method. Extract 5 g with ethey in \na continuous extraction apparatus such as a Soxhlet apparatus, \nfor 4 hours in such a way that the rate is at least four extractions \nper hour. Evaporate the ether and dry the residue to constant\nweight at 105°.\nWater-soluble substances. Not more than 0.7 per cent, \ndetermined by the following method. Boil 5 g with 500 ml of \nwater for 30 minutes, stirring frequently and replacing the \nwater lost by evaporation. Decant the liquid into a beaker, \nsqueeze the residual liquid from the material carefully with a \nglass rod, mix the liquids and filter the extract whilst hot: \nEvaporate 400 ml of the filtrate and dry the residue to constant \nWcightat 105°. \ny. m"},"sections":[{"name":"REBAMIPIDE","text":"After the sinking time has been recorded in test A, remove the \nbasket from the water, allow it to drain for 30 seconds with its \nlong axis in the horizontal position over the beaker, transfer it \nto a tared beaker and weigh to the nearest 10 mg. Calculate the \nweight of water retained by the sample. Repeat the procedure \non two further samples and calculate the average value.\nColouring matter. Slowly extract 10 g in a harrow percolator \nwith ethanol (95 per- cent) until 50 ml of extract is obtained. \nThe extract is not more intensely coloured than reference \nsolution YS5 or GYS6, (2.4.1) or a solution prepared in the \nfollowing manner. Tp 3.0 ml of CSS add 7.0 ml of a solution qf \nhydrochloric acid containing,! per cent w/y of hydrochloric \nacid and dilute 0.5 ml of the resulting solution to 10 mfwith \nthe same solution of hydrochloric acid.\nEther-soluble substances. Not more than 0.5 per cent, \ndetermined by the following method. Extract 5 g with ethey in \na continuous extraction apparatus such as a Soxhlet apparatus, \nfor 4 hours in such a way that the rate is at least four extractions \nper hour. Evaporate the ether and dry the residue to constant\nweight at 105°.\nWater-soluble substances. Not more than 0.7 per cent, \ndetermined by the following method. Boil 5 g with 500 ml of \nwater for 30 minutes, stirring frequently and replacing the \nwater lost by evaporation. Decant the liquid into a beaker, \nsqueeze the residual liquid from the material carefully with a \nglass rod, mix the liquids and filter the extract whilst hot: \nEvaporate 400 ml of the filtrate and dry the residue to constant \nWcightat 105°. \ny. m"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reboxetine-methanesulphonate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reboxetine Methanesulphonate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Reboxetine Methanesulphonate\n...... ;\n' \"NH , CH3SO3H\nCH3 < \n.■ : \n:\nGoH27N06S v ..... .r;,• • y . , . Mol. Wt.409,5\nReboxetine Mcthanesulphonate is (±)-2-[(2-Ethoxypherioxy) \nbenzyljmorpholine methane sulphonic acid. . ■ ■ to:> ■; ;\nReboxetine. Methane sulphonate contains not less than \n98.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per,cent of C2oH27N06S, \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n:\nCategory. Antidepressant, ; \n.......\nDescription. A white crystalline powder. \n•••’• ww,\ny :T.i\\G7 C 1. t ;• j • ;R-J,'if •  • 1 . ,r j ; • • •. <"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Reboxetine Methanesulphonate\n...... ;\n' \"NH , CH3SO3H\nCH3 < \n.■ : \n:\nGoH27N06S v ..... .r;,• • y . , . Mol. Wt.409,5\nReboxetine Mcthanesulphonate is (±)-2-[(2-Ethoxypherioxy) \nbenzyljmorpholine methane sulphonic acid. . ■ ■ to:> ■; ;\nReboxetine. Methane sulphonate contains not less than \n98.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per,cent of C2oH27N06S, \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n:\nCategory. Antidepressant, ; \n.......\nDescription. A white crystalline powder. \n•••’• ww,\ny :T.i\\G7 C 1. t ;• j • ;R-J,'if •  • 1 . ,r j ; • • •. <"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with' reboxetine \nmcthanesulphonate IPRS or with the reference spectrum of' \nreboxetine mcthanesulphonate.\nB. In the.Assay, the: principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained , with the test solution corresponds to the principal \npeak inthe,chromatogram obtained with the refcrence solutiom"},{"name":"Tests","text":".7 \n....\n.■ \n: ... :■ ■' - .--j .-■■ ■ : j.;' fO e ,>u\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 5;5, determined in 1 per cent w/v solution in\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. ■\nIf 2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reboxetine-methanesulponate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reboxetine Methanesulponate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"REBOXETINE METHANESULPONATE \nPURIFIED RAYON \t\nIP 2011\nin the Colour of extract, add 1.9 ml of water, 0.15 ml of dilute \nacetic acid and 1 ml of lead acetate solution. After 2  minutes, \nthe solution is not more intensely coloured than a referenc e\nchromatographic system\nReboxetine Methanesulphonate is (±)-2-[(2-Ethoxyph,enoxy) \nbenzyl]morpholine methane sulphonic acid.\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \n- mobile phase: a mixture of 49 volumes of methanol and\nReboxetine Methanesulphonate contains not less than \n98.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C 20 1-127NO6S, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\n solution prepared at the same time using 0.15 ml of \ndilute\n acetic acid, \n1.2 ml of thioacetamide reagent, 1.7 ml of lead \nstandard solution (10 ppm Pb) and 10 ml of filtered extract.\n51 volumes of a 1.0 per cent w/v solution of sodium \nacetate and 2 volumes of glacial acetic acid, \nflow rate: 1.0 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 327 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 gl.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 1.5 per cent.\nDescription. A white crystalline powder.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 13.0 per cent, determined \non 5 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nInject the test solution. The sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 1.5 per cent, calculated by"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"REBOXETINE METHANESULPONATE \nPURIFIED RAYON \t\nIP 2011\nin the Colour of extract, add 1.9 ml of water, 0.15 ml of dilute \nacetic acid and 1 ml of lead acetate solution. After 2  minutes, \nthe solution is not more intensely coloured than a referenc e\nchromatographic system\nReboxetine Methanesulphonate is (±)-2-[(2-Ethoxyph,enoxy) \nbenzyl]morpholine methane sulphonic acid.\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \n- mobile phase: a mixture of 49 volumes of methanol and\nReboxetine Methanesulphonate contains not less than \n98.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C 20 1-127NO6S, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\n solution prepared at the same time using 0.15 ml of \ndilute\n acetic acid, \n1.2 ml of thioacetamide reagent, 1.7 ml of lead \nstandard solution (10 ppm Pb) and 10 ml of filtered extract.\n51 volumes of a 1.0 per cent w/v solution of sodium \nacetate and 2 volumes of glacial acetic acid, \nflow rate: 1.0 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 327 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 gl.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 1.5 per cent.\nDescription. A white crystalline powder.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 13.0 per cent, determined \non 5 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nInject the test solution. The sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 1.5 per cent, calculated by"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with reboxetine \nmethanesulphonate RS or with the reference spectrum of \nreboxetine methanesulphonate.\nHareavnyorm\nmeatlaislas 2.3 \nnormalisation."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/recombinant-human-parathyroid-hormone-rhpth","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Recombinant Human Parathyroid Hormone (rhPTH' -\")"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Recombinant Human Parathyroid Hormone (rhPTH' -\")\ninjection \nRecombinant Human Parathyroid Hormone (rhPTH' -\n) \ninjection, is a sterile solution for injection containing \nteriparatide (rhPTH' -34) as an active ingredient with suitable \nbuffering and preservative agents.\nTeriparatide injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the labeled amount of \nteriparatide.\nUsual Strength. Pre-filled cartridge  /  vial contains 250 lig per\nml of teriparatide (rhPTH' -34).\nDescription. Clear, colorless or slightly yellowish solution."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Recombinant Human Parathyroid Hormone (rhPTH' -\")\ninjection \nRecombinant Human Parathyroid Hormone (rhPTH' -\n) \ninjection, is a sterile solution for injection containing \nteriparatide (rhPTH' -34) as an active ingredient with suitable \nbuffering and preservative agents.\nTeriparatide injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the labeled amount of \nteriparatide.\nUsual Strength. Pre-filled cartridge  /  vial contains 250 lig per\nml of teriparatide (rhPTH' -34).\nDescription. Clear, colorless or slightly yellowish solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test C may be omitted if tests A and B are carried out.  Tests B \nmay be omitted if tests A and C are carried out.\nA. The biological activity of teriparatide is estimated to be \nequipotent to that teriparatide RS based on its comparative \nability to stimulate the accumulation of cAMP in UMR-106 \ncells with respect to the teriparatide RS.\nDetermination of the biological activity of teriparatide is based \non the stimulation of adenylate cyclase activity in the rat \nosteosarcoma cell line UMR-106. Activation of the PTH \nreceptor initiates a cascade event which culminates in an\nintracellular rise in cAMP concentration.\nThe method uses cAMP end-point measurement by ELISA.\nIncubate UMR-106 cells in tissue culture plate until an even \nmonolayer is observed. Maintain the cells in serum free media \nfor 18 to 26 hours in order to decrease the levels of endogenous \ncAMP. Suitably wash the cells and incubate with varying \ndilutions of test and reference preparations for up to 8 dilutions \nfor the suitable time. Collect the cell lysate containing the \ncAMP and determine the cAMP accumulation by using ELISA \nor suitable detection assay.\nAnalyze the data by fitting a sigmoidal dose-response curve \nto the data obtained and by using a suitable statistical method, \nfor example the 4- parameter or parallel line model.\nThe relative potency is not less than 75 percent and not more \nthan 125 percent of the stated potency.\nB. Determine by Liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as described \nin assay. In assay, the retention time of the Teriparatide peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorresponds to that of the peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution.\nTERIPARATIDE INJECTION\nSample treatment. Boil for 2 minutes.\nGel dimensions. 1.5 mm thick, 10 cm  x 10.5 cm.\nTime \n(in min)\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\nResolving gel. 20 per cent acrylamide. Mix the components \nin the order shown.\nApplication:\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n24 \nWell \nSolution(s) \nVolume (11)\nSolution \tComponent volume (ml) \ncomponents \t\nper gel volume of 7.5 ml\n1 \nReference solution (a) \n50 \n26.01 \n66.5 \n33.5 \n2 \nReference solution (b) \n50 \n30 \n60 \n40 \n3 \nTest solution \n50 \n30.01 \n76 \n24 \n4 \nReference solution (c) \n50 \n40 \n76 \n24\nWater \n1.20\n50 per cent acrylamide solution* \n3.00\nTris-SDS solution (pH 8.45) \n2.49\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the reference solution relative \ntetention time of three peaks corresponding to oxidized \nteriparatide with reference to principal peak are 0.79, 0.66 and \n0A6."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/recombinant-streptokinase-bulk-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Recombinant Streptokinase Bulk Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RECOMBINANT STREPTOKINASE BULK SOLUTION\nRecombinant Streptokinase Bulk\nSolution\nStreptokinase Concentrated Solution\nIAGPEWLLDR PSVNNSQLW SVAGTVEGTN QDlSLKFFEl DLTSRPAHGG KTEQGLSPKS KLFATDSGAM\nPHKLEKADLL KA1QEQLIAN VHSNDDYFEV IDFASDATIT DRNGKVYFAD KÙGSVTLPIQ PVQEFLLKGH\nVRVRPYKEKP VQNQAKSVDV EYTVQFTPLN PDDDFRPALK DTKL.LKTLA1 GDTITSQELL AQAQS1LNKN\nHPGYTIYERD SSIVTHDND1 FRTILPMOQE FTYHVKNREQ AYRINKKSGL NEEINNTDLt SEKYYVLKKG\nEKPYDPFDRS HLKLFTIKYV DVNTNELLKS EQLLTASERN LDpRDLYDPR QKAKLLYNNL DAFGIMCYTL -\nTGKVEDNHDD TNRIITVYMG KRPEGENASY HLAYDKDRYT EEEREVYSYL RYTGTPIPDN PNDK\nRecombinant Streptokinase Bulk. Solution is a fibrinolytic \nenzyme produced by a method based on recombinant DNA \ntechnology using bacteria or suitable genetically engineered \nhost cells. ' \n• • ' • ' \n■ ; \n'\nRecombinant Streptokinase Bulk Solution has a potency of \nnot less than 60.0 IU per microgram of nitrogen.\nCategory. Thrombolytic agent."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RECOMBINANT STREPTOKINASE BULK SOLUTION\nRecombinant Streptokinase Bulk\nSolution\nStreptokinase Concentrated Solution\nIAGPEWLLDR PSVNNSQLW SVAGTVEGTN QDlSLKFFEl DLTSRPAHGG KTEQGLSPKS KLFATDSGAM\nPHKLEKADLL KA1QEQLIAN VHSNDDYFEV IDFASDATIT DRNGKVYFAD KÙGSVTLPIQ PVQEFLLKGH\nVRVRPYKEKP VQNQAKSVDV EYTVQFTPLN PDDDFRPALK DTKL.LKTLA1 GDTITSQELL AQAQS1LNKN\nHPGYTIYERD SSIVTHDND1 FRTILPMOQE FTYHVKNREQ AYRINKKSGL NEEINNTDLt SEKYYVLKKG\nEKPYDPFDRS HLKLFTIKYV DVNTNELLKS EQLLTASERN LDpRDLYDPR QKAKLLYNNL DAFGIMCYTL -\nTGKVEDNHDD TNRIITVYMG KRPEGENASY HLAYDKDRYT EEEREVYSYL RYTGTPIPDN PNDK\nRecombinant Streptokinase Bulk. Solution is a fibrinolytic \nenzyme produced by a method based on recombinant DNA \ntechnology using bacteria or suitable genetically engineered \nhost cells. ' \n• • ' • ' \n■ ; \n'\nRecombinant Streptokinase Bulk Solution has a potency of \nnot less than 60.0 IU per microgram of nitrogen.\nCategory. Thrombolytic agent."},{"name":"Production","text":"If intended for- use ; in the manufacture of parenteral \npreparations, the method of manufacture is validated to\ndemonstrate that the product, if tested, would comply with \nthe following test. .\nHost cell-derived proteins (HCP). Not more than 100 ppm.\nHost cell and vector-derived DNA. Not more than 10 ng per \ndose. ■ \n•• ■'\nDescription. A clear, colourless to slightly yellowish liquid."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Clot Lysis method: Place 0.5 Wof citrated human plasma in \na haemolysis tube maintained in a water-bath at 37°. Add Ó. 1 \nml of a solution of the contents of the container containing\n10000 IU of streptokinase activity per ml in phosphate buffer \npH 7.2 and 0.1 ml of a solution of humanthrombin Containing \n20 IU per ml in phosphate buffer pH 7.2. Mix immediately. A \nclot forms and lyses within 30 minutes. Repeat the procedure \nusing citrated bovine plasma.Thecloldocsnot lyse, within\n1 hour. \n. a.- \nHq./iyhyd ,;g;-\nB. Determine by western blotting method, Sodium dodecyl \nsulphate polyacrylamide gel'electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) \n(2.4.12) under reducing condition. \n: ; ' . \"\nRun a 12 per cent SDS-PAGE gel after loading the lanes with \npreparation under examination equivalent to 10-2Q pg\nRecombinant Streptokinase Bulk Solution and bovine serum \nalbumin as negative control. Set up the transfer blotting \napparatus, Develop the blot with swj^bl^de^qlpp^rv:'5hg end \nresult is the development of colour onlyinthesample protein\n““ lane (Recombinant Streptokinase main band) which is identified\nand confirmed by the corresponding antibody. ;\nC. Determine purity by Sodium dodecyl sulphate \npolyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) (2.4:12) under \nreducing condition.\nIn SDS-PAGE the mobility depends on the protein’s molecular \nsize. Run a 12 per cent resolving gel mix and 5 per cent stacking \ngel after loading the lanes with 10-20 pg equivalents \nRecombinant Streptokinase Bulk Solution. Stain the gel with \ncoomassie blue and destain.\nNo artefacts are visualized in the sample protein lane. Protein\nband intensity is appropriate to the protein load. Main band \nin same position as standard and in line with the molecular \nweight markers. Main band >97 per cent of total integrated \nbands.\nD. Determine purity by Sodium dodecyl sulphate \npolyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE)(2.4.12) under \nnon-reducing condition.\nIn native polyacrylamide gel'electrophoresis the mobility \ndepends on both the protein’s charge and its hydrodynamic \nsize. The electric charge driving the electrophoresis is \ngoverned by the intrinsic charge on the protein at the pH of \nthe running buffer.\nRun 8 per cent native resolving gel after loading the lanes \nwith 10-20 pg equivalents Recombinant Streptokinase Bulk\nSolution. Stain the gel with coomassie blue and destain.\nNo artefacts are visualized in the sample protein lane. Protein\nband intensity is appropriate to the protein load. Main band \nsame position as standard and iriam band > 98 per cent of total \nintegrated bands. \n■\nE. Determine by isoelectric .focussing polyacrylamide gel \nelectrophoresis (PAGE) (2.4.33) under non-reducing condition.\nIsoelectric focusing is a method of electrophoresis that \nseparates; proteins^according to their net charge.(pl): \nSeparation is carried out in poly acrylamide gel which contains \nthe mixture of amphoteric electrolytes called poly ampholytes \n(small multicharged polymers that have many pl values);\nRun 6 per cent resolving gel mix with ampholytes (3 -10, Broad \nrange pl) after loading the lanes with 10-20 ug equivalents \nRecombinant Streptokinase Bulk Solution. Stain the gel with \ncoomassie blue and destain. \n, .\nNo artefacts are visualized in the sample protein lane. Protein \nband intensity is appropriate to the protein load; The sample \nshould have single main band at Rf position pl 5.0 5.3 is \nobserved. \n~\nTests . \n- - \n, . ’ . \n„ ■ xh\npH (2:4.24).,6.8.to 7.5, determined in a solution prepared by \ndiluting the preparation under examination vacaKbondioxide-\nRECOMBINANT STREPTOKINASE BULK SOLUTION\nIP 20221\nfree water to produce a solution containing at least 105 IU of\nstreptokinase activity per ml.\nProtein. Üetermine by nitrogen content (2.3.30). 1 mg of N is \nequivalentto 6.25 mg of protein."},{"name":"Assay","text":"Potency. The potency of streptokinase is determined by \ncomparing its capacity to activate plasminogen to form plasmin \nwith the same capacity of a ; reference preparation of\nstreptokinase calibrated in International Units; the formation \nof plasmin is determined using a suitable chromogenic \nsubstrate. • \n>. ■■\nThe International Unit is the activity of a stated amount of the \nInternational Standard for streptokinase. The equivalence in \nInternational Units of the International Standard is as stated \nby the World Health Organization.\nReference and test solutions: Prepare 2 independent series of \n4 dilutions of each of the substance under examination and of \nthe reference preparation of streptokinase in tris\n(hydroxymethyl) amiriomethane sodium chloride buffer pH \n7.4, in the range of 0.5-4.0IU per ml. Prepare and maintain all \nsolutions at 37°.\nSubstrate solution: Mix 1.0 ml of tris (hydroxymethyl) \naminomethane buffer pH 7.4 with 1.0. ml . of chromophore \nsubstrate. Add 5 pl of a 10 per cent w/v solution oîpolysorbate \n20. Keep. at. 37° in a water-bath. Immediately before \ncommencing the activation assay, add 45 pt of a 1 mg per ml \nsolution of human plasminogen.\nAnalyse each.streptokinase dilution, maintained at.37°,.in \nduplicate. Initiate the activation reaction by adding 60 pl of \neach dilution to 40 pl of substrate solution. For blank wells, \nuse 60 pl of 'tris (hydroxymethyl) aminbméthânè sodium \nchloride buffer solution pH 7.4.instead bf the reference and \ntest solutions. Allow the reaction to proceed at 37° for 20 \nminutes and read- the absorbance at 405 nm. If a suitable \nthermostated plate reader is available, this may be used to \nmonitor the reaction; Alternatively, iUmay be necessary to \nstop the reaction after 20 minutes using 50/pl of a 50 per cent \nv/y solution oî glacial ace tic acid.; Best results are obtained; \nwhen the,, absorbance for the highest streptokinase \nconcentration is betwecn O.l anfrO.2 (after blank subtraction). \nIf necessary, adjust the time of incubation, in order to reach \nthis range of absorbances.\nCalculate the regression of the absorbance on log \nconcentrations,of the. solutions of;the substance under \nexamination and of the reference preparation of streptokinase \nand calculate the potency of the substance under examination \nusing the usual statistical methods for parallel-line assays:\nThe; estimated' potency is not less than 90 per cent and not \nmore than 11 Lper cent of the stated potency. The confidence\nlimits (P = 0.95) of the estimated potency are not less than 80 \nper cent and not more than 125 percent of the stated potency,\nRecombinant Streptokinase Bulk Solution intended for use in \nthe manufacture of parenteral preparations without a further \nappropriate procedure for the removal of bacterial endotoxins \ncomplies with the following additional requirement.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2,3). Mot more than 0,02 Endotoxin \nUnit per 100 IU of streptokinase activity.\nStorage. Store protected from light and at a temperature of \nabout -20°. If it is intended for the manufacture of parenteral \npreparations, the container should be sterile and sealed so as \nto exclude micro-organisms.\nLabeling. The label states (1) .the number of International Units \nof streptokinase activity per mg, calculated on the dried basis; \n(2) the name and quantity of any added substance; (3) where \napplicable, that the substance is free from bacterial endotoxins;1 \n(4) where applicable, that the substance is suitable for use in: \nthe manufacture of parenteral preparations."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/recombinant-streptokinase-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Recombinant Streptokinase for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Recombinant Streptokinase for \nInjection\nRecombinant Streptokinase for Injection is a sterile preparation' \nin a sealed container consisting of Streptokinase with or \nwithout auxiliary agents.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile water for \ninjection, immediately before use.\nUsual strengths. 750,000 IU per vial and 1,500,000 IU per vial.;\nDescription. A white powder or a white, friable solid, \nhygroscopic. . . \n,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Recombinant Streptokinase for \nInjection\nRecombinant Streptokinase for Injection is a sterile preparation' \nin a sealed container consisting of Streptokinase with or \nwithout auxiliary agents.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile water for \ninjection, immediately before use.\nUsual strengths. 750,000 IU per vial and 1,500,000 IU per vial.;\nDescription. A white powder or a white, friable solid, \nhygroscopic. . . \n,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by clot lysis method. Place 0.5 ml of citrated human \nplasma in a haemolysis tube maintained in a water-bath at 37°. \nAdd 0.1 ml bf a solution of the contents of the container\ncontaining 10 000 IU of streptokinase activity per millilitre in \nphosphate buffer pH 7.2 and 0.1 ml of a solution of human \nthrombin containing 20 IU per ml in phosphate buffer pH7.2.;\nMix immediately. A clot forms and lyses within 30 min. Repeat , \nthe procedure using citrated,bovine plasma. The clot does ; \nnot lyse within 1 hour."},{"name":"Tests","text":"• \n-\nPhysical Form. Lyophilized powder.\nParticulate contamination (2.5;9). The reconstituted solution \ncomplies with the limit test for particulate contamination.\nIP 2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/recommended-solutions-and-culture-media","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Recommended Solutions and Culture Media"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Recommended solutions and culture media\nAdjust the pH so that after sterilisation it is 7.1  + 0.2. Boil for\n1 minute to effect solution.\nThe following solutions and culture media have been found \nto be satisfactory for the purpose for which they are prescribed \nin the tests for microbial contamination in the Pharmacopoeia. \nOther media may be used provided their suitability can be \ndemonstrated.\nMedium 9. Rappaport Vassiliadis Salmonella Enrichment \nbroth\nMedium 5. Enterobacteria enrichment broth -11ossel\nPancreatic digest of gelatin \t\n10.0\nDextrose monohydrate \t\n5.0 g\nSoya peptone \n4.5\nMagnesium chloride hexahydrate \n29.0\nDehydrated ox bile \t\n20.0 g\nBuffered sodium chloride -peptone solution pH 7.0\nSodium chloride \n8.0\nPotassium dihydrogen phosphate \t2.0 g\nPotassium dihydrogen phosphate \t3.6 g \nDisodium hydrogen phosphate dihydrate \t\n7.2 g \nSodium chloride \t\n4.3 g \nPeptone (meat or casein) \t\n1.0 g \nPurified water \t\n1000 ml\nDipotassium phosphate \n0.4 \t\ng"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Recommended solutions and culture media\nAdjust the pH so that after sterilisation it is 7.1  + 0.2. Boil for\n1 minute to effect solution.\nThe following solutions and culture media have been found \nto be satisfactory for the purpose for which they are prescribed \nin the tests for microbial contamination in the Pharmacopoeia. \nOther media may be used provided their suitability can be \ndemonstrated.\nMedium 9. Rappaport Vassiliadis Salmonella Enrichment \nbroth\nMedium 5. Enterobacteria enrichment broth -11ossel\nPancreatic digest of gelatin \t\n10.0\nDextrose monohydrate \t\n5.0 g\nSoya peptone \n4.5\nMagnesium chloride hexahydrate \n29.0\nDehydrated ox bile \t\n20.0 g\nBuffered sodium chloride -peptone solution pH 7.0\nSodium chloride \n8.0\nPotassium dihydrogen phosphate \t2.0 g\nPotassium dihydrogen phosphate \t3.6 g \nDisodium hydrogen phosphate dihydrate \t\n7.2 g \nSodium chloride \t\n4.3 g \nPeptone (meat or casein) \t\n1.0 g \nPurified water \t\n1000 ml\nDipotassium phosphate \n0.4 \t\ng"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/redblood-cells","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"RedBlood Cells"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Red Blood Cells\nRouleaux\nAnti Human\n0\nAbsent\n; , Globulin\nA\nAbsent\nB\nAbsent\nThe antithrombin III is purified and concentrated and a \nsuitable stabiliser may be added. The specific activity is not \nless than 3 IU of antithrombin III per mg of total protein, \nexcluding albumin. The antithrombin III concentrate is passed \nthrough a bacteria-retentive filter, distributed aseptically into\nvolume of 2-3 per cent sensitized red cell suspension in a testy \ntube and in another tube Anti Human Globulin (AHG) reagent \nis mixed with 1 volume of 2-3 per cent unsensitized red cell \nsuspension in normal saline. Centrifuge the tubes at lOOOrpm \nfor 1 minute. Red blood cells with (Rjt and R^r) antigen givers \npositive reaction and the tube which lacks the antigen gives \nnegative reaction. \n,\nTest with Enzyme treated Red Blood Cells\nAnti Human Globulin (AHG) reagent should not agglutinate \n0.1 per cent enzyme (trypsin or papain or ficin) treated red \nblood cells.\nits final, sterile containers and immediately frozen. It is then \nfreeze-dried and the containers are closed under vacuum or in \nan atmosphere of inert gas. No antimicrobial preservative is \nadded at any stage of production.\nType of Reagent\nEnzyme treated \nRed Blood Cells\nRouleaux\nType of Reagent\nTest Red Blood Cells\nSpecificity7\nAnti Human\nO\nAbsent\nO +ve (Rp and Ror) -\nAnti Human\nPositive\nIt shall be demonstrated that the manufacturing process yields \na product that consistently complies with the fol lowing, test.\nGlobulin\nsensitized with IgG\nGlobulin\nA\nAbsent\nNegative .T\nO +ve (Rp and Ror) -\nB\nAbsent '\nHeparin-binding fraction. Examine by agarose gel \nelectrophoresis (2.4.12): Prepare a 1.0 per cent w/v solution of \nagarose for electrophoresis containing 15 IU of heparin per \nml in barbital buffer solution pH 8.4. Pour 5 ml of this solution \nonto a glass plate 5 cm square. Cool at 4° for 30 minutes. Cut \n2 wells, 2 mm in diameter, 1 cm and 4 cm from the side of the \nplate and 1 cm from the cathode. Introduce into one wel! 5 pl \nof the preparation under examination, diluted to an activity of \nabout 1 IU of antithrombin III per ml. Introduce into the other \nwell 5 pl of a solution of a marker dye such as brbmophenol \nblue.' Allow the electrophoresis to proceed at 4°, using a \nconstant electric field of 7 V/cm, until the dye reaches the \nanode.\nunsensitized\nExpiration date. Its expiration date is not later than 1 year after \nthe date of issue from manufacturer’s cold storage (5°, 1 year; \nor0°,2years).\nReactivity\nHaemolysis \n<\nStorage. Storage at a temperature between 2° to 8°.\nIt is defined as the release of haemoglobin from the red cells; \nas a result of osmotic effect or due to the breaking up of the \nred blood cells: No immune haemolysis should be observed \nwith the red blood cells used for specificity. \n■ \n.\nLabeling. Label states (1) Source of Product; (2) Test method \n(s) recommended; (3) Storage temperature; (4) Preservative \nused and its concentration; (5) Statement “FOR IN VITRO \nDIAGNOSTIC USE”\nType of Reagent \nTest Red Blood Cells Haemolysis .\nNQTE— The lettering on the label of the general-purpose\nAbsent .-I polyspecific reagent is black on a white background. The\nAnti Human\nO+ve (RirandRoO-\n; I label of all other Anti-Human Globulin Serum containers is\nAbsent ■■ I fiwhite lettering on a black background.\nGlobulin\nsensitized with IgG\nCut across the agarose gel, 1.5 cm from that side of the plate \non which the preparation under examination was applied and \nremove the larger portion of the gel leaving a band 1.5 cm \nwide containing the material under examination. Replace the \nremoved portion with an even layer consisting of 3.5 ml of a\nO +ve (Rp and R^r) -\nunsensitized\nRouleaux"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Red Blood Cells\nRouleaux\nAnti Human\n0\nAbsent\n; , Globulin\nA\nAbsent\nB\nAbsent\nThe antithrombin III is purified and concentrated and a \nsuitable stabiliser may be added. The specific activity is not \nless than 3 IU of antithrombin III per mg of total protein, \nexcluding albumin. The antithrombin III concentrate is passed \nthrough a bacteria-retentive filter, distributed aseptically into\nvolume of 2-3 per cent sensitized red cell suspension in a testy \ntube and in another tube Anti Human Globulin (AHG) reagent \nis mixed with 1 volume of 2-3 per cent unsensitized red cell \nsuspension in normal saline. Centrifuge the tubes at lOOOrpm \nfor 1 minute. Red blood cells with (Rjt and R^r) antigen givers \npositive reaction and the tube which lacks the antigen gives \nnegative reaction. \n,\nTest with Enzyme treated Red Blood Cells\nAnti Human Globulin (AHG) reagent should not agglutinate \n0.1 per cent enzyme (trypsin or papain or ficin) treated red \nblood cells.\nits final, sterile containers and immediately frozen. It is then \nfreeze-dried and the containers are closed under vacuum or in \nan atmosphere of inert gas. No antimicrobial preservative is \nadded at any stage of production.\nType of Reagent\nEnzyme treated \nRed Blood Cells\nRouleaux\nType of Reagent\nTest Red Blood Cells\nSpecificity7\nAnti Human\nO\nAbsent\nO +ve (Rp and Ror) -\nAnti Human\nPositive\nIt shall be demonstrated that the manufacturing process yields \na product that consistently complies with the fol lowing, test.\nGlobulin\nsensitized with IgG\nGlobulin\nA\nAbsent\nNegative .T\nO +ve (Rp and Ror) -\nB\nAbsent '\nHeparin-binding fraction. Examine by agarose gel \nelectrophoresis (2.4.12): Prepare a 1.0 per cent w/v solution of \nagarose for electrophoresis containing 15 IU of heparin per \nml in barbital buffer solution pH 8.4. Pour 5 ml of this solution \nonto a glass plate 5 cm square. Cool at 4° for 30 minutes. Cut \n2 wells, 2 mm in diameter, 1 cm and 4 cm from the side of the \nplate and 1 cm from the cathode. Introduce into one wel! 5 pl \nof the preparation under examination, diluted to an activity of \nabout 1 IU of antithrombin III per ml. Introduce into the other \nwell 5 pl of a solution of a marker dye such as brbmophenol \nblue.' Allow the electrophoresis to proceed at 4°, using a \nconstant electric field of 7 V/cm, until the dye reaches the \nanode.\nunsensitized\nExpiration date. Its expiration date is not later than 1 year after \nthe date of issue from manufacturer’s cold storage (5°, 1 year; \nor0°,2years).\nReactivity\nHaemolysis \n<\nStorage. Storage at a temperature between 2° to 8°.\nIt is defined as the release of haemoglobin from the red cells; \nas a result of osmotic effect or due to the breaking up of the \nred blood cells: No immune haemolysis should be observed \nwith the red blood cells used for specificity. \n■ \n.\nLabeling. Label states (1) Source of Product; (2) Test method \n(s) recommended; (3) Storage temperature; (4) Preservative \nused and its concentration; (5) Statement “FOR IN VITRO \nDIAGNOSTIC USE”\nType of Reagent \nTest Red Blood Cells Haemolysis .\nNQTE— The lettering on the label of the general-purpose\nAbsent .-I polyspecific reagent is black on a white background. The\nAnti Human\nO+ve (RirandRoO-\n; I label of all other Anti-Human Globulin Serum containers is\nAbsent ■■ I fiwhite lettering on a black background.\nGlobulin\nsensitized with IgG\nCut across the agarose gel, 1.5 cm from that side of the plate \non which the preparation under examination was applied and \nremove the larger portion of the gel leaving a band 1.5 cm \nwide containing the material under examination. Replace the \nremoved portion with an even layer consisting of 3.5 ml of a\nO +ve (Rp and R^r) -\nunsensitized\nRouleaux"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/references","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"References"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"References \n\t(h). To 15 mg of raloxi/ene hydrochloride \nRS, add 5.0 nitof reference solution (a) and 20 ml of the solvent \nmixture and then dilute to 50.0 ml with mobile phase A.\nto. pH '2:5 with orthophosphoric acid  or potassium\nhydroxide solution,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"References \n\t(h). To 15 mg of raloxi/ene hydrochloride \nRS, add 5.0 nitof reference solution (a) and 20 ml of the solvent \nmixture and then dilute to 50.0 ml with mobile phase A.\nto. pH '2:5 with orthophosphoric acid  or potassium\nhydroxide solution,"},{"name":"RALOXIF ENE HYDROCHLORIDE TABLETS","text":"Reference solution (c). A 0.006 per cent w/v solution of \nraloxifene hydrochloride RS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (d). Mix 5 ml of reference solution (c) and \n45 ml of the solvent mixture and dilute to 100.0 ml with mobile \nphase A to obtain a solution containing 0.0003 per cent w/v of \nRaloxifene Hydrochloride.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \ncolumn temperature: 35°, \nmobile phase: A. a mixture of 75 volumes of buffer \nsolution and 25 volumes of acetonitrile,\nB. a mixture of 50 volumes of buffer \nsolution and 50 volumes of acetonitrile, \na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 280 nm, \ninjection volume: 10[11.\nTime \t\nMobile phase A \t\nMobile phase B \n(in min) \t\n(per cent v/v) \t\n(per cent v/v)\n0 \t\n100 \t\n0\n5 \t100 \t\n0\n36 \t\n0 \t\n100\n38 \t\n1(x) \t\n0\n48 \t\n100 \t\n0\nName \t\nRelative \nretention time\nRaloxifene \t\n1.0\nRaloxifene N-oxide'\n1.17\nNethanone, [6-hydroxy-2-(4-hydroxyphenyl) benzo[b]thien-3-yl][4- \n[2-(1 -ox ido- 1 -piperidinyl)ethoxy]phenyl].\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to raloxifene and raloxifene \nN-oxide is not less than 3.0 and the tailing factor is not more \nthan 2.0.\nInject reference solution (d) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram twice the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof any peak corresponding to raloxifene N-oxide is not more \nthan 3 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (d) (0.3 per cent). The area of \nany other secondary peak is not more than twice the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (d) (0.2 per cent). The sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 10 times. the area.of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (d) (1.0 per cent)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/referencesolution-a-k5-0percentw-vsolutionufxylose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Referencesolution{a):·k5:0per··centw/vsolutionufxylose"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Referencesolution{a):·k5:0per··centw/vsolutionufxylose\nRS in water.\nReference solution (b). A mixture ofequal volumes ofthe test\nsolution and reference solution (a).\nApply to the plate 2 III of each solution. Develop the plate in\na continuous elution tank for about 4 hours. Dry the plate in\nwarm air, spray with a solution in acetone containing 1 per\ncent w/vsolution ofdiphenylamine, 1 per cent v/v ofaniline\nand 1per cent v/v ofphosphoric acid and heat for 10 minutes\nat 1300. The principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with\nthe test solution corresponds to that in the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (a). The principal spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) appears\nas a single, compact spot.\nB. When heated with potassium cupri-tartrate solution it\nproduces a copious precipitate of cuprous oxide."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Referencesolution{a):·k5:0per··centw/vsolutionufxylose\nRS in water.\nReference solution (b). A mixture ofequal volumes ofthe test\nsolution and reference solution (a).\nApply to the plate 2 III of each solution. Develop the plate in\na continuous elution tank for about 4 hours. Dry the plate in\nwarm air, spray with a solution in acetone containing 1 per\ncent w/vsolution ofdiphenylamine, 1 per cent v/v ofaniline\nand 1per cent v/v ofphosphoric acid and heat for 10 minutes\nat 1300. The principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with\nthe test solution corresponds to that in the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (a). The principal spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) appears\nas a single, compact spot.\nB. When heated with potassium cupri-tartrate solution it\nproduces a copious precipitate of cuprous oxide."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance ofsolution. A 10.0percentwIv solution in carbon\ndioxide-ji-ee water is clear (2.4.1), and colourless (2.4.1).\nAcidity. Dissolve 5.0 g in 50 ml ofcarbon dioxide-ji-ee water.\nNot more than 0.2 ml of0.1 M sodium hydroxide is required to\nneutralise the solution using dilute phenolphthalein solution\nas indicator.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). +l8S to +19S, determined\nat 200in a 10.0 per cent w/v solution containing 0.4 per cent\nv/v of5 M ammonia.\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 10.0 g ill 50 ml ofwater and add\n10 ml of stannated hydrochloric acid AsT. The resulting\nsolution complies with the limit test for arsenic (l ppm).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nheavy metals, Method A (20 ppm).\nXYLOSE\nIron (2.3.14). A solution of4.0 g in 25 ml ofwater complies\nwith the limit test for iron (l0 ppm).\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 3.0 g in 20 ml ofwater. 5 mlofthe\nresulting solution complies with the limit test for chlorides\n(330ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 0.5 percent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 1000at a pressure not exceeding\n0.7kPa.\nAssay. Carry out the follOWing procedure keeping strict\ncontrol oftime between steps.\nWeigh accurately about 1.0 g of the substance under\nexamination, dissolve in a saturated solution of benzoic acid\nin a 1OO-ml volumetric flask and dilute to volume with the same\nsolvent. Dilute 1.0 ml ofthis solution to 100.0 rn1 with the same\nsolvent. To 1.0 ml of the resulting solution, in two different\ntest-tubes, add 5 ml of 4-bromoaniline solution into each\ntube and mix. Loosely stopper one tube, place in a water-bath\nmaintained at 700for 10 minutes, remove, cool rapidly to room\ntemperature and mix. Keep the tube in the dark for 70 minutes\nand measure the absorbance at the maximum at about 520 urn\n(2.4.7), using the untreated solution in the second test tube as\nthe blank. Simultaneously, carry out the operation using\n1.0 ml ofa 0.01 per centw/v solution ofxyloseRSill a saturated\nsolution of benzoic acid beginning at the words \"add 5 ml of\n4-bromoaniline solution.....\".\nCalculate the content ofCsHIQOs.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nIP 20ID"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/referencesolution-b-dilute-1mloftest-solution-b-to-10ml","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Referencesolution(b); Dilute 1mloftest solution (b) to 10ml"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Referencesolution(b); Dilute 1mloftest solution (b) to 10ml\nwith chloroform.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve 10 mg of a-hexachloro-\ncyclohexaneRS in sufficient ofthe test solution (a) to produce\n5rnl.\nApply separately to the plate 1 f.ll ofeach solution. Allow the\nmobile phase to rise 12 em. Dry the plate in a current ofdry air\nand-miruiite wifuliltravl01et lIgnfaf23-4 iiiil-for-I5-iiiiiiiifes:\nSpray with a 0.6 per cent w/v solution of dicarboxidine\nhydrochloride in ethanol (90 percent) andexaminetlifispofs\nin daylight. Any secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution is not more intense than the spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b). The test\nis not valid unless the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (c) shows two clearly separated spots.\nChlorides (2.3.12). To 0.75 g, finely powdered, add 15 m1 of\nwater and boil for 1minute. Allow to cool, shaking frequently,\nand filter. To 10m1 ofthe filtrate add 3 m1 ofwater and 2 m1 of\nethanol (95 per cent). The solution complies with the.limjt\ntest for chlorides (l00 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.2 g, add 10 m1 of ethanol\n(95 per cent) and warm on a water-bath until dissolved. Cool,\nadd 20 m1 of 0.5 M ethanolic potassium hydroxide and allow\nto stand for 10 minutes, swirling frequently. Add 50 m1 of\nwater, 20 m1 of 2 M nitric acid, 25 m1 of 0.1 M silver nitrate\nand 5 m1 offerric ammonium sulphate solution. Titrate with\n0.1 M ammonium thiocyanate until a reddish-yellow colour is\nobtained. Repeat the procedure without the substance under\nexamination. The difference between the titrations represents\nthe amount of silver nitrate required.\n1 m1 of 0.1 M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0 009694 g of\nC6~CI6'\nStorage. Store protected from light."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Referencesolution(b); Dilute 1mloftest solution (b) to 10ml\nwith chloroform.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve 10 mg of a-hexachloro-\ncyclohexaneRS in sufficient ofthe test solution (a) to produce\n5rnl.\nApply separately to the plate 1 f.ll ofeach solution. Allow the\nmobile phase to rise 12 em. Dry the plate in a current ofdry air\nand-miruiite wifuliltravl01et lIgnfaf23-4 iiiil-for-I5-iiiiiiiifes:\nSpray with a 0.6 per cent w/v solution of dicarboxidine\nhydrochloride in ethanol (90 percent) andexaminetlifispofs\nin daylight. Any secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution is not more intense than the spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b). The test\nis not valid unless the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (c) shows two clearly separated spots.\nChlorides (2.3.12). To 0.75 g, finely powdered, add 15 m1 of\nwater and boil for 1minute. Allow to cool, shaking frequently,\nand filter. To 10m1 ofthe filtrate add 3 m1 ofwater and 2 m1 of\nethanol (95 per cent). The solution complies with the.limjt\ntest for chlorides (l00 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.2 g, add 10 m1 of ethanol\n(95 per cent) and warm on a water-bath until dissolved. Cool,\nadd 20 m1 of 0.5 M ethanolic potassium hydroxide and allow\nto stand for 10 minutes, swirling frequently. Add 50 m1 of\nwater, 20 m1 of 2 M nitric acid, 25 m1 of 0.1 M silver nitrate\nand 5 m1 offerric ammonium sulphate solution. Titrate with\n0.1 M ammonium thiocyanate until a reddish-yellow colour is\nobtained. Repeat the procedure without the substance under\nexamination. The difference between the titrations represents\nthe amount of silver nitrate required.\n1 m1 of 0.1 M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0 009694 g of\nC6~CI6'\nStorage. Store protected from light."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/refractive","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Refractive"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Refractive \t\nLoss on \nViscosity (centistokes) \t\ngravity \tindex \t\nheating \n(centi-\nvdrous \nsodium sulphate and centrifuging to obtain a clear supernatant\nor more at 25' ± 0.1°, using a rotational viscometer. The viscosity\n \nliquid. Determine the absorbance of a 0.5 -mm layer p  of t\nis within the limits specified in the accompanying table.\nstokes) Min. Max. \t\nMin. \t\nMax. \t\nMin. \t\nMax. Max. \n20 \t\n18 \t\n22\nsolution at the maximum at about 7.9 µm in a suitable i \t\ned \nn fra \nspectrophotometer (2.4.6), using the blank to set t \ninstrument. Calculate the content of [-(CH3)2Si0-]„ from\ni-r\nRefractive Index (2.4.27). Within the limits specified in the\n0.946 \t\n0.954 \t\n1.3980 1.4020 \n0.955 \t\n0.965 \t\n1.4005 1.4045 \n0.962 \t\n0.970 1.4005 1.4045 \n0.964 \t\n0.972 \t\n1.4013 1.4053 \n0.965 \t\n0.973 \t\n1.4013 1.4053 \n0.967 \t\n0.975 \t\n1.4013 1.4053 \n0.967 \t\n0.975 \t\n1.4013 1.4053\n20.0\n50 \t\n47.5 \t\n52.5\naccompanying \n     table. 5 \nb 11e. \nc:di \n \nt\n \ns.\n2.0\nfrom\nt \nabsorbance obtained by repeating the Assay on a 0.2 r \nw/v solution of dimethicone RS in place of the substance\n100 \t\n95 \t\n105 \n200 \t\n190 \t\n220 \n350 \t\n332.5 367.5\n15.0 g in a mixture of 15 ml of toluene and\n.Dissolve\n)\n0.3\nper cent\nnt\n15 ml of butyl alcohol, previously neutralized to bromophenol \nblue. and titrate with 0.050 M alcoholic potassium hydroxide \nto a bromophenol blue endpoint. Not more than 0.1 ml is \nrequired.\n0.3\nunder examination.\n0.3\n500 \t\n475 \t\n525\nFor silicon dioxide - Mix thoroughly and Weigh    \n)[\n0.3\ng:\n1000 \t\n950 1050\n i \ntransfer to a tared, sintered-glass filtering crucible ( r\no .o s)\nIr o \nNo. 4) and pass through the filter, with suction,\n0.3\nLoss on heating. Preheat an open aluminum vessel (60 mm in \ndiameter and 10 mm high) at 150° for 30 miriptes, and allow to \ncool to room temperature. Tare the vessel, Sansfer to it about\n12500 11875 13125\n1.4015 \t\n1.4055\n2.0\n200 ml of toluene, added with stirring in small portions, \nrtions,\n30000 27000 33000\n0.969 \t\n0.977 \t\n1.4010 1.4100\n2.0\nfollowed by similar washing of the material on the filter with \n200 ml of toluene, and discard the filtrates. Place the filtering \ncrucible in a muffle furnace at room temperature, raise the \ntemperature of the furnace to 550°. Heat at 550° ± 25' for \n2 hours. Cool the filtering crucible with its contents in a \ndesiccator, weigh and calculate the content of silicon dioxide.\nI g of Dimethicone, weighed, heat at 150° in a circulating air \noven for 2 hours, and allow to come to room temperature in a \ndesiccator before weighing: it loses not more than the maximum \npercentage of its weight specified in the accompanying table.\nDimethicone intended for use in the manufacture of \nparenteral preparations without a further appropriate \nprocedure for the removal of bacterial endotoxins complies\nwith the following additional requirement.\nBacterial Endotoxin (2.2.3). Not more than 10 Endotoxin Units \nper ml of the Dimethicone.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Mix 1.0 g of Dimethicone with 20 ml of \nchloroform. Add 1.0 ml of a freshly prepared 0.002 per cent \nw/v solution of dithizone in chloroform, 0.5 ml of water, and \n0.5 ml ofa mixture of 1 ml ofammonia and 9 ml ofa 0.2 per cent \nsolution of hydroxylamine hydrochloride. Prepare reference \nsolution by adding 1.0 ml ofa freshly prepared 0.002 per cent \nw/v solution of dithizone in chloroform to 20 ml of chloroform; \nadd 0.5 ml of lead standard solution (JO ppm pb) and 0.5 ml \nof a mixture of 1 ml of ammonia and 9 ml of a 0.2 per cent \nsolution of hydroxylamine hydrochloride. Immediately shake \nboth solutions vigorously for 1 minute. Any red color in the \ntest solution is not more intense than that in the reference \nsolution (5 µg per g).\nSiO2, in the sample taken.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nLabeling. Label it to indicate its nominal viscosity value. \nDimethicone intended for use in coating containers that come \nin contact with articles for parenteral use is so labelled."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Refractive \t\nLoss on \nViscosity (centistokes) \t\ngravity \tindex \t\nheating \n(centi-\nvdrous \nsodium sulphate and centrifuging to obtain a clear supernatant\nor more at 25' ± 0.1°, using a rotational viscometer. The viscosity\n \nliquid. Determine the absorbance of a 0.5 -mm layer p  of t\nis within the limits specified in the accompanying table.\nstokes) Min. Max. \t\nMin. \t\nMax. \t\nMin. \t\nMax. Max. \n20 \t\n18 \t\n22\nsolution at the maximum at about 7.9 µm in a suitable i \t\ned \nn fra \nspectrophotometer (2.4.6), using the blank to set t \ninstrument. Calculate the content of [-(CH3)2Si0-]„ from\ni-r\nRefractive Index (2.4.27). Within the limits specified in the\n0.946 \t\n0.954 \t\n1.3980 1.4020 \n0.955 \t\n0.965 \t\n1.4005 1.4045 \n0.962 \t\n0.970 1.4005 1.4045 \n0.964 \t\n0.972 \t\n1.4013 1.4053 \n0.965 \t\n0.973 \t\n1.4013 1.4053 \n0.967 \t\n0.975 \t\n1.4013 1.4053 \n0.967 \t\n0.975 \t\n1.4013 1.4053\n20.0\n50 \t\n47.5 \t\n52.5\naccompanying \n     table. 5 \nb 11e. \nc:di \n \nt\n \ns.\n2.0\nfrom\nt \nabsorbance obtained by repeating the Assay on a 0.2 r \nw/v solution of dimethicone RS in place of the substance\n100 \t\n95 \t\n105 \n200 \t\n190 \t\n220 \n350 \t\n332.5 367.5\n15.0 g in a mixture of 15 ml of toluene and\n.Dissolve\n)\n0.3\nper cent\nnt\n15 ml of butyl alcohol, previously neutralized to bromophenol \nblue. and titrate with 0.050 M alcoholic potassium hydroxide \nto a bromophenol blue endpoint. Not more than 0.1 ml is \nrequired.\n0.3\nunder examination.\n0.3\n500 \t\n475 \t\n525\nFor silicon dioxide - Mix thoroughly and Weigh    \n)[\n0.3\ng:\n1000 \t\n950 1050\n i \ntransfer to a tared, sintered-glass filtering crucible ( r\no .o s)\nIr o \nNo. 4) and pass through the filter, with suction,\n0.3\nLoss on heating. Preheat an open aluminum vessel (60 mm in \ndiameter and 10 mm high) at 150° for 30 miriptes, and allow to \ncool to room temperature. Tare the vessel, Sansfer to it about\n12500 11875 13125\n1.4015 \t\n1.4055\n2.0\n200 ml of toluene, added with stirring in small portions, \nrtions,\n30000 27000 33000\n0.969 \t\n0.977 \t\n1.4010 1.4100\n2.0\nfollowed by similar washing of the material on the filter with \n200 ml of toluene, and discard the filtrates. Place the filtering \ncrucible in a muffle furnace at room temperature, raise the \ntemperature of the furnace to 550°. Heat at 550° ± 25' for \n2 hours. Cool the filtering crucible with its contents in a \ndesiccator, weigh and calculate the content of silicon dioxide.\nI g of Dimethicone, weighed, heat at 150° in a circulating air \noven for 2 hours, and allow to come to room temperature in a \ndesiccator before weighing: it loses not more than the maximum \npercentage of its weight specified in the accompanying table.\nDimethicone intended for use in the manufacture of \nparenteral preparations without a further appropriate \nprocedure for the removal of bacterial endotoxins complies\nwith the following additional requirement.\nBacterial Endotoxin (2.2.3). Not more than 10 Endotoxin Units \nper ml of the Dimethicone.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Mix 1.0 g of Dimethicone with 20 ml of \nchloroform. Add 1.0 ml of a freshly prepared 0.002 per cent \nw/v solution of dithizone in chloroform, 0.5 ml of water, and \n0.5 ml ofa mixture of 1 ml ofammonia and 9 ml ofa 0.2 per cent \nsolution of hydroxylamine hydrochloride. Prepare reference \nsolution by adding 1.0 ml ofa freshly prepared 0.002 per cent \nw/v solution of dithizone in chloroform to 20 ml of chloroform; \nadd 0.5 ml of lead standard solution (JO ppm pb) and 0.5 ml \nof a mixture of 1 ml of ammonia and 9 ml of a 0.2 per cent \nsolution of hydroxylamine hydrochloride. Immediately shake \nboth solutions vigorously for 1 minute. Any red color in the \ntest solution is not more intense than that in the reference \nsolution (5 µg per g).\nSiO2, in the sample taken.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nLabeling. Label it to indicate its nominal viscosity value. \nDimethicone intended for use in coating containers that come \nin contact with articles for parenteral use is so labelled."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/regulatory-pathways-and-testing-aspects-for","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Regulatory Pathways and Testing Aspects for"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Regulatory Pathways and Testing Aspects for |\nAdjuvants \nI\nAdjuvants itself are not licensed but a specific adjuvant/ |\nantigen formulation is licensed. Therefore, an adjuvant is |\ndeveloped strictly according to the antigen present in the I\nspecific formulation.Vaccine regulatory procedures for j \nlicensing or lot release of adjuvants are not harmonized | \nglobally. Hence for harmonisation to some extent, it is |\nrecommended to follow various applicable International | \nguidelines such as WHO, ICH etc and national regulatory I \nrequirements. An international guideline ‘Guideline on j \nadjuvants in vaccines for human use’ clarify the difference | \nbetween immunomodulators and adjuvants. It has also been | \nstated that adjuvants should be a part of the formulation to be | \nadministered, whereas compounds that are given separately |\nand/or at a different time point are called immunomodulators, t \nAn adjuvant is intended to help the immune response by a | \nconfined, synchronized and concomitant action with the | \nantigen. Since vaccines are administered to healthy individuals | \nincludes infants and children and there may be safety related | \nissues with novel adjuvants, there is a need of substantial । \ntoxicological studies in animals for adjuvants and adjuvanted I \nvaccines, including local reactogenicity and repeated dose | \ntoxicity studies. Safety of any adjuvant must be adequately | \ndemonstrated by means of appropriately designed preclinical | \nand clinical safety studies. Various national and international I \nguidelines shall be referred for non-ciinical evaluation of |\nvaccine adjuvants and adjuvanted vaccines. \n|\nI\n■■■I \nI\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2.022\n2.7.16. ADJUVANTS FOR VACCINES\nperformance characteristics/Quality specifications of\nadjuvants\nAdjuvants are included in the formulation of a vaccine to \npotentiate and/or modulate the immune response to the \nantigen(s). Suitable characterisation and quality control of \nthe adjuvant(s), alone and in combination with the antigen(s), \njs essential for consistent production. Existing tests methods \nniaynot be sufficient to ensure the consistency in adjuvant \nmanufacturing. Eg. Aluminium or other adjuvant systems. \nVaccine manufacturer should identify/integrate the additional \ntests to ensure consistency.\nQuality specifications are established for each adjuvant, alone \nand in combination with the antigen(s).\nAdsorbents as adjuvants. Vaccines may be adsorbed on \naluminium hydroxide, aluminium phosphate, calcium \nphosphate or other suitable adsorbents. The adsorbents are \nprepared in special conditions that confer the appropriate \nphysical form and adsorptive properties.\nIn situadjuvants. Where an adsorbent is used as an adjuvant \nand is generated in situ during production of the vaccine, \nquality specifications should be established for each of \ningredients used in the production of the adsorbent and for \nthe generated adsorbent in the vaccine.\nQuality specifications are intended to control, in particular:\n— qualitative and quantitative chemical composition;\n— physical form and associated adsorptive properties, where\nrelevant, and particularly where the adjuvant will be \npresent as an adsorbent;\n— interaction between adjuvant and antigen;\n— purity, including bacterial endotoxin content and\nmicrobiological quality;\n— any other parameters identified as being critical for\nfunctionality such as particle size and distribution to be \nis established during development studies.\n— stability of each adjuvant, alone and in combination with\nthe antigen(s), particularly for critical parameters is \nestablished during development studies.\nIf the vaccine contains an adjuvant, the amount is determined \nand shown to be within acceptable limits with respect to the \nexpected amount unless otherwise stated.\nTable - Adjuvants Approved For Vaccines for Human Use\nAdjuvant\nName\nClass"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Regulatory Pathways and Testing Aspects for |\nAdjuvants \nI\nAdjuvants itself are not licensed but a specific adjuvant/ |\nantigen formulation is licensed. Therefore, an adjuvant is |\ndeveloped strictly according to the antigen present in the I\nspecific formulation.Vaccine regulatory procedures for j \nlicensing or lot release of adjuvants are not harmonized | \nglobally. Hence for harmonisation to some extent, it is |\nrecommended to follow various applicable International | \nguidelines such as WHO, ICH etc and national regulatory I \nrequirements. An international guideline ‘Guideline on j \nadjuvants in vaccines for human use’ clarify the difference | \nbetween immunomodulators and adjuvants. It has also been | \nstated that adjuvants should be a part of the formulation to be | \nadministered, whereas compounds that are given separately |\nand/or at a different time point are called immunomodulators, t \nAn adjuvant is intended to help the immune response by a | \nconfined, synchronized and concomitant action with the | \nantigen. Since vaccines are administered to healthy individuals | \nincludes infants and children and there may be safety related | \nissues with novel adjuvants, there is a need of substantial । \ntoxicological studies in animals for adjuvants and adjuvanted I \nvaccines, including local reactogenicity and repeated dose | \ntoxicity studies. Safety of any adjuvant must be adequately | \ndemonstrated by means of appropriately designed preclinical | \nand clinical safety studies. Various national and international I \nguidelines shall be referred for non-ciinical evaluation of |\nvaccine adjuvants and adjuvanted vaccines. \n|\nI\n■■■I \nI\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2.022\n2.7.16. ADJUVANTS FOR VACCINES\nperformance characteristics/Quality specifications of\nadjuvants\nAdjuvants are included in the formulation of a vaccine to \npotentiate and/or modulate the immune response to the \nantigen(s). Suitable characterisation and quality control of \nthe adjuvant(s), alone and in combination with the antigen(s), \njs essential for consistent production. Existing tests methods \nniaynot be sufficient to ensure the consistency in adjuvant \nmanufacturing. Eg. Aluminium or other adjuvant systems. \nVaccine manufacturer should identify/integrate the additional \ntests to ensure consistency.\nQuality specifications are established for each adjuvant, alone \nand in combination with the antigen(s).\nAdsorbents as adjuvants. Vaccines may be adsorbed on \naluminium hydroxide, aluminium phosphate, calcium \nphosphate or other suitable adsorbents. The adsorbents are \nprepared in special conditions that confer the appropriate \nphysical form and adsorptive properties.\nIn situadjuvants. Where an adsorbent is used as an adjuvant \nand is generated in situ during production of the vaccine, \nquality specifications should be established for each of \ningredients used in the production of the adsorbent and for \nthe generated adsorbent in the vaccine.\nQuality specifications are intended to control, in particular:\n— qualitative and quantitative chemical composition;\n— physical form and associated adsorptive properties, where\nrelevant, and particularly where the adjuvant will be \npresent as an adsorbent;\n— interaction between adjuvant and antigen;\n— purity, including bacterial endotoxin content and\nmicrobiological quality;\n— any other parameters identified as being critical for\nfunctionality such as particle size and distribution to be \nis established during development studies.\n— stability of each adjuvant, alone and in combination with\nthe antigen(s), particularly for critical parameters is \nestablished during development studies.\nIf the vaccine contains an adjuvant, the amount is determined \nand shown to be within acceptable limits with respect to the \nexpected amount unless otherwise stated.\nTable - Adjuvants Approved For Vaccines for Human Use\nAdjuvant\nName\nClass"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/remdesivir-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Remdesivir Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Reference solution. Dissolve 10 mg of rerndesivir IPRS. in 5 ml \nof acetonitrile and dilute to lOOiOanl with the solvent mixture. \nDilute 1.0 ml ofthe solution to 10.0 ml with thesolvent mixture.\nChromatographic system \n. ;\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsiiane with extra selectivity of a p enta fluoro \nphenyl bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such as ACE \nPFPC18), \n■■■■.■\n- column temperature: 40°, \n:\n- sample temperature: 5°, \nW\n- mobile phase: A. a buffer solution prepared by\ndissolving 1.36 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate \nin 1000 ml of water, \n‘ \n■\nB. a mixture of 90 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 10 volumes of water,\n- a gradient programme Using the conditions given below;\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, \n/ ; > :\n- spectrophotometer set at 245 nm, \n■ ’\n- injection volume: 5 pl. . \n,\nTime Mobile phase A Mobile phase B ' -\n. (inmin,). . (percent v/v).\n(per cent v/v).\n■■ 15 •\n60 \n•\n35 ■\ny. 44 .\n.80 \n. . /\n\"49\n15 \n/\nName\nRelative\nCorrection\nretention time \nfactor ,\nRerndesivir impurity A1\n0.17\n. . . 0.45 ■■ . .\nRerndesivir impurity B2*\n0.57\nRerndesivir (Retention time: \n:\nabout 21 minutes)\n1.0’\nRerndesivir impurity C3‘\n1.32\n—\nRerndesivir impurity D4’\n.1.8\n’Process impurity include for identification-only and-not included in the \ncalculation, of total'degradation products, .\n1(2RJ3R,4S,5R)?2-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,l-f|(.l,2,4]triazin-7-yl)-3,4- \ndihydroxy-5-(hydroxymethyl)tetrahydrofuran-2-carbonitrile (Fragment \nA), '\n2(3aR,4R,6R,6a7?)-4-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,l-f][l,2,4]triazin-7-yl)-6- \n(hydroxymethyl)-2,2-dimethyltetrahydrofufah[3,4-d][l,3]dioxole-4- \ncarbonitrile (Acetonide), \n; .■? \n' j '\n32-ethyIbutyl((S)'(((3aJ?)4I?,6J?,6aR:)-6-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,l-f] \n[1,2,4]triazinr7-yl)-6'Cyano-2,2-diinethyItetrahydrofuro[3,4-d] \n[l,3]dioxol'4-yl)methoxy)(phenbxy)phosphoryl)amino)-L-ataninate \n(Condensed),\nW-[(S)-(4-nitrophenoxy) piienoxyphdsphinyl]-l-AIame 2- ethylbutyl-\nester (Fragment'B).' J \n-;-\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3).Not more than 0.6 Endotoxin Unit \nper mg of remdesivir. \n;\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 40 volumes of acetonitrile and 60 volumes \nof water.\nTest solution. Reconstitute 5 vials with 20 ml each, with the \nsolvent mixture and pool the contents in 200-ml volumetric \nflask, dilute to volume with the solvent mixture. Dilute 2.0 ml \nof the solution to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution.A0.01 percent w/v solution of remdesivir \nIPRS ih.the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm. x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane with extra selectivity of a penta fluoro \nphenyl bonded to porous silica (5, pm) (Such as ACE \nPFPC18), ; \n: : \n?\n- column temperature; 40°,\n- - sample temperature:. 10°, ; > - >\n- mobile phase: a mixture’ of 50 volumes of a buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 1 ;36 g o fflbtassium dihydrogen \n\" ' phosphate in 1000 ml of water and 50 volumes of\n, , acetonitrilo, ..\n, - flow rate: 1 ml per minute, ,,\n- spectrophotometer set at 245 nm,\n— injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, the; \ntailing factor is more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent. \n."},"sections":[{"name":"REMDESIVIR: INJECTION","text":"Reference solution. Dissolve 10 mg of rerndesivir IPRS. in 5 ml \nof acetonitrile and dilute to lOOiOanl with the solvent mixture. \nDilute 1.0 ml ofthe solution to 10.0 ml with thesolvent mixture.\nChromatographic system \n. ;\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsiiane with extra selectivity of a p enta fluoro \nphenyl bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such as ACE \nPFPC18), \n■■■■.■\n- column temperature: 40°, \n:\n- sample temperature: 5°, \nW\n- mobile phase: A. a buffer solution prepared by\ndissolving 1.36 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate \nin 1000 ml of water, \n‘ \n■\nB. a mixture of 90 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 10 volumes of water,\n- a gradient programme Using the conditions given below;\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, \n/ ; > :\n- spectrophotometer set at 245 nm, \n■ ’\n- injection volume: 5 pl. . \n,\nTime Mobile phase A Mobile phase B ' -\n. (inmin,). . (percent v/v).\n(per cent v/v).\n■■ 15 •\n60 \n•\n35 ■\ny. 44 .\n.80 \n. . /\n\"49\n15 \n/\nName\nRelative\nCorrection\nretention time \nfactor ,\nRerndesivir impurity A1\n0.17\n. . . 0.45 ■■ . .\nRerndesivir impurity B2*\n0.57\nRerndesivir (Retention time: \n:\nabout 21 minutes)\n1.0’\nRerndesivir impurity C3‘\n1.32\n—\nRerndesivir impurity D4’\n.1.8\n’Process impurity include for identification-only and-not included in the \ncalculation, of total'degradation products, .\n1(2RJ3R,4S,5R)?2-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,l-f|(.l,2,4]triazin-7-yl)-3,4- \ndihydroxy-5-(hydroxymethyl)tetrahydrofuran-2-carbonitrile (Fragment \nA), '\n2(3aR,4R,6R,6a7?)-4-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,l-f][l,2,4]triazin-7-yl)-6- \n(hydroxymethyl)-2,2-dimethyltetrahydrofufah[3,4-d][l,3]dioxole-4- \ncarbonitrile (Acetonide), \n; .■? \n' j '\n32-ethyIbutyl((S)'(((3aJ?)4I?,6J?,6aR:)-6-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,l-f] \n[1,2,4]triazinr7-yl)-6'Cyano-2,2-diinethyItetrahydrofuro[3,4-d] \n[l,3]dioxol'4-yl)methoxy)(phenbxy)phosphoryl)amino)-L-ataninate \n(Condensed),\nW-[(S)-(4-nitrophenoxy) piienoxyphdsphinyl]-l-AIame 2- ethylbutyl-\nester (Fragment'B).' J \n-;-\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3).Not more than 0.6 Endotoxin Unit \nper mg of remdesivir. \n;\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 40 volumes of acetonitrile and 60 volumes \nof water.\nTest solution. Reconstitute 5 vials with 20 ml each, with the \nsolvent mixture and pool the contents in 200-ml volumetric \nflask, dilute to volume with the solvent mixture. Dilute 2.0 ml \nof the solution to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution.A0.01 percent w/v solution of remdesivir \nIPRS ih.the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm. x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane with extra selectivity of a penta fluoro \nphenyl bonded to porous silica (5, pm) (Such as ACE \nPFPC18), ; \n: : \n?\n- column temperature; 40°,\n- - sample temperature:. 10°, ; > - >\n- mobile phase: a mixture’ of 50 volumes of a buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 1 ;36 g o fflbtassium dihydrogen \n\" ' phosphate in 1000 ml of water and 50 volumes of\n, , acetonitrilo, ..\n, - flow rate: 1 ml per minute, ,,\n- spectrophotometer set at 245 nm,\n— injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, the; \ntailing factor is more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent. \n."},{"name":"REMDESIVIR INJECTION","text":"IP 2022.\nInject the refcrence solution. The test is not valid unless the Inject the reference solution and the test;solution.\ncolumn \nthe Calculate the content of C27HJ5N6O J5. \n.. \n..\ntailingfactor is more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation \n2 \n-• s ’ ■■ ■ ■' ; \n.. y\nfor replicate injections is not more than 10.0 per cent..,.; Storage. Store protected from light and moisture, at a\n>!s ‘■' \ntemperature not exceeding 30°. \n... J’.;:. \n■,\nInject the reference solution and: the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtainedwiththe test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to remdesivir impurity A is not more than \ntwice the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (2.0 per cent), the area \nany other secondary peak is not more, than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution (1.0 per cent) and, the sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks other than remdesivir impurity A is not more \nthan twice the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (2.0 percent). Ignore any \npeak with an area less than O .()5 tmies the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.05 per cent). - \n;"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/remdesivir","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Remdesivir"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"REMDESIVIR\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours.\n^iiy; Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution..Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexaniination in the mobile phase and dilute to 50.0 ml with the\nmobile phase. ,7. \n.,\nReference solution. A 0.Q5 per cent w/v solution of reboxetine, \nmethanesulphonate IPRS. in the mobile phase."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"REMDESIVIR\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours.\n^iiy; Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution..Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexaniination in the mobile phase and dilute to 50.0 ml with the\nmobile phase. ,7. \n.,\nReference solution. A 0.Q5 per cent w/v solution of reboxetine, \nmethanesulphonate IPRS. in the mobile phase."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Chromatographic system . ; \n,.f.L\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\n' l; / 'octylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n-mobile phase: a mixture of 32 volumes of acetonitrile\n’ arid 68 volumes of; buffer-solution prepared by\ndissolving 2.0 g of disodium hydrogen orthophosphate\n,. and 4.0 g of sodium dihydrogen orthophosphate, in\n.. 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 2.7 with \n2 orthophosphoric acid.\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute,"},{"name":"Tests","text":"' ■' - ■ spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,'\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless, the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the'relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject.thereference solution.and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C2ciH27NO6S.\nRemdesivir\n2-ylmethoxy]phenoxy-(1S)-phosphorylamino)propionic acid 2- \nethyl-butyl ester.\nRemdesivir contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C27H35N6O8P, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis. \n. ; o ■/-<■ :\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription. A white to off white to yellow powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with remdesivir IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of remdesivir. \n':\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nAppearance of solution.. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution in\nmethanol is clear (2.4.1) and colourless (2.4.1),\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). -23.0° to -17.0°, determined \nin a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in methanol.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 40 volumes of acetonitrile and 60 volumes \nof water.\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A 0.005 per cent w/v solution of \nremdesivir,IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 12.5 mg of remdesivir\nimpurity DIPRS (fragment-B) in 5 ml acetonitrile with the aid \nof ultrasound and dilute to 25.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 10.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nand 1.0 ml of reference solution (b) to 100.0 ml with the solvent \nmixture. •• \nM‘:’ \n''\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane with the extra selectivity of a penta fluoro\n• phenyl phase bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such as \nACEPFPC18),\n• \n• column temperature: 40°,\n- sample temperature: 10°,\n- mobile phase: A. a buffer solution prepared by\ndissolving 1.36 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate\nin 1000 ml of water,\nJ' ! \nB. a mixture of 90 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 10 volumes of water,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer sef.at245.nm and.27,5, nm,\ninjection volume: 5 pl. \n......r.\nMobile phase: B\nMobile phase A\nTime\n(in min.) \n(per cent v/v) \n(p<ir cent v/v.) . ;\n■ .. ...\nA 15\n25\n40\nr 60 .... . >; {\n35 '\n40\n60\n48’ ,\n. : ;80 \n■\n*>• Brnrn. \n. , . .15 .: \n,■\n.E: : ... 55 ffi: ci\n•85 \n-mse: 15 EmE\nName\nRelative\nCorrection\nretention time\nfactor c '\nRerndesivir impurity A1 ;\n0.17\n• 0.48 E\nRerndesivir impurity B2\n0:57\n0.53\nRerndesivir (Retention til \nabout 21 minutes) '\nme:\n1.0\n' '■ \n1' 1 27 : \n■•'/ ’< -T7YV\nI * JZ\nRerndesivir impurity D4\nL74\ni(2/fJ37?,4S,5A)-2-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,I-f][i,2,4]triaziii-7-yl)-3,4'\ndihydroxy-5-(hydroxymethyt)tetrahydrofuran-2-carbonitrile (Fragment \nA),\n2(3 aR>, 4R, &R,6a A)-4'-( 4-ami n opyfrolo[ 2,4 -f] [ 1,2; 4 ] tri azin -7*y I )-6 - - \n(hydrpxymethyl)72f2-dimethyltetrahydrofuran[3>4rd].[il,3],dipxel.e’4-- \ncarbonitrile (Acetonide), \n.\n12-ethyIbutyl((S)-(((3aR,4R,6R.6aR)-6-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,1-f] \n[1,2,4]t’ria:ziii-7-yl)-'6-cyaiio-2,2-dimethyItetrahydrofurd[3,4-d]1 \n[ 1,3]dioxol-4-yl)methoxy)(phen6xy)ph6!sphoryl)amiho)-Z;-aianinate - \n(Condensed), \n, . „ .\n4M-f(S)-(4Ynitrophenpxy) phenoxyphosphinyiJ-I'Ala.ine- 2- ethylbutyl \nester (Fragment 13). \n; .. . \n.\nInject reference solution (c}.; The. test is, not yalid.unless, they \ncolumn efficiency is not less than.2000 theoretical plates, the\nInjectreference solution (c) and the test solution at 2.45 nm. In \nthe chromatogram obtained with, the test solution; ,the. area of \nany peak corresponding to rerndesivir impurity A, B and C, \neach of, is not more than the area ofthe peak duo :t°rerndesivir \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) \n(0.5 per pent), and the ;area of anyother secondary peak is not \nmore than. 0.6 times the area of.the peak due to rerndesivir in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference: solution (c) \n(0.3 per cent). Ignore any peak corresponding to rerndesivir \nimpurity D. \n...... \n.... \n;V;,\n3468;\nREMDES1VIR\nIP 202;\ncE \\ a gradient programme using the conditions givenbelo w,. Inj ect reference solution (c) and the test solution at 275 nm. ty\nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of \nany peak corresponding to rerndesivir impurity D is not more< \nthan the area of the peak due to rerndesivir impurity D in the \ncHromatogf am'obtained with reference solution (c) (0.5 \ncent). Ignore all the peaks other than rerndesivir impurity f>.\nimpurity D is not more than 1.0 per cent. '\nThe sum of all secondary peaks at 245 nm and rerndesivir / |\ns Heavy metals (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test for Si\n,, heavy metals,MethodB(10ppm). I “if-• .Qjl\nWater (2.3.43)/ Not more than T.0 per cent, determined on i\n■■ \ni\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3),Not more than 0.5 Endotoxin Unit &\n- Assay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2,4.14). .\nper mg of rerndesivir. \n■■■■.■\nSolvent mixture. 46 volumes of acetonitrile and 60 volumes ■ : \nol water. \n\" \n'\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of. the. sub stance, r under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 50.0 ml with v\nthe solvent mixture. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to 10-0ml with \nthe solvent mixture;'' ” • •• • ' \n•• \n; \"d:\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of rerndesivir :\nIPRS in the solvent mixture. ’ \nt....\nChromatographic system \n7\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsiiane with the extra .selectivity of a pcniafluoro |\nphenyl phase bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such as\nACEPFP"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with, the test solution corresponds, to thepeak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nB. When examined in the range from 200 nmto 400 nm. (2.4.7),. \na 0.0012 per cent w/v solution in 50 per cent v/v solution of \nmelhanoLshows an absorption maximum as obtained with \nrerndesivir IPRS of the same concentration."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution, A reconstituted solution is clear (2.4,1) \nand not more intensely coloured than reference solution BYS5 \n(2.4.1). .. ■ \n• \n■\nPH(2.4.24). 3.0 to 4.0, determined on constituted solution.,\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\nSolvent mixture; ,25 volumes of acetonitrile and 75 volumes \nof w/e/: • h ir j, ,\nNf)TE--Prepare the solutions, immediately before, use.. .\nTest solution. Reconstitute 5 vials with 20 ml each; with the \nsoivent mixture and pool the contents in 200-ml volumetric \nflask, dilute to volume with the Solvent mixture. Dilute a\nSuitableyo].umewith the solvent mixture toobtaina solution \ncontaining 0.1 per cent w/v of Rerndesivir. ... , ;"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reo-virus-vaccine-inactivated","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reo Virus Vaccine, Inactivated"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"REO VIRUS VACCINE, INACTIVATED\nmore than two of the vaccinated: chickens die or.show signs\nof disease.; The testis valid only. \nif all-the control birds die \nwithin 6 days of inoculation of the virulent challenge strain.\nIf the poténcy test has been performed with satisfactory \nresults on a representative batch of the vaccine from the seed\nlot, it may be omitted'asa routine control test during production\non other batches of thie \n' vaccine prepared from the same seed\nlot:\nStorage. \n/ When, stored. \na prescribed scallion \nthe \nvaccine may be expected to retain its potency for not. less,\nthan 18 months from the date the virus titre was determined. \nThe zeconstituied. vaccine \n© should be used immediately after \npreparation.\nLabelling. The label/insert states \ns (1) strain of virus used;\n(2) the dose of vaccine. \n:"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"REO VIRUS VACCINE, INACTIVATED\nmore than two of the vaccinated: chickens die or.show signs\nof disease.; The testis valid only. \nif all-the control birds die \nwithin 6 days of inoculation of the virulent challenge strain.\nIf the poténcy test has been performed with satisfactory \nresults on a representative batch of the vaccine from the seed\nlot, it may be omitted'asa routine control test during production\non other batches of thie \n' vaccine prepared from the same seed\nlot:\nStorage. \n/ When, stored. \na prescribed scallion \nthe \nvaccine may be expected to retain its potency for not. less,\nthan 18 months from the date the virus titre was determined. \nThe zeconstituied. vaccine \n© should be used immediately after \npreparation.\nLabelling. The label/insert states \ns (1) strain of virus used;\n(2) the dose of vaccine. \n:"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reo-virus-vaccine-live","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reo Virus Vaccine, Live"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Reo Virus Vaccine, Live\nReo Virus Vaccine, Live is a preparation ofa suitable strain(s)\nof Reo virus known to be safe and immunogenic. This\nmonograph applies to vaccines intended for administration to\nchickens for protection against Malabsorption Syndrome and\nlor proventriculitis and lor Tenosynovitis in birds."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Reo Virus Vaccine, Live\nReo Virus Vaccine, Live is a preparation ofa suitable strain(s)\nof Reo virus known to be safe and immunogenic. This\nmonograph applies to vaccines intended for administration to\nchickens for protection against Malabsorption Syndrome and\nlor proventriculitis and lor Tenosynovitis in birds."},{"name":"Production","text":"Substrate for virus propagation\nThe vaccine virus is grown in embryonated SPF hens' eggs or\nin cell cultures derived from SPF flocks (2.7.7) or suitable cell\nline.\nThe master seed lot complies with the tests for extraneous\nagents as described in the General monograph for Veterinary\nVaccines."},{"name":"Identification","text":"When mixed with monospecific Reo virus antiserum, the\nvaccine no longer induces cytopathic effect in susceptible\ncell culture derived from. SPF eggs (2.7.7) or carry out\nimmunostaining test in cell culture derived from SPF eggs\n(2.7.7) to identify the vaccine virus."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Water (2.3.43). Not more than 3.0 per cent.\nMycoplasmas (2.7.9). Complies withthe test for mycoplasmas.\nSafety. Final container samples of completed product from\neach serial shall be tested as follows:\nA. For vaccines intended for use in very young chickens,\neach oflO, one day old SPF chickens (2.7.7, table 3) orhealthy\ntenosynovitis/malabsorption/proventriculitis susceptible\nchickens shall be vaccinated with the equivalent of 10 doses\nby one method recommended on the label.\nE. For vaccines intended for use in older chickens, each of\nten, 4-week-old or older SPF chickens (2.7.7, table 3) orhealthy\ntenosynovitis susceptible chickens shall be vaccinated with\nthe eqllival~ntof 10 d()s~s l:>Y one methodrecoilllTIended on\nth~Tabcl~\n.\n----c\n_\n.. -_.._. __._--\nThe vaccinates shall be observed each day for 21 days. If\nunfavourable reactions occur which are attributable to the\nproduct, the serial is unsatisfactory. If unfavorable reactions\noccur in more than two vaccinates which are not attributable\nto the product, the test is inconclusive and may be repeated.\nIf the test is not repeated, the serial is unsatisfactory.\nVirus titre. Titrate the vaccine in cell cultures derived from\nSPF embryos or in SPF eggs (2.7.7). One dose ofthe vaccine\ncontains not lessthan 103 TCIDso / EID50 per dose.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nPotency. Reo susceptible healthy chickens of same age and\nfrom the same source shall be used as test birds. Vaccine\nintended for use in very young chickens shall be administered\nto chickens of the youngest age for which vaccine is\nrecommended. Vaccines intended for use in older chickens\nshall be administered to 4 weeks or older birds. Ten SPF\nchickens (2.7.7, table 3) or healthy susceptible chickens\nvaccinates shall be used for each method of administration.\nOne dose will be injected to vaccinates. Ten chicks shall be'\nheld as unvaccinated controls.\nPotency test ofeach age group shall be conducted separately.\nTwenty one days post vaccination each vaccinate and control\nshall be challenged by injecting virulent virus into one foot\npad. The vaccinates & controls shall be observed for 14 days\npost challenge. If at least 90 per cent of the controls do not\ndevelop swelling and discolouration in the phalangeal joint\narea ofinjected foot pad typical ofinfection ofReo virus, the\ntest is inconclusive and may be repeated. Ifat least 18 out of\n20 vaccinates do not remain free ofthese signs, disregarding\ntransient swelling which subsides within 5 days post\nchallenge, the serial is unsatisfactory.\nThe serial is satisfactory when it gives 90 per cent protection\nto vaccinated group and 90 per cent controls develop positive .\nReo virus lesions on challenge.\nIf the potency test has been performed with satisfactory\nresults on a representative batch ofthe vaccine from the seed\nlot, it may be omitted as a routine control test during production\non other batches ofthe vaccine prepared from the same seed\nlot.\nStorage. When stored under the prescribed conditions, the\nvaccine may be expected to retain its potency for not less\nthan 18 months from the date the virus titre was determined.\nThe recopstituted vaccine should be used immediately after\npreparation.\nLabelling. The labeVinsert states (1) strain ofvirus used; (2)\nthe dose of vaccine."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/repaglinide-and-meteormin-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Repaglinide and Meteormin Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"REPAGLINIDE AND METEORMIN TABLETS\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution.\nCalculate the Content of C27H36N2O4 in the tablets.\ng^^geStoreprotected fromlight and m()isture.\nRejiaglini’de and Metformin Tablets\nRgpagihiide, and Metformin Hydrochloride Tablets ,\ngepaglinideand Metformin Hydrochloride Tablets contain \nnot jess than 90.0 per cent and,not more than 110.0.per cent of\nof,repaglinide, C?7H36N2O4 and metformin \nhydrochloride,C4lInN5,HCl.\nXJsiihf strengths. Repaglinide, 1 mg and Metformin\nHydrochloride 500 mg; Repaglinide, 2 mg and Metformin \nHydrochloride 500 mg. \n:'"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"REPAGLINIDE AND METEORMIN TABLETS\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution.\nCalculate the Content of C27H36N2O4 in the tablets.\ng^^geStoreprotected fromlight and m()isture.\nRejiaglini’de and Metformin Tablets\nRgpagihiide, and Metformin Hydrochloride Tablets ,\ngepaglinideand Metformin Hydrochloride Tablets contain \nnot jess than 90.0 per cent and,not more than 110.0.per cent of\nof,repaglinide, C?7H36N2O4 and metformin \nhydrochloride,C4lInN5,HCl.\nXJsiihf strengths. Repaglinide, 1 mg and Metformin\nHydrochloride 500 mg; Repaglinide, 2 mg and Metformin \nHydrochloride 500 mg. \n:'"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nTests.,;. ;\nDissolution(2.5.2):\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),.\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.1Mhydrochloric acid,\nSpeed and time. 75 rpm and 30 minutes.\nDetermineby liquid, chromatography (2.4.14), as described \nunder Assay with the following modifications.,\n~ injection volume: 50 pl.\nTest solution, fa). .Withdraw a suitable volume of the medium \nand filter. Dilute further, if necessary, with the dissolution \nmedium&r repaglinide.\nTest solution (b). Dilute’5.0 ml of the.filtrate to 100.0 ml with \nthe dissolution medium for metformin hydrochloride.\nReference solution (a). Weigh accurately about 22 mg of \nrepaglinide IPRS and transfer to a 200-ml volumetric flask. \nAdd about 20.0 ml of methanol and sonicate to dissolve and \nmake upthe volume with the dissolution medium. Dilute 5.0 ml \nofthesolution to 100.0 ml with dissolution medium.\nReference solution (b).. Weigh accurately about 28 mg of \n^fformin hydrochloride IPRS and transfer to a 200-ml \nvolumetric flask. Add about 150.0 ml of dissolution medium \nand sonicate to dissolve and make up the volume with the \ndissolution medium. :\nReference solution (c). Dilute reference solution (a) and (b) \nwith dissolution medium to obtain a solution having a.known \nc°ncentration similar to the test solution (a) and (b).\n(24.14).\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency for both repaglinide and metformin \nhydrochloride peaks, is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, \nthe tailing factor is riot rndre than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (c), testsolution;(a) and (b).\nCalculate the content of repaglinide, C27H36N2O4.and metformin \nhydrochloride, C4HnN5,HCl in the medium.\nQ. Not less than 75 percentofthe stated amount of C27H36N2O4 \nandC4HnN5,HCl.\nRelated substances.\nFor Metformin Hydrochloride — Determine by liquid \nchromatography(2.4.14). \n'\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity \nof powder containing 250 mg of Metformin Hydrochloride to \n250-ml. volumetric flask. Add .about. 15,0.0 ml of water and \nsonicate for 30 minutes. Allow the solution cool to room \ntemperature and dilute to volume with water and mix.\nReference solution. 0.0005 per cent solution of metformin\nhydrochloride IPRS in water.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column, 25 cm x 4.6 him packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (Such as Inertsil,\n. ODS)(5pm), ...\n- mobile phase: 9Q volumes of a buffer solution prepared\nby dissolving 0.5 g each of heptane sulphonic acid \nand sodium chloride in 1000 ml of water, adjusted to \npH 3.85 with brthophosphoric acid and 10 volumes of \nacetonitrile, \n' '\n- flow rate: I ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 218 nm,'\n~ injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for six replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per \ncent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nother secondary peak is not more than 0.2 times the area ofthe \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution (0.1. per cent) , and the sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary-peaks: is not more than 1.2 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution (0.6 per cent).\nFor Repaglinide Determine by liquid chromatography\nREPAGL1NIDEAND METFORMIN TABLETS\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 30 volumes of water and 70 \nvolumesof we/Mnof \n.•■.{.<; p/f\nTest solution. Weigh arid powder 20 tablets Weigh a quantity\nof powder containing 10 mg of Repaglinide to a 200-ml \nvolumetric flask arid add about 10.0 ml of water and sonicate \nto disperse. Further add I50.0.ml of solvent mixture and sonicate\nfor 3 Q minutes. Allow the solution cool to room temperature \narid rhake up the volume with solvent mixture and filter.\nReference solution. 0.000025 per cent solution of repaglinide \n/PM insol ventmixturc. \n' \" ''<JL ” v\n. /■ i •> ■ ; j : ■\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\n■:base deactivated, end-capped octadecylsilyl silica gel\n(3 pm) (Such as Hypersil BDS),. •. ■ ■.\n- column temperature: 40°,\n- mobile phase: 50 volumes bf a buffer solution prepared\nby dissolving;2:0 g ammonium■ dihydrogen ortho-\nJ 1 phosphate, adjusted to pH 2.5 with orthophosphoric\n; 'acid arid 50 voluiries 6fhce/orii7rz7e, \n-\n- How rate: 1 ml per minute,\n■ spectrophotometer set at 210 nm, .......\n- injection volume: 20 pl. , \n- .\nNOTE—Disregard the peak due to blank and placebo up to\nrelative ,retention time O.2.,\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor: is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nother secondary peak is not more than, the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.5 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than four times the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution (2.0 \npercent). \n■ \n-■« <■<■■■ yc- N\nUniformity of content. Complies with the tests stated under \nTablets.\nFar Repaglinide — Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14) as; described under Assay with the following \nmodifications. \n.\nTest solution. Transfer one intact tablet in al 00-ml volumetric \nflask, add sufficent mobile phase and sonicate"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/repaglinide-and-voglibose-tab-lets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Repaglinide and Voglibose Tab lets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"REPAGLINIDE AND VOGLIBOSE TABLETS\nRepag^n^e and Voglibose Tablets\nRepagiH-- aQd Voglibose Tablets contain not less than 90.0 \nner cent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amounts \nofrepaglmide. \nand voglibose, C1QH21NO7.\nUsual strengths.'Repaglinide, 0.5 mg and Voglibose 0.2 mg; \nRepaglinide, 0.5 mg and Voglibose 0.3 mg; Repaglinide, 1.0 \njpg and Voglibose, 0.2 mg; Repaglinide, 1.0 mg and Voglibose,\n03 mg - ,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"REPAGLINIDE AND VOGLIBOSE TABLETS\nRepag^n^e and Voglibose Tablets\nRepagiH-- aQd Voglibose Tablets contain not less than 90.0 \nner cent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amounts \nofrepaglmide. \nand voglibose, C1QH21NO7.\nUsual strengths.'Repaglinide, 0.5 mg and Voglibose 0.2 mg; \nRepaglinide, 0.5 mg and Voglibose 0.3 mg; Repaglinide, 1.0 \njpg and Voglibose, 0.2 mg; Repaglinide, 1.0 mg and Voglibose,\n03 mg - ,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay of repaglinide, the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthe peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution and m the Assay of voglibose, the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds \nto the peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsbiutibhQ"},{"name":"Tests","text":"; \n/\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nFor Repaglinide —\nApparatusNo. 2 (Paddle), \n. < s •\nMedium. 900 ml of a buffer solution prepared by dissolving\n10.3 g of\"citric, .acid monqhydrate arid 18.1 g of disodium \nhydrogen phosphate dihydrate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted \ntopH5.0‘\nSpeed and time. 75 rpm and 3'0 minutes,\nWithdraw a suitable volume ofthe medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), r\nTest solution. Use the filtrate, dilute if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium. \n,\nReference solution. A 0.0011 per cent w/v solution of \nrepaglinide JPRS in methanol. To 5.0 ml of the solution, add \n6 ml methanol and dilute to 100.0 ml with the dissolution \nmedium..\nChromatographic system\n.“ Astainless steel column 5 cm x 4.6 mm, packed, with\noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column temperature: 40°,\n- mobile phaseramixture of 30 volumes of a buffer solution\n/ ;; , ,\n■ sp prepared by dissolving 2 g. of ammonium dihydrogen\nphosphatem950 ml of water; adjusted to pH 2.5 with\n;r; : orthophosphoric acid,di\\ote to 1000 ml with water and\n\"’ flow rate: I ml per minute, \n= >\n- spectrophotometer set at 245 nm,\n- injection volume: 50 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C27H36N2O4 in the medium.\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nQ7H36N2O4. \n.\nUniformity of content. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), '\nFor Repaglinide — \n•;\nSolvent mixture:30 volumes of a buffer solution prepared by \ndissolving 2 gof ammonium, dihydrogen phosphate, in 95Q ml \nof water, adjusted to pH 4.0 with orthophosphoric acid, dilute \nto 1000 ml with water and 70 volumes of methanol.\nTest solution. Disperse 1 intact tablet in the solvent mixture, \nwiththe aid of ultrasound with intermittent shaking and dilute \nwith the solvent mixture,to obtain a solution containing \n0.005 per cent w/v of Repaglinide and filter.\nReference solution. A 0:05 per cent w/v solution of repaglinide \nIPRS inTKel/janoZ. Dilute 5.0 ml ofthe solution to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture.. .... \n.. \n. ■\nUse the chromatographic system as described under \ndissolution with the following modification.\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test ;is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CsyH^NjfV in the tablet.\nFor Voglibose\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Disperse 1 intact tablet in the mobile phase, \nwith the aid of ultrasound with intermittent shaking and dilute \nto 25.0 ml with the mobile phase, filter.\nReference solution. Dissolve an accurately weighed quantity\nof voglibose IPRS in the mobile phase to obtain a solution of \nthe same concentration as that of the test solution.\nChromatographic system\n..a stainless^steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm,,packed with\naminopropylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such \nas Hypersil APS-2),\n- column temperature: 20°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 30 volumes of a buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 0.6 g of potassium dihydrogen \nphosphate and 0.28 g of dipotassium hydrogen\nREPAGLINIDE AND VOGLIBOSE TABLETS\n- \nphosphate in 1000 ml of water and 70 volumes of \nacetonitrile, ■ b::;; O.C ■ \n•.••mm rex \"oyf;\n. - flow rate: 0.8 ml per minute, jo;m ■ v i: \nr > i •:r 1 •\n~ spectrophotometer set at 205 nm, . . \n, . . , :\n- injection volume: 100 pl. \n........\n(NOTE — Equilibrate the'column with1 the mobile phase for \nat least 4 hours.) /neo-p-m -ur-\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is hot?more than 2.0 and the;relative: standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 3.0 percent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. ■ v\nCalculate the content.of Ct(iH2iNO7 in the tablet... 1\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets. ;\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4..14).;.\nFor Repaglinide —\nSolvent mixture. 30 volumes of si buffer solution prepared by \ndissolving 2 g of ammonium dihydrogenphosphale in 950 ml \nof water, adjusted to pH 4.0 with orthbphosphoric acid, dilute \nto. 1000 ml with water and 70 volumes of methanol. „..\nTest solution; Disperse a sufficient quantity of intact tablets \ncontaining 8 mg of Repaglinide in the solvent mixture, with \nthe aid pf ultrasound with intermittent shaking and dilute with \nthe solvent mixture to pbtairfa solution containing 0.008per \ncent w/v of Repaglinide.\nReference solution. A0.08 per cent w/v solution of repaglinide \nIPRS hi methanol. Diluted .0 ml o f th e solution to 50.0 nilwith \nthe solvent mixture. \n;./■■■■ -\nUse the chromatographic systeih as described under \ndissolution with the following modification. : i\n-■ injection volume: 20 pl. 'c-.. ••• \n7. :\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid Unless the \ntailing factor is not more than,2.0 and. the relative .standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the. test solution.\nCalculate the content of C27H36N2O4 iri the tablets: ■ : \n'\nFor Voglibose - \n- \n•• --'A;.1.\nTest solution. Disperse a sufficient quantity of intact tablets \ncontaining 2.4 nig of Voglibose in the mobile phase, with the \naid of ultrasound with intermittent shaking and dilute with the \nmobile phase to obtain a solution cbntainingO.OO 12 per cent \nw/vofVoglibosc. : jeO'- . V \newwew\nReference solution. A 0.0012. per cent w/v solution of \nvoglibose IPRS in the mobile phasc. ' \n1 \n'\nUse the chromatographic system as described;under \nUniformity of content. \n. \n; \n.\n■3476\n^20)2\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless^ \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relativ"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/repaglinide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Repaglinide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"REPAGLINIDE TABLETS\nRepaglinide Tablets\nRepaglinide Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the:stated; amount of \nrepaglinide,C27H36N9O4. \n: \nr\nUsual strengths;0.5 mg; 1 mg; 2mg. un' ;"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"REPAGLINIDE TABLETS\nRepaglinide Tablets\nRepaglinide Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the:stated; amount of \nrepaglinide,C27H36N9O4. \n: \nr\nUsual strengths;0.5 mg; 1 mg; 2mg. un' ;"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (b).\nTests.\nDissolution (2.5.2). \nc\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium., 900 ml of buffer solution pH 5.0, prepared by \ndissolving 10.2 g of citric acid mono hydrate and 18.2 g of \ndibasic sodium phosphate dihydrate With 1000 ml of water.\nSpeed and time. 75 rpm and 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2:4.14).\nTest solution. Use the filtrate and dilute further with dissolution \nmedium, if necessary.\nReference solution. A O.OOl.per cent w/y sqlutiqn of \nrepaglinide IPRSm. methanol. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to\n100.0 ml with the dissolution medium. ' ' ' '\nChromatographic system r...\n- a stainless steel column 12.5 cm x 4 mm, packed with\n: 1 octadccylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), ‘ ;: \\:\n• column temperature: 40°,\n■ mobile phase: a mixture of 49 volumes of acetonitrile,\n40 Volumes of0.15per cent w/vof monobasicpotassium \nphosphate solution, adjusted to pH 2.3 with drtho- \nphosphoric acid and11 volumes of methanol,f ; i ' \n'' -' flow rate: 1 ml per minute, ■! ;:-1 ■ -; ;: '\n- fluorometric detector: excitation wavelength of244 nm\nand emission wavelength of348 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is riot valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is; not more \nthan 2.0 per cent and the tailing factor is not; more than 2.0.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. ; ..\nCalculate the content of ^HjeN^O^in the medium. \n'\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nC27H36N?.O4. \n' \n\" 1 \n’\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid, chromatography \n(2.4.14) as described under Assay with the foiloWing \nmodifications. \n* b-Gvoo/q wed\n2022i|\nReference solution (d). Dilute 2.5 ml of reference solution (b) / \nto 1000 ml with the solvent mixture. .. . v; . \n, J\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm, ' m ; \n; \n)\nInject reference solution (c) and (d). The test is.not valid unless i \nthe resolution between the peaks, due to repaglinide and ' \nrepaglinide impurity A is not less than 7.0 and the tailing factor \nis not more than 2.0 in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (c) and the relative standard deviation for \nreplicate injections is not more than 10.0 per cent in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (d).\nInject the test solution. The sum of areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 0.5 per; cent,. ;calculated?by area \nnormalisation. \n... \nm\nOther tests. .Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 70 volumes of methanol and 30 volumes of \n0.2 per cent w/v of monobasic ammonium phosphate solution, \nadjusted to pH 4.0 with orthophosphoric acid.\nTest solution. Disperse 8 intact tablets in the solvent mixture \nand dilute to obtain a solution containing 0.008 per cent w/v \nof Repaglinide in the solvent mixture, stir for 20 minutes and \nfilter. \n; oy Colma-- yrerme wd\nReference solution (a): A 0.08 per cent w/v solution of \nrepaglinide IPRS in methanol. '\"ri'\nReference solution (b). Dilute 5.0 ml of reference solution (aj\nto 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture. ■ ' 1 y ; yv; ■;\nReference solution (c). A 0.008 per cent w/v solution of \nrepaglinide impurity A IPRS (4~(carbdxymethyl)-2-eih6ty- \nbenzoic acid IPRS) in methanol. To 1.0 nil of the solutibii, add \n5.0; ml of reference solution (a) and dilute to 50.0 ml with, the \nsolvent mixture. \n\\ ■ IJ-A\nChromatographic system . ■ - ;. \n, v \n/\n- a stainless steel column 6.0 cm x 4 mm, packed, with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), /. < /;.;i \n- column temperature: 40°, \nt.\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 70volumes of methanol and\n30 volumes of 0.2 per cent W/v solution of monobasic \nv ; >• ammonium:phosphate, adjusted to pH 2.5 with\northophosphoric acid, ■ ; ■ ■ ■. >.• :■ ; J iv: ”4*1 G\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n; ,.......\n- spectrophotometer set at 245 nm,\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (b) and (c). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due-to. repaglinide and \nrepaglinide impurity A is not less than 7.0, the tailing factor is \nnot more than .2.0 m the chromatogram obtained withf eference \nsolution (c) and the relative standard deviation for replicate \ninjections is not more than 2.0 in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (b). \nb -c\nn>2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/repaglinide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Repaglinide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Repaglinide\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 3.'0 per cent, determined on\n0.1g. ..... \nCjyHjftNbCh\nMol; Wt 452.6\nRepaglinide is (5)-2-Ethoxy-42[2-[3 -methyl- i-(2-(piperidin-\nl-yl)phenyl)buty)amino]-2-oxoethyI]benzoic acid.. \n7\nRepaglinide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more; \nthan 101.0 per cent of C27I k,(,N2Oi, calculated on the dried 7\nbasis.\nCategory.Antidiabetic. • , \n,.y \n. ■ <7-^ g.\nDescription. A white Or almost white powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11).\nJ / ■ .' < ■ 17 ■ \n{ .■ ' 7 : : - '■ ■ ’■ \n: d ; 1 : \nt?; ■ ’ i -j” ■’ J 771’1 ft 7"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Repaglinide\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 3.'0 per cent, determined on\n0.1g. ..... \nCjyHjftNbCh\nMol; Wt 452.6\nRepaglinide is (5)-2-Ethoxy-42[2-[3 -methyl- i-(2-(piperidin-\nl-yl)phenyl)buty)amino]-2-oxoethyI]benzoic acid.. \n7\nRepaglinide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more; \nthan 101.0 per cent of C27I k,(,N2Oi, calculated on the dried 7\nbasis.\nCategory.Antidiabetic. • , \n,.y \n. ■ <7-^ g.\nDescription. A white Or almost white powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11).\nJ / ■ .' < ■ 17 ■ \n{ .■ ' 7 : : - '■ ■ ’■ \n: d ; 1 : \nt?; ■ ’ i -j” ■’ J 771’1 ft 7"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum With that obtained with repaglinide \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of repaglinide.\nB. Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 6.3° to+7.7°, determined \nina5.0 percent w/vsolution in methanol.;"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Enantiomeric purity: Determine by liquid chromatography;\n(2.4,14).\nNOTE ■ - Prepare the solutions in amber flasks andvialsd-\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 !mg of the substance under \nexamination in methanol and dilute to 10.0 ml with methanoic\nReference solution (a). A 0.01 per cent w/v solutidri-iof1 \nrepaglinideflnpurityEIPRS. (2~ethoxy~4-[2?[[(I R)-3-methyl-\nI-[f-fpippridin-l-yl)pfpnyl]buty.l]aminpj -2-oxoethyl]> \nbenzoic acid l.PRS) \\n meihanol. \n: - ph .Heft'\nReferencesolution (bJ; Dilute 2.0 ml of reference solutioh(a)\\ \nto 100.0 ml with methanol."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reserpine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reserpine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RESERPINE INJECTION\nq To about 1 mg add 0.1 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \n$0diurnmolybdaiein sulphuric acid', a yellow colour is\nproduced which changes to blue within 2 minutes.\nP To 1 mg add 0.2 ml of a freshly prepared 1 per cent w/v \nsolution of vanillin in hydrochloric acid', a pink colour \ndevelops within 2 minutes.\nE Mix about 0.5 mg with 5 mg of 4<limethylaminobenzal- \ndehyde ezodH.2 m\\ ol glacial acetic ac^ an4 add 0.2 ml of \nsulphuric acid; ,a green colour is produced. Add 1 ml of \nglacial acetic .acid; the colour changes to red."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RESERPINE INJECTION\nq To about 1 mg add 0.1 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \n$0diurnmolybdaiein sulphuric acid', a yellow colour is\nproduced which changes to blue within 2 minutes.\nP To 1 mg add 0.2 ml of a freshly prepared 1 per cent w/v \nsolution of vanillin in hydrochloric acid', a pink colour \ndevelops within 2 minutes.\nE Mix about 0.5 mg with 5 mg of 4<limethylaminobenzal- \ndehyde ezodH.2 m\\ ol glacial acetic ac^ an4 add 0.2 ml of \nsulphuric acid; ,a green colour is produced. Add 1 ml of \nglacial acetic .acid; the colour changes to red."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). --128° to-116°, determined \nin a solution prepared immediately before use by dissolving \n0.25 gin sufficient chloroform to produce 25 ml.\nOxidation products. Absorbance of a 0.02 per cent w/v solution \nin glacial acetic acid at about 388 nm, measured immediately \nafter preparation, not more than 0.10 (2.4.7).\nSulphated ash (213.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss bn drying (2,4,19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 0.5 g by drying in an oven over phosphorus pentoxide at \n60° atapressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa for 3 hours.\nAssay. J^pr total, alkaloids — Weigh 0.5 g and dissolve in a \nmixture of 40 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic acid and 6 ml of \nacetic anhydride. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, \ndetermining the cnd-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry \nout a? blanktitration.\n1 ml of (1. I M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.06087 g of \ntotal alkaloids. '\nFor reserpirte, C3ilf9 — Carry out the following\nprocedure protected from light. Weigh 25.0 mg, moisten with \n2 nil of ethanol (95 per cen/J) ;add 2 ml of 0.25 M sulphuric \nacid and 10 ml of ethanol (9.5 per cent) and warm gently to \ndissolve. Cool, dilute to .100.0, ml with ethanol (95 per-cent)\ndilute 5.0 ml to 50.0 ml with the same solvent (solution A). \nTransfer 10.0 ml to a boiling tube, add 2 ml of 0.25 Msulphuric \n*?^apd2 ml of a freshly prepared 0.3 per cent w/v solution of \nsodiumnitrite,vci\\x and heat in a water^bath at 55° for 35 minutes.\nadd, 1 ml of a freshly prepared 5 per cent w/v solution of \n^Pbamlcacidcinddilute to 25.0 ml with ethanol (95 per\nMeasure the absorbance. of the resulting .solution at \nmaximuni at about 38,8 nm (2.4.7), using as.the blank a \n^Mbhprepared by. treating a further 10.0 ml of solution A in \nthe same manner and at the same time but omitting the sodium \nnitrite solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH(2.4.24).3.0to4.0.\na^afe the content of CMI l40N2O9 from the absorbance \n0 ^ined by repeating the operation using reserpine IPRS in \nP ace of’the substance under examination.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nReserpine Injection\nReserpine Injection is a sterile solution of Reserpine in Water \nfor Injections prepared with the aid of a suitable acid. It may \ncontain suitable antioxidants.\nReserpine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of reserpine, \nww' : \n■\nUsual strengths. 1 mg per ml; 2.5 mg per ml.\nExtract a suitable volume of the injection containing 16 mg of \nReserpine with 10 ml of chloroform and evaporate the \nchloroform layer to. dryness. The residue complies with the \nfollowing tests..\nTest A may be omitted if tests B, C, D and E are carried out. \nTests B, C, D and E may be omitted if test A is carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with reserpine IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of reserpine.\nB. Dilute 1 ml of a 0.2 per cent w/v solution in chloroform to\n100 ml with ethanol (95per cent). When examined immediately \nafter preparation, in the range 230 nm tb 360 nm (2.4.7), the \nresulting solution shows an absorption maximum at about \n268 nm; absorbance at about 268 nm, about 0.55. Over the \nrange 288 nm to 295 nm, the spectrum exhibits a slight minimum \nand then a shoulder or a slight maximum; absorbance oyer \nthis range, about 0.34.\nC. To about 1 mg add 0.1 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium molybdate in sulphuric acid; a yellow colour is \nproduced which changes to blue within 2 minutes.\nD. To 1 mg add 0.2 ml of a freshly prepared 1 per cent w/v \nsolution of vanillin in hydrochloric acid;-, a: pink- colour \ndevelops within 2 minutes.\nE. Mix about-0.5 mg with 5 mg of 4-dimethylaminobenzal- \ndehyde and 0;2 ml of glacial acetic acid and add 0.2 ml of \nsulphuric acid; a green, colour is produced; Add 1 ml of \nglacial acetic acid; the colour changes to red.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 71.5 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of reserpine.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated .under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Protect the solutions from light throughout the assay.\nRESERPINE TABLETS\nMeasure a volume of the injection containing about 10 mg of \nReserpine and dilute with a 2 per cent w/v solution of citric \nacid to 100.0 ml: Extract 10:0 ml of the solution for \n2 minutes with three quantities, each of 15 ml; of chloroform. \nWash the combined extracts with 10 ml of a 1 per cent w/v \nsolution oisodium bicarbonate, add sufficient chloroform to \nproduce 50.0 ml, mix and evaporate 10.0 ml to dryness on a \nwater-bath. Dissolve the residue in 10 ml of ethanol (95 per \ncent), add 2 ml of 0.25 Msulphuric acid and 10 ml of ethanol \n(95 per cent) and warm gently to dissolve. Cool, dilute to\n100.0 ml with ethanol (95per cent) and dilute 5,6 ml to 50.0 ml \nwith the same solvent (solution A). Transfer 10.0 fnl to a bdiling \ntube, add 2 ml of 0.25 M sulphuric acid and 2 ml of a freshly \nprepared 0.3 per cent w/v solution of sodiumnitrite, mix and \nheat in a water-bath at 55° for 35 minutes. Cool, add 1 ini'of a \nfreshly prepared 5 per cent w/v solution of sulphamic acid \nand dilute to 25.0 ml with ethanol (95percent). Measure the \nabsorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at about \n388 nm (2.4.7), using as the blank a solution prepared by \ntreating a further 10,0 ml of solution Ain the same manner and \nat the same time but omitting the sodium nitrite solution.\nCalculate the content of C33H40N2O9 from the , absorbance \nobtained by repeating the operation using reserpine IPRS in \nplace ofthe substance under examination.\nStorage. Store protected from light in single dose (or if \nstabilising agents are present, in multiple dose) containers."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reserpine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reserpine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Reserpine Tablets\nReserpine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of reserpine, \nC33H40N2O9.\nUsual strengths. 100 pg; 250 pg; 500 pg; 1 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Reserpine Tablets\nReserpine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of reserpine, \nC33H40N2O9.\nUsual strengths. 100 pg; 250 pg; 500 pg; 1 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Powder a few tablets and extract with chloroform. Evaporate \nthe extract to dryness, add 0.1 ml bf a 0.1 per cent w/v solution \nof sodium molybdate in sulphuric acid', a yellow colour is \nproduced which changes to blue within 2 minutes.\nB. Powder a few tablets and extract with chloroform. Evaporate \nthe extract to dryness, add 0.2 ml of a freshly prepared 1 per \ncent w/v solution of vanillin in hydrochloric acid', a pink \ncolour develops within 2 minutes.\nTests • : ' \n' \n' 'An J:: - r; ?•\nUniformity of content. Protect the solutions from, light \nthroughout the test. \n? b\nPowder one tablet, disperse in 10 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution \nof citric acid and extract for 2 minutes with three quantities,\nIP 20225\neach of 5 ml, of chloroforms filter the extracts through a plUg \nof cotton moistened with chloroform. Wash the chlorofo^ \nextracts with 10 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of sodium ■ \nbicarbonate and evaporate the chlorofonn extracts to diyness \non a water-bath. For tablets containing upto 250 pg \nReserpine per tablet, dissolve the residue in 10.0ml of ethanol\n(95 per cent). For tablets containing more than 250 pg of \nReserpine per tablet, dissolve the residue in a suitable volume \nof ethanol (95 per cent) to give a concentration bf250 gg of \nReserpine per 10.0 ml; add 2 ml of 0.25 Msulphuric acid and \n2 ml of a freshly prepared 0.3 per cent w/v solution efi sodium \nnitrite, mix and heat in a water-bath at 55° for 35 minutes. Cool, \nadd 1 ml of a freshly prepared 5 per cent w/v solution of \nsulphamic acid and dilute to 25.0 ml, with ethanol (95 per \ncent). Measure the absorbance of the resulting solution at \nthe maximum at about 388 nm (2.4,7), using as the blank a \nsolution prepared by treating a further 10.0 ml of solution A in \nthe same manner and at the Same time but omitting the sodium \nnitrite solution.\nCalculate the content of C33H4oN209 in the tablet from the \nabsorbance obtained by repeating the operation using \nreserpine IPRS in place bf the substance under examination.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Protect the solutions from light throughoutthe dssay.\nWeigh a quantity of the powdered tablets containing about\n1 mg of Reserpine, add 10 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution of \ncitric acid and extract for 2 minutes with three quantities, \neach of 15 ml, of chloroform. Wash the combined extracts \nwith 10 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of sodium bicarbonate, \nadd sufficient chloroform to produce 50.0 ml and evaporate\n10.0 ml to dryness on a water-bath. Dissolve the residue in \n10 mF of ethanol (95 percent) and add 2 ml of 0.25 A/sulphuric \nacid and 10 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and warm gently to \ndissolve. Cool, dilute to 100.0 ml with ethanol (95 per cent) \nand dilute 5.0 ml to 50.0 ml with the same solvent (solution A). \nTransfer 10.0 ml to a boiling tube, add 2 ml of 0.25 Msulphuric \nacid and 2 ml of a freshly prepared 0.3 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium nitrite, mix and heat in a water-bath at 55° for 35 minutes:\nCool, add 1 ml of a freshly prepared 5 per cent w/v solution of \nsulphamic acid and dilute to 25.0 ml with ethanol (95 per­\ncent). Measure the absorbance of the resulting solution at \nthe maximum at about 388 nm (2.4.7), using as the blank a \nsolution prepared by treating a further 16.0 ml of solution Ain \nthe same manner and at the same time but omitting the sodium \nnitrite solution.\nCalculate the content of C33II.30N2O9 from the absorbance \nobtained by repeating the operation using reserpine. IPRSif \nplace of the substance under examination. .\nStorage. Store protected from light. . ."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reserpine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reserpine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Reserpine\nA. Mol. WL 608.7 v.|\nC33H40N2O9\nReserpine is methyl II, 17a-dimethoxy-18P-[(3,4;5-tri- \nmethoxybcnzoyl)oxy]-3p,20a-yohimbane-16p-carhoxylate i\nReserpine contains not less than 99.0 per cent and ndtihiore\nthan 101.0 per cent of total alkaloids andnot lessithan 98.0 per | \ncent and not more than 1,02.0 per cent of reserpine, \n1\nboth calculated on the dried basis. \n' ‘\nCategory.Antihypertensive. ....... ” ' ? 'J \nyg\nDescription. White to slightly yellow small crystals ora . | \ncrystalline powder which darkens slowly bn exposure tb light. . |"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Reserpine\nA. Mol. WL 608.7 v.|\nC33H40N2O9\nReserpine is methyl II, 17a-dimethoxy-18P-[(3,4;5-tri- \nmethoxybcnzoyl)oxy]-3p,20a-yohimbane-16p-carhoxylate i\nReserpine contains not less than 99.0 per cent and ndtihiore\nthan 101.0 per cent of total alkaloids andnot lessithan 98.0 per | \ncent and not more than 1,02.0 per cent of reserpine, \n1\nboth calculated on the dried basis. \n' ‘\nCategory.Antihypertensive. ....... ” ' ? 'J \nyg\nDescription. White to slightly yellow small crystals ora . | \ncrystalline powder which darkens slowly bn exposure tb light. . |"},{"name":"Identification","text":"•.<..•■ \\ •Ayh'ivy\n',j • < 'y • ( 1 i \n; ■' f • ' , 7 '• s J s \n\\ ■ '• ’-’'i ' • G ■ i1\nTest A may be omitted if tests B, C, D and E are carried out. | \nTests B, C, D and E may be omitted if test A is carried out. ||\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). |\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with reseipmelPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of reserpine . \n' / \n|\nB. Dilute 1 mlof a 0.2 per cent w/v solution in chloroform^ |\n100.0 ml with ethanol (95 per cent). When examined | \nimmediately after preparation, in the range 230 nm to 360 nm |; \n(2.4:7),1 the resulting'solution shows an absorption.maximum g \nat about 268 nm; absorbance at about 268 nm. about 0.55.\nOver the range 288 nm to 295 nm; the spectrum'exhibits a J \nslight minimum and . then a shoulder or a: slight maximum; | \nabsorbance over this range, about 0.34. \nvym ••\nIP 2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/residual-solvents-do-not-have-any-therapeutic-effect","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Residual solvents do not have any therapeutic effect"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Residual solvents do not have any therapeutic effect.\nTherefore, efforts should be made to remove them to the extent\npossible to meet the specifications prescribed. Tests for\nresidual solvents are not generally mentioned in specific\nmonographs since the solvents employed may vary from one\nmanufacturer to another and the requirements of this general\nchapter are applied by adopting the Good Manufaturing\nPractices and/or other quality based requirements. Normally,\nthe competent authority is to be informed of the solvents\nemployed during the production process. Pharmaceutical\nproducts should contain no higher levels of residual solvents\nthan can be supported by safety data.\nDepending upon the safety data and their risk to the human\nhealth, these solvents are classified as follows:\nClass 1 solvents\nThese solvents are known to cause\nunacceptable toxicities and should be avoided in the\nmanufacture of active pharmaceutical substances, excipients\nand medicinal products. However, iftheir use is unavoidable,\nthe restricted concentration limits show!].in Table 1should be\n.J<..\napplied.\n...\nTable 1\nSolvent\nConcentration Limit\n(ppm)"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Residual solvents do not have any therapeutic effect.\nTherefore, efforts should be made to remove them to the extent\npossible to meet the specifications prescribed. Tests for\nresidual solvents are not generally mentioned in specific\nmonographs since the solvents employed may vary from one\nmanufacturer to another and the requirements of this general\nchapter are applied by adopting the Good Manufaturing\nPractices and/or other quality based requirements. Normally,\nthe competent authority is to be informed of the solvents\nemployed during the production process. Pharmaceutical\nproducts should contain no higher levels of residual solvents\nthan can be supported by safety data.\nDepending upon the safety data and their risk to the human\nhealth, these solvents are classified as follows:\nClass 1 solvents\nThese solvents are known to cause\nunacceptable toxicities and should be avoided in the\nmanufacture of active pharmaceutical substances, excipients\nand medicinal products. However, iftheir use is unavoidable,\nthe restricted concentration limits show!].in Table 1should be\n.J<..\napplied.\n...\nTable 1\nSolvent\nConcentration Limit\n(ppm)"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/residual-solvents","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Residual Solvents"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Residual Solvents\n: \na \nTest Methods Oe eae \n/- [dentification a o oe \noe\nSCS \nES\n-GENERAL'NOTICRS \n© \n~ \n. \n: \nINDIAN: PHARMACOPOBIA 2022\nTests mid Alas \n:"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Residual Solvents\n: \na \nTest Methods Oe eae \n/- [dentification a o oe \noe\nSCS \nES\n-GENERAL'NOTICRS \n© \n~ \n. \n: \nINDIAN: PHARMACOPOBIA 2022\nTests mid Alas \n:"},{"name":"Tests","text":"“Quantities\nme \nApparatus -\nReagents; and’ Solutions\nIndicators \nfee\noe Reference Substances -\nTests Animals\nms Rounding Rules for Caletlation of Result"},{"name":"Storage","text":"oS Storage Containers\nLabeling,\nAIG\n| \nSe \nee \naggre \nomer \naa \nfe \nCe \nee\n“yp 2092"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/resolution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Resolution"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Resolution\n=(VRb — VOIV0\nThe resolution or resolution factor, R, is specified to ensure \nthat closely eluting compounds are resolved from each other, \nto establish the general resolving power of the system, and to \nensure that internal standards are resolved from the drug. \nResolution between peaks of similar height of two components \nmay be defined by the expression:\nWhere, VRb \t\n= retention time or distance along the\nbaseline between the point of \ninjection and perpendicular dropped \nfrom the maximum of the peak of \ninterest, \n= the distance along the baseline\nFig. 2.4.14-2 \nRelative retention\n2 (VRb — VR. )\nbetween the point of injection and \nperpendicular dropped from the \nmaximum of the peak of an unretained \ncomponent.\nR=\nRelative retention, ran, is calculated as an estimate from the \nexpression\nW2 +\nWhere, VRb and VRa = \nretention times or distances along the \nbaseline between the point of \ninjection and perpendiculars dropped \nfrom the maxima of two adjacent peaks, \ncorresponding widths at the bases of \nthe peaks obtained by extrapolating \nthe relatively straight sides of the \npeaks to the base line.\nt r h\nThe values of VRb and V0 must be expressed in the same unit of \nmeasurement.\nI I. a\nWhere, trh\nretention time of the peak of interest,\nColumn efficiency\nretention time of the reference peak (usually\nColumn efficiency can also be used as a sy,steirsintability \nrequirement. It is a measure of peak sharpness, which is\nthe peak corresponding to the substance \nunder examination).\nif\nSignal to noise ratio\nThe signal-to- noise ratio is determined from the expression:\nS/N = 2H / h\nWhere, H \t\nheight of the peak corresponding to the \ncomponent concerned, in the chromato-\ngram obtained with the prescribed reference \nsolution, measured from the maximum of \nthe peak to the extrapolated baseline of the \nsignal observed over a distance equal to \n20 times the width at half-height,\nh \t\n= range of the background noise in a\nchromatogram obtained after injection of a \nblank, observed over a distance equal to 20 \ntimes the width at half-height of the peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the \nprescribed reference solution and, if \npossible, situated equally around the place \nwhere this peak would be found.\n2.4.15. Paper Chromatography\nPaper chromatography is a technique in which separation of \nthe components of a mixture is achieved through the action of \na single liquid phase in a process similar to adsorption \nchromatography in columns and in which a sheet of paper of \nsuitable texture and thickness acts as the adsorbant. Since \nthe natural water content of the paper, or selective inhibition \nof a hydrophilic component of the liquid phase by the paper \nmay be regarded as a stationary phase, a partitioning of the \nsolute between two phases may contribute to the separation \nof components.\nAlternatively, a two-phase system may be adopted in which \nthe paper is impregnated with one of the phases, which then \nremains stationary and the chromatogram is developed by \nslow movement of the other, mobile phase over the sheet. \nDevelopment may be ascending in which case the mobile phase \nis carried up the paper by capillary forces, or descending in \nwhich case the mobile phase is also assisted by gravitational\nforce.\n.Apparatus\n(a) A vapour-tight tank of glass, porcelain or stainless steel\nprovided with inlets for addition of solvent or for releasing \ninternal pressure and so designed that the progress of \nthe chromatographic run can be observed without \nopening the tank. \n(b) A rack of corrosion-resistant material about 5 cm shorter\nthan the inside height of the tank, to serve-atipprt for\n:\n the solvent trough and for antisiphoning rods, vht h in \nturn, hold the chromatographic sheets.\n2.4.15. PAPER CHROMATOGRAPHY \t\nIP 2018 \t\nIP 2018 \t\n2.4.16. SIZE-EXCLUSION CHROMATOGRAPHY\n(c) Glass troughs, longer than the width of the chromatograph\nby an appropriate solvent, and the solutions may be made up \nto a known volume and quantitatively analysed by a"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Resolution\n=(VRb — VOIV0\nThe resolution or resolution factor, R, is specified to ensure \nthat closely eluting compounds are resolved from each other, \nto establish the general resolving power of the system, and to \nensure that internal standards are resolved from the drug. \nResolution between peaks of similar height of two components \nmay be defined by the expression:\nWhere, VRb \t\n= retention time or distance along the\nbaseline between the point of \ninjection and perpendicular dropped \nfrom the maximum of the peak of \ninterest, \n= the distance along the baseline\nFig. 2.4.14-2 \nRelative retention\n2 (VRb — VR. )\nbetween the point of injection and \nperpendicular dropped from the \nmaximum of the peak of an unretained \ncomponent.\nR=\nRelative retention, ran, is calculated as an estimate from the \nexpression\nW2 +\nWhere, VRb and VRa = \nretention times or distances along the \nbaseline between the point of \ninjection and perpendiculars dropped \nfrom the maxima of two adjacent peaks, \ncorresponding widths at the bases of \nthe peaks obtained by extrapolating \nthe relatively straight sides of the \npeaks to the base line.\nt r h\nThe values of VRb and V0 must be expressed in the same unit of \nmeasurement.\nI I. a\nWhere, trh\nretention time of the peak of interest,\nColumn efficiency\nretention time of the reference peak (usually\nColumn efficiency can also be used as a sy,steirsintability \nrequirement. It is a measure of peak sharpness, which is\nthe peak corresponding to the substance \nunder examination).\nif\nSignal to noise ratio\nThe signal-to- noise ratio is determined from the expression:\nS/N = 2H / h\nWhere, H \t\nheight of the peak corresponding to the \ncomponent concerned, in the chromato-\ngram obtained with the prescribed reference \nsolution, measured from the maximum of \nthe peak to the extrapolated baseline of the \nsignal observed over a distance equal to \n20 times the width at half-height,\nh \t\n= range of the background noise in a\nchromatogram obtained after injection of a \nblank, observed over a distance equal to 20 \ntimes the width at half-height of the peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the \nprescribed reference solution and, if \npossible, situated equally around the place \nwhere this peak would be found.\n2.4.15. Paper Chromatography\nPaper chromatography is a technique in which separation of \nthe components of a mixture is achieved through the action of \na single liquid phase in a process similar to adsorption \nchromatography in columns and in which a sheet of paper of \nsuitable texture and thickness acts as the adsorbant. Since \nthe natural water content of the paper, or selective inhibition \nof a hydrophilic component of the liquid phase by the paper \nmay be regarded as a stationary phase, a partitioning of the \nsolute between two phases may contribute to the separation \nof components.\nAlternatively, a two-phase system may be adopted in which \nthe paper is impregnated with one of the phases, which then \nremains stationary and the chromatogram is developed by \nslow movement of the other, mobile phase over the sheet. \nDevelopment may be ascending in which case the mobile phase \nis carried up the paper by capillary forces, or descending in \nwhich case the mobile phase is also assisted by gravitational\nforce.\n.Apparatus\n(a) A vapour-tight tank of glass, porcelain or stainless steel\nprovided with inlets for addition of solvent or for releasing \ninternal pressure and so designed that the progress of \nthe chromatographic run can be observed without \nopening the tank. \n(b) A rack of corrosion-resistant material about 5 cm shorter\nthan the inside height of the tank, to serve-atipprt for\n:\n the solvent trough and for antisiphoning rods, vht h in \nturn, hold the chromatographic sheets.\n2.4.15. PAPER CHROMATOGRAPHY \t\nIP 2018 \t\nIP 2018 \t\n2.4.16. SIZE-EXCLUSION CHROMATOGRAPHY\n(c) Glass troughs, longer than the width of the chromatograph\nby an appropriate solvent, and the solutions may be made up \nto a known volume and quantitatively analysed by appropriate \nchemical or instrumental methods.\nfractionation in the appropriate range of molecular sizes and \nthrough which the eluent is passed at a constant rate. The \ndimensions of the column are stated in the individual \nmonograph as (length x internal diameter). The mobile phase \nis passed through the column either by gravity or by means of \na pump. The outlet from the column is connected to a detector \nfitted with an automatic reorder that allows the monitoring of \nthe relative concentrations of the components of the sample. \nDetectors are usually based on photometric, refractometric or \nluminescent properties. An automatic fraction-collector may \nbe attached, if required.\nsheets and holding a volume of solvent greater than that \nneeded for one chromatographic run.\n(d) Antisiphoning rods of heavy glass to be supported by"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/results","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Results"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Results\nUsing a suitable method of analysis, determine the mass of \nactive substance collected on the filters and filter, holders\nsinusoidal sinusoidal\nWaveform sinusoidal\nduring each time interval. Determine the active substance \ndelivery rate by dividing the mass of active substance collected \non the first inhalation filter by the time interval used for\n1328\nwww.webofpharma.com\ncollection. Determine the total mass of active substance \ndelivered by summing the mass of active substance collected \non all inhalation filters and filter holders;\nAerodynamic assessment of nebulised Aerosols\nNebulised products need to be size-characterised at flow rates \nlower than the range that is normally used for powder inhalers \nand metered-dose inhalers. A flow rate of 15 litre per minutes \nis recommended because this value represents a good \napproximation to the mid-inhalation flow rate achievable by a \ntidally breathing healthy adult (500 ml tidal volume). Although \nlow-angle laser light scattering instruments - (laser \ndiffractometers) can provide rapid sae-distribution \nmeasurements of nebuliser-generated aerosols, these \ntechniques do not detect the active substance; rather they \nmeasure the size distribution of the droplets irrespective of \ntheir content. This may not be a problem with homogeneous\nsolutions, but can result in significant error if the product to \nbe nebulised is a suspension, or if droplet evaporation is\nsignificant as can be the case with certain nebuliser types. \nCascade impactors enable the aerosol to be characterised \nunambiguously in terms of the mass of active substance as a \nfunction of aerodynamic diameter. Laser diffraction may be \nused if validated against a cascade impaction method.\nApparatus D a cascade impactor, has been calibrated at\n15 litres per minutes and is therefore used for this test. \nDetermining mass balance in the same way as for powder \ninhalers and metered-dose inhalers is not straightforward, in \nthat the dose is being captured as a continuous output, and \nhence is not included. As part of method development, \nrecovery experiments must be performed to validate the \nmethod.\nIt is also recognised that the control of evaporation of droplets \nproduced by nebulisers may be critical to avoid bias in the \ndroplet size assessment process. Evaporation can be minimised \nby cooling the impactor to a temperature of about 5°, typically\nachieved by cooling the impactor in a refrigerator for about 90 \nminutes. Typically, at least after each day of use, the apparatus \nmust be fully cleaned, including the inter-stage passageways, \nin view of the greater risk of corrosion caused by the \ncondensation/accumulation of saline-containing droplets on \ninter-stage metalwork associated with cooling the impactor. It \nis recommended to dry all surfaces of the apparatus after each \ntest, for example with compressed air. Note: the micro-orifice \ncollector (MOC) should not be dried with compressed air.\nApparatus\nA detailed description of Apparatus D and the induction port \nis contained in preparation for inhalation, and includes details \nof critical dimensions and the qualification process for the \nimpactor (stage mensuration).\nIP 2022"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Results\nUsing a suitable method of analysis, determine the mass of \nactive substance collected on the filters and filter, holders\nsinusoidal sinusoidal\nWaveform sinusoidal\nduring each time interval. Determine the active substance \ndelivery rate by dividing the mass of active substance collected \non the first inhalation filter by the time interval used for\n1328\nwww.webofpharma.com\ncollection. Determine the total mass of active substance \ndelivered by summing the mass of active substance collected \non all inhalation filters and filter holders;\nAerodynamic assessment of nebulised Aerosols\nNebulised products need to be size-characterised at flow rates \nlower than the range that is normally used for powder inhalers \nand metered-dose inhalers. A flow rate of 15 litre per minutes \nis recommended because this value represents a good \napproximation to the mid-inhalation flow rate achievable by a \ntidally breathing healthy adult (500 ml tidal volume). Although \nlow-angle laser light scattering instruments - (laser \ndiffractometers) can provide rapid sae-distribution \nmeasurements of nebuliser-generated aerosols, these \ntechniques do not detect the active substance; rather they \nmeasure the size distribution of the droplets irrespective of \ntheir content. This may not be a problem with homogeneous\nsolutions, but can result in significant error if the product to \nbe nebulised is a suspension, or if droplet evaporation is\nsignificant as can be the case with certain nebuliser types. \nCascade impactors enable the aerosol to be characterised \nunambiguously in terms of the mass of active substance as a \nfunction of aerodynamic diameter. Laser diffraction may be \nused if validated against a cascade impaction method.\nApparatus D a cascade impactor, has been calibrated at\n15 litres per minutes and is therefore used for this test. \nDetermining mass balance in the same way as for powder \ninhalers and metered-dose inhalers is not straightforward, in \nthat the dose is being captured as a continuous output, and \nhence is not included. As part of method development, \nrecovery experiments must be performed to validate the \nmethod.\nIt is also recognised that the control of evaporation of droplets \nproduced by nebulisers may be critical to avoid bias in the \ndroplet size assessment process. Evaporation can be minimised \nby cooling the impactor to a temperature of about 5°, typically\nachieved by cooling the impactor in a refrigerator for about 90 \nminutes. Typically, at least after each day of use, the apparatus \nmust be fully cleaned, including the inter-stage passageways, \nin view of the greater risk of corrosion caused by the \ncondensation/accumulation of saline-containing droplets on \ninter-stage metalwork associated with cooling the impactor. It \nis recommended to dry all surfaces of the apparatus after each \ntest, for example with compressed air. Note: the micro-orifice \ncollector (MOC) should not be dried with compressed air.\nApparatus\nA detailed description of Apparatus D and the induction port \nis contained in preparation for inhalation, and includes details \nof critical dimensions and the qualification process for the \nimpactor (stage mensuration).\nIP 2022"},{"name":"INHALATION PREPARATIONS","text":"A back-up filter in addition to the micro-orifice collector (MOC) \nmust be used to ensure quantitative recovery of active\nsubstance from the nebulised aerosol at the specified flow \nrate of 15 litres per minutes. The filter is located below the \nMOC (internal filter option) or a filter in holder, external to the \nimpactor, is used to capture any fine droplets that pass beyond \nthe last size fractionating stage.\nA- pre-separator is not used for testing nebuliser-generated \naerosols.\nMethod validation\nImpactor stage overloading\nDuring method development and validation, it is important to \nconfirm that the volume of liquid sampled from the nebuliser \ndoes not overload the impactor. Visual inspection of the \ncollection surfaces on stages collecting most of the droplets \nmay reveal streaking if overloading has occurred. This \nphenomenon is usually also associated with an increase in \nmass of active substance collected on the final stage and \nback-up filter. Reducing the sampling period (To) is the most\neffective way to avoid overloading in any given system, \nbalancing overloading with analytical sensitivity.\nRe-entrainment\nDroplet bounce re-entrainment are less likely with nebuliser- \nproduced droplets than with solid particles from inhalers and \nfor that reason coating would not normally be required.\nMethod\nA. Nebuliser\nB. Induction port\nC. lmpactor(apparatus D)\nD. Flow control valve\nE. Vacuum source\nFig. 17: Apparatus D for measuring the size distribution of\npreparations for nebulisation\nPre-cool the assembled impactor and induction port in a \nrefrigerator (set at about 5°) for not less than 90 minutes and \nstart the determination within about 5 minutes of removal of\n1329\nwww.webofpharma.com\nthe impactor from the refrigerator. Other methods that maintain \nthe impactor at a constant temperature (for example, use of a \ncooling cabinet) can also be employed when validated.\nSet up the nebuliser with a supply of driving gas (usually air \nor oxygen), or use a compressor, at the pressure and flow rate \nspecified by the manufacturer of the nebuliser. Take \nprecautions to ensure that the gas supply line does not become \ndetached from the nebuliser when under pressure. Fill the \nnebuliser with the volume of the medicinal product as specified \nin the patient instructions.\nRemove the impactor from the refrigerator. Attach the induction \nport to the impactor, and connect the outlet of the impactor/ \nexternal filter to a vacuum source that is capable of drawing air \nthrough me system at 15 litres per minutes as specified in Fig.\n17. Turn on the flow through the impactor.\nConnect a flow meter, calibrated for the volumetric flow leaving \nthe meter, to the induction port. Adjust the flow control valve \nlocated between the impactor and the vacuum source to \nachieve a steady flow through the system at 15 litres per \nminutes (± 5 per cent). Remove the flow meter.\nMake sure the nebuliser is positioned in the same orientation \nas intended for use then attach the mouthpiece of the nebuliser \nto the induction port, using a mouthpiece adapter if required, \nensuring that connections are airtight. Switch on the flow/ \ncompressor for the nebuliser. Sample for a predetermined time \n(To). Once determined, this time (To) must be defined and used \nin the analytical method for a particular medicinal product to \nensure that mass fraction data can be compared. At the end of \nthe sampling period, switch off the driving gas flow/ \ncompressor to the nebuliser, remove the nebuliser from the \ninduction port and switch off the flow from the vacuum source \nto the impactor.\nDismantle the impactor and, using a suitable method of \nanalysis, determine the mass of active substance collected in \nthe induction port, on each stage and on the back-up filter/ \nexternal filter as described for Apparatus D. Add the mass of \nactive substance collected in the MOC to that deposited on \nthe back-up filter/extemal filter and treat as a single sample for \nthe purpose of subsequent calculations.\nCalculate the mass fraction (Fm^p) of the active deposited \non each component of the impactor, commencing with the \ninduction port and proceeding in order through the impactor, \nusing the following expression:\njrp \n_ \"*comp\nwhere, >«comp = mass associated with the components under\nevaluation;\nM = total mass collected by the system."},{"name":"INHALATION PREPARATIONS","text":"IP 2022\nPresent Fm;comp in order of location within the measurement \nequipment, beginning at the induction port and ending with \nthe back-up filter of the impactor (Fig. 18). Where appropriate,\nFm,comP f°r adjacent stages of the impactor may be combined \nin order to report the mass fraction collected on a group of \nstages as a single value.\nDetermine the cumulative mass-weighed particle-size \ndistribution of the aerosol size-fractionated by the impactor in \naccordance with the procedure given in inhalation preparation. \nStarting at the filter, derive a cumulative mass versus effective \ncut-off diameter of the respective stages (see Table 12 for the \nappropriate cut-off diameters at 15 litres per minutes). Plot the \ncumulative fraction of active substance versus cut-off diameter \nin a suitable format, for example logarithmic or log-probability \nformat. Where appropriate, determine by interpolation the \nfraction either below a given size or between an upper and a \nlower size limit.\nTable 12 - Cut-off sizes for Apparatus D at 15 litre per minutes\nStage\ncut-off diameter (pm)\n1\n14.1\n2\n8.61\n3\n5.39\n4\n3.30\n5\n2.08\n6\n1.36\n7\n0.98\nIf necessary, and where appropriate, determine values for the \nmass median aerodynamic diameter (MMAD) and the\ngeometric standard deviation (GSD), as appropriate.\nFig. 18: Example of mass fraction of droplets presented in terms\nof location within the sampling system\nContent of active ingredient on actuation of the valve test\nThe following test conditions are for use in preparations for \ninhalation. Specifically the methodology should be applied \nto pressurised inhalation products.\nContent of active ingredient delivered by actuation of the valve\nRemove the pressurised container from the actuator and remove \nall labels and markings which may be present on the container \nwith a suitable solvent. Dry the container, replace in its actuator, \nshake for 30 seconds and prime the metering valve as follows. \nDischarge once to waste, wait for no less than 5 seconds and \ndischarge again to waste. Remove the pressurised container \nfrom its actuator; clean the valve stem (internally and externally) \nand the valve ferrule by washing with a suitable solvent. Dry \nthe complete valve assembly using an air line fitted with an \nappropriate narrow jet to ensure that all solvent is removed \nfrom the inside of the valve stem.\nPlace a stainless steel base plate that has three legs and a \ncentral circular indentation with a hole about 1.5 mm in diameter \nin a small vessel suitable for shaking and add the volume of \nsolvent specified in the monograph. The size of the vessel is \nsuch that when the pressurised inhalation is discharged into \nthe specified volume of solvent as described below the \ndischarge takes place not less than 25 mm below the surface \nof the solvent.\nShake the pressurised container for about 30 seconds and \nplace it inverted in the vessel. Discharge 10 deliveries below \nthe surface of the solvent actuating the valve at intervals of \nnot less than 5 seconds, maintaining the pressurised container \nin the vertical plane and discharging the pressurised inhalation \nthrough the hole in the centre of the base plate. (It may be \nnecessary because of the nature of the formulation to shake \nthe pressurised container between each actuation of the valve; \nwhere this is the case shaking should be carried out without \nremoving the pressurised container from its inverted position\nin the vessel). Remove the pressurised container, wash it with \nthe specified solvent and dilute the combined solution and \nwashing to the volume specified in the monograph. Determine \nthe amount of active ingredient by the method described under \nthe assay and calculate the amount delivered from each \nactuation of the valve. The result lies within the range for the \ncontent of active ingredient stated in the monograph.\nInsulin Preparations"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/retinol-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Retinol Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Retinol Capsules\nVitamin A Capsules contain Vitamin A concentrate oil. The \ncapsule contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more \nthan 12 0.0 per cent of the stated amount of vitamin À.\nUsual strengths. 10,000 IU; 25,000 IU."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Retinol Capsules\nVitamin A Capsules contain Vitamin A concentrate oil. The \ncapsule contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more \nthan 12 0.0 per cent of the stated amount of vitamin À.\nUsual strengths. 10,000 IU; 25,000 IU."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Tests C and D may. be omitted'iftests A and B are carried out. \nTests B and D may be omitted if tests A and Care carried out.\nA.Determine by thimlayer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 12 volumes of cyclohexane and\n1 volume of ether.\nTest solution. Mix a quantity of the capsule containing about \n50,000 IU of vitamin A in 10ml of cyclohexane.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve about 50,000 ÏU Of retinol\nacetate/PRS in 10 ml ofcyclohexane. ;• ; < c:\nReference solution (b). Dissolve; about 50,000 IU of retinol \npropionate 77^5 in 10 ml of cyclohexane.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve about 50,000 IU of retinol \npalmitate IP RS in \nof cyclohexane. ,s - \n: \n!\nApply to the plate 2 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm. The principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe test solution corresponds to that in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solutions (a), (b) and,(c).\n4128.\n........................................................................... ............ ® 2022 J\nB. Dissolve a small quantity of retinolvinfabout 1-^ \ndichloromethane and add 5 ml of antimony trichloride^ \nblue colour is immediately produced which turns graduall ’  \nviolet-red. ;\n*\nC. In the Assay, the principal peak in thechromatograrn \nobtained with the test solution corrcsponds to the peak in th \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nD. To a quantity of retinol containing about50,000 ICJ of vitamin \nA, add 100 ml of ethanol (95 per cent). Dilute1 ml of the \nsolution to 50 ml with a mixture of 10.0.volumes of ethanol \n(95 per cent) and 1 volume of 5 M hydro ch/oric acid \nImmediately after preparation measure the absorbance in the \nrange 300 to 400 nm (2.4.7). The solution exhibits a single \nmaximum at about 326 nm. Heat the solution in à water-bath \nfor 30 seconds and cool rapidly. The absorption spectrum of \nthe resulting solution, when observed between 300 and \n400 nm, exhibits a shoulder at about 332 nm and maxima at \nabout 348,367 and 389 nm."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTÉ— Carry out the assay as rapidly as possible, avoiding \nexposure to actinic light and oxidising agents, and \nmaintaining whenever possible an atmosphere of. nitrogen.\nTest solution. Weigh and mix the contents of 20 capsules: \nDisperse a quantity of the mixéd content of capsules \ncontaining about 1,00,000 IU of vitamin A, into a 100-ml \nvolumétrie flask. Dissolve immediately in 5.0 ml of n-pentahe,\nadd 40 ml of 0.! M tetrabutylammonium hydroxide \n2-propanol.Svdr\\ gently and let the mixturereact for 10 minutes \nat 60° to 65° for hydrolysis, swirling occasionally. Allow to. \ncool to room temperature, dilute to volume with 2-propanol \ncontaining about 0.1 per cent w/v solution of butylated \nhydroxytoluene. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with 2- \npropanol. \n;.... \n,:.y\nReference solution (a). Weigh a quantity of retinol IPRS or \nretinolpalmitate IPRS containing about 1,00,000 IU of vitamin \nA, into a 100-ml volumetric flask. Dissolve immediately in \n5.0 ml ofn-pentane, add40 ml of Oil Mtetrdbutylarnmoniuni \nhydroxide in 2-propanol. Swirl gently and let the mixture react \nfor 10 minutes at 60° to 65° for hydrolysis, swirling occasionally \nAllow to cool to room temperature, dilute to volume with \n2-propanol cbntainiiig about 0.1 per cent w/y solution of \nbutylated hydroxytoluene. Dilute 5,0 ml of the solution to \n50.0 ml with 2-propanol.\nChromatographic system \n' \n;\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecysilane groups bonded to porous silica (5 gm),"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rh-blood-grouping-reagents-reconstituted-where-necessary","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rh blood-grouping reagents, reconstituted where necessary"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rh blood-grouping reagents, reconstituted where necessary \nas stated on the label, comply with the following \nrequirements.\nTest for agglutinins. The serum of the blood under examination\nABO group of blood is determined from the antigens on the \nred corpuscles and the antibodies in the serum or plasma. \nCollect a few ml of blood without anticoagulant in a test-tube \nand allow to clot. Remove the serum and prepare a 2.0 to 3.0 \nper cent suspension of red corpuscles in saline solution.\nis similarly tested against group A and B red corpuscles, 0.04 \nml of the suspension and 0.04 ml of the undiluted serum being \nused. In this test, small reactions are frequent, and in about \n50.0 per cent of infants under 1 year old, either one or both of \nthe agglutinins to be expected on the basis of agglutinogens \nof the red corpuscles, are not found. In all bloods, a \"w\" \nreaction is considered to be true, a \"?\" requires a retest. When \nthe expected agglutinins are not disclosed after retest, the \ndiagnosis is based on the agglutinogens of the red corpuscles.\nPotency. IgM anti-D blood-grouping reagent  —  It contains \nanti-D as a \"saline agglutinin\" in such quantities that it gives \na positive reaction at a dilution of 1 in 32 against corpuscles \nknown to contain the D antigens.\nTest for antigens. Mix 1 volume of the red corpuscle \nsuspension with 1 volume of anti-A blood-grouping reagent, \nanti-B blood-grouping reagent and anti-A,B (group 0) blood-\ngrouping reagent, the specificities of which have been\nIgG anti-D blood-grouping reagent — The potency of IgG \nanti-D blood-grouping reagent is determined by comparing \nits \"albumin agglutinin\" antibody activity with that of the \nappropriate International Standard of Blood-typing serum or \nwith that of another suitable reference preparation the potency \nof which has been determined in relation to the appropriate \nInternational Standard.\ndemonstrated by testing with known red corpuscles of group \nA,B and 0. Allow the mixture in the test-tube to stand at room \ntemperature for 1 to 2 hours and then tap them gently to \ndisperse the deposit of corpuscles. Examine the contents of \nthe tubes microscopically for agglutination.\nSlide or tile method. Blood obtained from the finger-prick is \ndiluted with saline solution so as to give a suspension \ncontaining approximately 5.0 per cent v/v red corpuscles. \nOxalated blood is also suitable for this purpose.\nThe determination is carried out by simultaneously titrating \nthe reagent under examination and the reference preparation \nagainst suspension of human red corpuscles containing the \nD antigens.\nTake two plain glass slides marked A and B, place one drop of \nred cell suspension on each of them, add one drop of anti-A \nserum on the slide marked B. Mix with a glass rod and gently \nrock the slides to and fro. The agglutinate appears almost at \nonce with the appropriate serum.\nTest for antibodies. Mix separate portions of the serum or \nplasma with suspension of human red corpuscles of group A \n(sub-groups A l and A2), group B and group 0 and determine \nthe antibodies present by examination of the resulting pattern \nof deposit of corpuscles. As antibodies are incompletely \ndeveloped in children under one year of age, the blood group \nof the child is based solely on the antigens present on red \ncorpuscles.\nIgG anti-D blood-grouping reagent contains not less than 32 \nUnits of anti-D antibody per ml, i.e. the titre of the reagent \nunder examination is not less than one-half of that of the \nappropriate reconstituted International Standard irrespective \nof the titre obtained.\nNegative readings should not be taken until the preparation \nhas stood at least for 10 minutes. The reaction is visible to the \nnaked eye but a hand lens or a low power microscope may be \nemployed in doubtful cases. When a large number of blood \nsamples have to be tested for blood groups, a long porcelain \nor opal glass tile may be employed in place of glass slides.\nAnti-C blood-grouping reagent—It "},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rh blood-grouping reagents, reconstituted where necessary \nas stated on the label, comply with the following \nrequirements.\nTest for agglutinins. The serum of the blood under examination\nABO group of blood is determined from the antigens on the \nred corpuscles and the antibodies in the serum or plasma. \nCollect a few ml of blood without anticoagulant in a test-tube \nand allow to clot. Remove the serum and prepare a 2.0 to 3.0 \nper cent suspension of red corpuscles in saline solution.\nis similarly tested against group A and B red corpuscles, 0.04 \nml of the suspension and 0.04 ml of the undiluted serum being \nused. In this test, small reactions are frequent, and in about \n50.0 per cent of infants under 1 year old, either one or both of \nthe agglutinins to be expected on the basis of agglutinogens \nof the red corpuscles, are not found. In all bloods, a \"w\" \nreaction is considered to be true, a \"?\" requires a retest. When \nthe expected agglutinins are not disclosed after retest, the \ndiagnosis is based on the agglutinogens of the red corpuscles.\nPotency. IgM anti-D blood-grouping reagent  —  It contains \nanti-D as a \"saline agglutinin\" in such quantities that it gives \na positive reaction at a dilution of 1 in 32 against corpuscles \nknown to contain the D antigens.\nTest for antigens. Mix 1 volume of the red corpuscle \nsuspension with 1 volume of anti-A blood-grouping reagent, \nanti-B blood-grouping reagent and anti-A,B (group 0) blood-\ngrouping reagent, the specificities of which have been\nIgG anti-D blood-grouping reagent — The potency of IgG \nanti-D blood-grouping reagent is determined by comparing \nits \"albumin agglutinin\" antibody activity with that of the \nappropriate International Standard of Blood-typing serum or \nwith that of another suitable reference preparation the potency \nof which has been determined in relation to the appropriate \nInternational Standard.\ndemonstrated by testing with known red corpuscles of group \nA,B and 0. Allow the mixture in the test-tube to stand at room \ntemperature for 1 to 2 hours and then tap them gently to \ndisperse the deposit of corpuscles. Examine the contents of \nthe tubes microscopically for agglutination.\nSlide or tile method. Blood obtained from the finger-prick is \ndiluted with saline solution so as to give a suspension \ncontaining approximately 5.0 per cent v/v red corpuscles. \nOxalated blood is also suitable for this purpose.\nThe determination is carried out by simultaneously titrating \nthe reagent under examination and the reference preparation \nagainst suspension of human red corpuscles containing the \nD antigens.\nTake two plain glass slides marked A and B, place one drop of \nred cell suspension on each of them, add one drop of anti-A \nserum on the slide marked B. Mix with a glass rod and gently \nrock the slides to and fro. The agglutinate appears almost at \nonce with the appropriate serum.\nTest for antibodies. Mix separate portions of the serum or \nplasma with suspension of human red corpuscles of group A \n(sub-groups A l and A2), group B and group 0 and determine \nthe antibodies present by examination of the resulting pattern \nof deposit of corpuscles. As antibodies are incompletely \ndeveloped in children under one year of age, the blood group \nof the child is based solely on the antigens present on red \ncorpuscles.\nIgG anti-D blood-grouping reagent contains not less than 32 \nUnits of anti-D antibody per ml, i.e. the titre of the reagent \nunder examination is not less than one-half of that of the \nappropriate reconstituted International Standard irrespective \nof the titre obtained.\nNegative readings should not be taken until the preparation \nhas stood at least for 10 minutes. The reaction is visible to the \nnaked eye but a hand lens or a low power microscope may be \nemployed in doubtful cases. When a large number of blood \nsamples have to be tested for blood groups, a long porcelain \nor opal glass tile may be employed in place of glass slides.\nAnti-C blood-grouping reagent—It contains anti-C antibody \nin such quantities that it gives a positive reaction at a dilution \nof I in 8 with red corpuscles known to contain the C antigens.\nB. Determination of Rh Group of Donors\nRh Blood-Grouping Reagents\nAnti-E blood-grouping reagent-- It contains anti-E antibody\nWeak reactions with anti-A serum in group AB. Care must be \ntaken in making the diagnosis of group B because the reaction \nof anti-A serum with the A agglutinogens is often weak when \nthe B agglutinogen is also present on the corpuscles; group \nAB corpuscles may therefore be falsely diagnosed as group B \ncorpuscles. This is particularly true of the sub–group A, B in \nwhich the reactions of the A agglutinins are always weak. The \nexamination of the serum for agglutinins does not necessarily \nserve as a check since the serum of a number ofA, B persons \ncontains the agglutinin anti-A, which reacts with A l but not \nwith A2 corpuscles. Since A, corpuscles are used to check the \nserum, agglutination may occur and the diagnosis of group B \ncorpuscles be apparently confirmed. The mistake is, however, \nunlikely to arise if an anti-A serum is used which has been \ntested for its capacity to react with known AB corpuscles. As \nan.aitional check the serum from every blood that has been\nSource. Rh blood-grouping reagents are derived from the sera \nor defibrinated plasma of one or more persons immunised by \nRh system or from cultures of mammalian lymphocytes. The \nmaterial to be used must be tested with negative results for \nthe presence of hepatitis B surface antigens and anti-bodies \nto HIV I and HIV II by suitable sensitive methods approved \nby the appropriate authority.\nin such quantities that it gives a positive reaction at a dilution \nof 1 in 8 with red corpuscles known to contain the E antigens.\nStorage. Rh blood-grouping reagents should be kept in sterile \ncontainers sealed so as to exclude micro-organisms.\nLiquid Rh blood-grouping reagent which do not contain \nantimicrobial preservative should"},{"name":"3. REFERENCE DATA","text":"2.8.18. ASSAY OF HUMAN PLASMIN INHIBITOR (arANTIPLASMIN) \nIP 2018\nper minute). Alternatively, an end-point assay might be used \nby stopping the reaction with acetic acid and measuring the \nabsorbance at 405 nm."},{"name":"3. REFERENCE DATA","text":"3.1."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rh-blood-grouping-reagents","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rh Blood-grouping Reagents"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rh Blood-Grouping Reagents\nSource. Rh blood-grouping reagents are derived from the sera \nor defibrinated plasma of one or more persons immunised by \nRh system or from cultures of mammalian lymphocytes. The \nmaterial to be used must be tested with negative results for \nthe presence of hepatitis B surface antigens and anti-bodies \nto HIV I and HIV-H by suitable sensitive methods approved \nby the appropriate authority.\nDescription. Rh blood-grouping reagents may be issued as \nliquids or they may be prepared by reconstitution from the \ndried reagents. Liquid reagents are clear or faintly opalescent, \nyellowish or colourless fluids without turbidity. They may \ncontain a suitable antimicrobial.preservative. Dried reagents \nare pale yellow powders or friable solids.\nRh blood-grouping reagents are of four types, namely IgM \nAnti-D blood-grouping reagent, IgG Anti-D blood grouping \nreagent, Anti-C blood-grouping reagent and Anti-E blood­\ngrouping reagent.\nProperties. Blended IgG and IgM monoclonal reagents are f \npreferably used for Rh blood-grouping in blood banks. Anti- \nD, Anti-C and Anti-E blood-grouping reagent should be shown \nI\nnot to agglutinate or to coat any of a comprehensive panel of \ni\ncorpuscles that do not contain the respective antigens |\nI\nRh blood-grouping reagents, reconstituted -where necessary I \nas stated on the label, comply with the following | \nrequirements. \n|\nPotency. IgManti-D blood-grouping reagent — It contains I\nanti-D as a “saline agglutinin” in such quantities that it gives I \na positive reaction at a dilution of 1 in 32 against corpuscles I \nknown to contain the D antigens. \n|\nIgG anti-D blood-grouping reagent — The potency of IgG |\nanti-D blood-grouping reagent is determined by comparing I \nits “albumin agglutinin” antibody activity with that of the | \nappropriate International Standard of Blood-typing serum or \nI\nwith that of another suitable reference preparation the potency \nI\nof which has been determined in relation to the appropriate I \nInternational Standard. \n|\nThe determination is carried out by simultaneously titrating I \nthe reagent under examination and the reference preparation | \nagainst suspension of human red corpuscles containing the | \nD antigens. \n|\nI \nIgG anti-D blood-grouping reagent contains not less than 32 \n|\nUnits of anti-D antibody per ml, i.e. the titre of the reagent I \nunder examination is not less than one-half of that of the I \nappropriate reconstituted International Standard irrespective | \nof the titre obtained. \nf\nt\nAnti-C blood-grouping reagent—It contains anti-C antibody |\nin such quantities that it gives a positive reaction at a dilution | \nof I in 8 with red corpuscles known to contain the C antigens. |\nAnti-E blood-grouping reagent — It contains anti-E antibody |\nin such quantities that it gives a positive reaction at a dilution । \nof 1 in 8 with red corpuscles known to contain the E antigens. | \nStorage. Rh blood-grouping reagents should be kept in sterile |\ncontainers sealed so as to exclude micro-organisms. \n|\nLiquid Rh blood-grouping reagent which do not contain | \nantimicrobial preservative should be kept frozen, preferably | \nat a temperature below-30°. \n|\nLiquid Rh blood-grouping reagents containing an antimicrobial | \npreservative should be kept at a temperature between 2° and । \n8° ; they should not be frozen unless the antimicrobial | \npreservative has been shown to be innocuous to the reagent | \nin the Frozen state.\nDried Rh blood-grouping reagents should be kept at a \ntemperature not higher than 20°. \nI\nLabelling. The label states (l) the name “IgM Anti-D blood- j \ngrouping reagent”, “IgG Anti-D blood-grouping reagent , I\n2.8.12. ASSAY OF HUMAN ANTI-THROMBIN III\n[P.2022\n“Anti-C blood-grouping reagent” or “Anti-E blood-grouping \nreagent” as appropriate; (2) the agglutination titre; (3) the \nbatch number; (4) the date after which the preparation may\nt be expected to retain its activity; (5) the storage \nconditions; (6) for a liquid reagent containing an "},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rh Blood-Grouping Reagents\nSource. Rh blood-grouping reagents are derived from the sera \nor defibrinated plasma of one or more persons immunised by \nRh system or from cultures of mammalian lymphocytes. The \nmaterial to be used must be tested with negative results for \nthe presence of hepatitis B surface antigens and anti-bodies \nto HIV I and HIV-H by suitable sensitive methods approved \nby the appropriate authority.\nDescription. Rh blood-grouping reagents may be issued as \nliquids or they may be prepared by reconstitution from the \ndried reagents. Liquid reagents are clear or faintly opalescent, \nyellowish or colourless fluids without turbidity. They may \ncontain a suitable antimicrobial.preservative. Dried reagents \nare pale yellow powders or friable solids.\nRh blood-grouping reagents are of four types, namely IgM \nAnti-D blood-grouping reagent, IgG Anti-D blood grouping \nreagent, Anti-C blood-grouping reagent and Anti-E blood­\ngrouping reagent.\nProperties. Blended IgG and IgM monoclonal reagents are f \npreferably used for Rh blood-grouping in blood banks. Anti- \nD, Anti-C and Anti-E blood-grouping reagent should be shown \nI\nnot to agglutinate or to coat any of a comprehensive panel of \ni\ncorpuscles that do not contain the respective antigens |\nI\nRh blood-grouping reagents, reconstituted -where necessary I \nas stated on the label, comply with the following | \nrequirements. \n|\nPotency. IgManti-D blood-grouping reagent — It contains I\nanti-D as a “saline agglutinin” in such quantities that it gives I \na positive reaction at a dilution of 1 in 32 against corpuscles I \nknown to contain the D antigens. \n|\nIgG anti-D blood-grouping reagent — The potency of IgG |\nanti-D blood-grouping reagent is determined by comparing I \nits “albumin agglutinin” antibody activity with that of the | \nappropriate International Standard of Blood-typing serum or \nI\nwith that of another suitable reference preparation the potency \nI\nof which has been determined in relation to the appropriate I \nInternational Standard. \n|\nThe determination is carried out by simultaneously titrating I \nthe reagent under examination and the reference preparation | \nagainst suspension of human red corpuscles containing the | \nD antigens. \n|\nI \nIgG anti-D blood-grouping reagent contains not less than 32 \n|\nUnits of anti-D antibody per ml, i.e. the titre of the reagent I \nunder examination is not less than one-half of that of the I \nappropriate reconstituted International Standard irrespective | \nof the titre obtained. \nf\nt\nAnti-C blood-grouping reagent—It contains anti-C antibody |\nin such quantities that it gives a positive reaction at a dilution | \nof I in 8 with red corpuscles known to contain the C antigens. |\nAnti-E blood-grouping reagent — It contains anti-E antibody |\nin such quantities that it gives a positive reaction at a dilution । \nof 1 in 8 with red corpuscles known to contain the E antigens. | \nStorage. Rh blood-grouping reagents should be kept in sterile |\ncontainers sealed so as to exclude micro-organisms. \n|\nLiquid Rh blood-grouping reagent which do not contain | \nantimicrobial preservative should be kept frozen, preferably | \nat a temperature below-30°. \n|\nLiquid Rh blood-grouping reagents containing an antimicrobial | \npreservative should be kept at a temperature between 2° and । \n8° ; they should not be frozen unless the antimicrobial | \npreservative has been shown to be innocuous to the reagent | \nin the Frozen state.\nDried Rh blood-grouping reagents should be kept at a \ntemperature not higher than 20°. \nI\nLabelling. The label states (l) the name “IgM Anti-D blood- j \ngrouping reagent”, “IgG Anti-D blood-grouping reagent , I\n2.8.12. ASSAY OF HUMAN ANTI-THROMBIN III\n[P.2022\n“Anti-C blood-grouping reagent” or “Anti-E blood-grouping \nreagent” as appropriate; (2) the agglutination titre; (3) the \nbatch number; (4) the date after which the preparation may\nt be expected to retain its activity; (5) the storage \nconditions; (6) for a liquid reagent containing an antimicrobial \npreservative, the name and concentration of the preservative \nused and that the reagent must not be frozen unless the \npreservative has been shown to be innocuous to the reagent\njn the frozen state; (7) for dried reagents the nature and volume \nofthe liquid to be used for reconstitution.\nMethod- Since the haemagglutinins of the Rh system are \npresent in human blood only in pathological conditions, it is \nnecessary to depend solely on the reaction of the red \ncorpuscles in order to determine the Rh group of a specimen \nofblood. Red corpuscles are obtained either by bleeding into \na 3,0 per cent w/v solution of sodium citrate or by re­\nsuspending in saline solution to give a suspension containing \napproximately 2.0 per cent v/v suspension of packed \ncorpuscles.\nMethod\nIn order to select Rh negative Blood, the untested donor blood\nis first tested with anti-D Rh serum and, in order to conserve \nthese very rare sera, only those bloods which are \nunagglutinated are further tested with anti-C Rh serum and \nanti-E Rh serum.\nFor economical routine work, 0.01 ml volumes each of serum \nand corpuscles suspension are used, though for inexperienced \nworkers, 0.04 ml volumes are preferable. With a Pasteur pipette, \ngraduated at 0.01 ml intervals, 0.01 ml of anti-D Rh serum is \ndeposited at the bottom of lipless test-tubes (50 mm x 6 mm); \n0.01 ml of red corpuscles suspension is then deposited on the \nwall of the tube about 4 mm above the serum and the two are \nmixed and incubated for 2 hours at 37°. Control test of the \nserum are made with D-positive and D-negative corpuscles. \nFor reading, the deposited corpuscles are gently taken up \nwith a Pasteur pipette, spread evenly on a slide with the stem \nofthe pipette, and examined microscopically at a magnification \nofx50 to xIOO. D-positive corpuscles form large clumps whereas \nD-negative corpuscles are unagglutinated. The tests with anti- \nC Rh serum and anti-E Rh serum are made similarly but, since \nthe agglutination "},{"name":"3. REFERENCE DATA","text":"3 1. \nInfra-red Reference Spectra\n3 2. \nThin-Layer Chromatograms of Herbs and Processed Herbs\n33. \nLiquid/HPTLC/Gas Chromatograms of Herbs\n■j\ni"},{"name":"3. REFERENCE DATA","text":"479\nwww.webofpharma.com\n3.1. INFRA-RED REFERENCE SPECTRA\nIP 2022\n3,1,.Infra-red Reference Spectra\nThe spectra were recorded either on a Dispersive Infra-Red \nSpectrophotometer (Dispersive IR) or a Fourier Transform \nInfra-red Spectrophotometer (FTIR). Comparisons can be made \nbetween a spectrum obtained with a dispersive instrument \nand a reference spectrum produced on a FT instrument and \nvice versa. Where a reference spectrum is not included in this \nsection, the spectrum obtained from the substance under \nexamination has to be compared with the spectrum obtained \nfrom the corresponding reference substance.\nSolid state spectra were recorded against air with liquid paraffin \nmulls placed between potassium bromide plates or with pressed\ndiscs, 13 mm in diameter, using potassium bromide or \npotassium chloride. Solution spectra were recorded against a \nsolvent reference using cells with potassium bromide \nwindows. The instrument and the Phase: in which the spectra \nwere recorded are indicated on top of the individual reference \nspectrum.\nFor solution spectra the absorption bands due to the \nsubstance under examination that coincide with strong solvent \nabsorption will not be recorded, as stated in (2.4.6). Solvent\n‘cut-offs’ in the reference spectra in such cases are recorded \nas horizontal straight lines.\n3.1. INFRA-RED REFERENCE SPECTRA\n!P2O22\npolystyrene\nDispersive IR\nPhase: Thin film Thickness 0.05 mm\n/xaaAAaJ lAAMA/t Ia\naaaAA/^ aA/WW\ni1 u v w “ “\nV\n1\n1 If\n4000 \n3600 \n3200 \n2800 \n2400 \n2000\nWavenumber (cm-1)\n3.1. rNFRA-RJED REFERENCE SPECTRA\nIP- 2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ribavirin-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ribavirin Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RIBAVIRIN CAPSULES\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2'4.14) same as \ndescribed. underRelated substances with the= following \nmodification. ■ ■ bv.\nCalculate the content of C8Hi2N4O5. ,\nInject reference solution (c) and the test solution.\nStorage. Store protected from light.' \n''■ :;\nRibavirin Capsules\nRibavirin Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the; stated amount of ribavirin, \nC8HI2N4O5.\nUsual strengths. 100 mg; 200 mg. /"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RIBAVIRIN CAPSULES\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2'4.14) same as \ndescribed. underRelated substances with the= following \nmodification. ■ ■ bv.\nCalculate the content of C8Hi2N4O5. ,\nInject reference solution (c) and the test solution.\nStorage. Store protected from light.' \n''■ :;\nRibavirin Capsules\nRibavirin Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the; stated amount of ribavirin, \nC8HI2N4O5.\nUsual strengths. 100 mg; 200 mg. /"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket), \nMedium. 900 ml of water, . . . .\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest. solution. ■. Use the filtrate, dilute if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium.\nReference solution. A 0.00225 per cent w/v solution of \nribavirin IPRS in the; dissolution medium.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 30 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with a\nstrong cation-exchange resin consisting of sulphonated\n. cross-linked styrene-divinylbenzene copolymer in the\nhydrogen form (9 pm),\n- \n- column temperature: 65°, \n.7\n- mobile phase: water, adjusted to pH 2.5 with sulphuric\nacid,. \\ ,.t ... ; \n/ \n' ' \n'.\n- \nflow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 207. nm,\n- injectionvolume:20ul. •' \n■\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. .\nCalculate the content of CgHjjN^Oj in the medium.\n3480 \nmBImIm\nI\nif 2022 ' I'\nQ. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of f H12N n 7/\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography li \n(2.4.14). \n;• j\nv.' \n' ■\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of the mixed content ? i \ncontaining 50 mg of Ribavirin in 50 ml of the mobile phase • \nwith the aid of ultrasound for 20 minutes with intermittent \nshaking and dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase, filter. S\nReference solution. A 0.0025 per cent w/v solution of ribavirin -W - \nIPRS in the mobile phase. \n; f J\nChromatographic system \n: iff.\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with a iSf\nstrong cation-exchange resin consisting of di \nsulphonated cross-linked styrene-divinylbenzene \ncopolymer in the hydrogen form (7 gm), \n. .’W|\n- \nccdumn temperature: 65°, \nfg\n- mobile phase: water, adjusted to pH 2.5 wiiti sulphuric i t\nv ■■ acid, ' : \n.■' \n.- ■■ ■ f g\n- - flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \nI\n- spectrophotometer set at 207 nm, ..... \ny|\n- injection volume: 10 pl. \n” .jg\nName \nRelative .. Correction . y|\n_________________________ retention time factor .'■'■I\nRibavirin  \n1,0\nRibose triazololc carboxylic acid' 0.7 \n1.43 • c f |\n1 l-p-U-Ribofuranosyl-IH-1,2,4-triazole-3-carboxyIic acid.'* \n1 : \nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the ,1 \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any | \npeak corresponding to ribavirin impurity A is not more than | \n0.05 times the area of principal peak in the chromatogram | \nobtained with the reference solution (0.25 per cent), the area | \nof any other secondary peak is not more than 0.02 times the : | \narea ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with | \nthe reference solution (0.1 per cent) and the sum of the areas | \nof all the secondary, peaks is not more than 0.2 times the area | \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the |\nreference solution (1.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area | \nless than 0.01 times the area of the principal peak in the | \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (0.05 per | \ncent). , \n... ,'.j j |\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). 1 .yjff |\nTest solution. Weigh and mix the content of 20. capsules, j \nDisperse a quantity ofthe mixed content containing 50 mgof | \nRibavirin in 50 ml of the mobile phase, with the aid of ultrasound g \nfor 20 minutes with intermittent shaking and dilute to 100.0 tri .. |f \nwith the mobile phase. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 100.0nn | \nwith the mobile phase. \ng\nReference solution; A 0.0025 per cent w/v solution of ribavirin \nIPRS in the mobile phase. \nda vmAb\nI\n \nV §\nA: g\nIf>2022\nUse chromatographic system as described under Related \nsubstances. \n'\nreference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 1.5 and the relative standard \ndeviation forreplicateinjections is not more than 2.0 per cent,\n[nject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C8H12N4O5 in the capsules.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature \nbetween 15° to 30°.\nRibavirin Inhalation Solution"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Ribavirin Solution for Inhalation\nCatogery. Antiviral.\n(2.4.14).\nRibavirin Inhalation Solution is a sterile solution of Ribavirin \nin Water for Injections. It is prepared by dissolving Ribavirin \nfor Inhalation in the requisite amount of Water for Injections.\nRibavirin for Inhalation\nRibavirin for Inhalation is a sterile ribavirin consisting of \nRibavirin with or without excipients. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe inhalation solution is constituted by dissolving the \ncontents of the sealed container in the requisite amount of \nsterilewaterfor injection, immediately before use.\nThe'constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nCldri^ &f solution and particulate matter stated under"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ribavirin-inhalation-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ribavirin Inhalation Solution"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ribavirin Inhalation Solution\nRibavirin Solution for Inhalation\nCatogery. Antiviral.\nRibavirin Inhalation Solution is a sterile solution ofRibavirin\nin Water for Injections. It is prepared by dissolving Ribavirin\nfor Inhalation in the requisite amount ofWater for Injections.\nRibavirin for Inhalation\nRibavirin for Inhalation is a sterile ribavirin consisting of\nRibavirin with or without excipients. It is filled in a sealed\ncontainer.\nThe inhalation solution\nis constituted by dissolving the\ncontents of the sealed container in the requisite amount of\nsterile water for injection, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and particulate matter stated under\nInhalation Preparations.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nRibavirin Inhalation Solution contains not less than 95.0 per\ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of\nCSH12N40 s.\nThe content of the sealed container complies with the\nrequirements stated under Inhalation Preparations and with\nthe following requirements."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Ribavirin Inhalation Solution\nRibavirin Solution for Inhalation\nCatogery. Antiviral.\nRibavirin Inhalation Solution is a sterile solution ofRibavirin\nin Water for Injections. It is prepared by dissolving Ribavirin\nfor Inhalation in the requisite amount ofWater for Injections.\nRibavirin for Inhalation\nRibavirin for Inhalation is a sterile ribavirin consisting of\nRibavirin with or without excipients. It is filled in a sealed\ncontainer.\nThe inhalation solution\nis constituted by dissolving the\ncontents of the sealed container in the requisite amount of\nsterile water for injection, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and particulate matter stated under\nInhalation Preparations.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nRibavirin Inhalation Solution contains not less than 95.0 per\ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of\nCSH12N40 s.\nThe content of the sealed container complies with the\nrequirements stated under Inhalation Preparations and with\nthe following requirements."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel G.\nMobile phase. A mixture of20 volumes of 0.1 M ammonium\nchloride and 90 volumes of acetonitrile.\nTest solution. Dissolve ribavirin inhalation solution containing\nabout 0.1 g ofRibavirin in 10 ml ofwater.\nReference solution. A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of ribavirin\nRS in water.\nApply to the plate 10 III ofeach solution. After development,\ndry the plate in air for 15 minutes, and spray the plate with the\nmixture of0.5 ml ofanisaldehyde, 0.5 ml ofsulphuric acid, 0.1\nml ofglacial acetic acidand 9 ml ofethanol (95per cent) and\nheat at 105° for 40 minutes and allow to cool.The principal\nRlBAVIRlN INHALATION SOLUTION\nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution is\nsimilar in position, colour and size to that in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution.\nB. In the Assay, the retention time ofthe principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to\nthat ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with\nthe reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of ribavirin in the\nmobile phase.\nReference solution (a). A 0.000125 per cent w/v solution of\nribavirin in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of\nribavirin RS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 10 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with\nstrong cation-exchange resin of sulphonated, cross-\nlinked styrene-divinylbenzene co-polymer in the\nhydrogen form (7 to 11 Ilm)(Such asArninexHPAH),\ncolumn temperature. 40°,\nmobile phase: water, adjusted to pH 2.5 with sulphuric\nacid,\nflow rate. 1ml perminute,\n-\nspectrophotometer set at 207 urn,\n-\ninjection volume. 10 Ill.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the\ntailing factor for the principal peak is not less than 0.7 and not\nmore than 1.5.\nInject the test solution and reference solution (a). In the\nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area ofany\nsecondary peak is not more than the area ofprincipal peak in\nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.25\nper cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks\nis not more than 4 times the area ofthe principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (1.0 per\ncent).\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Reconstitute the contents of one container as\ninstructed on the label using an accurately measured volume\nof diluent, dilute a suitable volume of the solution to 200\nvolumes with the mobile phase to\nproduce a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent w/v of Ribavirin, mix and dilute 5\nvolumes of the\nresulting solution to 20 volumes with the\nmobile phase.\nRlBOFLAVINE\nReference solution. A 0.025 per cent w/v solution ofribavirin\nRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 10 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with\nstrong cation-exchange resin of sulphonated, cross-\nlinked styrene-divinylbenzene co-polymer in the\nhydrogen form (7 to 11 J.Lm) (Such as Aminex HPAH),\n-\ncolumn temperature. 40°,\n-\nmobile phase: water, adjusted to pH 2.5 with sulphuric\nacid,\n-\nflow rate. 1ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 207 urn,\n-\ninjection volume. 10 J.Ll.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\ntailing factor is not less than 0.7 and not more than 1.5.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content of CgHI2N40s in inhalation solution.\nLabelling. The label of the sealed container states (1) the\nprocedure for preparing the solution, (2) the delivery system\nto be used for the constituted solution (3) the conditions\nunder which the constituted solution should be stored."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ribavirin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ribavirin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RIBAVIRIN\nIP 2022\nRibavirin\nCiHaNM:\" ' ' . \nMol.Wt 244.2\nRibavirin is i-P-D-ribofuranosyl-l#-l,2,4-triazole-3- \ncarboxamide.\nRibavirin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CSH12N4O5, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder. It \nshowspolymorph ism (2.5.11)."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RIBAVIRIN\nIP 2022\nRibavirin\nCiHaNM:\" ' ' . \nMol.Wt 244.2\nRibavirin is i-P-D-ribofuranosyl-l#-l,2,4-triazole-3- \ncarboxamide.\nRibavirin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CSH12N4O5, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder. It \nshowspolymorph ism (2.5.11)."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with ribavirin IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of ribavirin.\nTests’.';'//7.\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 6.5, determined in a 2.0 per cent w/v solution \nincarbondioxide-freewater.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). - 3 7° to - 33°, determined in \na 1.0 per cent w/v solution, use freshly prepared solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(24:14).\nTest solution.Dissolve 50 mg of substance under examination \nin70 ml o£ water and dilute to 100.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (a). In order to produce l-j3-D-rib- \nofiiranosyl-l/Z-l ,2,4-triazole-3-carboxylic acid (ribavirin \nimpurityA)insitu, mix; 5,0 ml of the test solution and 5.0 ml of \na A2 per cent w/v solution of sodium hydroxide and allow to \nstand for 90minutes. Neutralise with 5.6 ml of a 10.3 per cent \nw^v so^uribn of hydrochloric acid and mix well.\nReference solution (b) Dilute 1.0 ml ofthe test solution to\nIM-Oml with water. Dilute l.Omlofthe solution to 10.0ml with \nwater.\nReferencesolution (c) A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of \nribavirin IRRS in water.\nChromatographic system ;,\n- a stainless steel column? 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nend-capped octadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica \n(3 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. dissolve 1.0 g of anhydrous sodium\nsulphate in 950 ml of water, add 2.0 ml of a 5 per cent \nv/v solution of orthophosphoric acid, adjusted to pH\n2.8 with a 5 per cent v/v solution of orthophosphoric \nacid and dilute to 1000 ml with water,\nB. 5 volumes of acetonitrile and 95 \nvolumes of mobile phase A, \n< \n, / :\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 220 nm,\n- injection volume: 5 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent y/v)\n0\n100\n0\n15\n100\n0\n25\n0\n100\n35\n0\n100\n38\n100\n0\nThe relative retention time with reference to ribavirin (retention \ntime is about 6 minutes) for ribavirin impurity Ais about 0.8. \nThe correction factor for ribavirin impurity A is 2.3.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to ribavirin and ribavirin \nimpurity A is not less than 4.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak due to ribavirin, impurity A is not more than \ntwice the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 per cent). The area of \nany other, secondary peak is not more than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b) (0.1 per cent). The sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 3 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b),(0.3 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less \nthan 0.5 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.05 per cent))\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 4.0 g in 20 ml of water with \nheating if necessary and use 10 ml of the solution. The resulting \nsolution complies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method \nB(10ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 5 hour."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/riboflavin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Riboflavin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Riboflavin\nCalculate the content of Cg H] !NO3,HC1 in the medium.\n^ptifless. than 75 per cent of the stated amount of \n(¿^03»\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(^.4.1;7),rcpating the plate with silica gel Ç.\nMobile phase. Amixture of 65 volumes of acetone, 13 volumes \ntâàiïchloromethane, 13 volumes of tetrahydrofuran and \n9volumesof strong ammonia solution. \n: \n. .\nTeist solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 40 mg of Pyridoxine Hydrochloride with 10.0 ml of \nWer for 15 minutes, filter and use the filtrate.\n^efèrepçe solution. Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n200.0 ml with water, ...\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution; After development, \ndrytheplate: in air and spray with a 5 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium carbonate in a mixture of 70 volumes of water and\n30 volumes of ethanol (95 per cent). Dry it in a current of air, \nspray with a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of 2,6-dichloroquinone- \n4-chloroimide in ethanol (95 per cent) and examine \nimmediately. Any secondary - spot in- the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution is not more intense than the \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets; ; 1 ' '■ . \n■ i;,: \n• \n.................."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Riboflavin\nCalculate the content of Cg H] !NO3,HC1 in the medium.\n^ptifless. than 75 per cent of the stated amount of \n(¿^03»\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(^.4.1;7),rcpating the plate with silica gel Ç.\nMobile phase. Amixture of 65 volumes of acetone, 13 volumes \ntâàiïchloromethane, 13 volumes of tetrahydrofuran and \n9volumesof strong ammonia solution. \n: \n. .\nTeist solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 40 mg of Pyridoxine Hydrochloride with 10.0 ml of \nWer for 15 minutes, filter and use the filtrate.\n^efèrepçe solution. Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n200.0 ml with water, ...\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution; After development, \ndrytheplate: in air and spray with a 5 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium carbonate in a mixture of 70 volumes of water and\n30 volumes of ethanol (95 per cent). Dry it in a current of air, \nspray with a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of 2,6-dichloroquinone- \n4-chloroimide in ethanol (95 per cent) and examine \nimmediately. Any secondary - spot in- the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution is not more intense than the \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets; ; 1 ' '■ . \n■ i;,: \n• \n.................."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Powder one tablet, add 50 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid and \nheat on a water-bath for 15 minutes, swirling occasionally. \nCool, dilute to 100.0 ml with 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, filter, \ndiscarding the first 20 ml of the filtrate. If necessary, dilute \nquantitatively and stepwise with 01 M hydrochloric acid to \nproduce a solution containing 10 pg of the pyrjdoxine \nhydrochloride per ml and measure the absorbance of the\nresulting solution at the maximum at about 290 nm (2.4.7). \nCalculate the content of CgHuHOj, HCÏ taking 430 as the"},{"name":"Tests","text":"specific absorbance at 290 nm,\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nRIBOFLAVIN\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity of the \npowder containing about 20 mg of Pyridoxine Hydrochloride, \nadd 50 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric aezd and heat on a water­\nbath for 15 minutes, swirling occasionally. Cool, dilute to\n100.0 ml with 0.1 M hydrochloric acid and filter, discarding \nthe first 20 ml of the filtrate. Dilute 5 .0 ml of the filtrate to\n100.0 ml with 0.1 M hydrochloric acid and measure the \nabsorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at about \n290 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of CgHnNO3, HC1 taking \n430 as the specific absorbance at 290 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nLactoflavin; Vitamin B2\nCH2OH\nHO-C-H\nHO—C—H\nHO-G-H\nCH2\nH3C^^N N^O \nHaC-^N^n\no\nC17H20N4O6 \nMol.Wt.376.4\nRiboflavin is 3,10-dihydro-7,8-dimethyl-10-[(25',35',4j?)- \n2,3,4,5-tetrahydroxypentyl]benzo[g]pteridine-2,4-dione.\nRiboflavin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C17H2oN406, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. B-group vitamin.\nDescription. A yellow to orange-yellow, crystalline powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with riboflavin 1PRS \nor with the reference spectrum of riboflavin.\nB. Dissolve about 1 mg in 100 ml of wafer. The solution has a \npale greenish yellow colour by transmitted light and an intense \nyellowish green fluorescence by reflected light, which \ndisappears on addition of mineral acids or alkalis..\npH (2.4.24). 5.5 to 7.2, determined in a saturated solution.\nof Cl7I-l20N,.()(, taking 328 as the specific \n^^eat444nm. \n• \n; ■ / ' ’\ngiboflavin Sodium Phosphate\n¿jbpfl^yine-5-phosphate (Sodium Salt); Vitamin B2 \nSodim Phosphate\n■■■■ o\nH2C-O-P-ONa . \nHO-C -H OH ■ \nho-c-h\n■■UT. ’S.' \nHO-C-H\nÜ^füstlev \nCH2\nV-e • ' \n, 2H2O ; ; ”\nC^^NaO9K2H2O \n/ Mol. Wt. 514.4\nRiboflavin Sodium Phosphate is monosodium 3,10-dihydro- \n7,8-dimethyl-10-[(25\\3V47\\!)-2,3,4-trihydroxypcrityl] \nbenzo[g]pteridme-2,4-dione 5-phosphate dihydrate.\nRiboflavin Sodium Phosphate contains the equivalent of not \nless than 73.0 per cent and not more than 79.0 per cent of \nC17H,0N4O6;calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. B-group vitamin.\nDescription. A yellow to orange-yellow, crystalline powder; \nhygroscopic."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \nO.Oftkpèf cent w/v solution ixvphosphate buffer pH 7.0 shows\nan absorption maximum at about 267 nm; absorbance at about \n267hm,058to0.64.\nB. Dissolve about 10 mg in sufficient 2 Msodium hydroxide\nto produce 100 ml, expose 1 ml to ultraviolet light at 254 nm for \n5 minutes, add sufficient 5 Macetic acid to make the solution\nacidic Uy litmus paper and shake the mixture with 2 ml of \nd‘chtoromethane; the lower layer exhibits a yellow \nfluorescence;,',\nC-To O.5 g add 10 ml of nitric acid, evaporate the mixture to \ndryness on a water-bath, ignite the residue until the carbon is\nAmoved, dissolve the final residue in 5 ml of water and filter. \nThe filtrate gives the reactions of sodium salts and reaction \n(B) of phosphates (2.3.1).\nRiboflavin.\nRIBOFLAVIN SODIUM PHOSPHATE\nTests • \n\" }\npH (2.4.24). : 4.0 to 6.3, determined?in;a?2.0?per cent w/y \nsolution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22).+38.0° to+42.0°, determined \nin a 1.5 per cent w/v’ solution in 5 M hydrochloric acid.\nLumiflavine. Shake 35 mgwith 10 ml of dichloromethane for \n5 minutes and filter. The filtrate is not more intensely coloured \nthan reference solution BYS6 (2.4.1).\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nNOTE — Protect the solutions from actinic light.\nTest, solution. Dissolve 100 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 50 ml of water and dilute to 100.0 ml with the \nmobile phase. Dilute 8.0 mi of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution (a): Dissolve 60 mg of riboflavin IPRS in\n1ml of hydrochloric acid and dilute to 250.0 ml with water. \nDilute 4.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 100 mg of riboflavin sodium \nphosphate IPRS in 50 ml of water and dilute to 100.0 ml with\nthe mobile phase. Dilute 8.0 ml of thè solution to 5Ò.0 ml with \nthe mòbile phase.\nChromatographic system\n™ ■ a stain less steel column 25, cm, x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica,(5: pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 85 volumes of 0.73 per cent\nw/v solution, of potassium dihydrogen ortho­\nphosphate and 15 volumes of methanol,\n, - • flow rate: 2 ml per minute, \n... .\n- spectrophotometer, set at 266 nm,\n- injection volume: 100 p.1.\nName \n' ■ ■ •\"\nRelative \nretention time\nRiboflavin impurity A1\n0.2 ,\nRiboflavin impurity B2\n0.3\nRiboflavinimpurity C3 \nV\n0.5\nRiboflavin 3 '-monophosphate\n0.7\nRiboflavin 4'-monophosphate\n0.9\nRiboflavin 5'-monophosphate (Retention \ntime: about 20 minutes)\n1.0\nRiboflavin impurity D4\n2.0\n’riboflavin 3', 4'-diphosphate,\nRiboflavin 3', 5'-diphosphate,\nRiboflavin 4', 5'-diphosphate,\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peak due to riboflavin 4'- \nmonophosphate and riboflavinS'-monophosphate is not less \nthanl.5. \n■ n-u-\nCalculate the percentage content of free riboflavin (impurity \nD) and of riboflavin inthe form of the diphosphates of \nriboflavin (impurities. A,B,C) from the areas of the peaks in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution arid amount of \nfree riboflavin in reference solution (a). \n'\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution,the areaof any \npeak corresponding to riboflavin impurity D is not more than \nthe area of principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (6.0 per cent) and the sum of the areas of \nimpurity A, B arid C is not more than the area of the principal \npeak the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a)\n(6.0 per cent). \n: \n1\nInorganic phosphate. Not more than 1.5 per cent, determined \nby the following method. Dissolve O’. 1 g in sufficient water to \nproduce 100 ml.- Dilute 5 ml with 10 ml of waterand add 5 ml of\nbuffered cupric sulphate solution pH 4.0; 2 ml of a 3 per cent \nw/v solution ofammonium molybdate, I ml.,of a freshly \nprepared solution, containing 2 per cent w/v. of 4-methyl- \naminophenol sulphate and 5 per cent w/v of sodium meta­\nbisulphite and 1 ml of a 3 per cent v/v solution of perchloric \nacid. Add sufficient water to produce 25 ml, mix and measure \nthe absorbance pf the resulting solution at the maximum at \nabout 800 nm (2.4.7), within 15 minutes of'its preparation, \nusing as the Blank a solution prepared in-the same manner but \nomitting the substance under examination: The absorbance is \nnot greater-than that produced by repeating the operation \nusing a solution prepared in the same manner using 15 ml of \nphosphate standard solution (5 pp m P OJ and beginning at\nthe words “add 5 ml of buffered cupric sulphate solution \npH4.0f \n' \n--.A\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). To 2.0 g in a silica crucible add 2 ml of \nnitric acid dropwise followed by 0.25 ml of sulphuric acid. \nHeat cautiously until white fumes are evolved and ignite. Extract \nthe cooled residue with two quantities, each of 2 ml, of \nhydrochloric acid and evaporate the extracts to dryness \nDissolve the residue in 2 ml of 2 M acetic acid, and dilute to \n20 ml with water. 12 ml of the solution complies with the \nlimit test for heavy metals, Method D (10 ppm). Use 1.0 nil \nof lead standard solution (10 ppm Pb) to prepare the \nstandard. \n■ -\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 8.0 per cent, determined \non 0.5 gby drying in an oven at 100° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7kPafor5hours.\nAssay. Carry out the procedure protected from light.\nDissolve 0.1 g in 150 ml of water, add, 2 ml of glacial acetic \nacid and dilute to 1000.0 ml with water. To 10.0 ml add 3.5 ml of\nRIBOFLAVIN SODIUM PHOSPHATE\nÏP2022\na .1.4 percent w/v, solution of sodium acetate:, dilute to 50 0 i \nwith water and measure the absorbance of the result! \nsolution at the maximum at about 444 nm (2.4i7). Calculate the \npercentage content of C!7H20N4O6 taking 328 as the specific \nabsorbance at 444 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nRiboflavin Tablets"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/riboflavine-sodium-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Riboflavine Sodium Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RIBOFLAVINE SODIUM PHOSPHATE"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RIBOFLAVINE SODIUM PHOSPHATE"},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 6.3, determined in a 2.0 per cent w/v\nsolution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). +38.0° to +42.0°, determined\nin a 1.5 per cent w/v solution in 5 M hydrochloric acid.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). To 2.0 g in a silica crucible add 2 ml of\nnitric acid dropwise followed by 0.25 ml of sulphuric acid.\nHeat cautiously until white fumes are evolved and ignite. Extract\nthe cooled residue with two quantities, each of 2 ml, of\nhydrochloric acid and evaporate the extracts to dryness.\nDissolve the residue in 2 ml of2 M acetic acid and dilute to\n20 ml with water. 12 ml of the solution complies with the\nlimit test for heavy metals, Method D (10 ppm). Use 1.0 ml\nof lead standard solution (10 ppm Pb) to prepare the\nstandard.\nLumiflavine. Shake 35 mg with 10 ml ofdichloromethane for\n5 minutes and filter. The filtrate is not more intensely coloured\nthan reference solution BYS6 (2.4.1).\nInorganic phosphate. Not more than 1.5 per cent, determined\nby the following method. Dissolve 0.1 g in sufficient water to\nproduce 100mI. Dilute 5rnl with 10rnl ofwater and add 5 mI of\nbufferedcupric sulphate solutionpH4.0,2 ml ofa 3 per cent\nw/v solution of ammonium molybdate, 1 ml of a freshly\nprepared solution containing 2 per cent w/v of 4-methyl-\naminophenol sulphate and 5 per cent w/v of sodium meta-\nbisulphite and 1 ml of a 3 per cent v/v solution ofperchloric\nacid. Add sufficient waterto produce 25 ml, mix and measure\nthe absorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at\nabout 800 nm (2.4.7), within 15 minutes of its preparation,\nusing as the blank a solution prepared in the same manner but\nomitting the substance under examination. The absorbance is\nnot greater than that produced by repeating the operation\nusing a solution prepared in the same manner using 15 ml of\nphosphate standard solution (5 ppm PO,J and beginning at\nthe words \"add 5 ml of buffered cupric sulphate solution\npH 4.0,\"\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 8.0 per cent, determined\non 0.5 g by drying in an oven at 100° at a pressure not exceeding\n0.7 kPa for 5 hours.\nAssay. Carry out the procedure protectedfrom light.\nWeigh accurately about 0.1 g, dissolve in 150 ml ofwater, add\n2 ml ofglacial acetic acidand dilute to 1000.0 ml with water.\nTo 10.0 m1 add 3.5 ml ofa 1.4 per cent w/v solution ofsodium\nacetate, dilute to 50.0 ml with water and measure the\nabsorbance ofthe resulting solution at the maximum at about\n444 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the percentage content ofC17H2oN406\ntaking 328 as the specific absorbance at 444 nm.\n•\nStorage. Store protected from light."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/riboflavine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Riboflavine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RIBOFLAVINE TABLETS\nRiboflavin Tablets\nLactoflavin Tablets; Vitamin Bz Tablets\nRiboflavin Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not\nmore than 115.0 per cent ofthe stated amount ofriboflavine,\nC17HzoN406.\nUsual strengths. 2 mg; 5 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RIBOFLAVINE TABLETS\nRiboflavin Tablets\nLactoflavin Tablets; Vitamin Bz Tablets\nRiboflavin Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not\nmore than 115.0 per cent ofthe stated amount ofriboflavine,\nC17HzoN406.\nUsual strengths. 2 mg; 5 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 1 mg\nof Riboflavine with 100 ml of water and filter; the filtrate\nhas a pale greenish yellow colour by transmitted light\nand an intense yellowish green fluorescence by reflected\nlight, which disappears on addition of mineral acids or\nalkalis."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Uniformity of content. Comply with the test stated under\nTablets.\nPowder one tablet, add a mixture of 2.5 ml ofglacial acetic\nacid and 50 ml of water and heat on a water-bath for\n1 hour with occasional stirring. Dilute with 50 ml of water,\nadd 15 ml of 1 M sodium hydroxide with continuous\nstirring and then sufficient water to produce a solution\ncontaining 10 llg of Riboflavine per ml. Filter and discard\nthe first few ml of the filtrate. On the clear filtrate carry out\nthe Assay beginning at the words \"Measure the\nabsorbance....\".\nOther tests. Compiy with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. earlY out the procedure in subdued light.\nWeigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh accurately a quantity of\nthe powder containing about 10 mg of Riboflavine, add a\nmixture of5 ml ofglacial acetic acidand 100 ml ofwater and\nheat on a water-bath for 1hour with occasional shaking. Dilute\nwith 50 ml ofwater, cool, add 30 ml of1 M sodium hydroxide\nwith continuous stirring. Add sufficient water to produce\n1000.0mI, mixand filter, discardingthe first few mI ofthe filtrate.\nMeasure the absorbance ofthe filtrate at the maximum at about\n444nm(2.4.7).\nCalculate the content ofC17HzoN406 taking 328 as the specific\nabsorbance at 444 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ribose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ribose"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ribose\n■pest solution. In a volumetric flask of suitable volume take a \npreparation of a solution containing about 5 mg per ml of dry \npolysaccharide. Transfer the contents of a container\nquantitatively to the flask and dilute to volume with water. \nDilute the solution so that the volumes used in the test contain\n7 5 mg to 25 mg of ribose. Introduce 0.20 to 0.40 ml of the \ndiluted solution into tubes in triplicate.\nReference solutions. Dissolve 25 mg of ribose in water and \ndilute to 100.0 ml with the same solvent (stock solution \ncontaining 0.25 g per litre of ribose). Immediately before use, \ndilute 1 ml of the stock solution to 10.0 ml with water (working \ndilution: 25 mg per litre of ribose). Introduce 0.10 ml, 0.20 ml, \n0.40 ml, 0.60ml, 0.80 ml and 1.0ml ofthe working dilution into\nsix tubes.\nPrepare a blank using 2 ml of water.\nMake up to volume in each tube to 2 ml with waler. Shake well \nand add 2 ml of a 0.5 g per litre solution offerric chloride in \nhydrochloric acid to each tube. Shake, add 0.2 ml of a 100 g \nper litre solution of orcinol in absolute ethanol. Place the \ntubes in a water-bath for 20 minutes. Cool in iced water. \nMeasure the absorbance (2.4.7) of each solution at 670 nm \nusing the blank as the compensation liquid. Draw a calibration \ncurve from the absorbance readings for the six reference \nsolutions and the corresponding content of ribose and read \nfrom the curve the quantity of ribose in the test solution for \neach volume tested. Calculate the mean of the three values.\nSialic Acid\nTest solution. Transfer quantitatively the contents of one or \nseveral containers to a volumetric flask of a suitable volume \nso that the resulting solution will give a concentration of about \n250 pg per ml ofpolysaccharide and dilute with water to make \nup the required volume. With the help of a syringe, transfer \n4 ml of this solution to a 10 ml ultra filtration cell suitable for \nthe passage of molecules of relative molecular mass less than \n50,000. Rinse the syringe twice with water and transfer the \nrinsings to the ultrafiltration cell. Carry out the ultrafiltration, \nwith constant stirring, under nitrogen at a pressure of about\n150 kPa. Refill the cell with water each time the volume of \nliquid in it has decreased to I ml and continue until 200 ml has \nbeen filtered and the remaining volume in the cell is about\n2 ml. Using a syringe, transfer this residual liquid to a 10 ml \nvolumetric flask. Wash the cell with three quantities, each of \n2 ml, of water, transfer the washings to the flask and dilute to \n10.0 ml with water (test solution). In each of two test-tubes \nplace 2.0 ml of the test solution.\nReference solutions. Use the reference solutions prescribed\nin the monograph.\nPrepare two series of three test-tubes, place in the tubes of \neach series 0.5 ml, 1.0 ml and 1.5 ml respectively, ofthe reference \nsolution corresponding to the type of vaccine under \nexamination and adjust the volume in each tube to 2 ml with \nwater. Prepare blank solutions using 2 ml of water in each of \ntwo test-tubes. To all the tubes add 5.0 ml of resorcinol \nreagent. Heat at 105° for 15 minutes, copl in cold water and \ntransfer the tubes to a bath of iced water. To each tube add \n5 ml of isoamyl alcohol and mix thoroughly. Place in the bath \nof iced water for 15 minutes. Centrifuge the tubes and keep \nthem in the bath of iced water until the examination by \nabsorption spectrophotometry is made. Measure the \nabsorbance (2.4.7), of each supernatant solution at 580 nm \nand 450 nm using isoamyl alcohol as the compensation liquid. \nFor each wavelength, calculate the absorbance as the mean of \nthe values obtained with two identical solutions. Subtract the \nmean value for the blank solution from the mean values \nobtained for the other solutions. Draw a graph showing the \ndifference between the absorbances at 580 to 450 nm of the \nreference solution as a function of the content of \nN-acetylneuraminic acid and read from the graph the quantity\nof N-"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Ribose\n■pest solution. In a volumetric flask of suitable volume take a \npreparation of a solution containing about 5 mg per ml of dry \npolysaccharide. Transfer the contents of a container\nquantitatively to the flask and dilute to volume with water. \nDilute the solution so that the volumes used in the test contain\n7 5 mg to 25 mg of ribose. Introduce 0.20 to 0.40 ml of the \ndiluted solution into tubes in triplicate.\nReference solutions. Dissolve 25 mg of ribose in water and \ndilute to 100.0 ml with the same solvent (stock solution \ncontaining 0.25 g per litre of ribose). Immediately before use, \ndilute 1 ml of the stock solution to 10.0 ml with water (working \ndilution: 25 mg per litre of ribose). Introduce 0.10 ml, 0.20 ml, \n0.40 ml, 0.60ml, 0.80 ml and 1.0ml ofthe working dilution into\nsix tubes.\nPrepare a blank using 2 ml of water.\nMake up to volume in each tube to 2 ml with waler. Shake well \nand add 2 ml of a 0.5 g per litre solution offerric chloride in \nhydrochloric acid to each tube. Shake, add 0.2 ml of a 100 g \nper litre solution of orcinol in absolute ethanol. Place the \ntubes in a water-bath for 20 minutes. Cool in iced water. \nMeasure the absorbance (2.4.7) of each solution at 670 nm \nusing the blank as the compensation liquid. Draw a calibration \ncurve from the absorbance readings for the six reference \nsolutions and the corresponding content of ribose and read \nfrom the curve the quantity of ribose in the test solution for \neach volume tested. Calculate the mean of the three values.\nSialic Acid\nTest solution. Transfer quantitatively the contents of one or \nseveral containers to a volumetric flask of a suitable volume \nso that the resulting solution will give a concentration of about \n250 pg per ml ofpolysaccharide and dilute with water to make \nup the required volume. With the help of a syringe, transfer \n4 ml of this solution to a 10 ml ultra filtration cell suitable for \nthe passage of molecules of relative molecular mass less than \n50,000. Rinse the syringe twice with water and transfer the \nrinsings to the ultrafiltration cell. Carry out the ultrafiltration, \nwith constant stirring, under nitrogen at a pressure of about\n150 kPa. Refill the cell with water each time the volume of \nliquid in it has decreased to I ml and continue until 200 ml has \nbeen filtered and the remaining volume in the cell is about\n2 ml. Using a syringe, transfer this residual liquid to a 10 ml \nvolumetric flask. Wash the cell with three quantities, each of \n2 ml, of water, transfer the washings to the flask and dilute to \n10.0 ml with water (test solution). In each of two test-tubes \nplace 2.0 ml of the test solution.\nReference solutions. Use the reference solutions prescribed\nin the monograph.\nPrepare two series of three test-tubes, place in the tubes of \neach series 0.5 ml, 1.0 ml and 1.5 ml respectively, ofthe reference \nsolution corresponding to the type of vaccine under \nexamination and adjust the volume in each tube to 2 ml with \nwater. Prepare blank solutions using 2 ml of water in each of \ntwo test-tubes. To all the tubes add 5.0 ml of resorcinol \nreagent. Heat at 105° for 15 minutes, copl in cold water and \ntransfer the tubes to a bath of iced water. To each tube add \n5 ml of isoamyl alcohol and mix thoroughly. Place in the bath \nof iced water for 15 minutes. Centrifuge the tubes and keep \nthem in the bath of iced water until the examination by \nabsorption spectrophotometry is made. Measure the \nabsorbance (2.4.7), of each supernatant solution at 580 nm \nand 450 nm using isoamyl alcohol as the compensation liquid. \nFor each wavelength, calculate the absorbance as the mean of \nthe values obtained with two identical solutions. Subtract the \nmean value for the blank solution from the mean values \nobtained for the other solutions. Draw a graph showing the \ndifference between the absorbances at 580 to 450 nm of the \nreference solution as a function of the content of \nN-acetylneuraminic acid and read from the graph the quantity\nof N-acetylneuraminic acid (sialic acid) in the test solution."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifabutin-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifabutin Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rifabutin Capsules\nRifabutin Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not\nmore than110.0 per cent of the stated amount of rifabutin,\nj.\nUsual strength. 1,50 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rifabutin Capsules\nRifabutin Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not\nmore than110.0 per cent of the stated amount of rifabutin,\nj.\nUsual strength. 1,50 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A; Disperse; a quantity of capsule content in methanol to \nobtain absolution containing 0.002 per cent w/v, filter and\nexamine in the range 200 to 400 nm(2.4.7); The spectra obtained\nRIFABUTIN CAPSULES\nwith the test solution exhibits the maxima at the same \nwavelength with that ofthe reference solution.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.01 Mhydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume ofthe medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtered solution, suitably diluted with \nthe medium if necessary, at the maximum at about 280 nm \n(2.4.7). Calculate the content of C^H^N^n in the medium \nfrom the absorbance obtained by using a solution bf known \nconcentration of rifabutin IPRS.\nQ. Not less, than 75 per cent of the stated amount of \nC^H^^On.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay using the following \nmodifications.\nInject the test solution. The area of any secondary peak with \na relative retention time of about 0.5,0.6,0.8 or 1.4 with respect \nto rifabutin peak is not more than 1.0 per cent, the area of any \nother secondary peak is not more than 0.5 per cent and the \nsum of the areas of rill the secondary peaks is not more than \n4.5 per cent, calculated by area normalisation.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Mix the contents of 20 capsules. Disperse a \nquantity of powder containing 25 mg of Rifabutin in 5 ml of \nacetonitrile and dilute to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 25 mg ci rifabutin IPRS in 5 \nml of acetonitrile and dilute to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve about 10.0 mg of rifabutin \nIPRS and 2 ml of methanol, add 1 ml of 2 Msodium hydroxide\nand allow to stand for about 4 minutes i Add 1 ml of 2 M \nhydrochloric acid, and. dilute to 50.0 ml with mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column!2.5 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of equal volumes of acetonitrile\nand 0.1 M monobasic potassium phosphate, adjusted \nto pH 6.5 with 2 Msodium hydroxide,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifabutin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifabutin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rifabutin\nH3C‘\nC46H62N4O,! \n■' \nMol.Wt.847.0\nIP 2022 :1\nRifabutin is (95, 12£, 145,-15/?, 165,17/?,18/?,19/?,205,21$ ? \n,22E’,24Z)-6,16,18,20-Tetrahydroxy-1 ’isobutyl-14-methoxy. ■ \n7,9,15,17,19,21,25-heptamethyIspirol[9,4-(epoxypentadeca f \n[1,1 l,13]trienimino)-27/-luro[22,32:7,8]naphthfl,2-t/jimidazo]e^ \n2,42 -pipcrid ine]-5,10,26-(3/7,9//)-trione-16-yl- acetate. ' ! 4\nRifabutin contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0percentofC46H62N4Oli calculated on the anhydrous ■\nbasis. \n■■ ■: c-y--, ■ ■ ■;\nCategory. Antituberculosis. \n-y ; y\nDescription. An amorphous red-violet powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rifabutin\nH3C‘\nC46H62N4O,! \n■' \nMol.Wt.847.0\nIP 2022 :1\nRifabutin is (95, 12£, 145,-15/?, 165,17/?,18/?,19/?,205,21$ ? \n,22E’,24Z)-6,16,18,20-Tetrahydroxy-1 ’isobutyl-14-methoxy. ■ \n7,9,15,17,19,21,25-heptamethyIspirol[9,4-(epoxypentadeca f \n[1,1 l,13]trienimino)-27/-luro[22,32:7,8]naphthfl,2-t/jimidazo]e^ \n2,42 -pipcrid ine]-5,10,26-(3/7,9//)-trione-16-yl- acetate. ' ! 4\nRifabutin contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0percentofC46H62N4Oli calculated on the anhydrous ■\nbasis. \n■■ ■: c-y--, ■ ■ ■;\nCategory. Antituberculosis. \n-y ; y\nDescription. An amorphous red-violet powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rifabutin IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of rifabutin.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a); \n'"},{"name":"Tests","text":"N-isobutylpiperidone. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silicagel G.\nSolvent mixture: Equal volumes of chloroform and methanol\nMobile phase. A mixture of 100 volumes of hexane and 30 \nvolumes of acetone. \n■ \n■\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml ofthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution. Prepare 0.0005,0.001,0.002,0.005 and 0.01 \nper cent w/v solutions of N-isobutylpiperidone IPRS in the \nsolvent mixture.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and expose the plate to iodine vapour for \nabout 5 minutes, then spray with starph solution and allow to \nstand for 5 minutes. No spot in the chromatogram of the test \nsolution at Rf value corresponding to ,V-isobulylpipcridone is \nmore intense than that of the principal spot observed in the \nchromatogram obtained with die reference solution containing \n0.005 per cent solution (0.5 per cent);\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay using the following \nmodifications.\nInject the test solution. The area of any impurity detected-at a \nrelative retention time of about 0.5,0.6,0.8 or 1.4 with respect \nto rifabutin peak is not more than 1.0 per cent, the area of any \nother secondary peak is not more than 0.5 per cent and the \nsum of areas; of all the secondary peaks is not more than \n3.0 per cent, calculated by area normalisation. > A\n1P2022\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 2.5 per cent.\nAssay/petermine by liquid chn)mat(^raPhy (2-4.14).\nTest \nDissolve 25 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 5 ml of acetonitrile and dilute to 50.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Reference solution (a). Dissolve 25 mg ci rifabutin IPRS in 5 \nnd ofaceibhlirile and dilute to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 10 mg of rifabutin IPRS \nand 2 ml of methanol, add 1 ml of 2 Msodium hydroxide and \nallow to stand for about 4 minutesi Add 1 ml of 2 M \nhydrochloric acid, and dilute to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n_ astainless steel column!2.5 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of equal volumes of acetonitrile\nand 0.1 M monobasic potassium phosphate, adjusted \npH6.5 with 2 Msodium hydroxide,\n- flow rate: I ml per minute, \n1spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nThe, relative, retention time with reference to rifabutin for a \nmajor peak for a degradant, two minor peaks for degradants \nareabout0.5,6.6arid 0.8 respectively.\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the rifabutin peak and the degradant \npeak eluting at d relative retention time of about 0.8 is not less \nthan1'3 in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b) and the column efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical \nplates and the relative standard deviation for replicate \ninjectidnkis not more than 2.0 per cent in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a).\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate.the content of C46H62N4O11.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampicin-and-isoniazid-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampicin and Isoniazid Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rifampicin and Isoniazid Tablets\nRifampin andlsomcotinylhydrazid Tablets. \ni |\nRifampicin and Isoniazid Tablets contain not less th'a'ii \n90.0 per cent and. not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated | \namounts of rifampicin, C43H58N4Oi2 and isoniazid, CglfNjO- ■<!\nff 2022\nUsual strength. Rifampicin 50 mg and Isoniazid 150 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rifampicin and Isoniazid Tablets\nRifampin andlsomcotinylhydrazid Tablets. \ni |\nRifampicin and Isoniazid Tablets contain not less th'a'ii \n90.0 per cent and. not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated | \namounts of rifampicin, C43H58N4Oi2 and isoniazid, CglfNjO- ■<!\nff 2022\nUsual strength. Rifampicin 50 mg and Isoniazid 150 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution shows peaks that correspond to the peaks due to \nflfampicfn IPRS an^ isoniazid IPRS in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution.\nTests/;\"./.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, \nSpeed arid time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter promptly \nthrough a membrane filter disc with an average pore diameter \nnot greater than 0.8 pm. Reject the first few ml of the filtrate \nand dilute a suitable volume ofthe filtrate with the medium.\nReference stock-solution.. A solution containing 0,0165 per \ncent of rifampicin IPRS and 0.00825 per cent of isoniazid \nIPRS in the dissolution medium.\nFdrRifampicin — Measure the absorbance, of the sample \nsolution and the reference stock solution suitably diluted with \nthe dissolution medium at 475 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content \nof \niri the medium from the absorbance obtained\nfrom the reference stock solution.\nFor Isoniazid' — Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4J4).\nUse. the reference stock solution and sample solution suitably \ndiluted with a solution of 0.05 M potassium dihydrogen \northophosphate, adjust the pH to 6.2 with 0.1 M sodium \nhydroxide to obtain the reference solution and the test \nsolution, respectively.\nChromatographic system\n. - a stainless steel column 30 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 99 volumes of 0.05 Mpotassium\nL ■: dihydrogen1 ph osphate and 1 volume of acetonitrile\n■ with the pH adjusted to 4.0 £ 0.05 with 2 per cent w/v\nsolution of orthophosphoric acid, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n. - : injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntafling factor is not more than 2.0, the column efficiency innot \nless than 1500 theoretical plates and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent,\n//thereference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C6H7N3O in the dissolution medium.\nRIFAMPICIN AND ISONIAZID TABLETS\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amounts of \nC43II53N4O12 and CdT/N^O. :L;,\nRelated substances. Determine’by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n■ ■ ■■ ' ' .\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before: use.\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent \nw/v solution of citric acid, '23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent \nw/v solution of potassium dihydrogen phosphate, 77 volumes \nof a 17.42 per cent w/v solution of dipbtassium hydrogen \nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and 250 volumes of\nacetonitrile. \n' ■ -■\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 200 mg of Rifampicin in 100 ml of acetonitrile and \nfilter. Dilute 5 ml ofthe filtrate to.50 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve rifampicin IPRS in \nacetonitrile to obtain a solution containing 0.2 mg per ml. \nDilute 1.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 per cent \nw/v each of rifampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone.IPRS in \nacetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n, a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column temperature 30°,\n- \nmobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution \ncontaining 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid, \n6.19 per cent w/v bisodium perchlorate, 0.59 per cent \nw/v of citric acid and 2.09 per cent w/v of potassium \ndihydrogen phosphate and 3 5 volumes of acetonitrile,\n■ - flow fate: 1.5 ml per minute,1\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe relative retention times are 1.0,0.55,1.25,1.51 and 2.61 for \nrifampicin, rifampicin quinone, rifampicin N-oxide, \n3-formylrifamycin SV isonicotinyl hydrazone and \n3-formylrifamycin SV respectively. Multiply the area of each \nknown impurity by its response factor. The response, factors \nare 1.00, 1.19,1.03, 1.22 and 1.25 for rifampicin, jifampicin \nquinone, rifampicin N-oxide, isonicotinyl hydrazone and \n3-formylrifamycin SV respectively.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between rifampicin and rifampicin quinone is not \nless than 4, the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \npeak due to rifampicin quinone is not more than 4 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with\nRIFAMPICIN AND ISONIAZID TABLETS\nreference solution (a) (4.0 per cent), the area of any peak due \nto rifampicin N-oxide is no t more thanl.5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (1.5 per cent), the area of any peaks due to \n3-formylrifamycin SV and isonicotinyl hydrazone is not more \nthan 5 times the area of the principalpeak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (5.0 per cent) and the \narea ofanypeakduc to: 3-fonnylrifamycin SV is not more than \nthe area of the. principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (1.0 per cent). The area of any other \nsecondary peak is: not morethan 1.5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (1.5 percent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.,\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 1.4 g of disodium hydrogen \northophosphate anhydrous in 1000 ml of water and adjust \nthe pH to 6.8 with dilute phosphoric acid.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity \nof the powder containing about 40 mg of Isoniazid, dissolve \nin 100.0 ml of methanol and dilute to 500.0 mi with the solvent \nmixt"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampicin-and-isoniazidtablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampicin and IsoniazidTablets"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rifampicin and Isoniazid Tablets\nRifampin and IsonicotinylhydrazidTablets\nRifampicin and Isoniazid Tablets contain not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated\namounts ofrifampicin, C43HssN4012 and isoniazid, C6H7N30.\nUsual strength. Rifampicin 50 mg and Isoniazid 150 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rifampicin and Isoniazid Tablets\nRifampin and IsonicotinylhydrazidTablets\nRifampicin and Isoniazid Tablets contain not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated\namounts ofrifampicin, C43HssN4012 and isoniazid, C6H7N30.\nUsual strength. Rifampicin 50 mg and Isoniazid 150 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. In the Assay, the chromatogram obtained with the test\nsolution shows peaks that correspond to the peaks due to\nrifampicin RS and isoniazidRS in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nNOTE -\nPrepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 10 volumes ofa 21.01 per cent\nw/v solution of citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent\nw/v solution ofpotassium dihydrogenphosphate, 77 volumes\nof a 17.42 per cent w/v solution of dipotassium hydrogen\nRIFAMPICIN AND ISONIAZID TABLETS\nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and 250 volumes of\nacetonitrile.\nTest solution. Weigh accurately a quantity of the powdered\ntablets containing200 mg ofRifampicin in 100mlofacetonitrile\nand filter. Dilute 5 ml ofthe filtrate to 50 ml with the solvent\nmixture.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve rifampicin RS in acetonitrile\nto obtain a solution containing 0.2 mg per ml. Dilute 1 mlof\nthis solution to 100 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 per cent\nw/v each of rifampicin RS and rifampicin quinone RS in\nacetonitrile. Dilute 5 ml of this solution to 50 ml with the\nsolvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 rom, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 /lm),\n-\ncolumn temperature 30°,\n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid,\n0.19 per cent w/v ofsodium perchlorate, 0.59 per cent\nw/v of citric acid and 2.09 per cent w/v ofpotassium\ndihydrogen phosphate and 35 volumes of acetonitrile,\n-\nflowrate. 1.5 ml per minute,\n-\nspectrophotometer set at 254 urn,\ninjection volume. 20 Ill.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the\nresolution between rifampicin and rifampicin quinone is not\nless than 4, the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the\ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates.\nInject the test solution and reference solution (a). The relative\nretention times are 1.0,0.55, 1.25, 1.51 and 2.61 for rifampicin,\nrifampicin\nquinone,\nrifampicin\nN-oxide,\n3-formylrifamycin SV isonicotinyl hydrazone and\n3-formylrifamycin SV respectively. Multiply the area ofeach\nknown impurity by its response factor. The response factors\nare 1.00, 1.19, 1.03, 1.22 and 1.25 for rifampicin, rifampicin\nquinone, rifampicin N-oxide, isonicotinyl hydrazone and\n3-formylrifamycin SV respectively.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area\nof any peak due to rifampicin quinone should not be more\nthan 4 times the area ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution (4 per cent), the area of\nany peak due to rifampicin N-oxide should not be more than\n1.5 times the area ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution (1.5 per cent), the area of\nany peaks due to 3-formylrifamycin SV and isonicotinyl\nhydrazone should not be more than 5 times the area of the\nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference\nsolution (5 per cent) and the area of any peak due to\n3-formylrifamycin SV should not be more than the area ofthe\nRIFAMPICIN AND ISONIAZID TABLETS\nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference\nsolution (1.0 per cent). In the chromatogram obtained with the\ntest solution the area of any unknown peak should not be\nmore than 1.5 times the area of the principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (1.5 per\ncent).\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No.2,\nMedium. 900 ml of0.1 M hydrochloric acid,\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume ofthe mediumand filter promptly\nthrough a membrane filter discwith an average pore diameter\nnot greater than 0.8 Ilm. Reject the first few ml ofthe filtrate\nand dilute a suitable volume ofthe filtrate with the medium.\nReference stock solution. A solution containing 0.0165 per\ncent ofrifampicin RS and 0.00825 per cent ofisoniazidRS in\nthe dissolution medium.\nFor rifampicin\nMeasure the absorbance of the sample\nsolution and the reference stock solution suitably diluted with\nthe dissolution medium at 475 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content\nofC43HssN40J2 in the medium from the absorbance obtained\nfrom the reference stock solution.\nFor isoniazid -\nDetermine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nUse the reference stock solution and sample solution suitably\ndiluted with a solution of 0.05 .M potassium dihydrogen\northophosphate, adjust the pH to 6.2 with 0;1 M sodium\nhydroxide to obtain the reference solution and the test\nsolution, respectively.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 30 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 Ilm),\nmobilephase: amixture of99 volumes of0.05 Mpotassium\ndihydrogen phosphate and 1 volume of acetonitrile\nwith the pH adjusted to 4.0 ± 0.05 with 2 per cent w/v\nsolution ofphosphoric acid,\nflow rate. 1ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\ninjection volume. 20 Ill.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\ntailing factor is not more than 2.0, the column efficiency innot\nless than ·1500 theoretical plates and the relative standard\ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content ofC6H1N30 in the dissolution medium;\nD. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amounts of\nC43HssN40J2 and C6H7N30.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 1.4 g of disodium hydrogen\northophosphate anhydrous in 1000 ml of water and adjust\nthe pH to 6.8 with dilute phosphoric acid.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh accurately\na quantity ofthe powder containing about 40 mg ofIsoniazid,\ndissolve in 100.0 ml ofmethanol and dilute to 500.0 m1 with the\nsolvent mixture.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.08 per cent w/v of\nrifampicin RS and 0.04 per cent w/v of isoniazid RS in\nmethanol. Dilute 10.0 ml ofthis solution to 50.0 ml with the\nsolvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsi"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampicin-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampicin Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RIFAMPICIN CAPSULES\nIP 2022\nreference solution (1-0 per cent) and the sum of the areas of \naOy such peaks is not more than 3.5 times the area of the peak \ndue to rifampicin in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution (3.5 percent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1 -0 g by drying in an oven at 80° at a pressure not exceeding"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RIFAMPICIN CAPSULES\nIP 2022\nreference solution (1-0 per cent) and the sum of the areas of \naOy such peaks is not more than 3.5 times the area of the peak \ndue to rifampicin in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution (3.5 percent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1 -0 g by drying in an oven at 80° at a pressure not exceeding"},{"name":"Tests","text":"0.7 kPa for 4 hours.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\n$OTE— Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent\nw/v solution of citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent \nw/v solution of potassium dihydrogen phosphate, 77 volumes \nof a 17.42 per cent w/v solution of dipotassium hydrogen \nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and 250 volumes of\nacetonitrile.\nTest solution. Dissolve 20.0 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of acetonitrile and filter. Dilute 5.0 ml of \nthe filtrate to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.2 per cent w/v of\nrifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile: Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution \nto 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nC43H5JN4O12.\nUse the chromatographic system and the system suitability \nparameters described under the test for Related substances.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculatethecontent of\nStorage. Store protected from light, in an atmosphere of \nnitrogen;\nRifampicin Capsules"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampicin-isoniazid-and-ethambutol-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampicin, Isoniazid and Ethambutol Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rifampicin, Isoniazid and Ethambutol\nTablets\nRifampin, Isonicotinylhydrazid and Ethambutol \nHydrochloride Tablets\nRifampicin, Isoniazid and Ethambutol Tablets contain not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated amounts of rifampicin, C43H58N4O12, isoniazid, C6H7N3O \nand ethambutol hydrochloride C ]oH24N202.2HC1.\nUsual strength. Rifampicin 300 mg, Isoniazid 200 mg and \nEthambutol 200 mg. \n. ; ;'"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rifampicin, Isoniazid and Ethambutol\nTablets\nRifampin, Isonicotinylhydrazid and Ethambutol \nHydrochloride Tablets\nRifampicin, Isoniazid and Ethambutol Tablets contain not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated amounts of rifampicin, C43H58N4O12, isoniazid, C6H7N3O \nand ethambutol hydrochloride C ]oH24N202.2HC1.\nUsual strength. Rifampicin 300 mg, Isoniazid 200 mg and \nEthambutol 200 mg. \n. ; ;'"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. In the Assay for rifampicin and isoniazid, the principal \npeaks in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorrespond to the peaks in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution.\nB. In the Assay for ethambutol hydrochloride the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorresponds to the peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2),\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 mlof 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter promptly \nthrough a membrane filter disc with an average pore diameter \nnot greater than 0.8 pm. Reject the first few ml of the filtrate/\nReferencestock solution. A solution containing 0.0165 per \ncent w/vof rifampicin IPRS, 0.00825 per cent w/v of isoniazid \nIPRS and 0.031 per cent w/v of ethambutol hydrochloride\nIPRS in ■dissolution medium. Keep this reference stock \nsOlutibri in the dissolution bath during the test run.\nfor Rifampicin — Dilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the \nsame solvent. Measure the absorbance (2.4.7) of the resulting \nsolution at the maximum at about 475 nm. Calculate the content \nof \nin the medium from the absorbance obtained\nfrom suitably diluted reference stock solution.\nacetonitrile,\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nlest solution. Suitably dilute the filtered medium with 0.05 M \npotassium dihydrogen orthophosphate with the pH adjusted\nto 6.2 with 0.1, Msodium hydroxide.\nReference solution. Suitably dilute the reference stock solution \nwith 0^5 Mpptqssium dihydrogen orthophosphate with the \npH adjusted to 6.2 with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide..\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 30 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- : mobile phase: a mixture of 99 volumes of 0.05 Mpotassium\ndihydrogen, phosphate and 1 volume of acetonitrile\n. \n■ ^previously adjusted to pH 4.0 with a 2 per cent w/v\nsolution of phosphoric acid,\n~ flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,.\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject'the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0, the column efficiency in not \nless, than ,150,0 .theoretical plates and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C6H7N3O in the dissolution medium.\nFor Ethambutol Hydrochloride — Determine by liquid \nchromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Suitably dilute the filtered medium with 0.05 M \npotassium dihydrogen orthophosphate adjusted to pH 6.2\nwith 0.1 M sodium hydroxide.\nReference solution. Suitably dilute the reference stock solution \nwith 0.05 Mpotassium dihydrogen orthophosphate with the \npH adjusted to 6.2 with 0.1 Msodium hydroxide.\nUse the,chromatographic system described under the Assay\nethambutol hydrochloride.\nCalcuIatethecontcnt ofC10H24N2O2.2HCl inthe medium.\nQ’'Nd£4ess than 75 per cent of the stated amounts of\ncuH58N4O12, C6H7N3O and Cl0H24N2O2.2HCl.\nRIFAMPICIN, ISONIAZID AND ETHAMBUTOL TABLETS\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent \nw/v solution of citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent \nw/v solution of potassium dihydrogen phosphate, 77 volumes \nof a 17.42 per cent w/v solution of dipotassium hydrogen \nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and 250 volumes of\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 200 mg of Rifampicin, dissolve in 100 ml of \nacetonitrile and. filter. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml \nwith the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.02 per cent \nw/v of rifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 1.0 ml of the \nsolution to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 per cent \nw/v each of rifampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS in \nacetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- \ncolumn temperature: 30°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid, \n0.19 per cent w/v of sodium perchlorate, 0.59 per cent \nw/v of citric acid and 2.09 per cent w/v of potassium \ndihydrogen phosphate and 35 volumes of acetonitrile, \n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- injectionvolume:20pl.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid.unless the \nresolution between rifampicin and rifampicin quinone is not \nless than 4, the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. The relative \nretention times are 1.0,0.55,1.25,1.51 and 2.61 for rifampicin, \nrifampicin quinone, rifampicin N-oxide, 3-formylrifamycin SV \nIsonicotinyl hydrazone and 3-formylrifamycin SV respectively. \nMultiply the areas of each known impurity by its response \nfactor. The response factors are 1.00,1.19,1.03,1.22 and 1.25 \nfor rifampicin, rifampicin quinone, rifampicin N-oxide, \nIsonicotinylhydrazone and 3-formylrifamycin SV respectively.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof any peak due to rifampicin quinone should not be more \nthan 4 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (4 per cent), the area of \nany peak due to rifampicin N-oxide should not be more than\nRIFAMPICIN, ISONIAZID AND ETHAMBUTOL TABLETS\n1:5 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (1.5 per cent), the area of \nany peak due to 3-formylrifamycin SV Isonicotinyl hydrazone \nshould not be more than 5 times the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained7 with the reference solution \n( 5 per cent) and the area of any peak due to 3-formylrifamycin \nSV should not bemore than the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the reference solution (1 per \ncent). In the chromatogram1 obtained with the test solution \nthe area of any unknown peak should not be more than\n1.5 times.the .area of the principal pea"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampicin-isoniazid-and-pyrazinamide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampicin, Isoniazid and Pyrazinamide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RIFAMPICIN, ISONIAZID AND PYRAZINAMIDE TABLETS\nZP2022\nIdentified011\nh Assay of rifampicin, isoniazid and pyrazinamide, the \n1 hcipalpeaks in the chromatogram obtained with the test \n^olutibii’correspond to the peaks in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RIFAMPICIN, ISONIAZID AND PYRAZINAMIDE TABLETS\nZP2022\nIdentified011\nh Assay of rifampicin, isoniazid and pyrazinamide, the \n1 hcipalpeaks in the chromatogram obtained with the test \n^olutibii’correspond to the peaks in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pissolutioii (2.5.2).\nApparatus Np-1 (Basket), . . .\nyfedium.OOOnil of a solution containing 2 g of sodium chloride \nand 7.0 ml of hydrochloric acid in 1000 ml of water, with a pH \nofabdutl.2, '\nSpeedandtime. TOOrpm and45 minutes.\nWithdraw a; spitable volume of the medium and filter through \na membrane filterdisc withan average pore diameter not greater \nthan 0.8 prn/Rej ect the first few ml of the filtrate.\nReference stock solution. A solution containing 0.0165 per \ncentw/vofr//uwpri.’irt IPRS, 0.00825 per cent w/v of isoniazid \nIPRS'and 0.04375 per cent w/v ofpyrazinamide IPRS in the \ndissolutiomrnedium and filtered. Keep this reference stock\nsolution in the dissolution bath during the test run.\nJ hriU.-Oi.: \n: \nI-/?:?.\"' '■\nFor Rifampicin — Dilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the\nsame solvent. Measure the absorbance (2.4,7) ofthe resulting \nsolution at the maximum at about 475 nm. Calculate the ebhtent \nof C43H58N4O12 in the medium from the absorbance obtained \nfrom suitably diluted reference stock solution.\nForfsbnidzid^ahd'Pyrazindmide —1 Determine ’by liquid\nchromatography (2.4.14)/ \n: \n.:■ -T ? - »\nNOTE^Use.this solution within.one. hour from prppqratiopi\nTest solution. To 15.0 ml ofthe filtered medium, add 15 ml of \nIM dibasic potassium phosphate, dilute to 100.0 ml with the \nmobile phase and mix. : ■ \n■■ \n. > p- .\nRefer^hcefoldtidn (a). To 15.0'ini of the reference stock \nsolution, add 15- ml of 1 M dibasic potassium phosphate, \ndilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase and mix;\nReference solution (bf To 10.0 mlof a 0.0125 per cent w/v \nsolution of isonicotinic acid in 'the dissolution medium, add \n4.0 ml ofthe reference stock solution and 15 ml of 1 fl dibasic \npotassiumphosphate, dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase\n3ndmix. ; c\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 30 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\nr mobile phaser '< a mixture of 860 volumes of 'water, \n( 1 !100 volumes of / Af monobasic potassium phosphate\n' and 40 volumes of acetonitrile,\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n••• \n.\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 rim,\n- injection volume: 50 pl. 1\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and die test solution. The \nrelative retention times are about 0.7 for isonicotinic acid, 1.0\nfor pyrazinamide and 1.8 for isoniazid.\nThe test is not valid unless in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (b) the resolution between isonicotinic \nacid and pyrazinamide is not less .than 2.5 and between \npyrazinamide and isoniazid is not less than 4.0.\nCalculate the contents of CJLhhO and C5H5N3O in the medium.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amounts of \nC43H58H1Ol2,C6H7N3OandC5H5N3O. \n\\ \n‘\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n. \n- \n: \n...\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent \nw/v solution oi citric acid,, 23. volumes of a 13.61 per cent \nw/v solution of potassium dihydrogenphosphate., 77 volumes \nof 17.42 per cent w/v solution of dipotassium hydrogen \nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and 250 volumes of\nacetonitrile.. \n......\nTest solution.- Weigh a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining about 200 mg of Rifampicin,. dissolve; in\n100 ml of acetonitrile and filter. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to \n50.0 ml with the solvent mixturd.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing . 0.02 per cent \nw/v of rifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 1.0 ml of the \nsolution to a 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (bf A solution containing 0.01. per cent \nw/v each oirifampicln IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS in \nacetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml ofthe solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture. .......\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n-• column temperature: 30°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\n. containing 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid,\n0.19 per epnt w/v of sodiumperchlorate, .0,59 per cent \nw/v of citric acid arid 2.09 per cent w/v of potassium \ndihydrogen phosphate, 35 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flowrate: 1.5 nil per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,.\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between rifampicin and rifampicin quinone is not \nless than 4, the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. The relative \nretention times are 1.0,0.55,1.25,1.51 and,2.61 for rifampicin, \nrifampicin quinone, rifampicinN-oxide, 3-formylrifamycin SV \nIsonicotinyl hydrazone and 3-formylrifamycin SV respectively. \nMultiply the area of each known impurity by its response \nfactor. The response factors are 1.00,1.19,1.03,1.22 and 1.25 \nfor rifampicin, rifampicin quinone, rifampicin N-oxide, \nIsonicotinyl hydrazone and 3-formylrifamycin SV respectively.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof any peak due to rifampicin quinone should not be more \nthan 4 times the area of the principal peak inlhc chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution (4 per cent), the area of \nany peak due to rifampicin N-oxide should not be more than\n1.5 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (1.5 per cent), the area of \nany peak due to 3-formylrifamycin SV Isonicotinyl hydrazone \nshould not be more than 5 times the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(5 per cent) and the area of any peak due to 3-formylrifamycin \nSV should not be more than the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the reference solution (1 per \ncent). In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \nthe area of any unknown peak should not be more than\n1.5 times the area ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the refe"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampicin-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampicin Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rifampicin Oral Suspension\nRifampicin oral suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nrifampicin, C43H5gN40i2.\nUsual strengths. 10 mg per ml; 20 mg per ml."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rifampicin Oral Suspension\nRifampicin oral suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nrifampicin, C43H5gN40i2.\nUsual strengths. 10 mg per ml; 20 mg per ml."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. To a quantity containing 0.1 g of Rifampicin add? 30 ml of ; \nwater and shake with two quantities, each of 50 ml, of / \nchloroform. Dry the combined extracts with anhydrous sodium i \nsulphate, filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at a \ntemperature not exceeding 70°. Wash the residue with 1 ml of ; \nether and dry at 70°. The residue complies with the following ; \ntest. \" \n' \n...........\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (24.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rifampicin .IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of rifampicin.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (a).. ; ."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 4.2 to 4.8.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(24.14). \n‘ \n:\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.....\nSolvent mixture, A mixture of 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent ; \nw/v solution of citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent \nw/v solution ofpotassium dihydrogenphosphate, 77 volumes \nof a 17.42 per cent w/v solution'of dipotassium hydrogen \nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and 250 volumes,of\nacetonitrile. \n; 'f\nTest solution. Add 5 ml of water to a quantity of the'oral ?? \nsuspension containing 20 mg of Rifampicin and extract with <\nQuantities, each of 10 ml, of dichloromethane, filter the \nbined extracts and evaporate to dryness at a temperature \nC°texceeding40°. Dissolve the residue in 10 ml of acetonitrile.\nDilute 5-0 ml of the resulting solution to 50 ml with the solvent\nmixture\nneference sohdion (a). A solution containing 0.02 per cent\nof rifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 1.0 ml of the \nsolution to ail 00.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 per cent\n5y/v each of nfampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS in \nacetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n; a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n~ column temperature: 30°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid,\n0.19 per cent w/v of sodium perchlorate, 0.59 per cent \nw/v of citric acid and 2.09 per cent w/v of potassium \nJ dihydrogen phosphate and 35 volumes of acetonitrile, \n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \n-injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe relative retention times are 1.0, 0.55, 1.25 and 2.61 for \nrifampicin, rifampicin quinone, rifampicin N-oxide and \n3-formylrifamycin SV respectively. Multiply the areas of each \nknown impurity by their response factor. The response factors \nare 1.00,1.19,1.03,1.22 and 1.25 for rifampicin, rifampicin \nquinone, rifampicin N-oxide, Isonicotinyl hydrazone and \n3-formylrifamycin SV respectively."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampicin-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampicin Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Assay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the oral suspension \ncontaining 150.mg,of rifampicin and dilute to 100.0 ml with \nacetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with \nacetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml ofthe resulting solution to 50.0 ml \nwith the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A 0.15 per cent w/v solution of\nrifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml of the solution \nto 50.0 ml with acetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml ofthe resulting\nsolution to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 per cent\nw/v of each of rifampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS \nin acetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\nUse the chromatographic system and the system suitability \nparameters as described under the test for Related substances.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nDetermine the weight per ml of the suspension (2.4.29) and \ncalculate the content of C43H58N4OI2 weight in volume.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nRifampicin Tablets\nRifampicin Tablets contain not less than 92.5 per cent and not \nmore than 107.5 per cent bf the stated amount of rifampicin, \nc43h58n4o12.\nUsual strength. 150 mg.\nA. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 0.15g\nof Rifampicin with 5 ml of chloroform, filter and evaporate the \nfiltrate to dryness. The residue complies with the following \ntest..\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rifampicin IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of rifampicin.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (a).\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, ■■■■■■ \n, '\nSpeed and time. , 100 rpm and 45 minutes. \n, .\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter promptly \nthrough a membrane filter disc having an average pore diameter \nnot greater than 1.0 pm, rejecting the first 1 mlof the filtrate. \nDilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the dissolution medium.\nMeasure the absorbance (2.4.7) of the resulting solution at \nthe maximum at about, .475 nm. Calculate the content of \nC43H58N4O12 in the medium from the absorbance obtained from \na solution of know n concentration of rifampicin IPRS. . :\nQr Nori less than 75; percent’of the stated amount.of \nC43H58N4Ol2. ■ \n- 1\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. 10 volumes of a 21.01, per cent w/v solution \nof citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent solution w/v of \npotassium dihydrogen phosphate, 77 volumes of a 17.42 per\ncent w/v solution of dipotassium hydrogen phosphate, 640 \nvolumes of water and 250 volumes of acetonitrile.\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the,powdered tablets \ncontaining 200 mg of Rifampicin, dissolve in 100 ml of \nacetonitrile,idler. Dilute 5.6 ml'ofthe filtrate to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.02 per cent \nw/v of rifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile^ ‘Dilute 1 nil of the \nsolution to a 100 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference, solution (bf, A solution contaimng. 0.01 percent \nw/v of each qf rifampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS \nin acetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm,■ packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column, temperature. 30°, '\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent V/v of orthophosphoric acid, \n0.19 per cent w/v of sodium perchlorate, 0.59 per cent\n■ • w/v b"},"sections":[{"name":"RIFAMPICIN TABLETS","text":"Assay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the oral suspension \ncontaining 150.mg,of rifampicin and dilute to 100.0 ml with \nacetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with \nacetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml ofthe resulting solution to 50.0 ml \nwith the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A 0.15 per cent w/v solution of\nrifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml of the solution \nto 50.0 ml with acetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml ofthe resulting\nsolution to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 per cent\nw/v of each of rifampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS \nin acetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\nUse the chromatographic system and the system suitability \nparameters as described under the test for Related substances.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nDetermine the weight per ml of the suspension (2.4.29) and \ncalculate the content of C43H58N4OI2 weight in volume.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nRifampicin Tablets\nRifampicin Tablets contain not less than 92.5 per cent and not \nmore than 107.5 per cent bf the stated amount of rifampicin, \nc43h58n4o12.\nUsual strength. 150 mg.\nA. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 0.15g\nof Rifampicin with 5 ml of chloroform, filter and evaporate the \nfiltrate to dryness. The residue complies with the following \ntest..\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rifampicin IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of rifampicin.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (a).\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, ■■■■■■ \n, '\nSpeed and time. , 100 rpm and 45 minutes. \n, .\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter promptly \nthrough a membrane filter disc having an average pore diameter \nnot greater than 1.0 pm, rejecting the first 1 mlof the filtrate. \nDilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the dissolution medium.\nMeasure the absorbance (2.4.7) of the resulting solution at \nthe maximum at about, .475 nm. Calculate the content of \nC43H58N4O12 in the medium from the absorbance obtained from \na solution of know n concentration of rifampicin IPRS. . :\nQr Nori less than 75; percent’of the stated amount.of \nC43H58N4Ol2. ■ \n- 1\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. 10 volumes of a 21.01, per cent w/v solution \nof citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent solution w/v of \npotassium dihydrogen phosphate, 77 volumes of a 17.42 per\ncent w/v solution of dipotassium hydrogen phosphate, 640 \nvolumes of water and 250 volumes of acetonitrile.\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the,powdered tablets \ncontaining 200 mg of Rifampicin, dissolve in 100 ml of \nacetonitrile,idler. Dilute 5.6 ml'ofthe filtrate to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.02 per cent \nw/v of rifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile^ ‘Dilute 1 nil of the \nsolution to a 100 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference, solution (bf, A solution contaimng. 0.01 percent \nw/v of each qf rifampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS \nin acetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm,■ packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column, temperature. 30°, '\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent V/v of orthophosphoric acid, \n0.19 per cent w/v of sodium perchlorate, 0.59 per cent\n■ • w/v bT citric acid and 2.09 peri cent w/v of potassium\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, : z:\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \n: ;\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\ndihydrogenphovVza/e and 35 volumes of acetonitrile,\nThe relative1 retention times are 1.0, 0.55, 1.25 and 2.61 for \nrifampicin, rifampicin quinone, rifampicin N-oxide, and, 3- \nformylrifamycin SV respectively. The response factors are 1.00,\n1.19,1.03 and 1.25 for rifampicin, rifampicin quinone,' rifampicin \nN-oxide and 3-formylrifamycin SV respectively."},{"name":"3488.\nRIFAMPICIN TABLETS","text":"H’2022\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the I \nresolution between rifampicin and rifampicin quinone is not I \nless than 4, the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the I \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates.: .7 f\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution, fii thb 1 \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area5 of any | \npeak due to rifampicin quinone is not more than 4 times the I \narea of the principal peak in! the chromatogram obtained with I \nreference solution (a) (4.0 per cent), the area pf any peak due I \nto rifampicin N-oxide is not more than 1.5 times the area pf the i \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference |\nsolution (a) (1.5 per cent) and the area of any peak due to £ I \nformyl rifamycin SV is not more than the area of principal I \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution | \n(a) (1.0 per cent). The area of any other secondary peak is not \nmore than the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (1.0 per cent); ■ -\n■i\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). ■\nI\n1\nTest solution: Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity \nof the powder containing 300.0 mg bf Rifampicin, dissolve iif \n200.0 ml of acetonitrile, filter. Dilute 10.0 ml of the filtrate to \n50.0 ml with acetonitrile. Further dilute 5.0 ml ofthe solution \nto 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture. \na.\nReference solution (a); A 0.15 per cent w/v solution of \nrifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 mil of the solution \nto 50.0 nil with acetonitrile. Further dilute 5.0 ml ofthe solution \nto 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nI\nReference solution (b).. A solution containing 0.0,1 per cent \nw/v each of rifampicin IPRS andrifampicin quinoa \nacetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml bf the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture. \n' \n.;.:\nUse the chromatographic system and system suitability \nparameters described under the test for Related substances.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the | \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more | \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (a) andthetest solution. ?■\nCalculate the content of C^FbgN^^ in fhe tablets! ’'\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampicin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampicin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RIFAMPICIN :\nThe .relative retention time with reference to rifabutin for a \nmajor peak fora degradant, two minor peaks for degradants \nare about 0.5,0.6 and 0.8 respectively.\nInject reference solution (a): and reference solution (b).\nThe test is hot valid unless the resolution between the rifabutin \npeak and the degradant peak eluting at a relative retention \ntime of about 0.8 is not less than 1.3 obtained with reference \nsolution (b) and the column efficiency is not less than 2000 \ntheoretical plates and the relative standard deviation for \nreplicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.obtained with \nreference solution (a). \n. ...\nInject reference; solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C^H^NkO: b\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nRifampicin\nRifampin,\nC43H58N4Oi2 ' ’■ \n' ■■ Mol. Wt.823.0\nRifampicin is(1.2Z, l,4£,24£)-(25', 16.9,175,18/?, 197?,20/?, \n21 S,22R,2 3 5)-1,2-dihydro-5,6,9,17,19-pentahydroxy- \n23-meth oxy-2,4,12,16,18,20,22-heptamethyl-8-(4-methyl- \npiperazin-l-yl iminomethyl)-! ,1 l-dioxo-2,7-(epoxypcntadeca-\n1,11,13-trienimino)naphtho[2,l-fi]furan-21-yl acetate.\nRifampicin contains not less than 97.0 per cent arid not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C.rJl5SN.tO|2, calculated on the dried \nbasis. \n■ .w,.w \n7;'. ■’ \n■' 7 ■ w\nCategory. Antitubercular. ,\nDescription. A brick-red to reddish brown, crystalline powder.\n3484'"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RIFAMPICIN :\nThe .relative retention time with reference to rifabutin for a \nmajor peak fora degradant, two minor peaks for degradants \nare about 0.5,0.6 and 0.8 respectively.\nInject reference solution (a): and reference solution (b).\nThe test is hot valid unless the resolution between the rifabutin \npeak and the degradant peak eluting at a relative retention \ntime of about 0.8 is not less than 1.3 obtained with reference \nsolution (b) and the column efficiency is not less than 2000 \ntheoretical plates and the relative standard deviation for \nreplicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.obtained with \nreference solution (a). \n. ...\nInject reference; solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C^H^NkO: b\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nRifampicin\nRifampin,\nC43H58N4Oi2 ' ’■ \n' ■■ Mol. Wt.823.0\nRifampicin is(1.2Z, l,4£,24£)-(25', 16.9,175,18/?, 197?,20/?, \n21 S,22R,2 3 5)-1,2-dihydro-5,6,9,17,19-pentahydroxy- \n23-meth oxy-2,4,12,16,18,20,22-heptamethyl-8-(4-methyl- \npiperazin-l-yl iminomethyl)-! ,1 l-dioxo-2,7-(epoxypcntadeca-\n1,11,13-trienimino)naphtho[2,l-fi]furan-21-yl acetate.\nRifampicin contains not less than 97.0 per cent arid not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C.rJl5SN.tO|2, calculated on the dried \nbasis. \n■ .w,.w \n7;'. ■’ \n■' 7 ■ w\nCategory. Antitubercular. ,\nDescription. A brick-red to reddish brown, crystalline powder.\n3484'"},{"name":"Identification","text":"|\nA. Determine by infraredabsorptiori spectrophotometry (2.4 5^ I\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rifampicin Ipgg 1 | \nor with the reference spectrum of rifampicin. \n;r;.,.. I\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram | \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the I \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution, ' I\nTests ■ ■ \n...........: \n\"I\n< if ■■■ - ■ ; ■ \n■ - 1 \n; ' ■ ' f : A ?■ ■■ ■ .■ \n’ • :-:?i =r.’ ; r- ; '\npH (2.4.24). 4.5 to 6.5, determined, in a 1.0 pet cent w/v | \nsuspension.., \n..... \n1\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography I \n(2.4.14). \n.m w ■ \n' I\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use. ■\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent \nw/v solution of citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13,61 percent \nw/v solution potassium dihydrogenpAosp/mte, 77 volumes \nof a 17.42 per cent w/v solution of dipotassiym, hydrogen \nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and -250 volumes of\nacetonitrile.\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of powder containing 20 mg \nof Rifampicin, add 10 ml of acetonitrile^ shake and filter. Dilute \n5 ml of the filtrate to 50 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.02 per cent w/v of \nrifampicin quinone ilPRS in. acetonitrile, To 1 ml of the \nsolution, add 1 ml of the test solution and dilute to 100 ml with' \nthe solvcnt.mixture. \n...........,\nChromatographic system \n. ■> \n5\n— a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4,6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \n; -\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid, \n0.19 per cent w/v of sodium perchlorate, 0.59 per cent \nw/v of citric ufczt/ and 2,09. per cent .w/v of potassium: \ndihydrogen phosphate and 35 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flowrate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \n|\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \nB\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the J \nresolution between rifampicin arid1 rifampicin quinone is not | \nless than4. \n' ’'!i \n■ g\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area g \nof any peak due to rifampicin quinone is not more than |\n1.5 times the area of the peak due to rifampicin quinone in the g \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (1:5 per ’J \ncent); the area of any peak, other than thepeakdue to rifampicin. g. \nand rifampicin quinone, is not more than the areaof the peak | \ndue: to rifampicin in the chromatogram obtained :with: the g"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampin-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampin Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rifampin Capsules\nRifampicin Capsules contain not less than 92.5 per cent and\nnot more than 1,07.5 per cent of the stated amount of rifampicin, \n^'4jH5gN4O12. ’\nUsualstrengths. 150 mg; 300mg; 450 mg; 600 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rifampin Capsules\nRifampicin Capsules contain not less than 92.5 per cent and\nnot more than 1,07.5 per cent of the stated amount of rifampicin, \n^'4jH5gN4O12. ’\nUsualstrengths. 150 mg; 300mg; 450 mg; 600 mg."},{"name":"identification","text":"^• Shake a quantity .ofthe contents of the capsules containing\ng of Rifampicin with 5 ml of chloroform, filter and evaporate\nthe filtrate to dryness. The residue complies with the following \ntest. \n. . ■ -\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rifampicin IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of rifampicin.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the . chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (a).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium; 900 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter promptly \nthrough a membrane filter disc having an average pore diameter \nnot greater than 1.0 pm, rejecting the first 1 ml of the filtrate. \nDilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the same solvent. Measure \nthe absorbance (2.4.7) of the resulting solution at the maximum \nat about 475 nm. Calculate the content of C43HS8N40i2 in the \nmedium from the absorbance obtained from a solution of \nknown concentration of rifampicin IPRS.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. 10volumesofa21.01 percent w/v solution \nof citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium dihydrogen phosphate, 77 volumes of al 7.42 per\ncent w/v solution of dipotassium hydrogen phosphate, 640 \nvolumes of water and 250 volumes of acetonitrile.\nTest solution; Shake a quantity of the contents of the capsules \ncontaining 200 mg of Rifampicin, with 100 ml of acetonitrile \nand filter. Dilute 5 ml of the filtrate to 50 ml with the solvent \nmixture. \n■■ \n■\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.02 per cent \nw/v of rifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 1.0 ml of the \nsolution to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 per cent \nw/v each of rifampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS in \nacetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture. ,\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n. - - column temperature: 30°,\n-mobile phase:, a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid, \n0.19 per cent w/y of sodium perchlorate, 0.5 9 per cent \n.. w/v of citric acid and 2.09 per cent w/v of potassium\ndihydrogenphosphate and 35 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n-< spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, ‘\n; - injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe relative retention times are 1.0, 0.55, 1.25 and 3.56 for \nrifampicin, rifampicin quinone, rifampicin N-oxide and \n3-formylrifamycin SV respectively. The response factors are \n1.00, 1.19, 1.03 and 1.25 for rifampicin, rifampicin quinone, \nrifampicin N-oxide and 3-formylrifamycin SV respectively.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between rifampicin and rifampicin quinone is not \nless than 4, the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak due to rifampicin quinone is not more than 4 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (4.0 per cent), the area of any peak due \nto rifampicin N-oxide is not more than! .5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (1.5 per cent) and the area of any peak due to \n3-formylrifamycin SV is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (1.0 per cent). In the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution the area of any other secondary peak is not more \nthan the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (a) (1.0 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Determineby liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent w/v solution \nof citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium dihydrogen phosphate, 77 volumes of a 17.42 per\ncent , w/v solution of dipotassium hydrogen phosphate, \n640 volumes of water and 250 volumes of acetonitrile^ '•'\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the contents of the capsules \ncontaining about 300.0 mg of Rifampicin, with 200.0 ml of \nacetonitrile, filter. Dilute 10.0 ml of the filtrate to 50.0 ml with \nacetonitrile. Further dilute 5.Q ml of the solution to 50.0 ml \nwith the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A 0.15 per cent w/v solution of \nrifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml ofthe solution \nto 50.0 ml \nacetonitrile. Further dilute 5.0 ml of the;solution\nto 50.0 pal with the solvent mixture. \n.\nReference solution -(b). A solution containing 0:01- per cent \nw/v each of rifampicin IPRS and'rifampicinquinone IPRS in\nIP 202$\nRIFAMPICIN CAPSULES\nacetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the ® \nsolvent mixture. ■ \n■\nUse the chromatographic system and system suitability;'W \nparameters, as described under the test for Related substances IF\n. .. .. ... ■ ■ ■' . ■ ’ ■■\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more S \nthan 2.0percent. \n’ \n-S'\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. ; ; 'ft\nCalculate the content of C43H58N4OI2 in the capsules.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampin-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampin Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rifampin Tablets\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between rifampicin and rifampicin quinone is not \nless than.4; the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the \ncolumn efficiency is nbt less than 2000 theoretical plates."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rifampin Tablets\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between rifampicin and rifampicin quinone is not \nless than.4; the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the \ncolumn efficiency is nbt less than 2000 theoretical plates."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Inject reference solution , (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \npeak due to rifampicin quinone is not more than 1.5 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (1.5 per cent), the area of any peak due \nto rifampicin N-oxide is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (1.0 per cent) and the area of any peak due to \n3-formylrifamycin SV is not more than 5.0 times the area of the\nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (5.0 per cent). The area of any other secondary \nPeak is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nc^romatogram obtained with reference solution (a)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other Tests; Comply with the tests stated under Oral \nSuspensions."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampin"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rifampin\nub\nRiboflavin Tablets\nCH 3 CH 3\nLactoflavin Tablets; Vitamin B 2 Tablets\nacetonitrile.\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of powder containing 20 mg \nof Rifampicin, add 10 ml of acetonitrile, shake and filter. Dilute \n5 ml of the filtrate to 50 ml with the solvent mixture.\nRiboflavin Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of riboflavine, \nC, 7H2ON406.\nH3C\nCH3 \nO \nH3C0 ~\n7t) \nUsual strengths. 2 mg; 5 mg. \t\na.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.02 per cent w/v of \nrifampicin quinone RS in acetonitrile. To 1 ml of the solution, \nadd 1 ml of the test solution and dilute to 100 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\n \nbi\neft"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rifampin\nub\nRiboflavin Tablets\nCH 3 CH 3\nLactoflavin Tablets; Vitamin B 2 Tablets\nacetonitrile.\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of powder containing 20 mg \nof Rifampicin, add 10 ml of acetonitrile, shake and filter. Dilute \n5 ml of the filtrate to 50 ml with the solvent mixture.\nRiboflavin Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of riboflavine, \nC, 7H2ON406.\nH3C\nCH3 \nO \nH3C0 ~\n7t) \nUsual strengths. 2 mg; 5 mg. \t\na.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.02 per cent w/v of \nrifampicin quinone RS in acetonitrile. To 1 ml of the solution, \nadd 1 ml of the test solution and dilute to 100 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\n \nbi\neft"},{"name":"Identification","text":"O_ \n0\nShake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 1 mg \nof Riboflavine with 100 ml of water and filter; the filtrate \nhas a pale greenish yellow colour by transmitted light \nand an intense yellowish green fluorescence by reflected \nlight, which disappears on addition of mineral acids or \nalkalis.\nCH3 \t\nOH\nChromatographic system\nN. CH3\n-\na stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution \ncontaining 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid, \n0.19 per cent w/v of  sodium perchlorate, 0.59 per cent \nw/v of citric acid and 2.09 per cent w/v of potaysium \ndihydrogen phosphate and 35 volumes of acetonitrile, \n-\nflow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 µl.\nC43H58N40 12 \t\nMol. Wt. 823.0\nIi"},{"name":"Tests","text":";t\nRifampicin is ( 12Z,14E,24E)-(2S, 16S,17S,18R, 19R,20R, \n21S,22R,235)-1,2-dihydro-5,6,9,17,19-pentahydroxy-\n23-methoxy-2,4,12,16,18,20,22-heptamethyl-8-(4-methyl-\nP'perazin-l -yliminomethyl)-1 , l l -dioxo-2,7-(epoxypentadeca-\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated undei \nTablets.\n1,11,13-trienimino)naphtho[2,1-h] furan-2 l -yl acetate.\nPowder one tablet, add a mixture of 2.5 ml of glacial acetic \nacW\"1nd 5.0'ml of water and heat on a water-bath for\nRifampicin contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not•niore \nthan 102.0 per cent of C43HSKN 40 12, calculated on the dried \" \nbasis.\nInjeet`theieferi nce solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution.between  rifampicin and rifampicin quinone is not\n1. hoar with occasional stirring. Dilute with 50 ml of water, \nadd 15 ml of 1 M sodium hydroxide with continuous\nless than 4:\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof any peak due to rifampicin quinone is not more than\n1.5 times the area of the peak due to rifampicin quinone in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (1.5 per \ncent); the area of any peak, other than the peak due to rifampicin \nand rifampicin quinone, is not more than the area of the peak \ndue to rifampicin in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution (1.0 per cent) and the sum of the areas of \nany such peaks is not more than 3.5 times the area of the peak \ndue to rifampicin in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution (3.5 per cent ).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 80° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa for 4 hours.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTE - Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent \nw/v solution of citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent \nw/v solution ofpotassium dihydrogen phosphate, 77 volumes \nof a 17.42 per cent w/v solution of  dipotassium hydrogen \nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and 250 volumes of\nacetonitrile.\nTest solution. Dissolve 20.0 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of acetonitrile and filter. Dilute 5.0 ml of \nthe filtrate to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.2 per cent w/v of \nrifampicin RS in acetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to\n100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nUse the chromatographic system and the system suitability \nparameters described under the test for Related substances.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C43F158N4012.\nStorage. Store protected from light, in an atmosphere of \nnitrogen."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rilpivirine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rilpivirine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rilpivirine\nC22H18N6.HC1 \nMol Wt 402.9\nRilpivirine is 4^{[4-({4-[(£)-2-Cyanovinyl]-2,6-dimethylphehyl} \nami no)pyrimidin-2-yl] amino) benzonitrile. 5\nRilpivirine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and-not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C23H18N6.HCI, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral. \n..\nDescription. A white to pale brown colour powder; ■ m"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rilpivirine\nC22H18N6.HC1 \nMol Wt 402.9\nRilpivirine is 4^{[4-({4-[(£)-2-Cyanovinyl]-2,6-dimethylphehyl} \nami no)pyrimidin-2-yl] amino) benzonitrile. 5\nRilpivirine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and-not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C23H18N6.HCI, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral. \n..\nDescription. A white to pale brown colour powder; ■ m"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rilpivirine IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of rilpivirine. ■’ u 1\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the'principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nTests . ; .. \n; \n, \n, . .., .ki mi Of-;\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n< ■ \n■■■ .t-;. \n'-.•Mvn'/T\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in methanol apA dilute to 100.0 mTwitii methanol\nReference solution. A0.003 per cent w/v solution of rilpivirfye \nIPRS, impurity A IPRS and impurity B IPRS \\r\\ methanol.\nDilute 0.5 ml of the solution td 20.0 ml with methanol.\"\nChromatographic system \n;\nr a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), ,\n- column temperature: 45°, \n- ; i j b\n. , - mobile phase: A. dissolve 3.08 g of ammonium acetate\n. in 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 6.0 withacetic acid,\nB. methanol, . \n■. ..o wcT -•\nfl*2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rinderpest-vaccine-live","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rinderpest Vaccine, Live"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rinderpest Vaccine, Live\nRinderpest Vaccine, Live is a freeze-dried preparation ofa live\nattenuated strain of rinderpest virus that has been modified\nby adaptation to and propagation in suitable cell cultures in\nRINDERPESTVACClNE,LIVE\nsuch a manner that it remains avirulent but retains its\nimmunogenicity in cattle. It is reconstituted immediately before\nuse with a suitable diluent."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rinderpest Vaccine, Live\nRinderpest Vaccine, Live is a freeze-dried preparation ofa live\nattenuated strain of rinderpest virus that has been modified\nby adaptation to and propagation in suitable cell cultures in\nRINDERPESTVACClNE,LIVE\nsuch a manner that it remains avirulent but retains its\nimmunogenicity in cattle. It is reconstituted immediately before\nuse with a suitable diluent."},{"name":"Production","text":"SEEDLOT\nThe seed lots should be validated for the following tests:\na) Purity. It should be free from contaminations with viruses,\nbacteria, fungi and mycoplasmas;\nb) Should not induce any abnormal clinical reaction on\ninoculation into rinderpest susceptible cattle;\nc) Efficacy. It should induce an immunity to rinderpest in the\nsusceptible cattle.\nCELLS. The primary cells/subcultured cells/continuous cell\nlines when used should be free from BVD and other\ncontaminating viruses."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. The vaccine protects cattle against virulentrinderpest virus.\nB. The seed and the vaccine must be titrated in a suitable cell\nculture system capable ofsupporting the multiplication ofthe\nrinderpest virus.\nC. When neutralised with a specific rinderpest antiserum, the\nvaccine is no longer capable of protecting cattle against\nrinderpest infection."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Water (2.3.43). Not more than 3.0 per cent.\nMycoplasmas (2.7.4). Complies with the test for absence of\nmycoplasmas.\nExtraneous pathogens. Complies with the requirements stated\nunder Veterinary Vaccines.\nSafety\nA. Use four healthy guinea-pigs, each weighing not less than\n400 g. Inject two of them intramuscularly and two\nintraperitoneally with 0.5 rnl ofthe vaccine under examination.\nIn addition, inject intraperitoneally each of six mice, each\nweighing between 18 and 25 g, with OJ ml of the vaccine.\nObserve the animals for 21 days. All th;e animals remain healthy\nduring the observation period. At the end of the observation\nperiod sacrifice the animals and perform autopsy on each.\nNone of the animals shows any unusual changes.\nB. Inject subcutaneously each of two susceptible cattle, free\nfrom specific antibodies, with a quantity of the vaccine'\ncontaining not less than 100 times the minimum dose stated\non the label, using pooled reconstituted contents of not less\nthan ten containers taken at random. Observe the animals for"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ringer-lactate-solution-for-irrigation-hartmann-s","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ringer-Lactate Solution for Irrigation; Hartmann's"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ringer-Lactate Solution for Irrigation; Hartmann's\nSolution for Irrigation\nCompound Sodium Lactate Solution for Irrigation is a sterile\nsolution containing 0.24 per cent v/v ofLacticAcid (equivalent\nto 0.32 per cent wIv ofsodium lactate) with 0.6 per cent wIv of\nSodium Chloride, 0.04 per cent w/v ofPotassium Chloride and\n0.027 per cent w/v ofCalcium Chloride in Water for Injections.\nCompound Sodium Lactate Solution for Irrigation contains\nnot less than 0.27 per cent and not more than 0.32 per cent\nw/v ofsodium, Na, not less than 0.019 per cent and not more\nthan 0.022 percentw/v ofpotassium, K, not less than 0.37 per\ncent and not more than 0.42 per cent w/v oftotal chloride, Cl,\nnot less than 0.025 per cent and not more than 0.029 per cent\nw/v of calcium chloride, CaClz,2HzO, and not less than\n0.23 per cent and not more than 0.28 per cent w/v oflactate,\ncalculated as C3H60 3• It contains no antimicrobial agent.\nCategory. Irrigation fluid.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Ringer-Lactate Solution for Irrigation; Hartmann's\nSolution for Irrigation\nCompound Sodium Lactate Solution for Irrigation is a sterile\nsolution containing 0.24 per cent v/v ofLacticAcid (equivalent\nto 0.32 per cent wIv ofsodium lactate) with 0.6 per cent wIv of\nSodium Chloride, 0.04 per cent w/v ofPotassium Chloride and\n0.027 per cent w/v ofCalcium Chloride in Water for Injections.\nCompound Sodium Lactate Solution for Irrigation contains\nnot less than 0.27 per cent and not more than 0.32 per cent\nw/v ofsodium, Na, not less than 0.019 per cent and not more\nthan 0.022 percentw/v ofpotassium, K, not less than 0.37 per\ncent and not more than 0.42 per cent w/v oftotal chloride, Cl,\nnot less than 0.025 per cent and not more than 0.029 per cent\nw/v of calcium chloride, CaClz,2HzO, and not less than\n0.23 per cent and not more than 0.28 per cent w/v oflactate,\ncalculated as C3H60 3• It contains no antimicrobial agent.\nCategory. Irrigation fluid.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. When warmed with potassium permanganate gives\nacetaldehyde, recognisable by its odour.\nB. The residue on evaporation, when moistened with\nhydrochloric acid and introduced on a platinum wire into a\nflame imparts a yellow colour to the flame. When viewed\nthrough a suitable blue glass, the flame is tinged reddish\npurple.\nC. Gives reaction C ofcalcium salts and reactionAofchlorides\n(2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.0to 7.0.\nBacterialendotoxins (2.2.3). Notmore than 0.5 Endotoxin Unit\nperml.\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Parenteral\nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Forsodium -\nDilute suitably with water and determine\nby MethodAfor flame photometry (2.4.4), orby MethodAfor\natomic absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.2), measuring at\n589 nm and using sodium solution FP or sodium solution\nAAS respectively, suitably diluted with water for the standard\nsolutions.\nFor potassium -\nDilute suitably with water and determine\nby MethodAfor flame photometry (2.4.4), or by MethodAfor\natomic absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.2), measuring at\n767 nm and using potassium solution FP or potassium\nsolution AAS respectively, suitably diluted with water for the\nstandard solutions.\nFor calcium chloride\n-\nTo 50.0 ml add 5.0 ml of\nO.O} M magnesium sulphate and 5 ml of ammonia buffer\npH 10.9 and titrate with 0.01 M disodium edetate using\neriochrome black T mixture as indicator. From the volume of\n0.01 M disodium edetate required subtract the volume of\n0.01 M magnesium sulphate added.\n1ml ofthe remainder ofO.01 M disodium edetate is equivalent\nto 0.00147 gofCaCh, 2HzO.\nFor total chlorides-\nTo 20.0 ml add 30 ml ofwater, 50.0 ml of\n0.1 M silver nitrate and 2 ml of nitric acid. Filter, wash·the\nprecipitate with water slightly acidified with nitric acid and\ntitrate the excess of silver nitrate with 0.1 M ammonium\nthiocyanate using ferric ammonium sulphate solution as\nindicator until a reddish yellow colour is produced. Carry out\na blank titration.\n1ml of0.1 M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545 g oftotal\nchloride, calculated as Cl.\nFor lactate -\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. The preparation under examination.\nReference solution. A 0.28 per cent w/v solution of lithium\nlactate RS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 20 em x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 /lm),\nmobile phase: a mixture of 90 volumes of water and\n10 volumes ofa 2 per cent v/v solution ofoctylamine in\nacetonitrile, the pH of which is adjusted to 7.0 with a\n10 per cent v/v solution ofphosphoric acid,\n-\nflow rate. 2 ml per minute,\n-\nspectrophotometer set at 210 urn,\n-\ninjection volume. 10 /ll.\nInject separately the test solution and the reference solution\nand measure the responses for the major peak.\nCalculate the content ofC3H60 3, in the injection.\nStorage. Store in single dose containers of glass or plastic.\nOnkeeping, small solid particles may separate from the solution\nin glass containers. The container may be designed to empty\nrapidly and may contain a volume ofmore than one litre.\nLabelling. The label states (1) that the irrigation solution\ncontains, in millimoles per litre, the following approximate\namounts of the ions. sodium,131, potassium, 5, calcium,\n2, bicarbonate (as lactate), 29, and chloride, 111; (2) that the\nsolution should not be used ifit contains visible particles; (3)\n'For irrigation only' and 'Not for injection';-(4) that once the\ncontainer is opened, the unused portion should be discarded."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ringer-s-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ringer's Solution"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ringer's Solution\nCompound Sodium Chloride Solution is a solution containing\n0.86 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride, 0.03 per cent w/v of\nPotassium Chloride and 0.033 percentw/v ofCalcium Chloride\nin Purified Water.The solution may be clarified by filtration.\nCompound Sodium Chloride Solution contains not less than\n0.82 per cent w/v and not more than 0.90 per cent of sodium\nchloride, NaC!, not less than 0.025 per cent and not more than\n0.035 per cent w/v ofpotassium chloride, KCl, and not less\nthan 0.030 per cent and not more than 0.036 per cent wlv of\ncalcium chloride, CaC12, 2H20.\nCategory. Irrigation solution (forextemal use).\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Ringer's Solution\nCompound Sodium Chloride Solution is a solution containing\n0.86 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride, 0.03 per cent w/v of\nPotassium Chloride and 0.033 percentw/v ofCalcium Chloride\nin Purified Water.The solution may be clarified by filtration.\nCompound Sodium Chloride Solution contains not less than\n0.82 per cent w/v and not more than 0.90 per cent of sodium\nchloride, NaC!, not less than 0.025 per cent and not more than\n0.035 per cent w/v ofpotassium chloride, KCl, and not less\nthan 0.030 per cent and not more than 0.036 per cent wlv of\ncalcium chloride, CaC12, 2H20.\nCategory. Irrigation solution (forextemal use).\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Gives reaction B of sodium salts and reaction A of chlorides\n(2.3.1). When concentrated to one halfofits original volume,\nCOMPOUND SODIUM CHLORIDE SOLUTION\nit gives reactionA ofpotassium salts and reaction B ofcalcium\nsalts (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.0to 7.5.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Evaporate 67 mlto about20 rnI, add2 rnl\nofdilute acetic acidand dilute to 25 ml with water. The solution\ncomplies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method A\n(0.3 ppm).\nAssay. Forsodium chloride-\nDilute appropriately with water\nand determine by Method A for flame photometry (2.4.4),\nmeasuring at 589 nm or by Method A for atomic absorption\nspectrophotometry (2.4.2), using sodium solution Fp, suitably\ndiluted with water for the standard solutions.\n1 g ofNa is equivalent to 2.54 g ofNaCI.\nFor potassium chloride -\nDilute appropriately with water\nand determine by Method A for flame photometry (2.4.4),\nmeasuring at 767 nm or by Method A for atomic absorption\nspectrophotometry (2.4.2), using potassium solution Fp,\nsuitably diluted with water for the standard solutions.\n1 g ofK is equivalent to 1.007 g ofKCl.\nFor calcium Chloride -\nTo 50.0 ml add 5.0 ml of 0.01 M\nmagnesium sulphate and 5 ml ofammonia bufferpH10.9 and\ntitrate with 0.01 M disodium edetate using mordant black II\nmixture as indicator. From the volume of 0.01 M disodium\nedetate required subtract the volume of O.OIM magnesium\nsulphate added.\n1ml ofthe remainder ofO.01 M disodium edetate is equivalent\nto 0.00147 g ofCaClz, 2HzO.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nLabelling. The label states the strength as the percentages\nw/v of Sodium Chloride, Potassium Chloride and Calcium\nChloride. Ifthe contents ofthe container are sterile, the label\nstates (1) Sterile Compound Sodium Chloride Solution; (2)\nthat the solution is not intended for injection."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ringers-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ringer’s Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ringer’s Injection ; ;\nCompound Sodium Chloride Injection is a sterile solution \ncontaining 0,86 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride, 0.03 pet cent \nw/v of Potassium Chloride and 0.033 per cent w/v of Calcium \nChloride in Water for Injections. It contains no antimicrobial \nagent \np.: \n.... . .. ,\nCompound Sodium Chloride Injection.containsnot less than \n0.82 per cent w/v and not more than 0.90 per cent w/v of \nsodium chloride, NaCl, not less than 0.0285 per cent w/v and \nnot more than 0.0315 per cent w/v of potassium chloride, KC1, \nand not less than 0.030 per cent w/v and. not more than \n0.036 per cent w/v of calcium chloride, CaCl^ 2H2Q.\nDescription; Aclear, colourless solution."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Ringer’s Injection ; ;\nCompound Sodium Chloride Injection is a sterile solution \ncontaining 0,86 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride, 0.03 pet cent \nw/v of Potassium Chloride and 0.033 per cent w/v of Calcium \nChloride in Water for Injections. It contains no antimicrobial \nagent \np.: \n.... . .. ,\nCompound Sodium Chloride Injection.containsnot less than \n0.82 per cent w/v and not more than 0.90 per cent w/v of \nsodium chloride, NaCl, not less than 0.0285 per cent w/v and \nnot more than 0.0315 per cent w/v of potassium chloride, KC1, \nand not less than 0.030 per cent w/v and. not more than \n0.036 per cent w/v of calcium chloride, CaCl^ 2H2Q.\nDescription; Aclear, colourless solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"It gives reaction (B) of sodium salts and reaction (A) of chlorides \n(2.3.1). When concentrated to one half of its original volume, \nit gives.reaction (A) of potassium salts and reaction (B) of \ncalcium salts (2.3.1). \n-........."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 7.5. \n;\nHeavy metals (2.3; 13). Evaporate 67 ml to about 20 ml, add 2 ml \noidilute acetic acid and dilute to 25 ml with water. The solution \ncomplies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method A\n(0.3 ppm). \nw'w./.F\nIp2O22\nI\nBacterial endotoxins (2,2.3); Not more than 0.5 Endotoxin |\nUnitperml. ms . ■■■■■.:.. ■■■■ \n: ::..ss \\ o. |\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral I\nPreparations (Intravenous Infusions). \n; ■ 4: . : \\ ; |\nAssay. For sodium chloride—Dilute appropriately with water I \nand determine by Method A1 for flame photometry (2.4,4) 1 \nmeasuring at 589 nm or by Method A for atomic absorption I \nspectrophotometry (2.4.2), using sodium solution FP, suitably 1 \ndiluted with waterfor the standard solutions. \ni\n1 g of Na is equivalent to 2.54 g of NaCl. \n|\nFor potassium Chloride — Dilute appropriately with wafer |\nand determine by Method À for flame photometry (2.4.4), | \nmeasuring at 767 nm or by Method A for atomic'\"absorption | \nspectrophotometry (2.4.2), using potassium solution FP, I \nsuitably diluted with water for the standard solutions. ; \n|\n1 g of Kis equivalent to 1.907 g ôf KC1. \n’ \nf\n- . \n.„-.WW.-:\nFor calcium chloride —To 50.0 ml add 5.0 ml of.0.01 M | \nmagnesium sulphate and 5 ml of ammonia buffer pH10.9 and \ntitrate with 0.01 M disodium edetate using mordant black 11 \nmixture as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.01Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.00147 g of\nCaCl2,2H2O, : \n-\nStorage. Store in single dose containers of glass, or plastic. \nOn keeping; small solid particles may separate from a glass \ncontainer,./' ■ ■ w: \n.,\nLabelling. The label states (1) the strength as the percentages \nw/v bf Sodium Chloride,' Potassium Chloride and Galcium \nChloride; (2) that a Solution containmg visible solid particles \nmust not be used. \" \n\" \n;L\nWhen the preparation is intended for intravenous infusion, \nthe label states that the Injection contains in millimoles per \nlitre, the followingapproximate atriounts of ions; sodium; 147.5; \npotassium, 4; calcium, 2.25 and chloride, 156. \n;"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/risedronate-sodium-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Risedronate Sodium Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Risedronate Sodium Tablets\nRisedronate Sodium Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per \ncent and notmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nrisedronate sodium. C7HwNNaO7P2.\nUsual strengths. 35 mg; 75 mg; 150 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Risedronate Sodium Tablets\nRisedronate Sodium Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per \ncent and notmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nrisedronate sodium. C7HwNNaO7P2.\nUsual strengths. 35 mg; 75 mg; 150 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Disperse a quantity of the powdered tablets containing \n50 mg of risedronate sodium in 10 ml of water, shake and filter. \nAdd 10 ml-of 0.2M.cupric chloride solution, mix well arid \nallow the solution to stand.for about 10 minutes. Add 2 ml of \ndehydrated alcohol, mix well and allow the solution to stand \nfor a minimum of 1 hour, to form a blue precipitate of the \ncopper complex. Collect the precipitate using a nylon filter of \n0.45-pm pore size, wash it with 10 ml of dehydrated alcohol, \nand allow it to dry on the filter paper, determine by infrared \nabsorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum \nwith that obtained with risedronate sodium IPRS, treated in \nthe samemanner or with the reference spectrurti of risedronate \nsodium. -J\nNOTE — Dry the precipitate under ambient conditions, do \nnot heat'the precipitate. A modest change of colour (from \nblue to green) may be observed upon drying.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution ."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution(2.5.2),\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of wa/er,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by. liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium. \n.' = • .\nReference solution. Dissolve a quantity of risedronate sodium \nIPRS in the dissolution medium to obtain a solution of known \nconcentration similar to the expected concentration of the \ntest solution.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 5 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with a\nstrong anion-exchange microbeads consisting of \nsulphonated cross-linked polystyrene (10 pm),\n- mobile phase: a buffer solution prepared by dissolving\n1.8 g of disodium edetate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted \nto pH 9.5 with dilute sodium hydroxide,\n- flow rate: 0.8 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 263 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of \nC7H10NNaO7P2.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof the powder containing 25 mg of Risedronate Sodium in \n50 ml of mobile phase,- shake for 10 minutes and sonicate for \n5 minutes, dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase, filter.\nReference solution. A 0.025 per cent w/v solution of \nrisedronate sodium IPRS and 0.0004 per cent w/v solution of \nrisedronate related compound A IPRS [(2- pyridinil isomer \n[1- hydroxyl-2-(2~. pyridinyl) ethyl- idene]. bis (phosphonic \nacid) monohydrate IPRS] in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with a\nstrong anion-exchange microbeads consisting of \nsulphonated cross-linked polystyrene (10 pm), .\n- mobile phase: a buffer solution prepared by dissolving\n1.8 g of disodium edetate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted \nto pH 9.5 with dilute sodium hydroxide,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 263 nm,\n- injection volume: 50 pl.\nInject the reference solution . The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between risedronate related compound Apeak and \nrisedronate peak is not less than 2.0, the tailing factor for \nrisedronate peak is not more than 1.5 and the relative standard \ndeviation of risedronate peaks for replicate injections is not \nmore than 1.5 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C7HwNNaO7P2 in the tablets."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/risedronate-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Risedronate Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RISEDRONATE SODIUM\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \nflow rate: 0.6 nil per minute,\nSpectrophotometer set at 295nm, \ninjection volume: 10 pl.\nTime\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent\n(in mini)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n40\nWo ■■■■>\n40\n60\n12\n20\n20\n80\n15\n20\n15\n20\n80\n18\n40\n40\n60\n20\n40.\n20\n40\n60\nInject the reference solution.\nInject reference solution. The test is not valid unless the the \nresoIution .'between rilpivirine impurity A (2E)-3-(4-amino- \n3 5dirnethyI phenyl) acrylonitrile hydrochlorid and rilpivirine \nimpurity B(4-(4-chloropyrimidin -2 yl-amino)- benzonitrile) is \nnot less than 5.0.\nInject reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of \npeak due to rilpivirine impurity A is not more than area of \nreference solution (0,3 per cent), the area of peak due to \nrilpivirinempurity B is not more than area of reference solution \n(0.3 per cent), area of any other secondary peak is not more \nthan area of rilpivirine peak in the chromatograpm obtained \nwith the reference solution (0.3 per cent) and the sum of area \nof all the secondary peaks is not more than 3.3 times the area \nof rilpivirine peak in the chromatograpm obtained with the \nreference solution (1.0 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with limit test for heavy \nmetalSjMethodB (20 ppm).\nC7H10NNaO7P2\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2^3143). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined on\nAssay; Determineby liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\n^e^t solution. \n25 mg of the substance under\nexamination in methanol and dilute to 100.0 ml with methanol. \nDilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with methanol.\nReference solution. A 0.0025 per cent w/v solution of\nIPRS in methanol.\nChromatographic system\n' a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RISEDRONATE SODIUM\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \nflow rate: 0.6 nil per minute,\nSpectrophotometer set at 295nm, \ninjection volume: 10 pl.\nTime\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent\n(in mini)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n40\nWo ■■■■>\n40\n60\n12\n20\n20\n80\n15\n20\n15\n20\n80\n18\n40\n40\n60\n20\n40.\n20\n40\n60\nInject the reference solution.\nInject reference solution. The test is not valid unless the the \nresoIution .'between rilpivirine impurity A (2E)-3-(4-amino- \n3 5dirnethyI phenyl) acrylonitrile hydrochlorid and rilpivirine \nimpurity B(4-(4-chloropyrimidin -2 yl-amino)- benzonitrile) is \nnot less than 5.0.\nInject reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of \npeak due to rilpivirine impurity A is not more than area of \nreference solution (0,3 per cent), the area of peak due to \nrilpivirinempurity B is not more than area of reference solution \n(0.3 per cent), area of any other secondary peak is not more \nthan area of rilpivirine peak in the chromatograpm obtained \nwith the reference solution (0.3 per cent) and the sum of area \nof all the secondary peaks is not more than 3.3 times the area \nof rilpivirine peak in the chromatograpm obtained with the \nreference solution (1.0 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with limit test for heavy \nmetalSjMethodB (20 ppm).\nC7H10NNaO7P2\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2^3143). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined on\nAssay; Determineby liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\n^e^t solution. \n25 mg of the substance under\nexamination in methanol and dilute to 100.0 ml with methanol. \nDilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with methanol.\nReference solution. A 0.0025 per cent w/v solution of\nIPRS in methanol.\nChromatographic system\n' a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with"},{"name":"Identification","text":"-1; octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \n' column temperature: 45°,\n. mobile phase: A. dissolve 3.08 g of ammonium acetate\nin 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 6.0 with acetic acid,\nB. a mixture of 50 volumes of acetonitrile\n-v and 50 volumes of methanol,\na gradient programme using the conditions given below,\n- flow rate: 0.6 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 295nm, \n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\nv/v)\n(per cent v/v)\nThe test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 3000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution and.the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C22Hi8N6.HCl.\nRisedronate Sodium\nOH\nH°\nH°4\nMoLWt 305.1\nMol. Wt. 323.1\nC7H10NNaO7P2,H2O(Monohydrate)\nMol. Wt 350.1\nC7Hi0NNaO7p2,2.5H2O (Hemi-pentahydrate)\nRisedronate Sodium is l-Hydroxy-2-(3-pyridyl)ethane-\nl,l-diylbis(phosphonic acid) monosodium salt.\nRisedronate Sodium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C7H10NNaO7P2, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Calcium regulator.\nDescription. Awhite to off-white powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6), \ncompare, the spectrum with that obtained with risedronate \nsodium IPRS or with the reference spectrum of risedronate \nsodium.\nB. A 1.0 percent w/v solution gives, the reactions of sodium \nsalt (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. A. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n_\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in mobile phase A and dilute to 10.0 ml with mobile \nphase A.\nReference solution (a). A 0.0001 per cent w/v solution of \nrisedronate sodium IPRS in mobile phase A.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.1 per cent \nw/v of risedronate sodium IPRS and 0.0001 per cent w/v of \nrisedronate impurity A in mobile phase A. ;\nChromatographic system ,\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (3.5 pm),\n■ ■ mobile phase: A. a mixture of 900 volumes of < buffer\nsolution prepared by dissolving 1.42 g of disodium \nhydrogen orthophosphate, 1.7 g of tetrabutyl \nammonium phosphate and 0.38 g of ethylenediamine \ntetraacetate in 950 ml of water, adjusting to pH 7.5 with \nammonium hydroxide solution .and 100 volumes of \nacetonitrile,\nB. a mixture of 800 volumes of buffer \nsolution and 200 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the condition given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 263 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl; 12\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n100\n0\n10\n100\n0 •: :\n40\n. \n0 ...\n100 : ■\n55 ...\n0\n100 .\n67\n100. -.\n0\n, 75 •\n100\n0\nName\nRelative \nretention time\n3-Pyridyl acetic acid\n0.3\n1.32\nRisedronate impurity A1\n0.51\n1.76\nRisedronate sodium\n1.0\n1 [l-hydroxy-2-(2-pyridinyl)ethylidene] bisphosphonic acid.\nEquilibrate the column with the mobile phase for at least\n2 hours. ■ • \n'•\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between; the peaks-due to risedronate and\nrisedronate impurity A is not less than 5.0. \n■\nRISEDRONATE SODIUM\nU>2()22\nInject'reference solption (a), and the test solution,.In \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area pf the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.1 percent).\nB. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). . <;\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 0.46 g of disodium edetate in\n500 ml of water, adjusted to pH 7.5 with 1 Msodium hydroxide.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 25.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of\nrisedronate sodium impurity B IPRS (3,6-bis[(3-pyridinylf \nmethyl]-2,5-dihydroxy-2,5-dioxido-l, 4,2,5 dioxadipho- \nsphorinane-3,6-diyl]bis[phosphonic acid] disbclium \ntetrahydrate IPRS) in the solvent mixture. J ;\nChromatographic system\n- \na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm packed with \noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: dissolve 16.15 g of dibasic potassium\nphosphate and 0.46 g oidisodium edetate in 400 ml of\nwater, add 5 ml of tetrabutyldmmonium- dihydrogen \nphosphate and 1 ml of hydrochloric acid, adjusted to\npH 7.5 With 1 Msodium hydroxide or 1 Mhydrochloric \nacid arid dilute to 480 ml with water, add 20 ml of \nmethanol, ' ' '\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n■ spectrophotometer set at 263 nm, \nwm/w; :\n- injection volume: 10 pl. \n: \n.. , 1\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 1.5. \n. >\nInject the test solution. The area of any peak corresponding \nto risedronate impurity B is not more than 0.1 per cent arid the \narea of any other secondary peak is not more than 0.1'per \ncent. The sum of areas of all the secondary peaks of tests A \nand B is not more than 0.5 per cent, calculated by area \nnormalization. Ignore any peak eluting before risedronate \nimpurity B.\nRelative \nresponse factor\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metal s, Method B (20 ppm).\nWater (2.3.43). 5.5 to 7.5 per cent for monohydrate; 11.9 to\n13.9 per cent for hemi-pentahydrate, determined on about \n0.25g.' \n......\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance, under \nexamination in mobile phase Aand dilute to 10.0 ml withmobile \nphase A.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of risedronate \nsodium IPRS in mobile phase A.\nrisedronate sodium tablets\nIP 2022\nUse chromatographic system as described under Related \n$ubstances, test A.\ni * ct the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate inj ections, is not more\nthan 2.6 per cent. .;\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the'content of C7H i0NNa()7P2.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nLabelling. Label to indicate whether it is monohydrate or hemi- \npentahydrate."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/risperidone-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Risperidone Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Risperidone Oral Solution\nRisperidone Syrup contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nrisperidone, C23H27FN4O2. It may contain suitable preservative.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml.\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nTests ■ ■\npH (2.4.24). 2.0 to 4.O., \n... .\nRelated substances. Determine . by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n......\nBuffer solution. AO.5 per cent w/v solution of ammonium \nacetate in water.\nTest solution. Transfer a suitable quantity ofthe syrup \ncontaining. 10 mg of Risperidone to a 50-ml volumetric flask, \nadd 25 ml of the buffer solution, fill with methanol almost to \nvolume and mix. Allow to cool to room temperature and dilute \nwith methanol to volume. \n,\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 2.5 mg of risperidone related \ncompounds mixture IPRS in 2 ml of methanol. Add 2 ml of \nwater and 5 ml of the buffer solution and allow to cool to room \ntemperature, dilute to 10.0 ml with methanol.\nReference solution (bf A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \nrisperidone IPRS in methanol.\nReference solution (c). Transfer 10.0 ml of reference solution \n(b) to a 50-ml volumetric'iflask. Add 10 ml of water and 25 ml of \nthe buffer solution and allow to cool to room temperature, \ndilute, with wezAmm/.to volume.\nReference solution (^.Transfer 2.0 ml of reference solution\n(b) to a 50-ml volumetric flask. Add 10 ml of water, and 25 ml of \nthe buffer solution and allow to cool to room temperature, \ndilute with methanol to volume.\nChromatographic system \n.\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (3 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 78 volumes of the buffer\nsolution and 22 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 275 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor\nRisperidone m-A’-oxidc'\n0.33\n1.03\nB icyclori speridone2\n0.43\n1.49,\nRisperidone Z-oxirne3*\n0.53 ' ' \"\n—\nRisperidone\n; 1.0 ■ •\n—\n♦Process'impurity included for identification only. Process impurities \nare controlled in the-drug substance,\" and are. not to .be reported or \nincluded in the total impurities for the drug product,\n1 c is-3 - {2 -[4-(6-Fluoro-1,2-benzisoxazpl-3 -yl)-1 -piperidin yl] ethyl }-2- \nmethyI-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-4H-pyrido[i,2-(2]pynmidih-4-drie TV-oxide,\n23-(4-Fluoro-2-hydroxypheny])-l-{2-(6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-2-methyl-4- \noxo-4/f-pyrido-[l ,2-a]pyrimidin-3-yI)ethyl}-2-az4'-l-azo'nia- \nbicyclo[2.2.2]oct-2-ene iodide,\n33-(2-{4-[(Z)'-(2,’4-Difluoroplienylj(hydroxyiminojmethyl]piperidin-l- \nyI}etliy])-2-methyl-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-477-pyrido[l,2-alpyrimidin-4-\n3502 \nOllliilllMi\n„"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Risperidone Oral Solution\nRisperidone Syrup contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nrisperidone, C23H27FN4O2. It may contain suitable preservative.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml.\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nTests ■ ■\npH (2.4.24). 2.0 to 4.O., \n... .\nRelated substances. Determine . by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n......\nBuffer solution. AO.5 per cent w/v solution of ammonium \nacetate in water.\nTest solution. Transfer a suitable quantity ofthe syrup \ncontaining. 10 mg of Risperidone to a 50-ml volumetric flask, \nadd 25 ml of the buffer solution, fill with methanol almost to \nvolume and mix. Allow to cool to room temperature and dilute \nwith methanol to volume. \n,\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 2.5 mg of risperidone related \ncompounds mixture IPRS in 2 ml of methanol. Add 2 ml of \nwater and 5 ml of the buffer solution and allow to cool to room \ntemperature, dilute to 10.0 ml with methanol.\nReference solution (bf A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \nrisperidone IPRS in methanol.\nReference solution (c). Transfer 10.0 ml of reference solution \n(b) to a 50-ml volumetric'iflask. Add 10 ml of water and 25 ml of \nthe buffer solution and allow to cool to room temperature, \ndilute, with wezAmm/.to volume.\nReference solution (^.Transfer 2.0 ml of reference solution\n(b) to a 50-ml volumetric flask. Add 10 ml of water, and 25 ml of \nthe buffer solution and allow to cool to room temperature, \ndilute with methanol to volume.\nChromatographic system \n.\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (3 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 78 volumes of the buffer\nsolution and 22 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 275 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor\nRisperidone m-A’-oxidc'\n0.33\n1.03\nB icyclori speridone2\n0.43\n1.49,\nRisperidone Z-oxirne3*\n0.53 ' ' \"\n—\nRisperidone\n; 1.0 ■ •\n—\n♦Process'impurity included for identification only. Process impurities \nare controlled in the-drug substance,\" and are. not to .be reported or \nincluded in the total impurities for the drug product,\n1 c is-3 - {2 -[4-(6-Fluoro-1,2-benzisoxazpl-3 -yl)-1 -piperidin yl] ethyl }-2- \nmethyI-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-4H-pyrido[i,2-(2]pynmidih-4-drie TV-oxide,\n23-(4-Fluoro-2-hydroxypheny])-l-{2-(6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-2-methyl-4- \noxo-4/f-pyrido-[l ,2-a]pyrimidin-3-yI)ethyl}-2-az4'-l-azo'nia- \nbicyclo[2.2.2]oct-2-ene iodide,\n33-(2-{4-[(Z)'-(2,’4-Difluoroplienylj(hydroxyiminojmethyl]piperidin-l- \nyI}etliy])-2-methyl-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-477-pyrido[l,2-alpyrimidin-4-\n3502 \nOllliilllMi\n„"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/risperidone-syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Risperidone Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RISPERIDONE SYRUP\nsi r^-r^Z.^-DiflworbbwizpyBpiperidin-l-yl]ethyl]-2-methyi-6,7,8,9- \nt’*rahydro-47/ 7pyrido[l ,2-aJpyrinudin-4-one,\n’3 f2-[4’(5-fI:U!:>ro''l’2\",?enzis-o^azol'3-yl)piperidin-l-yl]ethyl]-27 \nY^]^,7'8)9-tetrahydro-4H-pyrido[l,2-tz]pyrimidin-4-one (also \nJ^nown as 5-fluordrisperidonc),\n^(6RS y3-[2-[4-(6-P'uor9'1 ,2-behzisoxazol-3-yl)piperidin-1 -yljethyl]-\n4;g;jinie(hyl-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-4Z7-pyrido[l,2-<3]pyrin!iidin-4-one.\nInject reference solution (a), and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between .the peaks due to risperidone and \nrisperidone related compound G is not less than 1.5 in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a), the tailing \nfactor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per cent in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nInject, reference solution (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to E-oxime, Z-oxime, 9-hydroxy- \nrisperidone, 5-fluororisperidone and 6-methylrisperidone is \nnot more.than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatograin obtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 per \ncent), the area of any peak due to desfluoro risperidone, \nrisperidonedifluoroketone and risperidone related compound \nG is not more than 0.5 times the area of-the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.1 \nper cent)/the area of any other secondary peak is not more \nthan 0.5 times the area ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtainedwith the reference solution (b) (0.Tper cent) and the\nsum of areas of all the secondary peaks is not more than 1.5 \ntimes the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram\n? obtained-withreference solution(b) (0.3 per cent). Ignore any\npeakwith an area less than 0.25 times the area ofthe principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution\n; (b) (0.05 per cent). \n.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavymetals, Method B (10 ppm). ;\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \noh 1.0 g, dry under vacuum at 80° for 4 hours.\nDetermine by.liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolution A'. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of ammonium acetate, \na4iusted to pH ,6,0 with glacial acetic acid and filter. .\nSdliitiotiiB: A mixture of 80 volumes of acetonitrile and 20 \nvolumesof ■ tetrahydrofuran. : \n• •<•••■\nDissolve a suitable quantity ofthe substance \nUnderexamination in methanol containing 20 per cent of the \nfinal volume and dilute with the mobile phase to obtain a 0.1"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RISPERIDONE SYRUP\nsi r^-r^Z.^-DiflworbbwizpyBpiperidin-l-yl]ethyl]-2-methyi-6,7,8,9- \nt’*rahydro-47/ 7pyrido[l ,2-aJpyrinudin-4-one,\n’3 f2-[4’(5-fI:U!:>ro''l’2\",?enzis-o^azol'3-yl)piperidin-l-yl]ethyl]-27 \nY^]^,7'8)9-tetrahydro-4H-pyrido[l,2-tz]pyrimidin-4-one (also \nJ^nown as 5-fluordrisperidonc),\n^(6RS y3-[2-[4-(6-P'uor9'1 ,2-behzisoxazol-3-yl)piperidin-1 -yljethyl]-\n4;g;jinie(hyl-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-4Z7-pyrido[l,2-<3]pyrin!iidin-4-one.\nInject reference solution (a), and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between .the peaks due to risperidone and \nrisperidone related compound G is not less than 1.5 in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a), the tailing \nfactor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per cent in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nInject, reference solution (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to E-oxime, Z-oxime, 9-hydroxy- \nrisperidone, 5-fluororisperidone and 6-methylrisperidone is \nnot more.than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatograin obtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 per \ncent), the area of any peak due to desfluoro risperidone, \nrisperidonedifluoroketone and risperidone related compound \nG is not more than 0.5 times the area of-the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.1 \nper cent)/the area of any other secondary peak is not more \nthan 0.5 times the area ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtainedwith the reference solution (b) (0.Tper cent) and the\nsum of areas of all the secondary peaks is not more than 1.5 \ntimes the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram\n? obtained-withreference solution(b) (0.3 per cent). Ignore any\npeakwith an area less than 0.25 times the area ofthe principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution\n; (b) (0.05 per cent). \n.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavymetals, Method B (10 ppm). ;\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \noh 1.0 g, dry under vacuum at 80° for 4 hours.\nDetermine by.liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolution A'. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of ammonium acetate, \na4iusted to pH ,6,0 with glacial acetic acid and filter. .\nSdliitiotiiB: A mixture of 80 volumes of acetonitrile and 20 \nvolumesof ■ tetrahydrofuran. : \n• •<•••■\nDissolve a suitable quantity ofthe substance \nUnderexamination in methanol containing 20 per cent of the \nfinal volume and dilute with the mobile phase to obtain a 0.1"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Per cent w/v solution. \n■ ■\nTest solution (&/-Dilure test solution (a) with the mobile phase \nto obtain a 0.02 per cent w/v solution of risperidone.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve a suitable quantity of \nrisperidone IPRS in methanol containing 20 per cent of the \nfinal volume and dilute with the mobile phase to obtain a 6.1 \nper cent w/v solution of risperidone: > . 1 x;; \n- .:■■■■■\nReference solution (bf Dilute reference solution (a) with the \nmobile phase to obtain a 0.02 per cent w/v solution of \nrisperidone.\nReference solution (c). A 0.002 per cent w/v solution of \nrisperidone related compound G IPRS in reference solution \n(b). \n■■ ■ \n‘ \n■\nChromatographic system ‘\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\n.octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n-- column temperature; 50°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 80 volumes of solution A and\n20 volumesof solution B,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 275 nm,\n- - injection volume: 10 pl.\nRun the chromatogram twice the retention time of the principal \npeak..\nThe relative retention time with reference to risperidone for \nrisperidone related compound G is about 1.1.\nInject reference solution (b) and (c). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to risperidone and \nrisperidone related compound G is not less than 1.5 in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c), the tailing \nfactor is not more than.2.0 and the relative standard.deviation \nfor replicate injections-is not more, than 1.0 per cent in the \nchromatogram obtained with; reference solution (b) for \nrisperidone peak. \n.uU-p \n. \n•\nInject reference solution (b) and test solution (b).\nCalculate the content of C23H27FN4O2. . , ,\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at temperature not \nexceeding 30°.\nRisperidone Syrup"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/risperidone-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Risperidone Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RISPERIDONE TABLETS\nIP 202? ... .........   '....................................\nIP\nRISPERIDONE SYRUP\nStore protected from light, at a temperature not Inject the reference solution and the test solution,\nInject reference solution (a), (c) and (d). The test is not valj^ \nunless the resolution between the peaks due \nbicyclbrisperidone and risperidone Z-oxime is not less than\"\nexceeding 30°. Do not freeze. \nCalculate the content of C23H27FN4O2 in the medium.\n' \nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\n1.5 in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n■\nthe relative standard deviation for replicate injections is not I \nmore than 5.0 per cent in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (c) and the signal-to-noise ratio for the \nprincipal peak is not less than 10.0 in the chromatogra^SlI \nobtained with reference solution (d). \n;\nC23H27FN4O2.\nRisperidone Tablets\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay with the following \nmodifications.\nRisperidoneTablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nDilute sodium hydroxide solution. To 1000 ml of water in a\nrisperidone, CojH? 7FN4O2.\nbeaker, add 0.1 Msodium hydroxide dropwise to obtain a pH \nof about 8.5.\nUsual strengths. 1 mg; 2 mg; 3 mg; 4 mg.\nInject reference solution (c) and the test, solution. In the 11 \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the. area of any f 1 \npeak corresponding to risperidone ciy-JV-pxide and \nbicyclorisperidone, each .of,, is not more than the area of the H \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c) (0.5 per cent), the area bf any other secondary | \npeak is not more than 0.4 times the area ofthe principal peak in. \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) (0.2 /1 \nper cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks \nis not more than twice the area of'the principal peak in the ? | \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) (1.0 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.2 times the area ■; | \nof the principalpeak in the\" chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (c) (0.1 per cent). ' \n\"\" J"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RISPERIDONE TABLETS\nIP 202? ... .........   '....................................\nIP\nRISPERIDONE SYRUP\nStore protected from light, at a temperature not Inject the reference solution and the test solution,\nInject reference solution (a), (c) and (d). The test is not valj^ \nunless the resolution between the peaks due \nbicyclbrisperidone and risperidone Z-oxime is not less than\"\nexceeding 30°. Do not freeze. \nCalculate the content of C23H27FN4O2 in the medium.\n' \nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\n1.5 in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n■\nthe relative standard deviation for replicate injections is not I \nmore than 5.0 per cent in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (c) and the signal-to-noise ratio for the \nprincipal peak is not less than 10.0 in the chromatogra^SlI \nobtained with reference solution (d). \n;\nC23H27FN4O2.\nRisperidone Tablets\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay with the following \nmodifications.\nRisperidoneTablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nDilute sodium hydroxide solution. To 1000 ml of water in a\nrisperidone, CojH? 7FN4O2.\nbeaker, add 0.1 Msodium hydroxide dropwise to obtain a pH \nof about 8.5.\nUsual strengths. 1 mg; 2 mg; 3 mg; 4 mg.\nInject reference solution (c) and the test, solution. In the 11 \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the. area of any f 1 \npeak corresponding to risperidone ciy-JV-pxide and \nbicyclorisperidone, each .of,, is not more than the area of the H \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c) (0.5 per cent), the area bf any other secondary | \npeak is not more than 0.4 times the area ofthe principal peak in. \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) (0.2 /1 \nper cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks \nis not more than twice the area of'the principal peak in the ? | \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) (1.0 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.2 times the area ■; | \nof the principalpeak in the\" chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (c) (0.1 per cent). ' \n\"\" J"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Dilute hydrogen peroxide solution. Dilute 1 ml of hydrogen \nperoxide solution (10 volume) with water to 500 ml.\nA. Powder an appropriate number of tablets to prepare about \n0.055 per cent w/v solution of risperidone in ethyl acetate, \nshake for30minutes and centrifuge for 20 minutes. Evaporate \n5 ml ofthe supernatant liquid with the aid of a stream of nitrogen \nto dryriess; the residue complies with the following test.\nReference solution.Drs>so\\ve 10 mg of risperidone IPRS in 10 \nml of dilute sodium hydroxide solution in a 100-ml volumetric \nflask. Store the flask at 90° for 24 hours. Cool the solution to \nroom temperature. Add 10 ml of dilute hydrogen peroxide \nsolution to the flask, and store at 90° for an additional two \nhours. Cool the mixture to room temperature, and dilute with \nmethanol to volume.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare’ the spectrum with that obtained with risperidone \nIPRS' or with the reference spectrum of risperidone.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nCorrection\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Total microbial count not more : | \nthan, 1.00 CFU per ml, total yeast and mould count not more t \nthan 1Q CFU per ml. 1 ml is free from Escherichia coli. / \n|\nBicyclorisperidone1\n0.68\n1.23\nRisperidone\n1.0 ■\n—"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nRisperidone /ram-V-oxidc2\n1.65\n—\nOther tests: Comp ly with the tests stated under Oral Liquids! |\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 500 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid,\nRisperidone c/x-A-oxide3\n1.81\n1.05\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as : g \ndescribed under Related substances with the following g \nmodifications. . . \nj\nIndividual unidentified impurities —\n—\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nTotal impurities\n—\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 2.5 mg risperidone related | \ncompounds mixture IPRS in 2 ml of methanol. Add 2 ml of g \nwater and 5 ml of the buffer solution and allow to cool to room | \ntemperature, dilute to 10.0 ml with methanol. \nI\n'3“(4-fluoro-2-hydroxypheriyl)-l-[2-(6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-2-methyl-4- \noxo-4//-pyrido-[i,2-a]pyrimidin-3-yl)ethyl]-2-aza-l-azoniabicyclo \n[2.2.2]oct-2-ene iodide,\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium.\n2/rnM5-3-[2-[4-(6^fluoro-l ,2-benzisoxazoi-3-yl)-l-piperidinyl]ethyl]- \n6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-2-methyl-4//-pyrido[l,2-a]pyrimidin-4-one, A7- \noxide monohydrate,\nReference solution. Dissolve a quantity of risperidone IPRS \nin the dissolution medium and dilute with the same solvent to \nobtain a solution having a known concentration similar to the \ntest solution.\nCorrection\nReference solution (bf A 0.1 per cent w/y. solution of \nrisperidone IPRS in methanol. Transfer 10.0 ml ofthe solution \nto a 50-ml volumetric flask. Add 10 ml of water and 25 ml ofthe \nbuffer solution and allow to cool to room temperature, dilute \nto volume with methanol.\n3c/s-3-[2-[4-(6-fluoro-1,2-benzisoxazol-3-yl)-1 -piperidinyl]ethyl]- \n6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-2-methyl-427-pyrido[l ,2-a]pyrimidin-4-one, N-\noxide monohydrate.\nChromatographic system\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to risperidone /ram-A-oxide \nand risperidone cA-7V-oxide is not less than 1.2.\n~ a stainless steel column 15 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\n- octadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test ishot valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to bicyclorispqndoiie \nand risperidone 2-oxime is not less than 1.5 in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 1.0 per cent \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b). ,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of water, 35.\nInject the test solution. The area of any peak corresponding \nto bicyclorisperidone, and risperidone m-A'-oxide is not more \nthan 0.5 per cent, the area of any other secondary peak is not \nmore than 0.3 per cent and the sum of areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated by area\nvolumes of acetonitrile and 0.1 volume of trifluoroacetic \nacid, adjust the pH to 3.0 with ammonia solution,\n~ flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n'• \" Spectrophotometer set at 237 nm,\ninjection volume: 50 pl.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. ;;-x, :\nnormalisation.\ninject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nRelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \n^2.0 per cent.\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets.\nDetermine the weight per ml ofthe oral solution (2.4:29) and \ncalculate the content of (^I^FNR)?.. \n- -;i*\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), using the \nchromatographic system as described under Dissolution, \nexcept using\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n.\nTest solution. Transfer 1 tablet to a 100-ml volumetric flask. \nAdd 50 ml of 0.7 ^hydrochloric acid and shake mechanical ly \nfor 30 minutes, dilute to the volume with 0.1 Mhydrochloric \nacid, filter.\nReference solution. Weigh accurately arid dissolve the \nrisperidone IPRS in I'M hydrochloric acid to obtain a \nsolution having a known concentration similar to the 'test \nsolution.' V •• \n. mW. ■'.y.z.r--, ••\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content bf C23H27FN4O2 in the tablet.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated tinder Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 80 volumes of methanol and 20 volumes of \nwater. '\nTest solution. Transfer not less than 10 tablets to a suitable \nvolumetric flask to obtain a 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \nrisperidone. Add an appropriate amount of water equivalent \nto 20 per cent of the total volume of the volumetric flask, and \nshake mechanically for about 30 minutes. Add a volume of \nmethanol equivalent to. 60 per cent of the total volume of the \nvolumetric flask, and shake mechanically for about 30 minutes, \ndilute with methanol to volume and filter.\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/y solution of risperidone \nIPRS in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system \n' ?/ _\n- ; a,stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous .silica (5 pm), ■;\n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 80 volumes of water, 19.5\nvolumes of acetonitrile and 0.1 volume of trifluoroacetic \nacid, adjust the pH to 3.0 with ammonium hydroxide,\nB. a mixture of 61 volumes of water, 39 \nvolumes of methanol and 0. Ivolume of trifluoroacetic \nacid, adjust the pH to 3.0 with ammonium hydroxide, \n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\nflow rate: 2.5 ml per minute, ■ •i\"\n- ! spectrophotometer set at 275 nm, ?\n- injection volume: 20 pl. : \n1 j\nTime\nMobile phase:A\n. .Mobile phase B\n(in min.)\n...' (per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v):\n. 0\n: TOO.: .\n8 \n. ; ■ , \n100 uw.■■■ W'V-W; TW-W v:\n. ' 16\n■ 100 ■\n20\n0\n100\n..21\n100-\n25\n100\n0 \n■'./\nIP 2i\nRISPERIDONE TABLETS\nInject the ■reference solution. The test is not Valid unless ti \ntailing factor is not more than 2.5 and the relative standair \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cenf\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. |\nCalculate"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorptionspectrophptometry (2;.4;6): \nCpmpare the spectrum with that obtained with ritodrine \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of ritodrine \nhydrochloride.\nB. A.1.0 per cent w/v solution gives the reactions, of chl^Kfe \ncm'. ... \n.7'■ . L/77S"},{"name":"Tests","text":"... .\npH (2.4.24). 4.5 to 6.0, determined on a 2.0 percent w/ysolution.\nRelated substances. Determine