0.94 g of sodium I-hexanesulphonate S \nto 1000 ml with water, adjusted to pH 4.0 with dilute f \nhydrochloric acid, and 45' volumes 'ofdcefcmitrile. ’y\n- flow rate: 2.5 ml per minute, \n; \n5- \ny\n- spectrophotometer set at 239 rim, i\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate inj ectionsisnot more i \nthan 2.0 per cent. \n.-b’ \ny/.t\nInject the reference solution and,the test soluti"},{"name":"Identification","text":"ui \"/-¿dOUvVD? ■ // \n\\ ff'ff y. //. \ndj TI m T Acf\nA.Detcrminc byinfrared absorption spectrophotometry(2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rivdstigttii^\nIp2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ritonavir","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ritonavir"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ritonavir\nMol. Wt. 721.0\nRitonavir is (5S,8S,lOS,IIS)-10-hydroxy-2-methyl-\n5-(1-methylethyl)-I-[2-(1-methylethyl)-4-thiazolyl]-3,6-dioxo-\n8,11-bis(phenylmethyl)-2,4,7,12-tetraazatridecan-13-oic acid\n5-thiazolylmethyl ester.\nRitonavir contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent of C37H48N60SS2, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDose. 100 mg twice daily.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Ritonavir\nMol. Wt. 721.0\nRitonavir is (5S,8S,lOS,IIS)-10-hydroxy-2-methyl-\n5-(1-methylethyl)-I-[2-(1-methylethyl)-4-thiazolyl]-3,6-dioxo-\n8,11-bis(phenylmethyl)-2,4,7,12-tetraazatridecan-13-oic acid\n5-thiazolylmethyl ester.\nRitonavir contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent of C37H48N60SS2, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDose. 100 mg twice daily.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometrY (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with ritonavir RS or\nwith the reference spectrum ofritonavir.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak due\nto ritonavir in the chromatogram obtained with the reference\nsolution.\nC.Meltingpoint.119°to 123°(2.4.21)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). +7.0° to +10.5°, determined\nin a 0.2 per cent w/v solution in methanol.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14), as described in the Assay and calculate the percentage\nofeach impurity peak in the chromatogram obtained with the\ntest solution. Not more than 0.5 per cent of any individual\nimpurity and not more than 1.0 per cent oftotal impurities is\nfound.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent\nWater (2.3.43). Notmore than 0.5 per cent, determined on 2.0 g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 25.0 mg of the substance under\nexamination in sufficient of a mixture of 45 volumes of\nacetonitrile and 55 volumes ofwater to produce 100.0 ml.\nReference solution. A 0.025 per cent w/v solution ofritonavir\nRS in a mixture of45 volumes ofacetonitrile and 55 volumes\nofwater.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 /lm),\n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 45 volumes ofacetonitrile\nand 55 volumes of a buffer solution prepared by\nRlTONAVIR CAPSULES\ndissolving 3.4 g ofsodium acetate and 0.94 g ofsodium\nhexanesulphonate in 1000 ml of water and adjusting\nthe pH to 4.0 with hydrochloric acid,\n-\nflow rate. I ml per minute,\n-\nspectrophotometer set at 239 urn,\n-\ninjection volume. 20 Ill.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\ncolumn efficiency determined from the ritonavir peak is not\nless than 1000 theoretical plates, the tailing factor is not more\nthan 2.0, and the relative standard deviation for replicate\ninjections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content ofC37H48N60sS2.\nStorage. Store protected from light."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rituximab-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rituximab Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rituximab Injection\nRituximab is a genetically engineered chimeric murine/human \nmonoclonal antibody directed against the CD20 antigen found \non the surface of normal and malignant B lymphocytes. This \nIgG l kappa antibody contains murine light and heavy chain\nvariable regions, and human gamma I heavy chain and kappa \nlight chain constant regions. Rituximab is composed of two \nheavy chains each having 451 amino acids and two light chains \neach having 213 amino acids.\nRituximab Injection is a sterile mixture of rituximab with suitable \nbuffering and stabilizing agents,\nRituximab contains not less than 80 per cent and not more \nthan 125 percent of the stated potency\nThe injection complies with the requirements stated under \nParenteral Preparations and the following requirements. .\nUsual strengths. 100 mg per vial, 500 mg per vial, 600 mg per. \nvial. ■ - :\nDescription. A clear to opalescent, colourless to pale yellow \nliquid."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rituximab Injection\nRituximab is a genetically engineered chimeric murine/human \nmonoclonal antibody directed against the CD20 antigen found \non the surface of normal and malignant B lymphocytes. This \nIgG l kappa antibody contains murine light and heavy chain\nvariable regions, and human gamma I heavy chain and kappa \nlight chain constant regions. Rituximab is composed of two \nheavy chains each having 451 amino acids and two light chains \neach having 213 amino acids.\nRituximab Injection is a sterile mixture of rituximab with suitable \nbuffering and stabilizing agents,\nRituximab contains not less than 80 per cent and not more \nthan 125 percent of the stated potency\nThe injection complies with the requirements stated under \nParenteral Preparations and the following requirements. .\nUsual strengths. 100 mg per vial, 500 mg per vial, 600 mg per. \nvial. ■ - :\nDescription. A clear to opalescent, colourless to pale yellow \nliquid."},{"name":"Identification","text":"By method A.BorD and C\nA. It complies with the biological activity as described under \nassay.\nB. Determine by capillary zone electrophoresis (2.4.32).\nTest solution. Dilute a suitable volume of the injection with \nwater to obtain a solution having concentration 0.05 per cent \nw/v of rituximab.\nReference solution. A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of rituximab \nIPRS in water.\nBlank control solution. Dilute suitable volume of product \ndiluent with waterto obtained similar concentration to that of \ntest solution.\nCapillary system\n- neutral capillary (50 pm 0 x 560 mm) or N-CHO coated\ncapillary (50 pm 0 x 650 mm) or other suitable capillary,'\n- temperature: 20°,\n- sample temperature. 10°, \n• •'.-¿sD\n- spectrophotometer set at 214 nm, \n; : a q\n- injection volume: lOOpl \n: h i y\nCZE buffer. 0.04 MÉ-aminó-n-caproic acid containing \n0.2 per cent w/v of hydroxyprOpylmethylcellulose, adjusted \nto pH 4/5 with glacial acetic acid. \n1 \n.\nPreconditioning of the capillary. Rinse the capillary using!\nCZE buffer until visual evaluations of the electropherograms \ndemonstrate consistent profiles for pre-conditioning of \ncapillary. Inject blank, the reference solution and the test \nsolution. Complete the analysis with an injection of the \nreference solution and the reagent blank.\n. .. ;,V.\nMigration. Apply field strength of 30 kV for 15 minutes using' \nthe CZE buffer as the electrolyte in both buffer reservoirs. \nThe main peak elutes in between 15 to 17 minutes Ar\nIP 2022\nRITUXIMAB 'injection\nThe difference in the migration time for the main peak of the \nfirst and last reference solution injections must be equator \nless than 0.1 minute\nPositive identity is confirmed if the difference in migration \ntime between the main peak of the reference solution and test \nsolution is equal or less than 0.1 minute."},{"name":"Tests","text":"C. Determine by Sodium Dodecyl Sulphate-Polyacrylamide \nGel Electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) (2.4.12).\nIn the tests for Impurities with molecular masses differing \nfrom that of rituximab. The principal band in the \nelectropherogram obtained with test solution is similar in \nposition and intensity to the principal band in the \nelectropherogram obtained with reference solution.\nD. Iso Electric Focusing (IEF) using capillary electrophoresis \n(CE) (2.4.33). Determine by Capillary Electrophoresis (2.4.32).\nSolution (a). Catholyte (0.2 M orthophosphoric acid).\nSolution (b). Anolyte (0.3 M sodium hydroxide).\nSolution (c). Chemical mobiliser (0.35 M acetic scid).\nSolution (d). 0.5 Marginine.\nSolution (e). 0.2 M iminodiacetic acid.\nSolution '(f). 4.3 M urea.\nSolution (g). 3 M Urea- cIEF Gel). A solution prepared by \ndissloving 1.80 g urea in cIEF Gel and dilute to 10.0 ml with \ncIEF gel.\nTest solution. Prepared by mixing 200 pl of solution® 12.0 pl \nofpharmalyte 3-10 carrier ampholytes, 20.0 pl of solution (d), \n2.0 pl of solution(e), 2.0 pl of pl marker 10.0, pl marker 7.0, pl \nmarker 5.5 and pl marker 4.1 and add 10 pl of protein \nconcentration within 5 mg per ml to 10 mg per ml.\nReference solution. Prepared in the same manner as the test \nsolution using rituximab IPRS.\nCapillary System\n- neutral Capillary. 50 pm,\n- size: effective length is at least 40 cm,\n- temperature: 10°,\n- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm.\nTurn on the UV lamp and allow the system to warm up for at \nleast 30 minutes prior experimentation. Thé protein \nconcentration within 5 mg per ml to 10 mg per ml. The protein \nsample must have a salt concentration below 0.05 M since \nhigh salt content is detrimental to cIEF separation. The protein \nsample can be buffer exchanged before use. The identity is \nestablished comparing the product with reference preparation. \nIt complies with the limits approved by National Regulatory \nAuthority.\nIn the electropherogram obtained with reference solution, the \npl of principal band is 9.3 ± 0.2. In the electropherogram\nobtained with the test solution^ no band other than the principal \nband is more intense than the principal ban d in the \nelectropherogram obtained with reference solution.\npH (2:4.24). 6.3 to 6.7.\nOsmolality (2.4.23). Not less than 250mosmol per kg\nImpurities with molecular masses differing from that of \nrituximab\nDetermine by us ing Method A or B.\nA. Determine by using Capillary Electrophoresis by SDS (CE- \nSDS) (2.4.32) under reducing and non-reducing conditions \nusing IgG heterogeneity kit or equivalent\nProcedure. Analyze the preparation under examination and \nrituximab IPRS against the molecular weight marker using an \nelectrophoretic method capable of giving separation in the \nrange 10- 225/250 kDa followed by UV detection.\nDilute the preparation under examination and rituximab IPRS \nin an appropriate diluent to obtain a minimum concentration \nto be detected by the capillary electrophoresis for example the \ntotal protein concentration should be within the range of 0.5mg \nper ml to 4mg per ml and use a validated method for reducing \nand non-reducing condition.\nTest solution for CE-SDS under reducing condition. Mix \n68.0 pl of water, 100 pl of SDS sample buffer, 2 pl of 10 kDa \ninternal standard, 10 pl of mercaptoethanol and 20 pl of \npreparation under examination. Vortex it and heat the mixture \nat 70° for 3 minutes. Cool it down to room temperature. Transfer \nit to vials for capillary electrophoresis analysis. Test solution \nmay also be prepared as per application note.\nReference solution for CE-SDS under reducing condition. \nPrepare in the same manner as the te.s/ solution for CE-SDS \nunder reducing condition using rituximab IPRS.\nTests solution for CE-SDS under non-reducing condition. \nMix 68.0 pl of water, 100 pl of SDS sample buffer, 2 pl of 10 kDa \ninternal standard, 10 pl of 0.25M iodoacetamide, \n20 pl of preparation under examination. Vortex it and heat the \nmixture at 70° for 5 minutes. Cool it down at room temperature. \nTransfer it to PCR vials for analysis. Test solution may also be \nprepared as per application note.\nReference solutionfor CE-SDS under non-reducing \n'condition. Prepare in the same manner as the test solution for\nCE-SDS under non-reducing condition using rituximab \nIPRS.\nCapillary system\n- \nv material. Bare fused silica coated,\n- capillary: 30.2 cm x 50 pm,\neffective length: 20 cm,\n- detector: PDA 220 nm,\nIP 2022\nRITUXIMAB INJECTION\n. \ncapillary temperature: 25°,\n- sample temperature: 10“,\n- polarity. Anode at the inlet and Cathode at the outlet, , \n- injection time: 5.0 kV for 20.0 seconds at the electrode\npolarity reverse,\n- separation voltage: 15.0 kV for 30.0 minutes at polarity\n. reverse.\nFollowed by pre-conditioning of capillary with suitable buffer, \ninject test solution and reference solution by keeping between- \nrun rinsing using suitable buffer.\nMigration. Apply a field strength 5.0 kV for 20.0 seconds at \nthe electrode polarity reverse at the time of injection and\n15.0 kV for 30.0 minutes at polarity reverse as separation \nvoltage. Maintain polarity anode at the inlet and cathode at \nthe outlet.\nUnder reducing condition, in electropherogram obtained with \nreference solution and test solution identify the peaks \ncorresponding to light chain, non-glycosylated heavy chain \nand heavy chain.\nCalculate the percentage of non-glycosylated heavy chain in \nthe solution. Calculate the. corrected percentage area of light \nchain, non-glycosylated heavy chain and heavy chain. The \ncorrected percentage area of non-glycosylated heavy chainis. \nNot more than 2.0 per cent.\nUnder non-reducing condition, in electropherogram obtained \nwith reference solution and test solution identify the peaks \ncorresponding tolow molecular weight impurities and IgG. \nCalculate the percentage of impurities in the sample solution. \nCalculate the corrected percentage area of low molecular \nweight impurities and IgG.. The corrected percentage of low \nmolecular weight impurities is not mo"},{"name":"Assay","text":"Bacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 1EU per mg.\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations.\nProtein. Determine by ultraviolet/visible spectrophotometry \n(2.4.7) using Method A or B.\nMethodA\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation under examination with \nwater to obtain a solution having concentration, 0.5 mg per \nml\nReference solution. Dissolve suitable quantity of Rituximab \nIPRS in water to obtained a solution having concentration, \n0.5 mg per ml.\nBlank control solution. The appropriate product formulation \nbuffer is diluted in a comparable manner as the test solution.\nSpectral range. 240 - 500 nm.\nAbsorption maximum. 278 nm.\nPipette the test and reference solutions into a quartz cuvette \nand scan the absorbance in the spectral range against the \nblank control solution. Record the absorbance \nat (278 ±\n2 nm) and A320 at 320nm. Calculate j A320! /A^ x 100. Calculate \nthe corrected Aniax by subtracting the A320 value from A^.\n| A32Of /Amax x 100 must be equal or less than 3.0 per cent for \neach sample measurement.\nCalculate the protein concentration as follows.\nProlein concentration =\ncorrected Am„ of sample x concentration of reference . . ... .\n---------- - \n:-----------------------x total dilution factor \ncorrected of reference\nAlternately, an established extinction coefficient may be used \nto calculate protein concentration.\nAcceptance criteria. Not less than 90 per cent and not more\nthan 110 per cent of the stated amount of protein.\nDetermine the protein content as per appendix (2.7.1) or take \nreading at 280 nm and calculate the concentration of the \nsample(s) using following formula\n_ \n. \n. ,, Average absorbance at 280 nm x dilution factor\nConcentration (mg/ml) = —--------------:::-----------------------\nExtinction coefficient\nNOTE — Value of Extinction coefficient to be provided by\nthe manufacturer.\nAcceptance criteria. Not less than 90 per cent and not more \nthan 110 per cent of the stated amount of protein.\nPotency. Determine by complement dependent cytotoxicity \nassay by using WIL2-S cells.\nMedium. Dissolve 485 ml of RPMI1640,10 ml of1MHEPES,\n6.8 ml of 6.5 g of bovine serum albumin and 5 ml of Penicillin \nand Streptomycin solution (Final concentration to be 100-\n120 units per ml of Penicillin and 0.10-0.12 mg per ml of \nstreptomycin) or any suitable antibiotic in water or any other \nsolvent, if indicated and filter aseptically through 0.22 pm \nfilter using sterile filtration assembly.\nMix 500 ml of RPMI 1640, 10 ml of 1MHEPES, 6.8 ml of \n7.5 per cent bovine serum albumin and 1 ml of gentamycin \nsulphate (50 mg per ml) or any suitable antibiotic in water or \nany other solvent, if indicated and filter aseptically through \n0.22pm filter using sterile filtration assembly. \n0\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation under examination with \nmedium to obtain a concentration of0.0098 to 2.5 pg per ml.\nReference solutions (standard carve). Prepare several \nconcentrations of rituximab IPRS bracketing the test solution \nconcentration to determine an assay calibration curve.: ; ■\nAssay control solution. Use a reference solutiba<and/or;\nqualified lot of drug product as an assay control. Dilute in the \nsame manner as the test solution. \n, ,< w:! 1: -\nHuman complement. Human complement is diluted\nappropriately based on titration assays. 5 i A ;\nSOMATROPIN\nIP 2022\nCell culture solution. Prepare cultures of WIL2-S cells, a \nhuman B-lymphoblastoid cell line (ATCC CRL-8885) or \nequivalent, in the medium.\nCell suspension.Adjust the cell concentration to 0.8 to \n1.2 xlO6 cells per ml in the medium.\nTo individual wells of a 96-well tissue culture plate, add 50 pl \nof each standard curve solution, test solution or assay control \nsolution to be tested, followed by 50 pl of the cell suspension \nand then 50 pl diluted human complement. Incubate in a \nhumidified CO2 incubator for 1 - 2 hours at 37°. Add 50 pl \nresazurin reagent.per well. Incubate plate for an additional\n15-26 hours. Agitate plate for 5 to 10 minutes at room \ntemperature on a mini orbital shaker. Evaluate using a \nfluorescence plate reader with excitation wavelength at \n530 nm and emission wavelength at 590 nm.\nRelative fluorescence units of the standards are plotted against \nstandard concentrations to generate a standard curve using a \n4-parameter curve fitting program. Sample and control potency \nvalues are calculated using the standard curve.\nThe estimated potency is not less than 80 per cent and not \nmore than 125 per cent of the stated potency. The Confidence \nlimits (P=0.95) of the estimated potency are not less than 69.2 \nper cent and not more than 132.6. per cent of the stated potency\nStorage. Store at 2 to 8° in an airtight container.\nLabelling. The label states (1) Content of rituximab in mg per \nml (2) name of product with generic name (3) drug product \n(injection) in mg per ml (4) Potency; (4) storage temperature.\nSomatropin\nSomatrbpinum\nFPTIPLSRLF \nDNAMLRAHRL \nHQLAFDTYQE \nFEEAYfPKEQ\nKYSFLQNPQT \nSLCFSES1PT \nPSNREETQQK \nSNLELLRiSL\nLLIQSWLEPV \nQFLRSVFANS \nLVYGASDSNV \nYDLLKDLEEG\nIQTLMGRLED \nGSPRTGQIFK \nQTYSKFDTNS \nHNDDALLKNY\n. r~—J \nr------“i\nGLLYCFRKDM , DKVETFLRIV \nQCRSVEGSCG \nF\nC<)9oili5;^N'2i?030iiS' \n■ \nMol. Wt. 22.125\nSomatropin is a protein having the structure (191 amino-acid \nresidues) of the major component of growth hormone \nproduced by the human pituitary. Somatropin contains not \nless than 91 per cent and not more than 105 per cent \n(anhydrous substance) of the stated potency. By convention, \nfor the purpose of labelling somatropin preparations, 1 mg of \nanhydrous somatropin (CwoHis^N^OjooS?) is. equivalent to\n4682\nwww.webofpharma.com\n3.0 JU of biological activity.\nSomatropin is produced by a method based on recombinant \nDNA (rDNA) technology. During the course of product \ndevelopment, it must be demonstrated that the manufacturing \nprocess produces a product having a biological activity of \nnot less than 2.5 IU per mg, using a validated bioassay based \non growth promotion and appr"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by capillary electrophoresis (capillary zone \nelectrophoresis) (2.4.32) as described in the test for charged \nvariants with the following modifications.\nInject test solution (b) under pressure or vacuum, in the \nfollowing sequence: sample injection for at least 3 seconds? \nthen CZE buffer injection for 1 second.\nIn the electropherogram obtained with the test solution (b) \nonly one principal peak corresponding to somatropin is \ndetected and no doubling of this peak is observed.\nB. Determine by Liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as described \nin the test for related proteins\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the' \nretention time of principal peak is similar to that ofthe principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference, solution.\nC. Determine by peptide mapping (2.3.47).\nTest solution. Prepare a solution of the substance under \nexamination in 0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution \npH 7.5 to obtain a solution containing 2.0 mg per ml of \nsomatropin and transfer about 1.0 ml to a tube made from a? \nsuitable material such as polypropylene. Prepare a 1 mg per ml \nsolution of trypsin for peptide mapping in 0.05M tris--, \nhydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 and add 30 pl to the \nsolution of the preparation under examination. Cap the tube \nand place in a water-bath at 37° for at least 4 hours. Remove \nsample from the water-bath and stop the reaction immediately, \\ \nfor example by freezing. If analyzed immediately using an \nautomatic inj ector, maintain at 2° to 8°.\nReference solution. Prepare at the same time and in the same \nmanner as for the test solution, but using somatropin IPRff \ninstead of preparation under examination. \n;....\nChromatographic system. \n••d\n.- a: stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilyl silica gel (5-10 pm) with a pore size of 30 nm,r\nIP 2022\nSOMATROPIN\n- column temperature: 30°,\n- mobile phase: A. dilute 1 ml of trifluoroacetic acid to\n1000 ml with water,\nB. to 100 ml of water, add 1 ml of \ntrifluoroacetic acid and dilute to 1000 ml with \nacetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 214 nm,\n- injection volume: 100 pl\nTime \nMobile phase A \nMobile phase B\n(in min) \n(per cent v/v) (per cent v/v)\n0 \n100 \n0\n20 \n80 \n20\n40 \n75 \n' \n25\n65 \n50 \n50\n70 \n20 \n80\nInject the test solution and the reference solution. The \nchromatogram obtained with each solution is qualitatively \nsimilar to the reference chromatogram of somatropin IPRS \ndigest.\nThe profile of chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorresponds to that of the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution.\nD. Determine by Size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16) as \ndescribed in assay.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the \nprincipal peak is similar in retention time and size to the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related proteins. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) \nusing the normalisation procedure.\nMaintain the solutions at 2° to 8°and use within 24 hours. If an \nautomatic injector is used, maintain it at 2° to 8°.\nTest solution. Dilute preparation under examination with \n0.05Mtris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5, to obtain a \nconcentration of 2 mg per ml of somatropin.\nReference solution (a). Dilute somatropin IPRS in 0.05 M \ntris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5, to obtain a \nconcentration of 2 mg per ml of somatropin.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve the contents of a vial of \nsomatropin/desamidosomatropin IPRS in 0.05M tris­\nhydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 to obtain a concentration \nof 2 mg per ml of somatropin. \n. \n;\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, singly end­\ncapped butylsilyl silica gel (5 pm) and with pore size of\n30 nm; a silica saturation column is placed between the \npump and the injector valve,\n- column temperature: 45°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 29 volumes ofpropanol and\n71 volumes of 0.05 M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution \npH 7.5\n- flow rate: 0.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 220 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nPrecondition the column with 200 ml to 500 ml of a 0.1 per cent \nv/v solution of trifluoroacetic acid in a 50 per cent v/v solution \nof acetonitrile-, repeat as necessary, to improve column \nperformance.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks corresponding to \ndesamido somatropin and somatropin is at least 1.0.\nInject reference solution (a) and test solution, the sum of the \nareas of all the peaks other than the principal peak is not \nmore than 6.0 per cent of the total area of all the peaks. \nRelative retention with reference to somatropin: for \ndesamidosomatropin is about 0.85.\nDimer and related substances of higher molecular mass. \nDetermine by size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16), using \nthe normalization procedure.\nTest solution. Dilute the substance under examination in \n0.025Mphosphate buffer solution pH 7.0, to obtain a \nconcentration of 1.0 mg per ml of somatropin.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve the contents of a vial of \nsomatropin IPRS in 0.025 Mphosphate buffer solution pH \n7.0 and dilute with the same solution to obtain a concentration \nof 1.0 mg per ml.\nReference solution (b). Place 1 vial of somatropin IPRS in an \noven at 50° for a period sufficient to generate 1 to 2 per cent of \ndimer (typically 12-24 hours). Dissolve its contents in \n0.025 Mphosphate buffer solution pH 7.0 and dilute with the \nsame solution to obtain a concentration of 1 mg per ml.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 30 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with\n, hydrophilic silica gel, of a grade suitable for fractionation\nof globular, proteins in the relative molecular mass range \nof5000 to 150000,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 3 volumes ol 2-pro panel and\n97 volumes of 0.063M tris-hydrochloride buffer \nsolution pH 7.0,\n- flow rate: 0.6 ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 214 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (a) and test solution. Relative \nretention time with reference to somatropin monomer: related\nSOMATROPIN\nsubstances of higher molecular mass is about 0.65; somatropin \ndimer is about 0.9. \n:\nInject reference solution (b), the test is hot valid unless the \nratio of the peak height of the dimer to the height above the \nbaseline of the lowest point of the curve separating the dimer \nfrom the monomer peak is not less than 2.5.\nCalculate the content of dimer, oligomers and aggregates. The \nsum of the peaks areas with retention times less than that of \nthe principal peak is maximum 4.0 per cent of total area of all \nthe peaks.\nCharged variants. Determine by Capillary electrophoresis \n(2.4.32).\nCZE buffer. Dissolve 13.2 gram of ammonium phosphate in\n1000 ml of water and adjust the pH to 6.0 using \northophosphoric acid.\nTest solution (a). Dilute the preparation under examination to \nobtain a concentration of 1 mg per ml of somatropin.\nTest solution (b).' Mix equal volumes of test solution (a) and \nthe reference solution.\nReference solution. Dissolve the contents of a vial of \nsomatropin IPRS in water and dilute with the same solvent to \nobtain a concentration of 1 mg per ml."},{"name":"Assay","text":"Capillary system\n- material, uncoated fused silica,\n- size: effective length is at least 70 cm, internal diameter\nis 50 pm,\n- temperature: 30°,\n- spectrophotometer set at 200 nm,\nSet the autosampler to store the samples at 4° during analysis.\nPreconditioning of the capillary. Rinse with IM sodium \nhydroxide for 20 minutes, with water for 20 minutes and with\nCZE buffer for 20 minutes at 2 0 psi.\nBetween-run rinsing. Rinse using 0.1 M sodium hydroxide \nfor 2 minutes and with the CZE buffer for 6 minutes.\nRinsing times may be adapted according to the length of the \ncapillary and the equipment used.\nInject test solution (a) and the reference solution under \npressure or vacuum, using the following sequence. Inject with \npressure the reference standard solution and each test sample \nfor at least 3 seconds, then CZE buffer injection for 1 second. \nThe injection time and pressure may be adapted in order to \nmeet the system suitability criteria.\nMigration. Apply a field strength of 217 V/cm (20 kV for\ncapillaries of 92 cm total length) for 80 minutes using CZE \nbuffer as the electrolyte in both buffer reservoirs. It is \nrecommended to. use fresh buffer for each separation to \ndecrease the risk of ion depletion.\nC990H1528N262O300S7\n4684\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022^\nThe electropherogram obtained with test solution (a) and \nreference solution are similar to the electropherogram of \nsomatropin supplied with somatropinlPRS. Two peaks (I15I2) \neluting prior to the principal peak and at least 2 peaks (13,I4) \neluting after the principal peak are clearly visible. Peak I2 \ncorresponds to the cleaved form and peak I4 corresponds to \nthe deaminated forms, eluting as a doublet. Relative migration \nwith reference to somatropin: deamidated forms is 1.02 to 1.11. \nIn the electropherogram obtained with the test solution the \narea of the peak due to deamidated forms is not more than \n5.0 per cent; the area of any other impurity peak is not be more \nthan 2.0 per cent and sum of areas of any peaks other than the \nprincipal peak is not more than 10.0 per cent of the total area of \nall the peaks\n„ . . ,.. \n. \n. . Migration time of peak i\nRelative Migration peak i =-----------------------------------------------------\nMigration time of Somatropin peak\nwhere, i represents a peak number. \n;\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 10.0 per cent w/w determined; \nby the semi-micro determination of water\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 5 IU per mg of \nprotein. \n-;T\nDetermine by Size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16) as \ndescribed in the test for dimer and related substances of higher1 \nmolecular mass. \n‘ ’i,s\nCalculate the content of somatropin (CggoH^gNaezGbooS?) froth.' \nthe declared content of CggoH^Ns^CbooS? in somatropirl \nIPRS. \n' ’\nStorage. Store in an airtight container, at a temperature of \n2° to 8°. If the substance is sterile, store in a sterile, airtight5; \ntamper-proof container. \n. ,\nLabelling. The label states (1) the somatropin content in mg;? \n(2) the activity in IU per mg; (3) the name and the concentration \nof any other excipients.\nSomatropin Concentrated Solution\nFPTIPLSRLF \nDNAMLRAHRL HQLAFDTYQE \nFEEAYIPKEQ r,\nKYSFLQNPQT \nSLCFSESIPT \nPSNREETQQK SNLELLRISL\nLUQSWLEPV QFLRSVFANS\nLVYGASDSNV YDLLKDLEEG\nQTYSKFDTNS HNDDALLKNY\nIQTLMGRLED GSPRTGQIFK\nQCRSVEGSCG F \n' }\nJ-----\nGLLYCFRKDM DKVETFLRIV\n' - Mol. Wt. 22,125\nIP 2022\nSOMATROPIN CONCENTRATED SOLUTION\nSomatropin Concentrated Solution is a solution containing a \nprotein having the structure (191 amino-acid residues) of the \nmajor component of growth hormone produced by the human \npituitary. It may contain buffer salts and other auxiliary \nsubstances. Somatropin Concentrated Solution contains not \nless than 91 per cent and not more than 105 per cent of stated \npotency. By convention, for the purpose of labelling \nsomatropin preparations 1 mg of anhydrous somatropin is \nequivalent to 3.0 IU of biological activity.\nSomatropin is produced by a method based on recombinant \nDNA (rDNA) technology. During the , course of product \ndevelopment, it must be demonstrated that the manufacturing \nprocess produces a product having a biological activity of \nnot less than 2.5 IU per mg, using a validated bioassay based \non growth promotion and approved by the competent \nauthority.\nHost cell derived proteins (HCP). Not more than 100 ppm.\nHost cell or vector derived DNA. Not more than 10 ng per \ndose.\nCategory. Growth Hormone\nDescription. Clear or slightly turbid, colourless solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by capillary electrophoresis (capillary zone \nelectrophoresis) (2.4.32) as described in the test for charged \nvariants with the following modifications.\nInject test solution (b) under pressure or vacuum, in the \nfollowing sequence: sample injection for at least 3 seconds \nthen CZE buffer injection for 1 second.\nIn the electropherogram obtained with the test solution (b) \nonly 1 principal peak corresponding to somatropin, is detected \nand no doubling of this peak is observed.\nB. Determine by Liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as described\nin the test for related proteins\nIn the chromatogram obtained, with the test solution the \nretention time of principal peak is similar to that of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution.\nC. Determine by Peptide mapping (2.3.47).\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation under examination in \n0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 to a \nconcentration of 2 mg per ml of somatropin in a tube made \nfrom a suitable material such as polypropylene. Prepare a 1 mg \nper ml solution of trypsin for peptide mapping in /?. 05M tris­\nhydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 and add 30 gl to the \nsolution of the preparation under examination. Cap thé tube \nand place in a water-bath at 37° for 4 hours. Remove the sample \nfrom the water-bath and stop the reaction immediately by \nfreezing. At the end of the digestion period place digests in\nthe refrigerator for later analysis (within 36 hours) or analyze \nimmediately. Digests are stable at room temperature for\n16 hours and at 5° for 48 hours.\nReference solution. Prepare reference solution as per the \nprocedure given for the test solution, ensuring that all\nprocedures are carried out simultaneously, and under identical \nconditions but using somatropin 1PRS instead of the \npreparation under examination.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilyl silica gel (5-10 gm) with a pore size of 30 nm,\n- column temperature: 30°\n- mobile phase: A. diluted ml of. trifluoroacetic acid to\n1000 ml with water,\nB. to 100 ml of water, add 1 ml of \ntrifluoroacetic acid and dilute to 1000 ml with \nacetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 214 nm,\n- injection volume: 100 gl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n'in min)\n(percent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n100\n0\n20\n80\n20\n40\n75\n25\n65\n50\n50\n70 ■\n20\n80\nInject the test solution and the reference solution. The \nchromatogram obtained with each solution is qualitatively \nsimilar to the reference chromatogram of somatropin IPRS \ndigest.\nThe profile of chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorresponds to that of the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution.\nD. Determine by Size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16) as \ndescribed in assay.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the \nprincipal peak is similar in retention time to the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nTests '\nRelated proteins. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) \nuse the normalization procedure.\nMaintain the solutions at 2° to 8°and use within 24 hours. \nIf an automatic injector is used, maintain it at 2° tp 8°.\nTest solution. Dilute preparation under examination with \n0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5, to obtain a \nconcentration of 2 mg per ml of somatropin.\n4685\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSOMATROPIN CONCENTRATED SOLUTION\nIP 2022\nReference solution (a); Dilute somatropin IPRS in 0.05 M tris­\nhydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 to obtain a concentration \nof 2 mg per ml of somatropin.\nReference solution (b): Dissolve'the contents of a vial of \nsomairopin/desamidosbmatrdpin IPRS in 0.05M tris- \nhydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 to obtain a concentration \nof 2 mg per ml of somatropin.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nbutylsilyl silica gel (5 pm), ■\n’ - column temperature: 45°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of29 volumes ofpropanol and\n71 volumes of 0.05 Mtris-hydrochloride buffer solution \npH 7.5,\n- flow rate: 0.5 ml per minute,\n; - spectrophotometer set at 220 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nPrecondition the column with 200-500 ml of a 0.1 percent v/v \nsolution of trifluoroacetic acid in a 50 per cent v/v solution \nof acetonitrile', repeat as necessary, to improve column \nperformance.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks corresponding to \ndesamidosomatropin and somatropin is at least 1.0.\nInject reference solution (a) and test solution, the sum of the \nareas of all the peaks other than the principal peak is not more \nthan 6.0 per cent of the total area of all the peaks. Relative \nretention with reference to somatropin for desamidosomatropin \nis about 0.85.\nDimer and related substances of higher molecular mass. \nDetermine by size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16), using \nthe normalization procedure.\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation under examination with \n0.025M phosphate buffer solution pH 7.0 to obtain a \nconcentration of 1 mg per ml of somatropin.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve the contents of a vial of \nsomatropin IPRS in 0.025M phosphate buffer solution \npH 7.0 and dilute with the same solution to obtain a \nconcentration of 1 mg per ml:\nReference solution (b). Place 1 vial of somatropin IPRS in an\noven at 50° for a period sufficient to generate 1 to 2 per cent of \ndimer (typically 12-24 hours). Dissolve its contents in 0.025M \nphosphate buffer solution pH 7.0 and dilute with the same\nsolution to obtain a concentration of 1 mg per ml.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 30 cm x‘7.8 mm, packed with\nhydrophilic silica gel, of a grade suitable for fractionation \nof globular proteins in the relative molecular mass range \nof5000 to 150000,\nbofpharma.com\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 3 volumes of 2-propanol and\n97 volumes of 0.063M tris-hydrochloride buffer \nsolution pH 7.0,\n- flow rate"},{"name":"Assay","text":"Determine by Size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16) as \ndescribed in the test for dimer and related substances of higher \nmolecular mass.\nCalculate the content of somatropin (C^qIJ is.^N^CfrooS ■) from \nthe declared content of C^oH^N^OsooSy in somatropin \nIPRS.\nStorage. In an airtight container, at a temperature below -20°. \nAvoid repeated freezing and thawing. If the substance is \nsterile, store in a sterile, airtight, tamper-proof container.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the somatropin content in mg \nper ml; (2) the activity in IU per mg; (3) the name and the \nconcentration of any other excipients.\nSomatropin for Injection\nSomatropin for Injection is a freeze-dried, sterile preparation \nofa protein having the structure (191 amino-acid residues) of \nthe major component of growth hormone produced by the \nhuman pituitary.\nSomatropin for Injection contains not less than 89 per cent \nand not more than 105 per cent of the stated potency. By \nconvention, for the purpose of labelling of somatropin \npreparations 1 mg of anhydrous somatropin is equivalent to \n3.0 IU of biological activity.\nSomatropin for Injection is prepared either from somatropin or \nfrom somatropin concentrated solution, or by a method based \non recombinant DNA (rDNA) technology in which the \ninjectable preparation is produced without the isolation of an \nintermediate solid or liquid bulk. In the later case, during the \ncourse of product development, it must be demonstrated that \nthe manufacturing process produces a product having a \nbiological activity of not less than 2.5 IU per mg, using a \nvalidated bioassay based on growth promotion and approved \nby the competent authority. The purified preparation, to which \nbuffers and stabilizers may be added, is filtered through a \nbacteria-retentive filter, aseptically distributed in sterile \ncontainers of glass type I (6.2.1) and freeze-dried. The \ncontainers are immediately sealed so as to exclude microbial \ncontamination and moisture.\nUsual strength. 5 mg (15 IU), 6 mg (18 IU), 10 mg (30 IU) and\n12 mg (36 IU).\nHost cell derived proteins (HCP). Not more than 100 ppm.\nHost cell or vector derived DNA. Not more than 10 ng per \ndose.\nDescription. White or almost white powder"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Where Somatropin for injection is prepared from Somatropin \nor from Somatropin concentrated solution compliance with \nthe requirements for host cell derived proteins, host cell and \nvector-derived DNA, identification test A, identification test \nC, and charged variants need not be reconfirmed by the \nmanufacturer during subsequent production of Somatropin \nfor injection.\nA. Determine by capillary electrophoresis (capillary zone \nelectrophoresis) (2.4.32) as described in the test for charged \nvariants with the following modifications.\nInject test solution (b) under pressure or-vacuum, in the \nfollowing sequence: sample injection for at least 3 seconds \nthen CZE buffer injection for 1 second.\nIn the electropherogram obtained with the test solution (b) \nonly one principal peak corresponding to somatropin, is \ndetected and no doubling of this peak is observed.\nSOMATROPIN FOR INJECTION\nIP 2022\nB. Determine by Liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as described \nin the test for related proteins.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the \nretention time of principal peak is similar to that of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution.\nC. Determine by Peptide mapping (2.3.47),\nTest solution. Prepare a solution of the substance under \nexamination in 0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH\n7.5. to obtain a solution containing 2 mg per ml of somatropin \nand transfer about 1.0 ml to a tube made from a suitable material \nsuch as polypropylene. Prepare a 1 mg per ml solution of \ntrypsin in 0.05M .tris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 \nand add 30 pl to the solution of the preparation under \nexamination. Cap the tube and place in a water-bath at 37° for \n4 hours. Remove sample horn the water-bath and stop the \nreaction immediately, for example by freezing. If analyzed \nimmediately using an automatic injector, maintain at 2° to 8°.\nReference solution. Prepare reference solution as per the \nprocedure, given for the test solution, ensuring that all \nprocedures are carried out simultaneously, and under identical \nconditions but using somatropin IPRS instead of the \nsubstance under examination.\nChromatographic system\n- stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilyl silica gel (5-10 pm) with a pore size of 30 nm, \n- column temperature: 30°,\n- mobile phase: A, dilute 1 ml of trifluoroacetic acid to\n1000 ml with water,\nB. to 100 ml of water, add 1 ml of \ntrifluoroacetic acid and dilute to 1000 ml with \nacetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 214 nm,\n- inj ection volume: 100 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase\n(in min)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v\n0\n100 ‘\n0\n20\n' \n80\n20\n40\n75 '\n25\n65\n. 50\n50\n■ 70 .\n20\n80\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. The \nchromatogram obtained with each solution is qualitatively \nsimilar to the reference chromatogram of somatropin IPRS \ndigest.\nThe profile of the chromatogram-obtained with the test \nsolution corresponds to that of the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution.\nD. Determine by Size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16) as \ndescribed in assay.\nThe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution is similar in retention time to the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related Proteins. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), using the normalization procedure.\nMaintain the solutions at 2° to 8° and use within 24 hours. If \nan automatic injector is used, maintain it at 2° to 8°,\nTest solution. Prepare the solution of the preparation under \nexamination in 0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution \npH 7.5 to obtain a concentration of 2 mg per ml of somatropin.\nReference solution (a). Prepare a solution of somatropin IPRS\nin 0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 to obtain \na concentration of 2 mg per ml of somatropin.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve the contents of a vial of \nsomatropin/desamidosomatropin IPRS in 0.05M tris- \nhydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 to obtain a \nconcentration of 2 mg per ml of somatropin. \n:<••••\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, singly end-\ncapped butylsilylsilica gel (5 pm) and with pore size of \n30 nm; a silica saturation column is placed between me \npump and the injector valve,\n- column temperature: 45°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 29 volumes ofpropanol and\n71 volumes of 0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution \npH7.5 \n' '\n- flow rate: 0.5 ml per minute,\n- \nspectrophotometer set at 220 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n,,\nPrecondition the column with 200mlto500mlofa0.1 per cent \nv/v solution of trifluoroacetic acid in a 50 per cent v/v solution \nof acetonitrile', repeat as necessary, to improve column \nperformance. \n. - 0\nInject'reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks corresponding tb \ndesamidosomatropin and somatropin is at least 1.0. ;' ■\nInject reference solution (a) and test solution, the sum of the \nareas of all the peaks other than the principal peak is not more \nthan 13.0 per cent of the total area of all the peaks. Relative \nretention with reference to somatropin: ; for \ndesamidosomatropin is about 0.85.\nDimer and related substances of higher molecular mass! \nDetermine by size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16). usetfi^ \nnormalization procedure.\nw\n4688\nIP 2022\nSOMATROPIN ['bR-iftjECTidN-\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation under examination in \n0.025Mphosphate Buffer solution pH 7.0 to obtain a \nconcentration of 1.0 mg per ml of somatropin^ 1\nReference solution. Dissolve the contents of a vial of \nsomatropin IPRS in 0.025M phosphate buffer solution pH\n7.0 and dilute with the same solution to obtain a concentration \nof1 mgper ml, ' \n:-\nReference solution (b). Place 1 vial of somatropin IPRS in an \noven at 50° for a period sufficient to generate 1 to 2 per cent of \ndimer (typically 12 to 24 hours). Dissolve nts contents in \n0. 025M'phosphate buffer solution'pH 7.0 and dilute with the \nsame solution to obtain a concentration of 1 mg per mL\nChromatographic system...........\n- \"a sfainless'steel column 30 cm x 7.8 mm, packed, with\n’ hydrophi 1 ic silica gel, of a grade suitable for fractionation\nof globular proteins in the relative molecular mass range\n■' \nof5000to 150000, ' '.J \n:\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 3 volumes of2-propanol and\n97 volumes of 0.063M tris-hydrochloride buffer \nsolution pH 7.0,\n- flow rate: 0.6 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 214 nm,\n. injection volume: 20 pl, \n.\nInject reference solution (a) and test solution.'Relative \nretention time vyith reference tp somatropin monomer: related \nsubstances of higher molecular mass is about 0.65; somatropin \ndimer is about 0.9.\nInject reference solution (b), the test is not valid unless the \nratio of the peak height of the dimer, to the height above the \nbaseline.of the lowest point of the curve separating the .dimer\nfrom the monomer peak is not less than 2.5.\nCalculate the content of dimers, oligomers and aggregates. \nThe sum of the peaks with retention times less than that ofthe \nprincipal peak is not more than 6.0 per cent.\nCharged variants. Determine by Capillary electrophoresis \n(2.4.32). . \n'\nAll,the solutions should be filtered through 0.45 urn membrane \nfilter before use, . \n. .¿.-a, \nm,.\nCZE buffer. Dissolve 13,2 gram c>i ammonium phosphate in\n1000 ml of water and adjust the p.H tp 6.0 using, \northophosphoric acid. ,\nTest'solution (a).'Di lute-the preparation under examination to \nobtain a concentration of 1 mg per ml of somatropin.\nTest .solution (b).,Mix equal volumes of test solution (a) and \nthe reference, solution. r ..... . \n.\nReference solution. Dissolve the contents ¿of a vial of \nsomatropin IPRS in water and dilute with the same solvent to\nobtain a concentration of I mgper ml ■ A;\nCapillary System \nvn&sa\n- material: Uncoated fused silica, ... \n...................\n- size: effective length is at least 70 cm, internal dianteter’\nis 50 pm, '\n- temperature: 30°.\n- spectrophotometer set at 200 nm,- m 3NiPiiftrS-\nSet the autosampler to store the samples at# during analysis?\nPreconditioning of the capillary. Rinse with\" IM sbdiimf \nhydroxide for 20 minutes, with waler for20miriufo§:and'Withs \n\\hcC7.T buffer for 20 minutes at 20 psi. mto\nBetween-run rinsing. Rinse using 0.1M sodium hydroxide \nfor 2 minutes and with the CZE buffer for 6 minutes.\nRinsing times may be adapted according to .the lepgth, ofthe.' \ncapillary and the equipment used. .. \n,\nInject, test solution (a) and the reference solution .under \npressure or vacuum, using the following sequence:: sample? \ninjection for at least 3 seconds then CZE buffer injection for\n1 second. The injection time and pressure may be adapted in \norder to meet the system suitability criteria.\n■ g i''? ?r''r ? - -Z j ■’$ f¿5 \nMigration. Apply a field strength of 217 V/cm (20 kV for \ncapillaries of 92 cm total length) for <80 minutes using-the CZE \nbuffer as the electrolyte in both buffer reservoirs. It is \nrecommended to use fresh buffer for each separation to \ndecrease the risk of ion depletion'. \nz;:?'z: \nR\nThe electropherogram obtained with test solutionRa)'; and)\nreference solution are similar to the electropherogram of \nsomatropin supplied with somatropin IPRS. Twb peaks (ft,ft) \neluting prior to the prin"},{"name":"Assay","text":"Determine by Size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16) as \ndescribed in the test for dimer and related substances of higher \nmolecular mass.\nCalculate the content of somatropin from the. declared content \nof somatropin IP RS.\nStorage. Store in a sterile, airtight, tamper-proof container, at a \ntemperature of 2° to 8°.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the content of Somatropin \nin the container,, in milligrams; (2) the . composition and \nvolume of the liquid to be added for reconstitution; \n(3) the time within which the reconstituted solution shall be \nused and the storage conditions during this period; (4) the \nname and quantity of any excipient; (5) the storage temperature; \n(6) that the preparation shall not be shaken during \nreconstitution."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rituximab","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rituximab"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"RITUXIMAB\nOther tests. The content of the preparation complies with the \ntests stated under Parenteral preparations (Injections).\nResidual moisture content (2.3.43)1. Determine on the individual \ncontents of three containers by titrimetric (Karl Fischer) \nmethod.\npH (2.4.24). 6.8 to 7.5. Determined in a solution prepared by \ndiluting the preparation under examination in carbon dioxide- \nfree water to produce a solution containing at least 105 IU of\nstreptokinase activity per ml.\nPotency. Determine on the individual and mixed contents of \nthree containers.\nThe potency of streptokinase is determined by comparing its \ncapacity to activate plasminogen to form plasmin with the \nsame capacity; of a reference preparation of streptokinase \ncalibrated in International Units; the formation of plasmin is \ndetennined using a suitable chromogenic substrate.\nThe International Unit is the activity of a stated amount of the \nInternational Standard for streptokinase. The equivalence in \nInternational Units of the International Standard is stated by \nthe World Health Organization.\nReference and test solutions. Prepare 2 independent series of \n4 dilutions of each of the substance under examination and of \nthe reference preparation of streptokinase in tris \n(hydroxymethyl) aminomethane sodium, chloride buffer pH\nRituximab\n7.4, in the range o 1'0.5-4.0 IU per ml. Prepare and maintain all \nsolutions at 37°.\nSubstrate- solution. Mix 1.0 ml.of tris (hydroxymethyl) \naminomethane buffer pH 7. 4 with 1.0 ml of chromophore \nsubstrate. Add 5 pl of a 10 per cent w/v solution oipolysorbate \n20. Keep at, 37° in a,water-bath. Immediately before \ncommencing the activation assay, add 45 pl of a 1 mg per ml \nsolution of human plasminogen.\nAnalyse each streptokinase dilution,; maintained at 37°, in \nduplicate. Initiate the activation reaction by adding 60 pl of \neach dilution to 40 pl of substrate solution. For blank wells, \nusé 60 pl of tris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethane sodium \nchloride buffer solution pH 7.4 instead of the reference and \ntest solutions. Allow the reaction to proceed\" at 37° for \n20 minutes and read the absorbance at 405 nm. If a suitable \nthermostated plate reader is available, this may be used, to \nmonitor the reaction; Alternatively it may be necessary to \nstop the reaction after 20 minutes using 50 pl of a 50 per cent \nv/v solution of glacial acetic acid. Best results are. obtained \nwhen the absorbance for the highest streptokinase \nconcentration is between 0.1 and 0.2 (after blank.subtraction). \nIf necessary, adjust the time of incubation in order to reach \nthis range of absorbances.\nCalculate the regression of the absorbance on log \nconcentrations of the solutions of the substance under\n. examination and of the reference preparation.of streptokinase\nand calculate the potency of the substance under examination \nusing the usual statistical methods for parallel-line; assays.\nThe estimated potency is not less than 90 per cent and riot \nmore than 111 per cent of the stated potency. The confidence \nlimits (P = 0.95) of the estimated potency are not less than 80 \nper cent and not more than 125 per cent of the stated potency.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 23.3 Endotoxin \nUnit per 10000 IU per ml of streptokinase activity. .;\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nAbnormal toxicity (2.2.1). Determine by using a solution \ncontaining 50,000 Units in 0.5 ml of water for injection \nadministered in 15 to 20 minutes.\nStorage. Store in sealed containers, protected from light and \nat a temperature of about 2°-8°. The container should be sterile \nand sealed so as to exclude micro-organisms.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the number of International \nUnits of streptokinase activity per vial; (2) the name of any \nadded substance; (3) where applicable, that the substance is \nfree from bacterial endotoxins.\nRituximab Heavy Chain Chimeric\nQVqLQQPGAELVKPGASVKMSCKASGYTFrSYNMHWVKQTPGaGLEWlGAIYPGNGDTSY\nNQKETCGKATLTADKSSSTAYMQLSSLTSE"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"RITUXIMAB\nOther tests. The content of the preparation complies with the \ntests stated under Parenteral preparations (Injections).\nResidual moisture content (2.3.43)1. Determine on the individual \ncontents of three containers by titrimetric (Karl Fischer) \nmethod.\npH (2.4.24). 6.8 to 7.5. Determined in a solution prepared by \ndiluting the preparation under examination in carbon dioxide- \nfree water to produce a solution containing at least 105 IU of\nstreptokinase activity per ml.\nPotency. Determine on the individual and mixed contents of \nthree containers.\nThe potency of streptokinase is determined by comparing its \ncapacity to activate plasminogen to form plasmin with the \nsame capacity; of a reference preparation of streptokinase \ncalibrated in International Units; the formation of plasmin is \ndetennined using a suitable chromogenic substrate.\nThe International Unit is the activity of a stated amount of the \nInternational Standard for streptokinase. The equivalence in \nInternational Units of the International Standard is stated by \nthe World Health Organization.\nReference and test solutions. Prepare 2 independent series of \n4 dilutions of each of the substance under examination and of \nthe reference preparation of streptokinase in tris \n(hydroxymethyl) aminomethane sodium, chloride buffer pH\nRituximab\n7.4, in the range o 1'0.5-4.0 IU per ml. Prepare and maintain all \nsolutions at 37°.\nSubstrate- solution. Mix 1.0 ml.of tris (hydroxymethyl) \naminomethane buffer pH 7. 4 with 1.0 ml of chromophore \nsubstrate. Add 5 pl of a 10 per cent w/v solution oipolysorbate \n20. Keep at, 37° in a,water-bath. Immediately before \ncommencing the activation assay, add 45 pl of a 1 mg per ml \nsolution of human plasminogen.\nAnalyse each streptokinase dilution,; maintained at 37°, in \nduplicate. Initiate the activation reaction by adding 60 pl of \neach dilution to 40 pl of substrate solution. For blank wells, \nusé 60 pl of tris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethane sodium \nchloride buffer solution pH 7.4 instead of the reference and \ntest solutions. Allow the reaction to proceed\" at 37° for \n20 minutes and read the absorbance at 405 nm. If a suitable \nthermostated plate reader is available, this may be used, to \nmonitor the reaction; Alternatively it may be necessary to \nstop the reaction after 20 minutes using 50 pl of a 50 per cent \nv/v solution of glacial acetic acid. Best results are. obtained \nwhen the absorbance for the highest streptokinase \nconcentration is between 0.1 and 0.2 (after blank.subtraction). \nIf necessary, adjust the time of incubation in order to reach \nthis range of absorbances.\nCalculate the regression of the absorbance on log \nconcentrations of the solutions of the substance under\n. examination and of the reference preparation.of streptokinase\nand calculate the potency of the substance under examination \nusing the usual statistical methods for parallel-line; assays.\nThe estimated potency is not less than 90 per cent and riot \nmore than 111 per cent of the stated potency. The confidence \nlimits (P = 0.95) of the estimated potency are not less than 80 \nper cent and not more than 125 per cent of the stated potency.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 23.3 Endotoxin \nUnit per 10000 IU per ml of streptokinase activity. .;\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nAbnormal toxicity (2.2.1). Determine by using a solution \ncontaining 50,000 Units in 0.5 ml of water for injection \nadministered in 15 to 20 minutes.\nStorage. Store in sealed containers, protected from light and \nat a temperature of about 2°-8°. The container should be sterile \nand sealed so as to exclude micro-organisms.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the number of International \nUnits of streptokinase activity per vial; (2) the name of any \nadded substance; (3) where applicable, that the substance is \nfree from bacterial endotoxins.\nRituximab Heavy Chain Chimeric\nQVqLQQPGAELVKPGASVKMSCKASGYTFrSYNMHWVKQTPGaGLEWlGAIYPGNGDTSY\nNQKETCGKATLTADKSSSTAYMQLSSLTSEDSAVYYCARSTYYGGDWYFNVWGAGTTVTVS\nAASrKGPSVFPLAPSSKSTSGGTAALGCLVKDYFPEPVTVSWNSGALTSGVHTFPAVLQS\nSGLYSLSSWTVPSSSLGTQTYICNVNHKPSNTKVDKKAEPKSCDKTHTCPPCPAPELLG '\nGPSVFLFPPKPKDTLMISRTPEVTCVVVDVSHEDPEVKFNWYVDGVEVHNAKTKPREEQY\nNSTYRVVSVLTVLHQDWLNGKEYKCKVSNKALPAPIEKT1SKAKGQPREPQVYTLPPSRD\nELTKNQVSLTCLVKGFYPSDIAVEWESNGQPENNYKTTPPVLDSDGSFFLYSKLTVDKSR\nWQQGNVFSCSVMHEALHNHYTQKSLSLSPGK \n'\nRituximab Light Chain Chimeric\nQIVLSQSPAILSASPGEKVTMTCRASSSVSYIHWFQQKPGSSPKF'WIYATSNLASGVPVR.\nFSGSGSGTSYSL.TISRVEAEDAATYYCQQWTSNPPTFGGGTKLEIiatTVAA’PSVFfFPPS\nDEQLKSGTASVVCLLNNFYPREAkVQWKVD^ALQSGNSQESVTEQDSKDsfYSLSSTLTL\nSKADYEKHKVYACEVTHQGLSSPVTKSFNRGEC\nStructure of Rituximab \n' Mol. Wt. 143859.7\nRituximab Drug Substance is a monoclonal antibody \nconsisting of 1328 amino; acid, residues composed of two \nheavy chains of 451 amino acids and two light chains of 213 \namino acidsthat targets the CD20 antigen, a specific protein \nfound on the surface of normal and malignant B lymphocytes.\nRituximab (anti-CD20) is a genetically engineered chimeric \nmouse-human monoclonal antibody representing a\nRITUXIMAB\nIP 2022\nglycosylated immunoglobulin with human IgG Kappa constant \nregion (Fc domainfand mouse light and heavy chain variable \nregions (Fab: domain). Rituximab contains not less than 80 per \ncent and; not more than 125 per cent of the stated potency\nHost cell proteins. Not more than 100 ppm.\nHost cell DNA. Not more than 10 ng per dose.\nCategory. Anticancer\nDescription. A clear colorless to pale yellow liquid free from \nparticles that can be observed by visual observation.;"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by method B and any two. methods from methodA,\nC, D,E,\nA. Bioassay\nB. Determine by peptide mapping (2.3.47).\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation under examination in 0.025 \nM Tris buffer solution pH 8.0 to obtained a solution having \nconcentration of 10 mg per ml. Add 900 mg of guanidine \nhydrochloride, to 900 pl of test solution, and mix. Add 20 pl of \n2 M of Tris base and 27 pl of 1 M DTT and incubate at 50° for\n1 hour. Add 54 pl of 1Mlodoaceticacid to the above solution \nand incubate at room temperature for 45 minutes. Desalt the \nabove solution using K9 column filled with sephadex G25 \nresin or 3kD a cut off membrane filters using the desalting \nbuffer (0.025 M Tris base, 3 54 Urea.,adjusted to pH 7.5 with \nhydrochloric acid using Hydrochloride). Collect the peaks \nrespectively and check for protein concentration using \nBradford assay. Adjust the concentration of sample to 1.2 mg \nper ml. Add Trypsin enzymein.the ratio of 1:20 and incubate \nat 37° for 16 hours and analyze on HPLC. . j .......\nReference solution. Prepare in the same manner as the test\nsolution using rituximab IPRS. ,.\nBlank control solution. Prepare in the same manner as the\ntest solution using suitable diluent.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecyisilane bonded to porous silica (3 pm) such as \nSupelco, Biowide (300 A),\n- column temperature: 60°,\n■ ■ acid in water, • ’ m \n;\n— mobile phase: A. a 0.01 per cent v/v of trifluoroacetic\n; \nB. a 0.01 percent v/v of trifluoroacetic\nacid in acetonitrile y\n- \na linear gradient programme using conditions given \nbelow, ' \n■■ ■\n- \n- flow rate: 1ml per minute, \n.\n- \nspectrophotometer set at 215 nm, . .\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n■ (per cent v/v)'\n0\n100 .\n• \n.0 \n;\n- 3 . -L\n100\n0\n118\n59\n41\n130\n0\n100\n133\n0\n100 \n•-\n135 ‘\n100\n0\n145\n100\n0\nInject the test solution and réference solution and reagent \nblank. Integrate all peaks that elute between approximately\n10 and 120 minutes. A suitable resolution criterion should be \nestablished for a critical peak pair for the reference solution.\nVisually compare the elution profiles between the test solution \nand the adjacent reference solution in terms of peak retention \ntimes, number of peaks, and relative peak heights. Thé peaks \nin chromatogram obtained with the test solution correspond \nto that of the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution.\nThe retention time of established marker peaks should be \nwithin ± 0.7 minutes of the reference solution marker peaks...\nNOTE— there may be some regions with variable peak height\ndue to carbohydrate differences or . oxidation products, h :\nC. Determine by electrophoresis (sodium dodecyl sulphate - \npolyacrylamide gel électrophoresis) (SDS-PAGE) (2.4.12)/\nIn the tests for Impurities with molecular masses differing \nfrom that of rituximab. The principal band in the \nelectropherogram obtained with test solution is similar in \nposition and intensity to the principal band in the \nelectropherogram obtained with reference solution; ■■■■■” 4 7\nD. Determine by- capillary electrophoresis (capillary zone\nelectrophoresis) (2.4.32). \n''\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation under examination with \nwater to obtain a concentration of 0.5 mg per ml. \nJ\nReference solution. Prepare in the same manner as the test \nsolution using rituximab IPRS\nBlank control so/Mpon. Prepare ;in the same manner as The \ntest solution using appropriate product diluent. : : <\nCapillary system \"• \n■--■■US\n- \nneutral capillary (50pm 0 x 560 mm) orN-CHO co\ncapillary (50 pm 0 x 650 mm) Of other suitable capillary, \n- temperature: 20° ±2°, \n- \n/ \n. /\n- \nsample temperature 10° ±2°, \n;\n- \nspectrophotometer set at 214 nm,\n- \ninjection volume: 100 pl. \n; 4\nCZE buffer. (0.04 Me-amino-n-caproic acid containing 0.2 \nper cent w/v of hydroxypropylmethylcellulose, pH adjusted\nto 4.5 with glacial acetic acid. \n<\nIP 2022\nRITUXIMAB\nPreconditioning of the capillary\nRinse the capillary using CZE buffer until visual evaluations \nof the electropherograms demonstrate consistent profiles for \npre-conditioning of capillary.;\ninject suitable volume of the reference solution followed by \nthe test solution. Complete the analysis with an injection of \nthe reference solution and the reagent blank.\nMigration.Apply field strength of 30 kV for 15 min using the\nCZE buffer as the electrolyte in both buffer reservoirs.The \nmain peak elutes between 15 to 17 minutes."},{"name":"Tests","text":"The difference in the migration time for the main peak of the \nfirst and last reference solution injections bracketing each \ntest solution must be less than or equal to 0.1 minute.\nPositive identity is confirmed if the difference in migration \ntime between the main peak of the reference solution and test \nsolution is less than or equal to 0.1 minute.\nE. Determine by isoelectric focusing (2.4.33) Capillary \nElectrophoresis (CE).\nSolution (a). Catholyte: 0.2 M phosphoric acid.\nSolution(b). Anolyte: 0.3 M sodium hydroxide.\nSolution (c). Chemical: 0.35 M acetic acid.\nSolution (d). 0.5 M arginine.\nSolution (e). 0.2 M iminodiacetic acid. \n-\nSolution (J). 4.3 M urea.\nSolution (g) (3 M Urea- cIEF Gel). Weigh 1.80 g of urea and \nadd cIEF Gel to the volumetric flask so that the final volume is\n10 ml. \n/\nTest solution: Add 200 pl of solution (f), 12;0 pl of Pharmalyte \n3-10 carrier ampholytes, 20.0 pl oisolution (d), 2.0 plof solution \n(e), 2.0 pl ofpl marker 10.0, pl marker 7.0, pl marker 5;5 and pl \nmarker 4.1 and add 10 pl of protein concentration within 5 mg \nper ml to 10 mg per ml. “\nReference solution. Prepare in the same manner as the.sample \nusing rituximab IPRS. \n.....\nCapillary System\n- neutral capillary. 50 pm internal diameter and length about\n40cm,\n- sample temperature.10°,\n- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm.\nTurn on the UV lamp and allow the system to warm up for at \nleast 30 minutes prior experimentation. The protein \nconcentration within 5 mg per ml to : 10 mg per ml. The protein \nsample must have a salt concentration below 0.05 M since \nhigh salt content is detrimental to cIEF separation. The protein \nsample can be buffer exchanged before use. The identity is \nestablished comparing the product with reference preparation.\nIt complies with the limits approved by National Regulatory \nAuthority.\nIn the electropherogram obtained with reference solution, the \npl of principal band is 9.3 ± 0.2. In the electropherogram \nobtained with test sohition no band other than the principal \nband is more intense than the principal band in the \nelectropherogram obtained with reference solution.\nIt complies with the biological activity as described under \nAssay.\npH (2.4.24). 6.3 to 6.7.\nImpurities with molecular masses differing from that of \nRituximab. Determine by method A or B\nA. Determine by using Capillary Electrophoresis by SDS (CE-\nSDS) (2.4.32) under both reducing and non-reducing conditions \nusing IgG heterogeneity kit or equivalent.\nProcedure. Analyse preparation under examination and\nrituximab IPRS against the molecular weight marker using an \nelectrophoretic method capable of giving separation in the \nrange 10- 225/250 kDa followed by UV detection. Dilute the \npreparation under examination and rituximab IPRS in an \nappropriate diluent to obtain a minimum concentration to be \ndetected by the capillary electrophoresis for example the total \nprotein concentration, should be within the range of 0.5 mg \nper ml to 4 mg per ml and use a validated method for reducing\nand non-reducing condition.\nTest solution for CE-SDS under reducing condition. Add \n68.0 pl water, 100 pl SDS sample buffer, 2 pl of 10 kDa internal \nstandard and 10; pl mercaptoethanol to 20 pl of preparation \nunder examination in a E5 ml eppendorf tubes. Vortex it and \nheat the mixture at 70° for 3 minutes. Cool it down at room \ntemperature. Transfer it to vials for capillary electrophoresis \nanalysis.Test solution may also be prepared as per application \nnote.\nReference solution for CE-SDS under reducing condition. \nPrepare in the same manner as the test , solution for CE-SDS \nunder reducing condition using, rituximab IPRS.\nTests solution for CE-SDS under-non- reducing condition'. \nMix,68.0 pl ofwater, 100 pl of SDS sample buffer, 2 plof 10 kDa \ninternal standard, 10 pl 0.25 M iodoacetamide, 20 pl of \npreparation under examination. Vortex it and heat the mixture \nat 70° for 5 minutes. Cool it down at room temperature. Transfer \nit to PCR vials for capillary electrophoresis analysis. Test \nsolution may also be prepared as per application note.\nReference solutionfor CE-SDS under non-reducing\ncondition; Prepare in the same manner as the test solution for \nCE-SDS under non-reducing condition using rituximab \nIPRS. ■■■ . \n;\nIP 2022\nRITUXIMAB\nCapillary system\n- \nmaterial. Bare fused silica coated,\n- size:effective length = aboutSO. .cm,. internal diameter\n, ... ._ -50pm, .... ,< \n. \n, \n. ■\n. - capillary: 30.2 cm x 50 pm,\n- effective length: 20 cm, .\n- capillary Temperature: 25,\n. - spectrophotometer set at 220 nm,\n- detector: PDA 220 nm,\n- capillary temperature: 25°,\n- sample temperature: 10°,\n- polarity: Anode at the inlet and Cathode at the outlet, \n- Injection time: 5.0 kV for 20.0 seconds at the electrode\npolarity reverse,\n- separation voltage: 15.0 kV for 30.0 minutes at polarity\nreverse.\nFollowed by pre-conditioning of capillary with suitable buffer, \ninject test solution and reference solution by keeping between- \nrun rinsing using suitable buffer.\n■Migration. Apply a field strength 5.0 kV for 20.0 seconds at \nthe electrode polarity reverse at the time of injection and:\n15.0 kV for 30.0 minutes at polarity reverse as separation \nvoltage. Maintain polarity anode: at the inlet and cathode at \nthe outlet.\nUnder reducing condition, in electropherogram obtained with \nreference solution and test solution identify the peaks \ncorresponding to light chain, nori-glycosylated heavy chain \nand heavy chain. Calculate the percentage of non­\nglycosylated heavy chain in the solution. Calculate the \ncorrected percentage, area of light chain, non-glycosylated \nheavy.chain and heavy chain. The corrected percentage area \nof non-glycosylated heavy chainis not more than 2.0 per dent.\nUnder non-reducing condition, in electropherogram obtained \nwith reference solution, and test solution identify the peaks \ncorresponding tolow molecular weight impurities and IgG. \nCalculate the percentage of impurities in the sample solution. \nCalculate the corrected percen"},{"name":"Assay","text":"Bacterial endotoxins (2.2.3).Not more than 1.0 EU per mg or \nequivalent to EU per ml."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rivastigmine-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rivastigmine Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rivastigmine Capsules\nRivastigmineTartratcCapsules ' \n„\nRivastigmine Capsules ; contain; Rivastigmine Tartrate \nequivalent to not less than 95.0 percent and not more than\n105.0 per cent of the stated amount of rivastigmine, C^I I22N2O2.\nUsual strengths. 1.5 mg; 3 mg; 4.5 mg; 6 mg. ■\nIdentification \" ‘ \"A 'J \n1j1 \nJ y \n\" f ' 'r :\nIn the Assay,; the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith, the 'test> solution - corresponds to; the peak in > the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). {; ; ; -’m\n3512 \n—"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rivastigmine Capsules\nRivastigmineTartratcCapsules ' \n„\nRivastigmine Capsules ; contain; Rivastigmine Tartrate \nequivalent to not less than 95.0 percent and not more than\n105.0 per cent of the stated amount of rivastigmine, C^I I22N2O2.\nUsual strengths. 1.5 mg; 3 mg; 4.5 mg; 6 mg. ■\nIdentification \" ‘ \"A 'J \n1j1 \nJ y \n\" f ' 'r :\nIn the Assay,; the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith, the 'test> solution - corresponds to; the peak in > the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). {; ; ; -’m\n3512 \n—"},{"name":"Tests","text":"- d\nDissolution (2.5.2). \nA\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle), ( :\nMedium. 5.00ml of water. ....\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 30 minutes. \n\\.\nWithdraw a suitable vol