ile phase B. \n. : \n, } »\nTest solution. Dissòlvè 25 mg of the; substance under \nexamination in 25.0 itril of the sòl vent mixture.; \n-\nReference solution (a). À solution containing Ò.0Ò5 per cent \nw/v of each rppjmrplfhydrochlpride IPRS. ropinirole \nimpurity A IPRS and ropinirple impurity B IPRS in the solvent \nmixture.? ... \ni\nReference solution (b). A 0.0003. per cent, w/v solution of \nropinirole impurity B IRRS, imthe solvent mixture. f\nChromatographic system\n-i a stainless steel column;25: cm >< 4.6 mnmpacked with\nMobile phase B\n: octylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), ■:m;\n- column temperature: 40°, ? \nu\n- mobile phase: A. dissolve 3.85 g of ammonium acetate\n(per cent v/v) \n, :/'\"o\nin 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 2.5, with ortho \nphosphoric acid, r \n?\"\nB. a mixture of 70 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 30volumes ofmethanol,w.w;OHi \n- a gradient programme using the conditionsgivenbelow,\n, 40 . .\n; ,.......\nflowrate: I ml per minute, \n;ji?;\n: -. speelrophotomctcr set at 25.0. nm, ; ... x\n- injection volume: 10 pl.,;: \ni;,f!,\n;;. Time,. ? ,,, ^obilejjhase A ; . . Mobile phaseB^j\n, (inmin.) ..... (per cent v/v) , .. (P^r centy/y);??\n0 ? i: <>•?! ■/: i 84 - - ■; >: \n■■ X ?-■;!'\n; .84. . .-.-v M m \nM\n36 \n.■ \n20 \n;\nomw- v:-37; x? v .84 nv: v. j ' :. ■? M ■'\n. \n’ 40 \n84 n\n? IP 2022\nRelative \nretention time\ni\nunnoprôpylfopîniroie1\n1\n1\nÎ\nRopiniipie>Pun|yÆ ;.,f..°-70\n. WW-WW \n037-\n1\nRopinirole impurity B3\n; . 0.85\n1.0 ,\nJ\nMethylene ropinirole4 .\nJg5 \n. : \n. 1.64 :\nRopinirole isohexyl anal«\nW •;... \n.......... • \n1.70 .\npropylidenerppinirole6\nadolin-2-onc, \n:;W ■„ J:\n-2-one,\nu-/2-Hv4roxyethyl) indolin-\n54q24Diprôpÿlàinino)ethyl]'indoline-2,3-dione hydrochloride,\nM42-(DipropyHrnino)ethyl]-3-mëthyleneindolin-2-one,\nM-{2-(2-Methylpentylamino)ethyl]indolin-2-one,\n‘(2)4-[2r.(pipiOpyIaniino)ethyi]-3-propylideneiiidolin-2-one.\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to ropinirole impurity A \nand ropinirole impurity .B is not less than 2.0, the resolution \nbet#,e$n' the: peaks, due; to ropinirole hydrochloride . and \nropinirole impurity B. is not less than 3.0, the tailing factor is \nnot more than 1.5 in the chromatogram obtained .with reference \nsolution (a) and the relative standard deviation for replicate \ninjections is hot more than 5.0 per cent in' the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b). \n- ■ ' \n1\nRopinirole\nInjectifiie.'tes't solution. The area of each peak due to\nmbnbpropyl ropinirole, methylene ropinirole andpropylidene \nropinirole is not more than 0.2 per cent, the area of each peak\ndue to ropinirole impurityBandropinirole isohexyl analog is \nnot more than 0.3 per cent', the area of any other secondary \npeakis<n0thnore- thah 0.1 percent and the sum of areas of all \nthe secondary peaks is not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated \nby area normalisation. , .\nC. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 32 volumes of acetonitrile and 68 volumes \nofwafer. y : \n. \n: \n; ,f\nZest ib/wfidn. Dissolve 30 mg of the substance under \nexaminationin 25.0 nil of the solvent mixture. ' ?; ;\nRtf&f nee solution (a). A 0.00012 per cent w/v solution of\nhydrochloride IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 6.1 per cent\nropinirole hydrochloride IPRS and 0.001 per cent w/v \n^ropinirole impurity B IPRS 'm the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic.system \n1 7\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 3.9 mm, packed With \nbctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \nmobile phase: A. dissolve 3.95 g ofammonium\nz := bicarbonate in 1000 ml ofwufer, adjusted to pH 9.2 to\n9.3 with 25 per cent ammonia solutioni :"},{"name":"ROPINIROLE HYDROCHLORIDE","text":"B. acetonitrile,. .\n- : a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, ; \n; \n;\n- spectrophotometer set at 220 nm, : , <\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\n- Mobile phase A\nMobile phase B\nTime \n(in min.)!\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n■ ' O'\n12\n68\n' 32 ; \n‘\n28 . ,\n20 .. .\n, , . . 80\n34\n20\n80\n34.1\n68\n32\n.68\n• 32\nName \"f '.f \" ' ......\nRelative \nretention time\nCorrection\nfactor\n: LO . .\nRopinirple oxime1\n■ \n1:0\n1.2\nRopinirole impùrity B\n1.0\nRopinirole indole derivatiye2 . 2.0\n0.25 .\nl4-[2-(Dipropylamino)ethyJ]-3-(hydroxyiTnjno)indolin-2-one; .\nW,Y-Dipropyl-Af-(2-indol-4-ylethyl)aniine.\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution' between the peaks due to ropinirole \nhydrochloride and ropinirole impurity B is not less than 1.5 in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b), the \ntailing factor-is between 0-.8 to 1.5 and the relative standard\ndeviation for replicate injections is! not more than 5.0 per cent \nfor ropinirole in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a). \n.. .\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In.the \nchromatogram obtained with, the test spfotion, the area of \neach peak due to ropinirole oxime and ropinirole impurity B is \nnot more than 0.15 per cent, the area of each peak due to \nropinirole indole derivative and any other secondary peaks is\nnot more than 0.1 per cent, and the sum of areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 6:5 per cent, calculated by \narea normalisation.; \n•••. •fo-\nPalladium. Not more than 10 ppm.\nNOTE—-Perform 'this teist if Pd is a known inorganic impurity:\nSolution A. Dissolve 2.4 g of sodium hydroxide in 20 ml of \nwafer, and dilute with glycerol to 100 ml.\nSolution B. A 4.0 per cent w/v solution of thioacetamide in \nwater.\nNOTE —The solution may be filtered if necessary.\nSolvent mixture: Carefully add 27 ml of hydrochloric acid to \n25i-’g of ammonium acetate, anddilute with water to 100.0 ml."},{"name":"ROPINIROLE HYDROCHLORIDE","text":"Test solution. Transfer 1 g of the substance under examination \nto a platinum crucible, andheat until thernaterial is completely \ncarbonized. Moisten the contents with 0.51ml Of sulphuric \nacid> and heat until fumes are no longer emitted. Place the \ncrucible in a muffle furnace at 700? and heat. Cool, add'5 ml of \nhydrochloric acid to the residue^ and evaporate to' dryness. \nAdd 1 ml of 2 Mhydrochloric acid and,if necessary,'dissolve \nthe residue with gentle heating. Cool and transfer the contents \nto a 50-ml beaker. Wash the crucible twice with 1-ml aliquots \nof waier, and add the washings to the beaker. Pass the solution \nthrough a membrane filter of 2-um pore size into á centrifuge \ntube. \n< \nK .\nReference solution. Pipet 1.0 ml óf 10 ppm Pd solution into a \nplatinum crucible, and gently evaporate to drynebs. Moisten \nthe residue with 0.5 ml.qfsulphuric acR/, and heat until fumes \nare no longer emitted! Place the crucible in a muffle furnace at \n700°, and heat for additional 5 minutes, Cool, add 5 ml of \nhydrochloric acid to the residue, and evapórate to diynésS. \nAdd 1 ml of 2 Mhydrochloric acid and, if necessary, disso lve \nthe residue with gentle heating. When cool,, .transfer the \ncontents, to a 50-ml beaker. Wash the crucible twice with 1-ml \naliquots nf water, and add the washings to the beaker. Pass \nthe solution through a membrane filter of 2-pm pore size into a \ncentrifuge tube. \n> , \n, \n; '.y .. \n; .u\nTofflie filtered reference solution and the-test„solution, add\n1 ml of Solution A,0.2 ml of Solution B, and 2 ml of solvent \nmixture. Cap the centrifuge tubes and. i ncubate at: 3 70 for \n2 hours. Pass the test solution and reference.solution through\na, membrane filter of 2-um pore size* wash the membrane, with \n2-mI aliquots of water, and allow the membrane to dry.,; ¡;: i\nThe intensity ofthespotduetothe test is not more intense \nthan the spot due to the reference.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 2.Ó g'coínpiiés with the limit tesf’for\nheavy metals, Method B (10 ppm). 5' ' ' ' '\nSulphated ash (2,3.18). Not more than 0. f per cent. \n,.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.5 percent. ■ :;7 • •\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).;; , .\nSolvent mixture. 20 volumes of acetonitrile and 80 volumes \nof water. \n...... \n. ,,, z \n.-■<\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 100,0 ml of the solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A.0,01 per cent w/v solution of rppinirole \nhydrochloride ZPR5 in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system \n... ;;\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), 1\n; ; 7- mobile phase:ámixturé:ó£80 volumesof abuffer solution\n; : prepared by. dissolving 1.88. g. of <«.sodium\niP20«\nhexariesulphonate, 1 goi orthophosphoric acid \n/ini' of water and adjusting the pH to 6.5 with\ntriethylamine (1:10) and 20 volumes of acetonitrile, yil\n- flow rate: 1ml per minute, \n.. . ■ \n'VS|\n- spectrophotometer set at 215 nm, '■ \n- injection volume: 10 pl. \n■ '■ \n• ’\nInject the'reference solution. The test is not valid unless ^11\ntailing factor is not more than 1.6 and the relative'standa^fs®\ndeviationfor replicate injections is not more than! .5 percenta\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.! \n71\nCalculate the content of C^H^N^C), HOLr\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not ? J\nexceeding 30°. \n;'■'■■■ .gj"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ropivacaine-hydrochloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ropivacaine Hydrochloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ropivacaine Hydrochloride Injection\nRopiVadaiheHydfochloride Injection is a sterile solution of\nopivacaine Hydrochloride in Water for Injections. •\nRopivacaine Hydrochloride Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than LI 0.0 per cent of the stated \namount of C17H26N2O,HC1.\nUsual strengths. 2.0 mg per ml; 5.0 mg per ml; 7.5 mg per ml; 10 \nmg per ml.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nA. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution.\nB. In the Enantiomeric purity, the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthe peak in thè chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution.\nLimit of Ropivacaine related compound A (2,6-Dimethylaniline \nhydrochloride). Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) \nas described under Assay with the following modifications.\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of injection to obtain a solution \ncontaining 0.2 per cent w/v of ropivacaine hydrochloride in \nthe mobile phase.\nUse chromatographic system and reference solution as \ndescribed under assay.\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to ropivacaine hydrochloride \nand ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity Ais not less than 5.0 \nand signal- to- noise ratio for the ropivacaine hydrochloride \nimpurity A is not less than 10.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution, the area of the \npeak due to ropivacaine impurity A is not more than 0.1 times \nof the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution (0.01 per cent).\nEnantiomeric purity. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n.\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of injection with mobile phase \nto obtain a solution containing 0.0075 per cent w/v of \nRopivacaine Hydrochloride.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.0075 per cent w/v\nof ropivacaine hydrochloride IPRS and 0.000075 per cent of \nropivacaine hydrochloride impurity B IPRS in the mobile \nphase.\n3527\nwww.webofpharma.com\nChromatographic system \n■ y;,::\nL A t a »stainless steel (column 10 cm x 4.O mni packed with\nimmobilised a-1 acid glycoprotein on spherical silica \nparticles(5pm), , \nmobile phase: take 35.0 ml of isopropylalcohol into a \n500-ml volumetric flask and make up the volume with \nbuffer solution prepared =by diluting 7.5 ml of / :M \nmonobasic sodium phosphate solution and 28.5 ml of \n0.5 M dibasic sodium phosphate dihydrate solution to \na 1000 ,ml with water and mix. Adjusted to pH 7.2, if \n\"• necessary, \n: ‘ '• \n: \n: '\n- flow rate: 1ml per minute, ' \n!\n- spectrophotometer set at 220 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n• /\nThe relative retention times are about 0.75 for ropivacaine \nimpurity B and 1.0 for ropivacaine. \n/\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to ropivacaine hydrochloride \nand ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity B is not less than 1.5.\nInject the test solution. The area of any secondary peak \ncorresponding to ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity B is hot \nmore thari 2.0 per cent, calculated by area normalisation;\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral\nPreparations (Injections).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 60 Endotoxin Unit \nper g of Ropivacaine Hydrochloride.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2;4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of injection with mobile phase \nto obtain a solution containing 0.025 per cent w/v of \nRopivacaine Hydrochloride.\nReference solution: A solution, containing 0i025 per cent w/v\nof ropivacaine hydrochloride IPRS and 0.000025 per cent w/ \nv of ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity AIPRS in the mobile \nphase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\n. : octadecylsilane bonded.to porous silica (5 "},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Ropivacaine Hydrochloride Injection\nRopiVadaiheHydfochloride Injection is a sterile solution of\nopivacaine Hydrochloride in Water for Injections. •\nRopivacaine Hydrochloride Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than LI 0.0 per cent of the stated \namount of C17H26N2O,HC1.\nUsual strengths. 2.0 mg per ml; 5.0 mg per ml; 7.5 mg per ml; 10 \nmg per ml.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nA. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution.\nB. In the Enantiomeric purity, the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthe peak in thè chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution.\nLimit of Ropivacaine related compound A (2,6-Dimethylaniline \nhydrochloride). Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) \nas described under Assay with the following modifications.\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of injection to obtain a solution \ncontaining 0.2 per cent w/v of ropivacaine hydrochloride in \nthe mobile phase.\nUse chromatographic system and reference solution as \ndescribed under assay.\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to ropivacaine hydrochloride \nand ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity Ais not less than 5.0 \nand signal- to- noise ratio for the ropivacaine hydrochloride \nimpurity A is not less than 10.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution, the area of the \npeak due to ropivacaine impurity A is not more than 0.1 times \nof the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution (0.01 per cent).\nEnantiomeric purity. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n.\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of injection with mobile phase \nto obtain a solution containing 0.0075 per cent w/v of \nRopivacaine Hydrochloride.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.0075 per cent w/v\nof ropivacaine hydrochloride IPRS and 0.000075 per cent of \nropivacaine hydrochloride impurity B IPRS in the mobile \nphase.\n3527\nwww.webofpharma.com\nChromatographic system \n■ y;,::\nL A t a »stainless steel (column 10 cm x 4.O mni packed with\nimmobilised a-1 acid glycoprotein on spherical silica \nparticles(5pm), , \nmobile phase: take 35.0 ml of isopropylalcohol into a \n500-ml volumetric flask and make up the volume with \nbuffer solution prepared =by diluting 7.5 ml of / :M \nmonobasic sodium phosphate solution and 28.5 ml of \n0.5 M dibasic sodium phosphate dihydrate solution to \na 1000 ,ml with water and mix. Adjusted to pH 7.2, if \n\"• necessary, \n: ‘ '• \n: \n: '\n- flow rate: 1ml per minute, ' \n!\n- spectrophotometer set at 220 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n• /\nThe relative retention times are about 0.75 for ropivacaine \nimpurity B and 1.0 for ropivacaine. \n/\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to ropivacaine hydrochloride \nand ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity B is not less than 1.5.\nInject the test solution. The area of any secondary peak \ncorresponding to ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity B is hot \nmore thari 2.0 per cent, calculated by area normalisation;\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral\nPreparations (Injections).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 60 Endotoxin Unit \nper g of Ropivacaine Hydrochloride.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2;4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of injection with mobile phase \nto obtain a solution containing 0.025 per cent w/v of \nRopivacaine Hydrochloride.\nReference solution: A solution, containing 0i025 per cent w/v\nof ropivacaine hydrochloride IPRS and 0.000025 per cent w/ \nv of ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity AIPRS in the mobile \nphase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\n. : octadecylsilane bonded.to porous silica (5 pm). ;\n- mobile phase: a mixtureof 40 volumes of a buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 1.8 g of monobasic sodium\n' ' ' hydrogen phosphate arid 2.9 g of disodlumhydrogen\nphosphate dihydrate in 1000 ml of water adjusted to\npH8.0, if necessaryand 60volumes of acetonitrile, \n- flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute, ;\n- spectrophotometer set at 240 nm, ,, ; \n; gg. > >\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the .reference , solution.; The test is.not valid, unless the \nresolution between the principal peak and the peak due to \nropivacaine hydrochloride impurity A RS is not less than 5\nIP 2021\nROBIVACA1NE INJECTION\nand the relative standard deviation for replicate,injection i$S® \nnot more than 1.0 per cent obtained with the reference solutio -\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. \ni\nCalculate the content ofCl7H26N2O,HCl in the Injection. ;\nStorage. Store in single dose or multiple dose containers \npreferably of Type 1 glass or of suitable plas tic: - \n‘ 11\nLabelling. The label states the quantity of active ingredient in \nterms of the equivalent amount of ropivacaine hydro,chloride v I"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ropivacaine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ropivacaine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ropivacaine Hydrochloride\nC17H26N2O,IIC1,H2O \nMol. Wt. 328.9\nRopivacaine Hydrochloride is (5)-()-l-Propylpiperidmc-2- \ncarboxylic acid (2,6-dimethylphenyl)amide hydrochloride \nmonohydrate; (/)-(-)- l-Propyl-2’,6’-pipccoloxylicline \nhydrochloride monohydrate.\nRopivaqaine Hydrochloride: contains not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 ¡per ccnt of CDDN’Oj ICL \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis. . : ;\nCategory. Local anaesthetic.\nDescription . A white, crystalline powder. 1"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Ropivacaine Hydrochloride\nC17H26N2O,IIC1,H2O \nMol. Wt. 328.9\nRopivacaine Hydrochloride is (5)-()-l-Propylpiperidmc-2- \ncarboxylic acid (2,6-dimethylphenyl)amide hydrochloride \nmonohydrate; (/)-(-)- l-Propyl-2’,6’-pipccoloxylicline \nhydrochloride monohydrate.\nRopivaqaine Hydrochloride: contains not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 ¡per ccnt of CDDN’Oj ICL \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis. . : ;\nCategory. Local anaesthetic.\nDescription . A white, crystalline powder. 1"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with ropivacaine \nhydrochloride, IP RS or with the reference spectrum of \nropivacaine hydrochloride.. \n; j ,\nB. A1.O per cent w/v solution gives reaction (A)of chlorides \n(2.3.1). \n' ... -"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution (2.4.1). A 2,0 per cent w/v solution in \ncarbon dioxide free water is' not more intensely coloured \nthan reference solution YS7 (2.4.1) and isnOt more opalescent \nthan standard OS 1 (2.4.1). \n:' r '\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). ; \n;\nTest solution, (a).. Dissolve 30 mg of the .substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of mobile phase. \n. ,v\nTest solution (&).■ Diluted. 0 ml of test solution (a) to 100.0 ml \nwith mobile phase. Further dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to 10.0 \nml with mobile phase. ...... \no -\nReference solution.,^..solution containing 0.001 per cent w/v\neach of ropivacaine hydrochloride IPRS and bupivacaine \nhydrochloride IPRS in the mobile phase/\nROPIVACAINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nIP 2022\nChromatographic system\n~ a stainless steel column 15 cm x 3 ;9 mm; packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (4 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 50 volumes of a buffer solution ^prepared by \ndissolving 0.18 g of monobasic'sodium hydrogen \nphosphate and 2,9 g of disodium hydrogen phosphate\n: dihydrate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted tq pH 8,0, \n- flow rale: 1 ml per minute, \n;s,.\n- spectrophotometer set at 240 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe relative retention times are about 1.0 for ropivacaine and\n1.6 for bupivacaine respectively.\nInject the reference solution and test solution (b). The test is \nnot valid unless the resolution between the peaks due to \nropivacaine and bupivacaine obtained with reference solution \nis not less than 6.0 and the signal to noise ratio for the principal \npeak obtained with test solution (b) is not less than 10.0.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution (a). The \narea of any peak due to bupivacaine is not more, than 0.2 per \ncent. The area of any other secondary peak is not more than \n0.1 per cent and the sum of the areas of'all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 0.5 per cent, calculatedrby area \nnormalisation.\nLimit of Ropivacaine related compound A (2,6-Dimethylaniline | \nhydrochloride). Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). |\nTest solution. Dissolve 100 mg of the substance under | \nexamination in 10. Oml.ofmobile phase. \n....... , |\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.0000T per cent | \nw/v of ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity A' IPRS in the . | \nmobile phase,'' \n• \nJ.\nUse chromatographic system as described under Related | \nsubstances. \n’ \n|\nInject the reference solution. In the chromatogram obtained . | \nwith the reference solution signal- to-noise ratio for the | \nprincipal peak is not less than 10. \n|\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the | \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any | \npeak due to ropivacaine impurity A is not more than the area | \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram, obtained with the | \nreference solution (0.001 percent). . \n. \n|\nEnantiomeric purity.Determine by liquid chromatography | \n(2.4.14). \n.......... ....... ■ \n|\nTest solution, Dissolve 75 mg of the substance under | \nexamination in water and dilute to 100.0 ml with water. Dilute |\n10.0. ml ofthis resulting solution to 100.0 ml with mobile phase g\nL \" '. \" ' , \n' \n... \n.. \n■m M\nReference solution.containing 0.0075 per cent w/v ..\nof ropivacaine hydrochloride IPRS ari