tablets to a suitable v0*unie/C O\nflask and dilute to volume with the solvent mixture to\n{P 2022\nntrâùon of 0.1 per cent w/v of sitagliptin. Stir vigorously \nr° hour Transfer 8.0 ml of the solution to a 100-ml volumetric\nn t and dilute with the solvent mixture to volume and mix.\na portion of the solution until clear and use the\nsupernatant- \n;\nReference solution (a).A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \n¿frptin phosphate /PPS in the soIvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (c). In order to prepare sitagliptin fumarate\nadduct in-situ, place 10 mg of sitagliptin phosphate IPRS and\nifrgoîsôdiurn stedryl fumarate in a vial, add 1 ml of water \nand tightly seal the vial.Heat at 80° for 48 hours. Transfer the \ncontents of the vial into a 100-ml volumetric flask using à\nsmall amount of the solvent mixture. Rinse the vial three times \nwith the solvent mixture. Transfer the rinsates into the flask \nand dilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture and centrifuge. \nUse the supernatant liquid.\nChromatographic system \n,\n- a stainless.steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\ncyanopropylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such \nas SupclcoDiscovcryCyano),"},{"name":"Identification","text":"— mobile phase: a mixture of 15 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 85 volumes of buffer solution prepared; by \ndissolving 1.36 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate \nin about 900 ml of water, adjusted to pH 2.0 with \northophosphoric acid arid dilute to 1000 ml with water, \n- flow rate: I ml per minute, '\n~ spectrophotometer set at 205 nm, ■’\n- injection volume: 20 ul..\n: ••• :< i A ’ ■ x ' '■ \\ * ■•.3- \\ S. î x \n; \n. ’ ' \n\\ \n■ . ‘ \n' \n■\nThe relative retention time with reference to the sitagliptin \npeak for the fumarate adduct is about 1.2."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Inject reference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between sitagliptin and the, fumarate adduct is not \nless than T .Ô . '\nInject reference solution .(b) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogramaboutôtimes thcretention time, of the principal\nPeak- The area of any secondary peak is not more than 0.2 \ntim^s 4he ;area. of the principal peak in the chromatogram \n^^’^d with reference solution (b) (0.2 per cent) and the sum \n°f the areas of all the secondary peaks is not more than 0.5\ntlrnes the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtain^ with reference solution (b) (0i5 per cent). Ignore any\nwith an area less than 0.1 times the area of the principal\n*e chromatogram obtained with reference solution \nw)(0.1 perrierit); \n'\n^erîests. Comply with the tests, stated under Tablets.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14.) as \n^^bédmdêritest for Related substances with the following\nEdifications.\niron (20 ppm)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/snake-venom-antiserum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Snake Venom Antiserum"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Snake Venom Antiserum\nSnake Antivenin; Snake Antivenom Serum; Snake \nVenom Antitoxin\nEach mililiter of snake venom antiserum contains, sufficient \nantitoxic globulins to neutralize not less than 0.6 mg of Indian \ncobra (Naja haja)yaid Russell’s viper (vipera russelti) suitable \nvenoms and riotless than 0.45 mg of common krait(Bcmgtirus \ncaeruleus) and Saw scaled Viper (Echis cdrinatus) suitable \nvnoms.It may be obtained from the serum or plasma of healthy \nequines or other suitable animals immunised against venoms \nof specific species usually eiapine and viperine. It may be a \npurified liquid preparation or a freeze-dried product.. It may \ncontain a suitable preservative.\nDescription. A clear to slightly opalescent,colourless or pale \nyellow liquid, free from suspended particles Or cream-coloured \npowder or pellet which when reconstituted with the, diluent \nsupplied by; the manufacturer with the freeze-dried product \nyields a clear, colourless or pale yellow liquid.\n4470\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIdentification--\nSpecifically renders the corresponding venom or venoms \nharmless to susceptible animals. It may also be identified fry \nany other alternate suitable in vitro method. j"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Snake Venom Antiserum\nSnake Antivenin; Snake Antivenom Serum; Snake \nVenom Antitoxin\nEach mililiter of snake venom antiserum contains, sufficient \nantitoxic globulins to neutralize not less than 0.6 mg of Indian \ncobra (Naja haja)yaid Russell’s viper (vipera russelti) suitable \nvenoms and riotless than 0.45 mg of common krait(Bcmgtirus \ncaeruleus) and Saw scaled Viper (Echis cdrinatus) suitable \nvnoms.It may be obtained from the serum or plasma of healthy \nequines or other suitable animals immunised against venoms \nof specific species usually eiapine and viperine. It may be a \npurified liquid preparation or a freeze-dried product.. It may \ncontain a suitable preservative.\nDescription. A clear to slightly opalescent,colourless or pale \nyellow liquid, free from suspended particles Or cream-coloured \npowder or pellet which when reconstituted with the, diluent \nsupplied by; the manufacturer with the freeze-dried product \nyields a clear, colourless or pale yellow liquid.\n4470\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIdentification--\nSpecifically renders the corresponding venom or venoms \nharmless to susceptible animals. It may also be identified fry \nany other alternate suitable in vitro method. j"},{"name":"Tests","text":"S of. ■ \nBungjf ■\nPotency. In view of the numerous toxic fractions in venoms \nand varying requirements of different localities, no standard \nfor potency is prescribed.\nThe potency of the snake venom antiserum is determined by \nestimating the ability of the antiserum to protect mice or other \nsuitable animals against the lethal effect of a fixed dose of a \nreference preparation of snake venom of the relevant species \nor by comparing its ability to do so with that of a reference \npreparation of antiserum of established potency at two or \nmore dose levels of the venom. \n. ; <\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Antisera?-\nStorage. Store the freeze-dried preparation in a cool, darkplace \nand avoid exposure to ,excessive heat. Store, the liquid \npreparation at a temperature between 2° and.8°. It should not \nbe allowed to freeze. \n. '‘\nLabelling. The label states (1) the volume ofthe contents and \nin case of freeze-dried preparation, the directions for \nreconstitution; (2) the species of snake against whose venom \nthe antiserum is effective; (3) the animal species from which \nthe antiserum has been obtained; (4) the nariie and proportion \nof any preservative added; (5) that in case of a liquid \npreparation, it should not be allowed to freeze. \n5"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/snake-venomantiserum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Snake VenomAntiserum"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Snake Venom Antiserum\nSnake Antivenin; Snake Antivenom Serum; Snake\nVenomAntitoxin\nSnake Venom Antiserum is a sterile preparation containing\nantitoxic globulins or their derivatives that have the power of\nspecifically neutralising the venom ofone or more species of\nsnakes. It may be obtained from the serum or plasma ofhealthy\nequines or other suitable animals immunised against venoms\nof specific species usually elapine and viperine. It may be a\npurified liquid preparation or a freeze-dried product. It may\ncontain a suitable preservative.\nDescription. A clear to slightly opalescent, colourless orpale\nyellow liquid, free from suspended particles or cream-coloured\npowder or pellet which when reconstituted with the diluent\nsupplied by the manufacturer with the freeze-dried product\nyields a clear, colourless or pale yellow liquid."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Snake Venom Antiserum\nSnake Antivenin; Snake Antivenom Serum; Snake\nVenomAntitoxin\nSnake Venom Antiserum is a sterile preparation containing\nantitoxic globulins or their derivatives that have the power of\nspecifically neutralising the venom ofone or more species of\nsnakes. It may be obtained from the serum or plasma ofhealthy\nequines or other suitable animals immunised against venoms\nof specific species usually elapine and viperine. It may be a\npurified liquid preparation or a freeze-dried product. It may\ncontain a suitable preservative.\nDescription. A clear to slightly opalescent, colourless orpale\nyellow liquid, free from suspended particles or cream-coloured\npowder or pellet which when reconstituted with the diluent\nsupplied by the manufacturer with the freeze-dried product\nyields a clear, colourless or pale yellow liquid."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/soa-ka-tail","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Soa ka tail"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Soakatail\nDill Seeds Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation \nof the mature crushed seeds of Anethum sowa (Fam. \nUmbelliferae).\nDill seed oil contains not less than 40.0 per cent of limonene \nand not less than 43.0 per cent and not more than 63.0 per cent \nof carvone.\nDescription. A clear, pale yellow liquid, free from sediment, \nsuspended matter and fresh green spicy with a strong \ncamphoraceous odour. \n>"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Soakatail\nDill Seeds Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation \nof the mature crushed seeds of Anethum sowa (Fam. \nUmbelliferae).\nDill seed oil contains not less than 40.0 per cent of limonene \nand not less than 43.0 per cent and not more than 63.0 per cent \nof carvone.\nDescription. A clear, pale yellow liquid, free from sediment, \nsuspended matter and fresh green spicy with a strong \ncamphoraceous odour. \n>"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4; 13).\nTest solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of the oil under \nexamination in ethanol (95 per cent).\nReference solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of dill seed \noil IPRS vu ethanol (95 per cent).\nUse chromatographic system as described under Assay.\nThe; chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorresponds to the peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution. :\nB. Flash point (2.4.44). Not less than 100.0°.\nTests'\nRelative density (2.4.29) .0.925 to 0.980; ;\nRefractive index (2.4.27).1:4860to 1.4950. \n:\nOptical rotation (2.4.22). + 84.0° to+95.0®.\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of the \nexamination in ethanol (95 per cent).\noil under\nReference solution (a). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of cis- \ndihydrocarvone IPRS in ethanol (95 per cent). ■\nERGOT\nReference solution. A 0:005 iper cent w/v solution of\nReference solution (b) A 2 0 n \nH\n^^^ethanol^^ w/v soJuiio| Ration'\nresveratrol 1PRS in ethanol. \n■\nChromatographic system. \n1' - .«for ■\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm k 4.6 mm packed with\nJ)matographic system . >7; !; . r . , \nJ .f^croscopic—Mature dried fruit dark brown to black in\ncapillary column 30 m ¿0^5 \njl «¿5 $o 2,5 cm long and 0.5 to 1.5 cm wide; outer skin is\n- tem^ Cent Pkeny^lysiloxane;. ’Coale^ with niet^i •'wrinkled forming ridges and furrows; seeds,\nmperature: \n. \\ \n4| I fo 4 in number, 4 to 7 mm long, ovoid-rounded to\nsilica gel consisting of porous spherical particles with\n.. :.... ■ <\ncolumn: 50° for 1 minute, increase from 50° to 220° ,aù| \nrate of 10° per minute and maintain at this temperature! \nfor 13 minutes, \n: 7 MM- \n4\n- inlet port at 250° and detector at280®, \n- detection by flame ionization detector, \n|\nflow rate 1 rid per minute using hitrogen as carrier gas. 1 \n- injection volume: 1.0 pl,’ \ni\n-split ratio: 1:25,\nchemically bonded nitrile groups (5pm)MM: 7lit a \n- mobile phase: A. a solution prepared by dissolving\n'¿angular or simply ovoid, brown or black.\n0.136 g of potassium di-hydrogen dfthp^hofphate iri \n500 ml of water, add 0.5 ml of orthophosphoric acid and\nA.single layered epidermis cells filled with \n^¿¿ish-brown contents; mesocarp pulpy, made up of thin- \n^jedfirregular cells containing prismatic crystals of calcium\ndilute to 1000 ml with water,\nB. acetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\noxalatc> some fibro-vascular bundles also present in this\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, ' \n■ . -\n- spectrophotometer set at 310 nm, \nAM ,>\nregion^\nC^tlpmine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\n- injection volume: 20, pl.\nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 25 volumes of chloroform, 10\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\nvdlwneS of ethyl acetate and 1 volume of formic acid.\nCalculate the cis-dihydrocarvone, and LA carvone contents in \nthe oil under examination using the ratios.\nPV-’ . \n' \n■'\nj^^tiition, Extract 5 g of the substance under examination \nMtjyiffnilpf ethanol iot 15 minutes. Cool and filter.\n0\n100\n0\n18\n100\n0\nStorage. Store.protected from light and moisture.\n25 '\n55\n45\nj$J$encesolution: Extract 2 g of draksha IPBRS with 10 ml\n30\n20\n80\noF^dHo/for 15 minutes. Cool and filter.\n■|pplyirithe plate 10 pl of each solution as bands 10 mm by 2\n33\n100\n0\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injections is not"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-2-hydroxy-1-2-hydroxy-1-naphthylazo-naphthalene","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium 2-hydroxy-1-(2-hydroxy-1-naphthylazo)-naphthalene-"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium 2-hydroxy-1-(2-hydroxy-1-naphthylazo)-naphthalene-\n4-sulphonate: C2oH13N2NaOsS == 416.38\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nBrownish black powder with a violet sheen. Gives a purple-\nred colour with calcium ions in alkaline solution. When metal\nions are absent, for example, in the presence of an excess of\ndisodium edetate, the solution is blue.\nCalcon Mixture: A mixture of 1part ofcalcon with 99 parts of\nfreshly ignited anhydrous sodium sulphate.\nComplies with the following test."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium 2-hydroxy-1-(2-hydroxy-1-naphthylazo)-naphthalene-\n4-sulphonate: C2oH13N2NaOsS == 416.38\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nBrownish black powder with a violet sheen. Gives a purple-\nred colour with calcium ions in alkaline solution. When metal\nions are absent, for example, in the presence of an excess of\ndisodium edetate, the solution is blue.\nCalcon Mixture: A mixture of 1part ofcalcon with 99 parts of\nfreshly ignited anhydrous sodium sulphate.\nComplies with the following test."},{"name":"SENSITIVITY -","text":"Dissolve 0.1 gin 2.5 ml ofwater. To 1ml ofthe\nsolution add 50 ml of water, 10 ml of 1 M sodium hydroxide\nand 1 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of magnesium sulphate;\nthe solution is blue. Add 0.1 ml ofa 0.15 per cent w/v solution\nof calcium chloride; the solution becomes violet and on\nsubsequent addition of 0.1 ml of 0.01 M disodium edetate\nturns to blue again.\nCongo Red; CI 22120; Disodium (4,4'-biphenylbis-2,2-azo)\nbis(l-arninonaphthalene-4-sulphonate): C32H22N6Na206S2 ==\n696.66\nDark red or reddish brown powder. Decomposes on exposure\nto acid fumes."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-acetate-anhydrous-because-if-sodium-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium acetate anhydrous because if sodium acetate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium acetate anhydrous because if sodium acetate \ntrihydrate should be used, the trihydrate forms gives lower \nresults.\nDiluted Folin's Ciocalteau Reagent. Dilute 1 ml of Folin's \nCiocalteau reagent with 2 ml of water to get 3 ml of the reagent.\nNOTE-Folin's Ciocalteau reagent should be of the protein \nestimation grade and not the indicator grade. Folin's reagent \nshould be diluted to just before addition to the tubes since it\nis sensitive to light. Do not prepare this  solution at the \nbeginning of the assay.\nTyrosine reference solution. Dissolve 160 mg of tyrosine in \n100 ml of 0.2 M hydrochloric acid.\nDilute 1 ml of this solution to 100 ml with 0.2 M hydrochloric \nacid. Use 2 ml of this solution for colour development.\nTrisodium phosphate buffir pH 6.8. Weigh 19 g of trisodium \nphosphate and 6.4 ml of hydrochloric acid in 1000 ml of water,\nadjusted to pH 6.8.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing about 120 mg of Serratiopeptidase with\n100 ml of trisodium phosphate buffer pH  6.8. Stir on magnetic \nstirrer for 1 hour. Further dilute 5.0 ml to 25 ml with  5.0 per cent \nammonium sulphate solution, stir for 5 minutes and filter.\nDilute 2 ml of the filtrate to 100 ml with sodium tetraborate \nbuffer. 1 ml of resulting solution is used for analysis. Set the \nwater bath to 37°.\nFor each sample, keep four test tubes. Mark two test tubes as \ntest and two as blank. Add 5 ml of Casein Substrate to the \ntubes marked as test and 5 ml. of TCA to the test tube marked \nas blank. Prewarm these tubes for five minutes along with the \ntubes containing the sample dilutions. Add 1 ml of enzyme \nsolution to all these tubes and vertex the tubes for exactly 10 \nseconds. Keep the test tubes in the water-bath at 37° for 20 \nminutes for the reaction. After exactly 20 minutes, add 5 ml of \nTCA to tubes marked as test and 5 ml of Casein substrate to \nthe tube marked as blank Vortex all the tubes exactly for 30 \nseconds. Keep the tubes in the water-bath at 37° for 30 minutes \nfor precipitation, filter.\nNOTE- For tablets, the settlement may take  a longer time \nthan 30 minutes. So filter the solution after the settlement is \nclearly seen (Vortex in between for better precipitation).\nMark tubes as test, blank, tyrosine and tyrosine blank.\nAdd the following respectively;\nTest - 2 ml of the test filtrate.\nBlank - 2 ml of blank filtrate.\nTyrosine - 2 ml of tyrosine standard solution:\n\".."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium acetate anhydrous because if sodium acetate \ntrihydrate should be used, the trihydrate forms gives lower \nresults.\nDiluted Folin's Ciocalteau Reagent. Dilute 1 ml of Folin's \nCiocalteau reagent with 2 ml of water to get 3 ml of the reagent.\nNOTE-Folin's Ciocalteau reagent should be of the protein \nestimation grade and not the indicator grade. Folin's reagent \nshould be diluted to just before addition to the tubes since it\nis sensitive to light. Do not prepare this  solution at the \nbeginning of the assay.\nTyrosine reference solution. Dissolve 160 mg of tyrosine in \n100 ml of 0.2 M hydrochloric acid.\nDilute 1 ml of this solution to 100 ml with 0.2 M hydrochloric \nacid. Use 2 ml of this solution for colour development.\nTrisodium phosphate buffir pH 6.8. Weigh 19 g of trisodium \nphosphate and 6.4 ml of hydrochloric acid in 1000 ml of water,\nadjusted to pH 6.8.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing about 120 mg of Serratiopeptidase with\n100 ml of trisodium phosphate buffer pH  6.8. Stir on magnetic \nstirrer for 1 hour. Further dilute 5.0 ml to 25 ml with  5.0 per cent \nammonium sulphate solution, stir for 5 minutes and filter.\nDilute 2 ml of the filtrate to 100 ml with sodium tetraborate \nbuffer. 1 ml of resulting solution is used for analysis. Set the \nwater bath to 37°.\nFor each sample, keep four test tubes. Mark two test tubes as \ntest and two as blank. Add 5 ml of Casein Substrate to the \ntubes marked as test and 5 ml. of TCA to the test tube marked \nas blank. Prewarm these tubes for five minutes along with the \ntubes containing the sample dilutions. Add 1 ml of enzyme \nsolution to all these tubes and vertex the tubes for exactly 10 \nseconds. Keep the test tubes in the water-bath at 37° for 20 \nminutes for the reaction. After exactly 20 minutes, add 5 ml of \nTCA to tubes marked as test and 5 ml of Casein substrate to \nthe tube marked as blank Vortex all the tubes exactly for 30 \nseconds. Keep the tubes in the water-bath at 37° for 30 minutes \nfor precipitation, filter.\nNOTE- For tablets, the settlement may take  a longer time \nthan 30 minutes. So filter the solution after the settlement is \nclearly seen (Vortex in between for better precipitation).\nMark tubes as test, blank, tyrosine and tyrosine blank.\nAdd the following respectively;\nTest - 2 ml of the test filtrate.\nBlank - 2 ml of blank filtrate.\nTyrosine - 2 ml of tyrosine standard solution:\n\".."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-acetate-anhydrous","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Acetate, Anhydrous"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium acetate, Anhydrous\nInitial test must be performed to ensure that the test solution \ndoes not have interfering factors. It is necessary to re-validate \nthe test method when any changes are effected to the test \nprocedure that are likely to influence the result of the test. \nPrepare dilutions not exceeding MVD of the product and spike \nwith endotoxin concentration near or equal to the mid-point \nof the standard curve or equal to twice the LOD. The mean \nrecovery needs to be calculated by subtracting the mean \nconcentration of endotoxin equivalents in the solution (if any) \nfrom that in the spiked solution. The test solution is considered \nfree of interfering factors if the recovery of endotoxin is within \n50 to 200 per cent of the spiked endotoxin concentration.\nCell Sources and Qualification\n5m1\nAdenine-Guanine —Uracil Solution\nRiboflavin-Thiamine Hydrochloride-\nBiotin Solution"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium acetate, Anhydrous\nInitial test must be performed to ensure that the test solution \ndoes not have interfering factors. It is necessary to re-validate \nthe test method when any changes are effected to the test \nprocedure that are likely to influence the result of the test. \nPrepare dilutions not exceeding MVD of the product and spike \nwith endotoxin concentration near or equal to the mid-point \nof the standard curve or equal to twice the LOD. The mean \nrecovery needs to be calculated by subtracting the mean \nconcentration of endotoxin equivalents in the solution (if any) \nfrom that in the spiked solution. The test solution is considered \nfree of interfering factors if the recovery of endotoxin is within \n50 to 200 per cent of the spiked endotoxin concentration.\nCell Sources and Qualification\n5m1\nAdenine-Guanine —Uracil Solution\nRiboflavin-Thiamine Hydrochloride-\nBiotin Solution"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-acetate-of-the-indian-pharmacopoeia","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Acetate: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Acetate: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nSodium Acetate, Anhydrous: CH3COONa = 82:03\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS \t\nIP 2018 \n1111T\nIP 2018 \t\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nConsists of sodium bisulphite, NaHSO 3, and sodium \nmetabisulphite, Na2S2O5, in varying proportions and yielding \nnot less than 58.5 per cent w/w and not more than 67.4 per \ncent w/w of SO 2, determined by the following method.\nSodium Citrate: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nWhite, pale-grey or very pale brown, crystalline masses; \nhygroscopic.\nSodium Cobaltinitrite; Sodium Hexanitritocobaltate(III): \nNa3Co(NO2)6 = 403.94\nComplies with the following test.\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nASSAY - Weigh accurately about 0.2 g, transfer to a glass-\nstoppered flask, add 50 ml of 0.05 M iodine and insert the \nstopper. Allow to stand for 5 minutes, add 1 ml of hydrochloric \nacid and titrate the excess of iodine with 0.1 M sodium \nthiosulphate using starch solution, added towards the end \nof the titriation, as indicator. 1 ml of 0.05 M iodine is equivalent \nto 0.003203 g of 502.\nLOSS ON DRYING - Not more than 2 per cent when dried at\nOrange-yellow powder."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium Acetate: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nSodium Acetate, Anhydrous: CH3COONa = 82:03\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS \t\nIP 2018 \n1111T\nIP 2018 \t\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nConsists of sodium bisulphite, NaHSO 3, and sodium \nmetabisulphite, Na2S2O5, in varying proportions and yielding \nnot less than 58.5 per cent w/w and not more than 67.4 per \ncent w/w of SO 2, determined by the following method.\nSodium Citrate: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nWhite, pale-grey or very pale brown, crystalline masses; \nhygroscopic.\nSodium Cobaltinitrite; Sodium Hexanitritocobaltate(III): \nNa3Co(NO2)6 = 403.94\nComplies with the following test.\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nASSAY - Weigh accurately about 0.2 g, transfer to a glass-\nstoppered flask, add 50 ml of 0.05 M iodine and insert the \nstopper. Allow to stand for 5 minutes, add 1 ml of hydrochloric \nacid and titrate the excess of iodine with 0.1 M sodium \nthiosulphate using starch solution, added towards the end \nof the titriation, as indicator. 1 ml of 0.05 M iodine is equivalent \nto 0.003203 g of 502.\nLOSS ON DRYING - Not more than 2 per cent when dried at\nOrange-yellow powder."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Inject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent. \n;\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of Ci6H]5F6NsO in the tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nLabelling. The label states the quantity of active ingredients \nin terms of the equivalent amount of sitagliptin.\nSodium Acetate\nCH3COONa,3H2O\nC^NaO* 3H2O \nMol. Wt. 136.1\nSodium Acetate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C2H3NaO2, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (for peritoneal dialysis fluids).\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder.\nDissolve 10.0 g in sufficient carbon dioxide-free water to \nproduce 100.0 ml (solution A).; 1ml of solution A gives reaction \n(B) of acetates and reaction (A) of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nAppearance\" of solution.; Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and \ncolourless(2.4.1). \nP -\npH (2.4.24). 7.5to 9.0, determined in a 5.0 per cent w/v solution.\nArsenic (2.3.1'0). Dissolve 5.0 g in 50 ml of \nand add 15 ml\nof stannated hydrochloric acid AsT. The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic (2 ppm) .\nCalcium and magnesium. Not more than 50 ppm, calculated \nas Ca, determined by the following niethod. Mix 200 ml of \nwater with 10 ml of ammonia buffer pH 10.0,0.1 g of mordant \nblack 11 mixture and 2 ml of 0.05 Mzinc chloride. Add \ndrop wise 0.02 M disodium edetate until the- colour changes \nfrom violet to blue. To this solution add 10 g of the substance \nunder examination, shake to dissolve and. titrate with \n0.02 M disodium 'edetate uritil the blue colour is restored. Not \nmore than 0.65 ml of 0.02 M disodium edetate is required.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 12 ml of solution A complies with the \nlimit test for heavy metals, Method D (10 ppm), using 10 ml of \nlead standard solution (1 ppm Pb).\nIron (2.3.14). 20 ml of solution A complies with the limit test for\nChlorides (2.3.12). 10 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (250 ppm).\n3589\nwww.webofpharma.com"},"sections":[{"name":"SODIUM ACETATE","text":"Inject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent. \n;\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of Ci6H]5F6NsO in the tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nLabelling. The label states the quantity of active ingredients \nin terms of the equivalent amount of sitagliptin.\nSodium Acetate\nCH3COONa,3H2O\nC^NaO* 3H2O \nMol. Wt. 136.1\nSodium Acetate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C2H3NaO2, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (for peritoneal dialysis fluids).\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder.\nDissolve 10.0 g in sufficient carbon dioxide-free water to \nproduce 100.0 ml (solution A).; 1ml of solution A gives reaction \n(B) of acetates and reaction (A) of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nAppearance\" of solution.; Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and \ncolourless(2.4.1). \nP -\npH (2.4.24). 7.5to 9.0, determined in a 5.0 per cent w/v solution.\nArsenic (2.3.1'0). Dissolve 5.0 g in 50 ml of \nand add 15 ml\nof stannated hydrochloric acid AsT. The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic (2 ppm) .\nCalcium and magnesium. Not more than 50 ppm, calculated \nas Ca, determined by the following niethod. Mix 200 ml of \nwater with 10 ml of ammonia buffer pH 10.0,0.1 g of mordant \nblack 11 mixture and 2 ml of 0.05 Mzinc chloride. Add \ndrop wise 0.02 M disodium edetate until the- colour changes \nfrom violet to blue. To this solution add 10 g of the substance \nunder examination, shake to dissolve and. titrate with \n0.02 M disodium 'edetate uritil the blue colour is restored. Not \nmore than 0.65 ml of 0.02 M disodium edetate is required.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 12 ml of solution A complies with the \nlimit test for heavy metals, Method D (10 ppm), using 10 ml of \nlead standard solution (1 ppm Pb).\nIron (2.3.14). 20 ml of solution A complies with the limit test for\nChlorides (2.3.12). 10 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (250 ppm).\n3589\nwww.webofpharma.com"},{"name":"SODIUM ACETATE","text":"Sulphates (2.3.17). 7;5 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for sulphates (200 ppm). : :: <■\nReducing substances. Dissolve 1.0 g in 100 ml of boiling water, \nadd 5 ml of 1Msulphuric acid and 0.5 ml of0.002 Mpotassium \npermanganate, mix and boil gently for 5 minutes; the pink\ncolour is not completely discharged.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). 39.0 to 40.5 per cent, determined on \n0.2 g by drying in an oven at 130°. Place the substance under \nexamination in the oven while the oven is still cold.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.25 g in 50 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid, add 5 ml of acetic anhydride, mix and allow to stand for \n30 minutes. Titrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid, using Ó.3 nil of \n1-naphtholbenzein solution as indicator, until a green colour \nis produced. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.00820 g of \nC2H3NaO2. ■ \n' ' \n: ; \n-/\nSodium Acetate intended for use in the preparationof dialysis \nsolutions complies with the following additional requirement.\nAluminium. Dissolve 20 g in 100 ml of water and adjust to \npH 6.0 by the addition of about 10 ml of 1 M hydrochloric \nacid. Extract with successive quantities of 20,20 and 1.0 ml of \na 0.5 per cent w/v solution of 8-hydroxyquinoline in \nchloroform and dilute 'the combined1 extracts to 5 0 ml with \nchloroform ': Use as thé’ standard solution a mixtüré ôf 2.0 ml of \naluminium 'standard solution f 2 ppm Al), 10 ml of acetate \nbuffer pH 6.0 and 98 ml of water treated in the same manner \nand as the blank solution a mixture of 10 ml of acetate buffer \npH6.0‘àndTOO ml of water treated in the sâme'manner. Measure\nthe fluorescence (2.4.5) of the test solution and the standard \nsolution,,u$ing an excitation wavelength ,qf about 392 nm and \nemission: wavelength of about 518 nm, and .setting thé \ninstrument to zero with the blank solution in each casé. The \nfluorescence of the test solution is not more than that of the \nstandard solution (0.2 ppm).' ’ ' \n' \n■'••••:\nStorage. Store protectedfrom light, , . \n. . . . .\nLabelling. The label states whether or hot the; material is \nintended for. use in: thé manufacture of dialysis solutions."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-alendronate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Alendronate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"SODIUM ALENDRONATE TABLETS\nmore than the area ofthe principal peakinthe chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (d) (0.5 per cent). <\nHeavy metals (2:3:13).1 .O g compì ies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). \n■\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Between 16.1 per. cent to 17.1 per cent, \ndetermined on 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 140° to 145°.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as \ndescribed under Impurities B and C with the following \nmodifications. \nr ; • • u\nInject the reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CjH^NNaCMV\nSodium Alendronate Tablets\nAlendronic Acid Tablets; Alendronate Sodium Tablets\nSodium Alendronate Tablets contains Sodium Alendronate \nTrihydrate.\nSodium. Alendronate Tablets contain , sodium alendronate \nequivalent to not less than 95.0 per cent and not more than\n105.0 per cent of the stated amount of alendronic acid , \nC4HI3NO?P?.. \n■- \n.\nUsual strength. 10 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"SODIUM ALENDRONATE TABLETS\nmore than the area ofthe principal peakinthe chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (d) (0.5 per cent). <\nHeavy metals (2:3:13).1 .O g compì ies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). \n■\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Between 16.1 per. cent to 17.1 per cent, \ndetermined on 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 140° to 145°.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as \ndescribed under Impurities B and C with the following \nmodifications. \nr ; • • u\nInject the reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CjH^NNaCMV\nSodium Alendronate Tablets\nAlendronic Acid Tablets; Alendronate Sodium Tablets\nSodium Alendronate Tablets contains Sodium Alendronate \nTrihydrate.\nSodium. Alendronate Tablets contain , sodium alendronate \nequivalent to not less than 95.0 per cent and not more than\n105.0 per cent of the stated amount of alendronic acid , \nC4HI3NO?P?.. \n■- \n.\nUsual strength. 10 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution(2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes. ”\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Use the filtrate, dilute if,necessary, with water, \nto produce a solution, containing 0.00084 per cent w/v of \nalendronic acid.\nReference solution. A. 0.0011 per cent w/v solution of sodium \nalendronate IPRS in water.\nChromatographic system , o <: . . - \n...\n- a stainless steel column 15. cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nanion exchange resin (7 pm) (Such as Allsep Anion),\n- column temperature: 35°,\n...............................................................   «*2022\n- mobile phase: a 0.02 per cent v/v solution of\n: acid, pH adjusted.to 3.5 with 2 M sodium hydroxide\n. — flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute,. \n;!rT.\n.. : - , refractive index detector, maintained at 40°, ;. ,? , •\n-- injection volume: 100 pl. \n/.'m\nInject thé reference solution and the test solution;\nCalculate the content of Ç4H13NO7P2 in the medium. , ;\nQ. Not less than; 75, per cent of the .stated'amount of \nC4HI3NO7P2.. \n,;.a \n. ...\nPhosphate and phosphite. Détermine; by liquid \nchromatography (2.4.14). \n'\n■ \nf \nV /'■ \n.•••’.■■••■••'.a ìfiT \n'■\nTest solution. Shake a, quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 0.1 g of alendronic acid with 20 ml of water and mix \nwith the aid of ultrasound for 30 minutes. Add sufficient water \nto produce 25.0 ml and filter.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.0024.per cent w/v \nof orthophosphoric acid and 0.002 per.cent w/v of \nphosphorous acid vn. water. ■ \n;■ \n: T-k ?;\nUse thè chromatographic system as dèseribéd under \nDissolution. \n1 3\nRun the chromatogram twice the retention time of the principal \npeak.\nInject the reference solution; The relative retention time with \nreference to alendronic acid (retention time, about i 7 minutes) \nare: phosphate, about 1.3; phosphite, about 1.6. \n;\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nsignal to noise ratios of the peaks due to phosphate and \nphosphite are not less than 10, \n.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram-obtained with the test solution,, the area of \neach; peak due to phosphate and phosphite is not more than \nthe area of the corresponding peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (0.5 per cent).\n4-Aminobutanoic acid. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). . ,\nBuffer solution. A solution containing 0.294 per cent w/v of \nsodium citrate and 0,142 per cent w/y of anhydrous disodiiffl \nhydrogen orthophosphate, adjusted to pH 8.0 using \northophosphoric acid and filter.\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 23 mg of alendronic acid with a 2.94 per cent w/v \nsolution of sodium citrate and dilute to 50.0 ml with the same \nsolvent, mix and filter; discard the first 5 ml of filtrate. To 5 ml . \nof the filtrate, in a screw cap centrifuge tube, add 5 -ml of a 1.91 ; \nper cent w/v solution of sodium borate, 10 ml of a 0.2 per cent \nw/v. solution of (9-fiuorenyl)methyl chloroformate m : \nacetonitrile, shake for L minute and allow to stand at room ;■\n3592\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-alendronate-trihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Alendronate Trihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Alendronate Trihydrate\nSodium Alendronate \n; \n. < u .r:\nHO'P-ONa . \n;\n- \n. 3H2O . \n,\n. ho'oh .It\"!\nC4H12NNaO7P2,3H2O \n. : : / Mol. Wt. 325.1\n3590 \nMllillliBWM\n..........             ^'2tW\nSodium Alendronate Trihydrate is monosodium trihydj-Qg^ | \n(4-amino-1 -hydroxybutylidene)bisphosphonate trihydrate. I\nSodium Alendronate Trihydrate contains not lds§ than 1 \nper cent and hot more than 102.0 per cent of C4Hl2NNaQ | \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n■ ; \n|\nI\nCategory. Bisphosphonate; treatment of osteoporosis\nDescription, A white or almost white, crystalline powder ;"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Alendronate Trihydrate\nSodium Alendronate \n; \n. < u .r:\nHO'P-ONa . \n;\n- \n. 3H2O . \n,\n. ho'oh .It\"!\nC4H12NNaO7P2,3H2O \n. : : / Mol. Wt. 325.1\n3590 \nMllillliBWM\n..........             ^'2tW\nSodium Alendronate Trihydrate is monosodium trihydj-Qg^ | \n(4-amino-1 -hydroxybutylidene)bisphosphonate trihydrate. I\nSodium Alendronate Trihydrate contains not lds§ than 1 \nper cent and hot more than 102.0 per cent of C4Hl2NNaQ | \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n■ ; \n|\nI\nCategory. Bisphosphonate; treatment of osteoporosis\nDescription, A white or almost white, crystalline powder ;"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6), \nCompare the spectrum with, that- obtained with sodium\nalendronate IP RS or with the reference spectrum of sodium I\nalendronate. .. - \n... •••. \n1\nB.It gives reaction (a) of sodium salt (2.3.1). ■ \n> \n|\nTests--. -. \n•; . \n: --..yro \n. |\nAppearance of solution. A1.0 per cent w/v solution is clear I\nand not more intensely coloured than reference solution BYS7 f\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 5.0 determined in a 1.0 per cent w/y solution. |\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography 1 \n(2.4.14). \n' . ■ - \nH . w- J\nSolution A. Dissolve 29.4 g of sodium citrate in water and M \n;'i ' \n!? \n.-.t ... \n»/'.• ' ■ \n' ' j * •'' : U C. / ;• ; ■:t\". y;\ndilute to 1000 ml with the same solvent. .\nSolution B. Dissolve 19.1 g of disodium tetraborate in water |\nand dilute to 1000 ml with the same solvent. \n|\nSolution C. Dissolve 0.200 g of (9-fluorenyl) methyl \nchloroformate in acetonitrile and dilute to 50.0 -ml with the \nsame solvent. \n•: w;\nNOTE — Prepare solutions 'immediately before use.\nTest solution. Dissolve 30 mg'of the substance under \nexamination in 50 ml of solution A. Transfer 5.0 ml of the \nsolution to a 50 ml polypropylene screw cap ccntfifuge tube \ncontaining 5.0 ml of solution B ; Add 5.0 ml of acetonitrile and \n5.0 ml of solution C. Shake and allow to stand at room \ntemperature for 30 minutes; Add 20 ml of méthylène chloride \nand shake'vigorously for; oneminute. Centrifugé' for 5-10\nminutes and use the clear upper layer..............\nReference solution fa).-,Dissolve 15 mg of 4-aminobutanoic \nacid (impurity N) in 100.0 ml of solution A. Dilute 10.0 nil of \nthe solution to 50.0 mi with solution A. Transfer 5.0 mlofl^ \nsolution to a 50 ml polypropylene screw cap centrifuge tub? \ncontaining; 5.0 ml of solution B. Add 5.0 ml of acetonitrile^ \n5.0 ml of solution C. Shake and allow to stand at room \ntemperature for 30 minutes: Add 20 ml of methylene chloride \nand shake vigorously for one minute; Centrifuge for 5-1\nminutes and use the clear upper layer.\nSODIUM ALENDRONATE TRIHYDRATE\nIP 2022......  _.................................................................\n... ; \nib). Dissolve 3 mg of sodium alendronate\n^Sn»iBi«g:iifilPurity D) 'and dilute to 5.0 ml with\n■ \n' a? .Transfer this solution to-a 50 ml polypropylene\n50lu \nCentrifuge tube containing 5.0 ml of solution B.\n' 0 nJ \nan^ 5 0 so^ut'on C- Shake and\nto stand at room temperature for 30 minutes. Add 20 ml\nand siiake vi2orous,y for one minute. \nCentrifuge for 5 to 10 minutes and use the clear upper layer.\n^Iank solution; Transfer 5 ml of solution A to a 50 ml \nnolypropylene screw cap centrifuge tube containing 5.0 ml of \n^utipn ^gAdd 5*0 ;ml pf aceipnzirzZe and 5 .0 ml of solution G,\nShake and allow to stand at room temperature for 30 minutes. \nAdd 20 ml of methylene chloride and shake vigorously for a \nminute. Centrifuge for 5 to 10 fninutes and use the clear upper\nChromatographicsystem\n-. a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.1 mm, packed with\nstyrene-divinylbenzcne copolymer (10 pm)\n-column temperature: 45°,\n_ mobile- phase: A. 15 volumes of acetonitrile and 85\nvolutnes.of a buffer solution, prepared by dissolving\n; 2.84 g of anhydrous disodium hydrogen phosphate and\n5.88 g of sodium citrate in 2000 ml water, adjusted to pH \n8.0 with orthophosphoric acid or sodium hydroxide,\nBi 70 volumes of acetonitrile and’30 \nvolumes of a buffer solution prepared by dissolving \ns, 2.84gofnn/?ydrai/.S' disodium hydrogen phosphate and\n5.88 g of sodium citrate in 2000 ml water, adjusted to pH \n8.0 with orthophosphoric acid or sodium hydroxide, \n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1.8 fnl per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 266 nin, \n- injectionvolume: 20ul. r;\nTithe?: : Mobile phase A Mobile phase B \n(inmin.)-' (per cent v/v) (per cent v/v)\n' 0 '\n10Q {) .\n■ ■ \n50 \n- \n50\n28 \n0 \n100\n/”;35W-v:;.c Joo \n- . \n- o\nT^7^ntiop time of alendronate is about 7 minutes.\nThe relative retention time with reference to alendronate, for\n’mPurity D and impurity Ais about 1.4 and 1.9 respectively.\nrsference solution (a) and reference solution (b) to\n1 \nthe pc^k due to impurity A and impurity D.\ninject the reterence solution, (b). The test is not valid unless\ne resolution; between the peak.due to alendronate and \nlmPwityDisnot-less than 5.0.\nj$ferehCe solution (a) and the test solution. In the \n°matogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any\npeak corresponding to impurity A multiply by correction factor \n0.4 is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.15 per \ncent), the area of any secondary peak is. not more than the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (0.1 per cent) and the sum of areas of all \nthe secondary peaks is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0,5 per cent), calculated by area normalization.\nImpurities B and C. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). . . \n. t.t, . . \n.\nTest solution,- Dissolve 50;Q mg of the substance under \nexamination in water and dilute to 25.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 50.0 mg of sodium \nalendronate trihydrate IPRS in water and dilute to 25.0 ml \nwith the water. \n' w\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 3.0 g ofphosphoric acid in\nwater and dilute to 100.0 ml with the water. Dilute 1.0 ml of the \nsolution to 100.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve 2,5 g ofphosphorous acid in \nwater and dilute to' 100.0 ml with the water. Dilute 1.0 ml of the \nsolution to 100.Q ml with water.\nReference solution (d). Mix 2.0 ml of reference solution (b) \nand 2.0 ml of reference solution (c) and dilute to 50.0 ml with \nwater.\nChromatographic system \n-\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nanion-exchange resin containing quaternary ammonium \ngroups. [CILN 4ÇCH3)3] attached to a lattice consisting \nof methacrylate (7 pm),\n-"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-alendronate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Alendronate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium  Alendronate\nHeavy metals (2.3.13).12 ml of solution A complies with the \nlimit test for heavy metals, Method D (10 ppm), using 10 ml of \nlead standard solution (1 ppm Pb).\nO OH\nH2N \t\nHO P - 0Na\nP  -_0 \nOH 3H 20\nIron (2.3.14). 20 ml of solution A complies with the limit test for \niron (20 ppm).\nHO,\nChlorides (2.3.12). 10 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (250 ppm).\nC4H12NNa07P2, 3H10 \t\nMol. Wt. 325.1\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 3 mg ofsodium alendronate \nRS  (containing impurity D) and dilute to 5.0 ml with \nsolution A. Transfer this solution to a 50 ml polypropylene \nscrew cap centrifuge tube containing 5.0 ml of solution B. \nAdd 5.0 ml of acetonitrile and 5.0 ml of solution C. Shake and \nallow to stand at room temperature for 30 minutes. Add 20 ml \nof methylene chloride and shake vigorously for one minute. \nCentrifuge for 5 to 10 minutes and use the clear upper layer.\nSodium Alendronate Trihydrate is monosodium trihydrogen \n(4-amino-l-hydroxybutylidene)bisphosphonate trihydrate.\nSulphates  (2.3.17). 7.5 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for sulphates (200 ppm).\nSodium Alendronate Trihydrate contains not less than 98.0 \nper cent and not more than 102.0 per cent of  C4  HI2NNa07P2, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nReducing substances. Dissolve 1.0 g in 100 ml of boiling water, \nadd 5 ml of 1 M sulphuric acid and 0.5 ml of 0.002 M potassium \npermanganate,  mix and boil gently for 5 minutes; the pink\nCategory. Bisphosphonate; treatment of osteoporosis.\ncolour is not completely discharged.\nDose. 10 mg daily or 70 mg weekly.\nboss on drying (2.4.19). 39.0 to 40.5 per cent, determined on \n0.2 g by drying in an oven at 130°. Place the substance under \nexamination in the oven while the oven is still cold.\nBlank solution. Transfer 5 ml of solution A to a 50 ml \npolypropylene screw cap centrifuge tube containing 5.0 ml of \nsolution B. Add 5.0 ml ofacetonitrile and 5.0 ml of solution C. \nShake and allow to stand at room temperature for 30 minutes. \nAdd 20 ml of methylene chloride and shake vigorously for a \nminute. Centrifuge for 5 to 10 minutes and use the clear upper \nlayer.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium  Alendronate\nHeavy metals (2.3.13).12 ml of solution A complies with the \nlimit test for heavy metals, Method D (10 ppm), using 10 ml of \nlead standard solution (1 ppm Pb).\nO OH\nH2N \t\nHO P - 0Na\nP  -_0 \nOH 3H 20\nIron (2.3.14). 20 ml of solution A complies with the limit test for \niron (20 ppm).\nHO,\nChlorides (2.3.12). 10 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (250 ppm).\nC4H12NNa07P2, 3H10 \t\nMol. Wt. 325.1\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 3 mg ofsodium alendronate \nRS  (containing impurity D) and dilute to 5.0 ml with \nsolution A. Transfer this solution to a 50 ml polypropylene \nscrew cap centrifuge tube containing 5.0 ml of solution B. \nAdd 5.0 ml of acetonitrile and 5.0 ml of solution C. Shake and \nallow to stand at room temperature for 30 minutes. Add 20 ml \nof methylene chloride and shake vigorously for one minute. \nCentrifuge for 5 to 10 minutes and use the clear upper layer.\nSodium Alendronate Trihydrate is monosodium trihydrogen \n(4-amino-l-hydroxybutylidene)bisphosphonate trihydrate.\nSulphates  (2.3.17). 7.5 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for sulphates (200 ppm).\nSodium Alendronate Trihydrate contains not less than 98.0 \nper cent and not more than 102.0 per cent of  C4  HI2NNa07P2, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nReducing substances. Dissolve 1.0 g in 100 ml of boiling water, \nadd 5 ml of 1 M sulphuric acid and 0.5 ml of 0.002 M potassium \npermanganate,  mix and boil gently for 5 minutes; the pink\nCategory. Bisphosphonate; treatment of osteoporosis.\ncolour is not completely discharged.\nDose. 10 mg daily or 70 mg weekly.\nboss on drying (2.4.19). 39.0 to 40.5 per cent, determined on \n0.2 g by drying in an oven at 130°. Place the substance under \nexamination in the oven while the oven is still cold.\nBlank solution. Transfer 5 ml of solution A to a 50 ml \npolypropylene screw cap centrifuge tube containing 5.0 ml of \nsolution B. Add 5.0 ml ofacetonitrile and 5.0 ml of solution C. \nShake and allow to stand at room temperature for 30 minutes. \nAdd 20 ml of methylene chloride and shake vigorously for a \nminute. Centrifuge for 5 to 10 minutes and use the clear upper \nlayer.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Assay. Dissolve 0.25 g in 50 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid, add 5 ml of acetic anhydride,  mix and allow to stand for \n30 minutes. Titrate with  0.1 Mperchloric acid,  using 0.3 ml of \n1-naphtholbenzein solution as indicator, until a green colour \nis produced. Carry out a blank titration.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with sodium\n-oonnaattee. RS or with the reference spectrum of sodium \n;\nalend\nalendronate\nChromatographic system\nTB.eIsttgsives  reaction (a) of sodium salt (2.3.1).\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.00820 g of\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.1 mm, packed with \nstyrene-divinylbenzene copolymer (101.1m) \n-\ncolumn temperature: 45\", \n-\nmobile phase: A.15 volumes of acetonitrile and 85 \nvolumes of a buffer solution prepared by dissolving \n2.84 g of anhydrous disodium hydrogen phosphate and \n5.88 g ofsodium citrate in 2000 ml water, adjusted to pH \n8.0 with orthophosphoric acid  or sodium hydroxide, \nB. 70 volumes of acetonitrile and 30\nC2H3Na02.\nSodium Acetate intended for use in the preparation of dialysis \nsolutions complies with the following additional requirement\nAppearance of solution. A 1.0  per cent w/v solution is clear \n(a2n.4d.n14:more intensely coloured than reference solution BYS7\nAluminium. Dissolve 20 g in 100 ml of water and adjust to \npH 6.0 by the addition of about 10 ml of 1 M hydrochloric \nacid. Extract with successive quantities of 20, 20 and 10 ml of\n(2.4.1).\nPH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 5.0 determined in a 1.0 per cent w/v solution. \nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n,\na 0.5 per cent w/v solution of 8-hydroxyquinoline  in \nchlgroform and dilute the combined extracts to 50 ml with \nchlotOrm. Use as the standard solution a mixture of 2.0 ml of \naluminium standard solution ( 2 ppm Al), 10 ml of  acetate\nSolution A.  Dissolve 29.4 g of sodium citrate in water and \ndilute to 1000 ml with the same solvent.\n-voltunes of a buffer solution prepared by dissolving\n2.84 g of anhydrous disodium hydrogen phosphate  and\nSODIUM ALENDRONATE TRIHYDRATE\n5.88 g ofsodium citrate in 2000 ml water, adjusted to pH \n8.0 with orthophosphoric acid or sodium hydroxide, \n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \nflow rate: 1.8 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 266 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nMobile phase B\nTime \n(in min.)\nMobile phase A\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0 \n100 \n0\n3 \n100 \n0\n18 \n50 \n50\n28 \n0 \n100\n35 \n100 \n0\nThe retention time of alendronate is about 7 minutes.\nThe relative retention time with reference to alendronate for \nimpurity D and impurity A is about 1.4 and 1.9 respectively.\nInject reference solution (a) and reference solution (b) to \nidentify the peak due to impurity A and impurity D.\nInject the reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peak due to alendronate and \nimpurity D is not less than 5.0.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to impurity A multiply by correction factor \n0.4 is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.15 per \ncent), the area of any secondary peak is not more than the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (0.1 per cent). The sum of areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.5 per cent), calculated by area normalization.\nImpurities B and C. Determine by liquid chromatography  \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 50.0 mg of the substance under \nexamination in water and dilute to 25.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 50.0 mg of sodium \nalendronate trihydrate RS in water and dilute to 25.0 ml with \nthe water.\nReference solution (h).  Dissolve 3.0 g of phosphoric acid in \nwater and dilute to 100.0 ml with the water. Dilute 1.0 ml of this \nsolution to 100.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve 2.5 g of phosphorous acid in\nwater and dilute to 100.0 ml with the water. Dilute 1.0 ml of this \nsolution to 100.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (d). Mix 2.0 ml of reference solution r(b) \nand 2.0 ml of reference solution (c) and dilute to 50.0 int with \nwater."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-alginate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Alginate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"SODIUM ALGINATE\nIP 2022\nrature for 30 minutes, add 20 ml of dichloromethane \nshake vigorously f°r * minutei centrifuge and use the\n... .\n„ferencesolutionfa). Dilute 5.0 ml solution containing 0.0003\n■ cent w/v oi4-aminobutanoic acid in a 2.94 per cent w/v \nsolution of sodium citrate in a screw cap centrifuge tube add \n5 ml of a 1 9i Pe]r cent w^v solution cfi sodium borate, 10 ml of \na 0 2 percent w/v solution of (9-fluorenyl)methyl chloroformate \nin acetonitrile, shake for 45 seconds and allow to stand at \nroom, temperature for 30 minutes, add 20 ml of\nshake vigorously for 1 minute, \ncentrifuge and use the aqueous layer.\n^eferencesohition (b). ‘Dilute 5.0 ml solution containing 0.06 \nper cent w/v ofsodium alendronate IPRS arid 0.01 per cent w/ \nv (£4-aminpfutanoic. acid in a 2.94 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium-citraterip a screw cap centrifuge tube add 5 ml of a\n1.91 per. cent w/y solution oisodium borate, 10 ml of a 0.2 per \ncent w/v solution of (9-fluorenyl)methyl .chloroformate in \nacetonitrile, shake for 45 seconds and allow to stand at room \ntemperature for 30 minutes, add 20 ml of dichloromethane \nand shake vigorously for 1 minute; centrifuge and use the \naqueouslayer.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm * 4.1 mm, packed with \n/ styrenq-diyinylbenzene co-polymer (10 pm) (Such as\nHamilton PPP-1),\n- column temperature: 45°,\n-mobile phase: A. a mixture of 15 volumes of acetonitrile\n. and 85,volumes of buffer solution;\n. \nB. a mixture of 70 volumes of acetonitrile\n/ and 30 volumes of buffer solution,\n- flow rate: 1.8 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 266 nm, \n- injection volume; 20pl.\nTime Mobile phase A \n(inmin.) (percentv/v)\nMobile phase B\n■ 100\n(per cent v/v)\n......100 .\n.50 .\n■.7i ,q .^32;.^ . \n100\n• \n... ioo\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to sodium alendronate and \namhiobutanoic acid, is not less than 10.0. \n:"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"SODIUM ALGINATE\nIP 2022\nrature for 30 minutes, add 20 ml of dichloromethane \nshake vigorously f°r * minutei centrifuge and use the\n... .\n„ferencesolutionfa). Dilute 5.0 ml solution containing 0.0003\n■ cent w/v oi4-aminobutanoic acid in a 2.94 per cent w/v \nsolution of sodium citrate in a screw cap centrifuge tube add \n5 ml of a 1 9i Pe]r cent w^v solution cfi sodium borate, 10 ml of \na 0 2 percent w/v solution of (9-fluorenyl)methyl chloroformate \nin acetonitrile, shake for 45 seconds and allow to stand at \nroom, temperature for 30 minutes, add 20 ml of\nshake vigorously for 1 minute, \ncentrifuge and use the aqueous layer.\n^eferencesohition (b). ‘Dilute 5.0 ml solution containing 0.06 \nper cent w/v ofsodium alendronate IPRS arid 0.01 per cent w/ \nv (£4-aminpfutanoic. acid in a 2.94 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium-citraterip a screw cap centrifuge tube add 5 ml of a\n1.91 per. cent w/y solution oisodium borate, 10 ml of a 0.2 per \ncent w/v solution of (9-fluorenyl)methyl .chloroformate in \nacetonitrile, shake for 45 seconds and allow to stand at room \ntemperature for 30 minutes, add 20 ml of dichloromethane \nand shake vigorously for 1 minute; centrifuge and use the \naqueouslayer.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm * 4.1 mm, packed with \n/ styrenq-diyinylbenzene co-polymer (10 pm) (Such as\nHamilton PPP-1),\n- column temperature: 45°,\n-mobile phase: A. a mixture of 15 volumes of acetonitrile\n. and 85,volumes of buffer solution;\n. \nB. a mixture of 70 volumes of acetonitrile\n/ and 30 volumes of buffer solution,\n- flow rate: 1.8 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 266 nm, \n- injection volume; 20pl.\nTime Mobile phase A \n(inmin.) (percentv/v)\nMobile phase B\n■ 100\n(per cent v/v)\n......100 .\n.50 .\n■.7i ,q .^32;.^ . \n100\n• \n... ioo\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to sodium alendronate and \namhiobutanoic acid, is not less than 10.0. \n:"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Inject, reference solution , (a) and the test solution. In the\n,c omatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of\nPeakdue to4-ammobutanoic acid is not more than the area of\nprincipal peak in,the chromatogram obtained with reference \n^iudon(a)(Q;5 cent).\nUniformity of content. Complies with tests stated under \nTablets.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as described \nunder Assay with the following modification.\nTest solution. Disperse 1 tablet in 10 ml of water, with the aid \nof ultrasound for 20 minutes and dilute to 25.0 ml with water \nand filter.\nCalculate the content of C4H13NO7P2 in the tablet.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablet.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof the powder containing 50 mg of alendronic acid with 20 ml \nof water for 30 minutes with the aid of ultrasound and \noccasional shaking and agitate until cool, add sufficient water \nto produce 25.0 ml, mix arid filter\nReference solution. A 0.25 per cent w/v solution of sodium \nalendronate IPRS in water.\nUse chromatographic system as described under Dissolution.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C4Hl3NO7P2 in the tablets.\n1 mg of C4Hl2NNad7p2,3H2O is equivalent to 0.7662 mg of\nalendronic acid, C4H13NO7P2.\nLabelling. The label states the strength in terms of the \nequivalent amount of alendronic acid.\nSodium Alginate"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-aminosalicylate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Aminosalicylate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Aminosalicylate Tablets\nSodium aminosalicylate tablet contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent not more than 105.0 per cent of C7H6NNaO3,2H2O.\nUsual strengths. 500 mg; 1 g.\nDigest a quantity of powdered tablets containing about 3.0 g \nof sodium aminosalicylate, with 40 ml of water,'and filter. Add \nto the filtrate. 15.ml of IM acetic acid, and allow it to stand \nuntil precipitation has occurred. Collect the precipitate on a \nfilter, wash well with water and dry at 105°C for 30 min. The \nresidue complies with the following tests.\nA. Place about 1 g of the residue in a small, round-bottom \nflask, and add 10 ml of acetic anhydride. Heat the flask oh a \nsteam bath for 30minutes, and add 40 ml of water, mix, filter, \ncool, and allow to stand until diácetyl derivative crystallizes, \nwash well with water, and dry at 105° for 1 hour The diacetyl \nderivative so obtained melts at 191° to 197°. \n. v\nB. Shake 0.1 g of the residue with 10 ml of water and filter. To \n5 ml of the filtrate add 1 drop of ferric chloride-solution, A. \nviolet colour is produced. \n;\nC. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in.the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution;,\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml water, ., \n■ \n.\nSpeed and time. 100 ipm for 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. The filtrate diluted with the mobile phase to \nproduce a 0.055 per cent w/v solution.\nReference solution. A 0.055 per cent w/v solution of sodium \naminosalicylate 1PRS in the mobile phase. . : \n■\nChromatographic system \n''\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Aminosalicylate Tablets\nSodium aminosalicylate tablet contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent not more than 105.0 per cent of C7H6NNaO3,2H2O.\nUsual strengths. 500 mg; 1 g.\nDigest a quantity of powdered tablets containing about 3.0 g \nof sodium aminosalicylate, with 40 ml of water,'and filter. Add \nto the filtrate. 15.ml of IM acetic acid, and allow it to stand \nuntil precipitation has occurred. Collect the precipitate on a \nfilter, wash well with water and dry at 105°C for 30 min. The \nresidue complies with the following tests.\nA. Place about 1 g of the residue in a small, round-bottom \nflask, and add 10 ml of acetic anhydride. Heat the flask oh a \nsteam bath for 30minutes, and add 40 ml of water, mix, filter, \ncool, and allow to stand until diácetyl derivative crystallizes, \nwash well with water, and dry at 105° for 1 hour The diacetyl \nderivative so obtained melts at 191° to 197°. \n. v\nB. Shake 0.1 g of the residue with 10 ml of water and filter. To \n5 ml of the filtrate add 1 drop of ferric chloride-solution, A. \nviolet colour is produced. \n;\nC. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in.the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution;,\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml water, ., \n■ \n.\nSpeed and time. 100 ipm for 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. The filtrate diluted with the mobile phase to \nproduce a 0.055 per cent w/v solution.\nReference solution. A 0.055 per cent w/v solution of sodium \naminosalicylate 1PRS in the mobile phase. . : \n■\nChromatographic system \n''\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),"},{"name":"SODIUM AMINOSALICYLATE TABLETS","text":"- mobile phase: dissolve. 6.0 g of disodium hydrogen\northophosphate and 6,6 ,g of sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate dihydrate in 1600 ml with water, add\n19 ml of tetran-butyl'ammonium hydroxide (20 per \ncent aqueous solution) and make the volume to 1700 ml \nwith water, add 300 ml of methanol, mix well and degas,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer-set at 254 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The tailing factor is not more \nthan 2.0 and the relative standard deviation for replicate \ninjections is not more than 2.0 per cent\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CTCNNaCUdH-O in the medium;\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent, of the stated amount of \nC7H6NNaO3.2H2O. : ;\n3-anrinophenol. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof the powder containing about 50 mg of Sodium \nAminosalicylate and dissolve in 100 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a)..A 0.024 per cent w/v solution of \nm-aminophenol 1PRS in the mobile phase. Dilute 5 ml to 100 \nml with the mobile phase. Dilute 5 ml of the resulting solution \nto 50 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (bf A 0.024 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium aminosalicylate IPRS in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (c). Mix 5 ml each of reference solution (a) \nand reference solution (b) and dilute to 100 ml with the mobile \nphase. \n. c .\nChromatographic system \n.,...■ i..- •\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous base deactivated \nsilica (5 pm) (Such as Hypersil BDS),\n— mobile phase: dissolve 6.0 g of disodium hydrogen\northophosphate and 6.6 g of sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate dihydrate in 1600 ml with water, add\n19 ml of tetra n-butyl ammonium hydroxide (20 per \ncent aqueous solution) and make the volume to 1700 ml \nwith wafer, add 300. ml of methanol, mix well and degas,\n- \nflow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- \nspectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\n- \n• injection volume: 20 ul..:\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 5.0 per cent, -a: \nw\nInject reference solution (c). The resolution between \n3-aminophenol and sodium aminosalicylate is not less \nthan5.0."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-aminosalicylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Aminosalicylate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Aminosalicylate\nSodiumPAS ,\nC7H6NNaO3,2H2O. \nMol. Wt. 211.2\nSodium Aminosalicylate is sodium 4-amino-2-hydrpxy- \nbenzoate dihydrate. : \n: : - 1 \n'\nSodium Aminosalicylate contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of Ç7H6NNaO3, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis. ' \n:\nCategory.Amitubcrcular. \n, .. ...\nDescription. A white to cream coloured crystalline powder.\nb f h"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Aminosalicylate\nSodiumPAS ,\nC7H6NNaO3,2H2O. \nMol. Wt. 211.2\nSodium Aminosalicylate is sodium 4-amino-2-hydrpxy- \nbenzoate dihydrate. : \n: : - 1 \n'\nSodium Aminosalicylate contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of Ç7H6NNaO3, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis. ' \n:\nCategory.Amitubcrcular. \n, .. ...\nDescription. A white to cream coloured crystalline powder.\nb f h"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Compare the spectrum with that obtained wife \naminosalicylate 1PRS or with the reference spcctrh^f \nsodium aminosalicylate. \n\\\nB. A 5 per cent w/v solution complies with the testsjfor \nsalts (2.3.1),,' ' \n1"},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 6.5 to 8.5, determined in a 2,0.per.cent w^solyt^\n3-aminophenol. Determine by liquid duomatography (2A[4y\nTest solution. Dissolve 50.0 mg of the substanceuyidcr \nexamination in TOO ml/of the mobile phase./....\nReference solution (a): Dissolve 25.0 mg of S^dhiihpfhèiiid \nIPRS in 100.0 ml of the mobile phase (Solution À):DÌÌùtè!5;Òiìjl\nof fee solution tò 100.0 ml with the mobile phase, Dilutes .Òìnj \nof the resulting solution to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 25.Q mg of sodiuir,i :\naminosalicylate.iPRSvn. 10Ò.0 ml ófthé mobile phase. Dilute ? \n5.0 mlof thè solution and 5 ml of solution Ato 100.0 fnl with fee i \nmobile phase. \n1\nChromatographic system \n' ' r\" ■' \n'\"k\"\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with .\noctadccylsilane bonded to porous base deactivated \nsilica (5 pm) (Such as Hypersil BDS),1 \n: \\ \\\n- mobile phase: dissolve 6.0 g of disodium Hydrogen\n’ orthophosphate and 6,6 g of sodium dihydrogen\northophosphate dihydrate in 1600 ml with water, add\n19 ml of tetra n-butyl ammonium hydroxide (20 \ncent solution) and make thè volume to 1700 ml with//\nwater and add 300 ml of methanol, \n; |\n- \n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\n- injectionVólume:20pl.:/ 1 - \nkg\nInject reference solution (a). The relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per cent\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the. \nresolution between w-aminophenol and sodium amiiO\nsalicylate1 is1 at least 5.0.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area ofth<C \npeak corresponding to, 3-aminophenol is not more than^ \narea ofthe corresponding peak in the chromatogram obtain^yg \nwith reference solution (a) ( 0.25 percent). \nk®\nHydrogen Sulphide arid Sulphur dioxide. Dissolve 0.5-A\n5 ml of LMsodium hydroxideadd 6 mlof 3 M hydrochlo^k \nacid and stir vigorously. No odour of hydrogen sulphidi||| \nsulphur dioxide is perceptible, and not more than a faint od® \nof Amyl Alcohol is perceptible. A piece of moistened\nIP5022 ....................     „...\n. . .nnpr held over the mixture does not become \nacetate pap^\n¿¡scoioured.\nhie (2.340)--0 g with 10ml pi bromine solution and \n^r-orate t0 dryness on a water-bath, ignite gently, dissolve\ncooled residue, ignoring any carbon, in 50 ml of -water and \n¡4 ml of brominated hydrochloric acid AsT and remove the \nexcess bromine with a few drops of stannous chloride solution \n2sT The resulting solution complies with the limit test for"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-aminosalieylate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Aminosalieylate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Aminosalieylate Tablets\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nSodium PAS Tablets\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSodium aminosalicylate tablet contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent not more than 105.0 per cent of C 7H6NNa03,2H20.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity \nof the powder containing about 0.5 g of sodium \naminosalicylate, add sufficient mobile phase to produce\nUsual strengths. 500 mg; 1 g.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 5.0 per cent.\n100.0 ml and filter. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to 50.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Aminosalieylate Tablets\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nSodium PAS Tablets\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSodium aminosalicylate tablet contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent not more than 105.0 per cent of C 7H6NNa03,2H20.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity \nof the powder containing about 0.5 g of sodium \naminosalicylate, add sufficient mobile phase to produce\nUsual strengths. 500 mg; 1 g.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 5.0 per cent.\n100.0 ml and filter. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to 50.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Digest a quantity of powdered tablets containing about 3.0 g \nof sodium aminosalicylate, with 40 ml of water, and filter. Add \nto the filtrate 15 ml of 1M acetic acid, and allow it to stand \nuntil precipitation has occurred. Collect the precipitate on a \nfilter, wash well with water and dry at 105°C for 30 min. The \nresidue complies with the following tests.\nInject reference solution (c). The resolution between \n3-aminophenol and sodium aminosalicylate is not less \nthan 5.0.\nReference solution. A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of sodium \naminosalicylate RS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of the \npeak corresponding to 3-aminophenol is not more than the \narea of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) ( 1.0 per cent).\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \n-\nmobile phase: dissolve 6.0 g of disodium hydrogen \northophosphate and 6.6 g of sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate dihydrate in 1600 ml with water, add\nA. Place about 1 g of the residue in a small, round-bottom \nflask, and add 10 ml of acetic anhydride. Heat the flask on a \nsteam bath for 30 minutes, and add 40 ml of water, mix, filter, \ncool, and allow to stand until diacetyl derivative crystallizes. \nwash well with water, and dry at 105° for 1 hour. The diacetyl \nderivative so obtained melts at 191° to 197\".\n19 ml of tetra n-butyl ammonium hydroxide (20 per \ncent aqueous solution) and make the volume to\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2, \nMedium. 900 ml water, \nSpeed and time.100 rpm for 45 minutes.\n1700 ml with water, add 300 ml ofmethanol, mix well and \ndegas, \nflow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \n- injection volume: 20\nB. Shake 0.1 g of the residue with 10 ml of water and filter. To \n5 ml of the filtrate add 1 drop of ferric chloride solution. A \nviolet colour is produced.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nC. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative \nstandard deviation for replicate injections is not more than \n2.0 per cent\nTest solution. The filtrate diluted with the mobile phase to \nproduce a 0.055 per cent w/v solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Reference solution. A 0.055 per cent w/v solution of sodium \naminosalicylate RS in the mobile phase.\n3-aminophenol. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nTest. ..go/la/op.-Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity \nof the poWter containing about 50 mg of Sodium \nAlninosalieylate and dissolve in 100 ml of the mobile phaged\nChromatographic system\nCalcUlatellie content of C7H,NNa03. 2f1,0 in the tablets. \n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 inm. packed with \t\n. \t\n. \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pun), \t\nStoiage. StOre protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-antimony-gluconate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Antimony Gluconate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Antimony Gluconate\nSodium Stibogluconate is mainly the disodium salt of \np-oxy-bis[gluconato(3 )-O2, O2 //-hydraxoanti mony].\nThe method of manufacture is such as to ensurc consistently \ncontrolled reaction stoichiorrietry in order to yield sodium \nstibogluconate that is satisfactory with regard to intrinsic \ntoxicity.\nSodium Stibogluconate contains ridt'less than 30.0 per cent \nand not more than 34.0 per cent of pentavalent antimony, \ncalculated on the dried and methanol-free basis.\nCategory. Antiprotozoal.\nDescription. A colourless, mostly amorphous powder.\nA; Ari aqueous solution is dextro-rotatory.\nB, Pass hydrogen sulphide into a ,5 per centw/v solution for \nseveral minutes; an orange precipitate is produced.\nC. When heated, it chars without melting, emitting an odour \nof burnt sugar and leaving a residue which gives the reactions \nof antimony compounds and the reactions of sodium salts \n(2.3.1). ■ \n/ \n' ■ \n■ \n■ \n■■■' \n■ •’\npH (2.4:24). 5.0 to 5.6, determined in the solution obtained in \nthe test for Stability of solution.\n3627\nwww.webofpharma.com"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Antimony Gluconate\nSodium Stibogluconate is mainly the disodium salt of \np-oxy-bis[gluconato(3 )-O2, O2 //-hydraxoanti mony].\nThe method of manufacture is such as to ensurc consistently \ncontrolled reaction stoichiorrietry in order to yield sodium \nstibogluconate that is satisfactory with regard to intrinsic \ntoxicity.\nSodium Stibogluconate contains ridt'less than 30.0 per cent \nand not more than 34.0 per cent of pentavalent antimony, \ncalculated on the dried and methanol-free basis.\nCategory. Antiprotozoal.\nDescription. A colourless, mostly amorphous powder.\nA; Ari aqueous solution is dextro-rotatory.\nB, Pass hydrogen sulphide into a ,5 per centw/v solution for \nseveral minutes; an orange precipitate is produced.\nC. When heated, it chars without melting, emitting an odour \nof burnt sugar and leaving a residue which gives the reactions \nof antimony compounds and the reactions of sodium salts \n(2.3.1). ■ \n/ \n' ■ \n■ \n■ \n■■■' \n■ •’\npH (2.4:24). 5.0 to 5.6, determined in the solution obtained in \nthe test for Stability of solution.\n3627\nwww.webofpharma.com"},{"name":"SODIUM STIBOGLUCONATE","text":"Stability of solution. Heat a solution containing 10.0 per cent \nw/v of pentavalent antimony in an autoclave at 115.5° and at a \npressure of 70 kPa:for 30 minutes. The resulting solution is \ncolourless or almost colourless. \ni ;\nTrivalent antimony. Dissolve 2.0 g in 3 0 ml of water, add 15 ml \nof hydrochloric acid and titrate with 0.00833 M_ potassium \nbromate using methyl orangé solution as indicator. Not more \nthan 1.3 ml of 0.00833 Mpotassium bromate is required.\nChlorides. Dissolve 2.5 g in 50 ml of water and add 2 ml of \n2 M nitric acidwA 75 ml oj acetate buffer pH 5f). Titratewith \n0.1 M silver nitrate, determining the end-point potentio­\nmetrically (2.4.25). Not more than 3.0 ml of 0.1 Msilver nitrate \nis required.\nMethanol. Not more than 2.0 per cent w/w.\nDetermine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution (a). Dissolve 0.5 g of the substance under \nexamination in water and dilute to 10.0 ml with water.\nTest solution-(b). , Add 5 -ml of a 0.2 per cent v/v solution of \nethanol (internal standard) to 0.5 g of the substance under \nexamination and dilute to 10.0 ml with water.\nReference solution. Add 1 ml of a 1.0 per cent v/v solution of\nmethanol to 5 ml of a 0.2 per cent v/v solution of the internal \nstandard and dilute to 10,0 ml with water.\nChromatographic system\n- a glass column 1.5 m x-4 mm, packed with porous polymer\nbeads(80to 100mesh)(SuchasPorapakQ),\n- temperature: \n/ w. .\ncolumn: 130°,\n, , inlet port 180° and detector at 180°, \nr. . ,\n- flame ionization detector,. \n/ )\n- flow rate: 40 ml per minute using nitrogen as carrier gas. \n- injection volume: 2 pl.\nInject the reference solution, test solution (a) and (b).\nCalculate the content of methanol taking 0.792 g as its weight \nper ml (2.4.29) at 20°. \nAi.-.•••.?\nUndue toxicity. Dissolve a suitable quantity of the substance \nunder examination in water for injections to give a solution \ncontaining 28 mg of pentavalent antimony per ml. Inject \nintravenously 0.3 ml of the solution into each of 10 mice that \nhave been deprived of food for not less than 17 hours. After \ninjections allow the mice access to food and water. None of \nthe mice dies within 24 hours. If one of the mice dies within \n24 hours, repeat the test. None of the second group of mice \ndies within 24 hours.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 15.0 per cent, \ndetermined on 0.25 g by drying in an oven at 130° at a pressure \nnot exceeding 0.7 kPa. <... : \n-, - \n,3;\n|\nAssay.-Dissolve 0.16 g in 30 ml of hydrochloric acid I\n70 ml of phosphoric acid and stir carefully until compkUk I\nmixed. Titrate with 0:05 M ferrous ammonium sulpha f \nprepared using sulphuric acid (.1 per cent), determining g* ■. I \nend-po int potentiometrically (2.4.25), using a p latin^ I \nelectrode and a silver-silver chloride reference electrode i\n1 ml of 0.05 Mferrous ammonium sulphate is equivalent to I \n0.003044 g of pentavalent antimony. \n' J I\nStorage. Store protected from moisture. . \n, . \nI"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-ascorbate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Ascorbate"},"editi