e principal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the \nárda of any peak corresponding to '4-hydroxybenzoic acid \nmultiplied by 1.4 is not more than 8 times the area the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference; solution \n(c) (4.0 per cent), the area of any other secondary peak is not \nmore than the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) (0.5 per cent) and the sum \nof areas of all the secondary peaks other than 4- \nhydroxybenzoic acid is not more than twice the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c) (1.0 per cent). Ignore the peak with an area less \nthan 0.2'times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) (0.1 per cent).\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve TO gin20 ml of water, add 0.4 ml \nof nitric acid and filter.: 15 ml of the filtrate complies with the \nlimittestforchlorides(330ppm). \n; .\n3624 \niwBilllllMl\nSODIUM PROPYLPARABEN\nIP2022\nSODIUM STARCH GLYCOLLATE (TYPE A)\nTestsolution.; Dissolve 5.0 mg of . the, substance under Sulphates (2.3.17). Dissolve 0.5 g in40 ml of-water, add3$ 1 \nexamination in 2.5 ml of methanol and dilute to 5 0.0 ml -with \nof 2 Mhydrochloric acid, dilute to 60 ml with water and film 1\nthemobile phase.Diluie 10.0 mlof the solution to 100.p,ml \nIfrml of thé fíltrate complies with the limit tcstfor sulp^t.\nwith the mobile phase. . \n-.:;= \n(0.12 percent). \n. \n; ml\nreddish violet colour; not more than 2.0 ml of C. To an acidified solution, add iodinatedpotassium iodide \nId sodium hydroxide is required: \n■ solution', a blue or violet colour produced.\nsolution', a blue or violet colour produced.\n; >72 340). Mix 5-0 g with 10 ml of bromine solution and\nD. Solution A (see Test) gives reaction (à) of sodium salts \n(23.1). ;;\n* - orate to dryness Qn a watcr'^ath- ignite gently, cool, \nS^iye^the residue, ignoring any carbon, in 50 ml of water\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 5.0 per cent, determined41\nand 14 brominated hydrochloric acid AsT and remove \nthe excess of bromine with 2 ml of stannous chloride solution\ni.og. „ \n' \n..................................' \"■ ■'M\nTests'\nAssay. Determine by;liquid chromatography.(24.14^ 1 \ndescribed under Related substances. \n\"■ J\nSolution A, Dissolve 2.5,g of substance under, examination in \n2 ml of a 5 0 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric acid. Heat on a \nwater-bath, then cautiously over a naked flame, raising the \ntemperature progressively, then incinerate in a muffle furnace \nat 600 ± 25°. Continue heating until all black particles have \ndisappeared. Allow to cool, add a few drops of dilute sulphuric \nacid', heat and incinerate as above; Allow to cool, add à few \ndrops of ammonium carbonate solut"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-salicylate-of-the-indian-pharmacopoeia","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Salicylate: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Salicylate: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nSodium Salicylate Solution: A 10.0 per cent w/y solution of \nsodium salicylate.\nSodium silicate: Na2SiO3,xH2O\nChemically pure grade of commerce.\nWhite powder.\nSodium Sulphate, Anhydrous: Na2SO4= 142.04\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite crystalline powder or granules; hygroscopic\nComplies with the following test. . \n.\nloss on drying — Not more than 0.5 per cent when dried at \n.130° \n. y ,• \n\",\nSodium Sulphide: Na2S,9H2O=240.18\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce. ..........\nColourless crytals or cyrstallirie masses which turns yellow \non storage; deliquescent. 1 \n- - ;■\nSodium Sulphide Solution: A 10.0 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium sulphide. \n■■■■'■.■\nSodium Sulphite: Na2SO37H2O =252.15\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce containing not\nless than 95.0 per cent of Na2SO3,7H2O\nWhite colourless crystals.\nSodium Sulphite, Anhydrous: Na2SO3 -126.05\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce containing not less \nthan 95 per cent ofNa2SO3.\nSmall crystals or powder."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium Salicylate: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nSodium Salicylate Solution: A 10.0 per cent w/y solution of \nsodium salicylate.\nSodium silicate: Na2SiO3,xH2O\nChemically pure grade of commerce.\nWhite powder.\nSodium Sulphate, Anhydrous: Na2SO4= 142.04\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite crystalline powder or granules; hygroscopic\nComplies with the following test. . \n.\nloss on drying — Not more than 0.5 per cent when dried at \n.130° \n. y ,• \n\",\nSodium Sulphide: Na2S,9H2O=240.18\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce. ..........\nColourless crytals or cyrstallirie masses which turns yellow \non storage; deliquescent. 1 \n- - ;■\nSodium Sulphide Solution: A 10.0 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium sulphide. \n■■■■'■.■\nSodium Sulphite: Na2SO37H2O =252.15\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce containing not\nless than 95.0 per cent of Na2SO3,7H2O\nWhite colourless crystals.\nSodium Sulphite, Anhydrous: Na2SO3 -126.05\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce containing not less \nthan 95 per cent ofNa2SO3.\nSmall crystals or powder."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-salicylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Salicylate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Salicylate\nAppearance of solution. Centrifuge the suspension obtained \nin identification test B at 2500 rpm for 4P minutes. The \nsupernatant liquid is clear (2.4.1) and colourless (2.4.1).\nSulphates (2.3.17). 2.5 ml of solution A diluted to 15 ml with \ndistilled water complies with the limit test for sulphates\nCOONa\n&\n(600ppm).\nOH\npH (2.4.24). 5.5 to 7.5, determined in a 3.3 per cent w/v solution \nin carbon-dioxide-free water. \n.....\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non l.O gby drying in an oven at 105°.\nMol. Wt. 160.1 I\nHeavy metals (2.3.13).To 4.0 g in a silica or platinum dish, add \n2 ml of a 50 per cent w/v solution ofsulphuric acid; heat in a \nwater-bath and then cautiously over a flame at about600°. \nContinue heating until all black particles have/disappeared, \nallow to cool, add 0.1 ml of 1M sulphuric acid, heatto ignition \nonce again and allow to cool. Add Q4 ml of 2. Ifr ammonium \ncarbonate, evaporate to dryness and cautiously ignite, To \nthe residue, add 5 ml of hydrochloric acid, evaporate to \ndryness on a water-bath and dissolve the residue in 100 ml òf \nwater. 25 mlofasolution còmpliés with the lifnit test fór heavy \nmetals, Method A (20 ppm). ?\nAssay Weigh 0.15 g, dissolve in 30 ml of anhydrous glacial \nacetic acid. Titrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid,.determining \nthe end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank\nC7H5NaO3\nSodium Salicylate is sodium 2-hydroxybenzoate. -\n.titration., , .... ...\nS odium Salicylate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C7H5NaO3, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\n1 tidofOiPMperchloric acid is equivalent to.0.01601 g of \nG7H5NaO3.jp 77 i 7\nCategory. Anti-inflammatory; analgesic.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moi sture.\nDescription. Colourless, small crystals or shiny flakes or a \nwhite, crystalline powder."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium Salicylate\nAppearance of solution. Centrifuge the suspension obtained \nin identification test B at 2500 rpm for 4P minutes. The \nsupernatant liquid is clear (2.4.1) and colourless (2.4.1).\nSulphates (2.3.17). 2.5 ml of solution A diluted to 15 ml with \ndistilled water complies with the limit test for sulphates\nCOONa\n&\n(600ppm).\nOH\npH (2.4.24). 5.5 to 7.5, determined in a 3.3 per cent w/v solution \nin carbon-dioxide-free water. \n.....\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non l.O gby drying in an oven at 105°.\nMol. Wt. 160.1 I\nHeavy metals (2.3.13).To 4.0 g in a silica or platinum dish, add \n2 ml of a 50 per cent w/v solution ofsulphuric acid; heat in a \nwater-bath and then cautiously over a flame at about600°. \nContinue heating until all black particles have/disappeared, \nallow to cool, add 0.1 ml of 1M sulphuric acid, heatto ignition \nonce again and allow to cool. Add Q4 ml of 2. Ifr ammonium \ncarbonate, evaporate to dryness and cautiously ignite, To \nthe residue, add 5 ml of hydrochloric acid, evaporate to \ndryness on a water-bath and dissolve the residue in 100 ml òf \nwater. 25 mlofasolution còmpliés with the lifnit test fór heavy \nmetals, Method A (20 ppm). ?\nAssay Weigh 0.15 g, dissolve in 30 ml of anhydrous glacial \nacetic acid. Titrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid,.determining \nthe end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank\nC7H5NaO3\nSodium Salicylate is sodium 2-hydroxybenzoate. -\n.titration., , .... ...\nS odium Salicylate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C7H5NaO3, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\n1 tidofOiPMperchloric acid is equivalent to.0.01601 g of \nG7H5NaO3.jp 77 i 7\nCategory. Anti-inflammatory; analgesic.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moi sture.\nDescription. Colourless, small crystals or shiny flakes or a \nwhite, crystalline powder."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-salts","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Salts"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Salts\nA. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under examination in 2 rnl\nof water or use 2 rnl of the prescribed solution. Add 2 rnlof a\n15 per cent w/v solution ofpotassium carbonate and heat to\nboiling; no precipitate is produced. Add 4 rnl of a freshly\nprepared potassium antimonate solution and heat to boiling.\nAllow to cool in ice and if necessary scratch the inside of the\ntest-tube with a glass rod; a dense, white precipitate is formed.\nB. Acidify a solution ofthe substance under examinationwith\n1M acetic acid and add a large excess of magnesium uranyl\nacetate solution; a yellow, crystalline precipitate is formed.\nSulphates\nA. Dissolve about 50 mg of the substance,under examination\nin 5 rnl ofwateroruse 5 rnl ofthe prescribed solution. Add 1rnl\nof dilute hydrochloric acid and 1 rnl of barium chloride\nsolution; a white precipitate is formed.\nB. Add 0.1 rnl of iodine solution to the suspension obtained\nin test A; the suspension remains yellow (distinction from\nsulphites and dithionites) but is decolorised by adding,\ndropwise, stannous chloride solution (distinction from\niodates). Boil the mixture; no coloured precipitate is formed\n(distinction from selenates and tungstates).\nC. Dissolve about 50 mg of the substance under examination\nin 5 rnl ofwateroruse 5 rnl ofthe prescribed solution. Add 2 rnl\nof lead acetate ,solution; a white precipitate, soluble in\nammonium acetate solution and in sodium hydroxide\nsolution, is produced."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium Salts\nA. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under examination in 2 rnl\nof water or use 2 rnl of the prescribed solution. Add 2 rnlof a\n15 per cent w/v solution ofpotassium carbonate and heat to\nboiling; no precipitate is produced. Add 4 rnl of a freshly\nprepared potassium antimonate solution and heat to boiling.\nAllow to cool in ice and if necessary scratch the inside of the\ntest-tube with a glass rod; a dense, white precipitate is formed.\nB. Acidify a solution ofthe substance under examinationwith\n1M acetic acid and add a large excess of magnesium uranyl\nacetate solution; a yellow, crystalline precipitate is formed.\nSulphates\nA. Dissolve about 50 mg of the substance,under examination\nin 5 rnl ofwateroruse 5 rnl ofthe prescribed solution. Add 1rnl\nof dilute hydrochloric acid and 1 rnl of barium chloride\nsolution; a white precipitate is formed.\nB. Add 0.1 rnl of iodine solution to the suspension obtained\nin test A; the suspension remains yellow (distinction from\nsulphites and dithionites) but is decolorised by adding,\ndropwise, stannous chloride solution (distinction from\niodates). Boil the mixture; no coloured precipitate is formed\n(distinction from selenates and tungstates).\nC. Dissolve about 50 mg of the substance under examination\nin 5 rnl ofwateroruse 5 rnl ofthe prescribed solution. Add 2 rnl\nof lead acetate ,solution; a white precipitate, soluble in\nammonium acetate solution and in sodium hydroxide\nsolution, is produced."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-starch-glycolate-type-b","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Starch Glycolate (Type B)"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Starch Glycolate (Type B)\nSodium Starch Glycolate (Type B) is the sodium salt of a cross \nlinked partly Ó-carboxymethyíated starch. . :}.\nSodium StàrcfrGlycolate (Type B) conlains not less than 2.0 \nper cent and not more, than:3.4 per cent of sodium, Na,\nIP 20$\nWith ethanol (8Qperçe^\ncalculated on the material washed: \nand dried as described under Assa\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (tablet d isintegrant). ■, < F;.; z\nDescription; Á white oralmostwhite, fine,' free-flowing powder \nvery hygroscopic."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Starch Glycolate (Type B)\nSodium Starch Glycolate (Type B) is the sodium salt of a cross \nlinked partly Ó-carboxymethyíated starch. . :}.\nSodium StàrcfrGlycolate (Type B) conlains not less than 2.0 \nper cent and not more, than:3.4 per cent of sodium, Na,\nIP 20$\nWith ethanol (8Qperçe^\ncalculated on the material washed: \nand dried as described under Assa\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (tablet d isintegrant). ■, < F;.; z\nDescription; Á white oralmostwhite, fine,' free-flowing powder \nvery hygroscopic."},{"name":"Identification","text":": ■ ■ ■ \n■. -• \nx-■;u\nA. pH (see Test) \n. mqc. w\nB.Shake 4:0 g of the substance under examination-with 20 mJ \nof carbon-dioxide-freewater, without heating? This has the \nappéararíeébf agel. Addjl 00 ml of carbon-dioxidefree^-at?r \nand shake. A suspension forms that settles aftér standing.\nC. To‘ ah acidified solution; add iodinated pótássiurn iodide \nsolution-, a blue or violet colour produced. ■ ° : r;; •\nD. Solution A (see Test) gives reaction (a) of sodium salts"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Solution A. Dissolve 2.5,g of substance under examination in \n2 ml of a 50 per cent w/v solution oí sulphuric acid. Heat on a \nwater-bath, then cautiously over a naked flame, raising the \ntemperature progressively, then incinerate in a muffle furnace \naf 600 ±;25°.: Continue heating until'air black particles have \ndisappeared. Allow to cool, add a few drops of dilute sulphuric \nacid; heat and incinerate as:above. Allow to cool, add a few \ndrops of ammonium carbonate solution, evaporatefodryness \nand incinerate. Allow to cooband dissolve, the residuein,50 ml \nof water.\nAppearance of solution. Centrifuge the suspension obtained \nin identification test B at 2500 rpm for 10 minutes. The \nsupernatant liquid is clear (2.4.1) and colourless (2.4.1).\npH (2.4.24). 3.0 to 5.0, determined in a 3.3.per cqhtw/v'splution\nin carbon-dioxide-free water. ’ ' \n' \n' \"v !\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). To 4.0 g in a silica or platinum dish, add \n2 ml of a 50 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric acid; heft in a \nwater-bath and then cautiously over a flame at about 600°. \nContinue heating until all black particles have disappeared, \nallow to cool, add 0.1 ml of 1M sulphuric acid, heat to 'ignition \nonce again and. allow to cool. Add 0.1 ml of 2M.ammonu$ \ncarbonate, evaporate to dryness and cautiously ignite. To \nthe residue, add 5 ml of hydrochloric acid, evaporate to \ndryness on a water-bath and dissolve the residue inIQO.ml^\nwater. 25 ml of a solution complies with the limit test for \nmetals, Method A(20 ppm). \n?\nIron (2.3.14). 10 ml of solution A, complies with the limit test \nfor iron (20 ppm). \n' 't \n...\nSodium Chloride. Not more than 7.0 per cent, / ,\nœ-2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-starch-glycollate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Starch Glycollate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"SODIUM STARCH GLYCOLLATE \nip 2018 \nSODIUM PROPYLPARABEN\nChlorides (2.3.12). To 25 ml of solution A add 15 ml of water\nSodium Salic ∎ late\nwater-bath and then cautiously over a flame at about 600°. \nContinue heating until all black particles have disappeared, \nallow to cool, add 0.1 ml of 1 M sulphuric acid, heat to ignition \nonce again and allow to cool. Add 0.1 ml of 2 M ammonium \ncarbonate, evaporate to dryness and cautiously ignite. To \nthe residue add 5 ml of hydrochloric acid, evaporate to \ndryness on a water-bath and dissolve the residue in 100 ml of \nwater. 25 ml ofa solution complies with the limit test for heavy \nmetals, Method A (20 ppm).\nand 10 ml of  2 M nitric acid and filter. 25 ml of the filtrate\ncomplies with the limit test for chlorides (200 ppm).\nCOONa\nSulphates (2.3.17). 2.5 ml of solution A diluted to 15 ml with \ndistilled water  complies with the limit test for sulphates\nOH\n(600 PPn1)• \nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined\nC7H5Na03 \t\nMol. Wt. 160.1 \nSodium Salicylate is sodium 2-hydroxybenzoate.\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Weigh 0.15 g, dissolve in 30 ml of anhydrous glacial\nIron (2.3.14). 50 ml of the solution obtained in the test for \nHeavy metals complies with the limit test for iron (20 ppm).\nSodium Salicylate contains not less than 99.0 per cent  and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C 7H5Na03, calculated on  the dried \nbasis.\nacetic. acid.  Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, determining\nthe end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank\nSodium Chloride.  Not more than 10.0 per cent, determined by \nthe following method.\ntitration.\nCategory. Anti-inflammatory; analgesic.\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.01601 g of\nWeigh 0.5g, suspend in a mixture of 100 ml of water and 1 ml of \nnitric acid and mix. Titrate with 0.1 M silver nitrate, \ndetermining the end point potentiometrically (2.4.25), using a \nsilver indicator electrode. Carry out a blank titration.\nDose. 500 mg to 2 g, with food; in the treatment  of acute \nrheumatism, 5 to 10 g daily, in divided doses.\nC-1-1,Na03.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nDescription. Colourless, small crystals or shiny flakes  or a 00 \nwhite, crystalline powder.\n1 ml of 0.1 M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of NaCI."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"SODIUM STARCH GLYCOLLATE \nip 2018 \nSODIUM PROPYLPARABEN\nChlorides (2.3.12). To 25 ml of solution A add 15 ml of water\nSodium Salic ∎ late\nwater-bath and then cautiously over a flame at about 600°. \nContinue heating until all black particles have disappeared, \nallow to cool, add 0.1 ml of 1 M sulphuric acid, heat to ignition \nonce again and allow to cool. Add 0.1 ml of 2 M ammonium \ncarbonate, evaporate to dryness and cautiously ignite. To \nthe residue add 5 ml of hydrochloric acid, evaporate to \ndryness on a water-bath and dissolve the residue in 100 ml of \nwater. 25 ml ofa solution complies with the limit test for heavy \nmetals, Method A (20 ppm).\nand 10 ml of  2 M nitric acid and filter. 25 ml of the filtrate\ncomplies with the limit test for chlorides (200 ppm).\nCOONa\nSulphates (2.3.17). 2.5 ml of solution A diluted to 15 ml with \ndistilled water  complies with the limit test for sulphates\nOH\n(600 PPn1)• \nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined\nC7H5Na03 \t\nMol. Wt. 160.1 \nSodium Salicylate is sodium 2-hydroxybenzoate.\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Weigh 0.15 g, dissolve in 30 ml of anhydrous glacial\nIron (2.3.14). 50 ml of the solution obtained in the test for \nHeavy metals complies with the limit test for iron (20 ppm).\nSodium Salicylate contains not less than 99.0 per cent  and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C 7H5Na03, calculated on  the dried \nbasis.\nacetic. acid.  Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, determining\nthe end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank\nSodium Chloride.  Not more than 10.0 per cent, determined by \nthe following method.\ntitration.\nCategory. Anti-inflammatory; analgesic.\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.01601 g of\nWeigh 0.5g, suspend in a mixture of 100 ml of water and 1 ml of \nnitric acid and mix. Titrate with 0.1 M silver nitrate, \ndetermining the end point potentiometrically (2.4.25), using a \nsilver indicator electrode. Carry out a blank titration.\nDose. 500 mg to 2 g, with food; in the treatment  of acute \nrheumatism, 5 to 10 g daily, in divided doses.\nC-1-1,Na03.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nDescription. Colourless, small crystals or shiny flakes  or a 00 \nwhite, crystalline powder.\n1 ml of 0.1 M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of NaCI."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Sodium Starch Glycollate\nSodium glycollate. To 0.2 g add 5 ml of glacial acetic acid, \nmix well and add 5 ml of water, stirring occasionally until \nsolution is complete. Slowly add 50 ml ofacetone with stirring \nand then add 1 g ofsodium chloride.  Filter, wash the residue \nwith acetone and dilute the filtrate to 100 ml with  acetone. \nTransfer 2 ml of this solution to an open flask, heat on a water-\nbath for exactly 20 minutes, cool, add 5 ml of naphthalenediol \nreagent  and mix thoroughly. Add a further 15 ml of the same \nreagent, mix, cover the flask with aluminium foil and heat on a \nwater-bath for 20 minutes. Cool and dilute to 25 ml with \nsulphuric acid. The absorbance (2.4.7) of the resulting solution \nat the maximum at about 540 nm using water as the blank, is \nnot more than that of a solution prepared in the following \nmanner. To 5 ml ofa 0.062 per cent w/v solution  ofg/ycollic \nacid, previously dried at a pressure not exceeding 2 kPa for\nTest A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Tes \nmay be omitted if tests A and C are carried out."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-stibogluconate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Stibogluconate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Stibogluconate Injection\nSodium Antimony Gluconate Injection v \n|\nSodium Stibogluconate Injection is a sterile solution of Sodium | \nStibogluconate in Water for Injections. Inj ection in multiple | \ndose containers must not contain phenol as preservative, I\nSodium Stibogluconate, injection contains not’less than i \n9.5 per cent w/y and not more than 10.5 per cent w/v of | \npentavalent antimony, Sb. \n-, \n■ |\n■ ■ ■ \n■■ ■ \n■ ■ ■■■■• ■ >> ViV, W:. : g\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 100 mg of pentavalent |\nantimony per ml. ... - \n.. . ■ \n..... -, I\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured | \nsolution. \n.. \ng\n.. - . .... . . . . >... ...... - :■ | \n... . ■■■ ■■ , ■ ... .■■. . .■ . . ;■■■..■ .. |\nIdentification ■ \n-ww-- ■. \nI\nA. It is ¿fec/ro-rotatory. \n............ . \n|\nB. Dilute 2 rill to 10 ml with water arid pass hydrogen sulphide | \nthrough thè solution:' no immediate precipitate is;produced. |\nContinue to pass hydrogen sulphide through the solution for | \nseveral minutes; an orange precipitate is produced. ; \n|\nC. The residue obtained'by evaporation to7’ dryness, after | \nincineration, gives the reach ons of anti mony compounds and | \nthe reactions ofsodium salts (2.3.1). \nI\n.-yvo-w, \n.. \nG o «- \n|"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Stibogluconate Injection\nSodium Antimony Gluconate Injection v \n|\nSodium Stibogluconate Injection is a sterile solution of Sodium | \nStibogluconate in Water for Injections. Inj ection in multiple | \ndose containers must not contain phenol as preservative, I\nSodium Stibogluconate, injection contains not’less than i \n9.5 per cent w/y and not more than 10.5 per cent w/v of | \npentavalent antimony, Sb. \n-, \n■ |\n■ ■ ■ \n■■ ■ \n■ ■ ■■■■• ■ >> ViV, W:. : g\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 100 mg of pentavalent |\nantimony per ml. ... - \n.. . ■ \n..... -, I\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured | \nsolution. \n.. \ng\n.. - . .... . . . . >... ...... - :■ | \n... . ■■■ ■■ , ■ ... .■■. . .■ . . ;■■■..■ .. |\nIdentification ■ \n-ww-- ■. \nI\nA. It is ¿fec/ro-rotatory. \n............ . \n|\nB. Dilute 2 rill to 10 ml with water arid pass hydrogen sulphide | \nthrough thè solution:' no immediate precipitate is;produced. |\nContinue to pass hydrogen sulphide through the solution for | \nseveral minutes; an orange precipitate is produced. ; \n|\nC. The residue obtained'by evaporation to7’ dryness, after | \nincineration, gives the reach ons of anti mony compounds and | \nthe reactions ofsodium salts (2.3.1). \nI\n.-yvo-w, \n.. \nG o «- \n|"},{"name":"Tests","text":"|\npH(2.4.24).5.0to5.6. \n. . •  ..............I\nUndue toxicity. Dilute a suitable volume of the injectionunder | \nexamination with sufficient water, for injections to'give3 | \nsolution containing thè equivalent of 28 mg of pentavalent | \nantimony per ml. Inj ect intravenously 0.3 ml of the soluti on g \ninto eachof lOmice that have been deprived of foo d forno! | \nless than 17. ¡hours. After injections allow the: mice access w | \nfood and water None of the mice dies, within24hours. If °ne ■ \nof the mice dies within 24, hours, repeat thetest. None of th6 | \nsecond group of mice dies within24 hours, w; -w |"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-stibogluconate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Stibogluconate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"SODIUM STIBOGLUCONATE\n-^0 5‘g, suspend in a mixture of 100 ml of water add 1 mi \nf i/ricaciriandmix. Titrate with 0.1. M silver nitrate, \ndetermining the end point potentiometrically (2.4.25), using a \nsilver indicator electrode. Cany out a blank titration:\nj \n^silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of\nNaCI.\nSodiumglycolate.\nfest solution. To 0.2 g add 5 ml of glacial acetic acid, mix well \nand add 5 ml of -water, stirring until dissolution is complete \n(about 10 minutes). Add 50 ml of acetone and 1 g of sodium \nchloride. Filter through a fast filter paper impregnated with \nacetone^ and filter with acetone. Combine the filtrate, washing \nand dilute to 100.0 ml with acetone.; Allow to stand for 24 \nhours without shaking. Use the clear supernatant.\nReference solution. Dissolve 0.31 g of glycolic'acidpreviously \ndried:under; vacuum over phosphorus pentoxide at room \ntemperature overnight with water and dilute to 500 ml with \nwater. Take5.0 ml of the; solution, add 5 ml of acetic acidand \nallow to stand for about 30 minutes. Add 50 ml of acetone and\nI sodium chloride, . filter through a fast filter paper \nimpregnated with acetone. Combine the filtrate arid washing \nand dilute to 100.0 ml with acetone. Allow to stand for \n24 hours without shaking. Use the clear supernatant.\nTransfer 2,0 ml of the test solution to an open flask, heat on a \nwater-bathfor 20 : minutes, cool to room temperature^ add \n20.0 ml of.2,7-dihydroxynaphthalene solution. Shake and heat \non water-bath for 20 minutes. Cool under running water, \ntransfer to a volumetric flask and diluted to. 25.0 ml with \nsulphuric acid, maintaining the flask under running water.\nWithin 16 minutes, measure the absorbance at 540 nm using \nwateras blank.Theabsorbancc of the solution prepared with \nthe test solution is not more than the solution prepared at the \nsame time and in the same manner using 2.0 ml of the reference \nsolution (2.0 per cent).\nTo 70 nd of water ya a 100 ml graduated \ncylinder,^add 1 g of the substance under examination (dried \nsubstance), stir with a glass rod to avoid dumps and dilute to"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"SODIUM STIBOGLUCONATE\n-^0 5‘g, suspend in a mixture of 100 ml of water add 1 mi \nf i/ricaciriandmix. Titrate with 0.1. M silver nitrate, \ndetermining the end point potentiometrically (2.4.25), using a \nsilver indicator electrode. Cany out a blank titration:\nj \n^silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of\nNaCI.\nSodiumglycolate.\nfest solution. To 0.2 g add 5 ml of glacial acetic acid, mix well \nand add 5 ml of -water, stirring until dissolution is complete \n(about 10 minutes). Add 50 ml of acetone and 1 g of sodium \nchloride. Filter through a fast filter paper impregnated with \nacetone^ and filter with acetone. Combine the filtrate, washing \nand dilute to 100.0 ml with acetone.; Allow to stand for 24 \nhours without shaking. Use the clear supernatant.\nReference solution. Dissolve 0.31 g of glycolic'acidpreviously \ndried:under; vacuum over phosphorus pentoxide at room \ntemperature overnight with water and dilute to 500 ml with \nwater. Take5.0 ml of the; solution, add 5 ml of acetic acidand \nallow to stand for about 30 minutes. Add 50 ml of acetone and\nI sodium chloride, . filter through a fast filter paper \nimpregnated with acetone. Combine the filtrate arid washing \nand dilute to 100.0 ml with acetone. Allow to stand for \n24 hours without shaking. Use the clear supernatant.\nTransfer 2,0 ml of the test solution to an open flask, heat on a \nwater-bathfor 20 : minutes, cool to room temperature^ add \n20.0 ml of.2,7-dihydroxynaphthalene solution. Shake and heat \non water-bath for 20 minutes. Cool under running water, \ntransfer to a volumetric flask and diluted to. 25.0 ml with \nsulphuric acid, maintaining the flask under running water.\nWithin 16 minutes, measure the absorbance at 540 nm using \nwateras blank.Theabsorbancc of the solution prepared with \nthe test solution is not more than the solution prepared at the \nsame time and in the same manner using 2.0 ml of the reference \nsolution (2.0 per cent).\nTo 70 nd of water ya a 100 ml graduated \ncylinder,^add 1 g of the substance under examination (dried \nsubstance), stir with a glass rod to avoid dumps and dilute to"},{"name":"Identification","text":"100.0 ml with water and stir again until the substance is \nhomogeneously distributed. Allow to stand for 2 hours. The \nvolume of the sediment is less than 45.0 ml.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). 1.0 g is free from Escherichia \nc°h and l().0 g is free dom Salmonellae.\n^ss oji drying (2.4.19). Not more than 10.0 per cent, \n^^ripinedon 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 130° for 1.5 hours,\n^ssay- Weighl;0 g, add 20 ml of ethanol(80per cent), shake\nTests ...\n°rlO minutes and filter. Repeat the extraction three times, or \nuntil a test for chioride ions is negative using silver nitrate \n^w^°«.Dry thercsidueal 105° to constant weight. Weigh\ng of the residue, add 80 ml of anhydrous glacial, acetic\nacid, heat under a reflux condenser for 2 hours, cool. Titrate \nwith 0.1 M perchloric acid, determining the end-point \npotentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.0023 g of \nsodium. \n. \n,; , ; \n;\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nSodium Stibogluconate"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-tartrate-sodium-tartrate-c-4h406na2-21120","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Tartrate; Sodium(+)-Tartrate: C 4H406Na2,21120"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Tartrate; Sodium(+)-Tartrate: C 4H406Na2,21120\nPrepar:70;:d.s..iately before use.\n 230.08\nStarch Substrate: Stir an amount ofsoluble starch equivalent \nto 1.0 g of the dried substance in a beaker with 5 ml of water. \nAdd, stirring contionously, to 75 ml of boiling water. Rinse \nthe beaker with two quantities, each of 5 ml of water, add the \nwashings to the hot starch solution and reboil for 2 minutes, \nstirring continously. Cool to 25°, dissolve 5 g of sodium \nchloride in the starch solution and add sufficient water to \nproduce 100 ml. Dilute 10.0 ml of this solution to 100.0 ml with \nphosphate buffer solution pH 6.0 in the analysis of bacterial\namylase and with acetate buffer pH 5.0 in the analysis of \nfungal amylase. 1 ml of starch substrate contains 1.0 mg of dry \nsoluble starch.\nIt must be freshly prepared.\nStearic Acid: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nStearic Anhydride: C 36H7003 - 550.95\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite, waxy flakes; mp, about 70°.\nStrontium Carbonate: SrCO3 = 147.6\nWhite or almost white, crystalline powder.\nStrontium Chloride: SrC12,6H20 = 266.62\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless, odourless crystals or white granules; efflorescent \nin air; deliquescent in moist air."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium Tartrate; Sodium(+)-Tartrate: C 4H406Na2,21120\nPrepar:70;:d.s..iately before use.\n 230.08\nStarch Substrate: Stir an amount ofsoluble starch equivalent \nto 1.0 g of the dried substance in a beaker with 5 ml of water. \nAdd, stirring contionously, to 75 ml of boiling water. Rinse \nthe beaker with two quantities, each of 5 ml of water, add the \nwashings to the hot starch solution and reboil for 2 minutes, \nstirring continously. Cool to 25°, dissolve 5 g of sodium \nchloride in the starch solution and add sufficient water to \nproduce 100 ml. Dilute 10.0 ml of this solution to 100.0 ml with \nphosphate buffer solution pH 6.0 in the analysis of bacterial\namylase and with acetate buffer pH 5.0 in the analysis of \nfungal amylase. 1 ml of starch substrate contains 1.0 mg of dry \nsoluble starch.\nIt must be freshly prepared.\nStearic Acid: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nStearic Anhydride: C 36H7003 - 550.95\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite, waxy flakes; mp, about 70°.\nStrontium Carbonate: SrCO3 = 147.6\nWhite or almost white, crystalline powder.\nStrontium Chloride: SrC12,6H20 = 266.62\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless, odourless crystals or white granules; efflorescent \nin air; deliquescent in moist air."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-tartrate-sodium-tartrate-c4h406na2-2h20","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Tartrate; Sodium(+)-Tartrate: C4H406Na2,2H20 ="},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Tartrate; Sodium(+)-Tartrate: C4H406Na2,2H20 =\n230.08\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nTransparent crystals.\nSodium Thioglycollate; Sodium Mercaptoacetate;\nMercaptoacetateAcid Sodium salt: SHCH2COONa= 114.09\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nHygroscopic crystals; odour, slight and characteristic.\nSodium Thiosulphate: Ofthe Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nSodium Thiosulphate, x M: Solutions of any molarity x M\nmay be prepared by dissolving 248 x g ofsodium thiosulphate\nand 2x g of sodium carbonate in sufficient carbon dioxide-\nfree water to produce 1000 ml.\nSodiumTungstate: Na2W04,2H20 = 329.86\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless crystals or white, crystalline powder.\nSorbitol: Ofthe Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nSqualane; 2,6,10,15,19,23-Hexamethyltetracosane: C30H62 =\n422.81\nGas chromatographic reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless oily liquid; refractive index at 20\n0\n, 1.451 to 1.453 ;\nwt. per ml, 0.811 to 0.813 g."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium Tartrate; Sodium(+)-Tartrate: C4H406Na2,2H20 =\n230.08\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nTransparent crystals.\nSodium Thioglycollate; Sodium Mercaptoacetate;\nMercaptoacetateAcid Sodium salt: SHCH2COONa= 114.09\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nHygroscopic crystals; odour, slight and characteristic.\nSodium Thiosulphate: Ofthe Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nSodium Thiosulphate, x M: Solutions of any molarity x M\nmay be prepared by dissolving 248 x g ofsodium thiosulphate\nand 2x g of sodium carbonate in sufficient carbon dioxide-\nfree water to produce 1000 ml.\nSodiumTungstate: Na2W04,2H20 = 329.86\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless crystals or white, crystalline powder.\nSorbitol: Ofthe Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nSqualane; 2,6,10,15,19,23-Hexamethyltetracosane: C30H62 =\n422.81\nGas chromatographic reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless oily liquid; refractive index at 20\n0\n, 1.451 to 1.453 ;\nwt. per ml, 0.811 to 0.813 g."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-thiosulphate-content","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium thiosulphate content"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium thiosulphate content\nThiosulphate content is determined by titrating the sample \nagainst the 0.005 N standard iodine solution.\nTitration of blank solution. Dissolve 250 mg of sodium \nbicarbonate powder in 10 ml distilled water in a conical flask. \nAdd 0.5 ml of 1 per cent starch solution as indicator and titrate \nagainst 0.005 N standard iodine solution till the end point \n(color changes from colourless to. blue).\nVolume of standard iodine solution consumed for blank .=\nTitration of sample solution. Dissolve 250 mg of sodium \nbicarbonate powder in 10 ml distilled water in a conical flask. \nAdd 0.5 ml of Iper cent starch solution as indicator add 250 pl \nsample and titrate against 0.005 N standard iodine: solution till \nthe end point (colour changes from colourless to blue)..\nVolume of standard iodine solution consumed by 250 pl sample \n= ‘A’ml. \n: . . \n. .\nThe actual volume of standard iodine solution consumed for\n1 mlsample = (A-B)x4ml=Cmf\nSodium thiosulphate content is calculated as follows:\nCxO.005x 158 = D mg sodium thiosulphate per ml \n- i\nSodium thiosulphate content in mgper Ci of 131I calculated by \nthe formula:\n. DxlOOOmg/Ci \n.\nActivity (mCi/ml) .\nThe limits should be 25-100mg per Ci of 1-13Ion date of \npreparation:\nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding:\nLabelling. The label states (1) the time and date of calibration; \n(2) the amount of1311 as iodide expressed in megabecquerels \n(microcuries or millicuries) at the time of calibration; (3) the \nexpiration date; (4) the statement “Caution — Radioactive \nmaterial”.\nIP 2022\nSODIUM PERTECHNETATE (\"MTc) INJECTION (FISSION)\nSodium Pertechnetate (99mTc) Injection \n(Fission)\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Sodium \nPertechnetate (99mTc) Injection (Fission) is a sterile solution \ncontaining technetium-99m in the form of pertechnetate ion \nand made isotonic by the addition of sodium chloride. Sodium \nPertechnetate (99mTc) Injection (Fission) contains not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \ntechnetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated on \nthe label.\nRadiopharmaceutical Preparation. Sodium Pertechnetate \n(99mTc) is extracted from Technetium-99m column \nchromatography generator containing fission-produced \nMolybdenum-99 adsorbed on acidic alumina column. \nMolybdenum-99 is extracted from uranium fission products.\nSodium chloride solution, 0.9 per cent w/v, is used to elute \nSodium Pertechnetate (99tnTc) solution which is sterilized by \nultrafiltration through 0.22 p filter."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium thiosulphate content\nThiosulphate content is determined by titrating the sample \nagainst the 0.005 N standard iodine solution.\nTitration of blank solution. Dissolve 250 mg of sodium \nbicarbonate powder in 10 ml distilled water in a conical flask. \nAdd 0.5 ml of 1 per cent starch solution as indicator and titrate \nagainst 0.005 N standard iodine solution till the end point \n(color changes from colourless to. blue).\nVolume of standard iodine solution consumed for blank .=\nTitration of sample solution. Dissolve 250 mg of sodium \nbicarbonate powder in 10 ml distilled water in a conical flask. \nAdd 0.5 ml of Iper cent starch solution as indicator add 250 pl \nsample and titrate against 0.005 N standard iodine: solution till \nthe end point (colour changes from colourless to blue)..\nVolume of standard iodine solution consumed by 250 pl sample \n= ‘A’ml. \n: . . \n. .\nThe actual volume of standard iodine solution consumed for\n1 mlsample = (A-B)x4ml=Cmf\nSodium thiosulphate content is calculated as follows:\nCxO.005x 158 = D mg sodium thiosulphate per ml \n- i\nSodium thiosulphate content in mgper Ci of 131I calculated by \nthe formula:\n. DxlOOOmg/Ci \n.\nActivity (mCi/ml) .\nThe limits should be 25-100mg per Ci of 1-13Ion date of \npreparation:\nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding:\nLabelling. The label states (1) the time and date of calibration; \n(2) the amount of1311 as iodide expressed in megabecquerels \n(microcuries or millicuries) at the time of calibration; (3) the \nexpiration date; (4) the statement “Caution — Radioactive \nmaterial”.\nIP 2022\nSODIUM PERTECHNETATE (\"MTc) INJECTION (FISSION)\nSodium Pertechnetate (99mTc) Injection \n(Fission)\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Sodium \nPertechnetate (99mTc) Injection (Fission) is a sterile solution \ncontaining technetium-99m in the form of pertechnetate ion \nand made isotonic by the addition of sodium chloride. Sodium \nPertechnetate (99mTc) Injection (Fission) contains not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \ntechnetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated on \nthe label.\nRadiopharmaceutical Preparation. Sodium Pertechnetate \n(99mTc) is extracted from Technetium-99m column \nchromatography generator containing fission-produced \nMolybdenum-99 adsorbed on acidic alumina column. \nMolybdenum-99 is extracted from uranium fission products.\nSodium chloride solution, 0.9 per cent w/v, is used to elute \nSodium Pertechnetate (99tnTc) solution which is sterilized by \nultrafiltration through 0.22 p filter."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry. The gamma-ray \nspectrum obtained from an aliquot test solution corresponds \nto the spectrum-^obtained with reference solution of \nTechnetium-99m either by . direct comparison or by using an \ninstrument calibrated with thé aid of such a solution. The \nmost prominent gamma photon of technetium-99m has energy \nof0.140MeV. \n• .\nB. Half life of Technetium-99m in test solution as determined \nwith a calibrated'gamma ray counter or a: radioactivity dose \ncalibrator, .by not less than three measurements of the \nradioactivity of a sample in the same, geometrical conditions \nwithin- a suitable period of decay time (for: example, three \nreadings at 30 minute intervals over 90 minutes) is between \n5.7 hours to 6.3 hours. : . \n;\nC. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to thé identification of the distribution \nof radioactivity in the preparation. \"J ■ '"},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 8 .b using pH paper strips ■\nRadioactivity Assay. Radioactivity of an ■ aliquot of Test\nSolution is assayed using precalibratcd radioactivity dose \ncalibrator as described in measurement of radioactivity. It is \nexpressed as MBq (mCi) in voïumé \n^¿..particular,time\nand date. \" \n...\nRadionuclidic purity. Not dess than:99 :9spervcent< dùé ;to \nTechnetium-99m. \n■\nThe fission 99Mo to be used for extraction of Technetium-99m \n(fission) should meet specification with respect to the \nfollowing fission products:\nHowever99m Tc or Fission \"Mo need not be reanalyzed for \nradionuclide impurity for the fission products, if fission \"Mo \nto be used for the extraction of \"mTc is procured from reputed \nmanufacturers or suppliers who certifies the specification on \nradionuclide purity of fission \"Mo for the following \nradionuclide.\n' Radionuclide \nLimit (not more than)\niodine-131\n5 x 10\"3 percent\nruthenium-103\n5 x 10'3 percent\n- strontium-89\n6 x H)-5 percent\nstrontium-90\n6 x 10'6 percent\nalpha-emitting impurities\n1 x 1 O'7 per cent\n. other gamma-emitting impurities\n0.01 percent\nMolybderium-99, Iodine-131, Ruthenium-103, Strohtium-89, \nYttrium-90 and other gamma-emitting impurities should not \nbe more than 0.1 per cent of total radioactivity.\nMolybdenum-99. Molybdenum-99 is the principal \nradionuclidic impurity which is required to be determined in \nthe test solution. The prescribed Molybdenum-99 impurity in \ntest solution should not exceed 37 KBq (1 gCi) of \nMolybdenum-99mper 37 MBq (1 mCi) ofTechnetium-99m. \nThe radionuclidic purity of Technetium-99m in test solution \nshould not be less than 99.9 per cent. Determination of \nMolybdenum-99 impurity in test solution is referred to \n“Molybdenum-99 Breakthrough Test”. This test can be done \neither by Method A or Method B.\nMethod A. By using 4-7 mm thick lead canister (radiation shield) \nand calibrated dose calibrator for assaying Molybdenum-99 \nactivity in test solution.\nAn empty 4 -7 mm thick lead radiation shield is placed in dose \ncalibrator and background activity (a kBq) is measured at \nMolybdenum-99 setting of the dose calibrator. Pertechnetate \n(99mTc) test solution vial is placed inside the lead canister and \nMolybdenum-99 activity (b kBq) is measured in dose calibrator \nat the calibration factor for Molybdenum-99. The background \nactivity (a kBq) for Molybdenum-99 obtained is subtracted \nfrom Molybdenum-99 activity reading (b kBq). The correction \nfactor for Molybdenum-99 gamma photon attenuation by lead \nshield is taken as recommended for the dose calibrator to \ndetermine Molybdenum-99 radioactivity. The Technetium- \n99m test solution vial is removed from the Molybdenum-99 \nassay shield container and assayed for Technetium-99m \nradioactivity (c MBq) in dose calibrator at setting for\nTechnetium-99m. Molybdenum-99 activity ((b-a) kBq) divided \nby Technetium-99m activity ( c MBq) gives Molybdenum-99 \nBreakthrough in kBq/MBq Technetium- 99m."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/mono