ny secondary peak is not more than 0.05 times \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (0.05 per cent). The sum of the areas of \nall the other secondary peaks is not more than 0.2 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (0.2 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area \nless than 0.03 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (0.03 per cent).\n quickly becomes yelowish and finaly black.\nto stand for 12 hours; the lower layer (solution A) gives \nreactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nB. To a portion of solution A add a few drops of iodine \nsolution; the colour is discharged. \t\n%FP\nC.Dilute 2.5 ml of solution A to 5 ml with  water and add 1 ml of \nhydrochloric acid; a gas is evolved which turns starch-iodate \npaper blue and a precipitate of sulphur is produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. A 20.0 per cent w/v solution is not\nmore opalescent than opalescence standard 0S2 (2.4.1), and \nis not more intensely coloured than reference solution\nD. 1 ml of solution A gives reaction (A) of sodium salts (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":".YAcSi6d(i2.4\ntyo1r).alkalinity. To 10 ml of a 10.0 per cent w/v solution\npH (2.4.24). 7.0 to 9.0.\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 2.5 g in 40 ml of  water in a \nseparating funnel, add 10 ml of dilute nitric acid  and shake. \nAllow the mixture to stand for 12 hours. 25 ml of lower layer \ncomplies with the limit test for chlorides (200 ppm).\nin carbon dioxide free water add 0.1 ml of dilute \nphenolphthalein solution; not more than 0.75 ml of either\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.03 Endotoxin \nUnit per mg of sodium thiosulphate.\n0.1 M sodium hydroxide or 0.1 M hydrochloric acid is \nrequired to change the colour of the solution.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parent \nPreparations (Injections).\nSulphates (2.3.17). 15 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for sulphates (200 ppm).\nRelated substances. Determine by gas chromatography\nAssay. To a measured volume containing about 0.5 g of Sodium \nThiosulphate add about 20 ml of water and titrate with 0.05 M\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B. Use 2 ml of lead standard solution \n(10 ppm) to prepare the standard (20 ppm).\n(2e);3am.13. in)ation in 10 ml of water. Add 5 ml of dilute sulphuric \nTest solution. Dissolve 0.5 g of the substance under\niodine, using 3 ml of starch solution, added towards the end \nof the titration, as indicator.\nacid and shake with 3 quantities, each of 20 ml, of heptane. \nDilute the combined upper layers to 100.0 ml with heptane.\n1 ml of 0.05 M iodine is equivalent to 0.02482 g of \nNa,S,03,5H2O.\nLoss on d ing (2.4.19). Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nReference solution  (a). Dissolve 5 mg of valproic acid for \nsystem suitability RS (containing impurity K) in 1.0 ml of \nheptane.\nStorage. Store in single dose containers.\nAssay. Weigh 0.15 g and dissolve in 25 ml of anhydrous  glacial \nacetic acid. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, determining \nthe end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank \ntitration.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with heptane."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-valproate-elixir","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Valproate Elixir"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Valproate Elixir \nLeni \nbe\nSodium ‘Valproate Gastro-resistant-Tablets::.::\nSodium Valproate Injectiont.:./ sii! tech\nSodium Valproate Orai Solution \n-;\nSodium Vaiproate Tablets\nSofosbuvir\n:293,810,3635\nSomatropin Concentrated Solution\nSomatropin for Injection\nSomatropinum - \n, \nSothali, \nf°. \nSorafenib Tablets\nSoratenib Tosylate\nSorafenib Tosylate Tablets\nSorbic