ot exceeding 0.1 kPa for 24 hours.\nOther<tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay; Deteriirine on the mixed contents of ten containers by \nthe microbiological assay of antibiotics, Method A or B (2.2.10).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3): Not more than 0.25 Endotoxin \nUnit per mg of streptomycin. 1\nStorage.Store protected frommoisture. ■\nlabelling. The label states the strength in terms of the\ne9a iva ten tamo untof streptomycin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/succinylcholine-inj-ection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Succinylcholine Inj ection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Succinylcholine Injection\nSuxamethonium Chloride Injection\nSuccinylcholine Injection is . a sterile , solution of \nSuccinylcholine Chloride in Water for Injections.;,;\nSuccinylcholine Injection contains not less than90.0 percent \nand not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \nsuccinylcholine chloride, Ci4H30Cl2N2O4,2H2CX\nUsual strength. 50 mg per ml. \n; \n|"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Succinylcholine Injection\nSuxamethonium Chloride Injection\nSuccinylcholine Injection is . a sterile , solution of \nSuccinylcholine Chloride in Water for Injections.;,;\nSuccinylcholine Injection contains not less than90.0 percent \nand not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \nsuccinylcholine chloride, Ci4H30Cl2N2O4,2H2CX\nUsual strength. 50 mg per ml. \n; \n|"},{"name":"Identification","text":"I\nDilute a volume containing 20 mg of Succinylcho.line Chloride I\nto 5 0 ml with water. To 0.5 ml add 2 ml of chloroform, 2 ml of a | \nsolution containing 0.16 per cent w/y of citric acid wMLbh per | \ncent w/v of disodium hydrogen phosphate and 0.1 ml of a | \nsolution containing 0.15 per cent w/v of each of brpmothymol i \nblue and anhydrous sodium carbonate. Shake for 2 minutes | \nand allow to separate. The chloroform layer is yellow. ? ; |\n■ ■ ■ \n• Ig"},{"name":"Tests","text":"'' \nI\npH(2.4.28)..3.0to5.0.\nHydrolysis products. The volume of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide § \nrequired for the preliminary neutralisation in the Assayis not । \nmore than one tenth of the total volume of 0.1: M sodium | \nhydroxide required for the preliminaiy neutralisation and .the g \nhydrolysis. , \n... . - \n■■■ |\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 2.OrEndqtoxin | \nUnitspermgof succinylcholine chloride. \n- \n|\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral g\nPreparations (Injections). . .. \n. \n< j\nAssay. To a measured volurne containing about 0.25 g of | \nSuccinylcholine Chloride add 30 ml of carbon dioxide free | \nwater and shake with five quantities, each of 25 ml, of ether. | \nWash the combined ether solutions with two quantities, each | \nof 10 ml, of water and discard the ether. Shake the combined g \nwashings with two quantities, each of 1Ö ml, of etlier, add the g \nwashings to-the original aqueous solution and neutralise with I\n0.1 M sodium hydroxide using bromothymol blue solute g \nas indicator. Add 25.0 ml of 0.7 M sodium hydroxide, heat g"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/succinylcholine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Succinylcholine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"SUCCINYLCHOLINE INJECTION\n11)201 8\n1 P 2018"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"SUCCINYLCHOLINE INJECTION\n11)201 8\n1 P 2018"},{"name":"Identification","text":"acetate solution. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, using \ncrystal violet solution as indicator, until a bluish green colour \nis produced. Carry out a blank titration."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Triturate a quantity of the powdered tablets  containing \n0.2 g of streptomycin in a mixture of 2 ml of  methanol and \n0.1 ml of sulphuric acid, filter, if necessary, and allow  to stand \nat about 25°; crystals of streptidine sulphate separate in th e\nTest A may be omitted if tests B, C and D are carried out. Tests\nB and C may be omitted i f tests A and D are carried out.\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.01807 g of \nC 141-130C12N204.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectiophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with succinylcholine\n course of 2 to 3 days. Disolve the crystals in a solution \nof \n0.1 g of picric acid in 10 ml of hot water and cool; the\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nchloride RS  or with the reference spectrum of succinylcholine\nch\nB.\n precipitate, after recrystalisation from hot \nwater, melts at about \n283° (2.4.21).\nID°1-ide  \nDissolve about 25 mg in 1 ml of water, add 0.1 ml of a 1 per \ncent w/v solution of  cobalt chloride and 0.1 ml of potassium \nferrocyanide solution; a green colour is produced.\nSuccinylcholine Injection\nB. Boil a small quantity of the powdered tablets containin g\n 0.1 g of streptomycin with 5 ml of \n 1 Msodium hydroxide  fora \nfew minutes, add a slight excess of 2 M hydrochloric acid \nand 0.15 ml of a 10 per cent w/v solution offerric chloride. \nbrilliant violet colour is produced."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sucralfate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sucralfate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sucralfate Tablets\n. Him \nx .... o... y ■■ ■ . \n■■■ . ■\nSucraiiateTablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of sucralfate, \nAUÔH)t6(èi2HpO^^^^ \ncorresponding to\nTest solution. Weigh and transfer.about 450 mg of the. t \nsubstance under examination in 50-inl centrifuge tube, and I \nshake on a vortex mixer. While shaking add 10.0 ml of a mixture | \nof 2.0 Msulphuric acid and 2.2 Msodium hydroxide, sonicate | \nwith swirling for 5 minutes, keeping , the temperature of the | \nmixture below 30°, Without delay transfer the tube to a vortex f \nmixer and while shaking at moderate rate, add an accurately \nmeasured volume, V, in ml, of 0.1Msodium hydroxide to bring \nthe pH of the solution to approximately ^, and dilute the \nsolution with(15,0- V) ml of water. ShakefOr 1. minute, and \ncentrifuge for 5 minutes. Separate the clear supernatant layer, \nand allow it to stand at robin temperature until the pH stabilizes. \nIf the pH is not between 2.3 and 3.5, repeat the test using a \ndifferent Noiomc0.1 Msodium hydroxide, ■|\nReference solution. A 1.0 per cent wZv solution of anhydrous \npoiassiiim sucrose octasulfàtë IPRS in the mobile phase.\nnot leks than 30.6 per cent and not more than 37.4 per cent of \nsucrosebctasulphate, C|?IT4O35Ss.\nChromatographic system\nUsual strength. .1.000 mg.\n- a stainless steel column 30 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\naminoprqpylsilanebounded to porous silica (5 pm), :\n- mobile phase: a buffer solution prepared by dissolving\nIdentïïicâtiÔii\nA. In;the1 Assaÿy the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained yvith the .testsolution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference, solution.\n132 g of ammonium sulphate in 900 ml of water, dilute \nto 1000 ml with water and adjusted to pH 3.5 with \n7‘. orthophosphoric acid. \n\\ \n’\n- flow rate:T nil per minute, : 'L' \ny-'1' ' z\n- refractive index detector maintained at 30°, '\ng\nB. Dissolve 1.0 g of sucralfate in 3 Mhydrochloric acid. The \nresultingsolution gives the reactions of aluminium salts (2.3.1 ).\nReference solution. A-1.0 per cent w/v solution of' anhydrous | \npotassium ^sucrose-octasulphate IPRS.m the, mobilephase. |\n........ .... ... ■ ■ ■ ■■■■■. । \nChromatographic system \n|\ns • -, < injection volume: 50 pl; y mm - ; y.-'. . ; : : / . : :c"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sucralfate Tablets\n. Him \nx .... o... y ■■ ■ . \n■■■ . ■\nSucraiiateTablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of sucralfate, \nAUÔH)t6(èi2HpO^^^^ \ncorrespon