LFATE\nI?' IP 2022^\na reflux condenser for 40 minutes, allow to cool and \ntitrate the eXOess of alkali with (7.7 Mhydrochloric acid using\nindicat?r. Repeat the operation \n.dioxide-free water beginning, at the \n°fsodium hydroxide..........The\ndifference,between the titrations represents the amount of \nsodimnhydroxide required.\n■ ■ ', ... &...\nTests. ■\nto 0 01987 g of\nStorag|itoreprqtected from light, at a temperature between\n2° to 80. The injectioii should not be allowed to freezc.\nSucralfate\nnAl(OH)3, n'H2O .\n< yy iu \ni s ; Ji; ■■ H •• : t ' -■• ■ • j • • ■ • -, ■ J ' \n- • • ■ ' \n..................\nSucralfate is Als(OH)16(Ci2HMO35S8)[Al(OlI)3|n|lLO]rfin which \nn= 8 to 10; and nr-22 to 31. a-D-Glucopyranoside, p-D- \nfrûctdfufàhÔsÿR;6ctakis (hydrogensulphate), aluminium \ncomplex. \n.....\nSüçrâlfàtë is the hydrous basic aluminium salt of sucrose \noctaSuifate; If contains the equivalent of not legs than\n3(Mf percent and not more than 38.0 per cent of sucrose \noctasulfatë, Ci2l-IÎ4O35S8.\nCategory. Antiulcer.\nDescription. A white or almost white, amorphous powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A? In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nohromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nD- Dissolve 500 mg of the substance under examination in\niOO.O ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, and boil the mixture \ngently, with stirring, for 20 minutes until the sample is\ncompletely dissolved. Neutralize with 0.1M sodium hydroxide, \nand allow to cool. Add 4 ml of alkaline cupric tartrate solution. \nBoil a small amount Of the solution: a red copious precipitate \nof cuprous oxide is produced.\nC. A solution in 3 M hydrochloric acid gives.the reactions of \naluminium salts (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. A 10.0 per cent w/v solution in\n1. Msulphuric acid is clear and colourless (2.4.1).\nNeutralising capacity: Weigh and transfer 0.25 g of the \nsubstance under examination in 250 ml screw-capped bottle, \nadd 100.0 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid previously heated to \n37°, cap the bottle and place in waterrbath, maintaining the \ntemperature at 37° in water-bath, and stir continuously for\n1 hour. Cool to room temperature and transfer 20.0 ml of this \nresulting solution to a 100-ml beaker add 30 ml of water and \ntitrate with 0.1 Msodium hydroxide to a pH of 3.5. Carry out \na blank titration using a mixture of 3 0 ml of water and 20.0 ml of \n0.1 M hydrochloric acid.\nCalculate the mEq of acid consumed per g of sucralfate taken \nby the formula:\n•• • J , 5M(Vb-Vt) ; ..\ni- \n■ ’ • . ' w - . ■\nWhere 'M is’.the exact molarity of the 0.1 M'sodium \nhydroxide., VB and VT are’ the volumes, in ml of 0.1M sodium \nhydroxide consumed by the blank and the test solution, \nrespectively; arid W is the weight, in g;, of sainpleJ taken:\nNot less than 12.0 mEq of acid is consumed. . \n;\nChloride (2.3.12), Transfer 0.5,g to.a 100.0 ml volumetric flask, \nadd 30 ml of 2 M nitric acid, dissolve and dilute to volume \nwith water. 10.0 ml ofthe solution complies with the limit test \nfor chlorides (0.5 per cent). \n. \n■. ...\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 2.5 g in 15 ml of brominated \nhydrochloric acid, add 45 ml of water and remove excess of \nbromine with a few drops of stannous chloride solution AsT. \nThe resulting solution complies with: the limit for arsenic \n(4 ppm). :\nPyridine and 2-methylpyridine. Not more than 0.05 per cent \neach of pyridine and 2-methylpyridine, determine by gas \nchromatography (2.4.13). \n■■ ■ >'\nInternal standard solution. Dilute 1.0 ml of 3-methylpyridine\ninto, a 50.0 ml volumetric flask with chloroforms^ mix,. Further \ndilute 1,0 ml ofthe solution to 50,0 ml with chloroform.\nTest solution. Disperse 1.0 g of the substance under \nexamination inTO. 0 ml of IM sodium hydroxide with the aid of \nultrasound until a uniformly cloudy mixture is obtained, extract\nwith three -.5¿ml. quantities of chloroform and collect; the \nchloroform layer in 20.0. ml volumetric, flask,add 1.0; ml of \ninternal, standardsolution dilute tovolume with chloroform \nandmix. \n\" \n• - 1 .-¡J-,ny. d -rovo \nto\nReference solution fa) \nand dissolve 0.5 g of each of\n2-methyIpyridine and pyridine in sufficient chloroform to \nproduce 50.0 ml. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with \nchloroform. Further dilute 5.0 ml of this resulting solution to \n50.0 ml with \nandmix. . . ..... ...\nReference solution (b). Dilute 5.0 ml of reference solution (a),\nadd 1,0 ml of internal standard solution to 2.0.0. ml with \nchloroform. r. \n? \n.. .. \n.....•, /,'■ . ; .... \n.\nChromatographic system \n: .’m;. u.ui ar-\n1 - a capillary column 10 m x 0.53 mm, coated with 5 per cent\n' ■ ‘ phenyl and '95 per cent ■ methylpolysiloxane (film\nthickness2.65 pm), •ZI \n‘ \n;\n- column temperature: 50°,\ninjection port at 150° and detec tor at 200°, \n'\n- ' flame ionization detector, ■ • •1 • ' '■ •••J \n; ■' ; r : ’ ■\n- pressure: 0.7 psi using helium or nitrogen as carrier\n- inj ection volume: 1 pl. \n. ; 5 ; < : : . ;, ;\nInject reference solution (b). The relative retention times for \npyridine, 2-methy Ipyridine and 3-methylpyridine are about 0.42, \n0.72 and 1.0 respectively. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between thet,peaks due,to pyridine and.2r \nmethylpyridine is not less than 3.5 and for the peaks due to 2- \nmethylpynd.ine and 3-methylpyridine is not less than 2'5. The \nrelati ve standard deviation for the ratio of the area of analyte \npeak to internal standard for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent. \nov' • •\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. Calculate \nthe content of pyridine and 2-methylpyridine from the peak \nresponse ratios of pyridine to the internal standard and 2- \nmethylpyridine to internal standard obtained with reference \nsolution. (b) and. the; test; solution, respectively, y . ; < : \n;■\nCalculate the content of pyridine and 2-methylpyridine.\nSucrose Heptasulphate. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay with the following \nmodifications. \n.. \n.. \n.\n.>f-p .> '• j: J'?..; ■■,■>.>' i : ; . I ' : ?. * j ’ f ' j < ; ? • . -p. f¡_ ■_ •; ' : •) i • y- j >•• • \nj •. •. •: û y '• :\nMobile phase. A buffcr solution prepared by dissolving 99.1g\nof ammonium sulphate in 900 ml ofwater dilute to 1000 ml \nwith water and adjusted to pH 3.5 with orthophosphoric acid.\nInject the test solution. The rclati ve retention time for sucrose \nheptasulphafe and 'sucrose octasulfate is 0.6 and 1:0 \nrespectively.; ¿The ratio of the peak response of the sucrose\nheptasulphate peak to., that of the sucrose octasulfate peak is \nnot more than 0.1. \n, y .h;i/ ,\nSUCRALFATE\nIp2022\nSUCRALFATE TABLETS\n(P2Ô22\nAluminum. 15.5 to 18,5 percent of aluminium calculated on \"S \nis basis and determined by the following method. \n1\nCalculate thé content of C^HuOscSf; as ■\nby the blank and the test solution, respectively; and W is the \nweight, in g, of equivalent amount of sample taken.\ny®H¿qfi=<yi;1287-53' - ■■ r. '\nDissolve about 1.0 g of the: substance under examinât^ ; \naccurately weighed in a 250.0 mi volumetric flask, add 10 nflOf t' \n6.0 Mhydrochloric acid, mix"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sucralose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sucralose"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"SUCRALOSE.\n1 mg of anhydrouspotassium sucrose octasulfate is equivalent \nto 0.757 mg of sucrose octasulfate. ,:\nStorage. Store protected from moisture. \n: \n; \n,\nSucralose\nC12H19C13O8 1 \n; \nMol.Wt.397.6\nSucralose is 1,6-dichloro-1,6-di dcoxy-[l-D-fructofuranosyl- \n4-chloro-4-deoxy-a-D-galactopyranoside.\nSucralose contains not, less than 98.0; per .cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Çl2H19Cl3O8, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (sweetening vehicle),,\nDescription: A white to off-white crystalline powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"SUCRALOSE.\n1 mg of anhydrouspotassium sucrose octasulfate is equivalent \nto 0.757 mg of sucrose octasulfate. ,:\nStorage. Store protected from moisture. \n: \n; \n,\nSucralose\nC12H19C13O8 1 \n; \nMol.Wt.397.6\nSucralose is 1,6-dichloro-1,6-di dcoxy-[l-D-fructofuranosyl- \n4-chloro-4-deoxy-a-D-galactopyranoside.\nSucralose contains not, less than 98.0; per .cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Çl2H19Cl3O8, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (sweetening vehicle),,\nDescription: A white to off-white crystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with thatobtainedwithjwcra/oje/PTi.S' \nor with the reference spectrum of sucralose. ....\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the te§t solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution. ."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 84.0° to+87:5°, determined \nat 20° in a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in w/er. j.\nRelated substances: Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with octadecylsilanized silica gel.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 70 volumes of 5 per cent w/v \nsolution of sodium chloride and 30 volumes of acetonitrile.\nTest solution. Dissolve about 1.0 g of the substance under \nexamination in 10 mlof methanol.\nRéference solution (a). A l .0 per cent w/v solution of sucralose \nIPRSm methanol. / Ï \" \n' . \n. ' .\nReference solution (b). Dilute 5.0 ml ofreference solution (a)\nto 100.0 ml with methanol. \n. ;\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. After develop^ \ndry the plate in air and spray the plate with 15 per cent \nsolution of sulphuric acid in methanol./Heat the plate at 125» \nfor 10 minutes. The Rf value of the principal spbt obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the spot in \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). \nsecondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution is not more intense than the spot in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.5 per cent). ■r\nHydrolysis products. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel.\nNOTE—This test does not require a developing solvent.\nTest solution. Dissolve about 2.5 .g of the substance under \nexamination in 10 ml of methanol.\nReference solution (a). A 10.0 per cent w/v solution of \nmannitol in water. \n.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.04 percent \nw/v offructoseand 10 per cent w/v of mannitol in water.\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. Spray with a ..solution \ncontaining 1.23 g ofp-anisifme and 1.66 g of phthalic acid'm\n100 ml of methanol, heat the plate at 105° for. 15 minutes. If the \nspot from reference solution (a) has darkened, repeat the test, \nheating for a shorter period of time. Immediately after heating \nview the plate against a dark background: the color of the \nspot obtained from the test solution is not more intense than \nthat obtained from reference solution (b);(0,l per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3 .13). 2:0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method'B (10 ppm). ' ; \" '■\" ”'u •' ■'\nSulphated ash (2.3:18). Not more thari 0.7 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined on\n1.0g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14)/\nTest solution: Dissolve 250 mg'of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 25.0 ml with the\nmobile phase.\nReference solution. A 1,0 per cent w/y solution of sucralose \ndPRS in the mobile phase, \n.. ~ - ;r\nChromatographic system ; \n: ;;;; ’ r ’\nJ -a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 min, packed wits\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm)j\n. ~; mobile, phase: a mixture of 85 volumes of water and 1-\nvolumes of acetonitrile, \n:\n- flow rate: 1,5 ml per minute, \n- refractive index detector\n- injection volume: 20 pl.J\nInjcctthe reference solution. The test is= notwalid unless 6*\ntailing factor js not more than 2.0 per cent. ■; - - ■:\n3674\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sucrose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sucrose"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sucrose\nRefined Sugar\nHO\nOH \nOH \t\n \nHO\nOH\nOH\nMol. Wt. 342.3\nCIA-1220H\nSucrose is I3-D-fructofuranosyl a-D-glucopyranoside.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (sweetening agent; tablet \nexcipient).\nDescription. An almost white or colourless crystals, dry \ncrystalline powder; odourless; taste, sweet."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sucrose\nRefined Sugar\nHO\nOH \nOH \t\n \nHO\nOH\nOH\nMol. Wt. 342.3\nCIA-1220H\nSucrose is I3-D-fructofuranosyl a-D-glucopyranoside.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (sweetening agent; tablet \nexcipient).\nDescription. An almost white or colourless crystals, dry \ncrystalline powder; odourless; taste, sweet."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Dissolve 150.0 g in sufficient carbon dioxide free water \nprepared from distilled water to produce 300 ml (solution A).\nDilute 1 ml of solution A to 100 ml with water. To 5 ml of the \nsolution add 2 ml of freshly prepared  2 Msodium hydroxide \nand 0.15 ml of a freshly prepared copper sulphate solution; \nthe solution is clear and blue and remains so on boiling. To \nthe hot solution add 4 ml of 2 M hydrochloric acid, heat to \nboiling and add 4 ml of 2 M sodium hydroxide;  an orange \nprecipitate is produced immediately."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Acidity or alkalinity. To 10 ml of solution A add 0.3 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution. The solution is co-lopfless and not\nmore than 0.6 ml of 0.01 Msodium hydroxide IS -requited to \nchange the colour of the solution to pink. - -"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sugar-spheres","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sugar Spheres"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"SUGAR SPHERES\njp2022\nsolution and the test solution.\n^f^Sœprbtected from moisture, at a temperature not\nSucrose\nRefinedSugar\nSucrose is p-D-fructofuranosyl a-D-glucopyranoside.\nCategory? Pharmaceutical aid (sweetening agent; tablet \nexcipient).\nDescription. An almost white or colourless crystals, dry \ncrystalline powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"SUGAR SPHERES\njp2022\nsolution and the test solution.\n^f^Sœprbtected from moisture, at a temperature not\nSucrose\nRefinedSugar\nSucrose is p-D-fructofuranosyl a-D-glucopyranoside.\nCategory? Pharmaceutical aid (sweetening agent; tablet \nexcipient).\nDescription. An almost white or colourless crystals, dry \ncrystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Dissolve 150.0 g in sufficient carbon dioxide-free water \npreparedfrom distilledwater to produce 300 ml (solution A). \nDilute 1ml of solution A to 100 ml with water. To 5 ml of the \nsolution add 2 ml of freshly prepared 2 Msodium hydroxide \nand 0.15 ml of a freshly prepared copper sulphate solution', \nthe solution is clear and blue and remains so on boiling. To \nthe hot solution add 4 ml of 2 M hydrochloric acid, heat to \nboiling and add 4 ml of 2 M sodium hydroxide, an orange \nprecipitate is produced immediately."},{"name":"Tests","text":"or alkalinity. To 10 ml of solution A add 0.3 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution. The solution is colourless and not\nm°re than 0.6 ml of 0.01 Msodium hydroxide is required to \nchange the colour of the solution to pink.\nSatire optical rotation (2.4.22).+65.9° to+67.0°, determined\n,n a 10 per cent w/v solution.\n^tium. To 10 ml of solution A add 1 ml of 7 Msulphuric acid.\nen examined immediately and after 1 hour any opalescence \nK not more intense than that of a mixture of 1 ml of distilled \nUWerand 10 ml of solution A.\nCalcium. To 1 ml of solution A add 9 ml of water and 1 ml of \nammonium oxalate solution', the solution remains clear for at \nleast 1 minute.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Add 0.1 ml of dilute hydrochloric acid \nto 4 ml of solution A and dilute with sufficient water to produce \n25 ml. Thé solution complies with the limit test for heavy metals, \nMethodA(lOppm).\nSulphites. To 4 ml of solution A add sufficient water to produce \n20 ml, add 0.05 ml of 0.1 Miodine and 0.05 ml of starch solution', \na blue colour develops.\nDextrins. To 2 ml of solution A add 8 ml of water, 0.05 ml of \n2 M hydrochloric acid and 0.05 ml of 0.05 M iodine, the \nsolution remains yellow or becomes faint bluish green..\nGlucose and invert sugar. Dissolve 50 g in water and dilute to\n100.0 ml with the same solvent. To 5.0 ml of the solution in a \ntest-tube about 150 mm long and 16 mm in diameter add 5 ml of \nwater, 1.0 ml of 1Msodium hydroxide and 1.0 ml of a 0.1 per \ncent solution of methylene blue . Mix and place in a water­\nbath. After exactly 2 minutes, take the tube out of the bath and\nexamine the solution immediately. The blue colour does not \ndisappear completely. Ignore any blue colour at the air/solution \ninterface.\nColouring matter. A. To 100 ml of solution A in.a grpund- \nglass-stoppered tube add 1 ml of dilute hypophosphorous \nacid and allow to stand for 1 hour; no unpleasant odour is \ndetectable. \n'•'••• \n:\nB. Examine solution A under ultraviolet light at 365 hm. Any \nfluorescence is not more intense than that of a solution \ncontaining 0.4 pg per ml o\\ quinine sidphate io: .0.005 M \nsulphuric acid. ■■ \ny\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than O.Tper cent determined \nby dissolving 5.0 g in 5 ml of water, adding 2sulphuric \nacid, evaporating todryncss and igniting to constant weight.\nSucrose intended for use.in the manufacture of parenteral \npreparations without a further appropriate procedure for\nthe removal of bacterial endotoxins complies with the \nfollowing additional requirement. \n. .\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.25 Endotoxin \nunit per mg of Sucrose. \nf\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nSugar Spheres\nSugar Spheres contain not less than 62.5 per cent and not \nmore than 91.5 per cent of sucrose, CI2H22Olb calculated on \nthe dried basis, the remainder consisting mainly of starch. \nThey consist of approximately spherical particles of a labeled\n3675\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSUGAR SPHERES\nhominal: size range; They may contain Colour additives \npermitted for use in drugs.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Hard, brittle, free flowing, spherical masses \nranging generally in size from 1Ó to 60 mesh. Usually white, \nbut may be coloured. \n......... /"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. A 10 per cent w/v solution produce a violet to deep blue \ncolour on addition of iodine.\nB. Specific optical rotation (see Test)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"• \n¡4 ...-m. \n.. .•\nParticle size distribution estimation (2.5.7). Not less than 90 \nper cent, of it passes the coarser sieve size stated in thè labeling; \nall of it passes the next coarser 'sieve size listed in Table 1 of \nthe chapter; and not more than 10 per cent passes the finer\nsieve size stated on the label.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22).+ 41.0° to+61.0c, determined \nin a: lO.percent w/v solution prepared by dissolving 20 g in\n160 ml of water and dilute to 200.0 ml with water. Pass- the \nsolubilised sucrose solution by vacuum filtration through fine \nfilter paper. ‘ \n■ '■ \n■ '''\"'f'\"\nSulphated ash (2,3.. 1,8).-Not inore than 0.25 per cent, determined, \non 2.0 g at a temperature of about 700°.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 4.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° lor 4 hours. .. .\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Total aerobic viable count is \nnot more than 100 CFU per g. 1 g is free from Escherichia, coli, \nStaphylococcus, aureus and Pseudomonas aeruginosa, 10 g \nis free from Salmonella. . ,r>\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.' 1