methyl-1 -[3-(4-nitrophenoxy)phenyl]ethanamine.\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to rivastigmine related \ncompound A and rivastigmine related compound B is not less \nthan 1.5 in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b) and the relative standard deviation for replicate injections \nis not more than 10 per cent in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a), o ■ \\ .\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 8 times the retention time ofthe principal peak. \nThe area of any peak corresponding to phenol irnpurity is not \nmore than 3 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram.obtained with reference solution (a) (0.3 per \ncent); The area of any peak corresponding to DPTTA, nor \nimpurity, carbamate impurity and ether impurity, each of , is"},{"name":"RIVASTIGMINE TARTRATE","text":"not more than 1.5 times the; area bftheprincipalpeak in the \nchromatogram 'obtained with référencé solution (a) (0.15 per \ncent). The area of any other secondary peak is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solutipn (a) (Ó.1 per cent) and the sum of the \nareas of all the secondary peaks is not more than 5 times the \narea of &e pnncipdi'jie^ \nwith\nreference solution (a) (0.5 per cent). Ignore the peak due to \ntartaric acid? !n <rJ\"' \n’ ’• \n£'-' ' \n• ■ '*■■■■ •\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with limit test for heavy \nmetals, Method B (20 ppm). , i: - ; ■ \n■■■'>■ \"f\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. :i\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.5 per cent.\nAssay. Determine by liquid .chromatography (2.4.14), as \ndescribed under Related substances with the following \nmodification...........\nTest solution. Dissolve 20 mg of the substance, under \nexamination in 70 ml of the mobile phase and diluteto 100.0 ml \nwith the mobile phase. ...................\nReference solution. (a). ;A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of\nrivastigmine tartrate IP RS in the mobile phase.\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to rivastigmine related \ncompound A and rivastigmine related compound B is hot less \nthan 1.5 in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b), the. column efficiency is not, less than 5000 theoretical \nplates, the tailing factor is not more than 3.0 and the relative \nstandard deviation for replicate injections is riot more than \n2.0 per cent in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a). \n. \n. \n..... ,, :,..... .\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30e. \n,"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tauroursodeoxycholic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tauroursodeoxycholic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Assay. Dissolve 1.0 g in 25 ml of water and titrate with 1 M \nsodium hydroxide using 0.5 ml of phenolphthalein solution \nas indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.07505 g of \nC4H6O6.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTauroursodeoxycholic Acid\nC26H45NO6.S \nMol Wt. 499.7\nTauroursodeoxycholic Acid is 3a, 7 p-dihydroxy-5 P - \ncholanoyltaurine.\nTauroursodeoxycholic Acid contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C26H45 NO6S, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Use to dissolve cholesterol gallstones.\nDescription. A white to off white powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with \ntauroursodeoxycholic acid IPRS or with the reference \nspectrum of tauroursodeoxycholic acid.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 42° to + 48°, determined \non 1.0 per cent w/v solution in ethanol at 20°. \n;\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n- y 'r../. \n.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 25.0 ml with the \nmobilephase. \n; \n;\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \ntauroursodeoxycholic acid IPRS in the mobile phase."},"sections":[{"name":"TAUROURSODEOXYCHOLIC ACID","text":"Assay. Dissolve 1.0 g in 25 ml of water and titrate with 1 M \nsodium hydroxide using 0.5 ml of phenolphthalein solution \nas indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.07505 g of \nC4H6O6.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTauroursodeoxycholic Acid\nC26H45NO6.S \nMol Wt. 499.7\nTauroursodeoxycholic Acid is 3a, 7 p-dihydroxy-5 P - \ncholanoyltaurine.\nTauroursodeoxycholic Acid contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C26H45 NO6S, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Use to dissolve cholesterol gallstones.\nDescription. A white to off white powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with \ntauroursodeoxycholic acid IPRS or with the reference \nspectrum of tauroursodeoxycholic acid.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 42° to + 48°, determined \non 1.0 per cent w/v solution in ethanol at 20°. \n;\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n- y 'r../. \n.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 25.0 ml with the \nmobilephase. \n; \n;\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \ntauroursodeoxycholic acid IPRS in the mobile phase."},{"name":"TAUROURSODEQXYCHQLIC ACID","text":"Chromatographic system \n!\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded? to. porous silica (5 pm),\n~ column temperature: 40°, \n;\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 30 volumes of a solution\nprepared by dissolving 0.69 g of sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate into 1000 ml of water, 40 volumes of \nmethanol, 30 volumes of acetonitrile and 0.1 volume \nof triethylamine, adjusted to pH 3.0 with ortho - \nphosphoric acid,\n- flowrate: 1.2 ml per minute,\n- reflective index detector set at a constant temperature,\n- injection volume: 100 pl.\nInject the reference solution: The test, is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates n \nand the tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nInject the test solution. The sum of areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 3.0 per cent, calculated by area \nnormalization. \n’ v, .' \n.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy rpetals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2,3.18). Not more than 0.5 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 8.0 per cent,; determined on \n0-5g. \n.....: ;\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). : \n. . .\nTest solution. Dissolved mg of the substance under \nexamination, in the mobile phase and.diluteto 25.0 ml with: the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of\ntauroursodeoxycholic acid IPRS in the mobile phase.; ?\nChromatographic system , .\nL,. - a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n; octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column temperature: 40°, \n; ;\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 30 volumes of a solution\nprepared by dissolving 0.69 g frf sodium' dihydrogen \northophosphate into 1000 ini of water, 40 volumes of \nmethanol, 30 voluihesacetonitrile and' 0.1 volume \nof triethylamine, adjusted to pH 3.0 with ortho- \nphosphoric acid,\n“■flowrate: 1.2 mlper minute,\n- refractive index detector set at a constant temperature,\n. injection volume: 100 pl;.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unlessthe \ncolumn efficiency is not less, than 1500 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor :is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per pent.\nInject .the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculalc the content of CUU NO6iS. . .. m \n■ 2 ,"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tazobactam-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tazobactam Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TAZOBACTAM SODIUM\n„ tect the reference solution. The relative retention time with Sterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility,\nreference to tazobactam for tazobactam- related compound A \nis about 0.3 and for /.-phenylalanine is about 0.7.\nThetest is not valid unless the resolution between tazobactam \nand ¿-phenylalanine peaks is not less than 6.0.\nthe test solution. The area of any peak corresponding \nto tazobactam- related compound A is not more than 1.0 per \ncent.The area of any other, secondary peak is not more than \n01 ij^iìèent and the sum areas of .ali the secondary peaks \nother than tazobactam- related compound A is hot more than \n0/£per cent, calculated by area normalization.\nflehyy ntetals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy métals, Method B(20ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.6 per cent for anhydrous form \nand between 2.2 per cent to 3.8 per cent for hemihydrate form.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). The total viable aerobic count \ndoes notexceed TO3 cfu per g and the total combined moulds \nand yeasts count does not exceed 102 cfu per g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTE— iUse freshly prepared solutions, store and inject at \n3°, using a cooled autosampler.\nTési solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 100.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of tazobactam \nIPRS'm the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n¡r; a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadccylsiiane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n. A . sample temperature: 2° to 4°,\n~: mobile phase: a mixture of a buffer solution prepared by\n- dissolving E32 g of dibasic ammonium phosphate in\nabout 750 ml of water, adjusted to pH 2.5 with 5 per cent\n' y/v. solution of orthophosphoric acid, dilute to 1000 ml\nwith water and 3 0 ml of acetonitrile,\n- .flowrate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n— r .injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 1,8 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\n^niecf th,e reference solution and the test solution. ..\nCalculate the content of.CioH^N^S. \n\\ \n;\nTazobactam intended for use in the manufacture ofparenteral\nPreparations without a further appropriate sterilisation \nP^acedurewcomplies with, the following additional\nrequirement,\nTazobactam intended for use in the manufacture ofparenteral \npreparations without a further appropriate procedure for\nthe removal of bacterial endotoxins complies with the \nfollowing additional requirement.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.08 Endotoxin \nUnit per mg of tazobactam.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, preferably at \ntemperature below 30°.\nLabelling. The label states whether the material is anhydrous \nor hemihydrates form.\nTazobactam Sodium\nCkjHj 1N4NaOsS \nMol. Wt. 322.3\nTazobactam Sodium is Sodium (2S,3 S,5R)-3-methyl-7-oxo-3T \n(1H- 1,2,3-triazol-1 -ylmethyl)-4-thia-1 -azabicyclp[3.2.0] \nheptane-2-carboxylicacid,4,4-dioxide. \n. \n.\nTazobactam Sodium contains not less than 98.0, per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of .CioHnN4Na05S, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis. ’\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor.\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline hygroscopic \npowder. \n,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TAZOBACTAM SODIUM\n„ tect the reference solution. The relative retention time with Sterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility,\nreference to tazobactam for tazobactam- related compound A \nis about 0.3 and for /.-phenylalanine is about 0.7.\nThetest is not valid unless the resolution between tazobactam \nand ¿-phenylalanine peaks is not less than 6.0.\nthe test solution. The area of any peak corresponding \nto tazobactam- related compound A is not more than 1.0 per \ncent.The area of any other, secondary peak is not more than \n01 ij^iìèent and the sum areas of .ali the secondary peaks \nother than tazobactam- related compound A is hot more than \n0/£per cent, calculated by area normalization.\nflehyy ntetals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy métals, Method B(20ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.6 per cent for anhydrous form \nand between 2.2 per cent to 3.8 per cent for hemihydrate form.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). The total viable aerobic count \ndoes notexceed TO3 cfu per g and the total combined moulds \nand yeasts count does not exceed 102 cfu per g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTE— iUse freshly prepared solutions, store and inject at \n3°, using a cooled autosampler.\nTési solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 100.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of tazobactam \nIPRS'm the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n¡r; a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadccylsiiane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n. A . sample temperature: 2° to 4°,\n~: mobile phase: a mixture of a buffer solution prepared by\n- dissolving E32 g of dibasic ammonium phosphate in\nabout 750 ml of water, adjusted to pH 2.5 with 5 per cent\n' y/v. solution of orthophosphoric acid, dilute to 1000 ml\nwith water and 3 0 ml of acetonitrile,\n- .flowrate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n— r .injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 1,8 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\n^niecf th,e reference solution and the test solution. ..\nCalculate the content of.CioH^N^S. \n\\ \n;\nTazobactam intended for use in the manufacture ofparenteral\nPreparations without a further appropriate sterilisation \nP^acedurewcomplies with, the following additional\nrequirement,\nTazobactam intended for use in the manufacture ofparenteral \npreparations without a further appropriate procedure for\nthe removal of bacterial endotoxins complies with the \nfollowing additional requirement.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.08 Endotoxin \nUnit per mg of tazobactam.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, preferably at \ntemperature below 30°.\nLabelling. The label states whether the material is anhydrous \nor hemihydrates form.\nTazobactam Sodium\nCkjHj 1N4NaOsS \nMol. Wt. 322.3\nTazobactam Sodium is Sodium (2S,3 S,5R)-3-methyl-7-oxo-3T \n(1H- 1,2,3-triazol-1 -ylmethyl)-4-thia-1 -azabicyclp[3.2.0] \nheptane-2-carboxylicacid,4,4-dioxide. \n. \n.\nTazobactam Sodium contains not less than 98.0, per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of .CioHnN4Na05S, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis. ’\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor.\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline hygroscopic \npowder. \n,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tazobactam \nsodium IPRS or with the reference spectrum of tazobactam \nsodium;\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nC. Gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nTAZOBACTAM SODIUM"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. A10;0 per cent w/v solution in water\nis clear (2.4.1) and colourless (2.4.1).\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 140.0° to + 150.0°, \ndetermined in a 1,0 per cent w/v solution.\npH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 8.0, determined in a 10.0 per cent w/v solution.\nLight absorption (2.4.7), Absorbance of a 10 per cent w/v \nsolution in water at about 430 rim is riot more than 0.10.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 5.0 per cent, determined on \n0.2g. \n'\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 50.0 ml of the mobile phase. Dilute 5.0 ml of the \nsolution to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution, A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of tazobactam \nIPRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 3Ó volumes of methanol, 20\nvolumes of acetonitrile and 50 volumes of a buffer \nsolution prepared by dissolving 6.8 g of potassium \ndihydrogen orthophosphate in 1000 ml of waler.\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- injection volume : 2 0 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 1.5 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is riot more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and’ the test solution:\nCalcúlate the content of CjoHn^NaCbS by multiplying the\ncontent of CioH12N405S with 1.073.\nTazobactam Sodium intended for use in the manufacture of \nparenteral preparations without a further appropriate \nprocedure for the removal of bacterial endotoxins complies\nwith the following additional requirement.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3), Not more than 0.20 Endotoxin \nUnit per mg of tazobactam sodium, \n...........\nTazobactam Sodium intended for use'in thé manufacture of \nparenteral preparations without d further'appropriate\nsterilisation procedure complies with the following \nadditional requirement. \n,,,.. \n.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies-with the tesffor sterility. ;\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°. ' \n< \n?■\" '\nAn\nM.W.: 1564 \nH\nCategory. Glycopeptides antibacterial. \n': \n\\\nTeicoplanin is a mixture of the glycopeptides produced by the \ngrowth of certain strains of Actinoplanes teichomyceiicussp- \nfermentation product. The 6 principle contents of the mixturé \nare teicoplanin A2-i to A2.5 and teicoplanin A34. ... -.. : ;\nTeicoplanin has a potency of not less than 900 Units permg, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis and bn sodium'chloride \nfree substances. \n.\nA yellowish, amorphous powder.\nXJJetenuinc by infrared absorption spectfophotometery \niA 6)/<Çompare the. spectrum with that obtained, with\n■^glagfeiJPRS or with the reference spectrum of teicoplanin.\ng flithetest for Composition and Related substances, the\nand area Of the, principal peaks (teicoplanins\n-h. Am, Aa-3? A2-4 and A2.5) in the chromatogram obtained \ntest solution corresponds to the principal peaks in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nTests - ¿a \n; \n;. ■ .,...\nAppearance of solution (2.4.1). A 8.0 per cent w/v solution in\nfree water is clear and not more intensely \ncoloured than reference solution BYS3 or BS4.\n^(,(2.4.24). 6.5 to 7.5, determined in5.0percent w/v solution \niiicàrèû/î dioxide-free water.\nÇonipositionandRelated substances. Determine by liquid \nchromatography (2.4.14).\nfest; solution. Dissolve .100 mg of the substance under \nexamination in water and dilute to 50.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 20 mg of teicoplanin peak \nidentification IPRS in water and dilute to 10.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto10.0 ml with water. Dilute 1.0 ml of diesolution to 20.0 ml\nReference solution (c)i Dissolve*50 mg of mesityl oxide IP RS\n'in water and dilute to 25.0 ml with water. Dilute 1.0 ml of the\nl,QO.Qml;With water. . \n. ■\nsolution to 10.0 ml with water. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to\nChromatographic system\n,a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \n'7 spherical end capped octadecylsilane bonded to porous\n“ ' silica (5 pm),\n~ mobile phase: A. a mixture of 900 volumes of a solution\naDv 'prepared by dissolving 3.0 g of anhydrous sodium \nto i : dihydrogen phosphate in 1000 ml of water, adj usted to\npH 6.0 with 1 M sodium hydroxide solution and 100 \nivolumes of acetonitrile,\n' \nB. a mixture of300 volumes of a solution\nprepared by dissolving 3.0 g of anhydrous sodium \ndihydrogen phosphate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted to \nA: pH 6.0 with 1 Msodium hydroxide solution and 700 \n/ : volumes of acetonitrile.\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 2.3 ml per minute,,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\ninjection volume: 20 pl."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tazobactam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tazobactam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tazobactam\nC10H!2N4O5S . \nMol. Wt. 300.3\nClrtH12N;O5S,‘/2H2O ’ \nMoI.Wt.309T\nTazobactam is (2S,35,5/U-3-mcthyh7-oxo-3-(1//-l,2,3-triazok\nl-ylmethyl)-4“thia-l-azabicyclo[3.2.0]heptane-2-carboxylic \nacid,4,4-dioxide hemihydrate. \n. \n...\nTazobactam contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C|0Hi2N4OsS, calculated?bh\"the \nanhydrous basis. \n''•V.”.\";’. :\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor. \n■ ■F ' 4\nDescription. A white to pale;yellow, non-hygroscopic, \ncrystalline powder. ."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tazobactam\nC10H!2N4O5S . \nMol. Wt. 300.3\nClrtH12N;O5S,‘/2H2O ’ \nMoI.Wt.309T\nTazobactam is (2S,35,5/U-3-mcthyh7-oxo-3-(1//-l,2,3-triazok\nl-ylmethyl)-4“thia-l-azabicyclo[3.2.0]heptane-2-carboxylic \nacid,4,4-dioxide hemihydrate. \n. \n...\nTazobactam contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C|0Hi2N4OsS, calculated?bh\"the \nanhydrous basis. \n''•V.”.\";’. :\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor. \n■ ■F ' 4\nDescription. A white to pale;yellow, non-hygroscopic, \ncrystalline powder. ."},{"name":"Identification","text":"y\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry' (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum; with that .obtained with, tazobactam \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of tazobactam.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds, to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with, the reference; solution. . . \n;\nTests ■ ■■'■■■■ ■ ■ ' \n■' C'A.i-.? ■■ ruk y ;\npH, (2.4.24). 1.8 to 2.8, determined in a 0.25 per cent w/v solution\nin water. ’ , \n' \n\"\"f\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22).-+160.0° to+167®, determined \nat 20° in a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in dimethylformamide.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid- chromatography \n(2.4.14). . r . , .. \n. ; \n. , . .\nNOTE—Use freshly prepared solutions^ store anddnject al \n3°. \n.. \n.. ■ .. \n: \n; ■;\nTest solution. Use the test solution as given under Assay.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.0016-per cent w/v \nof L-phenylalaninei, 0.0050 per cent w/v of tazobactam 'IPRS \nand 0.0008 per cent w/v of tazobactam- related compound A \nIPRS [(2S, 3S)-2-amino-3-methyl-3-sul[)hino-4-(iII-l,2f-\ntriazol-l-yl)butyric acid]yn mobile phase. ; ... r\nUse the chromatographic conditions as described under\nAssay. \ni:;,?....-. ■.? vc y\nfl>2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tea-tree-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tea Tree Oil"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tea Tree Oil \na(o \ncapillary column 30 m x 0.25 mm, coated with macrogol \n2)\n(fa\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTea Tree oil is obtained by steam distillation of foliage and \nterminal branch- of Melaleuca alternifolia (Maiden and Betch)\nTest solution (a). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of the oil under \nexamination methanol (95 per cent).\ntemperature: \ncolumn. 50° for 1 minute, increase fror50' to-230° at \nrate of 5° per minute and maintain at this \n\t\nReference solution (a). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of Borneol \nfor 8 minutes, \t\nRS in ethanol (95 per cent). \n- \n1..\nii-nwiifolia Smith, M dissitiflora F. Mueller \n.• \n(Fani:Myrtaceae).\nTN-\nReference solution (b). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of\na-pinene RS in ethanol (95 per cent).\nChromatographic system\n-\na capillary column 30 m x 0.25 mm, coated with methyl \n5 per cent phenylpolysiloxane, \n-\ntemperature: \ncolumn. 50° for 1 minute, increase from 50° to 220° at a \nrate of 10° per minute and maintain at this temperature \nfor 13 minutes, \ninlet port at 250° and detector at 280°, \ndetection by flame ionization detector, \n-\nflow rate 1 ml per minute, using nitrogen as the carrier \ngas, \n-\ninjection volume: 1.0 \n-\nsplit ratio: 1 : 25.\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution.\nCalculate the contents of bomeol, and a-pinene in the oil \nusing area normalisation procedure.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Tea Tree Oil \na(o \ncapillary column 30 m x 0.25 mm, coated with macrogol \n2)\n(fa\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTea Tree oil is obtained by steam distillation of foliage and \nterminal branch- of Melaleuca alternifolia (Maiden and Betch)\nTest solution (a). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of the oil under \nexamination methanol (95 per cent).\ntemperature: \ncolumn. 50° for 1 minute, increase fror50' to-230° at \nrate of 5° per minute and maintain at this \n\t\nReference solution (a). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of Borneol \nfor 8 minutes, \t\nRS in ethanol (95 per cent). \n- \n1..\nii-nwiifolia Smith, M dissitiflora F. Mueller \n.• \n(Fani:Myrtaceae).\nTN-\nReference solution (b). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of\na-pinene RS in ethanol (95 per cent).\nChromatographic system\n-\na capillary column 30 m x 0.25 mm, coated with methyl \n5 per cent phenylpolysiloxane, \n-\ntemperature: \ncolumn. 50° for 1 minute, increase from 50° to 220° at a \nrate of 10° per minute and maintain at this temperature \nfor 13 minutes, \ninlet port at 250° and detector at 280°, \ndetection by flame ionization detector, \n-\nflow rate 1 ml per minute, using nitrogen as the carrier \ngas, \n-\ninjection volume: 1.0 \n-\nsplit ratio: 1 : 25.\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution.\nCalculate the contents of bomeol, and a-pinene in the oil \nusing area normalisation procedure.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99-ntc-ec-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99,nTc) EC Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Technetium (99,nTc) EC Injection\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Technetium (99mTc) \nEC Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated: \namount of technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time} \nstated on the label. Not less than 90 percent of the total \ntechnetium -99m is present as 99mTc-EC. Use thè Technetium \n(99mTc) EC Injection within 4 hours from the date and time of \npreparation. . .\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) EG \nInjection is prepared; aseptically following manufacturer’s: \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains diethyl: \nethylene .dicysteine, stannous chloride; and may contaiir \nantimicrobial agents and buffers. Sodium pertechnetate 99,llTc\nIP 2022"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Technetium (99,nTc) EC Injection\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Technetium (99mTc) \nEC Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated: \namount of technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time} \nstated on the label. Not less than 90 percent of the total \ntechnetium -99m is present as 99mTc-EC. Use thè Technetium \n(99mTc) EC Injection within 4 hours from the date and time of \npreparation. . .\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) EG \nInjection is prepared; aseptically following manufacturer’s: \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains diethyl: \nethylene .dicysteine, stannous chloride; and may contaiir \nantimicrobial agents and buffers. Sodium pertechnetate 99,llTc\nIP 2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99-rtc-succimer-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99:rTc) Succimer Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Technetium (99:rTc) Succimer Injection\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Technetium (99mTc) \nDMSA Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated \non the label. Not less than 95 percent of the total technetium - \n99m is present as 99mTc-DMSA. Use the Technetium (99tnTc) \nDMSA Injection within 4 hours from the date and time of \npreparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nSuccimer Injection is, prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer’s instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains a sterile solution of meso-2, 3-dimercaptosuccinic \nacid. It also contains a reducing substance, such as a tin salt \nand may contain stabilizers, antioxidants such as ascorbic \nacid, and inert additives. Sodium pertechnetate 99mTc (fission \nor.non-fission), complying with specification given for it, is \nused with the kit to make the Technetium (99inTc) DMSA \nInjection."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Technetium (99:rTc) Succimer Injection\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Technetium (99mTc) \nDMSA Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated \non the label. Not less than 95 percent of the total technetium - \n99m is present as 99mTc-DMSA. Use the Technetium (99tnTc) \nDMSA Injection within 4 hours from the date and time of \npreparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nSuccimer Injection is, prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer’s instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains a sterile solution of meso-2, 3-dimercaptosuccinic \nacid. It also contains a reducing substance, such as a tin salt \nand may contain stabilizers, antioxidants such as ascorbic \nacid, and inert additives. Sodium pertechnetate 99mTc (fission \nor.non-fission), complying with specification given for it, is \nused with the kit to make the Technetium (99inTc) DMSA \nInjection."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry. The most prominent\ngamma photon of Technetiumf 99nTc) has energy of 0.140 MeV\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of the Technetium \n(^Tc)DMSA.\nTests .\npH (2.4.24). 2.3 to 3.5.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of Test Solution\nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparation. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnetium-99m. \n'''' '■/'\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under, \nmonograph on Sodium Pertechnetate (99mTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate (99mTc) Injection (Non \nFission) used for preparation of Technetium (99mTc) DMSA \nInjection.\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by Method A or by Method \nB.\nMethod A. Determine by paper chromatography (2.4.15).\nSupport. Whatman chromatography paper No 3/No 12 strips \n(1 x8cm). ' \n' ' ' \n' ' '\nMobile phase. , acetone.\nSpot about two to five microlitres of \"mTc(III)-DMSA injection' \n(corresponding activity) with a capillary or a micropipette at\\ \nthe point of spotting marked at 1 cm from one end of the strip \n(1x8 cm) in duplicate. Develop the chromatogram by ascending \nchromatography in the solvents till the solvent front reaches \n6 cm from the point of spotting.\nCut the strip into 2 sections lower half (0-3 cm) and upper half \n(3-6 cm) and count in a well type scintillation counter. Per cent: \nof impurity free pertechnetate (Rf- upper half) is determined ! \nin the solvent, as follows.\nÏP 2022\nTECHNETIUM (^TC) OTPA INJECTION\nPer cent free pertechnetate = Activity in the 99mTc \npertechnetate zone x 100/Total Activity.\nThe percentage of activity in the 99mTc-pertechnetate zone \nshould not be more than 2 per cent of the total activity.\nThe radiochemical purity of 99mTc (III) DMSA(Rf=0) should \nnot be less than 95per cent.\n| Method B. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17),\n: \ncoating the plate with silica gel.\nj \n' \n'■■■ \" \n■■■ ' ■ \n\" - - \n'\nJ Mobile phase, methyl ethyl ketone.\n| Heat the plate at 110° C for 10 minutes. Use a plate such that\nduring development the mobile phase migrates over a distance \nof 10 cm to 15cm in about 10 minutes.\nj Apply to the plate 5 pl to 10 pl of the injection to be examined.\n1 \nAllow the mobile phase to rise 10 cm to 15 cm and dry the\n| \nplate. Determine the distribution of radioactivity using a\nI suitable detector. Technetium succimer complex remains at\nI the starting point. Pertechnetate ion migrates near to the \nI \nsolvent front. Not less than 95.0 per cent of the total\nj \nradioactivity is found in the spot corresponding to technetium\n1 succimer complex. The radioactivity corresponding to \npertechnetate ion represents not more than 2.0 per cent of the\n| Physiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \n| as described in : section 1.6 in general chapter on \n| radiopharmaceuticals. If not fewer than two of the three rats\ntotal radioactivity.\nused, the radioactivity in the kidneys is not less than 40 per \n| cent, that in the liver is not more than 10.0 per cent, that in the \n| stomach is not morethau2.0 per cent andthat in the lungs is\nnot more than 5.0 per cent of the injected dose at 1 hour post \n| \ninjection.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as described\n1 \nin general chapter .on Radiopharmaceutical preparation. The\n| injection may be released for use before completion of the \n| test. \n. ' . \n. ... \n'\nBacterial endotoxins (2:2.3). Less than 175 per VEU per ml, \nV being the maximumTecbmmehded dose in ml, alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8) in general \nchapter.\" ■ \n'\nStorage. Store at room temperature: with adequate shielding.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of 99mTc as labelled' \nsuccimer expressed as total mcgabecqucrels {or millicurics) \nand the radioactive concentration as mcgabecquerels per inl \n(or as millicuries per ml) on the date and time of reconstitution; \n(2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statement, /‘Caution -— \nRadioactive Material”. \na.ovgg\nTechnetium (\"“Tc) DTPA Injection\nTechnetium (99mTc) Pentetate Injection\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Technetium (99mTc) \nPentetate Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated technetiuny 99 m radioactivity at the date and time stated \non the label. Not less than 95.0 per cent of the total technetium- \n99m is present as Technetium (99mTc) Pentetate. Use the \nTechnetium (i!<)”Tc) Pentetate Injection within 4 hours from \nthe date and time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nPentetate Injection is prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer’s instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains di ethylenetriaminepentaacetic acid, stannous \nchloride and may contain antimicrobial agents and buffers. \nSodium pertechnetate \"\"Tc (fission or npn-fission), complying \nwith specification given for it, is used with the kit to make the \n(99m Tc) Pentetate Injection IP."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry. The most prominent \ngamma photon of technetium-99m has energy of 0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of the Technetium \n(99mTc) Pentetate."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 8.0.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of Test Solution\nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparation. It is expressed as \nMBq (mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnetium-99m.\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on Sodium Pertechnetate (99tnTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate (9yn’Tc) Injection (Non\nTECHNETIUM (99MTC) DTPA INJECTION \nIP 2022\nFission) usedforpreparation of Technctium(MirTc) Pentetate \nInjection. \n'\nRadiochemical purity . Use method A or method B.\nMethod A. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4:17), \nsilica gel G coated plates.\nMobile phase (a). A 0.9 pet cent w/v solution of sodium \nchloride in water. \n.\nMobile phase (b)..methyl ethyl ketone.\nTest solution. Injection under examination.\nHeat the plate at 110° for 10 minutes. Use a plate such that \nduring development the mobile phase migrates over a distance \nof 10 cm to 15 cminabout 10 minutes.\nUse two plates, one for mobile phase (a) and one for mobile \nphase (b). Spot about two to five microlitres of 99mTc- DTPA \nInjection with a capillary or a micropipette at the point of \nspotting marked at 3 cm from one end: of the TLC plate in \nduplicate. Develop the chromatogram by ascending \nchromatography in both the mobile phases (a) and (b) till the \nsolvent front reaches 10 - 15 cm from the point of spotting. \nAllow the plates to dry.\nScan the plates using a radiochromatogram scanner with its \ncollimator suitably adjusted to measure the distribution of\n99mTc; In TLC, with mobile phase (a), impurities in colloidal \nform remain at the starting point while Technetium DTPA \ncomplex and pertechnetate ions migrate near to the: solvent \nfront. With mobile phase (b), the radioactivity at the point of \nspotting gives the technetium DTPA ¿omplex and impurities \nin colloidal form while pertechnetate ions migrate to the \nsolvent front.\nThe sum of the percentages of radioactivity corresponding to \nimpurities in the chromatograms obtained in test (a) and (b) \ndoes not exceed 5.0 per cent.\nMethod B. Determine by Paper chromatography (2.4.15) with \nWhatman No l/No.3 paper.\nMobile phase (a). 0.9 per cent w/v solution of sodium chloride \nin wafer. \n3 : • \" : ’ ;\nMobile phase (b). methyl ethyl ketone.\nSpot about two to five microlitres of \"raTc- DTPA Injection \nwith' a capillary or a micropipette at the point of spotting marked \nat 3 cm from one end of the strip (3 X 23 cm) in duplicate. \nDevelop the chromatogram by ascending chromatography in \nboth the mobile phases (a);and (b) till the solventfront reaches\n15 cm from the point of spotting. Allow the strips to dry. ;\nScan the strips using a radiochromatogram scanner with its \ncollimator suitably adjusted to measure 99mTc; or alternately, \ncut the strip into one cm sections and count in a well type\nNal(Tl) scintillation counter, in paper chromatography with \nmobile phase (a), impurities in colloidal form remain at the \nstarting point while Technetium DTPA complex and \npertechnetate ions migrate near to the solvent front. With \nmobile phase (b), the radioactivity at the point of spotting \ngives the. hydrolysed technetium .and the technetium DTPA \ncomplex whereas, the pertechnetate impurity moyes to the \nsolvent front.\nThe sum of the percentages of radioactivity corresponding to \nimpurities in the chromatograms obtained in test (a) and (b) \ndoes not exceed 5.0 per cent.\nPhysiological distribution. To, be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in General, Chapter on \nRadiopharmaceuticals. The whole body radioactivity retention \nshould not be more than 5per cent of the injected dose in 24 \nhours post injection in 2 out of 3 mice. :\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as described\nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical. The injection may \nbe released for use before completion of the test.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8) in general \nchapter. - ■■ \n.\nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding:\nLabelling: The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of 99mTc as labelled \nPentetate expressed as total megabecquerels (or millicuries) \nand the radioactive concentration as megabecquerels per ml \n(or as millicuries per ml) on the date and time of reconstitution; \n(2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statement, '^Caution - \nRadioactive Material”. \n-"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99m","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium-99m"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Technetium-99m\n6.01 (1) hours\n: ce\n0.002\n74 .\nX 0.018-0.021\n• 73 -\n(99mTc)\neA\n0.015\n2.1\ny \n0.141\n89.1\nce\n0.120\n9.4\n0.137-0.140\n1.3 '\nTechnetium-99 (\"Tc) . 2.11 x 105 years\nß-\n0.085« (max: 0.294)\n100\nRuthenium-103 (t03Ru). 39.26 (2) days\neA\n0.017\n12\nX 0.020-0.023\n9.0\nin equilibrium with\nce\nO.O3O-O.O39\n88.3\ny \n0.497\n91\nRhodium-103m\n0.610\n5.8\n(103mRh)\nß~\n0.031«\n6.6\n(l03mRh: 56.114(20) min)\n0.064«\n92.2\nIndium-1.11 ('11 In) .\n2.8047 (5) days\neA\n0.019\n15.6\nX \n0.003\n6.9\n0.023-0,26\n82.3\n■■\n0.241-0.245\n1.0\nIodine-123 (123I)\n13.27 (8) hours\n. eA\n0.023\n12.3\nX \n0.004\n9.3\n0.027-0.031\n86.6\nce\n0.127\n13.6\n0.154\n1.8\nY \n0.159\n83.3\nIodine-124(l24I)\n4.18 days\nß4\n0.6870 (max: 1.5349)\n11.7\nIodine-125 (,25I)\n59.402 (14) days\n■ eA+ce\n0.004\n. 80\nX \n0.004\n0.023-0.035\n33\n0.027\n114\nIodine-126 (126I)\n12.93 (5) days\nce\nRange of energies\n51.62\nX 0.027-0.031\n42.2.........,\n: ß-\n0.111« (0.378)\n3.6\nY \n0.388'\n' \" 34 -\nElectronic emission\nPhoton emission\n, ce\n0.145\n7.8\n0.167-0.171\n1.3\nY \n0.171\n90.2\n0.219\n4.9\n0.245\n94.0\n0.158\nÖ.4\n0.346\n0.1\n0.440\n0.4\n0.505\n. 0.3.\n0.529\n1.4\n0.538\n0.4\nY \n0.511\n- 45*\n0.9747 (max: 2.1376)\n10.7\n0.6027\n62.9\n0.031\n26\nY \n0.035\n6.7, ;\n0.290« (0.869) ’\n33.4\n0.491\n2.9 5\n0:459« (1.258) '\n10.3\n0.666\n....... 33 .... ■\n■ 0.754.:\nKH-feyJ\n0.880\n0.8\n1.420\n0.3\n... ß+.\n0.469« (1.133)\n1.08\n0.511\n2.3(II)\nRADIOPHARMACEUTICAL PREPARATIONS\nIP 2022\nIodine-129 (I29I) ■ \n1.57 x 107 years\nß \n0.041 (max: 0.154)\n100 \nX \n0.02978 \n36.7\nIodine-131 (131I) \n8.02070 ( 11) days\nce 0.46\n3.5 \nX 0.029-0.030 \n3.9\nXenon-131m(131mXe) 11.84