s) and the \nradioactive concentration, as megabecquerels per ml (or as \nmillicuries per ml) on the date and time of reconstitution; \n(2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statement, “Caution- \nRadioactive Material”.\nTechnetium (99n,Tc) ECD Injection\no H o\n99mTc-Bicisate injection\nH3c o .y \\ ii / v xo ch3\nI [\"mTcl J H\n8 V\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Technetium (\"PTc) \nECD Injection is a sterile solution and contains not.less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time \nstated, on the : label. Not less than 90 percent of the total \ntechnetium-99m is present as 99nTc-ECD. Use the Technetium\nTECHNETIUM; (99MTC) ECD INJECTION\nIP 2022\n(99mTc) ECD Injection within 4 hours from the. date and.time of \npreparation. \n' : ; ? ■\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nBicisate Injection;.;is prepared aseptically, following \nmanufacturer’s instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains \n2”etliyienediyi)bis[(2X)-,2-aminQ-3.-\nsulphanylpropanoic acid] diethyl ester and reducing agents \nstich as a stannous salt and it may Contain stabilizers and \ninert additives such as mannitol and disodium edetate. Sodium \npertechnetate \"!r'Tc (fission or non-fission),; complying with \nspecification given for it, is used with the kit to make the \nTechnetium (<Wn‘Tc)ECD Injection."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry. The most prominent \ngamma photon of Technetium (99mTc) has energy of 0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of the Technetium \n(^Tc)ECD.\nTests ■\npH (2.4.24). 6.0 to 8.0.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of Test Solution \nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparation. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnetium-99m.\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on Sodium Pertechnetate (99mTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate (99mTc) Injection (Non \nFission) used for preparation of Technetium (99mTc) ECD \nInjection.\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by paper chromatography \n(2.4.15).\nSupport, chromatography paper Whatman No. 1/No. 3 strip \n(3 x 23 cm). ■ \n'■ \n;\nMobile phase, methanol.\nSpot about 2 to 5 microlitres of 99,'Tc ECD injection with a \ncapillary or a micropipette at the point of spotting marked at 3 \ncm from one end of the strip in duplicate: Develop the \nchromatogram by ascending chromatography till the solvent \nfront reaches 15 cm from the point of spotting. This takes \nabout 60 min. Allow the strip to dry. Scan the strip using a \nradiochromafogram scanner with collimator adjusted for^Tc \n(0.140MeV) or cut into one cm sections and count in a well \ntypeNaI(Tl) scintillation counter. The Rt values of99niTc-ECD,\n99mTc-pertechnetate and 99mTc -reduced, hydrolyzed \ntechnetium are 0.5- 1.0, 0.2 - 0.4 and 0 - 0.1 respectively. \nCalculate the per cent of activity present as 99mTc-ECD, as \nfollows: \n; ;\n, \nActivity in the [99mTc] ECD zone x 100\n(per cent) RCP = —=----- ------=—-------- ----------\n'■--------—\nTotal Activity\nThe Radiochemical Purity should not be less than 90per cent.\nSterility (2.2.11) Complies with the test for sterility as described \nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparation. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3) Less than 175 per V EU per ml, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml, alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8) in general \nchapter.\nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of <WinTc as labelled \nECD expressed as total megabecquerels (or millicuries) and \nthe radioactive concentration as megabecquerels per ml (or as \nmillicuries per ml) on the date and time of reconstitution; \n(2).the. expiration date and time; (3) the statement, “Caution— \nRadioactive Material”.\nTechnetium (99mTc) Exametazime \nInjection"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-exametazime-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Exametazime Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Exametazime \nInjection\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnetium-99m.\nRadioactive Material\".\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on Sodium Pertechnetate ( 99mTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate ( 99mTc) Injection (Non \nFission) used for preparation of Technetium ( 99mTc) ECD \nInjection.\nTechnetium (99\"'Tc ) HM PAO Complex Injection\nH C \n,CH3\nTechnetium (99mTc) ECD Injection\n\"mTc-Bicisate injection\nH3V\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by paper chromatography \n(2.4.15).\nNI 0 /N,F1\nH- \t\n[99m-rcl \t\n'CH 3\nand enantiomer\n, \nN 0 NH \n \nII,' \n[99mTc]\nH 3C \t\n\"r. \t\nN \t\nc1-1 3 \nO \t\n.6 \nH\nSupport. chromatography paper Whatman No. 1/ No. 3 strip \n(3 x 23 cm).\nH 3C\nH \nC H 3\nMobile phase. methanol.\nS \t\nS/\nCategory. Diagnostic\nSpot about 2 to 5 microlitres of 99mTc ECD injection with a \ncapillary or a micropipette at the point of spotting marked at 3 \ncm from one end of the strip in duplicate. Develop the \nchromatogram by ascending chromatography till the solvent \nfront reaches 15 cm from the point of spotting. This takes \nabout 60 min. Allow the strip to dry. Scan the strip using a \nradiochromatogram scanner with collimator adStod-for 9`'\"'Tc \n(0.140MeV) or cut into one cm sections and cOntin a well \ntype NaI(T1) scintillation counter. The R I- valueS'of 99mTc-ECD,\nDescription. Technetium (99mTc) Exametazime Injection is a \nclear, colourless aqueous solution. It contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the content \nof technetium-99m stated on the label at the reference date \nand time. Not less than 80.0 per cent of the total technetium-\n99'pradicragtiiiity is present as lipophi 1 ic ( 99mTc) exametazime \ncomplex. Teditietium ( 99mTc) Exametazime Injection should be \nused withiri 30 minutes from time of reconstitution.\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nECD Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time \nstated: cm the label. Not less than 90 percent of the total\nte .chfietium-99m is present as 99mTc-ECD. Use the Technetium\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nExametazime Injection is prepared aseptically following \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains a mixture \nof rac-N, N'-bis [(I R, 2E)-2-(hydroxyimino)-1-methylpropy1]- \n2, 2-dimethylpropane-1, 3-diamine, reducing agents and may \ncontain buffer, stabilizing agents and sodium chloride to make \nthe solution isotonic with blood. Sodium pertechnetate (\"mTc) \ninjection (fission or non -fission) is used for preparation of \nTechnetium ( 99mTc) Exametazime Injection."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Technetium (99mTc) Exametazime \nInjection\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnetium-99m.\nRadioactive Material\".\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on Sodium Pertechnetate ( 99mTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate ( 99mTc) Injection (Non \nFission) used for preparation of Technetium ( 99mTc) ECD \nInjection.\nTechnetium (99\"'Tc ) HM PAO Complex Injection\nH C \n,CH3\nTechnetium (99mTc) ECD Injection\n\"mTc-Bicisate injection\nH3V\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by paper chromatography \n(2.4.15).\nNI 0 /N,F1\nH- \t\n[99m-rcl \t\n'CH 3\nand enantiomer\n, \nN 0 NH \n \nII,' \n[99mTc]\nH 3C \t\n\"r. \t\nN \t\nc1-1 3 \nO \t\n.6 \nH\nSupport. chromatography paper Whatman No. 1/ No. 3 strip \n(3 x 23 cm).\nH 3C\nH \nC H 3\nMobile phase. methanol.\nS \t\nS/\nCategory. Diagnostic\nSpot about 2 to 5 microlitres of 99mTc ECD injection with a \ncapillary or a micropipette at the point of spotting marked at 3 \ncm from one end of the strip in duplicate. Develop the \nchromatogram by ascending chromatography till the solvent \nfront reaches 15 cm from the point of spotting. This takes \nabout 60 min. Allow the strip to dry. Scan the strip using a \nradiochromatogram scanner with collimator adStod-for 9`'\"'Tc \n(0.140MeV) or cut into one cm sections and cOntin a well \ntype NaI(T1) scintillation counter. The R I- valueS'of 99mTc-ECD,\nDescription. Technetium (99mTc) Exametazime Injection is a \nclear, colourless aqueous solution. It contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the content \nof technetium-99m stated on the label at the reference date \nand time. Not less than 80.0 per cent of the total technetium-\n99'pradicragtiiiity is present as lipophi 1 ic ( 99mTc) exametazime \ncomplex. Teditietium ( 99mTc) Exametazime Injection should be \nused withiri 30 minutes from time of reconstitution.\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nECD Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time \nstated: cm the label. Not less than 90 percent of the total\nte .chfietium-99m is present as 99mTc-ECD. Use the Technetium\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nExametazime Injection is prepared aseptically following \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains a mixture \nof rac-N, N'-bis [(I R, 2E)-2-(hydroxyimino)-1-methylpropy1]- \n2, 2-dimethylpropane-1, 3-diamine, reducing agents and may \ncontain buffer, stabilizing agents and sodium chloride to make \nthe solution isotonic with blood. Sodium pertechnetate (\"mTc) \ninjection (fission or non -fission) is used for preparation of \nTechnetium ( 99mTc) Exametazime Injection."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry as described in \ngeneral chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \nmost prominent gamma photon of technetium-99m has energy \nof0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of technetium (\"mTc) \nexametazime."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 10.0.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of test solution\nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparations. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnetium-99m. Complies with the radionuclidic purity tests \nas described under monograph of sodium pertechnetate \n(99\"Tc) injection (fission) or sodium pertechnetate ( 99mTc) \ninjection (non -fission) used for preparation of technetium \n(99mTc) exametazime.\nRadiochemical purity. Determine \t\nascending paper \nchromatography (2.4.15).\nTest solution. The injection under examination.\nMobile phase. Methyl ethyl ketone (system A), 0.9 per cent \nsodium chloride (system B) and a mixture of 50 volumes of \nacetonitrile and 50 volumes of water (system C).\nPaper. Cellulose paper strips (system A and system B), silica \ngel impregnated glass fiber paper strips (system C).\nTECHNETIUM (99MTC) EXAMETAZIME INJECTION \t\nIP 2018 \t\nII' 2018\nTECHNETIUM ( 99MTC) HYNIC-TOC INJECTION\nfrom the date and time of preparation.\nexametazime complex, the secondary exametazime complex and \nthe pertechnetate ion have an Rf value of 0.8 to 1.0. In system \n(C), the pertechnetate ion has an Rf value of 0.8 to 1.0, and the \nlipophilic exametazime complex, the secondary exametazime \ncomplex and reduced / hydrolysed technetium-99m have an\n(per cent) free pertechnetate  =  (Activity in therm Tc]  pertechnetate zone x 100)\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nGlucoheptonate Injection is prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer's instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains sodium gluceptate, stannous chloride and may \ncontain antimicrobial agents and buffers. Sodium \npertechnetate 99mTc. (fission or non-fission), complying with \nspecification given for it, is used with the kit to make the"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-glucoheptonate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Glucoheptonate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TECHNETIUM (99MTC) GLUCOHEPTONATE INJECTION\nIP 2022'\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nExametazime Injection is prepared aseptically following \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains a mixture \nof rac-N,N -bis [(17?, 2E)-2-(hydroxyimino)-l-methylpropyl]- \n2,2-dimethylpropane-l, 3-diamine, reducing agents and may \ncontain buffer, stabilizing agents and sodium chloride to make \nthe solution isotonic with blood. Sodium pertechnetate (\"mTcj-\ninjection, (fission or non-fissjon) is used for preparation of \nTechnetium (99nTcj Exametazime Injection.’"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TECHNETIUM (99MTC) GLUCOHEPTONATE INJECTION\nIP 2022'\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nExametazime Injection is prepared aseptically following \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains a mixture \nof rac-N,N -bis [(17?, 2E)-2-(hydroxyimino)-l-methylpropyl]- \n2,2-dimethylpropane-l, 3-diamine, reducing agents and may \ncontain buffer, stabilizing agents and sodium chloride to make \nthe solution isotonic with blood. Sodium pertechnetate (\"mTcj-\ninjection, (fission or non-fissjon) is used for preparation of \nTechnetium (99nTcj Exametazime Injection.’"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry as described in \ngeneral chapter bn Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \nmost prominent gamma photon of technetium-99m has energy \nof0J40MeV. '■ iC \n\"■\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of technetium (99mTc) \nexametazime. /__ . \\.................. ."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH(2.4.24).5.0tol0T \n, ’/ \n’ \n/ J ’\nRadioactivity assay, Radioactivityof an aliquot of test solution \nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described m. measurement of radioactiyity.ingeneralphapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparations. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V m! at a particular time and date. : ;\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per,cent due to \nTcchnetiuni-99m. Complies with the radionuclidic purity tests \nas described under monograph of sodium pertechnetate \n(\"Me). injection (fission) or sodium pertechnetate (99”’Tc) \ninjection (non-fission) used for preparation of. technetium \n(\"■\"Tc) exametazime.\nRadiochemicaLpurity. Determine by ascending paper \nchromatography (2.4.T5). \n■\nTest solution. The injection under examination. ■;; \n<\nMobile phase. Methyl ethyl ketone (system A), 0.9 per cent \nsodium chloride (system B) and a mixture of 50 volumes of \nacetonitrile and 50 volumes of water (system C).\nPaper. Cellulose paper strips (system A and system B), silica\ngel impregnated glass fiber paper strips (system C).\nApply about 5pl of the injection to the suitable paper strip \nunder examination, suitably diluted to give an optimum \nradioactive count rate. Allow the mobile phase to rise 15 cm \nand air dry the paper strip. Determine the distribution of \nradioactivity using a suitable method. In system (A), the \nsecondary exametazime complex and reduced/hydrolysed \ntechnetium-99m have an Rf value of 0, and the lipophilic \nexametazime complex and the pertechhetate ion have an Rt \nvalue of 0.8 to 1.0. In system (B), reduced I hydrolysed \ntechnetium-99m has an Rf value of 0, and the lipophilic\nexametazime complex, the secondary exametazime complex and \nthe pertechnetate ion have an Rf value of 0.8 to 1.0. In system \n(C), the pertechnetate ion has an Rf value of 0.8 to 1.0, and the \nlipophilic exametazime complex, the secondary exametazime \ncomplex and reduced I hydrolysed technetium-99m have an \nRevalue of 0. The sum of the percentages of radioactivity \ncorresponding to the pertechnetate ion in system (C) and \nreduced / hydrolysed technetium-99m in system (B) is less \nthan 10.0 per cent. Not less than 80.0 per cent of the total \ntechnetium-99m radioactivity is present as lipophilic \nexametazime complex.\nPhysiological distribution. To be, done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in general chapter on \nRadiopharmaceutical preparations using a set of three mice \neach weighing 20 g to 25 g. After 5 tó 10 minutes of the injection, \nthe activity in brain should not be less than 1.5 per cent of the \ninjected radioactivity; the activity in the intestine should not \nbe more than 20.0 per cent of the injected radioactivity and the \nactivity in the liver should not be more than 45.0 per cent of \nthe injected radioactivity.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test, for sterility as \ndescribed in general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical \npreparations. The injection may be released for use before \ncompletion of the test. \n. . p\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per VEUper ml, V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8).\nStorage. Store at a temperature between 2°to 8°with adequate \nshielding.;..\nLabeling. The label states, in addition to; thè dnformatiori \nspecified under injections (1) the amount of 99mTc as labelled \nexametazime complex expressed as total .MBq (mCi or pCi) \nand concentration as MBq (mCi or pCi) per ml at the time of \ncalibration; (2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statement \n“Caution—Radioactive Material. ”J \" * ' L ' ' \n' \" 'T\";\nTechnetium (99mTc) Glucoheptonate \nInjection \nwy\nTechnetium (99mTc) Gluceptate Injection; Technetium \n(99mTc) GHA Injection \n7\nCategory.Diagnostic. \n-v■;\nDescription. A clear colourless solution. Technetium (99mTc) \nGluceptate Injection is a sterile solution arid contains riot less \nthan 90.0 per cent and riot more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated amount of technetium 99m radioactivity at the time and \ndate indicated on the label. Not less thari 90 percent of the \ntotal technetium-99m is presentas y9mTc-Glucoheptonate. Use \nthe Technetium (99mTc) Gluceptate lnjection within ó hours \nfrom the date and time of preparation.\nIP 2022;\nTECHNETIUM (WMTC) GLUCOHEPTONATE INJECTION\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99,’Tc) \nGlucoheptonate Injection? is prepared; aseptically ¡following \nmanufacturer’s instructions; provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains sodium gluceptate, stannou^- chloride and; may \ncontain antimicrobial: agents and buffers.: Sodium \npertechnetate 99rt!Tc (fission or non-fission), complying with \nspecification given for it, is used with the kit to make the \nTephnetium (99raTc) ¡Glucoheptonate. Injection. < \n. <"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry The most prominent\ngamma photon of Technetium (\"mTc) has energy of0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of the Technetium \n(99mTc) Glucoheptonate.\nTests'\"'\npH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 8.0.\nRadioactivity Assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of Test \nSolution is assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose \ncalibrator as described in measurement of radioactivity. It is \nexpressed as MBq (mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time \nand date.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due: to \nTechnctium-99m.\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described tinder \nmonograph bri'Sodium Pertechnetate (99mTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate (99tnTc) Injection (Non \nFission) used for preparation of Technetium (99™Tc) Gluceptate\nInjection. \n; ■;\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by paper chromatography \n(2.4.15). \n■ \n■■■\nSupport. Whatman chromatography paper No.l/No.3; 4 strips \n(3x23 cm)\nMobile phase. 85 per cent methanol in 0.9 per cent w/v of \nsodium chloride solution in WFI.: \n: \n;\nSpot about two to five microlitres of 99mTc-Glucpheptonate \nInjection with a capillary or a micropipette at the point of \nspotting marked at 3 cm from one end of the strip in duplicate.\nDevelop the chromatogram by ascending chromatography in \nboth the solvents till the solvent front reaches 15 cm from the \npoint of spotting.; Allow: the strips to dry. Scan the strips\nusing a;radiochromatogram scanner with collimator suitably \nadjusted to measure 99nTc or .cut:into one cm sections and \ncount in a well type Nal(Tl) scintillation counter.¡Percentage \nof impurities, vizi free pertechnetate and reduced hydrolysed \ntechnetium are determined in the solvents, viz. . 85 per cent \nmethanol (Rf 0.55- 0.75) and saline (Rf 0.0-0.1) respectively as \nfollows:\nr \n. (Activity in the 15,'\"Tc] pertechnetate zone * 100)\n(percent) free pertechnetate - -—:——--------------—------::-,\nTotal Activity\n< G / \n:■ \n= .?■- SGJ . \n;V..' j ., ,■ , ■■ ,Gi\n: \nS.-, \n■ ; \n■ '■ > \n.. Total ACtlVÎty : \nO\n(percent) reduced hydrolysed technetium (RHT) - \n-■\nper cent Radiochemical Purity = 100 - [(per cent) free \npertechnetate + (per cent) reduced hydrolyzed technetium].\nThé Radiochemical Purity’should not be less than 90per cent.'\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in general chapter on \nradiopharmaceuticals. Activity in kidneys should not be less \nthan 15per cent, Blood should not be more than 3 per cent, \nGIT should not be more than 15per cent, Liver should not be \nmore than 5per cent injected dose in 2 out of 3 rats at 1 hour \npost injection.\nSterility (2.2. IT). Complies with the test for sterility as \ndescribed in general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical \npreparation. The injection may be released for use before \ncompletion of the test. \ny\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than H5 per V EU per \nml of the injection, in which V is the maximum recommended \ntotal dose in ml: Alternatively the preparation should pass the \npyrogen test (2.2.8) in general chapter. \n<>'\nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of 99mTc as labelled \nglucoheptonate expressed a's total megabecquercls (or \nmillicuries) and the radioactive concentration as \nmegabecquerels per nil (or as millicuries per ml) on the date \nand time of reconstitution; (2) the expiration date and time;1 \n(3) the statement, “Caution — Radioactive Material’^ . !\nTechnetium (”“Tc) HYMC-TOC if\nInjection\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nIP 2022\nTECHNETIUM («^TC) HYNIC-TOC INJECTION\nDescription. Technetium (99raTc) HYNIC-TOC Injection is a \nclear, colourless sterilc solution and contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \ntechnetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated on \nthe label. Not less than 90.0 per cent of the total technetium- \n99m is present as Technetium (\"\"Tc) HYNIC-TOC. Technetium \n(99mTc) HYNIC-TOC Injection should be used within 6 hours \nfrom the time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (\"\"Tc) HYNIC- \nTOC Injection is prepared aseptically following manufacturer’s \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains \nhydrazinonicptinyl-tyr3-octreotide (HYNIC-TOC), stannous \nchloride and may contain coligands such as disodium ethylene \ndiamine tetracetic acid, tricine, antimicrobial agents and buffers. \nSodium pertechnetate (\"n!Tc ) injection (fission or non­\nfission) is used with the kit to make the (99m Tc) HYNIC-TOC\ninjection.' ■"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A’. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry as described in \ngeneral chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \nmost prominent gamma photon of technetium~99m has energy \nof0.140McV.\nB. In the test for radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of the technetium \nC^Tc) HYNIC-TOC.\nTests . ..\npH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 8.0. \n, \n/ \n‘\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of testsolution\nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparations. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date, V being the \nmaximum recommended dose in ml.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \ntechnetium-99m.\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on sodium pertechnetate fi9mTc) injection \n(fission) or sodium pertechnetate fi9mTc) injection (non- \nfission) used for preparation of technetium (WmTc) HYNIC-\nTOC injection.\nRadiochemical purity. Use method A or method B.\nMethod A. Determine by instant thin layer chromatography \n(2.4.17).' •;\nMobile phase (a). Methyl ethyl ketone.\nMobile phase (b). A mixture of 50 volumes1 of acetonitrile \nand 50 volumes of water. : r :\nTest solution. The injection under examination.\n(a) Apply to the plate 5 pl to 10 pl of the injection under \nexamination. Allow the mobile phase (a) to rise 10 cm to 15 cm \nin about 10 minutes and dry the plate in air. Determine the \ndistribution of radioactivity using a suitable detector. \nPertechnetate ion migrates near to the solvent front. \nTechnetium HYNIC-TOC complex and impurities of hydrolysed \ntechnetium in colloidal form remain at the point of spotting.\n(b) Apply to the plate 5 pl to 10. pl of the injection under \nexamination. Allow the mobile phase (b) to rise 10 cm to 15 cm \nand dry the plate. Determine the distribution of radioactivity \nusing a suitable detector. Technetium HYNIC-TOC complex \nand pertechnetate ion migrate near to the solvent- front. \nImpurities of hydrolysed technetium in colloidal form remain \nat the point of spotting.\nThe sum of the percentages of radioactivity corresponding to \nimpurities in the chromatograms obtained in test solutions (a) \nand (b) should not be more than 10.0 per cent.\nMethod B. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. The injection under examination.\nChromatographic, system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. 0.1 per cent trifluoroacetic acid in\nacetonitrile,\nB. 0.1 per cent trifluoroacetic acid in \nwater,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\nradioactivity detector adjusted for 100 to 200 KeV energy, \n- injection volume: 20 pl\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent y/v)\n0\n0\n100\n3\n0\n100\n■5 i\n‘ 30 \n;\n■ 70\n18\n30\n70\n22\n50\n50\n24\n50\n50\n25\n50\n50\n30\n0 ■\n100\nThe 99nTc-HYNIC-TOC shall have a retention time of 17-20 \nminutes: The sum of the percentage of radioactivity \ncorresponding to impurities in the chromatograms obtained \nshould not more than 10.0 per cent.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in general chapter on\n4773\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-hynic-toc-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Hynic-TOC Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) HYNIC-TOC \nInjection\nSupport. Whatman chromatography paper No. l/No.3; 4 strips \n(3 x 23 cm)\nMobile phase. 85 per cent methanol in 0.9 per cent w/v of \nsodium chloride solution in WFI.\nTechnetium (99mTc) Glucoheptonate \nInjection\nOCT\nSpot about two to five microlitres of \"mTc-Glucoheptonate \nInjection with a capillary or a micropipette at the point of \nspotting marked at 3 cm from one end of the strip in duplicate.\nTechnetium ( 9%1Tc) Gluceptate Injection; Technetium \n(99mTc) GHA Injection\nDevelop the chromatogram by ascending chromatography in \nboth the solvents till the solvent front reaches 15 cm from the \npoint of spotting. Allow the strips to dry. Scan the strips \nusing a radiochromatogram scanner with collimator suitably \nadjusted to measure \"mTc or cut into one cm sections and \ncount in a well type Nal(T1) scintillation counter. Percentage \nof impurities viz. free pertechnetate and reduced hydrolysed \ntechnetium are determined in the solvents. -i(\n.85'need6nt; \nmethanol (Rf 0.55-- 0.75) and saline (R f \nfollows:\nApply about 5111 of the injection to the suitable paper strip \nunder examination, suitably diluted to give an optimum \nradioactive count rate. Allow the mobile phase to rise 15 cm \nand air dry the paper strip. Determine the distribution of Description. A clear colourless solution. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nradioactivity using a suitable method. In system (A), the Gluceptate Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less \nsecondary exametazime complex and reduced/hydrolysed than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \ntechnetium-99m have an Rf value of 0, and the lipophilic stated amount of technetium 99m radioactivity at the time and \nexametazime complex and the pertechnetatejolihaxe n R date-ifidicated on the label. Not less than 90 percent of the \nvalue of 0.8 to 1.0. In system (B), reduciiid..chydrOlyspd: ctiiial.t -Ohnetiiim-99m is present as 99mTc-Glucoheptonate. Use\nNH\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nO \n0, 00 \n:Tc, \nN\nOH\nHO \t\n0\nOH\nHO H\ntechnetium-99m has an Rf value of 0, anitihe lipophilic \nthe .fechnetium (\"mTc) Gluceptate Injection within 6 hours\n'agnostic.\nDescription. Technetium ( 99mTc) HYNIC-TOC Injection is a \nclear, colourless sterile solution and contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \ntechnetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated on \nthe label. Not less than 90.0 per cent of the total technetium-\n99m is present as Technetium ( 99mTc) HYNIC-TOC. Technetium \n(99mTc) HYNIC-TOC Injection should be used within 6 hours \nfrom the time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) HYNIC-\nTOC Injection is prepared aseptically following manufacturer's\ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains \nhydrazinonicotinyl-tyr'-octreotide (HYNIC-TOC), stannous \nchloride and may contain coligands such as disodium ethylene \ndiamine tetracetic acid, tricine, antimicrobial agents and buffers. \nSodium pertechnetate ( 99mTc ) injection (fission or non-\nfission) is used with the kit to make the (99m  Tc) HYNIC-TOC\ninjection."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Technetium (99mTc) HYNIC-TOC \nInjection\nSupport. Whatman chromatography paper No. l/No.3; 4 strips \n(3 x 23 cm)\nMobile phase. 85 per cent methanol in 0.9 per cent w/v of \nsodium chloride solution in WFI.\nTechnetium (99mTc) Glucoheptonate \nInjection\nOCT\nSpot about two to five microlitres of \"mTc-Glucoheptonate \nInjection with a capillary or a micropipette at the point of \nspotting marked at 3 cm from one end of the strip in duplicate.\nTechnetium ( 9%1Tc) Gluceptate Injection; Technetium \n(99mTc) GHA Injection\nDevelop the chromatogram by ascending chromatography in \nboth the solvents till the solvent front reaches 15 cm from the \npoint of spotting. Allow the strips to dry. Scan the strips \nusing a radiochromatogram scanner with collimator suitably \nadjusted to measure \"mTc or cut into one cm sections and \ncount in a well type Nal(T1) scintillation counter. Percentage \nof impurities viz. free pertechnetate and reduced hydrolysed \ntechnetium are determined in the solvents. -i(\n.85'need6nt; \nmethanol (Rf 0.55-- 0.75) and saline (R f \nfollows:\nApply about 5111 of the injection to the suitable paper strip \nunder examination, suitably diluted to give an optimum \nradioactive count rate. Allow the mobile phase to rise 15 cm \nand air dry the paper strip. Determine the distribution of Description. A clear colourless solution. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nradioactivity using a suitable method. In system (A), the Gluceptate Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less \nsecondary exametazime complex and reduced/hydrolysed than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \ntechnetium-99m have an Rf value of 0, and the lipophilic stated amount of technetium 99m radioactivity at the time and \nexametazime complex and the pertechnetatejolihaxe n R date-ifidicated on the label. Not less than 90 percent of the \nvalue of 0.8 to 1.0. In system (B), reduciiid..chydrOlyspd: ctiiial.t -Ohnetiiim-99m is present as 99mTc-Glucoheptonate. Use\nNH\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nO \n0, 00 \n:Tc, \nN\nOH\nHO \t\n0\nOH\nHO H\ntechnetium-99m has an Rf value of 0, anitihe lipophilic \nthe .fechnetium (\"mTc) Gluceptate Injection within 6 hours\n'agnostic.\nDescription. Technetium ( 99mTc) HYNIC-TOC Injection is a \nclear, colourless sterile solution and contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \ntechnetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated on \nthe label. Not less than 90.0 per cent of the total technetium-\n99m is present as Technetium ( 99mTc) HYNIC-TOC. Technetium \n(99mTc) HYNIC-TOC Injection should be used within 6 hours \nfrom the time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) HYNIC-\nTOC Injection is prepared aseptically following manufacturer's\ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains \nhydrazinonicotinyl-tyr'-octreotide (HYNIC-TOC), stannous \nchloride and may contain coligands such as disodium ethylene \ndiamine tetracetic acid, tricine, antimicrobial agents and buffers. \nSodium pertechnetate ( 99mTc ) injection (fission or non-\nfission) is used with the kit to make the (99m  Tc) HYNIC-TOC\ninjection."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry as described in \ngeneral chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \nmost prominent gamma photon of technetium-99m has energy \nof0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of the technetium \n(99mTc) HYN IC-TOC."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 8.0.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of test solution \nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparations. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date, V being the \nmaximum recommended dose in ml.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \ntechnetium-99m.\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on sodium pertechnetate (99mTc) injection \n(fission) or sodium pertechnetate (99mTc) injection (non-\nfission) used for preparation of technetium ( 99mTc) HYNIC-\nTOC injection.\nRadiochemical purity. Use method A or method B.\nMethod A. Determine by instant thin layer chromatography \n(2.4.17).\nMobile phase (a). Methyl ethyl ketone.\n50 volumei ot,i3c.etonitrite \n•...\nMobile phase (b). A mixture of \nand 50 volumes of water\n•\n_4'\nTECHNETIUM (99MTC) HYNIC-TOC INJECTION \t\nIP 2018 \t\n11) 2018 \t\nTECHNETIUM (99\"TC) LABELLED HUMAN SERUM ALBUMIN NANOCOLLOID INJECTION\nRadiopharmaceutical preparations. After 4hour of the injection, \nthe whole body radioactivity retention shall not be more than\nTest solution. The injection under examination.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of test solution \nis assayed using a pre calibrated dose calibrator. It is expressed \nas MBq (mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml.\n(a) Apply to the plate 5 ul to 10 ul of the injection under \nexamination. Allow the mobile phase (a) to rise 10 cm to 15 cm \nin about 10 minutes and dry the plate in air. Determine the \ndistribution of radioactivity using a suitable detector. \nPertechnetate ion migrates near to the solvent front. \nTechnetium HYNIC-TOC complex and impurities of hydrolysed \ntechnetium in colloidal form remain at the point of spotting.\n10.0 per cent of the injected dose in 2 out of 3 swiss mice.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as described \nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to\nTechnetium-99m.\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on Sodium Pertechnetate ('9mTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate (99mTc) Injection \n(Non Fission) used for preparation of\"mTc-HSA-NC Injection.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen tests (2.2.8.).\n(b) Apply to the plate 5 gl to 10 ul of the injection under \nexamination. Allow the mobile phase (b) to rise 10 cm to 15 cm \nand dry the plate. Determine the distribution of radioactivity \nusing a suitable detector. Technetium HYNIC-TOC complex \nand pertechnetate ion migrate near to the solvent front. \nImpurities of hydrolysed technetium in colloidal form remain \nat the point of spotting.\nStorage. Store at 2° to 8° with adequate shielding.\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by paper chromatography \n(2.4.15)\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of 99mTc as labelled \nHYNIC-TOC expressed as total MBq (or mCi) and the \nradioactive concentration as MBq per ml (or as mCi per ml) on \nthe date and time of reconstitution; (2) the expiration date and \ntime; (3) the statement, \"Caution - Radioactive Material.\"\nSupport. Chromatography paper Whatman paper No. 1 or \nequivalent. (3 cm x 15 cm)\nThe sum of the percentages of radioactivity corresponding to \nimpurities in the chromatograms obtained in test solutions (a) \nand (b) should not be more than 10.0 per cent.\nMobile phase. 0.9 per cent sodium chloride solution.\nApply 5 to 101.11 of the injection under examination 3 cm from \nbottom end of the chromatography strip. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 10 cm and dry the strip in air. Determine the \ndistribution of radioactivity using a suitable radioactivity \ncounting system. The free pertechnetate (Tc-99m) ion exhibits \nR, in the range 0.8 to 1.0 whereas 99mTc-HSA-NC remains at \nthe point of application (R f 0.1) . The percentage radioactivity \n(at Rf 0.8 to 1.0) should not more than 5 per cent.\nMethod B. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. The injection under examination.\nTechnetium ( 99mTc) Labelled Human \nSerum .Albumin Nanocolloid Injection\nChromatographic system\n— a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to silica (5 um), \n— mobile phase: A. 0.1 per cent trifluoroacetic acid in\n(99mTc-HSA-NC Injection)\nCategory: Diagnostic.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in General Chapter on \nRadiopharmaceutical preparations by using Wistar rats. The \nanimals are administered 50µl volume of the 99mTc-HSA-NC \nInjection subcutaneously to the footpad of the animal under \nanaesthesia. At 2 hour post injection the 99mTc-activity in \nrelevant organs and tissues are measured using suitable \nradioactivity counter. 99mTc-activity in the sentinel node should \nnot be less than 1.5 per cent and 'Tc-activity in popliteal \nnode indicates sentinel lymph node accumulation and activity \nin upstream nodes (iliac, inguinal) and other regions indicates \nfurther spread of the preparation through the lymphatic stream. \nNot more than 1.0 per cent of injected dose should be present \nin blood, not more than 1 per cent of injected dose should be \npresent in liver and not more than 1 per cent of injected dose \nshould be present in kidney in two out of three rats.\nacetonitrile,\nB. 0.1 per cent trifluoroacetic acid in \nwater, \n— a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n— flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n— radioactivity detector adjusted for 100 to 200 KeV energy, \n— injection volume: 20 tl\nDescription. 99mTc-HSA-NC Injection is a clear, colourless \n"},{"name":"Identification","text":"24 \n50 \n50\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as described \nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest.\n25 \n50 \n50\nA. Determine by gamma ray spectrometry using a gamma ray \nspectrometer. The prominent gamma photon of technetium-\n99m has energy of 0.140 MeV.\n30 \n0 \n100\nThe '''''Tc-HYNIC-TOC shall have a retention time of 17-20 \nminutes. The sum of the percentage of radioactivity \ncorresponding to impurities in the chromatograms obtained \nshould not more than 10.0 per cent.\nB. In the test for radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of \"Tc-HSA-NC.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe prguratiop, should pass the pyrogen tests (2.2.8.)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Physiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in general chapter on\nInj7... on may be stored at room temperature with \n4uate shielding.\npH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 6.0.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of \"mTc as labelled \nHSA-NC expressed as total megabecquerels (or millicuries) \nand the radioactive concentration as megabecquerels per ml \n(or as millicuries per ml) on the date and time of reconstitution; \n(2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statement, \"Caution -\nRadioactive Material.\"\nTechnetium ( 99n1Tc) Macrosalb\nInjection\nCategory. Diagnostic\nDescription. Technetium (99mTc) Macrosalb Injection is a \nwhite, sterile suspension of human albumin in the form of \nirregular insoluble aggregates obtained by denaturing human \nalbumin in aqueous solution. The particles are labelled with \ntechnetium-99m and have a typical diameter between 10 pm \nand 100 gm. Technetium (99rnTc) Macrosalb Injection contains \nnot less than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of \nthe stated technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time \nstated on the label. The human albumin employed complies \nwith the requirements prescribed in the monograph human \nalbumin. The Technetium ( 99mTc) Macrosalb Injection should \nbe used within 4 hours from date and time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nMacrosalb Injection is prepared aseptically following \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains human \nalbumin and other excipients such as suitable buffer, reducing \nagents and an antimicrobial preservative. Sodium \npertechnetate (99mTc) injection (fission or non-fission) is used\nwith the kit to make technetium ( 9omTc) macrosalb injection."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry as described in \ngeneral chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \nmost prominent gamma photon of technetium -99m has energy \nof0.141 MeV.\nB. The tests for non filterable radioactivity and particle size \ncontribute to the identification of the preparation."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 3.8 to 7.5.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of test solution \nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparations. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date.\nSpecific radioactivity. Not less than 37 MBq priec dm-Z. be-iug- the ,maximum recommended dose in mililitres.\n99m per milligram of aggregated albumin at theote.andtime :Alterf*tivelY-the preparation should pass the pyrogen test \nof administration. ' (2,18).\n4i.34"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-labelled-human-serum-albumin-nanocolloid-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Labelled Human Serum Albumin Nanocolloid Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TECHNETIUM (99MTC) LABELLED HUMAN SERUM ALBUMIN NANOCOLLOID INJECTION \nIP 2022\nRadiopharmaceuticalpreparations.- After4hour of the injection, \nthe whole body radioactivity retention shall not be more than\n10.0 per cent of the injected dose in 2 out of 3 swiss mice.\nSterility (2,2.11)., Complies with the test for sterility as described \nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest. . \nZ '.?;■■■ \n: : C .• ; . \n■\nBacterid! endotoxins (2.2:3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation.should pass the pyrogen tests (2.2.8.).\nStorage. Store at 2° to .8° with adequate shielding.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection, (1) the amount of 99raTc as labelled \nHYNIC-TOC expressed as total MBq (or mCi) and the \nradioactive concentration as MBq per ml (or as mCi per ml) on \nthe date and time of reconstitution; (2) the expiration date and \ntime; (3) the statement, “Caution - Radioactive Material?’\nTechnetium (99nlTc) Labelled Human \nSerum Albumin Nanocolloid Injection\n(99,’!Tc-IISA-NC Injection)\nCategory: Diagnostic.\nDescription. 99mTc-HSA-NC Injection is a clear, colourless\nsterile solution, containing not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated \"mTc-radioaCtivity at \nthe date and time stated on the label. Not less than\n90.'0 per cent of the total technetium-99m is present as\n99mTc“HSA-NC. 99mTc-HSA-NC injection should be used \nwithin 4 hours from the timc of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. 99tnTc-HSA-NC Injection \nis prepared aseptically following the manufacturer’s \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains human \nalbumin, stannous chloride, Tween 20, ascorbic acid and buffer.\n99mTc as sodium pertechnetate (fission or non-fission), is used \nwith the kit to make 99mTc-HSA-NC Injection."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TECHNETIUM (99MTC) LABELLED HUMAN SERUM ALBUMIN NANOCOLLOID INJECTION \nIP 2022\nRadiopharmaceuticalpreparations.- After4hour of the injection, \nthe whole body radioactivity retention shall not be more than\n10.0 per cent of the injected dose in 2 out of 3 swiss mice.\nSterility (2,2.11)., Complies with the test for sterility as described \nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest. . \nZ '.?;■■■ \n: : C .• ; . \n■\nBacterid! endotoxins (2.2:3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation.should pass the pyrogen tests (2.2.8.).\nStorage. Store at 2° to .8° with adequate shielding.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection, (1) the amount of 99raTc as labelled \nHYNIC-TOC expressed as total MBq (or mCi) and the \nradioactive concentration as MBq per ml (or as mCi per ml) on \nthe date and time of reconstitution; (2) the expiration date and \ntime; (3) the statement, “Caution - Radioactive Material?’\nTechnetium (99nlTc) Labelled Human \nSerum Albumin Nanocolloid Injection\n(99,’!Tc-IISA-NC Injection)\nCategory: Diagnostic.\nDescription. 99mTc-HSA-NC Injection is a clear, colourless\nsterile solution, containing not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated \"mTc-radioaCtivity at \nthe date and time stated on the label. Not less than\n90.'0 per cent of the total technetium-99m is present as\n99mTc“HSA-NC. 99mTc-HSA-NC injection should be used \nwithin 4 hours from the timc of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. 99tnTc-HSA-NC Injection \nis prepared aseptically following the manufacturer’s \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains human \nalbumin, stannous chloride, Tween 20, ascorbic acid and buffer.\n99mTc as sodium pertechnetate (fission or non-fission), is used \nwith the kit to make 99mTc-HSA-NC Injection."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma ray spectrometry using a gamma ray \nspectrometer. The prominent gamma photon of technetium- \n99m has energy of 0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for radiochemical,purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of 99mTc-HSA-NC."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 6.0.\nadequate shielding.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of test solution \nis assayed using a pre calibrated dose calibrator. It is expressed \nas MBq(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnctium-99m.\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on Sodium Pertechnetate (\"mTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate (\"mTc) Injection \n(Non Fission) used for preparation of 99mTc-HS A-NC Ihj ection.\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by paper chromatography \n(2.4.15) ..\nSupport. Chromatography paper Whatman paper No. 1 or \nequivalent. (3 cmx 15 cm)\nMobile phase. 0.9 per cent sodium chloride solution.\nApply 5 to 10 pl of the injection under examination 3 cm from \nbottom end of the chromatography strip. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 10 cm and dry the strip in air. Determine the \ndistribution ?of radioactivity using a suitable radioactivity \ncounting system. The free pertechnetate (Tc-99m) ion exhibits \nRf in the range 0.8 to 1.0 whereas 99mTc-HSA-NC remains at \nthe point of application (Rf 0.1). The percentage radioactivity\n(at Rf 0.8 to 1.0) should not more than 5 per cent.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in General Chapter on \nRadiopharmaceutical preparations by using Wistar rats. The \nanimals are administered 50 ul volume of the 99mTc-HSA-NC \nInjection subcutaneously to the footpad of the animal under \nanaesthesia; At 2 hour post injection the 99l71Tc-activity in \nrelevant organs and tissues are measured using suitable \nradioactivity counter. 99mTc-activity in the sentinel node should \nnot be less than 1.5 per cent and 99mTc-activity in popliteal \nnode indicates sentinel lymph node accumulation and activity \nin upstream nodes (iliac, inguinal) and other regions indicates \nfurther spread of the preparation through the lymphatic stream^ \nNot more than 1.0 per cent of injected dose should be present\nin blood, not more than 1 per cent of injected dose should be \npresent in liver and not more than 1 per cent of injected dose \nshould be present in kidney in two out of three rate. ;\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as describ,ed \nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3): Less than 175 per V EU per ml,V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen tests (2.2.8.), . :\nStorage: Injection may be stored at room temperature with\nIP 2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-macrosalb-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99mtc) Macrosalb Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TECHNETIUM (99MTC) MACROSALB INJECTION\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of 99raTc as labelled \nHSA-NC expressed as total megabecquerels (or millicuries) \nand the radioactiye concentration as megabecquerels per ml \n(or as millicuries per ml) on the date and time of reconstitution; \n(2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statement, “Caution 7 \nRadioactive Material,”\nTechnetium (99mTc) Macrosalb\nInjection\nCategory. Diagnostic\nDescription. Technetium (99inTc) Macrosalb Injection is a \nwhite, sterile suspension of human albumin in the form of \nirregular insoluble aggregates obtained by denaturing human \nalbumin in aqueous solution. The particles are labelled with \ntechnetium-99m and have a typical diameter between 10 fiiii \nand 100 pm. Technetium (S9l'Tc) Macrosalb Injection contains \nnot lessthah90.0 per centandhot more than 110.0 per cent of \nthe stated technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time \nstated on the label. The human albumin employed complies \nwith the requirements prescribed in the monograph human \nalbumim'The Technetium (99trttc) Macrosalb Injection should \nbe used within 4 hours from date and time of preparation. 1\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nMacrosalb Injection is prepared aseptically following \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains human \nalbumin and other excipients such as suitable buffer, reducing \nagents and an antimicrobial preservative. Sodium \npertechnetate injection (fission or non-fission) is used\nwith the kit to make technetium (99i:'Tc) macrosalb injection.,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TECHNETIUM (99MTC) MACROSALB INJECTION\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of 99raTc as labelled \nHSA-NC expressed as total megabecquerels (or millicuries) \nand the radioactiye concentration as megabecquerels per ml \n(or as millicuries per ml) on the date and time of reconstitution; \n(2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statement, “Caution 7 \nRadioactive Material,”\nTechnetium (99mTc) Macrosalb\nInjection\nCategory. Diagnostic\nDescription. Technetium (99inTc) Macrosalb Injection is a \nwhite, sterile suspension of human albumin in the form of \nirregular insoluble aggregates obtained by denaturing human \nalbumin in aqueous solution. The particles are labelled with \ntechnetium-99m and have a typical diameter between 10 fiiii \nand 100 pm. Technetium (S9l'Tc) Macrosalb Injection contains \nnot lessthah90.0 per centandhot more than 110.0 per cent of \nthe stated technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time \nstated on the label. The human albumin employed complies \nwith the requirements prescribed in the monograph human \nalbumim'The Technetium (99trttc) Macrosalb Injection should \nbe used within 4 hours from date and time of preparation. 1\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nMacrosalb Injection is prepared aseptically following \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains human \nalbumin and other excipients such as suitable buffer, reducing \nagents and an antimicrobial preservative. Sodium \npertechnetate injection (fission or non-fission) is used\nwith the kit to make technetium (99i:'Tc) macrosalb injection.,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry as described in \ngeneral chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \nmost prominent gamma photon bf technetium -99m has energy \nofO.141 MeV.\nB. The tests for non filterable radioactivity and particle size \ncontribute to the identification of the preparation......."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH(2.4.24).3.8to7.5. \n. \n.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivityof an aliquot of test solution \nis assayed using prccalibratcd radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical prepafaticihs. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular tinie and date.\nSpecific radioactivity. Not less than 37 MBq of technetium- \n99m per milligram of aggregated albumin at the date and time\nof administration. \n'\nNon filterable radioactivity. Not less than 90.0 per cent of the \ntotal radioactivity.\nUse a polycarbonate membrane filter 13 to 25 mm in diameter,\n10: jxm thick and with circular pores 3 pm in diameter. Fit the \nmembrane into a suitable holder. Place 0.2 ml of the preparation \nto be examined on the membrane and filter, adding 20 ml of a 9 \ng per liter solution of sodium chloride during the filtration. \nDetermine the radioactivity remaining on the membrane.\nParticle size. Not more than 10 particles have a maximum \ndimension greater than 100 pm and no particle having a \nmaximum dimension greater than 150 pm is present in a sample \nof at least 5000 particles.\nExamine using a microscope. Dilute the preparation under \nexamination if necessary so that the number of particles is just \nlow enough for individual particles to be distinguished. Using \na syringe fitted with a needle having a calibre not less than \n0.35 mm, place a suitable volume in a suitable counting chamber \nsuch as a haemocytometer cell, taking care not to overfill the \nchamber. Allow the preparation to be examined to settle for 1 \nmin and carefully add a cover slide without squeezing the \nsample. Scan an area corresponding to at least 5000 particles. \nThe preparation may be released for use before completion of \nthe test.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in general chapter on \nRadiopharmaceutical preparations. Inject a volume not greater \nthan 0.2 ml into a caudal vein of each of 3 rats weighing 150- \n250 g. Euthanise the animals 15 minutes after the injection, \nremove liver, spleen and lungs. Measure the radioactivity in \nthe organs using a gamma counter. Measure the radioactivity \nin the rest of the body, including the blood, after having \nremoved the tail. Determine the percentage of radioactivity in \nthe lungs, liver and spleen from the following expression:\nA— xl00\nB \n. ’ ' '\nWhere, A = radioactivity of the organ concerned;\nB = total radioactivity in the liver, spleen, lungs\nand rest of the body. \n-■; ,\nIn not fewer than 2 of the 3 animals used, not less than 80.0 per \ncent of the radioactivity is found in the lungs and not more \nthan a total of 5.0 per cent in the liver and spleen.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as \ndescribed in general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical \npreparations. The injection may be released Tor use before\ncompletion of the test.\nBacteria] endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in mililitres. \nAlternatively the preparation should pass the pyrogen test \n(2.2.8). \n7 \n■ \n' -\n— \n4775\nTECHNETIUM (Q9MTC) MEBROFENIN INJECTION\nIP 2022\nStorage.; Store at a temperature between 2° to 8 ° with adequate \nshielding.' \n. \n> \nmLm-\nLabelling, : The label states,: in addition?to ¡the : information \nspecified under injections (1) the amount of 99n:Tc as labelled \nmacrosalb expressed as tbtal MBq(mCi or pCi) and \nconcentration as M Bq (mCi or uCi); per. ml at the: time of \ncalibration; (2) the expiration date/¿nd time; (3) the statements \n“Caution—-Radioactive Material”, “shake wellbefore use” arid \n“the preparation is not to be used if after shaking, the \nsuspension does not appear, uniform”,.. \n. ."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-mebrofenin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Mebrofenin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Mebrofenin\nInjection\nCategory. Diagnostic. . \n.\nDescription. A clear colourless solution. Technetium (9‘>,rTc) \nMebrofenin Injection is a sterile solution and córitáins not \nless than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated technetium-99m radioactivity; at the date and lime statéd \non the label; Not less than 94 per cent of the totaltéchnetiúín- \n99rri is present as \"\"’Tc-Mebrofenin. Use the 99mTc Alebrófeniri \ninjection within 4h from the daté and time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nMebrofenin Injection is prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer’s instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains [[[(3-bromo-2,4,6-trirnethylphenyl)carbamoyl \n]methyl]imino]diacetic acid (mebrofenin) and a stannous salt. \nThe kit may have preadjusted pH and may contain reducing, \nchelating; stabilizing, filling arid antioxidizing agents as well \nas antimicrobial preservatives and buflers. The injection may \nalso:be prepared under aseptic processing,combined with \nsterilization by filtration. Sodium pertechnetate 99mTc (fission \nor nonAssion), complying ¡with specification given for it, is \nused with the kit to make the Technetium (99mTc) Mebrofenin \nInjection. 1 \n'"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Technetium (99mTc) Mebrofenin\nInjection\nCategory. Diagnostic. . \n.\nDescription. A clear colourless solution. Technetium (9‘>,rTc) \nMebrofenin Injection is a sterile solution and córitáins not \nless than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated technetium-99m radioactivity; at the date and lime statéd \non the label; Not less than 94 per cent of the totaltéchnetiúín- \n99rri is present as \"\"’Tc-Mebrofenin. Use the 99mTc Alebrófeniri \ninjection within 4h from the daté and time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nMebrofenin Injection is prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer’s instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains [[[(3-bromo-2,4,6-trirnethylphenyl)carbamoyl \n]methyl]imino]diacetic acid (mebrofenin) and a stannous salt. \nThe kit may have preadjusted pH and may contain reducing, \nchelating; stabilizing, filling arid antioxidizing agents as well \nas antimicrobial preservatives and buflers. The injection may \nalso:be prepared under aseptic processing,combined with \nsterilization by filtration. Sodium pertechnetate 99mTc (fission \nor nonAssion), complying ¡with specification given for it, is \nused with the kit to make the Technetium (99mTc) Mebrofenin \nInjection. 1 \n'"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry. The most prominent \ngamma photon of technetium-99m has energy of 0.140 MeV.;\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of 99rnTc-Mebrofenin."},{"name":"4776.\nTests","text":"/mvRm, \nyv/AJ.\npH(2.4.24).4.0to7.0. \n7. •\nRadioactivity Assay. Radioactivity of art aliquot bf Test \nSolution is assayed using precalibrated: radioactivity dose \ncalibrator as described in measurement of radioactivity. It is \nexpressed as MBq (mCi) in volume V nil at a particular time \nand date.\nRadionuclidic; purity. Not less than.99.9 per. cent due to \nTechnetium-99m. \n'\nComply with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on Sodium Pertechnetate (99mTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate (99mTc) Injection (Non \nFission) used for preparation: of Technetium (99mTc) \nMebrofenin Injection.\nRadiochemical purity. Not less than 94 per cent due to \nTechnetium-99m as 99mTc Mebrofenin. \n;\nImpurity A (Tc-99m in colloidal form). Determine by Thin Layer \nChromatography (2.4.17) coating the plate of I x 7.5 cm with \nReverse Phase modified Silica Gel. . \n,\nMobile phase, a mixture of 55 volumes of 0.385 per cent \nsolution of ammonium acetateand 45 volumes of acetonitrile.\nTest solution. The preparation to be examined. ....\nDetermine the distribution of radioactivity using a suitable \ncalibrated detector. r !; ... .\nRetardation factor. ■. \n: \n. ; \n.\n7 99mTc-Colloid ; \n, :0-0.1 \" \"\n99mTc-Mebrofenin : 0.4-0.6\nper cent of radioactivity due to Impurity A is the per cent \nactivity at Ry= 0-0.1. \n. ; ; y\nOther Radiochemical impurities. Determine by liquid \nchromatography (2.4.14). , r\nTest solution : The preparation under examination.\nChromatographic system \n;\n- a stainless steel column 25.0 cm x 4.6mm packed with\noctadecyl silane bonded,to porous silica (5 gm), ■\n- mobile phase: A. 0.385 per cent solution of ammonium\nacetate \n' ’J s\nB. acetonitrile. \n; . , \n/\n- flow rate: 1ml per minute,\n- \nradioactivity detector connected in scries, \n- injection volume. 20 pl\n. Time . Mobile phase A Mobile phase B \n(in min.) ; (per cent v/v). \n. (per cent v/v) .,,,.q\n■ \n0 \n70 \n. J; 7 30 ... . . 7.\n77;77; 20 7n7; \nJ,. ■■ ■.77 :i77;ÿv Î>7;77a\n-30 \n40 \n. ^.60 ; - 7ri:;o\nIP 2022 \nTECHNETIUM : W) MEDRONATE COMPLEX INJECTION\nRetention time of \"'\"Tc-Mebroi cnin: 14-16 minutes and 99rnTc- \nPertechnetate ion: about 4 minutes.\nCalculate the percentage of radioactivity due to 99riiTc- \nMebrofenin using the following expression:\n(100-A)xT\nIn which A = per cent of radioactivity due to Impurity A \ndetermined in the test for Impurity Aunder radiochemical purity \nand T= proportion of the radioactivity in the peak due to\n99mTc- Mebrofenin relative to the total eluted radioactivity in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution.\nNot less than 94 per cent of 99niTc is present as 99mTc- \nMebrofenin. ' d\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in General: Chapter oh \nRadiopharmaceuticals, Not less than 70per cent of the injected \nradioactivity, is present in the gallbladder and intestine not \nmore than 10 per cent of the injected radioactivity is present in \nthe. kidneys, not more than lOper cent present in liver,, not \nmore than 5per cent of the radioactivity isfound in the stomach \nand not more than 3per cent of. the injected radioactivity is \npresent in the blood in the 2 out of 3 mice at 30 minutes post\ninjection ,?'.»■ 6';.; ;;\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as described \nin general chapteron Radiopharmaceutical preparation. The \ninjection may be released for use before .completion of the \ntest. \nb;7\"' D* \n■■...■■ > .-.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, \nV being the- maximum recommended dose in milliliters■\nAlternatively the preparation should pass the pyrogen - test• \n(2.2.8) in general chapter. \n: \n’■ inn?..\nStorage. Store in a single-dose or in multiple-dose containers, \nat a temperature between 2° tq 8°. f .7.77. ..\nLabelling, The label states, iii addition to the information \nspecified for injections (I) the time and date of calibration; \n(2) the amount of 99nTc as labelled mebrofenin expressed as \ntotal megabecquerels (or millicuries) and the concentration as \nmegabccquercis per;mi'(bras mjliiciiries per ml) bn the date \nand time of calibration;(3)thecxpirationdate and time; (4) the \nstatement, “Caution — Radioactive Material’...\nTechnetium (”'\"Tc) Medronate"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-mertiatide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium ( 99mTc) Mertiatide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Mertiatide\nInjection\n110.0 per cent of the stated technetium-99m radioactivity, at \nthe date and time stated on the label. Not less than 95 percent \nof the total technetium-99m is present as 99mTc-MDP complex. \nUse the 99mTc-MDP complex injection within 6h from the date \nand time of preparation.\nDevelop the chromatogram by ascending chromatography in \nboth the mobile phase till the solvent front reaches 15 cm from \nthe point of spotting. This takes about 90 min and about \n60 min in 85 per cent methanol and acetate buffer respectively. \nAllow the strips to dry.\nTechnetium (\"'\"\"Tc) MAG, Injection\nP\n0 \n/-( \n0 \t\nN1 \t\nf\\J \nr99m \nL \t\nTC1\nRad iopharmaceutical  preparation. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nMedronate Complex Injection is prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer's instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains sodium methylene diphosphonate (sodium \nmedronate) and a stannous salt. The kit may have preadjusted \npH and may contain reducing, chelating, stabilizing, filling \nand antioxidizing agents as well as antimicrobial preservatives \nand buffers. The injection may also be prepared under aseptic \nprocessing combined with sterilization by filtration. Sodium \npertechnetate 99mTc (fission or non-fission), complying with \nspecification given for it, is used with the kit to make the \n(\"m Tc) MDP Injection.\nScan the strips using a radiochromatogram scanner with \ncollimator suitably adjusted to measure \"mTc or cut into one \ncm sections and count in a well type Nal(T1) scintillation \ncounter. Per cent of Impurities viz. free pertechnetate and \nreduced hydrolysed technetium are determined in the solvents, \nviz. 85 per cent methanol (R f 0.55- 0.75) and acetate buffer \n(Rf 0.0M.1) respectively as follows:\n2 Na\n4_\n0 \t\nI )0\nCategory. Diagnostic\n(percent) free pertechnetate = (Activity in the \"'\"Tc] pertechnetate zone x  100)\nDescription. Technetium (99mTc) Mertiatide Injection is a \nsterile, clear, colourless aqueous solution. The content of \ntechnetium-99m is not less than 90.0 per cent and not more \nthan 110.0 per cent of the stated technetium-99m radioactivity \nat the date and time stated on the label. Not less than 94.0 per \ncent of the total technetium-99m radioactivity is present as \ntechnetium (99mTc) mertiatide complex. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nMertiatide Injection should be used within 6 hours from the \ntime of preparation.\nTotal Activity\n(percent) reduced hydrolysed technetium (R HT)  — (Activity in RHT zone x 100)\nTotal Activity\nPercentage of each impurity should be not more than 5 per \ncent of the total activity. \nThe radiochemical purity of Tc-MDP = 100 - (per cent free \npertechnetate + per cent reduced hydrolyzed technetium). \nThe Radiochemical Purity should not be less than 95per cent \nof the total activity. \nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in General Chapter on Radio-\npharmaceuticals. Using a set of three Wistar rats, inject a volume \nless than 0.2 ml containing not more than 200 µg (0.2 mg) of \nMDP. Determine the radioactivity in the liver and kidney. \nBiodistribution pattern should not be less than 2 per cent in \nboth femurs, not more than 5 per cent in liver and not more \nthan 5 per cent in kidneys of Total Retained Dose (per cent \nTRD) in 2 out of 3 rats at 1 hour post injection. \nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as \ndescribed in general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical \npreparation. The injection may be released for use before \ncompletion of the test. \nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8) in general \nchapter. \nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding. \nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified for injections (1) the time and date of calibration; (2) \nthe amount of 99mTc as labelled mebrofenin expressed as total \nmegabecquerels (or millicu"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Technetium (99mTc) Mertiatide\nInjection\n110.0 per cent of the stated technetium-99m radioactivity, at \nthe date and time stated on the label. Not less than 95 percent \nof the total technetium-99m is present as 99mTc-MDP complex. \nUse the 99mTc-MDP complex injection within 6h from the date \nand time of preparation.\nDevelop the chromatogram by ascending chromatography in \nboth the mobile phase till the solvent front reaches 15 cm from \nthe point of spotting. This takes about 90 min and about \n60 min in 85 per cent methanol and acetate buffer respectively. \nAllow the strips to dry.\nTechnetium (\"'\"\"Tc) MAG, Injection\nP\n0 \n/-( \n0 \t\nN1 \t\nf\\J \nr99m \nL \t\nTC1\nRad iopharmaceutical  preparation. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nMedronate Complex Injection is prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer's instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains sodium methylene diphosphonate (sodium \nmedronate) and a stannous salt. The kit may have preadjusted \npH and may contain reducing, chelating, stabilizing, filling \nand antioxidizing agents as well as antimicrobial preservatives \nand buffers. The injection may also be prepared under aseptic \nprocessing combined with sterilization by filtration. Sodium \npertechnetate 99mTc (fission or non-fission), complying with \nspecification given for it, is used with the kit to make the \n(\"m Tc) MDP Injection.\nScan the strips using a radiochromatogram scanner with \ncollimator suitably adjusted to measure \"mTc or cut into one \ncm sections and count in a well type Nal(T1) scintillation \ncounter. Per cent of Impurities viz. free pertechnetate and \nreduced hydrolysed technetium are determined in the solvents, \nviz. 85 per cent methanol (R f 0.55- 0.75) and acetate buffer \n(Rf 0.0M.1) respectively as follows:\n2 Na\n4_\n0 \t\nI )0\nCategory. Diagnostic\n(percent) free pertechnetate = (Activity in the \"'\"Tc] pertechnetate zone x  100)\nDescription. Technetium (99mTc) Mertiatide Injection is a \nsterile, clear, colourless aqueous solution. The content of \ntechnetium-99m is not less than 90.0 per cent and not more \nthan 110.0 per cent of the stated technetium-99m radioactivity \nat the date and time stated on the label. Not less than 94.0 per \ncent of the total technetium-99m radioactivity is present as \ntechnetium (99mTc) mertiatide complex. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nMertiatide Injection should be used within 6 hours from the \ntime of preparation.\nTotal Activity\n(percent) reduced hydrolysed technetium (R HT)  — (Activity in RHT zone x 100)\nTotal Activity\nPercentage of each impurity should be not more than 5 per \ncent of the total activity. \nThe radiochemical purity of Tc-MDP = 100 - (per cent free \npertechnetate + per cent reduced hydrolyzed technetium). \nThe Radiochemical Purity should not be less than 95per cent \nof the total activity. \nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in General Chapter on Radio-\npharmaceuticals. Using a set of three Wistar rats, inject a volume \nless than 0.2 ml containing not more than 200 µg (0.2 mg) of \nMDP. Determine the radioactivity in the liver and kidney. \nBiodistribution pattern should not be less than 2 per cent in \nboth femurs, not more than 5 per cent in liver and not more \nthan 5 per cent in kidneys of Total Retained Dose (per cent \nTRD) in 2 out of 3 rats at 1 hour post injection. \nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as \ndescribed in general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical \npreparation. The injection may be released for use before \ncompletion of the test. \nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8) in general \nchapter. \nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding. \nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified for injections (1) the time and date of calibration; (2) \nthe amount of 99mTc as labelled mebrofenin expressed as total \nmegabecquerels (or millicuries) and the concentration as \nmegabecquerels per ml (or as millicuries per ini) on the\n-date \nand time of calibration; (3) the expiration date and,time; (4) the \nstatement, \"Caution — Radioactive Material.'.':"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry. The spectrum \nobtained corresponds to the spectrum obtained with reference \nsolution of technetium-99m. The most prominent gamma \nphoton of technetium-99m has energy of 0.140 MeV.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nMertiatide Injection is prepared aseptically following \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains S-\nbenzoylmercaptoacetyltriglycine, a chelating agent such as \ntartrate, and a stannous salt.  Sodium pertechnetate (99mTc) \ninjection (fission or non-fission)  is used with the kit to make \nthe Technetium (99mTc) Mertiatide Complex Injection. The \ninjection may have the pH adjusted and may contain reducing, \nchelating, stabilizing, filling and antioxidizing agents as well \nas antimicrobial preservatives, buffers and sodium chloride to \nmake the solution isotonic with blood.\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of 99mTc-Medronate.\npH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 8.0.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot ofTest Solution \nis assayed using pre-calibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparation. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Radionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to: \nTechnetium-99m (99mTc).\nA. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry as described in \ngeneral chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \nmost prominent gamma photon of technetium-99m has energy \nof 0.140 MeV.\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on Sodium pertechnetate ( 99mTc) injection (fission) \nor Sodium pertechnetate ( 99m)Tc injection (non-fission) used \nfor preparation of Technetium ( 99mTc) MDP Complex Injection.\nB. In the test for radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of technetium ( 99mTc) \nmertiatide.\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by ascending paper \nchromatography (2.4.15)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Support. Chromatography paper Whatman No. 1 /No3 strip\npH (2.4..24).4p :to 7.5.\n(3 ( 33,cm).\n':-Itiohikphas0;-85 per cent methanol in distilled water, 0.5 M\nRadiCkactivitYassay.  Radioactivity of an aliquot of test solution \ni,51iSsayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator\nweiate,b er, pH adjusted to 5.\n• \t\n'•\nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparations. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date, V being the \nmaximum recommended dose in mililitres.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \ntechnetium-99m. Complies with the radionuclidic purity tests \nas described under monograph of sodium pertechnetate\n(99\"'Tc) injection (fission) or sodium pertechnetate  (c9i'Tc)\ninjection (non-fission) used for preparation of technetium\n(99mTc) mertiatide.\nRadiochemical Purity. Determine by thin layer \nchromatography (2.4.17), using silica gel coated plates or \nready to use silica gel strips.\nMobile phase: a mixture of 45 volumes ofmethanol, 55 volumes \nof 0.9 per cent w/v solution of sodium chloride and lvolume \nof acetic acid. \nApply 5 1.1.1 of the injection under examination to the \nchromatographic plate and allow the mobile phase to rise 15 cm \nand dry the plate in air. Determine the distribution of \nradioactivity using a gamma counter. The Rf value of\nTechnetium (99mTc) mertiatide complex about 0.45 to 0.55, the\npertechnetate ion about 0.9 to 1.0 and reduced  / hydrolysed \ntechnetium-99m about 0 to 0.1. Not less than 94.0 per cent of\nthe total radioactivity is in the spot corresponding to the \ntechnetium (\"\"Tc) mertiatide complex.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done as described in section\n1.6 in general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations \nusing a set of three mice or rats. After 1 hour of the injection. \nthe activity in urine should not be less than 80.0 per cent of \nthe injected radioactivity; the activity in the kidneys should \nnot be more than 5.0 per cent of the injected radioactivity and \nthe activity in the liver should not be less than 1.0 per cent of \nthe injected radioactivity.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as \ndescribed in general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical \npreparations. The injection may be released for use before \ncompletion of the test.\nBacterial endotoxins  (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in mililiters. \nAlternatively the preparation should pass the pyrogen test \n(2.2.8).\nStorage. Store at a temperature between 2° to 8° with adequate \nshielding.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injections (1) the time and date of calibration: \n(2) the amount of 99mTc as labelled mertiatide expressed as \ntotal MBq (mCi or RCi) and the concentration as MBq per ml \n(mCi or µCi per ml) on the date and time of calibrition;(3) the \nexpiration date and time and (4) statement; ,\"Caution-"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Radioactive Material.\"\n41.3$\nTECHNETIUM (99MTC) MERTIATIDE INJECTION \t\nIP 2018 \t\nIP 2018\nTECHNETIUM (99MTC) TETROFOSMIN COMPLEX INJECTION\nTechnetium (99mTc) M I RI Injection\nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparation. It is expressed as \nMBq(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date.\n100 per cent - A,\nCf\nCH 3\nin which Cf is the correction factor, and A, is the mean area \npercentage for the colloid obtained from Method A. Obtain \ncorrected area percentage by multiplying the correction factor \n(C f) to the area percentage of the peaks present in the \nchromatogram. A mean of not less than 90per cent (corrected \narea percentage) of the total radioactivity is represented by \n99mTc-MIBI.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnetium-99m.\nH3C0 \t\nH3C0--1 \t\nCH\nCH3\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on Sodium Pertechnetate ( 99mTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate ( 99mTc) Injection (Non \nFission) used for preparation of Technetium ( 99mTc) MIBI \nInjection.\nC H3 \nOCH 3\nH 3C \t\nN \nCH3 N \t\nI I \t\nN \n0\n[99mTc  \n/ \t\nH 3C \nN \t\nI \t\nN\nCI\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in General Chapter on \nRadiopharmaceuticals. Activity should not less than 1.5 per \ncent in heart, not more than 1per cent in the blood, not more \nthan 1.5 per cent in the lungs and not more than 5 per cent in \nstomach of the injected dose at lh post injection.\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by a combination of two \nmethods Reverse phase TLC and HPLC.\nCH3\nH3C \nCH3 \nCH3 \t\nOCH 3\nH3C0\nMethod A. By Thin-layer chromatography method (2.4.17), \nusing the 10 mm x 7.5 cm reverse-phase chromatographic plate.\nCH3 \nOCH 3\nMobile phase. A mixture of 4 volumes of acetonitrile, 2.5 \nvolumes ofmethanol,  1.5 volumes of 3.85 per cent ammonium \nacetate and 2 volumes of tetrahydrofuran.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as described \nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparation. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest.\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription.  A clear colourless or a very slightly yellow \nsolution. Technetium ( 99mTc) Sestamibi Injection contains not \nless than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated \non the label. Not less than 90 percent of the total technetium -\n99m is present as  99mTc-MIBI. Use the Technetium ( 99mTc) \nMIBI Injection within 4 hours from the date and time of \npreparation.\nApply 1 to 2µl of Injection about 1 cm from the bottom of \nchromatographic plate, and allow to dry. Position the plate in \na chromatographic chamber and develop the chromatograms \nin a freshly prepared (not more than 4 hours) mobile phase \nuntil the solvent front has moved about 6 cm from the origin. \nRemove the plate and allow it to air-dry. Determine the \nradioactivity distribution by scanning the chromatogram with \na suitable radiation detector. A mean of not less than 90per \ncent (area per cent) of the radioactivity is found at an Rf value \nbetween 0.3-0.6. Free pertechnetate is located at about the \nRf 0.8-0.9, and radio-colloid is located at about Rf 0.05 - 0.1. \nThe sum of the mean percentages of free pertechnetate and \nradio-colloid does not exceed 10 per cent.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8) in general \nchapter.\nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of \"mTc as labelled \nMIBI expressed as total megabecquerels (or millicuries) and \nthe radioactive concentration as megabecquerels per ml (or as \nmillicuries per ml) on the date and time of reconstitution; \n(2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statement, \"Caution —\nRadioactive Material\".\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) MIBI \nInjection is prepared aseptically following manufacturer's \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains a mixture \nof [tetrakis(2-methoxy-2-methylpropy 1-1- isocyanide) \ncopper (1+)] tetrafluoroborate, a weak chelating agent, a \nstannous salt. Technetium (99mTc) MIBI Injection ((OC-6-11)- \nhex ak i s[ 1 -( is oc yano-KC)- 2-methoxy-2-methyl-\npropane][` mTe]technetium(1) chloride) may be prepared by \nadding Sodium pertechnetate \"mTc (fission or non-fission), \ncomplying with specification given for it, to the kit and heating \nit as instructed.\nMethod R. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nBuffer. Prepare a filtered and degassed mixture of 35 volumes \nof 0.05 M ammonium sulphate and 45 volumes of methanol."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-mibi-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) MIBI Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) MIBI Injection\nch3\n;c>L-ch3 ch3\nh3co\nH3Cfe\nN OCH3\nN\noch3\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear colourless or a very slightly yellow \nsolution. Technetium (99mTc). Sestamibi Injection contains not \nless than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated \non the label; Not less than 90 percent of the total technetium - \n99m is present as 99mTc-MIBI. Use the Technetium (99mTc) \nMIBI Injection within 4 hours from the date and time of \npreparation. .\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) MIBI \nInjection is prepared aseptically following manufacturer’s \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains a mixture \nof |tetrakis(2-methoxy-2-methylpropyl-1 -isocyanide) \ncopper (1+)] tetrafluoroborate, a weàk chelating agent, a \nstannous salt. Technetium (99mTc) MIBI Injection ((ÛC-6-11)- \nhexakisf 1 -(isocyano-xC)-2-methoxy-2-methyl-propane]\n[99tnTc]technetium(I) chloride)' may be prepared by adding \nSodium pertechnetate 99mTc (fission or non-fission), complying \nwith specification given for it, to the kit and heating it as\ninstructed.\nIdentification >•\nA. Determine by gamma-ray1 spectrometry. The most prominent \ngamma photon of Technetium 99m has energy of 0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the; identification of the Technetium \n(99mTc)MIBI."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Technetium (99mTc) MIBI Injection\nch3\n;c>L-ch3 ch3\nh3co\nH3Cfe\nN OCH3\nN\noch3\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear colourless or a very slightly yellow \nsolution. Technetium (99mTc). Sestamibi Injection contains not \nless than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated \non the label; Not less than 90 percent of the total technetium - \n99m is present as 99mTc-MIBI. Use the Technetium (99mTc) \nMIBI Injection within 4 hours from the date and time of \npreparation. .\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) MIBI \nInjection is prepared aseptically following manufacturer’s \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains a mixture \nof |tetrakis(2-methoxy-2-methylpropyl-1 -isocyanide) \ncopper (1+)] tetrafluoroborate, a weàk chelating agent, a \nstannous salt. Technetium (99mTc) MIBI Injection ((ÛC-6-11)- \nhexakisf 1 -(isocyano-xC)-2-methoxy-2-methyl-propane]\n[99tnTc]technetium(I) chloride)' may be prepared by adding \nSodium pertechnetate 99mTc (fission or non-fission), complying \nwith specification given for it, to the kit and heating it as\ninstructed.\nIdentification >•\nA. Determine by gamma-ray1 spectrometry. The most prominent \ngamma photon of Technetium 99m has energy of 0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the; identification of the Technetium \n(99mTc)MIBI."},{"name":"Tests","text":"■ • ■\npH(2.4.24).5.0to7.0. / \n’\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of Test Solution\nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator\n4779\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-succimer-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Succimer Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Succimer Injection\nSpot about two to five microlitres of\"mTc(III)-DMSA injection \n( corresponding activity) with a capillary or a micropipette at \nthe point of spotting marked at 1 cm from one end of the strip \n(1 x 8 cm) in duplicate. Develop the chromatogram by ascending \nchromatography in the solvents till the solvent front reaches \n6 cm from the point of spotting.\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nDMSA Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated \non the label. Not less than 95 percent of the total technetium -\n99m is present as 99mTc-DMSA. Use the Tee'hnetitarn ( 99inTe) \nDMSA Injection within 4 hours from the datand time of \npreparation. •\nin the injection under examination.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas- OtScribtd' in section 1.6 in general chapter on \nRadiopharmaceutical preparations. Inject a volume, not more \nthan 0.2 ml into a caudal vein of each of three mice, each\nC'ut the strip into 2 sections lower half (0-3 cm) and upper half \n(3-4-effi) and &hint in a well type scintillation counter. Per cent \nof impurity free pertechnetate (Rf – upper half) is determined \nin the sql*nt, as follows.\nPer cent free pertechnetate  = Activity in the 99'--fc \npertechnetate zone x 100 /Total Activity.\nThe percentage of activity in the 99mTc-pertechnetate zone \nshould not be more than 2 per cent of the total activity.\nThe radiochemical purity of 99mTc (III) DMSA (R, =0) should \nnot be less than 95per cent.\nMethod B. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.1 7), \ncoating the plate with silica gel.\nMobile phase. methyl ethyl ketone.\nHeat the plate at 110° C for 10 minutes. Use a plate such that \nduring development the mobile phase migrates over a distance \nof 10 cm to 15 cm in about 10 minutes.\nApply to the plate 5 IA to 10 pi of the injection to be examined. \nAllow the mobile phase to rise 10 cm to 15 cm and dry the \nplate. Determine the distribution of radioactivity using a \nsuitable detector. Technetium succimer complex remains at \nthe starting point. Pertechnetate ion migrates near to the \nsolvent front. Not less than 95.0 per cent of the total \nradioactivity is found in the spot corresponding to technetium \nsuccimer complex. The radioactivity corresponding to \npertechnetate ion represents not more than 2.0 per cent of the \ntotal radioactivity.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in general chapter on \nradiopharmaceuticals. If not fewer than two of the three rats \nused, the radioactivity in the kidneys is not less than 40 per \ncent, that in the liver is not more than 10.0 per cent, that in the \nstomach is not more than 2.0 per cent and that in the lungs is \nnot more than 5.0 per cent of the injected dose at 1 hour post \ninjection.\nSterility (2.2. 1 1 ). Complies with the test for sterility as described\nin general chapter on Radiophannaceutical preparation. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest.\nBacterial endotoxins  (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml, alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8) in general \nchapter.\nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of  99mTc as labelled \nsuccimer expressed as total megabecquerels (or millicuries) \nand the radioactive concentration as megabecquerels per ml \n(or as millicuries per nil) on the date and time ofx:eiOnstitut\n: \n(2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statel4ni:\"Cautiort\nRadioactive Material\".\nTECHNETIUM (\"mTC) DMSA INJECTION \nIP 2018 \nIP 2018"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Technetium (99mTc) Succimer Injection\nSpot about two to five microlitres of\"mTc(III)-DMSA injection \n( corresponding activity) with a capillary or a micropipette at \nthe point of spotting marked at 1 cm from one end of the strip \n(1 x 8 cm) in duplicate. Develop the chromatogram by ascending \nchromatography in the solvents till the solvent front reaches \n6 cm from the point of spotting.\nCategory. Diagnostic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nDMSA Injection is a sterile solution and contains not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated technetium-99m radioactivity at the date and time stated \non the label. Not less than 95 percent of the total technetium -\n99m is present as 99mTc-DMSA. Use the Tee'hnetitarn ( 99inTe) \nDMSA Injection within 4 hours from the datand time of \npreparation. •\nin the injection under examination.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas- OtScribtd' in section 1.6 in general chapter on \nRadiopharmaceutical preparations. Inject a volume, not more \nthan 0.2 ml into a caudal vein of each of three mice, each\nC'ut the strip into 2 sections lower half (0-3 cm) and upper half \n(3-4-effi) and &hint in a well type scintillation counter. Per cent \nof impurity free pertechnetate (Rf – upper half) is determined \nin the sql*nt, as follows.\nPer cent free pertechnetate  = Activity in the 99'--fc \npertechnetate zone x 100 /Total Activity.\nThe percentage of activity in the 99mTc-pertechnetate zone \nshould not be more than 2 per cent of the total activity.\nThe radiochemical purity of 99mTc (III) DMSA (R, =0) should \nnot be less than 95per cent.\nMethod B. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.1 7), \ncoating the plate with silica gel.\nMobile phase. methyl ethyl ketone.\nHeat the plate at 110° C for 10 minutes. Use a plate such that \nduring development the mobile phase migrates over a distance \nof 10 cm to 15 cm in about 10 minutes.\nApply to the plate 5 IA to 10 pi of the injection to be examined. \nAllow the mobile phase to rise 10 cm to 15 cm and dry the \nplate. Determine the distribution of radioactivity using a \nsuitable detector. Technetium succimer complex remains at \nthe starting point. Pertechnetate ion migrates near to the \nsolvent front. Not less than 95.0 per cent of the total \nradioactivity is found in the spot corresponding to technetium \nsuccimer complex. The radioactivity corresponding to \npertechnetate ion represents not more than 2.0 per cent of the \ntotal radioactivity.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in general chapter on \nradiopharmaceuticals. If not fewer than two of the three rats \nused, the radioactivity in the kidneys is not less than 40 per \ncent, that in the liver is not more than 10.0 per cent, that in the \nstomach is not more than 2.0 per cent and that in the lungs is \nnot more than 5.0 per cent of the injected dose at 1 hour post \ninjection.\nSterility (2.2. 1 1 ). Complies with the test for sterility as described\nin general chapter on Radiophannaceutical preparation. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest.\nBacterial endotoxins  (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml, alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8) in general \nchapter.\nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of  99mTc as labelled \nsuccimer expressed as total megabecquerels (or millicuries) \nand the radioactive concentration as megabecquerels per ml \n(or as millicuries per nil) on the date and time ofx:eiOnstitut\n: \n(2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statel4ni:\"Cautiort\nRadioactive Material\".\nTECHNETIUM (\"mTC) DMSA INJECTION \nIP 2018 \nIP 2018"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-tetrofosmin-complex-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Tetrofosmin Complex Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) Tetrofosmin \nComplex Injection\nTest solution. The preparation under examination.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 inn), \nmobile phase: a mixture of 70 volumes of buffer and \n30 volumes of acetonitrile, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \na flow-through gamma-ray detector connected in series."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Technetium (99mTc) Tetrofosmin \nComplex Injection\nTest solution. The preparation under examination.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 inn), \nmobile phase: a mixture of 70 volumes of buffer and \n30 volumes of acetonitrile, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \na flow-through gamma-ray detector connected in series."},{"name":"Identification","text":"L.\nA. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry. The most prominent \ngamma photon of Technetium 99m has energy of 0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of the Technetium\n8 LI-No/X \nr\n(991\"Tc) MI BI.\nInject suitable volume the injection under test into the \nchromatograph, record the chromatograms, and measure the \narea percentage for all of the peaks present. The retention \ntime for 99mTc-MIBI is about 12 to 14 minutes, Correctforthe \npresence of colloid, that is not measured by this method, takpli-. \nby the equation: „\npH (2.4:24).5,0=to  7.0.\nRadiciactivitOssay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of Test Solution\nOfegurf:Dtagnostic\nis. assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator\nDescription. Technetium ( 99mTc) Tetrofosmin Complex\nInjection is a clear, colourless, sterile solution. The content of \ntechnetium-99m is not less than 90.0 per cent and not more \nthan 110.0 per cent of the stated content of technetium-99m at \nthe reference date and time stated on the label. Not less than \n90.0 per cent of the total technetium -99m radioactivity is \npresent as technetium (99mTc) tetrofosmin complex. Technetium\n(99mTc) Tetrofosmin Complex Injection should be used within \n6 hours from time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nTetrofosmin Complex Injection is prepared aseptically \nfollowing instructions provided with the kit. The kit contains \n6,9-bis(2-ethoxyethyl)-3,12-dioxa-6,9-diphosphatetradecane ,\n stanous chloride and may contain antimicrobial agents, \nbuffers, stabilizing agents and antioxidizing agents. Sodium\npertechnetate (99mTc) injection (fission or non -fission) is used\nwith the kit to make the Technetium ( 99mTc) Tetrofosmin\nComplex Injection ."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry as described in \ngeneral chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \nmost prominent gamma photon of technetium- 99m has energy \nof 0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for Radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of technetium ( 99mTc)\ntetrofosmin complex."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 7.0 to 9.0.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of test solution\nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparations. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date, V being the \nmaximum recommended dose in mililitres.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \ntechnetium-99m. Complies with the radionuclidic purity tests \nas described under monograph of sodium pertechnetate \n(\"mTc) injection (fission) or sodium pertechnetate (\"\"Tc) \ninjection (non -fission) used for preparation of technetium\n(99mTc) tetrofosmin complex injection.\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by ascending paper \nchromatography (2.4.15).\nMobile phase. a mixture of 35 volumes of acetone and 65\nvolumes of dichloromethane.\nChromatographic paper a suitable silica gel impregnated glass\nfiber paper strip. \n\"IN\nApply 5 ill of the suitably diluted injection underOtamitiation \t\ncrIption.Tee.;111netium (99mTc) TRODAT-1 Injection is a clear,\nto the chromatographic paper. Allow the mobile phase to rise colourless ster4 solution and contains not less than 90.0 per\nTECHNETIUM (99MTC) TETROFOSMIN COMPLEX INJECTION \t\nIP 2018 \t\nIP 2018\nTECHNETIUM (99MTC) TRODAT-I INJECTION\n10 cm and air dry the paper. Determine the distribution of\ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated technetium-\n99m radioactivity at the date and time stated on the label. Not \nless than 90.0 per cent of the total technetium-99m is present \nas Technetium ( 99mTc) TRODAT-1. Technetium ( 99mTc) \nTRODAT-1 Injection should be used within 4 hours from the \ndate and time of preparation.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 80 volumes of acetonitrile and 20 \nvolumes of 0.005 M aqueous solution of dimethyl glutaric \nacid.\nradioactivity using a suitable detector. The Rf value of the \ntechnetium-99m tetrofosmin complex is approximately 0.45 to\n0.55, the pertechnetate ion has an Rf value of 0.9 to 1.0 and \nreduced/ hydrolyzed technetium-99m has an Rf value of 0 to \n0.1. The sum of the percentage of radioactivity corresponding \nto the pertechnetate ion and reduced/ hydrolyzed technetium-\n99m is not more than 10.0 per cent. Not less than 90.0 per cent \nof the total technetium-99m radioactivity is present as \ntechnetium ( 99mTc) tetrofosmin complex.\nTest solution. The injection under examination.\nApply to the plate 5 ill to 10 µl of the injection under \nexamination. Allow the mobile phase to rise 10 cm to 15 cm \nand dry the plate in air. Determine the distribution of \nradioactivity using a suitable detector. Pertechnetate ion \nexhibits Rf between 0.3-0.5, impurities in colloidal form remain \nat the point of spotting, ( 99mTc) GHA impurity has an Rf of 0.5-\n0.6, whereas (99mTc) TRODAT-1 complex migrates near to the \nsolvent front (R fof 0.7-1.0). The percentage of activity in each \npeak should be calculated and the sum of the percentages of \nradioactivity corresponding to all impurities in the \nchromatograms obtained should not more than 10.0 per cent.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium ( 99mTc) \n1 RODAT-1 Injection is prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer's instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains (213(N,N'-bis(2-mercaptoethyl) ethylenediamino-\nmethyl)-3(3-(4-chlorophenyl)tropane) (TRODAT-1), stannous \nchloride and may contain transchelating agents such as \ndisodium ethylene diamine tetracetic acid, sodium \nglucoheptonate (GHA) other than antimicrobial agents and \nbuffers. Sodium pertechnetate ( 99mTc) injection (fission or \nnon-fission) is used with the kit to make the (99m Tc) TRODAT-\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in general chapter on\nRadiopharmaceutical preparations using a set of three wistar \nrats. At one hour post injection activity should not be less \nthan 1.0 per cent injected dose per gram in heart, not more \nthan 0.2 per cent injected dose per gram in the blood, not more \nthan 0.5 per cent injected dose per gram in the lungs and not \nmore than 0.5 per cent injected dose per gram in stomach of \nthe injected dose at least in 2 out of 3 rats.\n1 Injection.\nMethod B. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest sample. The injection under examination."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Chromatographic system\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as described \nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest.\nA. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry as described in \ngeneral chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \nmost prominent gamma photon of technetium-99m has energy \nof 0.140 MeV.\n— a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.1mm, packed with\noctadeeylsi lane bonded to silica (3 gm), \n— mobile phase: a mixture of 80 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 20 volumes of 0.005 M aqueous solution ofdimethyl \nglutaric acid \n— flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n— radioactivity detector adjusted for 100 to 200 keV energy, \n— injection volume: 20\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in ml, Alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8).\nB. In the test for radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of the Technetium \n(99mTc) TRODAT-1.\nStorage. Protect from light and store at a temperature between"},{"name":"Tests","text":"2° to 8°. \nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injections (1) the amount of 99mTc as labelled \ntetrofosmin expressed as total MBq (or mCi) and the \nconcentration as MBq per ml (or as mCi per ml) on the date \nand time of calibration; (2) the expiration date and time and; \n(3) a statement, \"Caution- Radioactive Material.\"\nThe retention time of ( 99mTc) TRODAT-1 shall have 13 minutes \nto 14 minutes.\npH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 8.0.\nThe sum of the percentages of radioactivity corresponding to \nall impurities in the chromatograms obtained in test should \nnot more than 10.0 per cent.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of test solution \nis assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas described in measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparations. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date, V being the \nmaximum recommended dose in ml.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in general chapter on \nRadiopharmaceutical preparations using three wister rats. \nThree wister rats weighing 150-200 g should be intravenously \ninjected with equivalent to 20 MBq of ( 99mTc) TRODAT-1. At\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnetium-99m.\nTechnetium (\"\"Tc) TRODAT-1\n1 hour post injection rats should be sacrificed and the per \ncent radioactivity in brain should be measured or alternately \nthe rats could be imaged in an animal SPECT camera and the \nper cent activity in the brain can be calculated. The per cent \nradioactivity in the brain should be not less than 0.4 per cent \nof injected dose in at least 2 out of 3 rats.\n1 n j ection\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on sodium pertechnetate (S' 9mTc) injection \n(fission) or sodium pertechnetate (\"\"'Tc) injection (non-\nfission) used for preparation of technetium (99mTc) TRODAT-\n1 injection.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as described \nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest.\nRadiochemical purity. Use method A or method B\nMethod A. Determine by thin layer chromatography (2.4.17), \ncoating the plate with polyethylene terephthalate (PET) foil \nprecoated Polyamide 6 or equivalent.\nostic.\n- \t\n• \nUse a plate such that during development the:0:104 .0.4a.\n- -- \nmigrates over a distance of 10 cm to 15 cm in alkii4 I\nBacterial \"dot° x ins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, V \n110ing-jtke maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \n*liteparation should pass the pyrogen tests (2.2.8.).\nStorage. Store at room temperature with adequate shielding.\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of 9\"mTc as labelled \nTRODAT-1 expressed as total MBq (or mCi) and the radioactive \nconcentration as MBq per ml (or as mCi per ml) on the date \nand time of reconstitution; (2) the expiration date and time; (3) \nthe statement, \"Caution - Radioactive Material.\""}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/technetium-99mtc-trod-at-1-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Technetium (99mTc) TROD AT-1 Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TECHNETIUM (99MTC) TRODAT-1 INJECTION\ncentand notmorethan 110.0 per cent of the stated technetium- \n99mradioactivity at the date and’ time staled on the label. Not \nless than 90.0 per cent of the total technetium-99mis present \nàs Technetium (99mTc) TRODAT-1. Technetium (99niTc) \nTRODAT-1 Injection should be used within 4 hours from the \ndate and time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99,aTc) \nTROD AT-1 Injection is prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer’s instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains (2¡3(N.N'-bis(2-mercaptoclhyl) ethylenediamino- \nmcthyl)-3 (3-(4-chlorophenyl) trepane) (TRODAT-1), stannous \nchloride and may contain transchelating agents such as \ndisodium ethylene diamine tetracetic acid, sodium \nglucoheptonate (GHÁ) other than antimicrobial agents and \nbuffers. Sodium pertechnetate f'9mTc) injection (fission or \nnon-fission) is used with the kit.to make the (99m Tc) TRODAT-\n1 Injection."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TECHNETIUM (99MTC) TRODAT-1 INJECTION\ncentand notmorethan 110.0 per cent of the stated technetium- \n99mradioactivity at the date and’ time staled on the label. Not \nless than 90.0 per cent of the total technetium-99mis present \nàs Technetium (99mTc) TRODAT-1. Technetium (99niTc) \nTRODAT-1 Injection should be used within 4 hours from the \ndate and time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99,aTc) \nTROD AT-1 Injection is prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer’s instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains (2¡3(N.N'-bis(2-mercaptoclhyl) ethylenediamino- \nmcthyl)-3 (3-(4-chlorophenyl) trepane) (TRODAT-1), stannous \nchloride and may contain transchelating agents such as \ndisodium ethylene diamine tetracetic acid, sodium \nglucoheptonate (GHÁ) other than antimicrobial agents and \nbuffers. Sodium pertechnetate f'9mTc) injection (fission or \nnon-fission) is used with the kit.to make the (99m Tc) TRODAT-\n1 Injection."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma-ray spectrometry as described in \ngeneral chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations^ The \nmost prominent gamma photon of technetium-99m has energy \nof 0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for radiochemical purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification Of the Technetium \n(99,rTc) TRODAT-1."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 8.0. \n\"\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of test solution \nis. assayed using precalibrated radioactivity dose calibrator \nas describedin measurement of radioactivity in general chapter \non Radiopharmaceutical preparations. It is expressed as MBq \n(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date, V being the \nmaximum recommended dose in ml.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnetium-99m.\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on sodium pertechnetate fi9mTc) injection\n(fission) or sodium pertechnetate (\"^Tc) injection (non­\nfission) used for preparation of technetium (99fflTc) TRODAT-\n1 injection.\nRadiochemical purity. Use method Aor method B\nMethod A. Determine by thin layer chromatography (2.4.17), \ncoating the plate with polyethylene terephthalate (PET) foil \nprecoated Polyamide 6 or equivalent. \\ \n:\nUse a plate such that during development the mobile phase \nmigrates oyer a.distance of 10 cm.to 15 cm in about JO.minutés.\nIP 202T\nMobile phase. A mixture of 80 volumes of acetonitrile and 20 \nvolumes of 0-005 :M aqueous solution of dimethyl glutaric \nacid: \n' ■ ■■ \n; \ny\nTest solution. The injection under examination. \n?\nApply to the plate 5 pl to 10.pl of the injêction under \nexamination. Allow the mobile phase to rise 10 cm to 15 cm \nand dry the plate in air. Determine the distribution of \nradioactivity using a suitable:detector. Pertechnetate ion \nexhibits Rf between 0.3-0.5, impurities in colloidal form remain \nat the point of spotting, (99mTc) GHA impurity has an Rf of 0,5- \n0.6, whereas (99mTc) TRODAT-1 complex migrates near to thé \nsolvent front (Rfof 0.7-1.0). The percentage of activity in each \npeak should be calculated and the sum of the percentages of \nradioactiyity corresponding to all impurities in the \nchromatograms obtained should not more than 10.0 per cent'.\nMethod B. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). \n.-■/\nTest sample. The injection under examination.\nChromatographic system. \n• \n---\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.1mm, packed with\noçtadecylsilane bonded.to silica (3 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 80 volumes of acetonitrile,\nand 20 volumes of Ô.005 M aqueous solution oîdimethyf \nglutaric acid\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-;\n- radioactivity detector adjusted for 100 to 200 keV energy,‘ \n- injectionvolume:20pl \nr \n? J\nThe retention time of (99mTc) TRODAT-1 shall have 13 minutes \nto 14 minutes.\nThe sum of the percentages of radioactivity corresponding tq \nall impurities in the chromatograms obtained in test should \nnot more than 10.0 percent.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals, \nas described in section 1.6 in general chapter on \nRadiopharmaceutical preparations ^using,three wister rats. \nThree wister rats weighing 150-200 g should be intravenously\ninjected with equivalent to 20 MBq of (99niTçj TRODAT-1. At\n1 hour post injection rats should be sacrificed and the per \ncent radioactivity in brain should be measured or alternately \nthe rats could be imaged in an animal SPECT camera and the \nper cent activity in the brain can be calculated. The per cent1 \nradioactivity in the brain should be not less than 0.4 per cent \nof injected dose in at least 2 butof 3 fats.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as described \nin général chapter oh Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest. ■ ■\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation: should pass the pyrogen tests (2,2.8.). \n1 ’\nIP 2022\nTHALLOUS (201TI) CHLORIDE INJECTION\nStorage. Store at room temperature withadequateshielding.- Testsolution. amixture of 50 volumes of the preparation under \nLabelling: The label states, in addition \nexamination and the 50 volumes of the 0.05 M Disodium\nspecified under inj ection ( 1 ) the amount of 9>Tc as làbelled E^etate s°lution- 1 : ; 1 /\nTRODAT-1 expressed as total MBq (or mCi) and theradioactive Soaka2.5cnix Ï5.0cih cellulose polyacetaté strip in 0.05 M \nconcentration as MBq per ml (or as mCi pêÿ ml) on Wd^e DisodiumEdetate solution for 45 to 60 minutes. Remove the \nand time of reconstitution; (2) the expirâtibh d^té ahH'iiirie-(3) strip with forceps, taking care to handle the outer edges only, \nthe statement, “Caution - Radioactive MàteHât” ■place the strip between 2 absorbent pads, and blot to remove\nThallous (20lTl) Chloride Injection^\nCategory. Diagnostic\nDescription. Thallous (2O’T1) chloride injection is:asterile, clear, \ncolourless, aqueous solution pgfhaU^\nchloride. The inj ection contains .not- less J^an $0$rper; cent, \nand not more than 116.6 per cent of the content of thallium-\n201 stated on the labehat the reference-date and timestatedon \nthe label. Thallous (^Tlj'fchldrideanjecticin may contain \nsodium chloride to make the solution isotonic with blood as \nwell as suitable antimicrobial preservatives and stabilizing \nagents. The specific radioactivity' is not less than \n3.7 gigabecquerel (100 millicuries) offhallium-201 per milligram \nof thallium at the reference date and. time stated on the label: \nThallous (10lTl) chloride injection should be used within \n7 days from the date of preparation.\nIdentification' ■ \n,i'.\\\nA. Determine by Gamma-ray and X-ray spectrometry using a \ngamma ray and X-ray spectrometer. The prominent gamma \nphotons of thallium-201have energies of 0.135 MeV,\n0.166 MeV and 0.167 MeV and the X-rays have energies of \n0.069 MeV to 0.083 MeV. \n/ : ’J \n'\nB. In the test for radiochemical purity, the. distribution of \nradioactivity contributes to the identification of the \npreparation. \" \n'"},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 7.0.\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivityof an aliquot of Thallous \n(2OIT1) chloride test solution is assayed using precalibrated \nradioactivity dose calibrator. It is expressed as megabecquefels \n(millicuries) per ml at a particular time and date.\nRadionuclidic purity. Determine by gamma-ray and X-ray \nspectrometry the relative amounts of thallium-201, thallium- \n202 and other radionuclidic impurities that may be present. \nNot less than 97.0 per cent of the total radioactivity is due to\nthallium-201. Not more than 2.0 per cent of the total \nradioactivity is due to thallium-202.\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by Zone electrophoresis \n(2.4.12)\n■ \nTâq excess solution. Apply not less than 5 pl of the test solution\nto the centre of the blotted strip, and mark the point of \napplication. Attach the strip to the support bridge of an \nelectrophoresis chamber containing equal portions of 0.05 M \nDisodium Edetate in each side of the chamber. Ensure that\neach end of the strip is in contact with the electrolyte solution. \nAttach the chamber cover and perform the electrophoresis at \n250 volts for 30 minutes. Remove the strip from the chamber \nand allow to air dry without blotting. Determine the \nradioactivity distribution using suitable method. Not less than \n95.0per cent of the radioactivity on the strip migrates towards \nthe cathode as a single peak.\nChemical Purity\nThallium. Transfer 1.0 ml of the Thallous (2O1T1) chloride \ninjection and 1.0 ml of thallium standard solution (2 ppm Tl) \nindividually to two 10 ml screw cap test tubes. To each tube, \nadd the following five solutions (A, B, C, D and E) and mix \nafter each addition: solution A. 2 drops of a solution prepared \nby carefully mixing 18 ml of nitric acid (~100 per cent w/v) and \n82 ml of hydrochloric acid (~25 per cent w/v); solution B. \n1.0 ml Ofsulfosalicylic acid (0.1 mol/1); solution C. 2 drops of \nhydrochloric acid (~25 per cent w/v); solution D. 4 drops of a \nsolution prepared by dissolving 50 mg of rhodamine B in \nhydrochloric acid (-25 per cent w/v) and diluting to 100.0 ml; \nsolution E. 1.0 ml of diisopropyl ether.\nScrew the caps tightly, shake the tubes for 1 minute, releasing \nany pressure build-up by loosening the caps slightly. Recap \nthe tubes and allow the phases to separate. The colour ofthe\nether layer obtained from the injection is not more intensely \ncoloured than that from the thallium standard .solution \n(2 ppm Tl).\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as \ndescribed in general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical \npreparations. The injection may be released for use before\ncompletion of the test.\nBacterial Endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 175 per V EU per ml, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen test (2.2.8).\nStorage. Store in single dose or multiple dose containers at a \ntemperature between 2° to 8°.\nLabeling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injections (1) the time and date of calibration;\nÎ \n4783\n(2). the amount of 201TI as labeled thallous chloride expressed \nas total megabecquerels (millicuries of microcuries) and \nconcentration as megabecquerels (millicuries or microcuries) \nper ml at the time of calibration; (3) the, expiration date and \ntime and; (4) the statement “Caution- Radioactive Material.”\ntechnetium\ncent and not\n99mradioactr \nless than 90.0 -pvt .. . \nas Technetium’- -(‘Wl\nTRODAT-1 Injection"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/teicoplan-in-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Teicoplan in Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Teicoplanin Injection\nThe area of any secondary peak corresponding to teicoplanin\nTeicoplanin Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nTeicoplanin with or without auxiliary substances. It is filled in \na sealed container.\nA2 group is not less than 80.0 per cent; teicoplanin A2.2 group \nis not less than 35.0 per cent and not more than 55.0 per cent. \nTeicoplanin A2.1 group is not more than 20.0 per cent, \nteicoplanin teicoplanin A2.3 group is not more than 20.0 per \ncent, teicoplanin A24 group is not more than 20.0 per cent, \nteicoplanin teicoplanin A2.5 group is not more than 20.0 per \ncent and teicoplanin A3 group is not more than 15.0 per cent. \nThe sum of the areas of all other secondary peak other than \nmesityl oxide with a relative retention time 1.25 times is not \nmore than 5.0 per cent. Ignore the peak due to teicoplanin\n1.25. \nCalculate the content of the different components using the \nfollowing equations.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of Sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nteicoplanin A 2 group = \t\nx 100 \nS. \nS. + 0.83 x S„ + S,\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nS. + 0.83 x Si, + S, \n\t\nx100 \nS 2\nteicoplanin A 2_,\nA2-2 with an area less than 0.5 times of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (b) (0.25 \nper cent).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nSi \n\tx 100 \nteicoplanin A,_, group = S. + 0.83 \nS b\nImpurity A. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTeicoplanin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nTeicoplanin.\nUse the Chromatographic system as described under test for \nComposition and related substances.\nteicoplanin A 23 group = \nS3 \n\tX 100\nS a + 0.83 x S, + S c\nName\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations (Powders\nRelative \nretention time\nS4 \n\t\nX 100 \nteicoplanin A 2_4\nSa + 0.83 x St, + Sc\nfor Injection) and with the following requirements.\nTeicoplann impurity A' \t\n.0.6 \nTeicoplann A2_2 (Retention time: about 18 minutes) 1.0-\nU.suil..Strerigths. 200 mg; 400 mg.\nteicoplanin A 2_, group = \t\n \nS5 \t\nx 100 \nS. + 0.83 x St, + Sc\nDesCription. A white or off white to yellowish powder.\n'4-methylpent-3-en-2-one  (mesityl oxide).\n- \t\n\"\nTEICOPLANIN INJECTION"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Teicoplanin Injection\nThe area of any secondary peak corresponding to teicoplanin\nTeicoplanin Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nTeicoplanin with or without auxiliary substances. It is filled in \na sealed container.\nA2 group is not less than 80.0 per cent; teicoplanin A2.2 group \nis not less than 35.0 per cent and not more than 55.0 per cent. \nTeicoplanin A2.1 group is not more than 20.0 per cent, \nteicoplanin teicoplanin A2.3 group is not more than 20.0 per \ncent, teicoplanin A24 group is not more than 20.0 per cent, \nteicoplanin teicoplanin A2.5 group is not more than 20.0 per \ncent and teicoplanin A3 group is not more than 15.0 per cent. \nThe sum of the areas of all other secondary peak other than \nmesityl oxide with a relative retention time 1.25 times is not \nmore than 5.0 per cent. Ignore the peak due to teicoplanin\n1.25. \nCalculate the content of the different components using the \nfollowing equations.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of Sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nteicoplanin A 2 group = \t\nx 100 \nS. \nS. + 0.83 x S„ + S,\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nS. + 0.83 x Si, + S, \n\t\nx100 \nS 2\nteicoplanin A 2_,\nA2-2 with an area less than 0.5 times of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (b) (0.25 \nper cent).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nSi \n\tx 100 \nteicoplanin A,_, group = S. + 0.83 \nS b\nImpurity A. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTeicoplanin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nTeicoplanin.\nUse the Chromatographic system as described under test for \nComposition and related substances.\nteicoplanin A 23 group = \nS3 \n\tX 100\nS a + 0.83 x S, + S c\nName\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations (Powders\nRelative \nretention time\nS4 \n\t\nX 100 \nteicoplanin A 2_4\nSa + 0.83 x St, + Sc\nfor Injection) and with the following requirements.\nTeicoplann impurity A' \t\n.0.6 \nTeicoplann A2_2 (Retention time: about 18 minutes) 1.0-\nU.suil..Strerigths. 200 mg; 400 mg.\nteicoplanin A 2_, group = \t\n \nS5 \t\nx 100 \nS. + 0.83 x St, + Sc\nDesCription. A white or off white to yellowish powder.\n'4-methylpent-3-en-2-one  (mesityl oxide).\n- \t\n\"\nTEICOPLANIN INJECTION"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. When examined in the range 200 nm to 400 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.005 per cent w/v solution in water shows an absorption \nmaximum at about 278 nm.\nB. In the composition and related substances, The principal \npeaks (teicoplanins A3.1, A2.1, A2.2, A2.3, A2_4 and A2.5) in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 6.0 to 8.0, determined in 5.0 per cent w/v solution \nin the carbon dioxide free  water.\nComposition and Related Substances. Determine by liquid \nchromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution.  Weigh a quantity of injection powder containing \n400 mg of Teicoplanin, disperse in sufficient water with the \naid of ultrasound and dilute to 200.0 ml with water.\nReference solution. A 0.2 per cent w/v solution of teicoplanin \nRS in water.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed  with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 p.m) (Such \nas Inertsil ODS 3V), \n-\nmobile phase: A. a mixture of 850 volumes of a solution \nprepared by dissolving 3.9 g of  sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate in 850 ml of water and 150 volumes of \nacetonitrile, adjusted to pH 6.0 with sodium hydroxide\nsolution.\nB. a mixture of 300 volumes of a solution \nprepared by dissolving 3.9 g of sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate in 300 ml of water and 700 volumes of \nacetonitrile, adjusted to pH 6.0 with sodium hydroxide \nsolution. \n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below,\nflow rate: 1.8 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nMobile phase B\nMobile phase A\nTime \n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\nl00\n30 \t 100 \t\n0\nRelative retention of groups and impurities witliYefereitce to 7: -The_ifea of Oaks corresponding to teicoplanin A, group is \n. \nteicoplanin A2.2 is. \nnot less than 75.0 per cent, teicoplanin  A3 group is not more\n0; 201 8"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/teicoplanin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Teicoplanin Injection","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Teicoplanin Injection\nTeicoplanin Injection is a sterile material consi'stihg,;bf \nTeicoplanin with or without auxiliary substances.lt is filled in \na sealed container.. ..>. ■■ •; ;\nThe mjectioh is'constituted'by dissolving the contents ofthe \nsealed container in the requisite amount of Sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use. \n:\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter: preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer. ' ; -v O.d Hq\nTeicoplanin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nTeicoplanin. \n~\nThe contents of the sealed container: comply: with \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations'(Powders \nfor Inj ection) ;and with thefollowing requirements.\nUsual Strengths. 200 mg; 400 mg.\nDescription. A white or off white to yellowishpowder.\n1P2022\nTEICOPLANIN INJECTION"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Teicoplanin Injection\nTeicoplanin Injection is a sterile material consi'stihg,;bf \nTeicoplanin with or without auxiliary substances.lt is filled in \na sealed container.. ..>. ■■ •; ;\nThe mjectioh is'constituted'by dissolving the contents ofthe \nsealed container in the requisite amount of Sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use. \n:\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter: pr