\nC^Hh^O.^. \n■\nUsual strength. 20 mg \n;"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tenoxicam Tablets\nTenoxicam Tablets contain not less than 92.5 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of tenoxicam, \nC^Hh^O.^. \n■\nUsual strength. 20 mg \n;"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution. ;\nIP2°22\nB. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.1.7), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254. ■■■■■\nMobile phase. A mixture of 4 volumes of anhydrouffbdtifc '■% \nacid, 30 volumes of acetone and 70 volumes '¿1 ? \ndichloromethane. \"\" \n:\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of the powdered tables ■ \ncontaining 20 mg of Tenoxicam in 20 ml of dichloromethane ; \nwith the aid of ultrasound for 15 minutes, centrifuge andUse \nthe supernatant liquid. \n:■ a.\nReference solution; A 6.1 per cent w/v solution oftenoxicam \nIPRS in dichloromethane. J\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. .After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at 254 \nnm„ The principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution corresponds to the spot in the chromatograins \nobtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2)\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of phosphate buffer pH 6.8 prepared by \ndissolving 6.8 g of potassium dihydrogen orthophosphate in \n500 ml of water, add 2 ml of IM sodium hydroxide and dilute \nwith water to 1000 ml. Adjusted to pH 6.8 with IM sodium \nhydroxide or 10 per cent w/v solution of orthophosphoric \nacid as required.\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes. \n'\nWithdraw a suitable volume of medium and filter. Reject the\nfirst few nil of the filtrate and dilute a suitable volume of filtrate \nwith dissolution medium if necessary. Measure the absorbance\nof filtered solution at maximum at about 368 nm (2.4!7). Calculate \nthe content of tenoxicam, C13H} JN3O4S2 in the medium from \nthe absorbance obtained from a solution of known \nconcentration of tenoxicam IPRS in dissolution medium.\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nC13H11N3O4S2.\nRelated substances? Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). •• \n■■ \n: \n' •'< ■\" \n■■ \n7\" ■■\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of acetonitrile and water.\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 100 mg of Tenoxicam, disperse, in 50 ml of the \nsolvent mixture with the aid of ultrasound for 70 minutes with \nintermittent shaking and dilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent \nmixture. Allow to stand for 10 minutes, dilute 5.0 ml of the clear \nsupernatant liquid to 20.0 ml with the mobile phase and filter.\nReference solution faf Dilute 1.0 ml of the lest.solution4p;\n200.0 ml with the mobile phase. \n>\njpi2Ö22"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tenoxicam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tenoxicam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TENOXICAM\nfp.2022\n•ectthe reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nmn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates for \nfovir disoproxil, 800 theoretical plates for lamivudine and\n1 ndO ¿¿¿Q^eticaiplates for efavirenz. The tailing factor for each\nT is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections of each peak is not more than 2.0 per\n...... .. .. ■ . :\njnject the rcference S()luti()n and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of efavirenz Ci4H9C1F3NO2, lamuvidine\nd tenofovir disoproxil fumarate\nciS^oftC^^inthetablets. ,.\nStorage- Store protected from light and moisture.\n¡abelling.The label states the strengthen terms of the amount \nof lamivtidine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate and efavirenz.\nTenoxicam\nTenoxicam is 4-Hydroxy-2-methyl-N-(pyridin-2-yl)-2H-\nIhieno [2,3 -e] 1,2-thiazine-3-carboxamide 1,1 -dioxide.\nTenoxicam contains not less than 9.9.0 per cent and not more \nthan 10Lb per cent of C^HuNat^Si, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory.Analgesic; anti inflammatory.\nDescription. A yellow crystalline powder. It shows \npolymorphism(2.5.11)."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TENOXICAM\nfp.2022\n•ectthe reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nmn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates for \nfovir disoproxil, 800 theoretical plates for lamivudine and\n1 ndO ¿¿¿Q^eticaiplates for efavirenz. The tailing factor for each\nT is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections of each peak is not more than 2.0 per\n...... .. .. ■ . :\njnject the rcference S()luti()n and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of efavirenz Ci4H9C1F3NO2, lamuvidine\nd tenofovir disoproxil fumarate\nciS^oftC^^inthetablets. ,.\nStorage- Store protected from light and moisture.\n¡abelling.The label states the strengthen terms of the amount \nof lamivtidine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate and efavirenz.\nTenoxicam\nTenoxicam is 4-Hydroxy-2-methyl-N-(pyridin-2-yl)-2H-\nIhieno [2,3 -e] 1,2-thiazine-3-carboxamide 1,1 -dioxide.\nTenoxicam contains not less than 9.9.0 per cent and not more \nthan 10Lb per cent of C^HuNat^Si, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory.Analgesic; anti inflammatory.\nDescription. A yellow crystalline powder. It shows \npolymorphism(2.5.11)."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tenoxicam IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of tenoxicam.\nIf the spectra obtained in solid state show difference, dissolve \nthe substance under examination and reference substance\nseparately inminimum volume of methylene chloride: \nEvaporate to dryness and record new spectra using the \nresidues? \n> • > v ‘ \n•"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution in \nnettyene chloride is clear (2.4.1).\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). “ •\nNOTE — Carry out the test protect from light.\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of acètohitrilë and water, \nadjusted to pH 3.2 with dilute orthophosphoric acid!\nTest solution. Dissolve 35 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture with the aid of ultrasound \nand dilute to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with the solvent mixture. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution \nto 1 Q.O ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution fb). Dissolve the content of a vial of\ntenoxicam impurity mixturelPRS (containingimpurities B, G \nand H) in 1.0 ml of the test solution. ;\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steelcolumn 15 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\ndiisopropylcyanosilane bonded to porous silica \n(3.5 pm),\n- column temperature: 35°,\nmobile phase: A. a mixture of 25 volumes of methanol, \n75 volumes of water, adjusted to pH 3.2 with dilute \northophosphoric acid,\nB. a mixture of 25 volumes of water and\n75 volumes of methanol,. adjusted to pH 3.2 with dilute \northophosphoric acid,\n- - a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute’\n- spectrophotometer set at 230 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase\nnmin.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v\no\n96\n4\n5\n96\n4\n16\n76\n•24\n’25\n76\n24\n26\n96\n4\n30’\n96\n4\nCorrection\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor\nTenoxicam impurity A1\n0.10\n. 0.2\nTenoxicam impurity G?\n0.85\n—\nTenoxicam impurity H3\n0.90\n—\nTenoxicam (Retention time:\nabout 12 minutes)\n1.0\n—\nTenoxicam impurity B4\n1.30\n2.0\n'Pyridm^-amine, ;\n24-hydroxy-2-methyl-2Z/-thieno[2,3-e] 1,2-thiazine-3-carboxamide 1,1 - \ndioxide, ’ ’r ';\nTENOXICAM\n[(methylajginp)sulfgiiyl]thiophene-2-!C,ai;boxylic •aqi.d,.; \n: ■; ;■\n“methyl 4-hydroxy-2-methyI-2if-thieno[2,3-e] 1,2-thiazine-3-carbp- \nxylate 1,1-dioxide, ...\nInject reference solution,(b). The test is. not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to tenoxicam and tenoxicam\nimpurity II (or G if peaks are inverted) is not less than!.3 and \nthe resolutibn'betweeii the peaks due to tenoxicam iihpurity G \nandterioxicam impurify H isnot less than 1.3. \n-\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with thetest solution the area of any \npeak correspondirig to tenoxicam impurity A multiplied with \ncorrection factor 0.2 and tenoxicam impurity B multiplied with \ncorrection; factor 2.0, each of,. is ?riot. more than . 1.5. times the \narea of principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (0.15 per cent) the area iof any other\nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0,1 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 3 times the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a)\n(0.3 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 times \nthe, area, of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.05 per cent). ...\nHeavy metals (2.3-.13): 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more thah6.1 per cent.\nWater (2.4.43). Not more than 0,5 per cent, determined on\n1.0 g. \n' \nY\nAssay! Dissolve 0.25 g of the substance under examination in \n5 ml Qipnhydrous formic add and add lO ml of anhydrous \nacetic acid.. Titrate with 0,1 M perchloric acid, determining \nthe end-point potentiometrically ,.(2.4.25). Carry out a blank \ntitration.\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is' equivalent to 0,03374 g of \n/ •\nStorage. Store protected from light."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terazosin-hydrochloride-dihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terazosin Hydrochloride Dihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terazosin Hydrochloride Dihydrate\nCl9H25N5O4,HCl,2H2O ; e , Mol. Wt. 459.9\nTerazosin. Hydrochloride is (7?5')-6,7-dimethoxy-2-[4- \n(tetrahydrofpran-2-carbonyl)piperazin“ 1-yl) quinazolin-4- \nylamine hydrochloride dihydrate.\nTerazosin Hydrochloride contains not less than’99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C19H25N5O4,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow, crystalline powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with terazosin \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of terazosin \nhydrochloride.\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. Al. 0 per cent w/v solution in carbon­\ndioxide free water is clear (2.4.1) and not more intensely \ncoloured thanreference solution YS7 (2;4.1). - ' ■ ’ ■\npH (2.4.24). 3.0 to 5.0, determined in 2.0 per cent w/v solution \nin carbon-dioxide free water. \n.\nImpurities N and O. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n; \n? \n'\"'7 \n•'\nSolvent mixture. 20 volumes of acetonitrile, and 80-volumes \noiyvater.,.. . .. ,.=:. rc..,, =\nTest solution. - Dissolve\" 501 mgof the - substance under \nexamination in 50 ml of the solvent mixture. ■ ■ . '\nReference solution (a). Dissolve'5 mg each 'of2-chloro-6,7- \ndimethoxyquinazolin-4-amine (terazosin impurity A iP'RS)\nand-. A-[[(2RS)~tetrahydr,ofuran-2-yl] carbonyl]piperazine \n1PRS (terazosin impurity NJIPRS in acetonitrile, add 5 ml of \nthe test solution and dilute to 50 ml with acetonitrile. Dilute\n10.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Terazosin Hydrochloride Dihydrate\nCl9H25N5O4,HCl,2H2O ; e , Mol. Wt. 459.9\nTerazosin. Hydrochloride is (7?5')-6,7-dimethoxy-2-[4- \n(tetrahydrofpran-2-carbonyl)piperazin“ 1-yl) quinazolin-4- \nylamine hydrochloride dihydrate.\nTerazosin Hydrochloride contains not less than’99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C19H25N5O4,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow, crystalline powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with terazosin \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of terazosin \nhydrochloride.\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. Al. 0 per cent w/v solution in carbon­\ndioxide free water is clear (2.4.1) and not more intensely \ncoloured thanreference solution YS7 (2;4.1). - ' ■ ’ ■\npH (2.4.24). 3.0 to 5.0, determined in 2.0 per cent w/v solution \nin carbon-dioxide free water. \n.\nImpurities N and O. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n; \n? \n'\"'7 \n•'\nSolvent mixture. 20 volumes of acetonitrile, and 80-volumes \noiyvater.,.. . .. ,.=:. rc..,, =\nTest solution. - Dissolve\" 501 mgof the - substance under \nexamination in 50 ml of the solvent mixture. ■ ■ . '\nReference solution (a). Dissolve'5 mg each 'of2-chloro-6,7- \ndimethoxyquinazolin-4-amine (terazosin impurity A iP'RS)\nand-. A-[[(2RS)~tetrahydr,ofuran-2-yl] carbonyl]piperazine \n1PRS (terazosin impurity NJIPRS in acetonitrile, add 5 ml of \nthe test solution and dilute to 50 ml with acetonitrile. Dilute\n10.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture."},{"name":"TERAZOSIN HYDROCHLORIDE","text":"Reference solution (b). Dilutei 0.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: dissolve 2.8 g of sodium laurylsulphate\nin 1000 ml ofwater and add 11 ini ofa solution containing \n20.24 per cent w/v of triethylàminè and 23.0 per cent \nw/v of orthophosphoric acid and adjusted to pH 2.5 \nwith orthophosphoric acid', mix 60 volumes of this \nsolution with 40 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.0 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference soldi ion (b). The relative retention time \nwith reference to terazosin for l,4-bis[(tctrahydrofuran- \n2-yl)carbonyl]piperazine (terazosin impurity O) is about 0.2, \nfor terazosin impurity N. is about 0.3 and for terazosin impurity \nA is about 0.4.;The test is not valid unless the resolution \nbetween the peaks due to terazosin impurity A and N is not \nless than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (b) and thè test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof each peak corresponding to terazosin impurity N and \nterazosin impurity O is not more than the area of the peak due \nto terazosin in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution(b)(0.1 percent). ■. m «\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n. . • . i- ; :■■■■'■ \n,-\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 100 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 2.0 ml of the test solution to\n100 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 5,0 ml of the solution to \n100 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve the contents of a vial of\nterazosin, far system suitability IPRS (containing, terazosin \nimpurity À, terazosin impurity B (l-(4-hydroxy-6,7- \ndimethoxyquinazolin-2-yl)-4-[[(2R5)-tetrahydrofuran-2-yl] \ncafbonÿl]piperazirie)', téràzosin impurity C (6,7-dimethoxy-2- \n(piperazin-1 -yl)quinazolin-4-amine), terazosin impurity I f D(4- \namino-6,7-dimcthoxyquinazolin-2-yl)-4-[(2/riS')-2-hydroxy- \npentanoyl]piperazine), terazosin impurity K (prazosin) and \nterazosin impurity M (1,4-bis(f’uran-2-ylcarbonyl)piperazine) \nin 10 ml ofthe mobile phase.\nReference so hit ion (c). Dissolve 5 mg of l-(furan~2- \nylcarbònytypiperazine IPRS (terazosin > impurity L■; IPRS) in\n100 ml of the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to \n100ml with the mobilephase. \n. m m\nReference solution (d)._ To 5 mg of 2,2-(piperazine-} 4\ndiyl)bis(6i 7-dimethoxyquinazolin-4-amine) IPRS (terazo^ \nimpurity E IPRS ) add 70 ml of methanol and 30 ml of water \nAllow to stand for at least ,1 hour to dissolve the substance*\nChrmatographic system\n... - a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column temperature: 30°, \n: -\n- mobile phase: mix 2 volumes of triethylamine, 35Q\nvolumes of acetonitrile, and 1650. volumes of a solution \ncontaining 0.6 per cent w/v of sodium citrate and 1425 \nper cent w/v of anhydrous citric acid, . ;\n- flow rate: I ml per minute, \n; ?\n- spectrophotometer set at 245 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl. .\nInject reference solution (b). The test is hot valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to terazosin impurity B and \nterazosin impurity J is not less than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a), (c) and the test solution. Run thè \nchromatogram 4 times the retention time ofthe principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof secondary peak corresponding to terazOsm impurity A, C; \nE and K, each of, is not more than 5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.5 per cent), the area of secondary peak \ncorresponding to terazosin impurity L is not more than the \narea of the corresponding peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (c) (0.1 per cent), the area of secondary \npeak corresponding to terazosin impurity B, J, M, each of, is \nnót more than the area of the principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per \ncent). Multiply the peak areas ofthe impuiritesby the correction \nfactor for calculating the contents, for impuirty C is 0.7 and for \nimpuirty M is 1.6, the area of any other secondary peak is not \nmore than the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained .with reference solution (a) (0.1 pencent) and the sum \nof the areas of all other secondary peaks is not more than 5 \ntimes the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (à) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any \npeàk with an àrèà less than Ò.5 times the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution\n(a) (0.05 per cent). \n■■ < .\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g compiles with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). < :. •\nSulphatedash (2.3.18). Not morethan0.1 percent.\nWater (.2.3,43).; 7.0 to 8,6 per cent, determined on 0.2 g.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.3 g ina mixture of 5 ml of 0.01 M hydrochloric \nacid and 50 ml of methanol. Titrate with 0.1 M sodium \nhydroxide, determining the erid-point potentiometrically \n(2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration. ;. . : ;\n3754\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terazosin-hydrochloride-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terazosin Hydrochloride Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terazosin Hydrochloride Tablets\nTerazosin Tablets contain Terazosin Hydrochloride.\nTerazosin Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of terazosin, \nC191125N504• \nUsual strengths. 1 mg; 2 mg; 4 mg; 5 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Terazosin Hydrochloride Tablets\nTerazosin Tablets contain Terazosin Hydrochloride.\nTerazosin Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of terazosin, \nC191125N504• \nUsual strengths. 1 mg; 2 mg; 4 mg; 5 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Disperse a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 10 \nmg of terazosin in 50 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid with the \naid of ultrasound for 10 minutes and dilute to 100.0 ml with  0.1 \nMhydrochloric acid. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to 100.0 ml \nwith 0.1 M hydrochloric acid and filter. The filtrate when \nexamined in the range from 200 nm to 400 nm (2.4.7) shows an \nabsorption maximum similar to that obtained with the same \nconcentration of terazosin hydrochloride RS in 0.1 Al \nhydrochloric acid.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to that in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2). \nApparatus No. 1, \nMedium. 900 ml of water,"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terazosin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terazosin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TERAZOSIN HYDROCHLORIDE\nftéferencesolution (b). A 0.0625 per cent w/v solution of 2-\nthe solvent mixture. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution\ntofOO-Onil Mth the solvent mixture. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution \n^ôWriû;with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n¿s;® tâ’stainless stéel column 25 cm * 4.0 mm, packed with \nÏAtToctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5s urn) (Such as\nNucleosilC8),\nIgBi'ihidbile phase: a mixture of 7 volumes of 0.012 per cent\nsolution of sodium dodecyl sulphate in. methanol \nand 10 volumes of 0,05 -M potassium dihydrogen \northophosphate 'adjusted to pH 2.8 with\n■ orthophosphoric acid, \n'\n„flow rate: 0.7 mlper minute, ' Oa: . ■ \nfArii spectrophotometer set at 254 nm and 290 nm, :\n. _ injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (a)¿ (b) and the test solution, in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to 2-pyridylamine is not more than the \n:ârçàlpf the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with\nreference solution (b) (0.25 per cent) at 290 nm, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \ninthe chromatogram obtained 