of 80 volumes of a buffer\nsolution prepared by dissolving 1.3 gm of dibasic \nammonium phosphate in 800 ml of water, adjusted to \npH?OwithorfAppAoip^orzcacz^anddiluteto 1000 ml \nwiththewa/er and 20 volumes of methanol, \nflowrate: 1 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 220 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the inference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between two ticarcillin peaks is not less than 1.5 \nand the relative standard deviation for replicate injections is \nnot more than 1.0 per cent for the sum of ticarcillin peaks."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TICARCILLIN AND CLAVULANIC ACID INJECTION\njp 2022\nChromatograph10 system \n.\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4 mm, packed with \n^octadecylsiiancbonded to porous Silica (5 pm),\n; 1 mobiiephasc. a mixture of 80 volumes of a buffer\nsolution prepared by dissolving 1.3 gm of dibasic \nammonium phosphate in 800 ml of water, adjusted to \npH?OwithorfAppAoip^orzcacz^anddiluteto 1000 ml \nwiththewa/er and 20 volumes of methanol, \nflowrate: 1 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 220 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the inference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between two ticarcillin peaks is not less than 1.5 \nand the relative standard deviation for replicate injections is \nnot more than 1.0 per cent for the sum of ticarcillin peaks."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Inject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C!5H]6N2O6S2.\nTicarcillinMonosodium Monohydrqte intended for use in \nthe manufacture qj'Parenteral Preparations without a further \nprocedure for the removal of bacterial, endotoxins complies"},{"name":"Tests","text":"with the following additional requirement\nBacterialendotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.05 Endotoxin \nunit per mg of ticarcillin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ticarcillin-monosodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ticarcillin Monosodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TICARCILLIN MONOSODIUM\nTiearcillin Monosodium b\nTiearcillin. Monosodium Monohydrate . : :\nC15H15N2NaO6S2,H2O \nMol.Wt.424.4\nTiCarcillih Mohosodiumis 4-Thia-1 -azabicyclo[3.2.0]hcptane- \n2-carboxy 1 icacid, 6-[(carboxy-3-thienylaccty 1)amino]-3,3- \ndimethyl-7-oxô, monosodium salt, [2S-[2a, 5a, 60 (S*)]]-, \nmbnohydratc. L';\nTiearcillin Monosodium Monohydrate contains not less than \n890 jig per nig of tiearcillin 'ClsHi6NiO6S2,calculated on the \nanhydrous, basis. . , ,\nCategory. Beta-Lactam Antibacterial. , \n.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TICARCILLIN MONOSODIUM\nTiearcillin Monosodium b\nTiearcillin. Monosodium Monohydrate . : :\nC15H15N2NaO6S2,H2O \nMol.Wt.424.4\nTiCarcillih Mohosodiumis 4-Thia-1 -azabicyclo[3.2.0]hcptane- \n2-carboxy 1 icacid, 6-[(carboxy-3-thienylaccty 1)amino]-3,3- \ndimethyl-7-oxô, monosodium salt, [2S-[2a, 5a, 60 (S*)]]-, \nmbnohydratc. L';\nTiearcillin Monosodium Monohydrate contains not less than \n890 jig per nig of tiearcillin 'ClsHi6NiO6S2,calculated on the \nanhydrous, basis. . , ,\nCategory. Beta-Lactam Antibacterial. , \n.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tiearcillin \nmonosodium mohohydrate IPRS òr with the reference \nspectrum of tiearcillin monòsodiurn.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peaks in the chromatogram \nobtained with the thst solution correspond to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nC. Specific optical rotation (see Test). \n: . \n:\nD. A 5 per cent w/v solution gives reaction (A) of sodium \n(2.3.i)/; \n:\nTests \"• •\nN, A-Dimethylaniline (2.3.21). Not more than 20 ppm,\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 4.0, determined in à1.0 per cent w/V solution \noftiearcillin. \n’\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 181.0°to + 197.0°, \ndetermined in a 1.0 percent w/v solution prepared by dissolving \n6.9 g of monobasic sodium phosphate ya. 800 ml. of \\ water. \nadjusted to pH 6.4 with 10 M sodium hydroxide wad dilute to\n1000 ml with water. \n;\nRelated substances. Determine .by liquid . chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n'■...............................................  ■\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 20 ml of mobile phase A and dilute to 50.0 ml. \nwith mobile phase A. \n; ■ \n. = \n. :\n37961\nReference solution. A0.002 per cent w/v solution of \nmonosodium monohydrate IPRSvn. mobile phase: A. y ;f.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4 mm,packed wi^\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- \nmobile phase: A. a buffer solution prepared by \ndissolving \\ 3 » ol dibasic ammonium phosphate \nwater, adjusted to pH 7.0 with orthbphbsphbric acid \nanddiluteto 1000 ml with water,\n; : \nB. a mixture of 5 0 volumes ^methanol\nand 50 volumes of mobile phase A, . < / h;.;. . .- y.\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below\n- \nflow rate: 1ml per minute, \n.-¡mum?\n- spectrophotometer set at 220 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/y) ,\n. (per cent v/v)\n0\n100\n0\n30\n30\n70;\n40'\n30\n70\n45\n100\n0\nName \nRelative\nretention time\nTiearcillin \n0.95,1.0\nTicarcillinrelatedcompoundA1 \n2.1\n'.Decarboxy tiearcillin; (2,5-3,3-dimethyI-7-oxo-6[[(thiophen-3yi) \nacetyl]-4j-thia-azabicycIo[3,2,0]heptane-2-qarboxyIic acid. <\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between two tiearcillin peaks is not less than 2.0 \nand the relative standard deviation for replicate injections is \nnot more than 5 per cent for the sum of tiearcillin peaks.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the te st solution,' the area of any \npeak corresponding to tiearcillin related compound A is not \nmore than twice the area of the sum of principal peaks in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (4.0 per \ncent) and the area of any other secondary peak is not more \nthan 1.25 times the area of the sum of principal peaks in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (2.5 per \ncent).\nWater (2.3 .43); 4.0 per cent to 6.0 per cent\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 20 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 70 ml of the mobile phase, with the aid 0 \nultrasound and dilute to 200.0 ml with the.mobile phase. +\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \nmono Sodium monohydrate IPRS in the mobile phase;"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tick-borne-encephalitis-vaccine-inactivated","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tick-Borne Encephalitis Vaccine (Inactivated^"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TICK-BORNE ENCEPHALITIS VACCINE (INACTIVATED)\nantibody titre is calculated using the usual statistical methods. \nA positive control serum and a negative- control serum are \nincluded on each plate to monitor assay performance,\nReagents and equipment\nf Round-bottomed, rigid polystyrene microtitre plates.\nflat-bottomed ELIS A plates.\nTetanus toxin or tetanus toxoid.\n; Clostridium tetaniguinea-pig antiserum (for vaccines-human\nuse) reference preparation.\n; Equine anti-tetanus IgG.\n: Peroxidase-conjiigated equine anti-tetanus IgG.\nCarbonate buffer pH 9.6. Dissolve 1.’5 goianhydrous sodium\n: carbonate, 2.39 g of sodium hydrogen carbonate and 0.2 g of\nsodium azide in 1000 ml of water, adjust to pH 9.6 and autoclave\nat 121° for 20 min.\nSodium acetate buffer pH 5.5. Dissolve 90.2 g of anhydrous \nsodium acetate in 900 ml of water, adjust to pH 5.5 using a\nsaturated solution of citric acid monohydrate and dilute to\n1000 ml with water..\nPhosphate buffered saline pH 7.2 (PBS). Dissolve 135.0 g of \nsodium chloride, 20.55 g of disodium hydrogen phosphate \ndihydrate and 4.80 g of sodium dihydrogen phosphate \nmonohydrate in water and dilute to 15 litres with the same \nsolvent. Autoclave at 100° for 60 min.\nDiluent buffer. PBS containing 0.5 per cent w/v of bovine \nalbumin and 0.05 per cent w/v of polysorbate 80.\nBlock buffer. PBS containing 0.5 per cent w/v of bovine \nalbumin. . ,,\nTetramethylbenzidine solution. 0.6 per cent w/v solution of \ntetramethyl-benzidine in alcohol. The substance dissolves \nwithin 30-40 min at room temperature.\nPeroxidase substrate. Mix 90 nil of water, 10 ml of sodium \nacetate buffer pH 5.5,1.67 ml of tetramethylbehzidine solution \nand 20 pl of strong hydrogen peroxide solution.\nWashing solution. Tap water containing 0.5 g/1 ofpolysorbate \n80.\nMethod\nBlock the round-bottomed: polystyrene microtitre plates by \nplacing in each well 150 pl of block buffer. Cover the plates \nwith a lid or sealer. Incubate in a humid atmosphere at 37° for\n1 h. Wash the plates thoroughly with washing solution. Place \n100 pl of PBS in each well. Place 100 pl of reference guinea pig \ntetanus. antitoxin in the first well ;bf a row. Place 100. pl of \nundiluted test sera in the first, well of therequired number of \nrows. Using a multichannel micropipette, make it two fold serial \ndilutions across the plate (up to column 10), by transfer of 100\npl to the following well. Discard 100 pl from the last column so \nthat all wells contain 100 pl. Prepare 0.1 Lf'ml solution of tetanus \ntoxin or toxoid using PBS a diluent. Add 40 pl of this solution \nto all wells except those column 12. The wells ofrow 11 area \npositive control. Add 40 pl of PBS to the wells of column 12\n(negative control). Shake the plates gently and cover them \nwith lids. Coat the ELISA plates: immediately before use make \na suitable dilution of equine anti-tetanus IgG in carbonate \nbuffer pH 9.6 and add 100 pl to all wells. Incubate the 2 series \nof plates overnight in a humid atmosphere at 37°. To avoid \ntemperature gradient effects, do not stack more than 4 plate \nhigh. Cover the plates with lids. On the following day, wash \nthe ELISA plates thoroughly with washing solution. Block \nthe plates by placing in each well 125 pl of block buffer. Incubate \nat 37° in a humid atmosphere for 1 h. Wash the plates \nthoroughly with washing solution. Transfer 100 pl of the pre­\nincubation mixture from the polystyrene plates to the \ncorresponding wells of the ELISA plates, starting with column\n12 and then from 1 to 11. Cover the plates with a lid. Incubate \nat 37° in a humid atmosphere for 2 h. Wash the ELISA plates \nthoroughly with washing solution. Make a suitable dilution \n(a 1 in 4000 dilution has been found suitable) of the peroxidase- \nconjugated equine anti-tetanus IgG in diluent buffer. Add\n100 pl of the dilution to each well, and cover the plates with a \nlid,: Incubate at 37° in a,humid atmosphere for 1.5 h. Wash the \nELISA plates thoroughly with washing solution. Add 100 pl \nofperoxidase"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TICK-BORNE ENCEPHALITIS VACCINE (INACTIVATED)\nantibody titre is calculated using the usual statistical methods. \nA positive control serum and a negative- control serum are \nincluded on each plate to monitor assay performance,\nReagents and equipment\nf Round-bottomed, rigid polystyrene microtitre plates.\nflat-bottomed ELIS A plates.\nTetanus toxin or tetanus toxoid.\n; Clostridium tetaniguinea-pig antiserum (for vaccines-human\nuse) reference preparation.\n; Equine anti-tetanus IgG.\n: Peroxidase-conjiigated equine anti-tetanus IgG.\nCarbonate buffer pH 9.6. Dissolve 1.’5 goianhydrous sodium\n: carbonate, 2.39 g of sodium hydrogen carbonate and 0.2 g of\nsodium azide in 1000 ml of water, adjust to pH 9.6 and autoclave\nat 121° for 20 min.\nSodium acetate buffer pH 5.5. Dissolve 90.2 g of anhydrous \nsodium acetate in 900 ml of water, adjust to pH 5.5 using a\nsaturated solution of citric acid monohydrate and dilute to\n1000 ml with water..\nPhosphate buffered saline pH 7.2 (PBS). Dissolve 135.0 g of \nsodium chloride, 20.55 g of disodium hydrogen phosphate \ndihydrate and 4.80 g of sodium dihydrogen phosphate \nmonohydrate in water and dilute to 15 litres with the same \nsolvent. Autoclave at 100° for 60 min.\nDiluent buffer. PBS containing 0.5 per cent w/v of bovine \nalbumin and 0.05 per cent w/v of polysorbate 80.\nBlock buffer. PBS containing 0.5 per cent w/v of bovine \nalbumin. . ,,\nTetramethylbenzidine solution. 0.6 per cent w/v solution of \ntetramethyl-benzidine in alcohol. The substance dissolves \nwithin 30-40 min at room temperature.\nPeroxidase substrate. Mix 90 nil of water, 10 ml of sodium \nacetate buffer pH 5.5,1.67 ml of tetramethylbehzidine solution \nand 20 pl of strong hydrogen peroxide solution.\nWashing solution. Tap water containing 0.5 g/1 ofpolysorbate \n80.\nMethod\nBlock the round-bottomed: polystyrene microtitre plates by \nplacing in each well 150 pl of block buffer. Cover the plates \nwith a lid or sealer. Incubate in a humid atmosphere at 37° for\n1 h. Wash the plates thoroughly with washing solution. Place \n100 pl of PBS in each well. Place 100 pl of reference guinea pig \ntetanus. antitoxin in the first well ;bf a row. Place 100. pl of \nundiluted test sera in the first, well of therequired number of \nrows. Using a multichannel micropipette, make it two fold serial \ndilutions across the plate (up to column 10), by transfer of 100\npl to the following well. Discard 100 pl from the last column so \nthat all wells contain 100 pl. Prepare 0.1 Lf'ml solution of tetanus \ntoxin or toxoid using PBS a diluent. Add 40 pl of this solution \nto all wells except those column 12. The wells ofrow 11 area \npositive control. Add 40 pl of PBS to the wells of column 12\n(negative control). Shake the plates gently and cover them \nwith lids. Coat the ELISA plates: immediately before use make \na suitable dilution of equine anti-tetanus IgG in carbonate \nbuffer pH 9.6 and add 100 pl to all wells. Incubate the 2 series \nof plates overnight in a humid atmosphere at 37°. To avoid \ntemperature gradient effects, do not stack more than 4 plate \nhigh. Cover the plates with lids. On the following day, wash \nthe ELISA plates thoroughly with washing solution. Block \nthe plates by placing in each well 125 pl of block buffer. Incubate \nat 37° in a humid atmosphere for 1 h. Wash the plates \nthoroughly with washing solution. Transfer 100 pl of the pre­\nincubation mixture from the polystyrene plates to the \ncorresponding wells of the ELISA plates, starting with column\n12 and then from 1 to 11. Cover the plates with a lid. Incubate \nat 37° in a humid atmosphere for 2 h. Wash the ELISA plates \nthoroughly with washing solution. Make a suitable dilution \n(a 1 in 4000 dilution has been found suitable) of the peroxidase- \nconjugated equine anti-tetanus IgG in diluent buffer. Add\n100 pl of the dilution to each well, and cover the plates with a \nlid,: Incubate at 37° in a,humid atmosphere for 1.5 h. Wash the \nELISA plates thoroughly with washing solution. Add 100 pl \nofperoxidase substrate to each well. A blue colour develops. \nIncubate the plates at room temperature. Stop the reaction at \na given time (within 10 min) by the addition of 100 pl of 2 M \nsulphuric acid to each well in the same order as the addition \nof substrate. The colour changes from blue to yellow. Measure \nthe absorbance (2.4.7) at 450 nm immediately after addition of \nthe sulphuric acid or maintain the plates in the dark until \nreading.\n(D) Any other validated serological assay in guinea-pigs/mice \napproved by National Regulatory Authority.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the human dose (ml); (2) the \nminimum units per single human dose, or the minimum \nInternational Units per single human dose if potency test done \nby challenge method; (3) the name and the amount ¡of the \nadsorbent and preservative; (4) that the vaccine must be \nshaken before use; (5) that the vaccine is not to be frozen.\nTick-borne Encephalitis Vaccine \n(Inactivated)\nTick-borne Encephalitis Vaccine (Inactivated): is a liquid \npreparation of a suitable strain of tick-borne encephalitis virus \ngrown in cultures of chick-embryo cells or other suitable cell \ncultures and inactivated by a suitable, validated method.\n4477\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTICK-BORNE ENCEPHALITIS VACCINE (INACTIVATED)\nGeneral provisions , \n;\nThe vaccine complies ;with;the General Requirements of"},{"name":"production","text":"Vaccines for HumanUse. \n■ ; ; J\nProduction of the vaccine is based on a virus seed lot system.\nharvest.\nThe production method shall have been shown to yield\nconsistently vaccines comparable with the vaccine of proven\nclinical efficacy and safety in man. Thevirus ip the final vaccine\nshall not have undergone more passages from the master seed\nlot than the virus in’.me vaccine used'in.clinical trials.\nThe production method is validated to demonstrate that the\nproduct, if tested, would comply with the test for abnormal\ntoxicity and immunogenicity. \n> \n:\nSubstrate for virus propagation\nThe virus is propagated in chick embryo cells prepared from\neggs derived from a chicken flock free from specified pathogens\n(2.7.7) or in other suitable cell cultures.\neggs de\n¡SEED LOT\nThe strain of virus used is identified by his torical records that\ninclude information on the origin of the strain and its\nsubsequent manipulation. Virus seed lots are stored at or below\n-60\". '\nOnly a seed lot that complies with the following requirements\nmay be used for virus propagation."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Each seed lot is identified as containing the vaccine strain of\ntick-bome encephalitis virus by a suitable immunochemical\nmethod, preferably using monoclonal antibodies.\nVirus concentration. The virus concentration of each seed\nlot is determined by titration in statable cell cultures to monitor\nconsistency of production.\nExtraneous agents (2.7.3). Each seed lot complies with the\nrequirements for extraneous agents inviral vaccines forhuman\nuse; the tests in cell cultures are carried but in human and\nsimian cells only. \nT m • ■_ ■■\nMycoplasma (2.7.4).Complies with the test for absence of 1\nmycoplasma.\nPROPAGATION AND HARVEST\nAll processing of the cell cultures .if.performedunder aseptic\nconditions in an area where no other cells are being handled.\nSerum and trypsin used in the preparation of cell suspensions\nand media used must be shown to be free from extraneous\nagents; The cell culture media may Contain a pH indicator\nsuch as phenol red and approved antibiotics at the: lowest\nENCEPHALITIS VACCINE (INACTIVATED)\nTICK-BORNE/ENLUrn. ... ~..................................... ___..........\nBacterial andfungalcontanrination. Complies withthe test\n^2022\nmt° (2-23> used\nIP 2022 ' ■\nfor sterility (2.2.1 !)• •\nrn 9 Complies with the test for pyrogens. Inject\nPyrogens (2 A8)X<W \nQne\npurification\nSeveral inactivated single harvests may be pooled before\n^infected\nConcentration and purification by suitable methods, preferably\nemployed for vaccine production is set aside as w\na va a f X test for pyrogens. If used, the content .should\n. hy continuous-flow, sucrosé density-gradient centrifugation.\nceil cultures (control cells).\nOnly a single harvest that complies with the following,\nOnly a purified, inactivated harvest that complies with the \n; following requirements may be used in the preparation of final\nbe «ata the limits approved by the National Regulatory\nrequirements may be used in the preparation of the inactivated\n€\nauthority.\ny bulk vaccine.\ni; Bacterial and fungal contamination. Complies with the test\n• ,i,.(1>i-mincd by comparing the dose necessary \nThe potency is ortion of mice against the effects of a"},{"name":"Assay","text":"for sterility (2.2.11 ), carried out using 10 ml for each medium."},{"name":"Identification","text":"The single harvest is shown to containtick-bome encephalitiBO\nvirus by a.suitable immunochemical method, preferably, using^\nSpecific activity. Determine the antigen content of the purified,\nto protect a given~ \nencephalitis virus, administered\nlethal dose o \nwith the quantity of a reference preparation\nintraperitoneal , vaccine necessary to provide the\n: inactivated harvest by a suitable immunochemical method\nmonoclonal antibodies, or by virus neutralization in cell\n(2.2.14). Determine the total protein content by a suitable\ncultures. \n.\nBacterial and fungal contamination. Complies with the testy\nmethod. The specific activity, calculated as the antigen content \nper unit mass of protein, is within the limits approved for the\nof tick-bome \nan approved reference\nsame protection. suitabie preparation of tick-borne\nvirus an aPPtoved StIain f01 use as the \nsx,kdk „ b\nfor sterility (2.2.11), carried out using 10 ml for each medium.\nfound suitable for a given vaccine. .\nMycoplasma (2.7.4). Complies with the test for mycoplasmas'\nspecific product.\ncarried out using 1 ml for each medium. \n. . v l ■\nControl cells. The control cells comply with the tests for\nFINAL BULK VACCINE\nThe final bulk vaccine is prepared from one or more purified,\nThe following is cited as an example of a method that has been\nextraneous agents (2.7.3). If the vaccine.is produced using a\ninactivated harvests.\nOnly a final bulk vaccine that complies with the following\nV Attribution of test animals. Use healthy mice\nSelection and i^t\ng derived from the same stock\nweighing between \nsix groups of a suitabl\nDistribute1 ® \nkements for vahdity of the test; for titration\nsize to meet th q - use not fewer than four group\ncell-bank system, the control cells comply with a test for\nrequirement may be used in the preparation of the final lot.\nidentification, \n; \n....\nVirus concentration. Determine the virus concentration by\nBacterial and fungal contamination. Cany 