Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tigecycline Injection\nTigecycline Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nTigecycline with or without excipients. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile, Water for \nInjections brother suitable, solvent,immediately before use.,\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections). \n;\n3800\nI\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediat S \nafter preparation but, in any case,, within the pefjl \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nTigecycline Injection contains not less than 96.0 per cent all \nnot more than 116.0 per cent of the stated amount^ \ntigecycline, C29l I39N5O8.\nUsual strength. 50 mg per vial.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparation \n(Powders for Injections) and with thefollowing requirement^"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tigecycline Injection\nTigecycline Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nTigecycline with or without excipients. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile, Water for \nInjections brother suitable, solvent,immediately before use.,\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections). \n;\n3800\nI\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediat S \nafter preparation but, in any case,, within the pefjl \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nTigecycline Injection contains not less than 96.0 per cent all \nnot more than 116.0 per cent of the stated amount^ \ntigecycline, C29l I39N5O8.\nUsual strength. 50 mg per vial.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparation \n(Powders for Injections) and with thefollowing requirement^"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). i"},{"name":"Tests","text":"NOTE — Handle standard and sample under moisture \ncontrolled condition, to prevent degradation. ' \n?\npH (2.4.24). 4.5 to 5.5, determined in the reconstituted solution\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n•?.\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 4.35 g of dibasic potassium \nphosphate and 0.5 g of sodium bisulphite in 900 ml ofw/e^\nadjusted to pH 8.0 with 1 Mpotassium hydroxide and dilute \nto 1000 ml with water.\nNOTE — Protect the solutions from light using low-actinic \nglass ware. Store these solutions at 10° immediately after \npreparation. \n; \n?'■■■\nTest solution. Mix the content of 5 vials. Disperse a quantity \ncontaining 50 mg of Tigecycline in the solvent: mixture and \ndilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture. - v :\nReference solution (a). A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \ntigecycline IPRS in the solvent mixture. .,f.;'fii\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.05 -per ceat \nw/v of tigecycline IPRS and 0.00024 per cent w/v of i(gecyc/uie \nimpurity B IPRS in the solvent mixture, .7.f\nReference solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml oiTefcrence solution(a) \nto 20.0 ml with the solvent mixture. \n!, \n.\nChromatographic system . - \n, -. .\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm * 4.6 mm, packed wiib\noctadecylsilane bonded tb porous silica (3 pm),\n- sample temperature: 10°,\n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 95 volumes of a buffer\nsolution prepared by dissolving 4.58 g of \npotassium phosphate and 0.98 g of disdfiiim^l^, P\n1000 ml of warez; adjusted topJ^ 6.4:^^ \northophosphoric acid and 5 volumes of aceionitri^>\nB. a mixture of 50 volumes .of buffer \nsolution prepared by dissolving 8.7 g of dibasic \n^:fr^s[umphosphate an<-i 1 -96 g of disodium edetate in\njO((Ç( rnil of water, adjusted to pH 6.4 with \n-ff^phosphoric acid and 50 volumes of acetonitrile,\n-¡^programme using condib°ns given below, \ngyrate;: 1mlper minute, \n.¿¿gçttophotorneter set at 248 nm, \n.\niniection volume. 25 pl.\n,-g \n\"\no-Time\n\\ :? Mobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n;?t ■ \n85\n15\n57\n43\n: i- \n0\n100\n0 ' ' \"\n100\nSÄC:\n85\n15\n85\n15\nRelative \nretention time\nTigecycline open ring1-2\n0.36\ntigecycline 12-oxo-11 -hydroxy3\n0.55\nTigecycline impurity B4\n0.64\nTigecyclineepimer5\n0.74”\nTigecycline\n1.0\nTigecyclinequinine analog6\n1.3\nMinocycline\n1.6\nTigeiiytline tricyclic analog7\n’ 1.7\n‘To beirepprtedJf.there is a peak with maximum absorbance at either \n390 or 640 nm. The impurity is quantitated at 248 nm. The system \nmay. resolve two peaks. The limit is for the sum of the two peaks,\n\\3S,4Ji)-.7-.[2-(teri-Butylamino)acetamido]-3-[4-carbanioyl-2- \n(dimethylarnino)-3,5,6-trihydroxybenzyl]~5-(dimethylimino)-l,4,8- \ntrihydroxy- 3,4,5,8-tetrahydronaphthalene-2-carboxylate,\n-J(4S ,4aS, 12 a S) -9 -[ 2 - (t e r t - B u t y 1 a m i n o) a c e t a m i d o ] - 4,7- \nbis(dimethylamino)- 3,10,11,12a-tetrahydrpxy-1,12-dioxo-l,4,4a,5,\n12,12a-hexahydro-2-naphthacenecarboxamide,\n40^a^.5a7^i2aS)-9-Amino-4,7-bis(dimethylaminoj-3,l 0,12,12a-\nletrahydroxy-1,1 T-dioxo-l,4,4a,5,5a,6,l-1,12a-octahydrotetracene-2- \ncarboxamide hydrochloride,\n^,4aS,5a./i,I2aS)-9-[2-(/er/-BLitylaniino)acetainido|-4,7- \nMdimethyiaminO)-l,4,4a,5,5a,6,ll,12a-octahydro-3,10,12,12a- \ntetrahydroxy-1,11- dioxo-2-naphthacenecarboxamide,\n^•4-({6-[2-(/eri-ButyIamino)acetamido]-8-(dimethylamino)-5- \n._ydroxy-4-oxo-l,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalen-2-yl}methyl)-2,5-\ns ydroxy- 3,6-dioxocyclohexa-l,4-dienecarboxamide,\nJM’^^>4b7f,10aA, 11 aS)-7-[2-(/err-Butylamino)acetamido]-9-\n। ^¿tKyramino)-1,4',4a,6-tetrahydroxy-2,5,1 2 -trioxo-\n~»^a,5,10,10a,l l.-l la- octahydro-1,4b-methanobenzo[b]fluorene-\n3-carboxamide.\ntigecygeine .injection\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and (c). The ¿test is not valid \nunless the resolution between the peaks due to tigecycline \nimpurity B and tigecycline epimer is not less than 1.5, in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b), the tailing \nfactor is between 0.7 to 1.5 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per cent,ip the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) and signal- \nto- noise ratio is not less than 10 in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (c).\nInject reference solution (a) and thje. test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to tigecycline open ring is not more than\n0.15 times the area of .the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.15 per cent). The area \nof any peak corresponding to tigecycline 12-oxo-11 -hydroxy \nand tigecycline tricyclic analog,each of, is not more than 0.5 \ntimes the . area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.5 per cent). The area of \nany peak corresponding to tigecycline quinine analog is not \nmore than 0.3 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.3 per \ncent). The area of any peak corresponding to tigecycline \nimpurity B is not more than 0.7 times the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.7 per cent). The area of any peak corresponding to \ntigecycline epimer is not more than twice the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (2.0 per cent), the area of any other secondary peak is not \nmore than 0.2 times the area of the principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.2 per \ncent) and the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks is not \nmore than 6 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (6.0 per \ncent).\nSterility (2.2.1.1). Complies with the test for sterility.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 1.75 Endotoxin \nUnit per mg of tigecycline.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 4.35 g of dibasic potassium \nphosphate and 0.5 g of sodium bisulphite in 900 ml of water,\nadjusted to pH 8.0 with 1 Mpotassium hydro"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tigecycline","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tigecycline"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Inject reference solution (a), (b) and (c).The test is not valid \nunless the resolution between the peaks due to tigecycline \nepimer and tigecycline impurity B is not less than 1.5, in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b), the tailing \nfactor is between 0.7 to 1.5 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per cent in the \nchromatogram obtained, with reference solution (a) and the \nsignal- to- noise ratio is not less than 10 in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c).\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to tigecycline open ring is not more than \n0.02 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.02 per cent); The area \nof any peak corresponding to tigecycline 12-oxo-11-hydroxy, \ntigecycline quinine analog, tigecycline tricyclic analog and at \nrelative retention time 0.12,0.23,0.5,0.76,1.5,1.9, each of, is \nnotmorethan 0.15 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.15 per \ncent). The area of any peak corresponding to tigecycline \nimpurity B and minocycline, each of, is not more than \n0.25 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.25 per cent)..The area \nof any peak corresponding to tigecycline epimer is not more \nthan the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (1.0 per cent), the area of \nany other secondary peak is not more than 0.1 times the area \nof the principalpeak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (0.1 per cent) and the sum of areas of all \nthe secondary peaks is not more than 2.2 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (2.2 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2,3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. ;\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 2.5 per cent, determined on \n0.5 g in 10 ml of dimethyl sulfoxide.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2,4.14).\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 4.35 g of dibasic potassium \nphosphate and 0.5 g of sodium bisulphite in 900.ml of water,\nadjusted to pH 8.0 with 1 Mpotassium hydroxide and dilute \nto 1000 ml with water.\nNOTE—Protect the solutions from light. Store these solutions \nat 10° immediately after preparation.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg; of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture..\nReference solution (a). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \ntigecycline IPRS in the solvent mixture."},"sections":[{"name":"TIGECYCLINE","text":"Inject reference solution (a), (b) and (c).The test is not valid \nunless the resolution between the peaks due to tigecycline \nepimer and tigecycline impurity B is not less than 1.5, in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b), the tailing \nfactor is between 0.7 to 1.5 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per cent in the \nchromatogram obtained, with reference solution (a) and the \nsignal- to- noise ratio is not less than 10 in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c).\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to tigecycline open ring is not more than \n0.02 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.02 per cent); The area \nof any peak corresponding to tigecycline 12-oxo-11-hydroxy, \ntigecycline quinine analog, tigecycline tricyclic analog and at \nrelative retention time 0.12,0.23,0.5,0.76,1.5,1.9, each of, is \nnotmorethan 0.15 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.15 per \ncent). The area of any peak corresponding to tigecycline \nimpurity B and minocycline, each of, is not more than \n0.25 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.25 per cent)..The area \nof any peak corresponding to tigecycline epimer is not more \nthan the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (1.0 per cent), the area of \nany other secondary peak is not more than 0.1 times the area \nof the principalpeak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (0.1 per cent) and the sum of areas of all \nthe secondary peaks is not more than 2.2 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (2.2 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2,3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. ;\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 2.5 per cent, determined on \n0.5 g in 10 ml of dimethyl sulfoxide.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2,4.14).\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 4.35 g of dibasic potassium \nphosphate and 0.5 g of sodium bisulphite in 900.ml of water,\nadjusted to pH 8.0 with 1 Mpotassium hydroxide and dilute \nto 1000 ml with water.\nNOTE—Protect the solutions from light. Store these solutions \nat 10° immediately after preparation.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg; of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture..\nReference solution (a). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \ntigecycline IPRS in the solvent mixture."},{"name":"TIGECYCLINE","text":"Reference solution (b). Dissolve 20 mg of tigecycline IPRS in\n10 ml of water. Add l or 2 drops trifluoro acetic acid, and heat \nthe solution at about 65° fori 5 minutes and cool. Dilute 5.0 ml \nof the solution to 100.0 ml with water. * ■\nChromatographic system \n:\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsi lane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- sample temperature: ! 0°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 86 volumes of à buffer solution\n; prepared by dissolving 4.3 5, g of dibasic potassium \n.phosphate and,0.93.g ft disodium edetate in: 1000 ml of\nwater, adjusted to pH 6.2 with orthophosphoric acid \nand 14 volumes : of. \n; ,\n, - flowrate: 1 ml per minute, ...\n~ spectrophotometer set at 248 nm, :\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to tigecycline and \ntigecycline epimer is not less than 3.0 in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b), the tailing factor is \nbetween 0.7 to 1.5 and the relative standard deviation for \nreplicate injections is not more than 1.0 percent in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of\nTigecycline intended for use in the manufacture of parenteral \npreparations without a further appropriate procedure for\nthe removal of bacterial endotoxins complies with the \nfollowing additional requirement.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2:3). Not more than 1.0 Endotoxin Unit \nper mg of tigecycline.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture, under \nnitrogen, at a temperature 2° to 8°. \n-■■■■- -o-.>\nLabelling. When it is intended for usé in preparing injectable \ndosage forms, the label states that it is sterile or must be \nsubjected to further processing during the: preparation of \ninjectable dosage forms."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/timolol-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Timolol Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TIMOLOL EYE DROPS\nTimolol Eye Drops\nTimolol Maleate Eye Drops\nTimolol Eye Drops are a sterile solution of Timolol Maleate in \nPurified Water. They may contain suitable antimicrobial \npreservatives, buffering agents, stabilisers and suitable \nsubstances to increase ,the viscosity of the; solution. \n>\nTimolol Eye Drops contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of timolol, \nC13H24N4O3S. \n, \n.... ... \n■■\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 0.25 per cent w/v and \n0.5 per cent w/v of timolol."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TIMOLOL EYE DROPS\nTimolol Eye Drops\nTimolol Maleate Eye Drops\nTimolol Eye Drops are a sterile solution of Timolol Maleate in \nPurified Water. They may contain suitable antimicrobial \npreservatives, buffering agents, stabilisers and suitable \nsubstances to increase ,the viscosity of the; solution. \n>\nTimolol Eye Drops contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of timolol, \nC13H24N4O3S. \n, \n.... ... \n■■\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 0.25 per cent w/v and \n0.5 per cent w/v of timolol."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Add a volume containing 50 mg of timolol to an equal \nvolume of carbonate buffer pH 9.7 and extract with two \nquantities, each of 40 ml, of dichloromethane. Reserve the \naqueous layer for testB. Filter the combined dichloromethane \nextracts with anhydrous sodium sulphate and evaporate to \ndryness. Dry the residue at 60p at a pressure of 2 kPa for\n15 minutes.\nOn the; residue; determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare; the spectrum with that \nobtained with timolol maleate IPRS, treated in the same \nmaimer or with the reference spectrum of timolol.\nB,. Filter the aqueous layer reserved in test A an d evaporate to \nabout 1ml. Add 1 ml of bromine so lution, heatin a.water-bath\nfor 10 minutes, boil, cool and add. 0.1 ml of the solution to a \nsolution of 10 mg resorcinol\\n 3 ml of sulphuric, acid; a bluish \nblack colour is produced on. heating in a water-bath for\n15 minutes. \n.\nTests ■’/” \n■\npH (2.4.24), 6.5 to 7.5., .. \n. '■ \n,\nRelated substances Determine * by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \nL,.,: • \n... .\nTest solution. Use the undiluted eye drops. \n’;\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1 volume of the eye drops to\n250 volumes with the mobile phase. \n: \n.6\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 volume of the eye drops to \n500 volumes with the mobile phase. . \n,\nReference solution (c). A0.3 per cent w/v solution of maleic \nadd in the mobile phase; ■ Lu ; m : ;; \nu L ;■ •\nChromatographic system 1 \n; : \n! L ■\n' a stainless \"steer column 20 cm x 4 mm, packed with\n, . octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (10 pm), . . .\n— mobile phase: a mixture of 57.5 volumes of methanol\nand 42.5 volumes of 0.02 Msodium octanesulphonate, \nadjusted to pH 3.0 with glacial acetic acid, W?''L\"\n- flowrate: 2 ml per minute, < \n- spectrophotometer set at 295 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pJL\nInject reference solution (c), for identification of peak due to\nmaleicacid. - \n\" \n-i\"''’ \n1 \n; 4\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test soluttpn^uQ \nchromatogram of the test solution for 4 times the retention \ntime of the principal peak. In the chromatogram obtained with \nthe test solution the area of any secondary peak, other than \nthe peak corresponding to maleic acid, is not greater than the \narea of thè peak ih thè chromatogram obtained withiéféfèhfe \nsolution (à) (0.4 per cent) and not more than two such peaks \nhave an area greater than that of the peakbbtainedwith \nreference solution (b) (0.2 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Eye Drops ?\nAssay. To a volume containing about 25 mg of timolol add \nwater to produce 50.0 ml and mix. To 5,0 ml add 15 ml of \ncarbonate buffer pH 9.7 and extract with three quantities, \neach of 20 ml , and one quantity of 10 ml of toluene. Wash each \nextract successively with the same 10-ml volume of carbonate \nbuffer pH 9.7. Combine the toluene extracts and extract with \nfour quantities, each of 20 ml, of 05 M sulphuric acid. \nCombine the extracts, dilute to 100.0 ml, filter and measure the \nabsorbance of .the filtrate at the maximum at about 295. nm \n(2.4,7), using as the .blank a. solution prepared by treating \n5.0 ml of water in the same manner beginning at the words \n“add 15.ml of carbonate bufferpH9.7...\".\nCalculate the content of CI3H24N4.O3S taking 279 as the specific \nabsorbance at 295 nm. \n.\nStorage. Store protected from lightand moisture; 'm\nLabelling. The label states (1) the strength in terms of the \nequivalent amount of timolol; (2) the names and concentration \nof any added antimicrobial preservatives. ■"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/timolol-maleate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Timolol Maleate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Timolol Maleate Tablets\nTirnolol Tablets contain not less than 90.0 percent and not\nmore than 110.0per centofthe stated amount oftimolol maleate,\nCI3H24N403S,C~404'\nUsual strength. 10 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Timolol Maleate Tablets\nTirnolol Tablets contain not less than 90.0 percent and not\nmore than 110.0per centofthe stated amount oftimolol maleate,\nCI3H24N403S,C~404'\nUsual strength. 10 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A.To a quantity ofthe powdered tablets containing 70 mg of\nTimolol Maleate add 20 ml of carbonate buffer pH 9. 7 and\nextract with two quantities, each of40 ml, ofdichloromethane.\nReserve the aqueous layer for test B. Dry the extracts with\nanhydrous sodium sulphate, evaporate to dryness using a\nrotary evaporator, dry the residue at 60° at a pressure of2 kPa\nfor 15 minutes.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption\nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that\nobtained with timolol RS or with the reference spectrum of\ntimolol.\nB. Filter the aqueous layer reserved in test A and evaporate to\nabout I ml. Add I ml ofbromine water, heat on a water-bath\nTIMOLOL TABLETS\nfor 15 minutes, then heat to boiling and cool. Add 0.1 ml ofthis\nsolution to a solution of10 mg ofresorcinol in 3 ml ofsulphuric\nacid and heat on a water-bath for 15 minutes; a bluish black\ncolour is produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Detennine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets\ncontaining 0.1 g ofTimolol Maleate with 10 ml ofthe mobile\nphase for 10 minutes and filter.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1 volume ofthe test solution to\n250 volumes with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 volume ofthe test solution to\n500 volumes with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 20 cm x 4 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (l0 /lm),\nmobile phase: a mixture of 57.5 volumes of methanol\nand 42.5 volumes of0.02 M sodium octanesulphonate,\nadjusted to pH 3.0 with glacial acetic acid,\nflow rate. 2 ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 295 nm,\ninjection volume. 20 /ll.\nRun the chromatogram of the test solution for 4 times the\nretention time of the principal peak. In the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution the area of any secondary\npeak, other than the peak corresponding to maleic acid, is not\nmore than the area ofthe peak in the chromatogram obtained\nwith reference solution (a) and not more than two such peaks\nhave an area more than that ofthe peak obtained with reference\nsolution (b).\nUniformity of content. Comply with the test stated under\nTablets.\nTo one tablet, add 25.0 ml of0.05 M sulphuric acid, shake for\n20 minutes and complete the Assay beginning at the words\n\"and centrifuge....\".\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh accurately a\nquantity of the powder containing about 15 mg of Timolol\nMaleate, add 25.0 ml of 0.05 M sulphuric acid, shake for\n20 minutes and centrifilge until clear. Add 5.0 ml ofthe resulting\nsupernatant liquid to 15 ml of carbonate bujfer pH 9.7 and\nextract with three quantities, each of20 ml, and one quantity\nof10 ml of toluene. Wash each extract successively with.the\nsame 10-ml volume ofcarbonate bujferpH 9. 7, combiue the\ntoluene extracts and extract with four quantities, each of20 ml,\nof0.05 M sulphuric acid. Combine the acid extracts, dilute to\n100.0 ml, filter and measure the absorbance ofthe filtrate at the\nmaximum at about295 nm (2.4.7), using as the blank a solution\nprepared by treating a mixture of 5 ml ofwater and 15 ml of\ncarbonate bufferpH 9. 7 in the same manner beginning at the\nwords \"and extract with three quantities,...\"\nCalculate the content of CI3H24N403S,C4H404 taking 204 as\nthe specific absorbance at 295 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/timolol-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Timolol Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Timolol Maleate\nN N \nH3C , COOH\n\\\\ // \n>oh3 iT\n^o/^v/^n ch3 ’U\n. OH H. \nCOOH\nCi3H24N4O3S,C4H4O4 \nMol. Wt. 432.5\nTimolol Maleate is (5)-l-r^r/-butylaminö-3-(4-morpholino-\n1,2,5-thiadiazol-3-yloxy)prppan-2-ol hydrogen maleate. ,\nTimolol Maleate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C13H24N403S,C4H4O4j calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Beta-adrenoceptor antagonist (Antiglaucoma).\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or\ncolourless crystals: ■ \n-\nTIGECYCLINE INJECTION\n,p"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Timolol Maleate\nN N \nH3C , COOH\n\\\\ // \n>oh3 iT\n^o/^v/^n ch3 ’U\n. OH H. \nCOOH\nCi3H24N4O3S,C4H4O4 \nMol. Wt. 432.5\nTimolol Maleate is (5)-l-r^r/-butylaminö-3-(4-morpholino-\n1,2,5-thiadiazol-3-yloxy)prppan-2-ol hydrogen maleate. ,\nTimolol Maleate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C13H24N403S,C4H4O4j calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Beta-adrenoceptor antagonist (Antiglaucoma).\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or\ncolourless crystals: ■ \n-\nTIGECYCLINE INJECTION\n,p"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Tèsi A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried oif Tests jf \nand C may be omitted if test A is carried out.\"' r 1 A\n?\nA. Determineby infrared absorption spectrophotometry Ó 4 a \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with* timolol male^ \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of timolol maleate.\nB. In the test for Related substances, the principal peak in i \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution correspond I \nto that in the chromatogram obtained with reference I \nsolution(a). ’'.A: \n1\nC. Triturate 0.1g with a mixture of 1 ml of 2 Msodium hydroxide I\nand 3 ml ofwater and shake with three quantities, each of 5 \nI\nof ether. To 0.1 ml of the aqueouslayer add a solution of 10 mg | \nof resorcinol in 3 ml of sulphuric acid and heat on a water- I \nbath for 15 minutes; no violet-red colour is produced. Neutralise I \nthe remainder of the aqueous layer with 1 M sulphuric acid I \nadd 1 ml of bromine water, heat on a water-bath for 15 minutes, | \nthen heat to boiling and cool. To 0.2 ml of the solution add a | \nsolution of 10 mg of resorcinol in 3 ml of sulphuric acid and i \nheat on a water-bath for 15 minutes; a violet-red. .colour is | \nproduced. Add 0.2 ml of a 10 per cent w/v solution of potassium I \nbromide and heat on a water-bath for 5 minutes; the colour I \nchanges to violet-blue. \nI"},{"name":"Tests","text":"p -..\nAppearance of solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon \ndioxidefree water is clear (2.4.1), and not.more intensely \ncoloured than reference solution BS8 (2.4.1).\npH (2.4.24). 3.8 to 4.3, determined in a 2.0 per cent w/y solution\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). - 6.2° to - 5.7°, determined in \na 10.0 per cent w/v solution in 1 Mhydrochloric acid: '\nI\nEnantiomeric purity. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nNOTE—Protect the solutions from light. .\nSolvent mixture. 10 volumes of dichloromethane and \n30 volumes of 2-propanol.\nTest solution. Dissolve 30 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to lO.O ml with \nthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A 0.3 per cent w/v solution of timold\nmaleate IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A 0.003 per cent,w/v solution ot \ntimolol impurity A IPRS ((RftimololIPRS) in the solvent\nmixture.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 1 ml of reference solution (a)\n100 ml with the solvent mixture. Mix 1 ml of the solution with\nml ofreference solution (b). \nA\nTIMOLOL MALEATE\nIP 2022 \n.............. . ......................................................................\nce solution (d). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to \nf^mlwith the solvent mixture.\n(TttofflätögäpMc system\n^stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \nr silica gel OD for chiral separations (5 pm),\nmobile phase: a mixture of 2 volumes of diethylamine,\n2-pr()panol and 960 volumes of hexane, \n_ ¿ÖW rate: I ml per minute,\n^ Spectrophotometer set at 297 nm,\n_ injection volume: 5 pl.\nTjjjiololimpurityAJS fluted first., The retention times of the \n^¡ncinalpeaks due to (^-enantiomer in the chromatograms \nobtained, withthelestsolution and reference solution (a) are\nidentical.\n¡nject reference.solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \njg^lurion;between the peaks due to timolol impurity A and\nthe \nafttiomier is not less than 4.0.\nInject reference solution (d) and the test solution. The area of \nany peak due to timolol impurity Ais not more than the area of\nihe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution(d) (1.0 per cent). .\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14)./ . bo:.:.-\nTest solution. ‘Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination inmobile phase A and dilute to 20 ml with mobile \nphase A. w'\nReference solution (a). A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of timolol \nmaleate IPRSin the mobile phase A.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with mobile phase A.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 12.5 cm x 4 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of equal volumes of\nmethanol and a 0.43 per cent w/v solution of sodium \noctanesulphonate previously adjusted to pH 3.0 with \nglacial acetic acid, \n.\nB. methanol,\n~ a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\nflow rate: 2 ml per minute,\n\"■ spectrophotometer set at 295 nm,\n~ injection volume: 20 u.).\nTime \nin min)\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n97.5\n. 2.5 \n•\n10 .\n, . 97.5\n2.5\nävto..r \n70\n30 -\n14.5 ,\n70\n. , .30\n17 ■\n97.5\n-2.5'\nCorrection\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/timolol-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Timolol Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Timolol Tablets\nTimolol Maleate Tablets\nTimolol Tablets contain not less5than-90:0 per cent ahd not \nmore than 1 i 0.0 per cent of the stated amount Of timolol maleate, \nCi3H24N4O3S,C4H4O4.\nUsual strength.10 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Timolol Tablets\nTimolol Maleate Tablets\nTimolol Tablets contain not less5than-90:0 per cent ahd not \nmore than 1 i 0.0 per cent of the stated amount Of timolol maleate, \nCi3H24N4O3S,C4H4O4.\nUsual strength.10 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A.To a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 70 mgèf \nTimolol Maleate add 20 ml of carbonate buffer pH 9.7 and \nextract with two quantities, each of 40 ml, of dichlordmetha^ \nReserve the aqueous layer for test B. Dry the extracts with\nTIMOLOL TABLETS\n? £2022\n>rOlts sodium sulphate, evaporate to dryness using a\nevaporator, dry the residue at 60° at a pressure of 2 kPa\ntor 15 minutes. -\n' the r^sidne, determine by infrared absorption \n$fl troDhotomctry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that\nbtained with timolol maleate IPRS, treated in the same \nmanner or with the reference spectrum of timolol.\ng Filter the aqueous layer reserved in test A and evaporate to \nabout 1 mt Add T mlof bromine water, heat on a water-bath \nfor 15 minutes, then heat to boiling and cool. Add 0.1 ml of the \nsolution to asolution of 10 mg of resorcinol in 3 ml of sulphuric \nuc/dand heat on a water-bath for 15 minutes; a bluish black \ncolour is.produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 500 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid,\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 20 minutes.\nWithdraw asui tab le volume of the medium and filter.\nDdermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. JJse the filtrate, dilute if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium.\nReference solution. Dissolve a suitable quantity of timolol \nmaleate IPRS in the dissolution medium to obtain a solution \nhaving the same concentration as that to the test solution.\nUsé chromatographic system as described under Assay.\nInject the reference solution and thé test solution.\nCalculate the content of CuHmN^SjC^CX, in the. medium.\nQ. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of \nCuH^ChS&lW\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4:14)/ \n• \n- \"\nTèstsq/unqw. .Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 0.1 g of Timolol Maleate with 10 ml of the mobile \nphase for 10 minutes and filter.\nReference solution (a). A 0.004 per cent w/v of timolol maleate \n^S with tHe mobile phase.\nsolution (b). Dilute 5.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nwith the mobile phase.\nChromatographic : system\n~ a stainless steel column 20 cm x 4 mm, packed with\n.-yoctadeeylsilane bonded to porous silica (10 pm),\n■' -tonobile phase: a mixtureiof 57.5 volumes of methanol\n- . and 42.5 volumes of 0.02 M Sodium octanesulphonate,\n■ Adjusted to pH 3.0 with glacial acetic acid,\n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 295 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram of the test solution for 4 times the retention \ntime of the principal peak. In the chromatogram obtained with \nthe test solution the area of any secondary peak, other than \nthe peak corresponding to maleic acid, is not more than the \narea of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.4 per cent) and not more than two such peaks \nhave an area more than that of the peak obtained with reference \nsolution (b) (0.2 per cent).\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test, stated under \nTablets.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as described\nunder Assay with the following modification. \n’ : ■\nTest solution. Disperse one tablet in 60 ml of the solvent \nmixture, with the aid of ultrasound and dilute to obtain a\nsolution having the similar concentration of reference solution \n(a) with the solvent mixture.\nCalculate the content of Cn^l^ChS^^Ch in the tablet;\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 80 volumes of a buffer solution \nprepared by dissolving 11.04 g of monobasic sodium \nphosphate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 2.8 with\northophosphoric acid and 20 volumes of methanol.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof the powder containing 10 mg of Timolol Maleate in the \nsolvent mixture, with the aid of ultrasound and dilute to 100.0 \nml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of timolol \nmaleate IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 percent \nw/v of timolol maleate IPRS and 0.001 per cent. w/v. timolol \nrelated compound D IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 2.1 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (2.6 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. a 0.05 per cent v/v solution of\ntrifluoroacetic acid in water,\nB. a 0.05 per cent V/v solution of \ntrifluoroacetic acid in acetonitrile,\"\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 0.4 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 295 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/timolol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Timolol"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Timolol\nTest solution. Dilute the eye drops to obtain a solution \ncontaining 0.025 per cent w/v of timolol in the mobile phase. \nReference solution (a). A 0.0342 per cent w/v solution of \ntimolol maleate RS in the mobile phase. \nReference solution (b). Add 8 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide \nto 90 mg of timolol maleate RS, heat at 70° for 15 hours, cool \nand dilute to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase. Mix 1 volume of \nthis solution with 4 volumes of a 0.14 per cent w/v solution of \ndorzolamide hydrochloride RS.\n1(4S,6S)-4-amino-6-methy1-5,6-dihydro-4H-thieno[2,3-b]thi opyran.\nTimolol impurity B2\n0.7\n 2- sulfonamide 7,7-dioxide, \n=(4RS,6SR)-4-(ethylamino)-6-methyl-5,6-dihydro-4H-thi eno [23.\n1.0\nTiiimill°o1o1 impurity D3\n b]thiopyran-2- sulfonamide 7,7-dioxide.\n1.5\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not v alid unless \n \nresolution between the peaks due to dorzolamide and \ndorzolamide impurity D is not less than 3.0.\n!\ns the\n:4.(morpholin-4-y1)-1,2,5-thiadiazol-3(2H)-one 1-oxide, \n2(20-3-[(191-dimethylethyl)amino]-24[4-(morpholin-4-y1)- 1 ,2,5-\n3t4hiaond rzpohl-031-i ny_41 Joyxoy_] ip,r2o,p5 a_ tnh-i la-doi\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution. l\na\nthe\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to tinaolol impurity G and \ntimolol impurity B is not less than 1.5.\n of \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area ( \npeak due to dorzolamide impurity B is not more than 1.1\ntimes\nUse chromatographic system as described under Related \nsubstances for timolol.\nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtai ned\nrence\n with reference solution (a) (1.1 per cent). The area of any peak \ndue to dorzolamide impurity D is not more than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with refe\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram twice the retention time of the timolol peak. In\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to timolol impurity G and \ntimolol impurity B is not less than 1.5.\nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of \nany peak corresponding to timolol impurities B, D or G is not \nmore than twice the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.4 per \ncent). The area of any other secondary peak is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent). The sum of areas of \nall the secondary peaks is not more than 2.5 times the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less \nthan 0.5 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per cent) and peaks \ndue to maleic acid.\n solution (b) (0.2 per cent). The area of any other \nsecondan \npeak is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 per \ncent). The sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks is not \nmore than 1.3 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (1.3 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 times th e area \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.1 per cent) and peaks due to timolol \nand maleic acid.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CI3H24N403S3, in the eye drops.\n1 mg of C 13H24N403S3,C4H404  is equivalent to 731.6 pg of \nC 3 H24N403 S3.\nLabelling. The label states the quantity of Dorzolamide \nHydrochloride in terms of the equivalent amount of \ndorzolamide and the quantity of Timolol Maleate in terms of \nthe equivalent amount of timolol.\nFor timolol - Determine by liquid chromatography (2. 4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of eye drops to obtain a solution \ncontaining 0.025 per cent w/v of timolo"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Timolol\nTest solution. Dilute the eye drops to obtain a solution \ncontaining 0.025 per cent w/v of timolol in the mobile phase. \nReference solution (a). A 0.0342 per cent w/v solution of \ntimolol maleate RS in the mobile phase. \nReference solution (b). Add 8 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide \nto 90 mg of timolol maleate RS, heat at 70° for 15 hours, cool \nand dilute to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase. Mix 1 volume of \nthis solution with 4 volumes of a 0.14 per cent w/v solution of \ndorzolamide hydrochloride RS.\n1(4S,6S)-4-amino-6-methy1-5,6-dihydro-4H-thieno[2,3-b]thi opyran.\nTimolol impurity B2\n0.7\n 2- sulfonamide 7,7-dioxide, \n=(4RS,6SR)-4-(ethylamino)-6-methyl-5,6-dihydro-4H-thi eno [23.\n1.0\nTiiimill°o1o1 impurity D3\n b]thiopyran-2- sulfonamide 7,7-dioxide.\n1.5\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not v alid unless \n \nresolution between the peaks due to dorzolamide and \ndorzolamide impurity D is not less than 3.0.\n!\ns the\n:4.(morpholin-4-y1)-1,2,5-thiadiazol-3(2H)-one 1-oxide, \n2(20-3-[(191-dimethylethyl)amino]-24[4-(morpholin-4-y1)- 1 ,2,5-\n3t4hiaond rzpohl-031-i ny_41 Joyxoy_] ip,r2o,p5 a_ tnh-i la-doi\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution. l\na\nthe\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to tinaolol impurity G and \ntimolol impurity B is not less than 1.5.\n of \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area ( \npeak due to dorzolamide impurity B is not more than 1.1\ntimes\nUse chromatographic system as described under Related \nsubstances for timolol.\nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtai ned\nrence\n with reference solution (a) (1.1 per cent). The area of any peak \ndue to dorzolamide impurity D is not more than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with refe\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram twice the retention time of the timolol peak. In\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to timolol impurity G and \ntimolol impurity B is not less than 1.5.\nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of \nany peak corresponding to timolol impurities B, D or G is not \nmore than twice the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.4 per \ncent). The area of any other secondary peak is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent). The sum of areas of \nall the secondary peaks is not more than 2.5 times the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less \nthan 0.5 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per cent) and peaks \ndue to maleic acid.\n solution (b) (0.2 per cent). The area of any other \nsecondan \npeak is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 per \ncent). The sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks is not \nmore than 1.3 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (1.3 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 times th e area \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.1 per cent) and peaks due to timolol \nand maleic acid.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CI3H24N403S3, in the eye drops.\n1 mg of C 13H24N403S3,C4H404  is equivalent to 731.6 pg of \nC 3 H24N403 S3.\nLabelling. The label states the quantity of Dorzolamide \nHydrochloride in terms of the equivalent amount of \ndorzolamide and the quantity of Timolol Maleate in terms of \nthe equivalent amount of timolol.\nFor timolol - Determine by liquid chromatography (2. 4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of eye drops to obtain a solution \ncontaining 0.025 per cent w/v of timolol in the mobile phase.\nDothiepin Hydrochloride\ntests. Comply with the tests stated under Eye Drops. \nAssay. For Dorzolamide-Determine by liquid"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tinidazole-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tinidazole Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tinidazole Tablets\nC19H22BrN04S2, H2O \t\nMol. Wt. 490.2\ntinidazole impurity B RS (142-(ethylsulphonyl)eth011 -2-\nTinidazole Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of tinidazole, \nCs1-113N304S.\n methyl-4-nitro-1H-imidazoleRS) \nin 10 ml of methanol and\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate is 6/3,7/3-epoxy-30-hydroxy-\n8-methyl-lall-5a11-tropanium bromide monohydrate.\ndilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 2.0 ml of this\nsolution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nUsual strengths. 150 mg; 300 mg; 500 mg.\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate contains not less than \n98.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per cent of tiotropium \nbromide, C I9H22NO4S2Br, calculated on the anhydrous basis. \n:\" \t\n- \t\n• = \n„ Category. &onchodilator.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution \n(b) to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tinidazole Tablets\nC19H22BrN04S2, H2O \t\nMol. Wt. 490.2\ntinidazole impurity B RS (142-(ethylsulphonyl)eth011 -2-\nTinidazole Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of tinidazole, \nCs1-113N304S.\n methyl-4-nitro-1H-imidazoleRS) \nin 10 ml of methanol and\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate is 6/3,7/3-epoxy-30-hydroxy-\n8-methyl-lall-5a11-tropanium bromide monohydrate.\ndilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 2.0 ml of this\nsolution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nUsual strengths. 150 mg; 300 mg; 500 mg.\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate contains not less than \n98.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per cent of tiotropium \nbromide, C I9H22NO4S2Br, calculated on the anhydrous basis. \n:\" \t\n- \t\n• = \n„ Category. &onchodilator.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution \n(b) to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. When examined in the range 230 nm to 369nni\nfinal solution obtained in the Assay shows 4-absotption_ \n--- \nmaximtun at about 310 nm. \t\nDose. 18 pg once daily.\n338O \n33-81\nA\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry \n(2.4:6). Compare the spectrum with that obtained with \ntiotropium bromide monohydrate RS or with the reference \nspectrum of tiotropium bromide monohydrate.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with test solution corresponds to the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances.  Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 100 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.002 per cent w/v solution of tiotropium \nbromide monohydrate RS  in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 Ilm), \n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 45 volumes of methanol and \n55 volumes ofa buffer solution prepared by dissolving \n3.85 g of ammonium acetate in 1000 ml of water and \nadjusting the pH to 5.5 with dilute acetic acid\n(10 per cent v/v), \n-\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 235 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 gal.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the column efficiency is \nnot less than 3000 theoretical plates.\nInject the test solution. Any individual impurity is not more \nthan 0.5 per cent and the sum of all the impurities is not more \nthan 1.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.3 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 4.5 per cent, determined on \n0.5 g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 20 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 100.0 ml of the mobile phase. Dilute 5.0 ml of \nthe resulting solution to 50.0 ml with the mobilephase. \t\n- \n-• \nReference solution. A 0.002 per cent w/v solution'atiotrdpit*. \nbromide monohydrate RS in the mobile phase."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tinidazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tinidazole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TINID AZOLE\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)-\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n16\n■ 2.4 --\n’ \n84\n■■ 16 ••• ■\nr 8.0 ■■■■\n2 1\n20\n: \n80\n; 0'1\n......\n- \n84 . ■\n. 16\n11' .\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to timolol maleate and \ntimolol related compound D is not less than 2.5 in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b), the tailing \nfactor is not more than 2.5 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CJ3H24N4O3S,C4H4O4 in the tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTinidazole\nNO2 o O\nJ \n'1’o/ r-u\nCH3\nC8H13N3O4S .. \nMol. Wt. 247.3\nTinidazole is T-[2-(ethylsulphonyl)ethyl]-2-methyl-5- \nnitroimidazole,\nTinidazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of QH| iN;O;S, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiprotozoal.\nDescription. Pale yellow crystals or a crystalline powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TINID AZOLE\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)-\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n16\n■ 2.4 --\n’ \n84\n■■ 16 ••• ■\nr 8.0 ■■■■\n2 1\n20\n: \n80\n; 0'1\n......\n- \n84 . ■\n. 16\n11' .\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to timolol maleate and \ntimolol related compound D is not less than 2.5 in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b), the tailing \nfactor is not more than 2.5 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CJ3H24N4O3S,C4H4O4 in the tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTinidazole\nNO2 o O\nJ \n'1’o/ r-u\nCH3\nC8H13N3O4S .. \nMol. Wt. 247.3\nTinidazole is T-[2-(ethylsulphonyl)ethyl]-2-methyl-5- \nnitroimidazole,\nTinidazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of QH| iN;O;S, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiprotozoal.\nDescription. Pale yellow crystals or a crystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Tests B \nand C may be omitted if test'A is carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tinidazole IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of tinidazole.\nB. When examined in the range 230 nmto 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.001 per cent w/v solution \\n methanol shows an absorption \nmaximum at about 310 nm; absorbance at about 310 nm, about \n0.35. \n' '•\nC. To about 5 mg, add 5 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid 5q \nof zinc powder, 4 ml of hydrochloric acid and allow to \nfor 30 minutes. Add 4 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution \nvanillin, heat on a boiling water-bath for 20 minutes, alfo«. \ncool to room temperature and dilute to 20 ml with water \ngreenish yellow colour is produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Determine by liquid chromatogranh ■ \n(2.4.14). \n. ' \n' ' ' ' \nI\nNOTE—Protect the solutions from light. \nI\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under I \nexamination in 10.0 ml of methanol and d i lute to 10 0.0 ml with I \nthe mobile phase. \n, I\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to 1\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Further dilute ì .0 ini òfthè I \nsolution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase. - \ng\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 5 mg each òf tinidazole I\nimpurity AIPRS (2-methyl-5-nitro~lH-imidazblé ÌPRS) ^ | \ntinidazole impurity B IPRS (lr[2-(ethylsulphonyl)ethyl]d* | \nmethyl-4-nitro-lH-imidazoleRS) in 10. ml of methanol and I \ndilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 2:0 ml of the I \nsolution to l 0.0 ml with the mobile phase. r ' ; \n|\nReference solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference'solution I\n(b) to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase. \nI\nChromatographic system \ng\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 3 mm, packed with |\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), ’ f |\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 10 volumes Qi.acetomtnh, I\n20 volumes of methanol and 70 volumes of water, |\n- flowrate: 0.5 ml per minute, ; \nI\n“ spectrophotometer set at 320 nm, \n. \n|\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n;; \n|\nThe relative retention time .with reference , to: tinidazole for |\ntinidazole impurity A is about 0.6 and for tinidazole impurity B |\nis about 0.7. \n. \n|\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the I\nresolution between the peaks due to tinidazole; impurityAand |\nB is not less than 2.0. \n|\nInj ect reference so lution (a), (c) and the test solution; \nI\nchromatogram 1.5 times the retention time of the principal |\npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, |\nthe area of any peak due to tinidazole impurity A arid B is not |\nmore than the area of the correspondingjpeak in the |\nchromatogram obtained with reference ¿solution («) |\n(0.2 per cent), the area of any other secondary peak is not |\nmore than the area of the principal peak in the’ chromatogram |\nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per cent), and the su® \nof areas of all the secondary peaks is not more than 4 time5 the\nIP2022------------------------- ---------------------------------------- ---\nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \n^^ce-solution (a) (0.4 per cent). Ignore any peak with an\nC less than 0.5 times the area of the principal peak in the \nX-oiriatogram obtained with reference solution (a)\n(0.05 per cent).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent.\non drying (2.4.19). Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined\non I Ogby drying in an oven at 105° for 4 hours.\n_\\ssay Dissolve 0.5 g in 30 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid Titrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid, using 0.15 ml of nile\nVindicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n. ml of 61 / \nac^ *s ecluivalent to 0.02473 g of\nUnas. ;; ■ .\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tiotropium-bromide-monohydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tiotropium Bromide Monohydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calculate the content of CsHi3N3O4S taking 356 as the specific \nabsorbance at 310 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate\nC19H22BrN04S2>H,O \nMol. Wt 490.4\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate is (lR,2R,4S,5S,ls)-1- \n[[Hydroxy[di(thiophen-2-yl)]acetyl]oxy]-9,9-dimethyl-3-oxa-9- \nazatricyclo[3.3.1,02’4]nonan-9-ium bromide monohydrate.\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate contains not less than 98.5 \nper cent and not more than 101.5 per cent of tiotropium \nbromide, C19H22NO4S2Br, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Bronchodilator.\nDescription. A white to off-whitepowder or crystals.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tiotropium \nbromide monohydrate IP RS or with the reference spectrum of \ntiotropium bromide monohydrate. \n■ -\nB.It gives reaction (a) of bromide (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. A1.0 per cent w/v solution is clear \n(2.4.1) and not more intensely coloured than reference solution \nYS6 (2.4.1).\nImpurities G and H. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with s ilica gel GF254.\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. Dilute 1 ml of 3Mhydrochloric acid to 100 \nml with methanol. \ni .... ,,\nMobile phase. A mixture of 10 volumes of water; 15 volumes of \nanhydrous formic acid, 35 volumes of acetonitrile and 50 \nvolumes of methylene chloride."},"sections":[{"name":"TIOTROPIUM BROMIDE MONOHYDRATE","text":"Calculate the content of CsHi3N3O4S taking 356 as the specific \nabsorbance at 310 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate\nC19H22BrN04S2>H,O \nMol. Wt 490.4\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate is (lR,2R,4S,5S,ls)-1- \n[[Hydroxy[di(thiophen-2-yl)]acetyl]oxy]-9,9-dimethyl-3-oxa-9- \nazatricyclo[3.3.1,02’4]nonan-9-ium bromide monohydrate.\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate contains not less than 98.5 \nper cent and not more than 101.5 per cent of tiotropium \nbromide, C19H22NO4S2Br, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Bronchodilator.\nDescription. A white to off-whitepowder or crystals.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tiotropium \nbromide monohydrate IP RS or with the reference spectrum of \ntiotropium bromide monohydrate. \n■ -\nB.It gives reaction (a) of bromide (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. A1.0 per cent w/v solution is clear \n(2.4.1) and not more intensely coloured than reference solution \nYS6 (2.4.1).\nImpurities G and H. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with s ilica gel GF254.\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. Dilute 1 ml of 3Mhydrochloric acid to 100 \nml with methanol. \ni .... ,,\nMobile phase. A mixture of 10 volumes of water; 15 volumes of \nanhydrous formic acid, 35 volumes of acetonitrile and 50 \nvolumes of methylene chloride."},{"name":"TIOTROPIUM BROMIDE MONOHYDRATE","text":"Test solution. Dissolve 0.4 g of the substances under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 10.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture. ,\nReference solution (a). Dissolve the contents of a vial of\ntiotropium impurity mixture IPRS (containing impurities G \nand H) in 1.0 ml of the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Mix 0.1 ml of the test solution with 0.1 \nml of reference solution (a).\nApply to the plate 10 pl of the test solution and reference \nsolution (a), and 20 pl of reference solution (b). After \ndevelopment, dry the plate in air and expose to iodine vapors \ntill spots appear (about 15 minutes), remove the plate and \nexamine immediately. In the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution, any secondary spot corresponding to, each of, \ntiotropium impurity G and H is not more intense than the \ncorresponding spot of tiotropium impurity G and H in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per \ncent). The test is not valid unless the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (b) shows three clearly separated spots, \nof each, tiotropium impurity G and H and tiotropium with \nRfvalues of about 0.33,0.38 and 0.64, respectively.\nRelated substances.Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2:4.14):\nNOTE — Carry out the test protected from light.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in mobile phase B and dilute to 25.0 ml with mobile \nphase B.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with mobile phase B. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to \n10.0 ml with mobile phase B.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 4 mg of tiotropium for system \nsuitability IPRS (containing impurity C) in 2.0 ml of mobile \nphase B.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 3.0 mm packed with\npropylsilane bonded to porous .silica (3.5 pm),\n- column temperature: 50°,\n- mobile phase A: dissolve 1.0 g of sodium methane­\nsulphonate and 5.0 g of potassium dihydrogen \nphosphate in 980 ml of water adjusted to pH 3.0 with\ndilute phosphoric acid and dilute to 1000 ml with water,\n. B: a mixture of 10 volumes of methanol, \n40 volumes of acetonitrile and 50 volumes of mobile\n' phase A, \n:\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\n— flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute,\n= 4 ; spectrophotometer set at 240 nm,.........................\n- injection volume: 5 pl. \n'\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobiie phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v) ■\n■ (per cent v/v)4 J\n0\n90\n777°' '10'\n3\n90\n.\"\"7 7'777^i\n17\n80\n. f. 20\n28\n25\n30\n7 .25 '\n■ ;;77iS7\"w\n35\n90\nThe relative retention time with reference, to tiotropi^ \n(retention time: about 15 minutes) for tiotropium impurityCi \nabout 1.2. ' \n.' \n7.’ \n' \n5\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless tfe \nresolution between the peak due to tiotropium and tiotropium \nimpurity C is not less than 2.4. \n■\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. Int^ \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of \nanysecondary peak is not more than the area-pf;the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solutioa \n(a) (0.1 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is hot more than twice the area of the principaipeak a \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (6.2 \nper cent). Ignore any peak with an area -less than Q,5 times\narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained wife \nreference solution (a) (0.05 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit testier \nheavy metals; Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent\nWater (2.3.43). 2.5 per cent to 4.0 per cent,: determined on \n0.3 g. f '.7....77.7,-<:w;l'77777r\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution XftssoVve 20 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 1 OOiO ml ’-with the \nmobile phase. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solutionto 50.0 ml with the\nmobile phase.\nReference solution. A0.002 per cent w/v solution ^tiotropium \nbromide monohydrate IPRS equivalent to tiotropium bromide\nin the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system \n'\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4,6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 55 volumes of a bufe\nsolution prepared:by dissolving 3.85 g of amtnoni^ \nacetate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 5.5 * \ndilute acetic acid and 45 volumes df methanol,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 235 nm,\n- - injection volume: 20 pl. \n■■■■ ;\n3808\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tiotropium-bromide-nlonohydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tiotropium Bromide Nlonohydrate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tiotropium Bromide Nlonohydrate\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in 30 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid. Titrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid, using 0.15 ml of nile \nblue A solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\nNOTE-Protect the solutions from light.\nH3C, -CH3\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of methanol and dilute to 100.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02473 g of \nC41-113N ;04S.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nBr , H2O \nSN \n0 OH\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Further dilute 1.0 ml of th0 \nsolution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 5 mg each of tinidazole \nimpurity A RS (2-methyl-5-nitro-1H-imidazole RS) \nand"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Tiotropium Bromide Nlonohydrate\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in 30 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid. Titrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid, using 0.15 ml of nile \nblue A solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\nNOTE-Protect the solutions from light.\nH3C, -CH3\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of methanol and dilute to 100.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02473 g of \nC41-113N ;04S.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nBr , H2O \nSN \n0 OH\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Further dilute 1.0 ml of th0 \nsolution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 5 mg each of tinidazole \nimpurity A RS (2-methyl-5-nitro-1H-imidazole RS) \nand"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tiotropium-bromide-powder-for","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tiotropium Bromide Powder for"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tiotropium Bromide Powder for\nInhalation\nTiotropium Powder for Inhalation consists of Tiotropium\nBromide in microfine powder either alone or admixed with\nLactose in a pre-metered unit for use in a suitable powder\ninhaler.\nTiotropium Powder for Inhalation contains not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 125.0 per cent of the stated\namount oftiotropium, CI9H22N04Sz per unit dose."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tiotropium Bromide Powder for\nInhalation\nTiotropium Powder for Inhalation consists of Tiotropium\nBromide in microfine powder either alone or admixed with\nLactose in a pre-metered unit for use in a suitable powder\ninhaler.\nTiotropium Powder for Inhalation contains not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 125.0 per cent of the stated\namount oftiotropium, CI9H22N04Sz per unit dose."},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other tests. Complies with the tests stated under Inhalation\nPreparations (Powders for Inhalation).\nFollow the procedure described under Assay with suitable\ndilution of the reference solution wherever the amount of\nactive substance is to be determined in any test.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 90 volumes of 0.05 per cent v/v ortho-\nphosphoric acid and 10 volumes of acetonitrile.\nTest solution. Dissolve a quantity of the mixed contents of\n20 capsules in sufficient ofthe solvent mixture to get a solution\ncontaining 1.8 J..Lg per ml ofTiotropium.\nReference solution. A solution of tiotropium· bromide RS\nequivalent to tiotropium 1.8 J..Lg per ml in the solvent mixture.\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 lnm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 J..Lm),\nmobilephase: a mixture of80 volumes ofabuffersolution\nprepared by dissolving 2 ml triethylamine in 1000 ml of\nwater and adjusting the pH to 2.5 with orthophosphoric\nacid, and 20 volumes of acetonitrile,\n-\nflow rate. 2 ml per minute,\n-\nspectrophotometer set at 237 nm,\n-\ninject200 fll.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\ncolumn efficiency is not less than 4500 theoretical plates, the\ntailing factor is not more than 1.7 and the relative standard\ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content ofC19H22N04Sz per unit.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at temperature not\nexceeding 30°.\nLabelling. The label states the quantity of active ingredient\nper pre-metered.unit."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/titan-yellow-paper-impregnate-ftlter-paper-with-titan-yellow","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Titan Yellow Paper: Impregnate ftlter paper with titan yellow"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Titan Yellow Paper: Impregnate ftlter paper with titan yellow\nsolution and dry at room temperature.\n4.4. Standard Solutions\nAcetaldehydeStandard Solution (100 ppm C2IltO): Dissolve\n1.0 g of acetaldehyde in sufficient 2-propanol to produce\n100mIanddilute5.0rnlofthe solutionto500.0rnl with2-propanol.\nAluminiumStandardSolution(2ppmAl): Dilute 2 volumes of\na 0.176 per cent w/v solution ofaluminiumpotassium sulphate\nin 0.1 M sulphuric acid to 100 volumes with water.\nAluminium Standard Solution (10 ppmAI): Dilute 1volume\nof a 1.39 per cent w/v solution of aluminium nitrate to\n100 volumes with water.\nAmmonium StandardSolution (1 ppmNH4): Dilute 10.0rnl of\na 0.0741%w/v solution ofammonium chloride to 25.0mI with\nammonia-free water. Dilute 1volume ofthe resulting solution\nto 100 volumes with ammonia-free water immediately before\nArsenic Standard Solution (1 ppm As): Dilute 1 volume of\narsenic standard solution (10 ppm As) to 10 volumes with\nwater.\nArsenic Standard Solution (10 ppm As): Dissolve 0.33 g of\narsenic trioxide in 5 mI of2M sodium hydroxide and dilute to\n250.0 mI with water. Dilute 1 volume of this solution to\n100volumes with water.\nBarium Standard Solution (10 ppm Ba): Dilute 1.0 mI of a\n0.178 per cent w/v solution of barium chloride to 100.0 mI\nwith water.\nCadmiumStandardSolution (10 ppm Cd): Dilute 2.0 mI ofa\n0.228 per cent w/v solution of cadmium sulphate to 200.0 mI\nwith water.\nCalciumStandard Solution (10ppm Ca): Dissolve 0.624 g of\ndried calcium carbonate in distilled water containing 3 mI of\n5 M acetic acid and dilute to 250.0 mI with distilled water.\nDilute I volume of this solution to 100 volumes with distilled\nwater."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Titan Yellow Paper: Impregnate ftlter paper with titan yellow\nsolution and dry at room temperature.\n4.4. Standard Solutions\nAcetaldehydeStandard Solution (100 ppm C2IltO): Dissolve\n1.0 g of acetaldehyde in sufficient 2-propanol to produce\n100mIanddilute5.0rnlofthe solutionto500.0rnl with2-propanol.\nAluminiumStandardSolution(2ppmAl): Dilute 2 volumes of\na 0.176 per cent w/v solution ofaluminiumpotassium sulphate\nin 0.1 M sulphuric acid to 100 volumes with water.\nAluminium Standard Solution (10 ppmAI): Dilute 1volume\nof a 1.39 per cent w/v solution of aluminium nitrate to\n100 volumes with water.\nAmmonium StandardSolution (1 ppmNH4): Dilute 10.0rnl of\na 0.0741%w/v solution ofammonium chloride to 25.0mI with\nammonia-free water. Dilute 1volume ofthe resulting solution\nto 100 volumes with ammonia-free water immediately before\nArsenic Standard Solution (1 ppm As): Dilute 1 volume of\narsenic standard solution (10 ppm As) to 10 volumes with\nwater.\nArsenic Standard Solution (10 ppm As): Dissolve 0.33 g of\narsenic trioxide in 5 mI of2M sodium hydroxide and dilute to\n250.0 mI with water. Dilute 1 volume of this solution to\n100volumes with water.\nBarium Standard Solution (10 ppm Ba): Dilute 1.0 mI of a\n0.178 per cent w/v solution of barium chloride to 100.0 mI\nwith water.\nCadmiumStandardSolution (10 ppm Cd): Dilute 2.0 mI ofa\n0.228 per cent w/v solution of cadmium sulphate to 200.0 mI\nwith water.\nCalciumStandard Solution (10ppm Ca): Dissolve 0.624 g of\ndried calcium carbonate in distilled water containing 3 mI of\n5 M acetic acid and dilute to 250.0 mI with distilled water.\nDilute I volume of this solution to 100 volumes with distilled\nwater."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/titanium-dioxide-practically-insoluble-in-water-and-in-dilute","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Titanium Dioxide, Practically insoluble in water and in dilute"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Titanium Dioxide, Practically insoluble in water and in dilute \nmineral acids, slowly soluble in hot sulphuric acid.\nTizanidine Hydrochloride.. Slightly soluble in wafer and \nmelhanol. \n.. \n. ' \n. ......\nTobramycin. Freely soluble in water; very slightly soluble in \nethanol (95 per. cent); practically; insoluble in chloroform\n■ and in ether.\nTocopheryl Acetate. Freely soluble in acetone, in chloroform \nin ethanol (95 per cent), in ether and in fixed oils; soluble in \netAario/;-practically insoluble in water.\nI I\nlofacitinib Citrate, Slightly soluble to practically insoluble in\n: methanol and waterj. practically insoluble in ethanol.\nTolazamide. Freely soluble in chloroform\", soluble inarcfewi; \nslightly soluble in ethanol (95per cent); very slightly soluble \nin wafer.\nTolbutamide. Soluble in. ethanol (95 percent), in acetone and \nin chloroform\", slightly soluble in ether, practically insoluble \nin wafer It dissolves in dilute solutions of alkali hydroxides.\nTolnaftate. Freely soluble in acetone and in dichloromethane\", \nvery slightly soluble in ethanol (95 per cent); practically\ninsoluble in water.\nTolterodine Tartrate. Sparingly soluble in methanol; slightly \nsoluble in ethanol (95 per cent).\nTolvaptan. Freely Soluble in benzyl alcohol; soluble in \nmethanol; insoluble in water.\nTolu Balsam. Freely soluble in ether, in ethanol (95per cent), \nin chloroform and in solutions of fixed alkalies usually leaving \nsome insoluble residue; insoluble in water and in hexane.\nTopiramate. Freely soluble in ethyl acetate and in ethanol; \nslightly soluble in water.\nTopotecan Hydrochloride. Soluble in water.\nTorsemide. Slightly soluble in 0.1 Msodium hydroxide, in 0.1 \nM hydrochloric acid, in ethanol (95 per cent), and in \nmethanol; very slightly soluble in acetone and in chloroform; \npractically insoluble in water and in ether.\nTramadol Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in water and in \nmethanol; very slightly soluble in acetone.\nTrandolapril. Soluble in methanol, in chloroform and in \ndichloromethane.\"\nTranexamic Acid. Freely soluble in water and in glacial acetic \nacid, practically insoluble in acetone and in ethanol (95per \ncent). \n. ' \n■ ■ . \n\"\nTranilast Soluble in ethanol, in dimethylformamide; insoluble\nin Maier.\nTravoprost, Vety soluble in methanol, in ethanol (95per cent), \nin acetonitrile and in chloroform; insoluble in water.\n' Trazodone Hydrochloride. Sparingly soluble in chloroform\nand in water.\nTriamcinolone. Sparingly soluble in ethanol (95per cent); \nslightly soluble in water; very slightly soluble in chloroform \nand in ether.\nTriamcinolone Acetonide. Sparingly soluble in chloroform \nand in ethanol (95 per cent); very slightly soluble in ether; \npractically insoluble in water.\nTriamterene. Very slightly soluble in wafer, in chloroform \nand in ethanol (95per cent); practically insoluble in water.\n2.4.26. SOLUBILITY\nIP 20??.\nTrichloromonofluoromethane. Soluble in ethanol-( 95 p,->f 1 \ncent), in ether and in other organic.solvents; practical^ ? \ninsoluble in wafer, \n?\nTriclofos Sodium. Freely soluble in wafer; slightly soluble m J \nethanol (95 per cent), practically insoluble in ether.\nTriethyl Citrate. Miscible with ethanol;(95per cent). Soluble \nin water; slightly soluble in fatty oils..\nTributyl Citrate. Freely soluble in alcohol, in isopropyl \nalcohol, in acetone, and in toluene, insoluble in water.\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in watei, \nsoluble in ethanol (95per cent); slightly soluble in chloroform, \ninsoluble in ether and in benzene.\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride. Soluble in water) in ethanol \n(95per cent) and in acetone; insoluble in ether.\nTrifluridine. Soluble in water.\nTrimebntine Maleate. Soluble in acetonitrile, sparingly \nsoluble in acetone; slightly soluble in wafer and in ethanol \n(95 per cent).\nTrimet azi di ne Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in water; \nsparingly soluble in ethanol (95per cent).\nTrimethobenzamide Hydrochloride. Soluble in water and in \nwarm alcohol, insoluble in etAer.and in benzene. ■"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Titanium Dioxide, Practically insoluble in water and in dilute \nmineral acids, slowly soluble in hot sulphuric acid.\nTizanidine Hydrochloride.. Slightly soluble in wafer and \nmelhanol. \n.. \n. ' \n. ......\nTobramycin. Freely soluble in water; very slightly soluble in \nethanol (95 per. cent); practically; insoluble in chloroform\n■ and in ether.\nTocopheryl Acetate. Freely soluble in acetone, in chloroform \nin ethanol (95 per cent), in ether and in fixed oils; soluble in \netAario/;-practically insoluble in water.\nI I\nlofacitinib Citrate, Slightly soluble to practically insoluble in\n: methanol and waterj. practically insoluble in ethanol.\nTolazamide. Freely soluble in chloroform\", soluble inarcfewi; \nslightly soluble in ethanol (95per cent); very slightly soluble \nin wafer.\nTolbutamide. Soluble in. ethanol (95 percent), in acetone and \nin chloroform\", slightly soluble in ether, practically insoluble \nin wafer It dissolves in dilute solutions of alkali hydroxides.\nTolnaftate. Freely soluble in acetone and in dichloromethane\", \nvery slightly soluble in ethanol (95 per cent); practically\ninsoluble in water.\nTolterodine Tartrate. Sparingly soluble in methanol; slightly \nsoluble in ethanol (95 per cent).\nTolvaptan. Freely Soluble in benzyl alcohol; soluble in \nmethanol; insoluble in water.\nTolu Balsam. Freely soluble in ether, in ethanol (95per cent), \nin chloroform and in solutions of fixed alkalies usually leaving \nsome insoluble residue; insoluble in water and in hexane.\nTopiramate. Freely soluble in ethyl acetate and in ethanol; \nslightly soluble in water.\nTopotecan Hydrochloride. Soluble in water.\nTorsemide. Slightly soluble in 0.1 Msodium hydroxide, in 0.1 \nM hydrochloric acid, in ethanol (95 per cent), and in \nmethanol; very slightly soluble in acetone and in chloroform; \npractically insoluble in water and in ether.\nTramadol Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in water and in \nmethanol; very slightly soluble in acetone.\nTrandolapril. Soluble in methanol, in chloroform and in \ndichloromethane.\"\nTranexamic Acid. Freely soluble in water and in glacial acetic \nacid, practically insoluble in acetone and in ethanol (95per \ncent). \n. ' \n■ ■ . \n\"\nTranilast Soluble in ethanol, in dimethylformamide; insoluble\nin Maier.\nTravoprost, Vety soluble in methanol, in ethanol (95per cent), \nin acetonitrile and in chloroform; insoluble in water.\n' Trazodone Hydrochloride. Sparingly soluble in chloroform\nand in water.\nTriamcinolone. Sparingly soluble in ethanol (95per cent); \nslightly soluble in water; very slightly soluble in chloroform \nand in ether.\nTriamcinolone Acetonide. Sparingly soluble in chloroform \nand in ethanol (95 per cent); very slightly soluble in ether; \npractically insoluble in water.\nTriamterene. Very slightly soluble in wafer, in chloroform \nand in ethanol (95per cent); practically insoluble in water.\n2.4.26. SOLUBILITY\nIP 20??.\nTrichloromonofluoromethane. Soluble in ethanol-( 95 p,->f 1 \ncent), in ether and in other organic.solvents; practical^ ? \ninsoluble in wafer, \n?\nTriclofos Sodium. Freely soluble in wafer; slightly soluble m J \nethanol (95 per cent), practically insoluble in ether.\nTriethyl Citrate. Miscible with ethanol;(95per cent). Soluble \nin water; slightly soluble in fatty oils..\nTributyl Citrate. Freely soluble in alcohol, in isopropyl \nalcohol, in acetone, and in toluene, insoluble in water.\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in watei, \nsoluble in ethanol (95per cent); slightly soluble in chloroform, \ninsoluble in ether and in benzene.\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride. Soluble in water) in ethanol \n(95per cent) and in acetone; insoluble in ether.\nTrifluridine. Soluble in water.\nTrimebntine Maleate. Soluble in acetonitrile, sparingly \nsoluble in acetone; slightly soluble in wafer and in ethanol \n(95 per cent).\nTrimet azi di ne Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in water; \nsparingly soluble in ethanol (95per cent).\nTrimethobenzamide Hydrochloride. Soluble in water and in \nwarm alcohol, insoluble in etAer.and in benzene. ■\nTrimethoprim. Sparingly soluble in chloroform; slightly \nsoluble in ethanol (95per cent); very slightly soluble in water; \npractically insoluble in ether.- \n>\nTriprohdine Hydrochloride. Very soluble in chloroform; freely \nsoluble in water and in ethanol (95 per cent); practically\ninsoluble in ether.\nTropieamidc. Freely soluble in chloroform, in ethanol (95psr\n■ cent) and in solutions of strong acids; slightly soluble in\nwater ■ \n\" \n■ ■\nTrospium Chloride. Very soluble in water, freely soluble in \nmethanol and practically insoluble in dichloromethane;\nTroxidone. Very soluble in chloroform, in ethanol (95 per ' \ncent) and in ether, soluble in water, \n■\nTryptophan. Soluble iii hot ethanol and inr/i/wfe hydrochloric \nacid.\nTubocurarine Chloride. Soluble in water and in ethanol (95 \nper cent); practically insoluble in acetone, in chloroform and\nin ether. It dissolves in solutions of alkali hydroxides? ■ \n,\nTyiosin. Freely soluble in methanol; soluble in ethanol (95 \nper cent) and in dichlorom'ethane; slightly soluble in' -water.\nIt dissolve in dilute mineral acids, \nf\nTyiosin Tartrate. Freely soluble in water and in \ndichioromethane; slightly soluble in \"ethanol (95 per cent);\n2.4.26. SOLUBILITY\nIP 2022\npractically insoluble in ether. It dissolves in dilute solutions \nof mineral acids. :\nTyrothricin. Soluble in ethanol (95perceni)andihmelhanol; \npractically insoluble in wateK \n..cM \n:\nUlipristal Acetatc. Freely soluble in dlchloromethane, soluble \nin methanol; acetone, ethanol and insoluble in water.\nVndecenoic Acid., Freely soluble in chloroform, in ethanol\n(95percent) in ether and in fixed and in volatile oils; practically , solvents. Soluble or partly soluble in ethanol; practically\n■ J * ■ \n1T1 111*^\n I in 111 zH-niJ \"D vh-l n 1 r\\ Lr-i 1 1 » -4--, \n. . — — —- ' - — Ta - ■ ■! - tai -\ninsoluble in water.\nUrea. Freely soluble in water and in boiling ethanol (95per \ncent); soluble in ethanol (95 per cent); practically insoluble \nin chloroform, in dichioromethane and in "}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/titanium-dioxide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Titanium Dioxide"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Titanium Dioxide\nTiotropium Powder for Inhalation contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 125.0 per cent of the s \namount of tiotropium, C 19H,NO4S2 per unit dose.\n100 ml of distilled water and boil. Filter while hot through a \nhardened filter paper until a clear filtrate is obtained. Wash the \nfilter with 60 ml of distilled water and dilute the combined \nfiltrate and washings to 200 ml with distilled water.To10 ml of \nthis solution add I ml of I ,1 /sulphuric  acid. After 30 minutes \nany opalescence is not more intense than that ofa mixture of\nMol. Wt. 79.9\nUsual strengths.914; 18 jig\nTitanium Dioxide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of TiO2 .\nIdenti fication\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid and topical protectant.\n10 ml of the test solution and 1  ml of distilled 14•ater.\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.  100\n1Icavy metals (2.3.13). Dilute 10 ml of the solution prepared in\nDescription. A white or almost white, infusible powder.\nI Ile test for Barium to 20 ml with  water. 12 ml of the solution \ncomplies with the limit test for  heavy metals. Method D \n(20 ppm), using 10 ml of standard solution (1 ppm p1 ). \nIron. To 8 ml of solution A, add 4 ml of It•ier, mix and add \n0.05 ml of bromine water, allow to stand for 5 minutes, remove \nthe excess of bromine with a current of air and add 3  ml of \npotassium thiocyanate solution. Any colour in the solution"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Titanium Dioxide\nTiotropium Powder for Inhalation contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 125.0 per cent of the s \namount of tiotropium, C 19H,NO4S2 per unit dose.\n100 ml of distilled water and boil. Filter while hot through a \nhardened filter paper until a clear filtrate is obtained. Wash the \nfilter with 60 ml of distilled water and dilute the combined \nfiltrate and washings to 200 ml with distilled water.To10 ml of \nthis solution add I ml of I ,1 /sulphuric  acid. After 30 minutes \nany opalescence is not more intense than that ofa mixture of\nMol. Wt. 79.9\nUsual strengths.914; 18 jig\nTitanium Dioxide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of TiO2 .\nIdenti fication\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid and topical protectant.\n10 ml of the test solution and 1  ml of distilled 14•ater.\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.  100\n1Icavy metals (2.3.13). Dilute 10 ml of the solution prepared in\nDescription. A white or almost white, infusible powder.\nI Ile test for Barium to 20 ml with  water. 12 ml of the solution \ncomplies with the limit test for  heavy metals. Method D \n(20 ppm), using 10 ml of standard solution (1 ppm p1 ). \nIron. To 8 ml of solution A, add 4 ml of It•ier, mix and add \n0.05 ml of bromine water, allow to stand for 5 minutes, remove \nthe excess of bromine with a current of air and add 3  ml of \npotassium thiocyanate solution. Any colour in the solution"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A.When strongly heated it becomes pale yellow; the colour \nis discharged on cooling."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other tests.  Comply with the tests stated under Inhalation\nB.To 0.5 g, add 5 g of anhydrous sodium sulphate and 10 ml \nof water and mix. Add 10 ml ofsulphuric acid and boil gently \nuntil clear; cool, add slowly 30 ml ofa 25 per cent v/v solution \nofsulphuric acid and dilute with water to 100 ml (solution A). \nTo 5 ml of solution A add 0.1 ml ofstrong hydrogen peroxide \nsolution; an orange-red colour is produced.\nPreparations (Powders for Inhalation).\nFollow the procedure described under Assay with suitable \ndilution of the reference solution wherever the amount of \nactive substance is to be determined in any test.\nis not more intense than that in a standard prepared at the \nsame time and in the same manner using a mixture of 4 ml of \niron standard solution  (2 ppm Fe) and 8 ml of a 20 per cent \nw/v solution 91:sulphuric acid (200 ppm). \n•\nAssay.. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolveiit - mixiiire.  90 volumes of 0.05 per cent v/v ort\nC.To 5 ml of solution A add 0.5 g of zinc. in giOnules;-aller_ \n45  minutes a violet-blue colour is produced.:.\ng, transfer to a 300 ml Kjeldahl flask, add  5 g \n•\n• • \nof4nhydroos:sodium sulphate and 10 ml of water, mix and\nphosphoric acid and 10 volumes of acetonitrile.\n- \t\n,\nadd 10 ml ofsulphuric acid. Boil gently until clear, cool, add \nslowly 40 ml of cooled sulphuric acid (25 per cent), cool \nagain and dilute with water to 100.0 ml (solution B). To 300 g \nof zinc, in granules, add 300 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution of \nmercuric nitrate and 2 ml of nitric acid, shake for 10 minutes \nand wash with water. Pack the zinc amalgam into a glass tube \n(400 mm x 20 mm) fitted with a tap and a filter plate. Pass \nthrough the column, at a rate of about 3 ml per minute, 140 ml \nof 1 M sulphuric acid followed by 100 ml of  water, ensuring \nthat the amalgam is covered with liquid throughout. Pass \nslowly through the column, at a rate of about 3 ml per minute, \n200 ml of 0.5 Msulphuric acid followed by 100 ml of water. \nCollect the combined eluates in a 500-m1 conical flask \ncontaining 50 ml of a 15 per cent w/v solution of ferric \nammonium sulphate in sulphuric acid (25 per cent) and \ntitrate immediately with 0.1 M ceric ammonium nitrate using \nferroin solution as indicator until a greenish colour is obtained\n(ni  ml). Pass slowly through the column 100 ml of 0.5 M \nsulphuric acid followed by 20.0 ml of solution B, wash with\n100 ml of 0.5 Msulphuric acid followed by 100 ml of water. \nCollect the combined eluates in a 500-m1 conical flask \ncontaining 50 ml of a 15 per cent w/v solution of  ferric \nammonium sulphate in sulphuric acid (25 per cent), rinse \nthe lower end of the column with water and titrate immediately \nwith 0.1 M eerie ammonium nitrate using ferroin solution as \nindicator until a greenish colour is obtained (n 2 ml).\nCalculate the percentage content of TiO 2 from the expression\n3.99(n2 - n, )/w\nWhere, w is the weight, in g, of the substance under exami-\nnation used in the preparation of solution A.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture. Avoid contact with \naluminium."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tizanidine-hydrochloride-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tizanidine Hydrochloride Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tizanidine Hydrochloride Tablets\nTizanidine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of tizanidine, \nC9HsC1N5S.\nUsual strengths. 2 mg; 4 mg; 6 mg.\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium: 900 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, suitably diluted with dissolution\n3811\nwww.webofpharma.com"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tizanidine Hydrochloride Tablets\nTizanidine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of tizanidine, \nC9HsC1N5S.\nUsual strengths. 2 mg; 4 mg; 6 mg.\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium: 900 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, suitably diluted with dissolution\n3811\nwww.webofpharma.com"},{"name":"TIZANIDINE TABLETS","text":"medium if necessary, at the maximum at about 320 nm (2.4.7). \nCalculate the content of C9HsC1NsS in the medium from the \nabsorbance obtained from a solution of known concentration \nof tizanidine Hydrochloride IPRS inthe same medium..\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nc9hscin5s. \n. ’ . • '\nUniformity of content. Complies with the tests stated under \nTablets.; . \n. .. \n....... \n;\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as described \nunder Assay. \n•• - '\nTest solution.. Crush one tablet and disperse in 50 ml phosphate \nbuffer pH 6.6, dilute to 100.0 ml with acetonitrile and filter.\nReference solution. Dissolve about 10 mg oftizanidine \nhydrochloride IP RS, in 25 ml ofphosphate buffer pH 6.6 and \ndilute to 50.0 ml with acetonitrile. Dilute a suitable volume of \nthe solution in a mixture of equal volumes of phosphate buffer \npH 6,6 and acetonitrile to obtain a concentration similar to\nthe test, solution, :\nCalculate the content of C9HaClN5S.\nOther tests. Comply witfi the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity \nof the powdered tablet containing about 20 mg of Tizanidine, \ndisperse in 50 ml phosphate buffer pH 6.6 and dilute to\n100.0 ml with acetonitrile and filter.\nReference solution. Weigh 10 mg of tizanidine hydrochloride \nIFRS, dissolve in 25 ml phosphate bufferpH, 6.6 and dilute to\n5 0.0 ml with acetonitrile.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 80 volumes of phosphate\nbuffer pH 6.6 and 20 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- \n. spectrophotometer set at 320 nm,. .\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution’ The test is: riot valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. :\nCalculate the content of C9H8C1N5S. \n'\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.:\nLabelling. The label states; the strength in terms, of the \nequivalent amount of Tizanid ine. .. ; \n■_ \n:,\n..... ....... ......... ......."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tizanidine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tizanidine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tizanidine Hydrochloride\nTizanidine Hydrochloride is 5-chloro-W-(2-imidazolin-2-yl>\n2,1,3-benzothiadiazoM-yiamine. \n1 <\nTizanidine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 percent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C9H8C1N5SHCÎ, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory, a-adrenergic agonist. \n!\nDescription. Awhite to yellowish white, crystalline powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tizanidine Hydrochloride\nTizanidine Hydrochloride is 5-chloro-W-(2-imidazolin-2-yl>\n2,1,3-benzothiadiazoM-yiamine. \n1 <\nTizanidine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 percent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C9H8C1N5SHCÎ, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory, a-adrenergic agonist. \n!\nDescription. Awhite to yellowish white, crystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":">\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2*4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with \nhydrochloride IPRS. . . \n.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak ia^\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\n3810\nwww.webofpharma.com\nrjtgivesreactionfA) for chlorides (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"3.5 to 5.3, determined on 5.0 per cent w/v solution\nin water.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n/- \"\nJest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 50.0 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \n¡^¡dinehydrochloride IP RS in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (bfDihitQ 1 ml ofreference solution (a) to\n100 ml with mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n_ a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\n. octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- columntemperature: 5 0°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 80 volumes of buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 3.5 g of sodium-1-pentane \nsulphonate in1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 3.0 with\n■ 12per cent orthophosphoric acid solution or 1 M\nsodium hydroxide and 20 volumes of acetonitrile, \n- flow rate: 1ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 230 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the\ntailing factoris not more than2.0 and the column efficiency is \nnot less than 5000 theoretical plates.\nInject reference? solution (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.5 times the area of the peak\nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b)\n(0,5 per cent) and the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks \nis not more than the area of the peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (1.0 per cent)."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Heavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with limit test for heavy\nmetals, Method A (20 ppm).\n'Total Chloride. 11.9 per cent to 12.5 per cent.\n^eigh 0.5 gand dissolve in 50 ml of water. Titrate with 0.1 M \nsilver nitrate. Determine the end point potentiometrically \n^•4.25). Carry but a blank titration."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nM. silver. nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545 g of \nchloride.\n^u^Phated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\n^oss <Jhdrying{2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined\nOa $ £ by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.or