ide, and 50 volumes of\nacetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 230 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate inj ections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution..\nCalculate the content.of C9HsC1NsS.HC1.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nTizanidine Tablets"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/to-fluprost","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"To fluprost"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tofluprost\n14 \n28 \n72 \n22 \n28 \n72\nHO\nInject the test solution. The area of any secondary peak is not \nmore than 0.5 per cent and the sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated by \narea normalization.\nOyCH3\nCH3\nHO\nF F\nWater  (2.4.43) . Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined on\n(95 per cent) and 4 volumes of a 1.0 per cent w/v solution of\n1 0 mg by thermo gravimetric analysis.\n1 .10-phenanthroline hydrochloride in ethanol (95 per cent). \nIn We chronfaiograms obtained with test solution (b) and \nrefereneesolution (b) the spot of lower Rf value a-tocopherY 1\n' Instrument condition\ntempetature: 25° to 200° at the rate of 10° per \nminute,using nitrogen.\nC22112RF2()\n is \noi-an&e.\n2\n-\nDetermine weight loss of sample between 25° to 200°.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 72 volumes of methanol and 28 volumes of\nwater.\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture. Dilute 10.0 ml of this solution to 20.0 ml \nwith the solvent mixture\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of toiluprost \nRS in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 5 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (3 pm), \n-\nmobile phase: A.water,\nB. methanol, \n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n-\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 219 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nMobile phase B\nMobile phase A\nTime \n(in min.)\n(per cent viv)\n(per cent v/v)\n0 \n28 \n72\n4 \n28 \n72\n8 \n20 \n80\n13 \n20 \n80\n14 \n28 \n72\n22 \n28 \n72\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless, the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 1000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C,H 20205"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Tofluprost\n14 \n28 \n72 \n22 \n28 \n72\nHO\nInject the test solution. The area of any secondary peak is not \nmore than 0.5 per cent and the sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated by \narea normalization.\nOyCH3\nCH3\nHO\nF F\nWater  (2.4.43) . Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined on\n(95 per cent) and 4 volumes of a 1.0 per cent w/v solution of\n1 0 mg by thermo gravimetric analysis.\n1 .10-phenanthroline hydrochloride in ethanol (95 per cent). \nIn We chronfaiograms obtained with test solution (b) and \nrefereneesolution (b) the spot of lower Rf value a-tocopherY 1\n' Instrument condition\ntempetature: 25° to 200° at the rate of 10° per \nminute,using nitrogen.\nC22112RF2()\n is \noi-an&e.\n2\n-\nDetermine weight loss of sample between 25° to 200°.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 72 volumes of methanol and 28 volumes of\nwater.\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture. Dilute 10.0 ml of this solution to 20.0 ml \nwith the solvent mixture\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of toiluprost \nRS in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 5 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (3 pm), \n-\nmobile phase: A.water,\nB. methanol, \n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n-\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 219 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nMobile phase B\nMobile phase A\nTime \n(in min.)\n(per cent viv)\n(per cent v/v)\n0 \n28 \n72\n4 \n28 \n72\n8 \n20 \n80\n13 \n20 \n80\n14 \n28 \n72\n22 \n28 \n72\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless, the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 1000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C,H 20205"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tobramycin-and-fluorometholone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tobramycin and Fluorometholone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tobramycin and Fluorometholone \nAcétate Ophthalmic Suspension\nTobramycin and Fluorometholone Acetate Ophthalmic \nSuspension is a sterile aqueous solution of Tobramycin \nSulphate and Fluorometholone Acetate ih a suitable aqueous \nvehicle. It may contain suitable buffers, dispersants, tonicity- \nadjusting agents and preservatives.\n^I^ycin and Fluorometholone Acetate Ophthalmic \nSuspension contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more\ntJ30 120.0 per cent of the labelled amount of tobramycin\nu^37^sO9and not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than\nJ$-Opericentpfthe labelled amount of fluorometholone acetate\na .. **®*gtti : Tobramycin 3.0 mg and Fluorometholone \nAcetate'1.0mgperml. \n:\nA. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.-17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF 254.\nSolvent mixture.. A mixture of 10Q volumes, of butyl alcohol \nand 1 volume of pyridine. \n,\nMobile phase. A mixture-of 60- volumes of methanol, \n25 volumes of chloroform and ,3,0: volumes, of ammonium \nhydroxide. . \n'\nTest solution. Allow the ophthalmic suspension to settle, and \ndecant 1 ml of the supernatant liquid into a test tube. Add \n0.1 g of sodium sulphate and centrifuge, Use the clear \nsupernatant liquid.\nReference solution (a). A 0.6 per cent w/v solution of \ntobramycin IPRS in water. ■\nReference solution (b). A mixture of equal volumes of the test \nsolution and reference solution (a). \n, : .\nApply to the plate 3 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 8 cm, After development, dry the plate in air, héàt \nthe plate at 110° for 15 minutes, spray with 1.0 per cent w/v \nsolution of ninhydrin in solvent mixture. The chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution exhibits a pink fluorescent \nband due to tobramycin, The Rf value of the principal spot in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution; and the \nchromatogram obtained with: reference solution (b), \nrespectively, correspond to those of reference solution (a).\nB. In the Assay for fluorometholone acetate, the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with thé test solution \ncorresponds to thé peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe réference solution.\npH (2.4.24). 6.0to 7.0. \n,\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Eye Drops.\nAssay. For tobramycin —: Determine, by liquid \nchromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture A. A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of 2,4- \ndinitrofluorobenzene in alcohol. \n,; , .t,..\nSolvent mixture B. A 1.5 per cent w/v solution of tris\n(hydroxymethyl), aminomethane in water. ...... \n; .\nSolvent mixture C. A 0.3 per cent w/v solution of tris\n(hydroxymethyl) aminomethane prepared as follows. Transfer \n40 ml of solvent mixture B to a 200.0 ml volumetric flask, add \ndimethyl sulphoxide while mixing and dilute with \ndimethylsulphbxide to the volume. ; ;\nNOTE — Use this reagent within 4 hours. If kept immersed in \nan ice-water bath below-10°, the reagent may be used up to \n8 hours). \n•' :\n3817\nwww.webofpharma.com\n.TOBRAMYCIN AND FLUOROMETHOLONE ACETATE OPTHALMIC SUSPENSION\nTest solution. Dilute a measured volume of ophthalmic solution \nto obtain a solution having a concentration of 0.009 per cent \nw/v of Tobramycin unwater.\nReference solution (a). Transfer an accurately weighed \nquantity of 55 mg\" of tobramycinIPRS to a 50.0 ml volumetric \nflask, add 1ml of 0.5Msulphuric acid and sufficient -water to \ndissolve and dilute to 50.0 ml with water. / \n? ; \n/ ;\nReference solution (b). Dilute reference solution (a) \\vith the \nwater to obtain a solution containing 0.022 per centSv/v of \ntobramycin. \n-rm Rv .•><.' .\nReference solution (c). A 0.024 per cent w/v solution of \np-naphiholbenzein in acetonitrile. . .\nNOTE — Use freshly prepared solution.\nReference solution (d). Dilute 2.0 ml of reference solution (c) \nto 10.0 ml with the derivatised reference solution (b).\nDerivatise the solutions prior to analysis by transferring \n4.0 ml of reference solution (b), 10.0 ml of test solution and 4.0 \nml of.water to separate 50 ml volumetric flask. Add 10 ml of \nsolven"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Tobramycin and Fluorometholone \nAcétate Ophthalmic Suspension\nTobramycin and Fluorometholone Acetate Ophthalmic \nSuspension is a sterile aqueous solution of Tobramycin \nSulphate and Fluorometholone Acetate ih a suitable aqueous \nvehicle. It may contain suitable buffers, dispersants, tonicity- \nadjusting agents and preservatives.\n^I^ycin and Fluorometholone Acetate Ophthalmic \nSuspension contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more\ntJ30 120.0 per cent of the labelled amount of tobramycin\nu^37^sO9and not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than\nJ$-Opericentpfthe labelled amount of fluorometholone acetate\na .. **®*gtti : Tobramycin 3.0 mg and Fluorometholone \nAcetate'1.0mgperml. \n:\nA. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.-17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF 254.\nSolvent mixture.. A mixture of 10Q volumes, of butyl alcohol \nand 1 volume of pyridine. \n,\nMobile phase. A mixture-of 60- volumes of methanol, \n25 volumes of chloroform and ,3,0: volumes, of ammonium \nhydroxide. . \n'\nTest solution. Allow the ophthalmic suspension to settle, and \ndecant 1 ml of the supernatant liquid into a test tube. Add \n0.1 g of sodium sulphate and centrifuge, Use the clear \nsupernatant liquid.\nReference solution (a). A 0.6 per cent w/v solution of \ntobramycin IPRS in water. ■\nReference solution (b). A mixture of equal volumes of the test \nsolution and reference solution (a). \n, : .\nApply to the plate 3 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 8 cm, After development, dry the plate in air, héàt \nthe plate at 110° for 15 minutes, spray with 1.0 per cent w/v \nsolution of ninhydrin in solvent mixture. The chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution exhibits a pink fluorescent \nband due to tobramycin, The Rf value of the principal spot in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution; and the \nchromatogram obtained with: reference solution (b), \nrespectively, correspond to those of reference solution (a).\nB. In the Assay for fluorometholone acetate, the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with thé test solution \ncorresponds to thé peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe réference solution.\npH (2.4.24). 6.0to 7.0. \n,\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Eye Drops.\nAssay. For tobramycin —: Determine, by liquid \nchromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture A. A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of 2,4- \ndinitrofluorobenzene in alcohol. \n,; , .t,..\nSolvent mixture B. A 1.5 per cent w/v solution of tris\n(hydroxymethyl), aminomethane in water. ...... \n; .\nSolvent mixture C. A 0.3 per cent w/v solution of tris\n(hydroxymethyl) aminomethane prepared as follows. Transfer \n40 ml of solvent mixture B to a 200.0 ml volumetric flask, add \ndimethyl sulphoxide while mixing and dilute with \ndimethylsulphbxide to the volume. ; ;\nNOTE — Use this reagent within 4 hours. If kept immersed in \nan ice-water bath below-10°, the reagent may be used up to \n8 hours). \n•' :\n3817\nwww.webofpharma.com\n.TOBRAMYCIN AND FLUOROMETHOLONE ACETATE OPTHALMIC SUSPENSION\nTest solution. Dilute a measured volume of ophthalmic solution \nto obtain a solution having a concentration of 0.009 per cent \nw/v of Tobramycin unwater.\nReference solution (a). Transfer an accurately weighed \nquantity of 55 mg\" of tobramycinIPRS to a 50.0 ml volumetric \nflask, add 1ml of 0.5Msulphuric acid and sufficient -water to \ndissolve and dilute to 50.0 ml with water. / \n? ; \n/ ;\nReference solution (b). Dilute reference solution (a) \\vith the \nwater to obtain a solution containing 0.022 per centSv/v of \ntobramycin. \n-rm Rv .•><.' .\nReference solution (c). A 0.024 per cent w/v solution of \np-naphiholbenzein in acetonitrile. . .\nNOTE — Use freshly prepared solution.\nReference solution (d). Dilute 2.0 ml of reference solution (c) \nto 10.0 ml with the derivatised reference solution (b).\nDerivatise the solutions prior to analysis by transferring \n4.0 ml of reference solution (b), 10.0 ml of test solution and 4.0 \nml of.water to separate 50 ml volumetric flask. Add 10 ml of \nsolvent mixture A and 10 ml of solvent mixture C to each flask. \nShake, vigorously for few seconds and heat in a water-bath at \n60°± 2° for 50 ± 5 minutes. Cool in cold water for 10 minutes \nand. dilute with acetonitrile. ■; \n;\nChromatographic system ■\n- a stainless steel column 30 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\nr octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n, - mobile phase: dissolve ,1.0 ,gof iris(hydroxymethyl)\n,;: aminomethane in 400 ml of water, add 10 ml of 0.5M\n■■. sulphuric acid, dilute with acetonitrileXo 1000 ml and\nmix. Cool and pass through a filter of 0.2 pm or .finer pore \nsize.\n- flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 365 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe relative retention time with reference to tobramycin for p- \nnaphtholbenzein is about 0.6.\nInject reference solution (d). The test is not valid unless the\nresolution between the peaks due to p-naphtholbenzein arid \ntobramycin is not less than 4.0.\nInject the derivatised reference solution (b). The test is hot \nvalid unless the relative standard deviation for replicate \ninjectionisnotmorethan2.0,percent. , y\nInject the derivatised reference solution (b) and the test \n'solution'. \n! ■'' ‘ \n' 7/ \"\nCalculate the content of Ci8H37N5O9 in; Ophthalmic"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tobramycin-inhalation-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tobramycin Inhalation Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TOBRAMYCIN INHALATION SOLUTION\nTime m\nMobile phase A\n1; Mobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n... ,... 0\n..............21\n... .14 ;;;\n... .66\n25 ..\n30\n>(i 35 .\n. 70 ........... ..\n40\n'• ' '20^';\n50\n5\n95\n2 Relative j \nretention time .\n; ; ■ f ; \\ \n\\ - i ■ ■ : '\nName\nSpecified unidentified impurity \n, : : ;\n; \n0.36\nDeoxystreptaniine kanosaminide\n0.66\n004 \n'\nKanàmycin B:\n0.96\nv \n10\nTobramycin\nInject derivatised reference solution (c).The test is not valid \nunless the capacity factor is not less than 15.5 for tobramycin, \nand .the; resolution is not less than 1.0 between the nebramine \nand kanamycin peaks. \n, .• ;r r... .,.\nCompare the ; derivatised reference solution (b) and- (c) \nchromatograms to • identify degradation -peaks. \nDeoxystrcptamine kanosaminide and nebramine will increase \nin response in derivatized reference solution (c) \n!\nInject the derivatized reference solution (b), (e) and blank. \nThe relative standard deviation for replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent with reference .solution (e). Disregard \nany peak corresponding to those from the derivatis