n dilute with acetonitrile to 50.0 n11-\n■■ ?\ns\nTOBRAMYCIN AND FLUOROMETHOLONE ACETATE OPTHALMIC SUSPENSION\njp2022\nsolutions thus obtained are the derivatized reference \ngolution(b),; derivatized test solution and derivatized blank \nælution, respectively."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Reference solution(c). A0.024 per cent ofp-naphtholbenzein\n¿ce/pnhrife:Transfer 2.0 ml of the solution to a 10.0 ml \nvolumetric flask, dilute with derivatized réference solution (b) to \nvolume, and use promptly. .\nChromatdjgrbphic system\nj a stainless steel column 30 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), ?\n~ mobile phase: Dissolve 2.0 g of tris (hydroxymethyl)\naminomethane inabout 800,ml of water. To this solution \nadd 20. ml of ,0.5, M sulphuric , acid, and dilute with \nacetonitrile to obtain 2000 ml of solution, r\n- flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 365 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject derivatized reference solution (b) and derivatized blank \nsolution, and reference solution(c). The.. relative retention \ntimes for p-naphfholbenzein and tobramycin are about 0.6 and\n1.0, respectively. Identify the solvent and reagent peaks using \nthe defivàtized blank solution. Resolution between \np-naphtholbenzein and tobramycin is not less than 4.0 \nobtained!with reference solution (c) and relative' standard \ndeviation is notmore than 2.0 per cent for derivatized reference \nsolution (b). .\nInject the réference solutidn(b) and the test solution.\nCalculate the contents of tobramycin,C;81137N5O9.\nStorage. Store protected from light and keep in a refrigerator."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tobramycin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tobramycin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tobramycin Injection\nTobramycin Sulphate Injection , ;.: \"7 ]\nTobramycin Injection is a sterile solution of Tobramycis I\nSulphate in Water for Injections or of Tobramycin in j \nfor Injections containing sufficient Sulphuric Acid to adiu^ !\ntO pH 3.5 tO 6.0. , . \n■ v ' : 7 7 \n. j\nTobramycin Inj ection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and j \nnot more ,than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntobramycin,Ci8H37N5O9. \n... \nj\nUsual: strengths. 10 mg per ml; 40 mg per ml. < ;. 7 ; ( ; .\nDescription. A colourless solution. \n'a1 \n<"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tobramycin Injection\nTobramycin Sulphate Injection , ;.: \"7 ]\nTobramycin Injection is a sterile solution of Tobramycis I\nSulphate in Water for Injections or of Tobramycin in j \nfor Injections containing sufficient Sulphuric Acid to adiu^ !\ntO pH 3.5 tO 6.0. , . \n■ v ' : 7 7 \n. j\nTobramycin Inj ection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and j \nnot more ,than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntobramycin,Ci8H37N5O9. \n... \nj\nUsual: strengths. 10 mg per ml; 40 mg per ml. < ;. 7 ; ( ; .\nDescription. A colourless solution. \n'a1 \n<"},{"name":"Identification","text":"?\nI\nA. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17),.coating \nthe plate with silicagelH. . \n... \n.......\nMobile phase. A mixture of 60 volumes of methanol \n40 volumes of strong ammonia solution and. 20 volumes of \nchloroform. • . , \n?\nTest solution. Dilute a suitable volume of the injection With \nwater to produce a solution containing Q.4. per cent w/v \nsolution of tobramycin. ....... i . ; . \n:\nReference solution (a). A’0;4 per cent'W/v)solution of \ntobramycin 1PRS in water. \naacac\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.4 per cent \nw/v each offanamycin sulphate IFRS, neotpycin sulphate \nIPRS and tobramycin IPRS in water. .-.a y7.\nApply to the. plate 5 pl of.each solution^ After deyelppment, \ndry the plate in warm air, spray with a mixturc.of equal volumes \nof a 46 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric acid and a 0.2 per \ncent w/v solution off,3-naphth'alenedidl in ethanol (95per \ncent) and heat at 105° for lOininutes. Thepiïricïpaïspotinthe \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained withreference solution (a). \nThe test is not valid unless the chromâtogra m obtained wuh \nreference solution ,(b) shows three clearly separated principal \nspots. '//. f\" ' ;i .' . ' '. . ... \n...\nB. It. gives the reactionsof sulphates (2.3.1 )."},{"name":"Tests","text":": H-.- X, A,;\npH (2.4.24). 3.5 to 6.0. \n! \n: '\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatograph i\n(2.4.17), coating the plate with.sv'/zcuge/ZA • \n; I\nMobile phase. Admixture of equal volumes of strong ainmoina | \nsolution, 2~butanone and ethanol (95 per; cent) ? \n|\nTest solution. Dilute a suitable volume of the injection \nI\n0.01 M ammonia to obtain a solution containing 40 mg \n|\n3814\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOBRAMYCIN INHALATION SOLUTION\nIP 20221\nT \net^er an^ use\nO'02 Per cent w^v- solution,of the \nexamination in 0.02 M ammonia.\nIvtotheplate 5 p.1 of each solution. After development, \n? ij,e platein warm air, heat at 110° for 10 minutes and spray\nsolution prepared immediately before use \nh'diM^S^,dium hypochlorite solution (3 per cent. Cl)\nvith ^contain 0.5 per cent w/v of available chlorine.\nin aTl^ht idf co'c^ a’r unt^ a sprayed area of the plate\nbelow die line pf application gives at most a very faint blue\neolour vvith a drQp of potassium iodide and starch solution; \navoid piolonged exposurc to cold air. Spray the plate: with \n^assiutn iodide and starch solution. Any secondary spot\nin the chromatogram obtained with the test solution is not \n[riofe'tiitW^ &an the spot in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the reference solution.\njal ¿hdptbxins (2.2.3). Not more than 2.0 Endotoxin \nUnits pertrig of tobramycin.\nOther teists. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations(Injections).\nAssay. Determine by the microbiological assay of antibiotics, \nMethodB (2.2.10). . . \n: . \n... . <■ \n;w.-;\nCalculate the content of tobramycin in the injection, taking \neach!000Units found to be equivalent to 1 mg of tobramycin.\nTobramycin Inhalation Solution\nTobramycin inhalation Solution is a sterile, non pyrogenic) \npreservative free solution of Tobramycin or Tobramycin \nSulphate in Watef for Injection containing sodium \nchloride, f f'\nTobramycin inhalation Solution contains hot less than 90.0\nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount \noftobramycin Cl8H37N5O9.\n^suMsfrength.3OO mg per 5 ml. ■ ■■ .\nMeijfificatioji ;\nAssay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \n(lie test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nC^mit°gram obtained with the reference solution(b).\nsubstances. Determine by liquid Chromatography\nTest solution A 0.0192 per cent of the substance under \nexaminationinwater.\nReference solution(a).A 0.11 per cent w/v solution of \ntobramycin IP RS in water, and adjusted to pH 6.0 with \n0.5 M sulphuric acidpnà. dilute with water.\nReference solutionfb). A 0.022 per cent of tobramycin from \nthe reference solution (a) in water.\nReference solution (c). Heat a portion of reference solution \n(a) in a suitable sealed glass container at 100° for 8-9 hours.Cool \nto room temperature, and dilute with water to obtain a 0.022 \nper cent w/v solution of tobramycin.\nReference solution(d). A 0.11 per cent w/v solution of \ntobramycin IPRS prepared by adding 0.5M sulphuric acid, \nusing 2.0 per cent of the final volume, and enough water to \ndissolve the tobramycin, and dilute with water to volume.\nReference solution(e). 0.00011 per cent of tobramycin from \nthe reference solution (d) in water.\nBlank.Water. . \n.......\nDeriv'dtization procedure. Heat all solutions at the sarhe \ntemperature and for the samé duration as indicated. Move all \nflasks to 60° constant water-bath at the samé time. To separate \n50 ml flasks, transfer 15.0 ml of reference solution(b), 15.0 rnl \nof reference solution (c), 15.0 ml of reference solution (e), 15,0 \nml of test solution, and 15.0 ml of blank. To.each flaskadd 10 \nml of 2,4-dinitxofluoro.benzene reagent, and 10 ml of \ntris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethane reagent, shake, and insert \nthe stopper. Place the flasks in a constant temperature bath at \n60°, and heat for 50 minutes. Remove the flasks from the bath, \nand allow to stand fór 10 minutes, Add 2.0 ml of acetonitrile \nallow to cool to room temperature, and dilute with acetonitrile', \nto 50.0 ml. Allow the solutions to stand for 16 hours. The \nsolutions thusobtained are derivatized reference solution (b), \nderivatized reference solution (c), reference solution (e), test \nsolution, and blank solution. ' \n' ...........' \" J\nChromatographic system \n.'■\"■■.-w :....\n- \n. a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \nphenyl group chemically bonded to porous silica (5 um),\n- mobile phase: ,A.a mixture of ^volumes, of acetonitrile,\n95 volumes of water and 0.08, volumes of. ortho-\n... \n■ phosphoric acid, .. \n.-.eg r .. \n■■ \n.\nB. a mixture of 75 .volumes of acetonitrile, \n25 volumes of water, and 0.08 .volumes pf ortho-\n.. phosphoric acid, , \nmot; ■ \nrm\nT- a gradient programme using the, conditions given\nbelow,-., j / .• \n..y, .-\n, - nowraie:1.2mlperminute, . \n. •\n■ spectrophotometer set at 365 nm,\n- injection volume: 45.pl."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tobramycin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tobramycin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tobramycin\nC18H37N5O9 \nMol. Wt. 4673\nTobramycin is 6-0-(3-amino-3-dcoxy-a-D-glucopyranosyl)-2- \ndeoxy-4-(7-(2,6-diamino-2,3,6-trideoxy-fr-D,i./^(i_\nhexopyranosyl)-D-streptamine, an antimicrobial substance \nproduced by Streptomyces tenebrarius or by any other means.\nTobramycin has potency not less than 900 mcg of CTTNA \nper mg, calculated on anhydrous basis. \n, ~\nCategory.Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.; P"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tobramycin\nC18H37N5O9 \nMol. Wt. 4673\nTobramycin is 6-0-(3-amino-3-dcoxy-a-D-glucopyranosyl)-2- \ndeoxy-4-(7-(2,6-diamino-2,3,6-trideoxy-fr-D,i./^(i_\nhexopyranosyl)-D-streptamine, an antimicrobial substance \nproduced by Streptomyces tenebrarius or by any other means.\nTobramycin has potency not less than 900 mcg of CTTNA \nper mg, calculated on anhydrous basis. \n, ~\nCategory.Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.; P"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel H. \n: '\nMobile phase. A mixture of 60 volumes,of methanol, \n40 volumes of strong ammonia solution and 20 volumes of \nchloroform. .\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.4 g of the substance under \nexamination in 100 ml of water.\nReference solution fa). A 0.4 per cent w/v solution of\ntobramycin IPRS in water.\nReference solution (b). A' solution containing 0.4 per cent \nw/v each of kanamycin sulphate IPRS, neomycin sulphate \nIPRS and tobramycin IPRS in water. \n} k :\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in warm air, spray with a mixture of equal volumes \nof a 46 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric acid and a 0.2 per \ncent w/v solution of 1,3-naphthalenediol in ethanol (95 PP \ncent) and heat at 105° for 10 minutes. The principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution CQ? \nThe test is not valid unless the chromatogram obtained wi \nreference solution (b) shows three clearly separated principa1\nspots. ■■ \n' -P¡pP ■- '\n3812\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOBRAMYCIN\næ2022\npjssolve about 5 mg in 5 ml ,qf wafer, acid 5 ml of a 0.1 per\n■ \n« water-bath for 3 minutes; a violét-blue 'colour\nheat in« .\ndevelop?:.;///:.• 7 7/• \n■' '\nU solution of ninhydrin in ethanol (95 per cent) and\nvu:.///■■■ \n.. \n2\nH(2 4 24). 9-0 t0 H O, determined in a 10.0 per cent w/v\nsolution. ; Q.Çfr ..■■■ : 1 r; •• .\nc^ificoDtical rotation (2.4.22). +138° to + 148°, determined\nin a 4.0 per cent w/v solution.\n2.Methyl-l-ProPai*°l- Not more than 1.0 per centw/w, \ndetermined by gas chromatography (2.4.13). \n; \n.\nTest solution (a).A10 per cent w/v solution of the substance\nunder examination in water..\nTeslsoliltiilnf^fl A solution containing 10 per cent w/v of the\nsubstance under examination and 0.2 per cent v/v of \n2-propanol (internal standard). . : \n. \nfr\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.1 per cent w/v of \n2-methyl-l’prppQnol and 0.2 per cent v/v of thé infernal\nstandard. / / ^hfr. 77 \n.•\nChromatographie system : \n, : \n,\n- aglasscolumn 1.5mx4mm,packedwithporouspdlymer\nbeads (80 to 100 mesh) (Such as Porapak Q),\n- temperature, column 165°, \n. , ; / \n,\nCalculate the percentage w/w of 2-methy l-l-propanol.\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-Iayér chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel II.\nMobile phase. A mixture'of equal Volumes of strong ammonia \nsolutlôri, 2-bûiâhbnè andethanol (95'per-cent) ', \"i 'x- \n7,7 / 0 i: hilC Vl'Ws.' ■/>';''■'■ \n:. ' ■. ' 07 V\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.8 g of the substance under \nexamination \n. r:\nReference solution. A 0.008 per cent'w/v solution of the \nsubstance under examination in 0.02<M ammonia. ■\nApplytothe plate 5 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in warm air, heat at 110° for 10 minutes arid spray \nthe hot plate, with a solution prepared immediately before usé \nby Cdudug sodiumhypochloriie solution (3 per cent Cl) \nwith water to contain 0.5 per cent w/v of available chlorine,\nin a current of cold air until a sprayed area of thé plate \nbelow the line of application gives at most a very faint blue\nwith a drop of potassium iodide and starch solution', \nav°id prolonged exposure to cold air. Spray the plate with\npotassium iodide and starch solution. Any secondary spot\nm ths .chromatogram obtained; with the test solution is hot\nintense than, the spot, in .the: chromatogram obtained\nU1{Jl reference solution.\n^ulpbated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.3 per.cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 8.0 per cent; determined on \n0.3g. \n7'U /-V \n.. . V - . \n• r. . • . \n?•\nAssay: Détermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\n2, 4-Dinitrofluorobenzene reagent. A1.0 per cent w/v solution \noi2,4-dinitrofluorobenzene in absolute alcohol: This solution \nmay be used for 5 days if refrigerated when not in use.\nTris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethqne reagent. A 1.5.per cent \nw/v solution offris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethcine in water. \nThis solution may be used for 1 month if refrigerated when \nnotin use. To 40 ml of the solution add dimethyl sulphoxide \nwith mixing, dilute with dimethyl sulphoxide to 20Q ml, and \nmix. Use this reagent within 4 hours (NOTE—Ifkept.immersed\nin an ice —water bath below I0° the reagent may be usedfor \nup to 8 hours). \n.. \n; .\nTest solution (a). Dissolve 55 mg of the substance under \nexarriinatioti ih 1 ml of 0:5M sulphuric ùczî/and sufficient \nwater and dilute to 50.0 ml with water. Dilute TO.O ini of tìiè \nsolution to 50.0. ml with water.... \n=,>.. , :.;1. ...\nTest solution (b). To 4.0 ml of test solution(a); add 10 ml of 2(4 \ndinitrofluorobenzene reagent and 10 ml of trisfhydroxy- \nmèthyl)àmìnpmethdhé'reagent, shake, and insert thè stopper. \nPlace the flask in a constant température bath at 60 ± 2°, and \nheat for 50 ± 5 minutcs.Remove the flasks from thé bath, and\nallow to stand for 10 minutes. Add acetonitrile to about 2 nil \nbelow the 50 ml mark, allow to cool to room temperature, then \ndilute with acetonitrile to volume, and mix.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 55;mg of tobramycin IPRS in\n1 ml of 0.5M sulphuric acid and enough water and dilute tq \n50.0 ml with water. Dilute 1 Ó.0 ml of the solution to 5Ò.Ó ml with\nwater. . p. -, xxXx-77 77 fr 7/..; 77 \nvvimvvfrd/\nReference solution (b). To4.0ml of reference solution (a), add\n10 ml of 2;4 dinitrofluorobenzene reagent and 10 ml of \ntris(hydroxymethyl)aminomeihane reagent, slfè&id, arid insert \ntire stopper. Place the flask in a consiant tcmperaturc. bath.at \n60 ± 2 °, and heat for 50 ± 5 minutes. Remove, thefrasks from \nthe bath, and allow to stand for 10 minutes. Add acetonitrile \nto about 2 ml below the 50 ml mark, allow to cool' to room \ntemperature, then dilute with acetonitrile, to volume, and mix...\nReference solution (c). A 0.024 per cent w/v solution of p- \nnaptholbenzein in acetonitrile. Dilute 2.0 ml of the solution \nwith reference solution (b) to 10.0 ml and use promptly.\nKlank solution, To 4"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tocopheryl-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tocopheryl Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TOCOPHERYL ACETATE\nIP2Q22\n1 011 of ft/M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.01191 g of\n$orage-Store protected from moisture.\nTocopheryl Acetate\na-Tocopheryl Acetate; a-Tocopherol Acetate; Vitamin\nE Acetate\nC31H52O3 (V-‘ \nMol. Wt. 472.8\nTocopherol Acetate is (22?5,,4*7?5,8W5)-6-acetoxy-2,\n5,7,8- \ntetramcthyl-2-(4', 8', 12’-trimethyl tri-decyl)chroman(n//-rac-a- \ntocopherol acetate).\nTocopheryl Acetate contains not less than 96.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C3i H52O3."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TOCOPHERYL ACETATE\nIP2Q22\n1 011 of ft/M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.01191 g of\n$orage-Store protected from moisture.\nTocopheryl Acetate\na-Tocopheryl Acetate; a-Tocopherol Acetate; Vitamin\nE Acetate\nC31H52O3 (V-‘ \nMol. Wt. 472.8\nTocopherol Acetate is (22?5,,4*7?5,8W5)-6-acetoxy-2,\n5,7,8- \ntetramcthyl-2-(4', 8', 12’-trimethyl tri-decyl)chroman(n//-rac-a- \ntocopherol acetate).\nTocopheryl Acetate contains not less than 96.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C3i H52O3."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Category. Vitamin E supplement.\nDescription. A clear, slightly greenish yellow, viscous, oily\nliquid;\n,*£:i 1’. •"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may be omitted iftests B and C are carried out. Tests B \nandCmay be omitted if test A is carried out.\nA: Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with ci-tocopheryl \ndceiafelPRS.\nB. When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.01 per cent w/v solution in ethanol exhibits a maximuni at \nabout 284 nm, a shoulder at about 278 nm and a minimum at \n.about 254 nm.\nC. Detemiine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\n. the plate with silica gel HF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 80 volumes of cyclohexane and \n20 volumes of ether.\nTest solution (a). Dissolve 0.5 g of the substance under \nexamination in 100 nA oi cyclohexane.\nTest solution (b). Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 2 ml of 5 Methanolic sulphuric acid, heat on \nWater-bath for 5 minutes, cool, add 2 ml of water and 2 ml of \ncyclohexane and shake for 1 minute; use the upper layer.\nReference solution (a). A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of \na-tocopheryl acetate IPRS in cyclohexane.\nReference solution (b). Prepare in the same manner as test\nsolution (b) but using 10 mg of a-tocopheryl acetate IPRS in \nplace of the substance under examination.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm. The principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with \ntest solution (a) corresponds to that in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a). There are two spots in \neach of the chromatograms obtained with test solution (b) \nand reference solution (b). The spots of higher Rf value are \ndue to a-tocopheryl acetate and correspond tó that in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). The spots \nof lower Rf value are due to a-tocopheryl. Spray the plate with \na mixture of 1 volume of hydrochloric acid, 4 volumes of a \n0.25 per cent w/v solution of ferric chloride in ethanol \n(95 per cent) and 4 volumes of a 1.0 per cent w/v solution of \n1,10-phenanthroline hydrochloride in ethanol (95 percent). \nIn the chromatograms obtained with test solution (b) and \nreference solution (b) the spot of lower Rf value a-tocopheryl \nis orange.\nRefractive index (2.4,27). 1.494 to 1.498, determined at 20°.\nAcid value (2.3.23). Not more than 2.0, determined on 2.0 g.\nFree tocopherol. Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined by \nthe following method. Weigh 0.5 g, dissolve in 100 ml of\n0.25 M ethanolic sulphuric acid) add 20 ml of water and \n0.1 ml of a 0.25 per cent w/v solution of diphenylamine in \nsulphuric acid and titrate with 0.01 Mceric ammonium nitrate\nuntil a blue colour is produced that persists for ut least \n5 seconds. Repeat the operation without the substance under \nexamination, The difference between the titrations represents \nthe amount of ceric ammonium nitrate required.\n1 ml of 0.01 M ceric ammonium nitrate is equivalent to \n0.002154 g of tocopherol. ................... ■\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). .\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1. per cent.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.3 g in 25 ml of ethanol (95 per cent), add \n20 ml of 2.5 Methanolic sulphuric acid and heat on a water­\nbath under a reflux condenser for 3 hpurs. Cool, transfer the \nsolution quantitatively to a 200-ml volumetric flask, rinse the \napparatus with ethahól (95 per cent) and add the rinsings to \nthe flask. Make up to volume with ethanol (95 per cent) and \nmix. To 25.0 ml òf thè resulting Solution in a flask add 25 ml of \n0.25 M ethanolic sulphuric acid, 10 ml of water and titrate \nwith 0.01 M ceric ammonium nitrate using 0.1 ml of\n1 at least 5 seconds is obtained. Repeat the operation without\nthe substance under examination. The difference between the \ntitrations represents the amount of ceric ammonium nitrate \nrequired. \n.!x,'\n1;. ml of 0.01 M- ceric ammonium nitrate is: equivalent to \n0.002364 g of C3iH52O3. \nV /\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nTryptophan contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.5 per cent of CnHnNjOî, calculated on the dried \nbasis. \n'\nCategory. Amino acid \n' \n’\nDescription. A white to slightly yellowish-white crystals."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with L-!ryptophan \nIPRS or with'the reference spectrum of ¿-tryptophan.\nTests .. \n.\npH (2.4.24). 5.5 to 7.0, determined in 1 per Cent \\V/v solution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). -32.8° to -29.4°, determined \nin 1.0 per cent w/v solution in water, héàt gently to dissolve.\nRelated substances: Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n.. - - ..........\nTest ’solution. Dissolve 100 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml Of wafer.\nReference solution, (a), A solution containing 0.0001 per cent \nw/v, each of, dryptophan. related compound A IPRS and \ntryptophan related compound B IPRS in; water. ; ■ ;.\nRéférence solution (b). A 0.000T per cent w/v solution of \ntryptophan related compound B IPRS in water. ' ■'"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tofacitinib-citrate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tofacitinib Citrate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tofaci tinib Citrate Tablets; ; \n; a ■. q d\nTofacitinib Tablets contain not jess than 90.0 per cent and not\nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of tofacitinib, \njXXXXrXhm -XX\nUsual strength. 5 mg. .;x.:\nIdentification ; : \nr o / :\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test soluti o n cprresp onds to the p e ak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the' reference solution, .\nTests ■ : \nxxx- xx XXxx ■W; \nr.ja:\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket), \nMedium, 900ml,o