ect the test solution. The area, of any secondary peak is not \nmore than 0.5 per cent and the, sum of the areas; of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 1.0 per cent j calculated by \narea normalization.\nWater (2.4,43). Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined on\n10 mg by thermo gravimetric analysis.\nInstrument condition\n- temperature: 25° to 200° at the rate of 10° per\nminute,using nitrogen. \n...............\nDetermine weight loss of sample between 25° to 200°.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.T4),\nSolvent mixture. 72 volumes of methanol arid 28 volumes of\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg. of the substance.under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 50.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture. Dilute 10.0 ini of the solution to 20.0 ml \nwith the solvent mixture\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of tofluprost \nIPRS in the solvent mixture.. \n. \n. :... : ;... : \n.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 5 cm x 4.6 mm/packed with\noctadecylsilàne bonded to porous silica (3 pm), ox-'\n— mobile phase: A.water, ■ \n■\n■' B.methanol,. .7 7.\n- < a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\n. flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-x.Yib .x/.'.\":.-''■\n- spectrophotometer set at 219 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl. - \n. , \n:\n: Time\nMobile phase A' -\nMobile phase B\n(in min.) ;\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n28\n■ 4 ■■\n28 \n.\n- ;.i. g ::V \n20 \n•' p’’-- Acty \ny\n' n ' ’ \n20 \\ ?\n72 ■ ■■ \n. 28.; ■. \n.. ; 72- \n■\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless, th?\ncolumn efficiency is not less than 1000 theoretical plates, \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative stand#® \ndeviation forreplicate injections is notmore than 2.0 perce\n3822\nwww.webofpharma.com\nInject referencesolutionand the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C22H28F2O5.\nTolazafflWe\nMol. Wl. 311.4\nTolazamideis 1-pcrhydroazcpin-1 -yl-3-toIyl-/?-sulphonylurea.\nTolazamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C14H21N3O3S, calculated on the dried\nbasis. < \n■ \n■ -\ntitration.\nCategory.Hypogiycaemic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nIdentifitatidii 1\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6), \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tolazamide \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of tolazamide.\nB. When examined in the range 230 to 350 nm (2.4.7), a0.04 per \ncentw/vi solution methanol (95 per cent) exhibits maxima at\n256 nm, 263 nm and 275 nm and a shoulder at 268 nm. The \nabsorbances at the maxima are about 0.78, about 0.83 and \nabout 0.62 respectively."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4,17), coating the plate with silica, gel G.\nMobilephase.A mixture of200 volumes of chloroform, 100 \nvolumes ofmethanol, 60 volumes of cyclohexane and 23 \nvolumes of 13.5 M ammonia.\nTests '■ \n/\n^¡solution. Dissolve 1.0 g of the substance under \nexaminationin 50 ml of acetone.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\n^fsfencesohiti()n.f\\. 0,01 per cent w/v solution of toluene- \nPfulphonamide in acetone.\nth the plate 10 pl of each solution. After removal of the\na current of cold air, heat at 110° for 10 minutes, \nP the hot plate in a tank of chlorine gas prepared by the\n3 \nof hydrochloric acid to a 5 per cent w/v solution of\ncontained in a beaker placed in the\n4° stand for 2 minutes. Dry it in a current of \n^tilah area of the plate below the line of application \n$Ves at tnost a very faint blue colour with a 0.5 per cent w/v"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolazamide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolazamide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TOLAZAMIDE TABLETS\nsolution of potassium iodide in starch mucilage', avoid \nprolonged exposure to cold air. Spray the plate with a 0.5 per \ncent w/v solution of potassium iodide in starch mucilage. \nAny secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution is not more intense than the spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 60° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7kPa.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in 20 ml of ¿wian^-onewith the aid of \ngentle heat. Allow to cool, add 30 ml of ethanol '(95 per cent) \nand titrate with 0.1 M sodium .hydroxide using \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank\n1 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.03114 g of \nc14h21n3o3s.\nTolazamide Tablets\nTolazamide Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of tolazamide, \nCi4H21N3O3S.\nUsual strengths. 250 mg; 500 mg. .\nTriturate a quantity of the powdered tablets containing about \n0.25 g of Tolazamide with 50 ml of acetone and filter. Evaporate \nthe filtrate to dryness and dry the residue at 60° at, a pressure \nof 2 kPa for 3 hours. On the residue, determine by infrared \nabsorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum \nwith that obtained with tolazamide IPRS or with the reference \nspectrum of tolazamide.\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.05 MTris (hydroxymethyl)aminomethane \npH 7.6, adjusted to pH 7.6 hydrochloric acid,\nSpeed and time. 75 rpm and 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, dilute suitably if necessary with \nthe medium, at the maximum at about 224 nm (2.4.7). Calculate \nthe content of C14H2iN3O3S in the medium from the absorbance \nobtained from a solution of known concentration of tolazamide \nIPRS in the dissolution medium.\n3823\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOLAZAMIDE TABLETS\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nCÌ4H21N3O3S; cay ■; \n:'\nRelatedsubstances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2 .’4.17), coating the plate with silica gèl G.\nMobile phase. Amixture of 23. volumes of 13.5 M ammonia, \n60 volumes of cyclohexane , 100 volumes of methanol and \n200 volumes of chloroform. :\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining about 0.2 g of Tolazamide with 10 ml Of acetone \nandfilter. .-r. ■■■ \n; c.o \n,,y\nReference solution. \\ 0.01 per cent w/v solution of toluene- \np-sulphonamide in acetone.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in a current of cold air, heat at 1109 for 10 minutes, \nplace the hot plate in a tank of chlorine gas, prepared by the \naddition of hydrochloric acid to a 5 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium permanganate contained in a beaker placed in the\ntank and allow to stand for 2 minutes. Dry the plate in a current \nof cold air until an area of the plate below the line of application \ngives at most a very faint blue colour with a 0.5 per cent w/v \nsolution of potassium iodide in starch mucilage avoid \nprolonged exposure to cold air. Spray the plate with a 0.5 per \ncent w/v solution of potassium iodide - in starch mucilage. \nAny secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution is not more intense than the, spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (0.5.per \ncent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under’Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Shake a quantity of the \npowdered tablets containing about 0.5 g of Tolazamide with \n50 ml of chloroform, filter, wash the residue with chloroform \nand evaporate the combined fíltrate and washings to dryness. \nDissòlve the residue in 20 ml Of butan-2-one with the aid of \ngentle heat. Allow to cool, acid 30 ml of ethanol (95per cent) \narid t"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TOLAZAMIDE TABLETS\nsolution of potassium iodide in starch mucilage', avoid \nprolonged exposure to cold air. Spray the plate with a 0.5 per \ncent w/v solution of potassium iodide in starch mucilage. \nAny secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution is not more intense than the spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 60° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7kPa.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in 20 ml of ¿wian^-onewith the aid of \ngentle heat. Allow to cool, add 30 ml of ethanol '(95 per cent) \nand titrate with 0.1 M sodium .hydroxide using \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank\n1 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.03114 g of \nc14h21n3o3s.\nTolazamide Tablets\nTolazamide Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of tolazamide, \nCi4H21N3O3S.\nUsual strengths. 250 mg; 500 mg. .\nTriturate a quantity of the powdered tablets containing about \n0.25 g of Tolazamide with 50 ml of acetone and filter. Evaporate \nthe filtrate to dryness and dry the residue at 60° at, a pressure \nof 2 kPa for 3 hours. On the residue, determine by infrared \nabsorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum \nwith that obtained with tolazamide IPRS or with the reference \nspectrum of tolazamide.\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.05 MTris (hydroxymethyl)aminomethane \npH 7.6, adjusted to pH 7.6 hydrochloric acid,\nSpeed and time. 75 rpm and 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, dilute suitably if necessary with \nthe medium, at the maximum at about 224 nm (2.4.7). Calculate \nthe content of C14H2iN3O3S in the medium from the absorbance \nobtained from a solution of known concentration of tolazamide \nIPRS in the dissolution medium.\n3823\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOLAZAMIDE TABLETS\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nCÌ4H21N3O3S; cay ■; \n:'\nRelatedsubstances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2 .’4.17), coating the plate with silica gèl G.\nMobile phase. Amixture of 23. volumes of 13.5 M ammonia, \n60 volumes of cyclohexane , 100 volumes of methanol and \n200 volumes of chloroform. :\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining about 0.2 g of Tolazamide with 10 ml Of acetone \nandfilter. .-r. ■■■ \n; c.o \n,,y\nReference solution. \\ 0.01 per cent w/v solution of toluene- \np-sulphonamide in acetone.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in a current of cold air, heat at 1109 for 10 minutes, \nplace the hot plate in a tank of chlorine gas, prepared by the \naddition of hydrochloric acid to a 5 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium permanganate contained in a beaker placed in the\ntank and allow to stand for 2 minutes. Dry the plate in a current \nof cold air until an area of the plate below the line of application \ngives at most a very faint blue colour with a 0.5 per cent w/v \nsolution of potassium iodide in starch mucilage avoid \nprolonged exposure to cold air. Spray the plate with a 0.5 per \ncent w/v solution of potassium iodide - in starch mucilage. \nAny secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution is not more intense than the, spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (0.5.per \ncent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under’Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Shake a quantity of the \npowdered tablets containing about 0.5 g of Tolazamide with \n50 ml of chloroform, filter, wash the residue with chloroform \nand evaporate the combined fíltrate and washings to dryness. \nDissòlve the residue in 20 ml Of butan-2-one with the aid of \ngentle heat. Allow to cool, acid 30 ml of ethanol (95per cent) \narid titrate the resulting solution with 0.1Msodium hydroxide, \nusiw¿ phenolphthalein solution as indicator. Cany out a blank \ntitration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.03114 gof \nC(4H21N3O3S.\n...................................................................... lp2022\nTolbutamide is 3-butyl-l-[(4-methylphenyl)sulphonyl]urei\nTolbutamide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and nót niore \nthan 101.0 per cent of Ci2H18N2O3S, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Hypoglycaemiq, \n: ? : ■■ \n;y.\nDescription. Awhite, crystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may be omitted if tests B, C and D are carried out. Tests \nB, C and D may be omitted if test A is carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4,6) \nCorripafé the spectrum with that obtained with tolbutamide \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of tolbutamide.\nB. Dissolve; 25 mg in sufficient methanol to, produce 100.0 ml.\nWhen examined in the range 245 nm to 360 nm (2.47), the \nresulting solution shows absorption maxima at about 258 nm, \n263 nm and 275 nm and a shoulder at about 268 nm. Dilute the \nsolution with methanol to produce a 0.001 per cent w/y \nsolution. When examined in the range 220’ rirri to 235 uni, thè \nresulting solution shows an absorption maximum at about \n228 nm; absorbance at about 228 nm, about 0.50.\nC. Boil 0. Tg with 8 ml of a 50 per cent v/v solution of sulphuric \nac/7 under a reflux condenser for 30 minutes. Make the solution \nstrongly alkaline with sodiiim hydròxidé solution and steam \ndistiLfor 30 riiiriutes, receiving the distillate in20 mlof 0:.l M \nhydrochloric acid. To 1 ml of the sòlutiom containing the \ndistillate add 0.1 g of sodium acetate and 10 ini of buffer \nsolution pH 9.4. Cool in an ice-bath for 10 minutes, add imi of \ndiazotised nitroaniline solution, set aside for 20minutes and \nadd drop wise 1 ml of sodium hydroxide solution', anorange- \nred colour is produced.\nD. Boil 0.1 g with 8 ml of a 50 per Cent v/v solution of sulphuric \nacid under a reflux condenser for 30 minutes. Cool in an ice­\nbath; a crystalline, precipitate of 4-toluenesulphonylamide\\s \nformed, which after recrystallisation from hot rwer and drying \nat 105° melts at 135° to 140° (2.4.21). \n; . . ; .\nTests ; \n.\nAppearance of solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution in 1M \nsodium hydroxide is clear (2.4.1), arid colourless (2.4.1).\npH (2.4.24). 4.5 to 5.5, determined in a solution prepared by \ndissolving 2:0 g in 50 ml of carbon dioxide-free' waterby\nKeating at 70° for 5 rriinutes, cooling rapidly and filtering-\nNon-sulphonyl urea. Dissolve 0.5 g in 1, ml ofdilute \nsolution and 9 ml of water, not more than a faintppal^?611^\nis produced, j \n.... . ;-.f\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatograp^'\n(2.4.14). ex; coo- ■■■oc oc\nJP 2Q22;\ny}0TE-7rPrePare so^ons immediaiely before use,\n¿¿jjttfort. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under\nmobile phase and dilute to 50.0 ml with the\naobilephase.\nferefjce solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100 0 nJ with the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to \n10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nff ference solution (b). A solution containing 0.001 per cent \ntf/v each of tolbutamide impurity A and tolbutamide impurity\nB in the mobile pbase.\nChromatographic system\n_ .a stainless.steel column .25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nendcapped octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica\n(5pm), \nr\n_ mobile phase: a mixture of 35 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 65 volumes of a 0.14 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium dihydrogen phosphate, adjusted to pH 3.5\nwith orthophosphoric acid,\n- flowrate:’1.5 ml perminute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 23 O' nm,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"; ’ injection volume:.20 pl.\n'' \nRelative '\nretention time\nTolbutamide impurity B1 \n0.2\nTolbutamide impurity A2 \n0.3\nTolbutamide (Retention time: about 18 minutes); 1.0\n'toluenesulphonamide,' 4 •\n:toluenesulphonylùrea.\nFor identification of impurities, use the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference so I ut i on (b) to i denti fy the p eak due to \ntolbutamide impurities A and B. . ;\nInject reference solution (b). The test is hot valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to tolbutamide impuritites \nA and B is nói less than 2.0.; : \n’"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Inject reference solution (a) and the test solution. Run the \ncl)roinatpgràm j .5 times thè retention time of the principal\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \nthe area óf any secondary peak is riot more than the area of\nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference\ns°Iurion(a)(0.1percent) and the sum of areas of all the\nsecondary peaks is not more than 3 times the area of the \nPnncjpal peak iri the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.3 per cent), ignore any peak with an area less \n^0.5 tirnes the area of the principal peak in thè chromatogram \no tainedwith reference solution (a) (0.Ó5 per cent).\n®»vy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \n\"^Wetals, Method B (20 ppm). ; \n'\n$°lphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolazamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolazamide"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolazamide\nN\nH \nH C\nC 4112 IN 303S \t\nMol. Wt. 311.4\nTolazamide is 1-perhydroazepin-l-y1-3-tolyl-p-sulphonylurea.\nTolazamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C 14112, N 303S, calculatc5ion the.dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Hypoglycaemic.\nrP 2018 \njp 20 18"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tolazamide\nN\nH \nH C\nC 4112 IN 303S \t\nMol. Wt. 311.4\nTolazamide is 1-perhydroazepin-l-y1-3-tolyl-p-sulphonylurea.\nTolazamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C 14112, N 303S, calculatc5ion the.dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Hypoglycaemic.\nrP 2018 \njp 20 18"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolbutamide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolbutamide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Loss on drying (2.4.19).Not more, than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in a mixture of 40ml ci ethanol (95 per \ncent) and 20 ml of water. Titrate with 0.1 Msodium hydroxide \nusing phenolphthalein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank \ntitration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.02704 g of \nC12H18N2O3S.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTolbutamide Tablets\nTolbutamide Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more, than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntolbutamide, C12Hi8N2O3S.\nUsualstrength. 500 mg.\nExtract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 1 g of \nTolbutamide with 1Ó ml of chloroform, filter, evaporate the \nfiltrate to dryness, scratching the sides of the vessel, if \nnecessary, to induce crystallisation, and dry the residue at\n105° for 30 minutes. The residue complies with the following \ntests.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4;6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tolbutamide \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of tolbutamide.\nB. Boil 0.1 g of residue with 8 ml of a 50per centv/v solution \nof sulphuric acid under a- reflux condenser for 30 minutes. \nCool in an, ice-bath; a crystalline precipitate of,4- \ntoluenesulphonylatnide is formed, which after reciystallisation \nfrom hpt water and drying at 105° melts at 135° to 140°.\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of a solution containing 2.04 per cent w/v of \ndisodium hydrogen phosphate and 0.135 per cent w/v of \npotassium dihydrogen phosphate,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate suitably diluted if necessary, at \nthe maximurn at about 228 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of \nCi2Hi8N2O3S in the medium taking 417 as the specific \nabsorbance at 228 mri.\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nC12H18N2O3S."},"sections":[{"name":"TOLBUTAMIDE TABLETS","text":"Loss on drying (2.4.19).Not more, than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in a mixture of 40ml ci ethanol (95 per \ncent) and 20 ml of water. Titrate with 0.1 Msodium hydroxide \nusing phenolphthalein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank \ntitration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.02704 g of \nC12H18N2O3S.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTolbutamide Tablets\nTolbutamide Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more, than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntolbutamide, C12Hi8N2O3S.\nUsualstrength. 500 mg.\nExtract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 1 g of \nTolbutamide with 1Ó ml of chloroform, filter, evaporate the \nfiltrate to dryness, scratching the sides of the vessel, if \nnecessary, to induce crystallisation, and dry the residue at\n105° for 30 minutes. The residue complies with the following \ntests.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4;6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tolbutamide \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of tolbutamide.\nB. Boil 0.1 g of residue with 8 ml of a 50per centv/v solution \nof sulphuric acid under a- reflux condenser for 30 minutes. \nCool in an, ice-bath; a crystalline precipitate of,4- \ntoluenesulphonylatnide is formed, which after reciystallisation \nfrom hpt water and drying at 105° melts at 135° to 140°.\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of a solution containing 2.04 per cent w/v of \ndisodium hydrogen phosphate and 0.135 per cent w/v of \npotassium dihydrogen phosphate,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate suitably diluted if necessary, at \nthe maximurn at about 228 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of \nCi2Hi8N2O3S in the medium taking 417 as the specific \nabsorbance at 228 mri.\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nC12H18N2O3S."},{"name":"TOLBUTAMIDE TABLETS","text":"Related sùbstances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel G.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 90. volumes of chloroform, \n8 volumes of. methanol and 2 volumes of anhydrous formic \nacid.' ... \níi \n\\\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining ,0.5 g of Tolbutamide with 10 ml of acetone and \nfilter.\nReference solution (a). A 0.015.per cent, w/v solution of\n4-toluenesulphonamide in acetone.\nReference solution (b). A mixture of equal volumes of the test\nsolution and reference solution (a). . ,. .\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each of test solution and reference \nsolution (a) and 10 pl of reference solution (b). After \ndevelopment, dry the plate in a current of warm air and heat at\n110° for 10 minutes. While still hot,1 place the plate in a \nchromatographic tank with an evaporating dish containing a \n5 per cent w/v solution of potassium permanganate, add an \nequal volume of hydrochloric acid and close the tank. Leave \nthe plate in the tank for 2 minutes, then place it in a current of \ncold air until the excess of chlorine is removed and an area of \ncoating below thé line of application gives only a very faint \nblue colour with potassium iodide and starch solution', avoid \nprolonged exposure to co Id air. Spray the plate with potassium,\niodide and starch solution and allow to stand for 5 minutes. \nAny secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with thé \ntest solution is : not more intense: tham the spot iníthé \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). The \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution-(b) shows twb \nclearly separated spots. .. .\nOther tests. Comply with.the tests stated under Tablets., ■ • ; \nAssay. Wei¿ñ arid powder 20tablets. Weigh a quantity of the\npowder containing about 0.5 g of Tolbutamide, add 50 ml of \nethanol- (95 per cent), previously neutralised to \nphenolphthalein solution, warm to dissolve, add 25 ml of\nwater and titrate with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide using \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator. — \n;\n1 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.02704 g of \nCI2HI8N2O3S. . • \n. , \n..\nStorage. Store protected from moisture. \nr"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolbutamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolbutamide"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TOLBUTAMIDE \nTOLNAFTATE\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless th e\nphenolphthalein solution, warm to dissolve, add 25 ml of"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TOLBUTAMIDE \nTOLNAFTATE\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless th e\nphenolphthalein solution, warm to dissolve, add 25 ml of"},{"name":"Tests","text":"resolution betwen the peaks due to tolbutamide impuriri\ntes\nwater and titrate with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide using \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator.\n A and B is not les than 2.0.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 1.5 times the retention time of the principal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \nthe area of any secondary peak is not more than the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.1 per cent). The sum of areas of all the seconda ry\n1 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.02704 g of \nCl2H181•1203S.\na solution containing 2.04 per cent w/v of \ne) n phosphate and 0.135 per cent w/v of\nMADPi esPds:iurlaunittui :s9hn0Ny:25\ndomr: g2,°\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\ndihydrogen phosphate,\nPsdpi:el:edsdsilan\n;unind time. 50 rpm and 30 minutes.\ni\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate suitably diluted if necessary, at"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolnaftate-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolnaftate Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TOLNAFTATE CREAM\n¡p 2022\njpectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \ninjectionvolume: 100 pl.\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n; Time\n; • (per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\nX\n(in min)\n■ 70'\n30\n70\n30\nSKZ\n■... o\n.100\n20\n0\n100\nThe relative, retention time with reference to tolnaftate \n(retention time: about 15 minutes) for tolnaftate impurity D is\nabout 0,25.\ninject iei^reri.ce solution (g); The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to tolnaftate impurity D and \ntolnaftate is not less than 5.0 and the tailing factor for the peak\ndue to tolnaftate impurity D is not more than 1.9.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solutiuon. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to tolnaftate impurity D is not more than\n¡he area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained\nwith reference solution (b) (20 ppm).\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(24.14).; - \n■ ■' ■ \n■ \n■. .\nTestsoiutidn. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexaminatibn in. 5!-ml of methanol and dilute to 25.0 ml with\nme/AanoZ.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n10.0 ml wifamethanol.\nReference:solujion:(b), Dilute 1.0ml of reference solution (a) \ntoXOO.Oxaiwhh methanol: \n■ \n. .\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 0.1 volume of triflUoro-\n■ \nacetic acid, 30 volumes of water and 70 volumes of \nmethanol,\nB. a mixture of 0.1 volume of trifluoro­\nacetic acid, 10 volumes of water and 90 volumes of \nmethanol, : ; \n...\n\" a gradient programme using the conditions given below,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TOLNAFTATE CREAM\n¡p 2022\njpectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \ninjectionvolume: 100 pl.\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n; Time\n; • (per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\nX\n(in min)\n■ 70'\n30\n70\n30\nSKZ\n■... o\n.100\n20\n0\n100\nThe relative, retention time with reference to tolnaftate \n(retention time: about 15 minutes) for tolnaftate impurity D is\nabout 0,25.\ninject iei^reri.ce solution (g); The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to tolnaftate impurity D and \ntolnaftate is not less than 5.0 and the tailing factor for the peak\ndue to tolnaftate impurity D is not more than 1.9.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solutiuon. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to tolnaftate impurity D is not more than\n¡he area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained\nwith reference solution (b) (20 ppm).\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(24.14).; - \n■ ■' ■ \n■ \n■. .\nTestsoiutidn. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexaminatibn in. 5!-ml of methanol and dilute to 25.0 ml with\nme/AanoZ.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n10.0 ml wifamethanol.\nReference:solujion:(b), Dilute 1.0ml of reference solution (a) \ntoXOO.Oxaiwhh methanol: \n■ \n. .\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 0.1 volume of triflUoro-\n■ \nacetic acid, 30 volumes of water and 70 volumes of \nmethanol,\nB. a mixture of 0.1 volume of trifluoro­\nacetic acid, 10 volumes of water and 90 volumes of \nmethanol, : ; \n...\n\" a gradient programme using the conditions given below,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"■ ~ sPectrophotomcier set at 254 nm, \n7injectionvolume: 10 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(inmin);\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v).\n100\n. \n0 \n..\n- 100 -\no\n. 0\n.100. .\n0 \n. :\n,100.\n100\n0 .\nThe relative retention time with reference to tolnaftate \n(retention time is about 18 minutes) for tolnaftate impurity Ais \nabout 0.7.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with thé test solution; the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) \n(0.1 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than twice the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 \nper Cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 times the\narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.05 per cent).\nSulphated ash (2.3. 18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19).Notmore than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in vacuum at 60° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa for 3 hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 50 mg in 250 ml of methanol. Dilute 2 ml pf the \nsolution to 50 ml with methanol. Measure the absorbance at \nthe maximum at 257 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of CÎ9HÎ7NOS taking 720 as the specific \nabsorbance at 257 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nTolnaftate Cream\nTolnaftate Cream contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nriibre than 110.0 per centof the stated amount of tolnaftate, \nCi9H17NOS. ;\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/w.\nDetermine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17)f coating the \nplate with silica gel GF254. \n• . • • • \n;\nMobile phase. Toluene. \n. I.....\nTest solution. Evaporate 10 ml of the Solution Aon steam bath \nto dryness and dissolve the residue in 1.0 ml of ethanol (95 \nper cent).\nReference solution. K 0.1 per cent w/v solution of to Inaflate \n1PRS in ethanol (95per cent),. ...:. \n.--.v ; \n;\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air;’and examine under \nultraviolet light at 25.4 nm. The’principal spot in the\n3827\nwww.webofpharma.com\nchromatogram obtained, withthe test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained;with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other tests. Comply with the tests stated under Creams..\nAssay. Transfer a weighed quantity of the cream containing \nabout 10 mg ofTolnaftate to a separator1 containing 75 ml of \nchloroform. Wash the chloroform solution successively ;with \ntwo 25 ml portions of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid and 25 , ml of \nwater,.¿Filter the chloroform layer through a. chloro form­\nwashed cotton pledget intoa 100-ml volumetric flask. Add \nchloroform tovokime and mix (Solution A). Dilute 5.0 ml of \nthe solution with chloroform to, 50 ml and mix. Measure the \nabsorbance of thè resulting solution at the maximumat about \n25.8 nm (2.4.7), using 0.001 per cent w/v solution of tolnaftate \nIPRS\\n chloroform. \n:\nCalculate the content of Ci9Hi7NOS:in the cream. > \n: ,\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolnaftate-gel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolnaftate Gel"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolnaftate Gel\nTolnaftate Gel contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of tolnaftate, \nCI9HI7NOS: \n; \n'\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/w."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tolnaftate Gel\nTolnaftate Gel contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of tolnaftate, \nCI9HI7NOS: \n; \n'\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/w."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating the \nplate with silica