 \nand finally brittle. Transparent in thin films; odour, aromatic \nand vanilla-like. Warmed and pressed between pieces of glass \nand examined with a lens, it exhibits crystals of cinnamic acid."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tolu Balsam\nBalsam of Tolu\nTolu Balsam is a solid or semi-solid, balsam obtained by incision\nfrom the trunk of Myroxylon balsamum (Linn.) Harms \n(Fam. Leguminosae).\nTolu Balsam contains not less than 35.0 per cent and not more \nthan 50.0 per cent of total balsamic acids, calculated as \ncinnamic acid, C911802, on the dry, ethanol-soluble matter.\nCategory. Expectorant, antiseptic.\nDescription. A soft, tenacious, brownish yellow or brown \nmass, when first collected; subsequently, becoming harder \nand finally brittle. Transparent in thin films; odour, aromatic \nand vanilla-like. Warmed and pressed between pieces of glass \nand examined with a lens, it exhibits crystals of cinnamic acid."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. To a solution in ethanol (90 per cent) add ferric chloride \ntest solution; a green colour is produced.\nB.To 1 g add to 5 ml of water, heat to boiling, filter, add 30 mg \nof potassium permanganate and continue heating; the odour \nof benzaldehyde is produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Acidity. A solution in ethanol (90 per cent) is acidic to litmus \nsolution.\nTOLU BALSAM \nTULASI \n0,208 \nIP 2018\nAcid value (2.3.23). 97 to 160, calculated on the dry, ethanol-\nsoluble matter, determined by the following method. Dissolve \n5.0 g in 50 ml of boiling ethanol (90 per cent), add 3 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution and titrate the hot solution with\nwith 0.1 M sodium hydroxide using phenol red solution as\nis formed. To the other half of the liquid, add a few drops of \nbarium chloride solution; no precipitate is formed (freedom \nfrom agar).\nindicator.\nI fni of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01482 g of\ntotal balsamic acids, calculated as cinnamic acid, C 9H802."},{"name":"Tests","text":"1 M ethanolic potassium hydroxide. When the colour \nbecomes dark brown, attach to a reflux condenser, boil for a\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. Avoid\nAcacia and other soluble gums. To 20 ml of a 2.5 per cent w/v \nsuspension of the powdered material prepared with freshly \nboiled water add 10 ml of lead acetate solution; a flocculent \nprecipitate is formed. Filter and add to the filtrate 10 ml of lead \nsubacetate solution; a slight cloudiness may appear, but there\nfew minutes to break up the precipitate and complete the \ntitration.\nexposure to excessive heat.\nEthanol-insoluble matter. Not more than 5 per cent, determi ned \nby the following method. Digest 2.5 g with 50 ml of ethanol\n(90 per cent), filter through a sintered glass crucible, transfer \nthe residue to the filter crucible with the aid of more ethanol \n(90 per cent), wash with hot ethanol (90 per cent) until all \nsoluble matter is removed and dry to constant weight at 100°"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/toluene-anhydrous-toluene-which-has-been-dried-over","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Toluene, Anhydrous: Toluene which has been dried over"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Toluene, Anhydrous: Toluene which has been dried over\n·······----Acid-Sodium-Salt;-'Thoronal~G16Hl0AsNzNa3Ql0SZ(approx)·--.Toluene, Prepared:Toluene preparedby\"fuSt shakingtoluene··--·······\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nwith a small quantity of water, separating the excess water\nThrombin: Driedhuman thrombin obtainedfromliquidplasma.\nand distilling the toluene.\nIt may be prepared by precipitation with suitable salts and\nToluene-o-sulphonamide; 2-Methylbenzene-\norganic solvents under controlled conditions of pH, ionic\nsulphonarnide;Toluene-2-sulphonarnide: C7H9NOzS = 171.21\nstrength and temperature.\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nmp, about 156°.\nYellowish white powder.\nToluene-4-sulphonamide; Toluene-p-sulphonamide;\nStore in sealed, sterile light-resistantcontainers under nitrogen,\n4- Methylbenzenesulphonamide: C7~OZS= 171.21\nat a temperature below 25°.\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Toluene, Anhydrous: Toluene which has been dried over\n·······----Acid-Sodium-Salt;-'Thoronal~G16Hl0AsNzNa3Ql0SZ(approx)·--.Toluene, Prepared:Toluene preparedby\"fuSt shakingtoluene··--·······\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nwith a small quantity of water, separating the excess water\nThrombin: Driedhuman thrombin obtainedfromliquidplasma.\nand distilling the toluene.\nIt may be prepared by precipitation with suitable salts and\nToluene-o-sulphonamide; 2-Methylbenzene-\norganic solvents under controlled conditions of pH, ionic\nsulphonarnide;Toluene-2-sulphonarnide: C7H9NOzS = 171.21\nstrength and temperature.\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nmp, about 156°.\nYellowish white powder.\nToluene-4-sulphonamide; Toluene-p-sulphonamide;\nStore in sealed, sterile light-resistantcontainers under nitrogen,\n4- Methylbenzenesulphonamide: C7~OZS= 171.21\nat a temperature below 25°.\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/toluene-p-sulphonamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Toluene-p-sulphonamide"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"; Toluene-p-sulphonamide; \n4- Methylbenzenesulphonamide: C 7119NO2S = 171.21\nTHE! u o roacetic acid: C 2HF302 = 114.0\nContent. Minium 99 per cent of C 2 1-1F302.\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nTricine; N-[2-Hydroxy-1,1-bis(hydroxymethypethyl]glycine: \nC6H13N0s = 179.2.\nLiquid, miscible with acetone and with alcohol; wt. per ml, \nabout 1.53g; bp, about 72°.\nmp, about 136°.\nComplies with the following test.\nUse electrophoresis-grade reagent; mp, about 183\"\nUse a grade suitable for protein sequencing.\nHOMOGENEITY - Carry out the test for Related substances \ndescribed in the monograph for Tolbutamide applying to the \nplate 5 1.11 of a 0.015 per cent w/v solution in acetone. The\nTrichloroacetic Acid: CI 3COOH = 163.40\nStore protected from moisture.\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nTrifluoroacetic Anhydride: C 4F601 = 210.0\nchromatogram shows only one spot.\nColourless, very deliquescent crystals or crystalline masses; \nodour, slight or pungent and characteristic; mp, about 56°.\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"; Toluene-p-sulphonamide; \n4- Methylbenzenesulphonamide: C 7119NO2S = 171.21\nTHE! u o roacetic acid: C 2HF302 = 114.0\nContent. Minium 99 per cent of C 2 1-1F302.\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nTricine; N-[2-Hydroxy-1,1-bis(hydroxymethypethyl]glycine: \nC6H13N0s = 179.2.\nLiquid, miscible with acetone and with alcohol; wt. per ml, \nabout 1.53g; bp, about 72°.\nmp, about 136°.\nComplies with the following test.\nUse electrophoresis-grade reagent; mp, about 183\"\nUse a grade suitable for protein sequencing.\nHOMOGENEITY - Carry out the test for Related substances \ndescribed in the monograph for Tolbutamide applying to the \nplate 5 1.11 of a 0.015 per cent w/v solution in acetone. The\nTrichloroacetic Acid: CI 3COOH = 163.40\nStore protected from moisture.\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nTrifluoroacetic Anhydride: C 4F601 = 210.0\nchromatogram shows only one spot.\nColourless, very deliquescent crystals or crystalline masses; \nodour, slight or pungent and characteristic; mp, about 56°.\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/toluene-p-sulphonic-acid-toluenesulphonic-acid-4","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Toluene- p-sulphonic Acid; Toluenesulphonic acid; 4-"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Toluene- p-sulphonic Acid; Toluenesulphonic acid; 4- \nmethylbenzenesulphonic acid: C7HKO3S,H2O = 190.2\nGeneral reagent grade of commerce..\nNot less than 87.0 per cent of C7H8O3S.\nA white, crystalline powder or crystals.\nTosylarginine Methyl Ester Hydrochloride; Methyl A-tosy- \nL-argininate hydrochloride: Ci4H22N2O4S,H2Cj=378.9\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce suitable for the assay, \nof trypsin.\nA white, crystalline solid; mp, about 145°.\nTosylphenylanychloromethane; A/-Tosy-L-phenyanyl- \nchloromethane; L-tosylaminophenethylchloromethyl\nketone: C17H18C1NO3S = 351.9 \n■\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nA white, crystalline solid; mp, about 105°.\nTriacetin; Glycerol Triacetate: .\nCH2(COOCH3)CH(COOCH3)CH2(COOCH3)=218.20\nClear, colourless orpale straw-coloured liquid; wt. per ml, about\n1.156 g; contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more than\n99.0 per cent w/w of C9Hi4O6' \n'\nassay — Carry out the method for the determination of esters\n(2.3.25). \n' '■ \n• \n•\n1 ml of 0.5 M ethanolic potassium hydroxide is equivalent to\n0.03637 g of C9H14O6.\nTriazolam; Chlorazam; 8-Chloro-6-(2-chlorophenyl)-l-methyl- \n4H-[l,2,4]triazolo[4,3-a][l,4] benzodiazepine: C]7Hi2C12N4!= \n34322\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nTan Crystals; mp, about 160°.\nTributylphosphine: C12H27P=202.3\nClear, colourless liquid.\nbp, about 240°..\nmp, about-60°.\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS\nIP 2022"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Toluene- p-sulphonic Acid; Toluenesulphonic acid; 4- \nmethylbenzenesulphonic acid: C7HKO3S,H2O = 190.2\nGeneral reagent grade of commerce..\nNot less than 87.0 per cent of C7H8O3S.\nA white, crystalline powder or crystals.\nTosylarginine Methyl Ester Hydrochloride; Methyl A-tosy- \nL-argininate hydrochloride: Ci4H22N2O4S,H2Cj=378.9\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce suitable for the assay, \nof trypsin.\nA white, crystalline solid; mp, about 145°.\nTosylphenylanychloromethane; A/-Tosy-L-phenyanyl- \nchloromethane; L-tosylaminophenethylchloromethyl\nketone: C17H18C1NO3S = 351.9 \n■\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nA white, crystalline solid; mp, about 105°.\nTriacetin; Glycerol Triacetate: .\nCH2(COOCH3)CH(COOCH3)CH2(COOCH3)=218.20\nClear, colourless orpale straw-coloured liquid; wt. per ml, about\n1.156 g; contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more than\n99.0 per cent w/w of C9Hi4O6' \n'\nassay — Carry out the method for the determination of esters\n(2.3.25). \n' '■ \n• \n•\n1 ml of 0.5 M ethanolic potassium hydroxide is equivalent to\n0.03637 g of C9H14O6.\nTriazolam; Chlorazam; 8-Chloro-6-(2-chlorophenyl)-l-methyl- \n4H-[l,2,4]triazolo[4,3-a][l,4] benzodiazepine: C]7Hi2C12N4!= \n34322\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nTan Crystals; mp, about 160°.\nTributylphosphine: C12H27P=202.3\nClear, colourless liquid.\nbp, about 240°..\nmp, about-60°.\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS\nIP 2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/toluene","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Toluene"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Toluene\nMethylbenzene\nl[ J\nClass 2\n1,1,1 -Trichloroethane\nMethylchloroform\nCH3CC13\nClass 1\n1,1,2-Trichloroethene\nTrichloroethene\nhciocci2\nClass 2\nTriethylamine\nA.jV-Diethylethanamine\nN(CH2CH3)3\nClass 3\nXylene\n*\nDimethylbenzene\nXylol\n* Usually 60 per cent m-xylene. 40 per cent p-xylene. 9 per cent of o- xylene with 17 per cent ethylbenzene.\n5.5. Impurities\nThis chapter provides guidance on the control of impurities in \ndrug substances and formulated preparations described in IP \nmonographs. It applies mainly to totally synthetic organic \nmedicinal substances and those substances obtained by \nsynthetic modification of a naturally produced precursor. It is \nnot necessarily relevant to other, organic substances e.g., \nherbal products and crude products of plant or animal origin, \nbiological and biotechnology products, oligonucleotides, \nradiopharmaceuticals, fermentation products and semi­\nsynthetic products derived there from; inorganic substances\nand pharmaceutical excipients. Although the thresholds stated \nin this general chapter do not apply, the general concepts of \nreporting, identification (wherever possible) and qualification \nof impurities are equally valid for these classes. It provides an\napproach to the setting of limits for impurities in articles for\nClass' 2\nv°\nClass 2 .,\nClass'!\nwhich the individual monographs do not provide either a test \nor specific limits.\nImpurities are critical quality attributes of drug substances \nand drug products because they have the potential to affect \nthe.safety and efficacy ofthe product. The monographs of\nthe Pharmacopoeia have been designed to ensure the minimum \nacceptable quality of drug substances and drug products for \nusers. Tests for related substances have also been provided \nin many monographs to limit impurities and degradation \nproducts. Although one of the primary objectives of the \nPharmacopoeia; is to guarantee the identity, strength, purity \nand quality of official articles, it is not possible to include in\neach monograph a test for every impurity or contaminant or \neven an adulterant that might be present. The exclusion of a \nlimit for impurities in a monograph does not absolve the \nmanufacturer of providing assurance to the user on the safety\n1174\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022 \n5.5. IMPURITIES\nof a drug. It is incumbent on the manufacturer to follow Good \nManufacturing Practices (GMP) and to ensure the limitation \nof impurities based on knowledge of the properties of the \nchemical entity and the likelihood of related substances being \nassociated with the end product during production and\nsubsequent storage.\nImpurity measurements for drug products using \nchromatographic methods could present a challenge to the \nPharmacopoeial standards setting due to low concentrations \nof the impurity and complexity of matrix. As a consequence,\nsome ofthe monographs for Pharmacopoeial preparations rely\non chromatographic assays. Where more significant impurities\nare known, some monographs set forth specific tests for these \nimpurities.\nIn general, the tests in a monograph are tests for purity that \nprovide information on the extent of known potential or actual \nimpurities rather than for guaranteeing freedom from all\npossible impurities. The tests are not necessarily designed to\ndetect any foreign contaminants or adulteration. Material \nfound to contain an impurity not detectable by the prescribed \ntests of a monograph may be deemed to be not of\nPharmacopoeial quality particularly if the nature ofthe impurity \n(ies) found is not compatible with GMP.\nChemical tests that limiting the levels of particular impurities \nor classes of impurities are often augmented by physical\ntests such as specific optical rotation, absorbance, melting \npoint, clarity and colour of solution and for liquids, refractive \nindex, boiling point range and weight per ml etc. Besides, non­\nspecific tests such as sulphated ash, loss on drying etc. \ncontribute to the assurance of the general quality of the article\nand"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Toluene\nMethylbenzene\nl[ J\nClass 2\n1,1,1 -Trichloroethane\nMethylchloroform\nCH3CC13\nClass 1\n1,1,2-Trichloroethene\nTrichloroethene\nhciocci2\nClass 2\nTriethylamine\nA.jV-Diethylethanamine\nN(CH2CH3)3\nClass 3\nXylene\n*\nDimethylbenzene\nXylol\n* Usually 60 per cent m-xylene. 40 per cent p-xylene. 9 per cent of o- xylene with 17 per cent ethylbenzene.\n5.5. Impurities\nThis chapter provides guidance on the control of impurities in \ndrug substances and formulated preparations described in IP \nmonographs. It applies mainly to totally synthetic organic \nmedicinal substances and those substances obtained by \nsynthetic modification of a naturally produced precursor. It is \nnot necessarily relevant to other, organic substances e.g., \nherbal products and crude products of plant or animal origin, \nbiological and biotechnology products, oligonucleotides, \nradiopharmaceuticals, fermentation products and semi­\nsynthetic products derived there from; inorganic substances\nand pharmaceutical excipients. Although the thresholds stated \nin this general chapter do not apply, the general concepts of \nreporting, identification (wherever possible) and qualification \nof impurities are equally valid for these classes. It provides an\napproach to the setting of limits for impurities in articles for\nClass' 2\nv°\nClass 2 .,\nClass'!\nwhich the individual monographs do not provide either a test \nor specific limits.\nImpurities are critical quality attributes of drug substances \nand drug products because they have the potential to affect \nthe.safety and efficacy ofthe product. The monographs of\nthe Pharmacopoeia have been designed to ensure the minimum \nacceptable quality of drug substances and drug products for \nusers. Tests for related substances have also been provided \nin many monographs to limit impurities and degradation \nproducts. Although one of the primary objectives of the \nPharmacopoeia; is to guarantee the identity, strength, purity \nand quality of official articles, it is not possible to include in\neach monograph a test for every impurity or contaminant or \neven an adulterant that might be present. The exclusion of a \nlimit for impurities in a monograph does not absolve the \nmanufacturer of providing assurance to the user on the safety\n1174\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022 \n5.5. IMPURITIES\nof a drug. It is incumbent on the manufacturer to follow Good \nManufacturing Practices (GMP) and to ensure the limitation \nof impurities based on knowledge of the properties of the \nchemical entity and the likelihood of related substances being \nassociated with the end product during production and\nsubsequent storage.\nImpurity measurements for drug products using \nchromatographic methods could present a challenge to the \nPharmacopoeial standards setting due to low concentrations \nof the impurity and complexity of matrix. As a consequence,\nsome ofthe monographs for Pharmacopoeial preparations rely\non chromatographic assays. Where more significant impurities\nare known, some monographs set forth specific tests for these \nimpurities.\nIn general, the tests in a monograph are tests for purity that \nprovide information on the extent of known potential or actual \nimpurities rather than for guaranteeing freedom from all\npossible impurities. The tests are not necessarily designed to\ndetect any foreign contaminants or adulteration. Material \nfound to contain an impurity not detectable by the prescribed \ntests of a monograph may be deemed to be not of\nPharmacopoeial quality particularly if the nature ofthe impurity \n(ies) found is not compatible with GMP.\nChemical tests that limiting the levels of particular impurities \nor classes of impurities are often augmented by physical\ntests such as specific optical rotation, absorbance, melting \npoint, clarity and colour of solution and for liquids, refractive \nindex, boiling point range and weight per ml etc. Besides, non­\nspecific tests such as sulphated ash, loss on drying etc. \ncontribute to the assurance of the general quality of the article\nand of the' use of GMP in its production, the avoidance of \ncontamination especially by inorganic substances and the \nremoval of volatile solvents.\nNotwithstanding this situation, there is a need to limit impurities \nthat may arise from various sources in the course of\nmanufacture.\nClassification of Impurities\nImpurities can be classified into the following categories:\n— Organic impurities\n— Inorganic impurities\n— Residual solvents\nOrganic impurities. Organic impurities may be drug-related \nor process-related and consist of identified, specified \nimpurities, unidentified, specified impurities or total unknown \nimpurities and are subject to control for all drug substances\nand drug products. In designing the specifications for any \ndrug substance, a manufacturer should determine the actual\nand potential impurities most likely to arise during the \nsynthesis, purification and storage, on the basis of scientific\nknowledge ofthe chemical reactions involved in the synthesis."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolvaptan","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolvaptan"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolvaptan\nC26H25C1N2O3 \nMol Wt .448.9\nTolvaptan is TV-{4-{[(5Æ)-7-chloro-5-hydroxy-2,3,4,5- \ntetrahydro-1 H-1 -benzazepin-1 -yl]carbonyl} -3 -methylphenyl} - \n2-mcthylbcnzamidc.\nTolvaptan contains not less than 98.0 per cent and, not more \nthan 102.0 per cent, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Vasopressin Receptor Antagonist. v yf-v..\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline powder. ; ; < ? ;"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tolvaptan\nC26H25C1N2O3 \nMol Wt .448.9\nTolvaptan is TV-{4-{[(5Æ)-7-chloro-5-hydroxy-2,3,4,5- \ntetrahydro-1 H-1 -benzazepin-1 -yl]carbonyl} -3 -methylphenyl} - \n2-mcthylbcnzamidc.\nTolvaptan contains not less than 98.0 per cent and, not more \nthan 102.0 per cent, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Vasopressin Receptor Antagonist. v yf-v..\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline powder. ; ; < ? ;"},{"name":"identification","text":"f\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spcctrophoiometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum .with that obtained with tolvaptan IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of tolvaptan. ; < 2 \n: ,; ; ;\nB. In the assay,;.the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with test solution corresponds to the the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid, chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n. A: \nTA ymm A\nSolvent mixture. 50 voltimes of solution A and 50 volumes of \nacetonitrile. \" ’..... J \" \n'\nSolution A. A mixture of 95 volumes of buffer prepared by \ndissolving 1.36 g of potassium dihydrogen orthophosphate\n........................................................   j\ninto 1000 ml of water, adj usted to pH 3.0 with/Vzosp/,^ \nA|\nand 5 volume of acetonitrile. \nCa^ f I\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance u :: s \nexamination in the solvent mixture and'dilute to 25 0 ml • l I \nthe solvent mixture. \n. \na > \n10 . |\nReference solution. A 0.1 percent w/vsolution of totvant \"■ \nIPRS in the solvent mixture. \n.. \n'\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 12.5 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded.to porous silica (5 pm),\n- \ncolumn temperature: 30°,\nA-; mobile phase: a mixture of 57 volumes of soIutionAand\n43 volumes of a mixture of 95 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 5 volumes of water,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, ’ '' \n'g'1'\n- \nspectrophotometer set at 260 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\ns\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumri efficiency is not less than 3000 theoretical plates and \nthe tailing factor is not more be than 2.0. \n;; '■■■'\nInject the test solution. The area of any secondary peak is not \nmore than 0.3 per cent and the sum ofareas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated by area \nnormalization: \nr< ■■: \n' *4 r ■ \n:-o.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1:0g complies with limittestforheaiy \nmetals, Method D (20 ppm). \n.; <\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent:'\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined on \n0 3 g. \ny-yy\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).. y ■\nTest solution.: Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the.solvent mixture and dilutc to ^O.Qmlwilh \nthe solvent ■. mixture,. Dilute.5.0 mh of the sqlutipn.to^Q.^ml \nwith the solvent mixture. ................. -A \nA\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution oftolyaplan \nIPRS in the solvent mixture. \n;.... ;,. ...,, 3 <. \na\nUse the chromatographic system as described jundp^; Related\nsubstances. . ....... \n~\nInject the reference solution. The test is npr^ali^upje^^: \ncolumn efficiency is riot lessthan 3000 iheorcititaPpMes» dte \ntailihg factor is hot more than 2.0 and the relativhstandard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0percenL\nInj ect the reference solution, and the test splutiony y -\n....... y i\nCalculate the content of CAl-UClNhOs. ;A: ■\n; \nw y Au A ■■■..\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.y ag \n'\n' \nMol.Wt.339.4\n^^®e;;U;2?3:4,5-di-p-l -isopropylidene-p-D-fructo- \npyranosesulphamate. . ;\nj0piraii>ate;contams tl°t less than 98.0 per cent and not more\n102,0 percent ofCi2H2iNOsS. calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis1. ' :\nCategory. Anti-convulsant. \n'\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with topiramate \nIPRSwvntit the reference spectrum of topiramate.\nB.Meltingrange (2.4.21). I20c to 130°."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). - 36.0° to-. 28.0°; determined\nin a 0,4 per cent w/v solution in methanol at 20°.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). , .....\nTest solution. Weigh 0.4 g of the substance under examination\nand dissolve in 10 ml of amixtureof50 volumes of water- and \n50 volumes of acetonitrile...........\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution each of \n(2,3:4,5-Bis-O-(l -methylethylidene)-beta-D-fructopyranose \nIPRS (topiramate impurity A IFRS) and 2,3-O-(l-methyl- \nethylidene)-beta-D-fructopyranqse sulphamate IPRS\n{topiramate impurity B IPRS) in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless,steel column 25 cm x 4,6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n“ column temperature: 40°,\nmobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of water and \n,i 50 volumes of acetonitrile,\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n~ refractive index detector, \n:,r> . 1 \n: 1j ..\ndetector cell temperature 50c,: \nI:..-\n’nJectionvolume:50pl."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/topiramate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Topiramate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TOPIRAMATE TABLETS\nThe retention times, of topiramate' impurity B and impurity A \nare about 2.9 and 4,5 minutes and the relative retention times \nwith respect to topiramate are 0.54 and 0.84 respectively.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peak due to topiramate impurity A and \ntopiramate impurity B is not less than 3. \ni /\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram for 40 minutes. For the òàièuiatiori of content,\nmultiply the peak areas of the following impurities by the \ncorresponding correction factor: topiramate impurity A=1.09; \ntopiramate impurity B - 0.94. The area of any secondary peak \ndue to topiramate impurity A and B is not more than 0.15 and \nthe area of any other secondary peak is not more than 0.1 per \ncent. Ignore any peak due to lactose. \n.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). \n;i ?0 -\nSulphated ash (2,3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.;\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined on 1.0 g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2:4.14). : : --\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination in mobile phase and dilute to 4 0.0 ml with the \nmobilephase.:\nReference solution. A1.0'per cent w/v solution of topiramate \nIPRS in the mobile phase. \nj a. : f: n s ; z\nChromatographic system ■\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), . ; )\n. - column temperature: 50° \n: \n;\n- \nmobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of water and 50 \nvolumes of acetonitrile, \n; \n..........\n- \nflow rate: 0.6 ml per minute,\n- refractive index detector (Detector cell temperature: 50°) \n- injection volume: 50 jri. \n'■ m \n/,//■':. ■\nInject the reference solution. The test is nOtAzalid unless thè \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injectionsis hòt more \nthan 2.0 per cent. \n;,,y\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. ; ;. ..\nCalculate the content of C12H21NO§S . ; \n4 \n; \n>\nStorage. Store protected from light, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°. \n' 'r \n............\nTopiramate Tablets\nTopiramate Tabléts contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the'stated amount of \ntopiramate, Ci2H2iNO8S. . ; \n:\nTOPIRAMATE TAB LETS\nUsual strengths. 25 mg,50 mg.100 mg, 200 mg,300 mg arid \n400mg. ; ;; \n37.iidm yrifhnñ aMbcrnc; AT mw 0.2 mods w>"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TOPIRAMATE TABLETS\nThe retention times, of topiramate' impurity B and impurity A \nare about 2.9 and 4,5 minutes and the relative retention times \nwith respect to topiramate are 0.54 and 0.84 respectively.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peak due to topiramate impurity A and \ntopiramate impurity B is not less than 3. \ni /\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram for 40 minutes. For the òàièuiatiori of content,\nmultiply the peak areas of the following impurities by the \ncorresponding correction factor: topiramate impurity A=1.09; \ntopiramate impurity B - 0.94. The area of any secondary peak \ndue to topiramate impurity A and B is not more than 0.15 and \nthe area of any other secondary peak is not more than 0.1 per \ncent. Ignore any peak due to lactose. \n.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). \n;i ?0 -\nSulphated ash (2,3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.;\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined on 1.0 g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2:4.14). : : --\n