rasoun \nfor 10 minutes; Add 45 ml of 0.02 Mpotassiumphospha^\nIP 2022 \n..........................................................................................\n& fferpH 3'5 ¡and dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase,\nfilter. --'••• \n\", t . \\ \n■\nt> ference solution (a). Dissolve 9.5 mg, each of, torsemide\n^effcompound \ntorsemiderelated compound,B\n\\p^ s^dpr^emide related compound CIPRS in 30 ml of \n^^¿>4 with the aid of ultrasound about 10 minutes and\ndd45 ml df 0- @2 Mpotassium phosphate buffer pH 3.5and \n¿iute tóTOO.O ml with the mobile phase: Dilute 1.0 ml of the \nelution to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 3 mg, each of torsemide \nIPRS and torsemide related compound A IPRS in 30 ml of\npjg/Jjanp/, y/ith the aid of ultraspund for IQ minutes and add \n45 ml of P- 02 Mpotassium phosphate buffer pH 3.5 and dilute \nto100 0 mlAVith thc mobile phase. .\nUse the chromatographic system as described under Assay.\nInject réference Solution (b).: The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to torsemide and torsemide \nrelated compound A is not less, thanl .0, the tailing factor is \nnot more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation for \nreplicate injections is hot more than 10.0 per cent for the\ntorsemide peak. -■■■.} r.;. .\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution; Run the \nchromatogram 3 times the retention time of the principal peak \nfortést;sdTuíiótí. The area: of any peakcorresponding To \ntorsemide related compound C is not mófe than 0.4 times the \nareaofthe corrcsponding peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.2 per Cent), the area of any peak \ncorresponding to torsemide related compbund B is not moré \nthan 0;6 times thé area of the corresponding peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (03 per \ncent),theareaof anypeak corresponding to torsemide. related\ncompound A is not. more than the area of the corresponding \npeak jn the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a)(0.5p.epcent),.\nThe area of any other secondary peak is hot morethan 0:1 per\ncent, the sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks excluding \ntorsémidérelated-compound A, B and C is not more than 0.2"},{"name":"Tests","text":"per cent and the sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks is \nnot more than 1.0 per cent, calculated by area normalization \nmethod. . ; A / • \ns-m -'-r. - .T.j:. ■\nm0als (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test for \nhw^Wls, Method B (10 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Notmore than 0.1per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined on\n^.Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nDissolve 38 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 30 ml of methanol, mix and sonicate to dissolve."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tosylarginine-methyl-ester-hydrochloride-methyl-n-tosy","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tosylarginine Methyl Ester Hydrochloride; Methyl N-tosy-"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tosylarginine Methyl Ester Hydrochloride; Methyl N-tosy-\nL-argininate hydrochloride: C14H22N204S,H20 = 378.9\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce suitable for the assay\nof trypsin.\nA white, crystalline solid; mp, about 145°.\nTosylph.enylanylanylchloromethane; N-TosY-L:-phenyanyl-\nchloromethane; L-tosylaminophenethylchloromethyl\nketone: C17H1SClN03S = 351.9\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nA white, crystalline solid; mp, about 105°.\nTriacetin; Glycerol Triacetate:\nCHlCOOCH3)CH(COOCH3)CHlCOOCH3)=218.20\nClear, colourless orpale straw-colouredliquid; wt.perml, about\n1.156 g; contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more than\n99.0 per cent w/w ofC9H140 6•"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tosylarginine Methyl Ester Hydrochloride; Methyl N-tosy-\nL-argininate hydrochloride: C14H22N204S,H20 = 378.9\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce suitable for the assay\nof trypsin.\nA white, crystalline solid; mp, about 145°.\nTosylph.enylanylanylchloromethane; N-TosY-L:-phenyanyl-\nchloromethane; L-tosylaminophenethylchloromethyl\nketone: C17H1SClN03S = 351.9\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nA white, crystalline solid; mp, about 105°.\nTriacetin; Glycerol Triacetate:\nCHlCOOCH3)CH(COOCH3)CHlCOOCH3)=218.20\nClear, colourless orpale straw-colouredliquid; wt.perml, about\n1.156 g; contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more than\n99.0 per cent w/w ofC9H140 6•"},{"name":"ASSAY","text":"Carry out the method for the determination ofesters\n(2.3.25).\n1 ml of 0.5 M ethanolic potassium hydroxide is equivalent to\n0.03637 gofCgH140 6•\nTriazolam;Chlorazam; 8-Chloro-6-(2-chlorophenyl)-1-methyl-\n4H-[1,2,4]triazolo[4,3-a][1,4] benzodiazepine: C17H12C12N4 =\n343.22\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nTan Crystals; mp, about 160°."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tragacanth","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tragacanth"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TRAGACANTH\nfp2022\n().] , M sodium hydroxide using phenol red solution as\ny]dicat°r-\nj mi of Oil M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01482 g of \n^[balsamic acids, calculated as cinnamic acid, C5H8O2.\nStorage Store protected from light and moisture. Avoid \nclosure & excessive heat.\nfragacanth\n^ragacanth is the air-hardened gummy exudate, flowing \n¿^ly or,obtained by incision, from the trunk and branches\nofds/raga/teS’ gummifer Labill. and certain other species of \nAstragalus (Fam. Fabaceae).\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Pale yellow, thin, flattened ribbons or brittle \npieces; odourless and almost tasteless. On the addition of \nabout 10 .times its weight of water, it forms a mucilaginous gel.\nIt has the macroscopic and microscopic characteristics \ndescribed under Identification tests A and B."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TRAGACANTH\nfp2022\n().] , M sodium hydroxide using phenol red solution as\ny]dicat°r-\nj mi of Oil M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01482 g of \n^[balsamic acids, calculated as cinnamic acid, C5H8O2.\nStorage Store protected from light and moisture. Avoid \nclosure & excessive heat.\nfragacanth\n^ragacanth is the air-hardened gummy exudate, flowing \n¿^ly or,obtained by incision, from the trunk and branches\nofds/raga/teS’ gummifer Labill. and certain other species of \nAstragalus (Fam. Fabaceae).\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Pale yellow, thin, flattened ribbons or brittle \npieces; odourless and almost tasteless. On the addition of \nabout 10 .times its weight of water, it forms a mucilaginous gel.\nIt has the macroscopic and microscopic characteristics \ndescribed under Identification tests A and B."},{"name":"Identification","text":"^.Macroscopic — Occurs as thin, flattened pieces, 30 mm \nlong, 10 mm wide and up to 1mm in thickness, more or less \ncurved, marked on the surface by fine longitudinal striae-and \nconcentric transverse ridges; white, translucent, horny; \nfracture, short. May also be in the form of thicker, less brittle. \npieces; white to pale yellow and more opaque.\nB. Microscopic — Reduce to powder. Examine under a \nmicroscope; the powder shows in the gummy mass numerous \nstratified cellular membranes which turn violét ón the addition \nof-iodinated zinc chloride solution. The mass includes starch \ngranules, isolated or in small clusters, rounded or occasionally \ndeformed, diameter 4 to 10 pm, and up to 20 pm, with a central \nhilum, visible in polarised light.\nC-Moisten 0.5 g ofthe powdered material withl ml of ethanol \nfitycr cent) and add gradually, while shaking, 5 Ó ml of water \nuntilahomogeneousmucilage is obtained. To 5 ml of the \nmucilage add 5 ml of water and 2 ml of barium hydroxide\nA slightly flocculent precipitate is formed which, \nWlfiin heated for 10 minutes on a water-bath, gives an intense \nyellow colour.\n1.0g..' -\nPiAddA.ml of a 0.5 per cent w/v dispersion in water to 0.5 ml \nhydrochloric acid and heat on a water-bath for 30 minutes. \nTo one half of the resulting liquid add 1.5 ml of sodium \nhydroxide solution and 3 ml of alkaline cupric-tartrate \nsolution and heat on a water-bath; a reddish brown precipitate\nis formed. To the other half of the liquid, add a few drops of \nbarium chloride solution^ no precipitate is formed (freedom"},{"name":"Tests","text":"...... ....................\nfromagar). \n; \n'\nAcacia and other soluble gums. To 20 ml of a 2.5 per cent w/v \nsuspension of the powdered material prepared with freshly \nboiled water add 10 nil of lead acetate solutionna flocculent \nprecipitate isformed.Filter and add to the filtrate 10 mi of tead \nsubacetate solutionna slight cloudiness may appear, but there\nis no precipitate. .'■'.ív \n; \n\"\nKaraya gum. Boil 1 g with20 nil of water untila mucilage is \nformed, add 5 ml of hydrochloric acid and again boil for \n5 minutes; no pink or red colour develops.\nSterculiá. A. Shake 012 g ofthe powdered materialwith 10 nd \nof ethanol (60 per cent) in a 10-ml stoppered cylinder; any \ngel formed occupies not more than 1.5 ml.\nB. Shake 1 g of the powdered material with 100 ml of water and \ntitrate with 0.01 M sodium hydroxide,t using methyl red \nsolution as indicator. Not more than 5.0 ml is required to change \nthe colour of the solution. \n\" ; ;\nForeign matter. Not more than 1.0 per cent, determinedby the \nfollowing method. To 2.0 g of the powdered material in a \n250.0 ml round-bottomed flask add 95 nd of methanol, swirl to \nmoisten the powder and add 60 ml of 7 M hydrochloric acid. \nAdd a few glass beads and heat under a reflux, condenser in a \nwater-bath for 3 hours, shaking occasionally. Remove the glass \nbeads and filter the hot suspension under reduced pressure \nthrough a sintered-glass crucible (porosity No, 1). Rinse the \nflask with a small quantity of water, passing the rinsings \nthrough the filter.,Wash the residue on the filter with about \n40 ml of methanol and dry to constant weight at 110°. ,\nArsenic (2.3.10). Mix 3.3 g with 3 g of anhydrous sodium \ncarbonate, add 10 ml of bromine solution and mix thoroughly.; \nEvaporate to dryness on a water-bath, gently, ignite,; and \ndissolve the cooled residue in 16 ml- of brominated \nhydrochloric acid AsTand 45 ml of water. Remove the excess \nof bromine with 2 ml of stannous chloride AsT.Ahc resulting \nsolution complies with the limit test for,arsenic (3 ppm).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 0.5 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals. Method B (20 ppm). \\\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 4.0 per cent, determined on\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). 1. g is Êçee frera Escherichia \ncoli and 10 g is free from Salmonella.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tramadol-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tramadol Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tramadol Capsules \n.\nTramadol Hydrochloride Capsules 1 \n■\nTramadol Capsules contain not . less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of tramadol\nhydrochloride, C)6H25NO2iHC1. . \n..•./•\n■_ 1 \nb’.;- \n• b':p <3 if<\nUsual Strengths. 50 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tramadol Capsules \n.\nTramadol Hydrochloride Capsules 1 \n■\nTramadol Capsules contain not . less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of tramadol\nhydrochloride, C)6H25NO2iHC1. . \n..•./•\n■_ 1 \nb’.;- \n• b':p <3 if<\nUsual Strengths. 50 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"¡ < 7 : u\nA. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 1 volume of anhydrous formic \nacid, 50 volumes of acetone and 50 volumes of:methanol-:\nTest solutipn. Disperse the contents of capsules contain^ \nabout 50 mg of Tramadol Hydrochloride in 25 mi of methano \nand filter t