solution to fill 75 per cent of the \nvolume of flask and dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TRAMADOL HYDROCHLORIDE\nIP 2022 \n.... .........................................................................................\nn/Mriöri.Dispcrse one;tablet in methanol to;fdl 30 per\nvolume of flask with the aid of ultrasound for \nn ñutes.- Add the buffer solution to.fiU.75 per cent, of the \n^olume of flask and dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase,\nDissolve a quantity of torsemide IPRS in \nWhb/tbfiH 30 per cent of the vblume-of flask with the aid \nof ultrasound for 10 minutes arid add buffer solution to fill 75 \nner cent bf the volume of the flask arid1 dilute to volume with\n¿nobilé phase to obtain a solution of the same concentration \nas that ofth'e test solution. \n.■ \n■\nCalcuiatethecontentofc^H2gN.|O3S in the tablet.\nntker tests? Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nv L Í J f p ' f Ti S \n: A f f i ¿ f i í Í // J'i S\"? ? ■ V ■ í L J ' j h' 5; ; ; ' Í ' ¡\" ¡ ;? > ! ' \\ ■\nAssay. Determine by.liquid chromatography (2.4.14). ;\nXOTE—The test solution is not stable at room temperature, \ni(isstablefor l5 hours at 6'\\ \nA;\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 2Ö tablets. Disperse a quantity\nof powder containing 40 mg of Torsemide in methanoltofill \n30 percent.ofthcvolumc of flask with the aid of ultrasound\nfor |Q minutes. Add.the buffer solution to fill 75 per cent of the \nvolume of flask and dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A'-.';\nReference solution. A 0.04 per cent w/v solution of torsemide \n/P/W?prepared by dissolving in methanol \\.c> fill 30 percent of \nthe volume of flask with the aid of ultrasound for 1 ó minutes\nand add the buffer solution to fill 75 per cent of the volume of\nthe flask and dilute to volume with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- astainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica;(5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of solution\ncontaining 10 volumes of acetonitrile and 90 volumes \nQfmethanol and 50 volumes of a buffer solution\n? 71: prepared by dissolving 2.72 g of monobasic potassium\n; phosphate in 1000 ml óf -water, adjusted to pH 4.0 with\northophosphoric acid,\n~ flowrate: 1 ml per minute, ? : \n,\n~ spectrophotometer set at 288 nm,\n“ injection volume: 20 pl. \n\\ ?\nfoe reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \n^*^8 factor is not more than 1.5 and the relative standard \ndeviation frit replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent,\n^jeet the reference;solution and the: test solution.\nCaiculatelhc content of C l6H20N4O3S in the tablets. /\n^fage. Store protected from moisture at a temperature not\nexceedingso0.\nTramadol Hydrochloride\nC16H76C1N()2 \n; \n. Mol. Wt. 299.8\nTramadol Hyrochloride is (12?S, 22?S)-2-:[(dimethylainmo)- \nmethyl]-l -(3-methoxyphenyl)cyclphexanol hydrochloride..\nTramadol Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C16H26C1NO2) calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory.Analgesic.' \n”•\nDescription. A white or almostwliite, crystalline powder.\nTest B may be omitted if tests A and C are carried but: Test A \nmay be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. ;\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4,6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tramadol \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of tramadol \nhydrochloride.\nB. In the test 'for impurity\"E, the principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution (b) corresponds to \nthe principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nSOluiiOn (a). \n■ • . ■\nC.Itgivesreaction(A)ofchlorides(2^3.1). r '' '■"},{"name":"Tests","text":"y r.\nAppearance of solution. A 5 per cent w/v solution is clear \n(2.4.1) and colourless (2.4.1). \n■■■-■ \n.\nAcidity. To 10 ml of 5 per cent w/v Solution, add 0.2 ml of \nmethyl red solution and 0.2 ml of 0.0 I M hydrochloric acid. \nThe solution is red. Not more than.0.4 ml of 0.01 Msodium \nhydroxide is required to change the colour of the indicator to \nyellow.\nOptical rotation (2.4.22).\" 0.10° to + 0.10°, determined on \n5.0 per cent w/v solution. ■ \n.c--\nImpurity E. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), \ncoating the plate with silica gel. GF254:\n3839\nwww.webofpharma.com\nMobile phase. A mixture of 4 volume of concentrated \nammonia, 19 volumes of 2-propanol and 80 volumes of \ntoluene.\nTest solution (a). Dissolve 0.1 g of the substances under \nexamination in 2 ml of methanol.\nTest solution (b) .Dilute 1ml of test solution (a)to 10ml with \nmethanol. *•- ’■\nReference solution (a). A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of\ntramadol hydrochloride IPRS in methanol.\nReference solution (b). A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of (2RS)- \n2-[(dimethylamino)methyl]cyclohexanone IPRS (tramadol \nimpurity E IPRS) in methanol. Dilute 1 ml of the solution to\n1Ó ml with methanol.\nReference solution (c)'. A0.5 per cent w/v solution of\n(1 RS, 2SR)~2-[(dimethylamino)methyl]-l-(3-m'ethoxyphènyl) \ncyclohexanol IPRS (tramadol impurity A IPRS) in reference \nsolution (a). ■ : \n.y., -. *\nSaturate the plate for 20 minutes with concentrated ammonia. \nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. Allow the . mobile \nphase to rise 15 cm. Dry the plate in air, expose the plate to \niodinë vapour for 1 hour, and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm, The chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(c) shows 2 clearly separated spots. In thé chromatogram \nobtained with test solution, (a), any secondary, spot \ncorresponding,toframadolimpurity E is not more intense than \nthe spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b) (0.2 per cent). ''' '' ' ■ ,J' ; ' ' ' ' ' ; \n: '\nRelated substances.. Determine by liquid, chromatography \n(24.14).................\" ' \" ' ' .......................... '¿¿T, ■■■.. ■\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.15 g of the substance under \nexamination in 100.0 ml of the mobile phase. \n;.......\nRéference solution (a). Dilute 2:0 mlof the test solution to\n10.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml of the ¡solution to \n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. ■ < ...\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 5 mg of tramadol impurity A \nIPRS in 4.0 ml of the test solution and dilute to 100.0 ml with\nthe mobile phase.,..\nChromatographic system i . \n.\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x. 4.0 mm packed with\nendcapped octylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \n- mobile phase : a mixture of 29.5 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 70.5 volumes of a mixture of 0.2 ml pitrifluproacetic \nacid and 100 ml of water,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\no ; - spectrophotometer set at 270 nm, ; \n. m’- ■ < v /\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \nz ; •\nThè relative retention time ; with reference to tramadol for \ntramadol impurity A is about 0.85. \n- / ; \n■\nIP20£\nTRAMADOL HYDROCHLORIDE\nInject reference; solution (b): The test is not valid unless^ \nresolution betweeri the peaks due to tramadof impunfy A^ ■ \ntramadol is not less' than 2.0. : v - \n■ ..L ', ; ;;: ■\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. Run)^ \nchromatogram 4 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution,.the area \nof the peak due to tramadol impurity A is ¡not more than the \narea of the principal peak-in thechromatogram obtained with \nreference; solution (a); (0.2 per cent), the area of any other \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.5 times, the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained wjth reference \nsolution (a) (0.1 per cent) and the sum of all the secondly \npeaks is not more than twice the area of the principal peakin \nthe chromatQgram obtained, with, reference, solution (a) (0,4 \npercent). Ignore anypeak with an area less than 0.1 timesthe \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtainedwitfr \nreference solution (a) (0.02 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies withthe limit test fa \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than O.l, per cent.\nWater (2.3.43): Not more than 0.5 per cent; determined bri 1,0 g.\nAssay., Dissolve 0.18 g in 25. ml of anhydrpus acetic acid wd \nadd 10 ml of acetic anhydride. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric \nacid, determining the end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25).\nCarry out a blank titration. : \n-■ v Ac:\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid, is equivalent to 0.02998 g of \nc16h26cino2. ’• \n?n’x X'\"'\nStorage. Store protected from light. ;. .,, ; ? ;, . ; ! :\\"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tramadol-prolonged-release-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tramadol Prolonged-release Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TRAMADOL PROLONGED-RELEASE TABLETS\nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent) and the sum \nof the areas of all other secondary peaks is not more than 5 \ntimes the area of the. principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (1.0 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply .with the tests stated under Capsules. ,\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14)^\nTest solution. Dissolve a quantity of the.mixed contents of 20 \ncapsules containing about 50 mg of Tramadol Hydrochloride \nin 150 ml ofthe mobile phase withthe aid of ultrasound and \ndilute to 200 ml with the mobile phase, filter. f■; '■■■'■ ■' ' \n’ 1 '\nReference solution (a). A 6.025 per cent w/v solution of \ntramadol hydrochloride IPRS in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.0015 per cent \nw/v each of tramadol hydrochloride IPRS and tramadol \nimpurity A IPRS in the mobile phase.\nUse the chromatographic system as described under Related\nsubstances. \n1\nInject reference solution (b). The test is'not’valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to tramadol impurity À and \ntramadol hydrochloride is not 