ion volume: 20 pl.\nHgfor3 hours.'\nAss^/cDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as \ndescribed ¡under Related substances with the following\noctddecylsilane bonded to porous silica (3.5 jarii),\n- column temperature :40°, \n: -\n- mobile phase: À. a 0.005 M ammonium bicarbonate in\nmodifications;\nwater, \n■ ’. ' \n1\nB. acetonitrile,' \n-\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- MS/MS (tandom mass spectrophotometer) set as,\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 900 theoretical plates and \nthé relative standard deviation for replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent\nTest solution. 'Weigh accurately 0.1g of the substance under\neyaminiitibh in the solvent mixture and dilute to 100.0 ml with\nthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of trazodone \nhydrochloride lPRS in the solvent mixture.\nIonization: Triple quadrupole ionization in positive ion \nmode. \n’’ \nn-W\nAquisition mode: Multiple reaction monitoring (MRM) \nof the following mass transitions; cyclophosphamide \nrelated compound A 142 -> 63 trazodone related \ncompound F 273 —> 120\n- flow rate : 1.5 ml per minute, \nc j : au -j F. ' ; ; -\n- flow rate to ion source: 0.5 ml perminute,. , ■ ■\n- injection volume: Adjust between 5 and 50 pl, depending\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4.5 times the retention time of the principal \npeak.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculatethecontent of C19H22C1N3O, HCL\nCalculate the content of C19H22C1N5O, HC1 in the medium.\nStorage.) Store protected from light and moisture, at a\nQ. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of \nCiyll^ClNsOJlCl.\ntemperature not exceeding 30°.\non the mass spectrometer. L\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2-4-14). \n... ...\nTrazodone Tablets\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\nSolvent mixture. 65 volumes ofmethanol, 35 volumes of water \nand 0.3 volume of hydrochloric acid.\nTrazodonellydrochloride Tablets\nTrazodone Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not\n0\n90 :\n; \\ \n.10\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 50 mg of trazodone in.the solvent mixture, with the \naid of ultrasound for 10 minutes and dilute to 100.0 ml with the\nnwre than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of trazodone \nhydrochloride, CI9H22C1N5O, HC1.\n6.5\n20\n80\n6.51\n90\n10\nUsual strengths. 25 mg; 50 mg; 100 mg.\nsolvent mixture.\n10.0\n90.\n10\nNOTE—A mixture of 80 volumes of 2-propandl, 20voly^s"},{"name":"identification","text":"Reference solution (a). An accurately weighed quantity of \ntrazodone hydrochloride IPRS containing 0.0005 per cent \nw/v solution of trazodone in the solvent mixture. Dilute 5.0 ml\nofwater and 0.1 volume of formic acid maybetisedfor injector \nwash to minimize sample carry over.\nThe relative retention time with reference to trazodone related \ncompound F for cyclophosphamide related compound A 6\nb the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained\n^ith the tést solution corresponds to the peak in the\ne«roniatdgram obtained with the reference solution .\nof the solution to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Reference solution (b). A solution containing 6.00007 per cent \nw/v of trazodone hydrochloride IPRS arid 0.00015 per cent \nw/v of trazodone related compound C IPRS in the solvent \nmixture.\nabout 0.4. \n, r...\nInject thc referencc solution. The test is not valid unless the\n(2.5.2).\nAPParatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nsignal-to-noise ratio is not less than 100 for; the trazodQ®6\n3853\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcino-lone-acetonide-inj-ection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcino lone Acetonide Inj ection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE INJECTION\nchromatogram1 obtained with reference solution (b) \n(0.25 per cent); the sum of the areas ofall the peaks other than \nthe principal peak is not greater than half the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak due to the solvent \nand any peak with an area,less than 0.05 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b). ; ,,\nSuIphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent. \n- -■\nWater (2.3.43); Notnidre than 2.0 per cent, determined oh 0.2 g.\nAssay. Dissolve 25 mg in sufficient ethanol to produce\n100.0 ml and mix. Dilute 5.0 ml to 100;0 ml with ethanol \n(95 per cent) and measure the absorbance of the resulting \nsolution at the maximum at about 239 nm (2.4.7)..\nCalculate the content of C24H3iFO6 taking 354 as the specific \nabsorbance at 239 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. ,\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection is a sterile suspension of \nTriamcinolone Acetonide in very fíne particles in Water for \nInjections containing suitable dispersing agents.\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount bftriamcinolone acetonide, C24H3iFO6.\nUsual strength. 40 mg per ml."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE INJECTION\nchromatogram1 obtained with reference solution (b) \n(0.25 per cent); the sum of the areas ofall the peaks other than \nthe principal peak is not greater than half the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak due to the solvent \nand any peak with an area,less than 0.05 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b). ; ,,\nSuIphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent. \n- -■\nWater (2.3.43); Notnidre than 2.0 per cent, determined oh 0.2 g.\nAssay. Dissolve 25 mg in sufficient ethanol to produce\n100.0 ml and mix. Dilute 5.0 ml to 100;0 ml with ethanol \n(95 per cent) and measure the absorbance of the resulting \nsolution at the maximum at about 239 nm (2.4.7)..\nCalculate the content of C24H3iFO6 taking 354 as the specific \nabsorbance at 239 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. ,\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection is a sterile suspension of \nTriamcinolone Acetonide in very fíne particles in Water for \nInjections containing suitable dispersing agents.\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount bftriamcinolone acetonide, C24H3iFO6.\nUsual strength. 40 mg per ml."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Extract a volume containing about 50 mg of Triamcinolone \nAcetonide with two quantities, each of 10 ml, of peroxide-free \nether and discard the ether extracts. Filter the aqueous layer \nthrough a sintered-glass filter, wash the residue with four \nquantities, each of 5 nil of waterand dry at 105° for 1 hour. The \nresidue complies with the following tests.\nTest A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Test C \nmay be omitted ij'tests A and B are carried out. ”\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with triamcinolone \nacetonide IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \ntriamcinolone acetonide.\nB. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel G.\nSolvent mixture. 90 volumes of acetone pxxA. 10 volumes of \nformamide. y / \n. ■ \n... ...... .......,\nMobile phase: A mixture of 115 volumes of cyclohexane, \n56 volumes of chloroform and 29 volumes of toluene.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance \nexamination in 10 ml of the solvent mixture.\nunder\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 25 mg of triamcinblo^ \nacetonide IPRS in 10 mi of thé solvent mixture. .\nReference solution (b). Mix equal volumes of the test solution \nand reference solution (a). \n> \n; ; \n< \n; ?\nPlace the dry plate in a tank containing a shallow layer of the \nsolvent mixture, allow the solvent mixture to ascend to the \ntop, remove the plate from the tank and allow the solvent to \nevaporate. Use within ? hours, with the, flow of the mobile \nphase in the direction in which the aforementioned treatment \nwas done.\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution, Allow, the mobile \nphase to rise 12 cm. Dry the plate in a current of warm air, allow \nthe solvent to evaporate, heat at 120° for 15 minutes and spray \nthe hot plate with~ethanolic'sulphuric acid (20per cent vA), \nHeat at 120° for a further 10 minutes, allow to cool and examine \nin daylight and under ultraviolet light at 365 nm. The principal \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the test.solution \ncorresponds to that in the. chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a). The principal spot in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) appears as a single, \ncompact spot.\nC. Heat 0.5 ml of chromic-sulphuric acid in a test-tube (5 cm x \nabout 6 mm) in a naked flame until white fumes are evolved; \nthe solution wets the side’s of the tube readily ahd there is no \ngreasiness.. Add about 2 mg of the substance.-.under \nexamination and again heat in a naked flame untif white fumes \nappear; the solution does not wet the sides of the tube and \ndoes not pour easily from the tube.\nTests '\npH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 7.5.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 4.4 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of triamcinolone acetonide. ; ,\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4; 14).: :\nTest so lution. Shake and mix contents of 10 containers. \nDissolve a volume of the freshly mixed injection with 0.01 per \ncent w/v solution of prednisolone in methanol (internal \nstandard) to obtainafmal concentrationof 0.02 per cent w/v\nof triamcinolone acetonide, shake and filter.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.02.per cent \nw/v of triamcinolone acetonide IPRS in internal standard.\nReference solution (b). Prepare in the same manner as test\nsolution hut omitting the internal standard. -\nrhroniatographic system\n\" a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4 mm, packed with\noCtadecylsilane bonded t0 Porous silica (10 Pra)’\n-mobile P^ase: a mixture of 56 volumes of methanol and\n-44voiumesof hy/Zcv;\n. ^ flow rate:1.4 ml per minute,\n■ - spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n; t _injectionvolumc: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (b) for identification of peak due to \n^anicinoloneacetonide.\n|n:ect reference solution (a). Adjust the flow rate of the mobile \nnhase such that the separation of thè triamcinolone acetonide \nand internal standard is optimised with a retention time of \nabout115 iainutes for triamcinolone acetonide:\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C24H3]FO6 in the injection.\nStdràgepSiqré protected from light.\nC|2HnN7 \nMol. Wt. 253.3\nTriamterene is 2,4,7-triamino-6-phenylpteridine.\nTriamterene contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan! 01Aper cent of C l2H! iN7, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Potassium sparing diuretic.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder.\n■:'<j iihpi'i.?:; A-..- \n■ . \n■"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. When examined in the range 250 nm to 380 nm (2.4.6), a\nMOI per cent w/v solution in a mixture of 9 volumes of ethanol\ncenr). and 1 volume of 1 M hydrochloric acid shows \nabsorption ihaxima at about 262 nm and 360 nm, and a shoulder\nat about 285 nm\nB- Ai). 1 per cent w/v solution in anhydrous formic acid, when\nexamined; under ultraviolet light at 365 nm shows an intense \nbluefluorescence. Solutions in other acids also exhibit a blue \nfluorescence."},{"name":"tests","text":"Acidity, Boil 1.0 g with 20 ml of water for 5 minutes, cool, filter\nwash the filter with three quantities, each of 10 ml, .of"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcinolone-acetonide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide Injection\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection is a sterile suspension of\nTriamcinolone Acetonide in very fine particles in Water for\nInjections containing suitable dispersing agents.\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection contains not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated\namount oftriamcinolone acetonide, C24H31F06.\nUsual strength. 40 mg per ml."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triamcinolone Acetonide Injection\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection is a sterile suspension of\nTriamcinolone Acetonide in very fine particles in Water for\nInjections containing suitable dispersing agents.\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection contains not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated\namount oftriamcinolone acetonide, C24H31F06.\nUsual strength. 40 mg per ml."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Extract a volume containing about 50 mg of Triamcinolone\nAcetonide with two quantities, each of10 ml, ofperoxide-free\nether and discard the ether extracts. Filter the aqueous layer\nthrough a sintered-glass filter, wash the residue with·four\nquantities, each of5 ml ofwater and dry at 105° for 1 hour. The\nresidue complies with the following tests.\nTest A may be omitted iftests Band C are carried out. Test C\nmay be omitted iftests A and B are carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectnim with that obtained with triamcinolone\nacetonide RS or with the reference spectrum oftriamcinolone\nacetonide.\nB. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel G.\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 90 volumes of acetone and\n10 volumes offormamide.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 115 volumes of cyclohexane,\n56 volumes ofchloroform and 29 volumes of toluene.\nTRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE INJECTION\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 10 ml ofthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 25 mg of triamcinolone\nacetonide RS in 10 ml ofthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Mix equal volumes ofthe test solution\nand reference solution (a).\nPlace the dry plate in a tanle containing a shallow layer ofthe\nsolvent mixture, allow the solvent mixture to ascend to the\ntop, remove the plate from the tank and allow the solvent to\nevaporate. Use within 2 hours, with the flow of the mobile\nphase in the direction in which the aforementioned treatment\nwas done.\nApply to the plate 5 III of each solution. Allow the mobile\nphase to lise 12 cm. Dry the plate in a current ofwarm air, allow\nthe solvent to evaporate, heat at 120° for 15 minutes and spray\nthe hot plate with ethanolic sulphuric acid (20 per cent v/v).\nHeat at 120° for a further 10 minutes, allow to cool and examine\nin daylight and in ultraviolet light at 365 nm. The principal\nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution\ncorresponds to that in the chromatogram obtained with\nreference solution (a). The principal spot in the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (b) appears as a single,\ncompact spot.\ne. Heat 0.5 ml ofchromic-sulphuric acidin a test-tube (5 cmx\nabout 6 mm) in a naked flame until white fumes are evolved;\nthe solution wets the sides ofthe tube readily and there is no\ngreasiness. Add about 2 mg of the substance under\nexamination and again heat in a naked flame until white fumes\nappear; the solution does not wet the sides of the tube and\ndoes not pour easily from the tube."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24).5.0 to 7.5.\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Parenteral\nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Detennine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Mix an accurately measured volume of the\ninjection, diluted with methanol, with sufficient ofa solution\nofprednisoloneRS(internal standard) with methanol to obtain\na final concentration of 0.02 per cent w/v of triamcinolone\nacetonide and 0.01 per cent w/v of prednisolone, centrifuge\nand use the clear supernatant liquid.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.02 per cent\nw/v of triamcinolone acetonide RS and 0.01 per cent w/v of\nprednisolone RS in methanol.\nReference solution (b). Prepare in the same manner as test\nsolution but omitting the internal standard."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcinolone-acetonide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide\n3856\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIp 202? \n...... _............................... ................ ..................... .......\ntolane Acetonide is 9a-fluoro-110,21 -dihydroxy- \nJ^jVa-isopropy^denedioxy'-1,4-pregnadiene-3,20-dione.\n- \n¡nolone Acetonide contains not less than 96.0 per cent\nper cent of C24H3!FO6, calculated on"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triamcinolone Acetonide\n3856\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIp 202? \n...... _............................... ................ ..................... .......\ntolane Acetonide is 9a-fluoro-110,21 -dihydroxy- \nJ^jVa-isopropy^denedioxy'-1,4-pregnadiene-3,20-dione.\n- \n¡nolone Acetonide contains not less than 96.0 per cent\nper cent of C24H3!FO6, calculated on"},{"name":"Tests","text":"the anhydrousbasis.;\nCategory. Corticosteroid.\nDescription-Awhile or almost white, crystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may \nomitted if tests B and C are carried out. Test C\nmay beomitted if tests A and B are carried out.\nA Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompareihespcctrum with that obtained with triamcinolone\nacetonide IPBS: • ■ \n•\nB. Determine bythin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel G.\nSolvent mixture .90 volumes of acetone and 10 volumes of\nformamide. vA\nMobile phase. A mixture of 115 volumes of cyclohexane,\n56 volumes of chloroform and 29 volumes of toluene.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10 ml of the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 25 mg of triamcinolone\nsolvent mixture.\nReference-solution (b). Mix equal volumes of the test solution\nand reference solution (a).\nPlace the dry plate in a tank containing a shallow layer of the \nsolvent mixture, allow the solvent mixture to ascend to the\ntop, remove, the plate from the tank and allow the solvent to \nevaporate. Use within 2 hours, with the flow of the mobile \nphase in the direction in which the aforementioned treatment\nlOminutes.\nwas done.\nApply to thp, plate 5 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 12 cm. Dry the plate in a current of warm air, allow \nthe solvent to evaporate, heat at 120° for 15 minutes and spray \nthe hotplate with ethanolic sulphuric acid (20per cent v/v). \nHeat at 120° for a further 10 minutes, allow to cool and examine\ndaylight and under ultraviolet light at 365 nm. The principal\nsP°t.ihthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution\nCOrresponds to that in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a). The principal spot in the chromatogram \nobtained witli reference solution (b) appears as a single,\ncompact spot.\n: \n9*5 ml of chromic-sulphuric acid in a test-tube (5 cm x\n* \n$ mrn) in a naked flame until white fumes are evolved;\nsolution; wets the sides of the tube readily and there is no \nEasiness. Add about 2 mg of the substance under\nTRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE\nexamination and again heat in a naked flame until white fumes \nappear; the solution does not wet the sides of the tube and \ndoes not pour easily from the tube.\nLight absorption (2.4.7). When examined in the range 230 nm \nto 360 nm, a 0.001 per cent w/v solution in ethanol (95 per \ncent) shows an absorption maximum at about 239 nm; \nabsorbance at about 239 nm, 0.34 to 0.37.\nSpecific optical rotation (2,4,22). + 100° to +107°, determined \nin a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in dioxan.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nNOTE - Carry out the test protectedfrom light.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 7 ml of methanol and dilute to 10 ml withwater.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 2 mg of triamcinolone \nacetonide IPRS and 2 mg of triamcinolone in the mobile phase \nand dilute to 100 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 ml of the test solution to\n100 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture prepared by mixing 525 ml of\nmethanol with 400 ml of water, allowing to equilibrate, \nadjusting the volume to 1000.0 ml with water and mixing \nagain, \n- \nr ‘\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 ul.\nEquilibrate the column with the mobile phase for about\nInject reference solution (b). Adjust the sensitivity, of the \nsystem so that the height of the principal peak is at least \n50 per cent of the full scale of the recorder.\nInject reference solution (a). The retention times are; \ntriamcinolone, about 5 minutes and triamcinolone acetonide \nabout 17 minutes. The test is not valid unless the resolution \nbetween the peaks corresponding to triamcinolone and \ntriamcinolone acetonide is at least 15; if necessary, adjust the \nconcentration of methanol in the mobile phase.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. Continue \nthe chromatography for 3.5 times the retention time of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \nthe area of any peak other than the principal peak is not more \nthan 0.25 times the area of the principal peak in the"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcinolone-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcinolone Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TRIAMCINOLONE TABLETS\n.............................................................................................\n^^Corticosteroid.\n' •Xtinn A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nne5cnpllun- .\nSl^^r0SC0P’C*\npetennine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\n* -»»¿fhe spectrum with that obtained with triamcino lone \njpjjy^xvlththe reference spectrum of triamcinolone.\ng^heii examined in the range 220 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \nO^ feH'W^v.solution in methanol shows an absorption\ninaXiinumatabout238 nm; absorbance at about 238 nm, about\n■ ■ '■ \n■ i\nC O *n 0 bf ethanol (95 per cent), add .5 ml of \na 1 per cent w/v solution of butylated hydroxytoluene in \nethanol (95 cwt) an^ 5 ml of 1. M sodium hydroxide and \nheat ¿n a Watei-bath under a reflux condenser for 20 minutes; \napihkish lavender colour is produced."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TRIAMCINOLONE TABLETS\n.............................................................................................\n^^Corticosteroid.\n' •Xtinn A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nne5cnpllun- .\nSl^^r0SC0P’C*\npetennine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\n* -»»¿fhe spectrum with that obtained with triamcino lone \njpjjy^xvlththe reference spectrum of triamcinolone.\ng^heii examined in the range 220 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \nO^ feH'W^v.solution in methanol shows an absorption\ninaXiinumatabout238 nm; absorbance at about 238 nm, about\n■ ■ '■ \n■ i\nC O *n 0 bf ethanol (95 per cent), add .5 ml of \na 1 per cent w/v solution of butylated hydroxytoluene in \nethanol (95 cwt) an^ 5 ml of 1. M sodium hydroxide and \nheat ¿n a Watei-bath under a reflux condenser for 20 minutes; \napihkish lavender colour is produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22).+65.0° to + 72.0°, determined\nin a h0 pet cent w/v solution'in dimethylformainide.\n. ■ -\nRelatedsubstances.Determinc by liquid chromatography\nNOTE—Prepare the following solutions immediately before \nuse and protect from light-.'\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in a mixture of equal volumes of methanol and \nwater and dilute to 10 ml with the same solvent mixture.\nReference solution. Dilute 1 mlof the test solution to 100 ml \nwith a mixture of equal volumes of methanol and water.I\nIdentification ;;\nChromatographic system\n- a.stainless;steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n: ba^e-deactiVated end-capped octadecyl silane bonded\ni to porous silica (5 pm), '\n~ nibbiléphàse: a mixture prepared by mixing 525 ml of\nmethanol with 400 ml of water, allowing to equilibrate, \nadjusting the volume to 1000.0 ml with water and mixing \nagain, \"\n~ flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n“ spectrophotometer set at 238 nm,\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\ninjectthe reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4.5 times the retention time of triamcinolone \n(about 11 minutes).' In the chromatogram obtained with the"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\n!esi solution the area of any peak other than the principal\nis not more than the area of the principal peak in the\nc Oinatôgram obtained with thé reference solution (1 per \n^nt), nbt more than two such peaks have an area more than\n• tunes' the; area of the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution (0.5 per cent); the sum of \nthe areas of all the peaks other than the principal peak is not \nmore than twice, the area of the; principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (2 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.05 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution (0.05 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 0.8 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy‘metals, Method B (25 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19): Not more than 2:0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 60° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa for 3 hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 25 mg in sufficient ethanol (95 per cent) to \nproduce 100.0 ml and mix. Dilute 2.0 ml to 50.0 ml with ethanol \n(95 per cent) and measure the absorbance of the resulting \nsolution at themaximum at about 238 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content bfC21H27FO6/taking 389 as the specific \nabsorbance at 238 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nTriamcinolone Tablets\nTriamcinolone Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntriamcinolone, C21H27FO6.\nUsual strengths. 2 mg; 4 mg; 8 mg.\nA. Dissolve 1 mg of the residue obtained in the test.for Related \nsubstances in 6 ml of ethanol (95 percent), add 5 mlof a 1 per \ncent w/v solution'of butylated hydroxy toluene in ethanol \n(95 per centf ww5.5 ml of / :M sodium hydroxide and heat on \na water-bath under a reflux condenser for 20 minutes; a pinkish \nlavender colour is produced.\nB. In the Assay, the chromatogram obtained with the principal \npeak obtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak \ndue to triamcinolone in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a). . \n. .\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.01Mhydrochloric acid,\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable, volume of the medium and filter.Measure \nthe absorbance Of the filtrate, dilute suitably if necessary with\n3855\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIAMCINOLONE TABLETS\nthe medium, at the maximum at about 238 nm (2.4.7). Calculate \nthe content of C?|H27FO6 in the medium from the absorbance \nobtained from a solution of known concentration of \ntriamcinolone IP RS the dissolution medium. '\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of thè stated amount of C211127FO6.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the, powdered tablets \ncontaining 15 mg of Triamcinolone with 15 ml of ethanol for\n15 minutes, filter under reduced pressure through a fine filter \npaper (such as Whatman No : 42) and evaporate the filtratelo \ndryness using a rotary evaporator. Reserve 1 mg of the residue \nfor Identification test A. Dissolve the remainder of the residue \nin 15 ml of methanol. . .\nReference solution (a).. Dilute 4 ml.of the. test solution to\n100 ml with methanol.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 5 ml of thè test solution to 50 ml \nwith methanol. Dilute 5 ml of the resulting solution to 50 ml \nwith the same solvent. \n.\nChromatographic system \n. ,\n- a stainless steel column 20 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of methanol and water, adjusted\nso that the retention time' of triamcinolone is about \n5 minutes (approximately equal volumes of methanol\n. and water), ....... \n, .\n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute, \nj\n- \n- spectrophotometer set at 238 nm, \n- injectionvolume:20pl.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency determined from the principal peak is not \nless than 2000 theoretical plates.\nInject.reference solution (b). Run the chromatogram 4 times \nthe retention time of the triamcinolone peak. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than twice the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b) (2.Ó per cent) and not more than one such peak has an area \nmore than that of the principal' peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (1.0 per cent) and the sum \nof the areas of any such peaks is not more than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (4.0 per cent).\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets.\nCrush one tablet to a fine powder, add 100 ml of ethanol \n(95per cent) and shake for 10 minutes. Filter, dilute 2.0 ml of \nthe filtrate with sufficient ethanol (95 per cent) to produce a\nsolution containing 0.002 per cent w/v of Triamcinolone and\n......................................................;..............   ’P2027\nmeasure the absorbance of the resulting solution- at iK \nmaximum at about 238 nm (2.4.7). \n> 7. \n6\nCalculate the content of C21H27FO6 in the tablet taking 339 \nthe specific absorbance at 238 nm.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14),\nInternal standard solution. A 0.Q6 per cent /w/vsolution af\ntestosterone in methanol. .\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof the powder containing 2.5 mg of Triamcinolonein 5.0 ml of \ninternal standard solution and dilute to 25.Ô ml with 50 \ncent v/v of methanol. Shake for 15 minutes, mix with the aid of \nultrasound for 10 minutes, centrifuge and use the supernatant \nliquid. 1 \n’ \n'■ ■ '\nReference solution. Add 5.0 ml of internal standard solution \nto 5.0 ml of a 0.05 per cent w/v solution of triamcinolonelPRS \nin methanol and dilute to 25.0 ml with 50 per cent v/v of \nmethanol.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless ste"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcinolone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcinolone"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TRIAMCINOLONE\nTest solution. Dilute the eye drops, if necessary wi \nsolvent mixture, to produce a solution containing 0. \ncent w/v of travoprost. \nReference solution (a). A 0.004 per cent w/v solutio \ntravoprost RS in the solvent mixture.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\ndescribed in the test for Related substances.\ninject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nNOTE- Prepare the following solutions immediately before \nuse and protect from light.\nCalculate the content of C 26H35F306.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in a mixture of equal volumes of methanol and \nwater and dilute to 10 ml with the same solvent mixture.\nstorage. Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference soluti . , \nto 10.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\nReference solution. Dilute 1 ml of the test solution to 100 ml \nwith a mixture of equal volumes of methanol and water.\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous \nsilica (5 pm) (Such as Hypersil ODS), \n-\ncolumn temperature: 35°, \nsample temperature: 10°, \n-\nmobile phase A: a buffer solution prepared by \ndissolving 1.5 g of 1-octane sodium sulphonic acid in\nTriamcinolone\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \nbase-deactivated end-capped octadecylsilane bonded \nto porous silica (5 gm), \n-\nmobile phase: a mixture prepared by mixing 525 ml of \nmethanol with 400 ml of water, allowing to equilibrate, \nadjusting the volume to 1000.0 ml with water and mixing \nagain, \n-\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 238 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\ntie\n1000 ml of water, \nadjusted to pH 2.5 with \northophosphosric acid,\nB: a mixture of 80 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 20 volumes of mobile phase A, \n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below,\nC21}1406 \t\nMol. Wt. 394.4\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 220 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 100 pl.\nTriamcinolone is 9a-fluoro- 1 1 p, 1 6a,1 7a,2 1 -tetrahydroxy-\npregna-1,4-diene-3,20-dione.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4.5 times the retention time of triamcinolone \n(about 1 1 minutes). In the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution the area of any peak other than the principal \npeak is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (1 per \ncent); not more than two such peaks have an area more than \n0.5 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (0.5 per cent); the sum of \nthe areas of all the peaks other than the principal peak is not \nmore than twice the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (2 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.05 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution (0.05 per cent).\nTime \t\nMobile phase A \t\nMobile phase B \n(in min.) \t\n(per cent v/v) \t\n(per cent v/v) ,*\nTriamcinolone contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of C 21 H27F06, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\n0 \t\n50 \t\n50\n50 \t 42 \t\n58\n52 \t\n50 \t\n50\nCategory. Corticosteroid.\n60 \t\n50 \t\n50 ,\nDose. 2 to 24 mg daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nslightly hygroscopic.\nEquilibrate the column for at least 60 minutes with the mobile \nphase at the initial composition.\nThe relative retention time with respect to travoprost for 5,6-\ntrans travoprost is 1.1 [NOTE -Travaprost RS contains a \nsmall percentage of the 5,6- trans isomer]."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TRIAMCINOLONE\nTest solution. Dilute the eye drops, if necessary wi \nsolvent mixture, to produce a solution containing 0. \ncent w/v of travoprost. \nReference solution (a). A 0.004 per cent w/v solutio \ntravoprost RS in the solvent mixture.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\ndescribed in the test for Related substances.\ninject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nNOTE- Prepare the following solutions immediately before \nuse and protect from light.\nCalculate the content of C 26H35F306.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in a mixture of equal volumes of methanol and \nwater and dilute to 10 ml with the same solvent mixture.\nstorage. Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference soluti . , \nto 10.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\nReference solution. Dilute 1 ml of the test solution to 100 ml \nwith a mixture of equal volumes of methanol and water.\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous \nsilica (5 pm) (Such as Hypersil ODS), \n-\ncolumn temperature: 35°, \nsample temperature: 10°, \n-\nmobile phase A: a buffer solution prepared by \ndissolving 1.5 g of 1-octane sodium sulphonic acid in\nTriamcinolone\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \nbase-deactivated end-capped octadecylsilane bonded \nto porous silica (5 gm), \n-\nmobile phase: a mixture prepared by mixing 525 ml of \nmethanol with 400 ml of water, allowing to equilibrate, \nadjusting the volume to 1000.0 ml with water and mixing \nagain, \n-\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 238 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\ntie\n1000 ml of water, \nadjusted to pH 2.5 with \northophosphosric acid,\nB: a mixture of 80 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 20 volumes of mobile phase A, \n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below,\nC21}1406 \t\nMol. Wt. 394.4\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 220 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 100 pl.\nTriamcinolone is 9a-fluoro- 1 1 p, 1 6a,1 7a,2 1 -tetrahydroxy-\npregna-1,4-diene-3,20-dione.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4.5 times the retention time of triamcinolone \n(about 1 1 minutes). In the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution the area of any peak other than the principal \npeak is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (1 per \ncent); not more than two such peaks have an area more than \n0.5 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (0.5 per cent); the sum of \nthe areas of all the peaks other than the principal peak is not \nmore than twice the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (2 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.05 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution (0.05 per cent).\nTime \t\nMobile phase A \t\nMobile phase B \n(in min.) \t\n(per cent v/v) \t\n(per cent v/v) ,*\nTriamcinolone contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of C 21 H27F06, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\n0 \t\n50 \t\n50\n50 \t 42 \t\n58\n52 \t\n50 \t\n50\nCategory. Corticosteroid.\n60 \t\n50 \t\n50 ,\nDose. 2 to 24 mg daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nslightly hygroscopic.\nEquilibrate the column for at least 60 minutes with the mobile \nphase at the initial composition.\nThe relative retention time with respect to travoprost for 5,6-\ntrans travoprost is 1.1 [NOTE -Travaprost RS contains a \nsmall percentage of the 5,6- trans isomer]."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A.Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with triamcinolone \nRS or with the reference spectrum of triamcinolone.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution obtained between travoprost and 5,6-trans \ntravoprost is not less than 1.5. The column efficiency is not \nless than 2000 theoretical plates, the tailing factor is not more \nthan 2.0 and the relative standard deviation for replicate \ninjections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 0.8 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (25 ppm).\nB.When examined in the range 220 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.002 per cent w/v solution in methanol shows an absorption \nmaximum at about 238 nm; absorbance at about 238 nm, about \n0.76.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 60° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa for 3 hours.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.4 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b) (4.0 per cent) and the sum of areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 0.6 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolun (b). 6.0 per cent).\nC.Dissolve 1 mg in 6 ml of ethanol (95 per cent), add 5 ml of\na 1 per cent w/v solution of butylated hydragtoluene in \nethanol (95 per cent) and 5 ml of 1 M sodium hydroxide and \nhe at on a water-bath under a reflux condenser for 20 minutes; \na pinkish lavender colour is produced.\nAssay. Dissolve 25 mg in sufficient ethanol (95 per cent) to \nproduce 100.0 ml and mix. Dilute 2.0 ml to 50.0 ml with ethanol \n(95 per cent) and measure the absorbance of the resulting \nsolution at the maximum at about 238 nm (2.4.7)."},{"name":"Tests","text":". Calculate the content of C21 H27F06 taking 389 as the specific\n---- \nSPedfic optical rotation (2.4.22). +65.0° to +72 .ft deterthined \nin a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in dimethylformiim ide.\nabsorb4nceat23 8 nm.\nOtket-lesik7-fomply with the tests stated under Eye\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamterene-and-hydrochlorothiazide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamterene and Hydrochlorothiazide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamterene and Hydrochlorothiazide \nTablets\nTriamterene and Hydrochlorothiazide Tablets contain not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 1 lO.O per cent of the\nstated 'amounts of triamterene, C12HnN7 andhydrochloro-\nthiazide, C^HsClNsO.^. .\n25mg. \n..... . h \n\"• •\nUsual strength. Triamterene, 50 mg and Hydrochlorothiazide,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triamterene and Hydrochlorothiazide \nTablets\nTriamterene and Hydrochlorothiazide Tablets contain not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 1 lO.O per cent of the\nstated 'amounts of triamterene, C12HnN7 andhydrochloro-\nthiazide, C^HsClNsO.^. .\n25mg. \n..... . h \n\"• •\nUsual strength. Triamterene, 50 mg and Hydrochlorothiazide,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the principal peaks in the chromatogram obtained \nwith thé test solution correspond to the peaks in th«\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5,2). \n; : :\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle), \nu i : : ;\nTRIAMTERENE AND HYDROCHLOROTHIAZIDE TABLETS\n1P2022......... .....................................................................................\n^-900 ini of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, \nS^eedandtime. 75 rpm and 30 minutes.\na suitable volume of the medium and filter.\npetern^^Iicluid chromatography (2.4.14).\nwater to volume.\nUse the filtrate, dilute if necessary, with the \n¿¡jsolutionmedium.\nDissolve a suitable quantity of triamterene \n^¿^^¿hydrochlorothiazide IPRS in the dissolution medium\nloobtaiirds°Iiition o*' known concentration similar to the \n^pected concentration of the test solution.\nChromatographic system\n_ a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 80 volumes of a buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 6.9 g of sodium dihydrogen \nphosphate and 1.43 g cd propylamine hydrochloride in\n1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 5.5 with IM sodium \nhydroxide and 20 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flowrate: 1.2 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm, \n-injection volume: 10 pl.\nThe relative retention times with reference to triamterene for \nhydrochlorothiazide is about 0.65.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the hydrochlorothiazide and triamterene \npeaksis not less than 3.0 and the relative standard deviation\nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent for both\nthe peaks.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C12HnN7 and C7H8C1N3O4S2 in the\nmedium:: :;\nQ. Notices than 80 per cent of the stated amount of C12H11N7\nandC7H8ClN3O4S2.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n.\nSolent mixture A. A buffer solution prepared by dissolving \n&8gofsdi/zwm acetate trihydrate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted\n10 pH 5.0 with glacial acetic acid.\nSolvent mixture B. 75 volumes of acetonitrile and 25 volumes\nmethanol.\nl^t solution. Disperse a quantity of the powdered tablets \nstaining 150 mg of Hydrochlorothiazide in 60 ml of \n^tonitrile and 6 ml of glacial acetic acid, with the aid of\nkasound for 10 minutes. Cool and dilute to 100.0 ml with\nw\nReferehce;solution. A 0.015 per cent w/v solution of\noihiadiazine related compound A (4-Amino-6-chloro­\n1,3-benzenedisulphonamide) IPRS in acetonitrile. Transfer \n10.0 ml of the solution to 100-ml volumetric flask, add 50 ml of \nacetonitrile and 6 ml of glacial acetic acid and dilute with\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 30 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (10 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 10 volumes of solvent mixture\nB and 90 volumes of solvent mixture A,\n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set 273 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nThe relative retention times with reference to benzothiadiazine \nrelated compound A for hydrochlorothiazide and triamterene \nare 1.5 and 10, respectively.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent. .\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area any \npeak corresponding to benzothiadiazine related compoundA \nis not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (liO per \ncent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 35 volumes of acetonitrile, 3.5 volumes of \nglacial acetic acid and 65 volumes of water.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a \nquantity of the powder containing 250 mg of \nhydrochlorothiazide in the solvent mixture, with the aid of \nultrasound for 30 minutes and dilute to 500.0 ml with the solvent\nmixture and filter. Dilute 3.0 ml of the solution to. 100.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.3 per cent \nw/v of triamterene IPRS and 0.15 per cent w/v \nci hydrochlorothiazide IPRS in the solvent mixture. Dilute\n1.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0,225 per cent \nw/v of triamterene IPRS, 0.15 per cent w/v of \nhydrochlorothiazide IPRS and 0.00075 per cent w/v of \nbenzothiadiazine related compound A IPRS in the solvent \nmixture. Dilute 1.0 ml ofthe solution to 100.0 ml with the mobile \nphase.. ■ ■ \n■.■■■■-■ x:\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded.to porous silica (5 pm),.\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 0.82 g of sodium acetate\nTRIBUTYL ÇITRATE\n, .anhydrous in 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH4.5 with\n' \n■ glacial'acetic acicZ and?3 5 volumes ofacetonitrile,\n- flowrate: 1 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 272 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe relative retention time with reference to \nhydrochlorothiazide for benzothiadiazine related compound \nA, is about 0,93. \n: J\nInject reference solution (a) and reference solution (b). The \ntest is not valid unless the resolution between the peaks due \nto hydrochlorothiazide and benzothiadiazine related \ncompound A is not less than 1.5 in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (b) and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate; injections is not more than 2.0 per cent for both \nthe peaks in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a).\nInject reference solution (a.) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram twice the retention time of hydrochlorothiazide \npeak. ... \n-\nCalculate the content of 'Ci2HiiN7 and C7H8C1N3O4S2 in the \ntablets, ; \n■■\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture, at a \ntemperature not exceeding 30°."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamterene-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamterene Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TRIAMTERENE CAPSULES\nB and C at 355 nm is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.1 per cent), the area of any other secondary peak at \n355 nm is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per \ncent) and the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks at 355 \nnm is not more than twice the area of the principal peak in thé\nchromatogram obtained with réference solution (a) (0.2 per \ncent). Ignore any peak at 355 nm with an areà less than 0.5 \ntimes the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.05 per cent).\nSulphated ash (2:3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. \n! ;\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1 .Oper cent, determined \nbn T.O g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.15 g in 5 ml oï anhydrous formic acid, add\n100 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic acid. Titrate with 0.1 M \nperchloric acid, determining the end-point potentiometrically\n(2.4.25). Cany out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02533 g of\nc12hi;n7. ■ ■\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nTriamterene Capsules\nTriamterene Capsules contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntriamterene, C^H^Ny.\nUsual strength. 50 mg. :"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TRIAMTERENE CAPSULES\nB and C at 355 nm is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.1 per cent), the area of any other secondary peak at \n355 nm is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per \ncent) and the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks at 355 \nnm is not more than twice the area of the principal peak in thé\nchromatogram obtained with réference solution (a) (0.2 per \ncent). Ignore any peak at 355 nm with an areà less than 0.5 \ntimes the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.05 per cent).\nSulphated ash (2:3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. \n! ;\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1 .Oper cent, determined \nbn T.O g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.15 g in 5 ml oï anhydrous formic acid, add\n100 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic acid. Titrate with 0.1 M \nperchloric acid, determining the end-point potentiometrically\n(2.4.25). Cany out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02533 g of\nc12hi;n7. ■ ■\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nTriamterene Capsules\nTriamterene Capsules contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntriamterene, C^H^Ny.\nUsual strength. 50 mg. :"},{"name":"Identification","text":"The final solution obtained in the Assay has a bluish \nfluorescence and when examined in the range 250 nm to \n380 nm (2.4.7), shows an absorption maximum at about 360 nm."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2,5.2),\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of 1 per cent w/v solution o£polysorbate 20\nin O.T M acetic acid,\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 120 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, dilute suitably if necessary with \nthe medium, at the maximum at about 357 nm (2.4.7). Calculate \nthe content of Ci^IInN- in the medium from the absorbance \nobtained from a solution of known concentration of triamterene\nTATAS’ in the dissolution medium.\nQ. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of C^HhN?.\n_________ _________ JMfl\nRelated substances. Determine by tiiin-layerchromatopra X \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gelGr — < ; i i\nMobile phase. A mixture of 90 volumes of ethvl n(.ot\n10 volumes of 18 M ammonia and 10 volumes of methanol'\nTest solution. Dissolve a quantity of the contents of \ncapsules containing 0.1 g of Triamterene in sufficient dimethyl \nsulphoxide to produce 20 ml and dilute 2 ml to 50 m|\nmethanol.\nReference solution. Dilute 1 volume of the test solution\n200 volumes with methanol.\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. After ;deyelopm^ \ndry the plate in air until the odour of solvent is no longer \ndetectable and examine under ultraviolet light at365 nm. Any \nsecondary spot in the chromatogram obtained, with the tea \nsolution is not more intense than the spot m me chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution.\nOther tests. Comply with thé tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Weigh a quantity of the contents of 20 capsules \ncontaining about 0.1 g of Triamterene, dissolve in 50 ml of a \nmixture of equal volumes of glacial acetic* acid and iva/er \nwith the aid of gentle heat, cool and add sufficient water to \nproduce 500.0 ml. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with\n1 M acetic acid and measure the absorbance of the resulting \nsolution at the maximum at about 360 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C12HUN7 from the absorbance \nobtained by repeating the operation using triamterene IPRS \nin place of the contents of the capsules.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamterene","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamterene"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"water. Combine the filtrate and washings and add 0.3 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution. Not more than 1.5 ml of 0.01 M\nsodium hydroxide is required to change the colour of the \nsolution. \n.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n■ \n'\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 10.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100,0 ml with the mobile phase. Further dilute l.O.ml of the \nsolution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \nnitrosotriaminopyrimidine IPRS (triamterene impurity A \nIPRS) in the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve a suitable quantity of 2,7- \ndiamino-6-phenylpteridin-4-ol IPRS (triamterene impurity \nB IPRS) in 0.5 ml of the mobile phase with the aid of ultrasound. \nTo this solution, add 0.5 ml of the test solution.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 0.2 volume of butylam ine, 20\nvolumes of acetonitrile, 20 volumes of methanol and \n60 volumes of water, adjusted to pH 5.3 with acetic \nacid,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 320 nm and 355 nm, L ■\n- injection volume: 50 pl.\nThe relative retention time with reference to triamterene'for \ntriamterene impurity Ais about 0.6, for triamterene impurity B \nis about 0.8 and for triamterene impurity C is about. 1,7. ■.\nInject reference solution (b) and (c). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to triamterene impurity \nB and triamterene at 355 nm is not less than 1.5 in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) if \nnecessary, increase the quantity of water in the mobile phase \nand signal-to- noisq ratio for the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) at 320 nm \nis not less than 10. Calculate the contentsuf the impurities in \nthe test solution using the correction factors, 1.8 for triamterene \nimpurity B and 1.5 for triamterene impurity C.\nInject.reference solution (a), (6) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with' the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to triamterene impurity A at 320 nm is not \nmore than the area of the corresponding peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (50 ppm). \nThe area of any peak corresponding to triamterene impurities"},"sections":[{"name":"TRIAMTERENE","text":"water. Combine the filtrate and washings and add 0.3 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution. Not more than 1.5 ml of 0.01 M\nsodium hydroxide is required to change the colour of the \nsolution. \n.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n■ \n'\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 10.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100,0 ml with the mobile phase. Further dilute l.O.ml of the \nsolution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \nnitrosotriaminopyrimidine IPRS (triamterene impurity A \nIPRS) in the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve a suitable quantity of 2,7- \ndiamino-6-phenylpteridin-4-ol IPRS (triamterene impurity \nB IPRS) in 0.5 ml of the mobile phase with the aid of ultrasound. \nTo this solution, add 0.5 ml of the test solution.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 0.2 volume of butylam ine, 20\nvolumes of acetonitrile, 20 volumes of methanol and \n60 volumes of water, adjusted to pH 5.3 with acetic \nacid,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 320 nm and 355 nm, L ■\n- injection volume: 50 pl.\nThe relative retention time with reference to triamterene'for \ntriamterene impurity Ais about 0.6, for triamterene impurity B \nis about 0.8 and for triamterene impurity C is about. 1,7. ■.\nInject reference solution (b) and (c). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to triamterene impurity \nB and triamterene at 355 nm is not less than 1.5 in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) if \nnecessary, increase the quantity of water in the mobile phase \nand signal-to- noisq ratio for the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) at 320 nm \nis not less than 10. Calculate the contentsuf the impurities in \nthe test solution using the correction factors, 1.8 for triamterene \nimpurity B and 1.5 for triamterene impurity C.\nInject.reference solution (a), (6) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with' the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to triamterene impurity A at 320 nm is not \nmore than the area of the corresponding peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (50 ppm). \nThe area of any peak corresponding to triamterene impurities"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tribasic-calcium-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tribasic Calcium Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tribasic Calcium Phosphate\nCalcium Hydroxide Phosphate; Calcium Phosphate\nTribasic Calcium Phosphate consists mainly of triçalcium \ndiorthophosphate, Ca3(Pp4)2, together with calcium \nphosphates of more acidic or, basic character.\nww.webofpharma.com\nwww.webofpharma.com\n................................ lp2022 \n”..... ••••'.... .\nTribasic Calcium Phosphate contains \ncent and not more than 100.5 per cent \ncalculated as Ca3(PO4)2.\nnot less than 90.0iper \nof calcium phosphate^\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (excipient). \n’ ■\nDescription. A white, amorphous powder. \n‘\" ■"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tribasic Calcium Phosphate\nCalcium Hydroxide Phosphate; Calcium Phosphate\nTribasic Calcium Phosphate consists mainly of triçalcium \ndiorthophosphate, Ca3(Pp4)2, together with calcium \nphosphates of more acidic or, basic character.\nww.webofpharma.com\nwww.webofpharma.com\n................................ lp2022 \n”..... ••••'.... .\nTribasic Calcium Phosphate contains \ncent and not more than 100.5 per cent \ncalculated as Ca3(PO4)2.\nnot less than 90.0iper \nof calcium phosphate^\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (excipient). \n’ ■\nDescription. A white, amorphous powder. \n‘\" ■"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Gives reaction (A) of calcium salts Ci.3.1). \n■\nB. Dissolve 0.1 g in a mixture of 5 ml of 2 Mnitric acid and 5nil \nof water; the solution gives reaction C of phosphates (2.3 J )"},{"name":"Tests","text":": ■ S. : ■\nAcid-insoluble substances. Heat 5.0 g with a mixture of40 tri \nof water and 10 ml of hydrochloric acid and dilute to 100 nil \nwith water. Filter, wash with hot water until the last washing \nis free from chloride and dry the residue at: ,105° for 1 hour \n(0.3 percent).\nWater-soluble substances. Digest 2.0 g with 100 ml of water \nfor 30 minutes on a water-bath, cool, add sufficient water to \nrestore the original volume, stir well and filter Evaporate 50 ml \nof the filtrate to dryness and dry the residue at 105® to constant \nweight (0.5 per cent). \np t;;;;\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 2.0 g in a mixture of 15 ml of \nbrominated hydrochloric acid, add 45 nil of water and remove \nthe excess of bromine with a few drops of stghyiausfhloride \nsolution AsT. The resulting solution complies with the limit \ntest for arsenic (5 ppm)-.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Warm 1.0 g with 4 ml of dilute \nhydrochloric acid’, -add sufficient water to produce 50 ml and \nfilter. 25 nil of the solution complies with the'limit test for \nheavy metals, Method A (40 ppm).\nIron (2.3.14). Dissolve 0.2 g in a mixture of5 ml of water and \n0.5 ml of iron free hydrochloric acid with the addition of 1 g \nof citric acid? Dilute the solution to 40 ml with wateriThe \nsolution complies with the limit test for iron (200 ppm).\nCarbonate. Suspend 1 g in 10 ml of water andjad&2-hil hf \nhydrochloric acid; no effervescence is produced. ;!;,u ;\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 0.25 g in 25 ml of waterbythe \naddition of 1 ml of nitric acid. The solution complie^itli \nlimit,test for chlorides (0.1 per cent,). \n.. .,..., if, -\nSulphates (2.3.17). Dissolve 100.0 mg in wa/dr with the.aidof\n3 ml of 1 Mhydrochloric acid and dilute to 60 \n15 ml of the resulting solution complies with the limit test for\nsulphates (0.6 per cent). ■ r- ■' \n■•-••••• ■\nProteinous impurities. Heat 0.5 ggently in a(^ryTeis)>t|l,oe> \nno change in colour is observed and no. unpleasant. \nemitted.' \n--■■ \n■■ \n■ \\\nji igr>ition (2.4.20). Not more than 8.0 per cent, determined \nQg by igniting at 800° for 30 minutes.\n|i)s(2i.3V43): Not more than 2.5 per cent, determined on\nWeigh accurately about 1.0 g and dissolve in 10 ml of \nchloric acid by heating on a water-bath, add 50 ml of \nF'Cbol arid dilute to 250.0 ml with water. To 25.0 ml of the \n[ring solution add 30.0 ml of 0.05 M disodium edetate.\n'of ammonia buffer pH 10.9 and 100 ml of water and \ntithe ¿xcess ofdisodium edetate with 0:05 Mzinc chloride \n-^Ffherdant black II solution as-indicator’-.Garry out the \n|||^ttation. ■\nffmZ&(fO5 Mdisodiiim edetate is equivalent to 6.00517 g of \nWtPO^-\njßüciüm and Vitamin D3 Tablets\n.Qlc.iumand Vitamin D3 Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per\ncenfahd not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount\n”, of calcium (Ca), derived from substances generally recognized\näs safe, and not less than 90.0 per cent of the stated amount of \n^(aiiiiri D, (C27H44O).\nIJsual strengths. Calcium, 250 mg and Vitamin D3, 200 IU; \nCalcium, 500 mg and Vitamin D?„ 200 IU; Calcium, 500 mg and\n: Vitamin D3,250 IU; Calcium, 500 mg and Vitamin D3,500 IU."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. In the Assay, the retention time of principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthe peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution.\nBr. Disperse a quantity of powdered tablets containing 10 mg \nof calcium in 5 ml of water and add 5 ml of \n5 M acetic acid or 1 ml of glacial acetic acid and filter. It \ngives the reaction (A) of calcium salts (2.3.1).\nTests ...\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 75 rpm and 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium arid filter the \nsolution through a filter paper (Whatman No 1), collect the \nfiltrate. Transfer 20 ml of the solution in to 250-ml volumetric \nflask, add 150 ml of water, add 3 ml of 7 M sodium hydroxide\nand 10 ml of ammonia solution: T itrate with 0.05 M disodium \nedetate, using hydroxy napthol blue as an indicator. Carry \n°ut a blank titration.\nCALCIUM AND VITAMIN D3 TABLETS\n1 ml of 0.05 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.0020Ó4 g of\nCa. ■ \n.. Z \"'ZZ-\"fZ'--..Z^- Z\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the statedamountofCa.\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets. \n■ q ■\nDetermine by liquid chrornatography (2.4.14),as described \nunder Assay. \n’’ V'-’!■'\nTest solution. Disperse one tablet in 2.5 ml of wa/erby shaking \nfor 5 minutes. Add about 15 ml of/zzei/zm?«/and sonicate for \n20 minutes with intermittent shaking and dilute to 25.0 ml with \nmethanol, mix and centrifuge the solution at‘4000 rpm for 5 \nminutes and filter. Dilute the filtrate, if necessary, with \nmethanol. \n.y\nReference solution. A 0.000025 per cent w/v solution of \ncholecalciferol IPRS in methanol (90 per cent, v/v). ? :\nUse chromatographic system as described under Assay.\nCalculate the content of C27H440 in the tablet.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. For Calcium — Weigh and powder 20 tablets^ Disperse \na quantity of the powder containing equivalent to 50.0 mg of \ncalcium add 50.0 ml of water and 5 ml oí hydrochloric acid. \nHeat the dispersion gently to boiling and continue to boil for \nabout 2 minutes. Allow to cool and add 50 ml of 0.05M \ndisodium edetate. Neutralise the solution using 2M sodium \nhydroxide, add 10 ml of ammonia buffer pH 10.9 and 50.0 ml \nof water. Titrate the excess of disodium edetate with 0.0 5 M \nzinc chloride using mordant black II solution as indicator.\nCarry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of ft 05 M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0.002004 ,gof \nCa.\nCalculate the content of Ca in the Tablets.\nFor Vitamin D> (Cholecalciferol) —\nNOTE — Use low-actinic glassware throughout the \nprocedure.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a \nquantity of powder equivalent to 0.1 mg of vitamin D3 in 20 ml \nof water by shaking for 5 minutes. Add about 120 ml of \nmethanol and sonicate for 20 minutes with intermittent shaking \nand dilute to 200.0 ml with methanol, mix and centrifuge the \nsolution at 4000 rpm for 5 minutes and filter. Use the filtrate.\nReference solution. A 0.00005 per cent w/v solution of\ncholecalciferol IFRS in methanol (90per cent, v/v). ..\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel.colunm 15 cm; x 4.6 mm, packed with\n; octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n, - mobile phase; .a mixture of 3 volumes of water and 97\nvolumes of methanol,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 264 nm,\n- injection volume: 50 ul. .\nInject the reference solution and the test solution; ? ; ,\nCalculate the content of C27H44O in the Tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the quantity of the active \ningredient in terms of the equivalent amount of calcium; (2) \nthe equivalent number of IU (units) of vitamin D3 per tablet.\nCholecalciferol\nVitamin D3; Colecalciferol\nQ/I-EmO \nMol Wt. 384.6\nCholecalciferol is .(5Z7£X35)-940-secocholesta-5,7,10(19> \ntriene-3-ol.\nCholecalciferol contains hot less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of C27H44O.\nCategory. Vitamin D (antirachitic).\nDescription. White or almost white crystals. It is sensitive to \nair, heat and light. A reversible isomerisation to \nprecholecalciferol may occur in solution, depending on \ntemperature and time."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may be omitted if tests Bt C andD are carried out. Tests\nB, C and D may be omitted if test A is carried out:\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with cholecalciferol \nIPRS or With the reference spectrum of cholecalciferol.\nB. Dissolve 1 mg in 1 ml of1,2-dichloroethane and 4 ml of \nantimony trichloride solution: a yellowish-orange colour is \nproduced.\nC. In the test for 7-Dehydrocholesterol, the principal spot in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds \nto that in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b). '\nww.webofpharma.com\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nCHOLECALCIFEROL\nD. To a solution of about 0.5'mg in. 5 ml oi chloroform \n0.3 ml of acetic anhydride O.i nil sulphuric acid\nshake vigorously; a bright red colour, is produced which ranTih \nchanges through violet and blue to green.\nTests ■\nSpecific optical rotation,(2,4.22). +105° to f 112°, determined \nwithin 30 minutes of preparation, in a solution prepared bv \ndissolving 0;2 g.rapidly and without heating in sufficient \naldehyde-free ethanol (95 per cent) to produce 25.0 ml\nLight absorption. Dissolve 10 mg, rapidly and without heating,\nin sufficient aldehyde-free ethanol (95 per cent) to produce\n100.0 ml. Dilute 5.0ml ofthe solution to 50.0 ml with aldehyde-free \nethanol (95 per cent). Absorbance of the resulting solution \nat the maximum at about 265 nm, measured within 30 minutes \nof preparation, 0.46 to 0.50 (2.4.7).\n7-Dehydrochqlesterol. Determine by thin-layer \nchromatography (2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel G.\nMobile phase. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of butylated \nhydroxytoluene in a mixture of equal volumes of cyclohexane \nand peroxide-free ether.\nNOTE— Prepare the following solutions immediately before use.\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.25 g of the substance under \nexamination in sufficient of 1,2-dichloroethanecontaining\n1 per cent w/v of squalane and 0.01 per cent w/v of butylated \nhydroxytoluene (solvent A) to produce 5.0 ml. s '\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.005 percent \nw/v of 7-dehydrocholesterol IPRS in solvent A,\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 2.5 per cent \nw/v of cholecalciferol IPRS in solvent A. \nA,.3c\nReference solution (c). Mix equal volumes of reference \nsolutions (a) and (b).\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. Develop the \nchromatograms immediately, protected from light. After \ndevelopment, dry the plate in air and spray three times with \nantimony trichloride reagent. Examine the chromatograms \nfor not more than 4 minutes after spraying. The principal spot \nin the chromatogram obtained with the test solution is initially \norange-yellow but becomes brown later. In the chrdmatograffl \nobtained with the test solution any violet spot with fril Rr \nvalue slightly lower than that of the principal spot (due to\n7-dehydrocholesterol and appearing slowly) is not more intense \nthan the spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a). The test is not valid unless the qhrpmajpgra® \nobtained with reference solution (c) shows two ciearly\nseparated principal spots.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). .■ r \nNOTE—Carry out the following procedure -as fapialf ^ \npossible in subdued light and protected from air. \"‘’\n..—..-..—...-.... -.... -..-___\n••¿^/^yei^accuratelyabout50.Qmg ofthe substance \n^J'eifeliination, dissolve in 10 ml of toluene without heating \n^^^^¡PQ.O with the mobile phase; dilute 5.0 ml o f\nto ,50.0 ml with the mobile phase; further .dilute"},{"name":"Identification","text":"solution to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\n^*ss0^ve 50.0 mg of cholecalciferol \niniofri' of toluene without heating and dilute to 100.0 ml \n^¿i^abbile phase; dilute 5.0 ml ofthe solution to 50.0 ml \n^.^anobile phase (Solution A); further dilute 5.0 ml of \nsbliitibnlAtb 5.0.0 ml with the mobile phase."},{"name":"Tests","text":"5.0 ml of solution A, under \nwater-bath for 60 minutes to obtain a solution\nOfchoiecalciferol, precholecalciferol and rraws-cholecalciferol.\nChromatographic system\n'...'‘^Xa/Stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \ng|i|oft>ussilicaparticles(5 pm) (SuchasNucleosil 50-S),\n-^ mobile phase: a mixture of 997 volumes of hexane and \n^Swolumes of 1-pentanol,\n-7 flow rate: 2 ml per minute,;\n^spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n. gUliijbction volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (b) and record the chromatogram \nadjusting the sensitivity so that the height of the peak due to \ncholecalciferol is more than 50 per cent of hill-scale deflection. \nTheapproximate relative retention times calculated with \nreference to.cholecalciferol are 0.4 for precholecalciferol and \n0.5 for cholecalciferol. The resolution between \nprecholecalciferol and tra«s-cholecalciferol should be not less \nthan. 1,0; if necessary adjust the proportions of the \nconstituents and flow rate of the mobile phase to obtain the \nrequired resolution.\nInject reference solution (a) and record the chromatogram \nadjusting; the sensitivity so that the height of the peak due to \ncholecalciferol is more than 50 per cent of full-scale deflection.\nInject the test solution. Measure the areas for the major peaks.\nCalculate the content of C27H44O.\nStorage: Store protected from light in hermetically sealed \ncontainers under nitrogen in a refrigerator. The contents of an \nopened container should be used immediately."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tribulus-terrestris","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tribulus terrestris"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tribulus terrestris\nCoffea arabicu\nGreen Coffee Bean Extract is obtained by extracting green \ncoffee bean (Coffea arahica Linn. Gaertner Fam. Asteraceae) \nwith methanol or any other suitable solvent.\n. \t ,\n. \n \n \n' \n8 iiie;\n'\nGreen Coffee Bean Extract contains not less than 4.0 per cent \nw/w of chlorogenic acid, calculated on the dried basis.\nI\n."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tribulus terrestris\nCoffea arabicu\nGreen Coffee Bean Extract is obtained by extracting green \ncoffee bean (Coffea arahica Linn. Gaertner Fam. Asteraceae) \nwith methanol or any other suitable solvent.\n. \t ,\n. \n \n \n' \n8 iiie;\n'\nGreen Coffee Bean Extract contains not less than 4.0 per cent \nw/w of chlorogenic acid, calculated on the dried basis.\nI\n."},{"name":"Tests","text":"/ 'i  '. -  \"Or •W'7. \t\nl r \n6\n'\nCategory. Central Nervous System Stimulant.\nForeign organic matter (2.6.1). Not more than 2.0 per cent. \nEthanol-soluble extractive (2.6.2). Not less than 3.0 per cent. \nWater-sol u ble extractive (2.6.3). Not less than 15.0 per cent \nby Method I.\nDescript ion. A greenish brown dry powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"1 \nA. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254. \nAcid-insoluble ash (2.3.19). Not more than 1,0 per cent. \t\n/ ,M/3/M.-phase A mixture of 10 volumes of ethyl acetate, 2.5 \nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with thejimit testfor volutties of dich/oromethane, 1 volume of formic acid, \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). \t\n1 volume of ace ir acid and 1.1 volume of water\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 11.0 per cent.\nGokhru consists of dried fruits of Tribulus terrestris L.\nZyg.ophyllaceae).\nNze,\nTest solution. Dissolve about 500 mg of the extract under \nexamination in methanol, sonicate for 5 minutes and dilute to \n50 ml with same solvent and filter.\nReference solution. Dissolve 50 mg of green coffee bean \nextract RS in 10 ml of methanol, and filter.\nApply to the plate 10 µl of each solution as bands 10 mm by \n2 mm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air \nand spray with anisaldehyde sulphuric acid reagent. Heat \nthe plate at 110° for 10 minutes and examine the plate under \nultraviolet light at 366 nm and day light. The chromatographic \nprofile of the test solution is similar to that of the reference \nsolution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Total ash (2.3.19). Not more than 10.0 per cent . \nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 12.0 per cent, \ndetermined on 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). \nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination test. \nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve about 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 25 ml of methanol then sonicate for 5 minutes \nand dilute to 50.0 ml with same solvent, and filter.\nReference solution. Dissolve about 10 mg chlorogenic acid\nRS in 50 ml of methanol, sonicate for 5 minutes, dilute 5.0 ml \nof this solution to 25.0 ml with same solvent, and filter.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 250 cm x 4.0 mm packed with \noctadecylsilane bound to porous silica (5 .tm), \n-\nmobile phase: A. 0.1 per cent v/v trifluoroacetic acid \nin water,\nB. 0.1 per cent v/v trifluoroacetic acid in \nacetonitrile, \n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 340 nm, \ninjection volume: 20\nTime \t\nmobile phase A mobile phase B \n(in min.) \t\n(per cent v/v) \t\n(per cent v/v)\n0.02 \t90 \t\n10\n21.0 \t73 \t\n27\n26.0 \t60 \t\n40\n31.0 \t90 \t\n10\n40.0 \t90 \t\n10\nInject the reference solution. The test is not-yOrd unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate in* .tions is not\nmore than 2.0 per cent.\nGREEN COFFEE BEAN EXTRACT \nGUDMAR\neavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCategory. Antidiabetic, febrifuge, antiinflammatory, diuretic, \nmeha.\nvy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nCalculate the content of chlorogenic acid. \t\n).ncl\nProtein. Not more than 5.0 per cent, determined by the \nfollowing method. Carry out the determination of nitrogen \n(2.3.30), using about 3.5 g, accurately weighed, and multiplying \nthe percentage of nitrogen determined by 6.25 to obtain the \npercentage of protein.\nDescription. Greenish-yellow in colour, surface pubescent \non both sides and characteristic odour with extremely bitter \nand acrid taste.\nStorage. Store protected from heat, moisture and against a \nby insects and rodents."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tributyl-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tributyl Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tributyl Citrate\no\n- \n<^sO-CH2CH2CH2CH3\nho-c^o-ch2ch2ch2ch3\n1^o~ch2ch2ch2ch3\no\nCUH32O7 \nMol.Wt.360.5\nTributyl Citrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent of \nCïsH32O7, calculated on thé anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Excipient ."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tributyl Citrate\no\n- \n<^sO-CH2CH2CH2CH3\nho-c^o-ch2ch2ch2ch3\n1^o~ch2ch2ch2ch3\no\nCUH32O7 \nMol.Wt.360.5\nTributyl Citrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent of \nCïsH32O7, calculated on thé anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Excipient ."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tributyl citrate \nIPRSoy with the reference spectrum of tributyl citrate.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution? ;"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Weight per ml (2.4.29). 1.037 to 1.045.\nRefractive index (2.4.27). 1.443 to 1.445.\nAcidity. Dissolve 32.0 g in 30 ml of ethanol (95 percent \npreviously neutralised to bromothymol blue, ^^ bromothyn^\nblue solution and titrate with 0.1 Msodium hydroxide^ \nfaint blue endpoint; not more than 1.0 ml is required.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.2 per cent.\nheavy metals, Method B (10 ppm). ;!-\nHeavy metals (2,3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test for\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. Dissolve about ,300 mg of substance under \nexamination in 10 ml of toluene*\nReference solution. A solution containing 3.0 per cent w/v of \ntributyl citrate 1PRS and acetyltributyl citrate IPRS \ntoluene, ■ \n■ \n- '\nChromatographic system\n- a glass or stainless steel column 30 m x 0.32 mm, packed\nwith bonded with a 0.5-pm layer of phase G42, ?\n- temperature:\ncolumn. 80° to 220° from 0.5 to 10 minutes, f ;\ninlet port. 85° to 250°?frpm 0.5:to 10 minutes (increased \n@ 20° per minute) and detector 275°,\n- a flame ionisation detector,\n- flow rate: 2.3 ml per minute of the nitrogenas the carrier\ngas. . '. \n/ \n.\nInject 1 pl of the reference solution. The test is hot valid unless \nthe relative retention times with reference to acetyltributyl \ncitrate for tributyl citrate is about 0.9, the resolution Between \nthe peaks corresponding to tributyl citrate and acetyltribuiyl \ncitrate is not less than 1.5 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0; per cent,\nInject 1 pl of the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content Ci8H32O7. \n, ? ■ < \n: : - ?■\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tributyl-orthophosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tributyl Orthophosphate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"; Tributyl Orthophosphate:\n[CH3(CH2)3hP04= 266.32\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nClear, colourless liquid; refractive index at 20°, about 1.425;\nwt.perml, about 0.977 g.\nIt should be washed 3 times before use with one-sixth of its\nvolume of a solution containing 10 per cent w/v sodium\nchloride and 1.3 percent w/v ofsodium dihydrogen phosphate.\nTricine; N-[2-Hydroxy-1,1-bis(hydroxymethyl)ethyl]glycine:\nC~13NOs= 179.2.\nUse electrophoresis-grade reagent; mp, about 183°\nTrichioroaceticAcid: ChCOOH= 163.40\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless, very deliquescent crystals or crystalline masses;\nodour, slight or pungent and characteristic; mp, about 56°.\nStore protected from light.\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS\nTrichioroaceticAcid Solution: Dissolve 10 g oftrichloroacetic\nacid in sufficient water to produce 100 ml.\n1,1,1-Trichloroethane; Methyl Chloroform: CH3CCl3= 133.42\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless, heavy liquid; bp, about 74°; wt. perml, about 1.32g.\nTriethanolamine: N(CH2CH20H)3 = 149.18\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nViscous, very hygroscopic, colourless liquid; odour, slightly\nammoniacal; becomes brown on exposure to air and light;\nrefractive index at 20 0, about 1.484; wt.per ml, about 1.13 g.\nStore protected from light.\nTriethylamine; N,N-Diethylethanarnine: (C2Hs)3N = 101.19\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nColourless liquid; odour, strongly ammoniacal; bp, about 90°;\nrefractive index at 20°, about 1.401; wt.per ml, about 0.73g.\nStore protected from moisture.\nTriethylenediamine; 1,4-Diazobicyclo[2.2.2]octane: C6H12N2\n= 112.17\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nHygroscopic crystals; sublimes readily at room temperature;\nmp, about 158°; bp, about 174°\nStore in tightly-closed containers.\nTrifluoroacetic acid: C2HF30 2= 114.0\nContent. Minium 99 per cent ofC2HF30 2.\nLiquid, miscible with acetone and with alcohol; wt. per ml,\nabout 1.53g; bp, about 72°.\nUse a grade suitable for protein sequencing.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTrimethylchlorosilane; Chlorotrimethylsilane: (CH3)3SiCI =\n108.65\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nClear, colourless liquid; bp, about 57°; refractive index at 20°,\nabout 1.460; wt.per ml, about 0.86 g\n2,2,4-Trimethylpentane; Iso-octane(CH3)3CCH2CH(CH)3 =\n114,23\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nColourless, liquid; flammable; bp, about 99°; refractive index\nat 20°, about 1.392; wt.per ml, about 0.694 g.\n2,2,4-Trimethylpentane intended for use in spectrophotometry\ncomplies with following additional test."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"; Tributyl Orthophosphate:\n[CH3(CH2)3hP04= 266.32\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nClear, colourless liquid; refractive index at 20°, about 1.425;\nwt.perml, about 0.977 g.\nIt should be washed 3 times before use with one-sixth of its\nvolume of a solution containing 10 per cent w/v sodium\nchloride and 1.3 percent w/v ofsodium dihydrogen phosphate.\nTricine; N-[2-Hydroxy-1,1-bis(hydroxymethyl)ethyl]glycine:\nC~13NOs= 179.2.\nUse electrophoresis-grade reagent; mp, about 183°\nTrichioroaceticAcid: ChCOOH= 163.40\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless, very deliquescent crystals or crystalline masses;\nodour, slight or pungent and characteristic; mp, about 56°.\nStore protected from light.\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS\nTrichioroaceticAcid Solution: Dissolve 10 g oftrichloroacetic\nacid in sufficient water to produce 100 ml.\n1,1,1-Trichloroethane; Methyl Chloroform: CH3CCl3= 133.42\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless, heavy liquid; bp, about 74°; wt. perml, about 1.32g.\nTriethanolamine: N(CH2CH20H)3 = 149.18\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nViscous, very hygroscopic, colourless liquid; odour, slightly\nammoniacal; becomes brown on exposure to air and light;\nrefractive index at 20 0, about 1.484; wt.per ml, about 1.13 g.\nStore protected from light.\nTriethylamine; N,N-Diethylethanarnine: (C2Hs)3N = 101.19\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nColourless liquid; odour, strongly ammoniacal; bp, about 90°;\nrefractive index at 20°, about 1.401; wt.per ml, about 0.73g.\nStore protected from moisture.\nTriethylenediamine; 1,4-Diazobicyclo[2.2.2]octane: C6H12N2\n= 112.17\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nHygroscopic crystals; sublimes readily at room temperature;\nmp, about 158°; bp, about 174°\nStore in tightly-closed containers.\nTrifluoroacetic acid: C2HF30 2= 114.0\nContent. Minium 99 per cent ofC2HF30 2.\nLiquid, miscible with acetone and with alcohol; wt. per ml,\nabout 1.53g; bp, about 72°.\nUse a grade suitable for protein sequencing.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTrimethylchlorosilane; Chlorotrimethylsilane: (CH3)3SiCI =\n108.65\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nClear, colourless liquid; bp, about 57°; refractive index at 20°,\nabout 1.460; wt.per ml, about 0.86 g\n2,2,4-Trimethylpentane; Iso-octane(CH3)3CCH2CH(CH)3 =\n114,23\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade ofcommerce.\nColourless, liquid; flammable; bp, about 99°; refractive index\nat 20°, about 1.392; wt.per ml, about 0.694 g.\n2,2,4-Trimethylpentane intended for use in spectrophotometry\ncomplies with following additional test."},{"name":"TRANSMITTANCE","text":"Not less than 98 per cent between 250\nand 420 nm using water as the blank.\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS\nTriphenylamine: C1sH1SN=245.32\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite, crystalline solid; mp, about 126°."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trichloroacetic-acid-reagent","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trichloroacetic acid reagent"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trichloroacetic acid reagent.\nDissolve 18 gm of\ntrichloroacetic Acid, 30 gm of anhydrous sodium acetate\nand 20 ml ofglacial acetic acid in 1000 ml ofwater.\nNOTE-TCA should be in the crystalform and not in liquid\nform as this will effect the quantity weighed and thereby\ninterfere with the complete precipitation of casein.\nUse\nSodium acetate anhydrous because if, sodium acetate\ntrihydrate should be used, the trihydrate forms gives lower\nresults.\nIP 2010"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trichloroacetic acid reagent.\nDissolve 18 gm of\ntrichloroacetic Acid, 30 gm of anhydrous sodium acetate\nand 20 ml ofglacial acetic acid in 1000 ml ofwater.\nNOTE-TCA should be in the crystalform and not in liquid\nform as this will effect the quantity weighed and thereby\ninterfere with the complete precipitation of casein.\nUse\nSodium acetate anhydrous because if, sodium acetate\ntrihydrate should be used, the trihydrate forms gives lower\nresults.\nIP 2010"},{"name":"COLLOIDAL SILICON DIOXIDE","text":"Diluted Folin 's Ciocalteau Reagent. Dilute 1 ml of Fo1in's\nCiocalteau reagentwith 2 ml ofwaterto get 3 ml ofthe reagent.\nNOTE-Folin's Ciocalteau reagent should be ofthe protein\nestimation grade andnot the indicatorgrade. Folin 's reagent\n. should be diluted to just before addition to the tubes since it\nis sensitive to light.\nDo not prepare this solution at the\nbeginning ofthe assay.\nTyrosine reference solution. Dissolve 160 mg of tyrosine in\n100 ml of0.2 M hydrochloric acid.\nDilute 1 ml ofthis solution to 100 ml with 0.2 Mhydrochloric\nacid. Use 2 ml ofthis solution for colour development.\nTrisodium phosphate bufferpH 6.8. Weigh 19 g oftrisodium\nphosphate and 6.4 ml ofhydrochloric acidin 1000 ml ofwater,\nadjusted to pH 6.8.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity\nofpowder containing about 120 mg ofSerratiopeptidase with\n100 ml oftrisodium phosphate btifferpH6.8. Stir on magnetic\nstirrer for 1hour. Further dilute 5.0 ml to 25 ml with 5.0percent\nammonium sulphate solution,\nstir for 5 minutes and filter.\nDilute 2 ml of the filtrate to 100 ml with sodium tetraborate\nbtiffer. 1 ml ofresulting solution is used for arialysis. Set the\nwater bath to 37°.\nFor each sample, keep four test tubes. Mark two test tubes as\nTEST and two as BLANK. Add 5 ml ofCasein Su,bstrate to the\ntubes marked as TEST and 5 ml. ofTCA to the testtube marked\nas BLANK. Prewarm these tubes for five minutes along with\nthe tubes containing the sample dilutions. Add 1ml ofenzyme\nsolution to all these tubes and vertex the tubes for exactly 10\nseconds. Keep the test tubes in the water-bath at 37° for 20\nminutes for the reaction. After exactly 20 minutes, add 5 ml of\nTCA to tubes marked as TEST and 5 ml ofCasein substrate to\nthe tube marked as BLANK. Vertex all the tubes exactly for 30\nseconds. Keep the tubes in the water-bath at 37° for 30 minutes\nfor precipitation, filter.\nNOTE- For tablets, the settlement may take a longer time\nthan 30 minutes. Sofilter the solution after the settlement is\nclearly seen (Vertex in between for better precipitation).\nMark tubes as TEST, BLANK, TYROSINE and TYROSINE\nBLANK.\nAdd the following respectively;\nTEST - 2 m1 ofthe TEST filtrate.\nBLANK- 2 ml ofBLANKfiltrate.\nTYROSINE - 2 ml ofTYROSINE standard solution.\nTYROSINE BLANK- 0.2 M hydrochloric acid.\nAdd 5 ml of6 per cent sodium carbonate solution to all tubes.\nAdd 1 ml ofdiluted Folin's reagent to all the tubes. Keep the\ntubes at 37° for 30 minutes for colour development. Measure\nthe absorbance at about 660 nm (2.4.7).\nSerratiopeptidase Units/Tablets = AI - A 2 x 176 x1-x\nA 3 -A 4\n20\n100\n25\n100\n.\n----x - x- x Avg.wt.mgm\nSpI.Wt.(g)\n5\n2\n% of LA = Serratiopeptidase Units/Tablet x 100\nL.A.\nConversion Co-efficient of Tyrosine to\nSerratiopeptidase\nReaction time [minutes]\nAbsorbance of test solution\nAbsorbance of blank solution\nAbsorbance of tyrosine reference solution.\nAbsorbance ofO.2M hydrochloric acid\n20000 Units/tablet.\nWhere,\n176\n20\nAI\nA2\nA3\nA 4\nL.A. =\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not\nexceeding 25°.\nLabeling. The label states the strength in terms of the\nequivalent fo Enzyme activity units ofSerratiopeptidase."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trichloroacetic-acid-solution-dissolve","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trichloroacetic Acid Solution: Dissolve"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trichloroacetic Acid Solution: Dissolve \ngof trichloroacetic\nacid in sufficient water to produce 100 ml.\n1,1,1 -Trichloroethane: Methyl Chloroform: CH3CC13= 133.42\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless, heavy liquid; bp, about 74°; wt. per ml, about 1.32 g.\nTricosane: C23H48 = 324.6\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite crystals, practically insoluble in water, soluble in\nhexane, mp, about, 48°.\nTriethanolamine: N(CH2CH2OH)3 = 149.18\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nViscous, very hygroscopic, colourless liquid; odour, slightly \nammoniacal; becomes brown on exposure to air and light; \nrefractive index at 20°, about 1.484; wt. per ml, about 1.13 g.\nStore protected from light.\nTriethylamine; M/V-Diethylethanamine: (QH^N = 101.19\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless liquid; odour, strongly ammoniacal; bp, about 90°; \nrefractive index at 20°, about 1.401; wt.per ml, about 0.73 g.\nStore protected from moisture. \n7 . .\nTriethylamine Hydrochloride: QH15N, IICI-137.65\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite to off white powder.\nmp, about 256° with decomposition.\nTriethylenediamine; 1,4-Diazobicyclo[2.2.2]octane: C6Hi2N2 \n-112.17\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nHygroscopic crystals; sublimes readily at room temperature; \nmp, about 158°; bp, about 174°\nStore in tightly-closed containers.\nTrifluoroacetic acid: C2HF3O2 = 114.0\nContent. Minium 99 per cent of C2HF3O2.\nLiquid, miscible with acetone and with alcohol', wt. per ml, \nabout 1.53g; bp, about 72°.\nUse a grade suitable for protein sequencing.\nStore protected from moisture.\nTrifluoroacetic Anhydride: C4F6O3=210.0\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nA colourless liquid; d™ , about 1.5."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Trichloroacetic Acid Solution: Dissolve \ngof trichloroacetic\nacid in sufficient water to produce 100 ml.\n1,1,1 -Trichloroethane: Methyl Chloroform: CH3CC13= 133.42\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless, heavy liquid; bp, about 74°; wt. per ml, about 1.32 g.\nTricosane: C23H48 = 324.6\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite crystals, practically insoluble in water, soluble in\nhexane, mp, about, 48°.\nTriethanolamine: N(CH2CH2OH)3 = 149.18\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nViscous, very hygroscopic, colourless liquid; odour, slightly \nammoniacal; becomes brown on exposure to air and light; \nrefractive index at 20°, about 1.484; wt. per ml, about 1.13 g.\nStore protected from light.\nTriethylamine; M/V-Diethylethanamine: (QH^N = 101.19\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless liquid; odour, strongly ammoniacal; bp, about 90°; \nrefractive index at 20°, about 1.401; wt.per ml, about 0.73 g.\nStore protected from moisture. \n7 . .\nTriethylamine Hydrochloride: QH15N, IICI-137.65\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite to off white powder.\nmp, about 256° with decomposition.\nTriethylenediamine; 1,4-Diazobicyclo[2.2.2]octane: C6Hi2N2 \n-112.17\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nHygroscopic crystals; sublimes readily at room temperature; \nmp, about 158°; bp, about 174°\nStore in tightly-closed containers.\nTrifluoroacetic acid: C2HF3O2 = 114.0\nContent. Minium 99 per cent of C2HF3O2.\nLiquid, miscible with acetone and with alcohol', wt. per ml, \nabout 1.53g; bp, about 72°.\nUse a grade suitable for protein sequencing.\nStore protected from moisture.\nTrifluoroacetic Anhydride: C4F6O3=210.0\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nA colourless liquid; d™ , about 1.5."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trichloromonofluoromethane","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trichloromonofluoromethane"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TRICHLOROMONOFLUOROMETHANE \nTRIAMTERENE"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TRICHLOROMONOFLUOROMETHANE \nTRIAMTERENE"},{"name":"Tests","text":"category. Excipient.\nTrichloromonotluoromethane\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography"},{"name":"Identification","text":"(2.4.17), coating the plate with \nsilica gel G.\nCI\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tributyl citrate\nMobile phase. A mixture of 90 volumes of ethyl acetate,\nCI - C - F\n10 volumes of 18 M ammonia and 10 volumes of methanol.\nCl\nRS or with the reference spectrum of tributyl citrate.\nTest solution. Dissolve a quantity of the contents of the \ncapsules containing 0.1 g of Triamterene in sufficient dimethyl \nsulphoxide to produce 20 ml and dilute 2 ml to 50 ml with \nmethanol.\n13. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nCC13F \t\nMol. Wt. 137.4\nTrichloromonofluoromethane contains not less than 99.6 per \ncent and not more than 100.0 per cent of CC1 3F, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nReference solution. Dilute 1 volume of the test solution to \n200 volumes with methanol."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Category. Pharmaceutical aid (Excipient).\nWeight per ml (2.4.29). 1.037 to 1.045.\nApply to the plate 5 .tl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air until the odour of solvent is no longer \ndetectable and examine under ultraviolet light at 365 nm. Any \nsecondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution is not more intense than the spot in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Refractive index (2.4.27). 1.443 to 1.445.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6), \ncompare the spectrum with the obtained with \ntrichloromonoflummethune RS or with the reference spectrum \nof trichloromonofluromethane.\nAcidity. Dissolve 32.0 g in 30 ml of ethanol (95 per cent), \npreviously neutralised to bromothymol blue, add bromothvmol \nblue solution and titrate with 0.1 Al sodium hydroxide to a \nfaint blue endpoint; not more than 1.0 ml is required.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules."},{"name":"Tests","text":"mim• \nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.2 per cent.\nAssay. Weigh a quantity of the contents of 20  capsules \ncontaining about 0.1 g of Triamterene, dissolve in 50 ml of a \nmixture of equal volumes of glacial acetic acid  and water \nwith the aid of gentle heat, cool and add sufficient water to \nproduce 500.0 ml. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to 100.0 ml with \n1 M acetic acid and measure the absorbance of the resulting \nsolution at the maximum at about 360 nm (2.4.7).\nBoiling temperature. Transfer a 100 ml sample at about 24° to \na tared, pear-shaped, 100-m1 centrifuge tube containing a few \nboiling stones, and weigh. Suspend a thermometer in the liquid, \nand place the tube in a medium maintained at a temperature of \n32° above the expected boiling temperature. When the \nthermometer reading becomes constant, record as the boiling \ntemperature the thermometer reading after at least 5per cent of \nthe substance under examination has distilled. Retain the \nremainder of the substance for the determination of High-\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (10 ppm).\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. Dissolve about 300 mg of substance under \nexamination in 10 ml of toluene.\nReference solution. A solution containing 3.0 per cent w/v of \ntributyl citrate RS and acetvltributvl citrate RS in toluene.\nCalculate the content of C l2F1 1 IN, from the absorbance \nobtained by repeating the operation using triamterene RS in \nplace of the contents of the capsules.\nBoiling Residues.\nChromatographic system\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.001 per cent (Method 3) with \nthe following modifications (a) provide the closed-system \ntitrating vessel with an opening through which passes a \ncoarse-porosity gas dispersion tube connected to a sample \ncylinder; (b) dilute the reagent with anhydrous methanol to \ngive a water equivalence factor of between 0.2 and 1.0 mg per \nml, age this diluted solution for not less than 16 hours before \nsanitation; (c) obtain a 100 g sample as directed under inhalation \npreparation, and introduce the same into the titration vessel \nthrough the gas dispersion tube at a rate of about 100 ml of \ngas per minute.\n-\na glass or stainless steel column 30 m x 0.32 mm, packed \n.to  with bonded with a 0.5-pm layer of phase G42,\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\ntemperature: \ncolumn. 80° to 220° from 0.5 to 10 minutes,\ninlet port. 85° to 250° from 0.5 to 10 minutes (increased \n@ 20° per minute) and detector 275°. \n-\na flame ionisation detector, \n-\nflow rate: 2.3 ml per minute of the nitrogen as the carrier \ngas.\nTributl Citrate\nO 0 C H2 CH 2C H 2C H3\nInject 1 pi of the reference solution. The test is not valid unless \nthe relative retention times with reference to acetyltributyl \ncitrate for tributyl citrate is about 0.9, the resolution between \nthe peaks corresponding to tributyl citrate and acetyltributyl \ncitrate is not less than 1.5 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nHO - C)(0 C H 2 CH 2 CH 2C H 3\nHigh-boiling residues. Not more than 0.01 per cent, Allow \n85 ml of the sample to distill as directed in the test for \nApproximate Boiling Temperature, and transfer the centrifuge \ntube containing the remaining 15 ml of substance to a medium \nmaintained at a temperature 10° above the boiling temperature. \nAfter 30 minutes, remove the tube from the water-bath, blot \ndry, and weigh,. Calculate the weight of the residue.\n- CH2CH 2CH 2CH 3\nO\nInject I ul of the reference solution and the test solution.\nMol. Wt. 360.5\nCalculate the content C 181-1 3207.\nInorganic chlorides. Place 5 ml of anhydrous methanol in a\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\ntest tube, add 3 drops of a saturated solution of silver nitrate\nt.\nin anhydrous methanol, shake, and add 7 g of \nTrichloromonofluoromethane; no opalescence or turbidity is \nproduced.\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. Introduce the liquid phase of Trichloromono-\nfluoromethane into an evacuated headspace vial\nReference solution. \nIntroduce a liquid-phase mixture of \ndichlorodifluoromethane, dichlorotetrafluoroethane, and \ntrichloromonofluoromethane into an evacuated headspace vial.\nChromatographic system\n-\na steel column 1.8 m x 2 mm, packed with support S12, \ncontaining 1 per cent phase G25, \n-\ntemperature:\ncolumn 70° to 170° @10° per minute and 170° for 5 \nminutes, \ninlet port at 110° and detector at 200°, \na flame ionisation detector, \n-\nflow rate: 20 ml per minute of the nitrogen as carrier gas \nHead-space conditions which may be used:\n-\nbath temperature 100°, \n-\nvalve/loop temperature 105 0, \n-\nsampling time is 3 seconds. \nl'he relative retention time with reference to \ntrichloromonofluoromethane for dichlorodifluoromethane is \nabout 0.5 and for dichlorotetrafluoroethane is about 0.8.\nInject the reference solution. The sum of these two peak areas \nis not more than 0.2 per cent of the total of all peak areas and \nthe sum of the areas of all peaks other than that for \ntrichloromonofluoromethane is not more than 0.4 per cent of \nthe total of all peak areas.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. The test is \nnot valid unless the resolution between the peaks due \ndichlorotetrafluoroethane and trichloromonofluoromethane is \nnot less than 2.0.\nCalculate the content of CC1 3F.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture and prevent exposure \nto excessive heat."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triclofos-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triclofos Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triclofos Sodium\nCI3C O\" xONa\nC2H3CI3NaO4P \nMol.Wt.251.4\nTriclofos Sodium is sodium 2,2,2-trichloroethyl hydrogen \northophosphate.\nTriclofos Sodium contains not less than 41:3 per cent and not \nmore than 43.2 per cent of Cl and not less than 97.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C2H3C13NaO4P, both \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; hygroscopic.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the; spectrum with that obtained with triclofos sodium \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of triclofos sodium.\nB. It gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\n3863\nwww.webofpharma.com\nC, Heat 0.1g with 1 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate to a \ndull red heat and maintain for 10 minutes, cool, extract the \nresidue with water and filter. The filtrate gives the reactions\nof chlorides (2.3 .Hand of phosphates Í2.3.T). ;\nTests •. \n„..i.\npH (2.4.24).3.0 to 4.5, determined ona2.0 per centw/v solution.\nAppearance of solution. A 2.0,per cent w/y solution is clear \n(2.4.1). \n. ;? ,7;\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 0.5, g in 50 ml of water. 15 ml of \nthe solution complies with the limit test for chlorides (0.17 per \ncent). \n,\nPhosphates. Not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated as PO4, \nwhen determined by dissolving 25 mg in 10. ml of water, add \n4 ml of 1 M sulphuric acid, 1 ml of ammonium molybdate \nsolution and 2 ml of methylaminophenol-sulphite reagent\nand allow to stand for 15 minutes and dilute to 25 ml with \nwater, allow to stand for a further 15 minutes and measure the \nabsorbance of the solution at 730 nm (2.4.7): Calculate the \ncontent of phosphate from a calibration curve prepared by \ntreating suitable volumes of a 0.00143 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium dihydrogen orthophosphate in the same manner.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 5.0 percent, determined \non 1 g by drying at 100°, at a pressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa \nfor 3 hours.\nAssay. For Cl — Mix 0.25 g with 1 g bi anhydrous sodium \ncarbonate in a nickel crucible about 3 cm in diameter, fill the \ncrucible completely with anhydrous sodium carbonate and \ninvert into a nickel crucible about 4 cm in diameter; cover the \nsmaller crucible with anhydrous sodium carbonate, well \npressed down, using 25 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate in\nall. Heat for 30 minutes at a dull red heat, cool, transfer to a \n400 ml beaker, add 150 ml of water and boil gently for \n10 minutes. Filter through absorbent cotton into a 600 ml beaker, \nwashing the residue with hot water, until 400 ml of filtrate has \nbeen collected. Cool, cautiously add nitric acid until the \nsolution is neutral to litmus paper and add 3 ml of nitric acid \nin excess. Add 50 ml of 0.1 M silver nitrate, allow to stand \nuntil precipitation is complete, filter, wash the precipitate with \nwater and titrate the combined filtrate and washings with \n0.1 M ammonium thiocyanate using ammonium iron(III) \nsulphate solution as indicator.\n1 ml of 0.1M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545 g of Cl.\nFor CdCCliNaO^P—Heat 0.2 g in a Kjeldahl flask with 2 ml \nof sulphuric dcid; and 2.5 ml of nitric acid until brown fumes \ncease to be evolved, cool, add 1 ml of nitric acid and heat \nagain. Continue adding nitric acid and heating until brown\n3864 \nMmBmIWI!\nTRICLOFOS SODIUM\n^2022\nTRIETHYL CITRATE\nfumes are no longer evolved and the solution is colour} \nwhen cold. Heat until dense, white fumes are evolved e \ntransfer the solution to a flask with the aid of 150 ml of\n,Q^iFf sulphuric acid to the combined aqueous layer \nthings, extract with four 10-ml quantities of isoamyl \nihol$0^ cac^ extract t0 stand until good separation \nf y ^^ed,combine the isoamyl alcohol extracts, remove\nB. To 0.5 ml add 5 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and 4 ml of \ndilute sodium hydroxide solution. Boil under reflux for about\n■■■■ Í\nadd 50 ml of citric-molybdic acid solution and heat slowlv ’’ I \nboiling. Swirli"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triclofos Sodium\nCI3C O\" xONa\nC2H3CI3NaO4P \nMol.Wt.251.4\nTriclofos Sodium is sodium 2,2,2-trichloroethyl hydrogen \northophosphate.\nTriclofos Sodium contains not less than 41:3 per cent and not \nmore than 43.2 per cent of Cl and not less than 97.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C2H3C13NaO4P, both \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; hygroscopic.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the; spectrum with that obtained with triclofos sodium \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of triclofos sodium.\nB. It gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\n3863\nwww.webofpharma.com\nC, Heat 0.1g with 1 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate to a \ndull red heat and maintain for 10 minutes, cool, extract the \nresidue with water and filter. The filtrate gives the reactions\nof chlorides (2.3 .Hand of phosphates Í2.3.T). ;\nTests •. \n„..i.\npH (2.4.24).3.0 to 4.5, determined ona2.0 per centw/v solution.\nAppearance of solution. A 2.0,per cent w/y solution is clear \n(2.4.1). \n. ;? ,7;\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 0.5, g in 50 ml of water. 15 ml of \nthe solution complies with the limit test for chlorides (0.17 per \ncent). \n,\nPhosphates. Not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated as PO4, \nwhen determined by dissolving 25 mg in 10. ml of water, add \n4 ml of 1 M sulphuric acid, 1 ml of ammonium molybdate \nsolution and 2 ml of methylaminophenol-sulphite reagent\nand allow to stand for 15 minutes and dilute to 25 ml with \nwater, allow to stand for a further 15 minutes and measure the \nabsorbance of the solution at 730 nm (2.4.7): Calculate the \ncontent of phosphate from a calibration curve prepared by \ntreating suitable volumes of a 0.00143 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium dihydrogen orthophosphate in the same manner.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 5.0 percent, determined \non 1 g by drying at 100°, at a pressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa \nfor 3 hours.\nAssay. For Cl — Mix 0.25 g with 1 g bi anhydrous sodium \ncarbonate in a nickel crucible about 3 cm in diameter, fill the \ncrucible completely with anhydrous sodium carbonate and \ninvert into a nickel crucible about 4 cm in diameter; cover the \nsmaller crucible with anhydrous sodium carbonate, well \npressed down, using 25 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate in\nall. Heat for 30 minutes at a dull red heat, cool, transfer to a \n400 ml beaker, add 150 ml of water and boil gently for \n10 minutes. Filter through absorbent cotton into a 600 ml beaker, \nwashing the residue with hot water, until 400 ml of filtrate has \nbeen collected. Cool, cautiously add nitric acid until the \nsolution is neutral to litmus paper and add 3 ml of nitric acid \nin excess. Add 50 ml of 0.1 M silver nitrate, allow to stand \nuntil precipitation is complete, filter, wash the precipitate with \nwater and titrate the combined filtrate and washings with \n0.1 M ammonium thiocyanate using ammonium iron(III) \nsulphate solution as indicator.\n1 ml of 0.1M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545 g of Cl.\nFor CdCCliNaO^P—Heat 0.2 g in a Kjeldahl flask with 2 ml \nof sulphuric dcid; and 2.5 ml of nitric acid until brown fumes \ncease to be evolved, cool, add 1 ml of nitric acid and heat \nagain. Continue adding nitric acid and heating until brown\n3864 \nMmBmIWI!\nTRICLOFOS SODIUM\n^2022\nTRIETHYL CITRATE\nfumes are no longer evolved and the solution is colour} \nwhen cold. Heat until dense, white fumes are evolved e \ntransfer the solution to a flask with the aid of 150 ml of\n,Q^iFf sulphuric acid to the combined aqueous layer \nthings, extract with four 10-ml quantities of isoamyl \nihol$0^ cac^ extract t0 stand until good separation \nf y ^^ed,combine the isoamyl alcohol extracts, remove\nB. To 0.5 ml add 5 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and 4 ml of \ndilute sodium hydroxide solution. Boil under reflux for about\n■■■■ Í\nadd 50 ml of citric-molybdic acid solution and heat slowlv ’’ I \nboiling. Swirling the flask continuously, add 25 ml of \nI\nsolution at first drop wise and then in a steady stream, heat *\n10 minutes, 2 ml of the solution gives the reactions of citrates \n(2.3.1).\njj^er water that separates and dilute to 50 ml with\nC. It gives the reactions of esters (2.3.1).\na water-bath for 5 minutes and cool. Filter, wash the Pfecipit^ |\nTransfer 10 ml of the solution to a 20 ml \nampoule, add 10 ml of alcoholic potassium hydroxide \nJo/u/ion» seal the ampoule, mix well and heat in an autoclave\nwith water until free from acid, transfer the precipitate to I \nflask with the aid of 100 ml of water, add 50 ml of 0.5 Msodium |"},{"name":"Tests","text":"2‘hours. Allow the ampoule to cool, transfer the \ncon^iits (o a A^k with ^e \n20 ml of 2 Mnitric acid and\nadd 25^0f $2 M silver nitrate. Titrate with 0.02 M \n^mbniumthiocyanate using ammonium iron(III) sulphate \n^/¿fojixd^indicator. Repeat the titration using 10 ml of\nhydroxide and shake until dissolved. Titrate the excess of $\nAppearance of solution. The substance under examination is \nclear (2.4.1) and not more intensely coloured than reference \nsolution BYS6 (2.4.1). \n■ \\\nalkali with 0.5 Mhydrochloric acid using phenolphthalein. I\nthymol blue solution as indicator. \"■’K\"--|\nAcidity. Dilute 10g with 10 ml ofpreviously neutralized et/zow/\n1 ml of 0.5 Mspdium hydroxide is equivalent to 4.835 mg of \nC2H3Cl;NaO4P. Correct the result for the content of phosphate \nas determined by the test described above;.! mg of PQ ¡$ \nequivalent to 2.65 mg of C2H3Cl3NaO4P..\n(95 per cent). Add 0.5 ml of bromothymol blue solution. Not \nmore than 0.3 ml of 0.7 M sodium hydroxide is required to \nchange the color of the indicator to blue.\nalcoholic potassium hydroxide solution in place of the\ncontentsof the ampoule. The difference between the titrations \nrepresents the amount of silver nitrate required.\nRefractive index (2.4.27). 1.440 to 1.446.\n.. §\nTriclofos Oral Solution is a solution of Triclofos Sodium in a I \nsuitable flavoured vehicle. , \n|\n1 ml of 0 02 AT silver nitrate is equivalent to 1.676 mg of \nCAOjNad^.\nRelated substances. Determine by gas chromatography \n(2.4.13)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triethyl-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triethyl Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triethyl Citrate\n.0 \nr^O”CH2CH3"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triethyl Citrate\n.0 \nr^O”CH2CH3"},{"name":"Identification","text":"|\nChromatographic system\nDisperse a quantity of the oral solution containing 0.25 g of I \nTriclofos Sodium with 40 ml'of water, add 5 ml of 1 Kisulphuric \n|\nacid and extract with 25 ml of isoamyl alcohol. The extract 1\n- a fused silica column 30 m x 0.32 mm, packed with poly\n(dimethyl) siloxane (5 pm),\n- temperature:\nHO”G^O-CH2CH3\n...I\\^O~CH2CH3\ncolumn.200°,\ninjection port and detector at 220°,\n- a flame ionization detector, \n'\ncomplies with the following tests. \nI\nA. \nEvaporate 5 ml to dryness, heat the residue with 1 ml of a |\n50 per cent v/v solution of sulphuric acid and 1 ml of a 5.0 per \n|\nas carrier gas. . \n,\n- linear velocity about 26 cm per second using nitrogen\ncent w/v solution of potassium permanganate in a water­\nbath for 5 minutes, add 7 ml of water and decolourise the I\nCpHjoOv \nMol. Wt.276.3\nInject 1 pl of the reference solution. The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to triethyl citrate and \nmethyl tridecanoate is not less than 1.5.\nsolution with 1 ml of a 5.0 per cent w/v solution of oxalic acid \n|\nTo 1 ml, add 1 ml of pyridine and 1 ml of 2 Pl sodium hydroxide, \n|\nheat in a water-bath for 1 minute, stirring continuously; a pink |\nTriethyl Citrate is triethyl 2-hydroxypropane-l, 2, 3- \ntricarboxylate.\nTriethyl Citrate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not\ncolour is produced in the pyridine layer. ■ \nI\nB. \nEvaporate 10 ml to dryness, heat the residue with 1 g of J \nanhydrous sodium carbonate at a dull red heat for 10 minutes, \nI\nInject 1 pl each of the test solution and the reference solution. \nRun the chromatogram twice the retention time of the principal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \nthe area of any secondary peak is not more than 0.2 per cent \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution. The sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 0.5 per cent the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution. Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.04 per cent \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution.\nmore than 101.0 per cent of Ci2H2qO7, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\ncool, extract the residue with water arid filter The residue । \ngives the reactions of chlorides (2.3.1) arid of phosphates |\nCategory. Excipient. ‘\n(2-3.1)., \n... 'I"},{"name":"Tests","text":"|\nDescription.A clear, viscous, colourless or almost colourless, \nhygroscopic liquid."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Other tests, Comply with the tests stated under. Oral Liquid |\n^Amaybe omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Tests B\nandCtnay be omitted if test A is carried out.\nAssay. To a quantity containing 16 mg of Triclofos Sodium, \n|\nadd 15 ml of water, 1 ml of 7 M sodium hydroxide and 15 ml \n|\nether, shake for 1 minute, allow to separate, wash the eih# I \nlayer with 1 ml of water and discard the .ether solution- A |\n■^•Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \n^W^-the spectrum with that obtained with triethyl citrate \n^8or with the reference spectrum of triethyl citrate.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 4.0 g in 8 ini of ethanol (95 per \ncent) and dilute to 20 ml with water. 12 ml of the resulting \nsolution complies with the limit test for the heavy metals,"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trifluoperazine-hydrochloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Hydrochloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Hydrochloride Injection\nA.Dissolve 20 mg in 10 ml of water, make the solution alkaline \nto litmus paper  with 5 M sodium hydroxide  and extract with \ntwo quantities,  each of 20 ml, of light petroleum (60° to 80°). \nCombine the extracts, wash with 10 ml of water, shake with 5 g \nof anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and evaporate the filtrate \ncarefully to dryness.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nTrifluoperazine Injection is a sterile solution of Trifluoperazine \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nI 4,4\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.25 per cent, determined on\n1.0 g. \nN \nAssay. To 1.5 g, add 25 ml of 2-propanol, 50 ml of water, \n25.0 ml of 1 M sodium hydroxide and a few glass beads into a \n250-m1 borosilicate-glass flask fitted with a reflux condenser. \nHeat under a reflux condenser for 1 hour and allow to cool. \nTitrate with 1 M hydrochloric acid, using 1 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\nTrifluoperazine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntrifluoperazine. C21 H24 F3N3S.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 1 mg of trifluoperazine per ml.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with trifluoperazine hydrochloride RS treated in \nthe same manner or with the reference spectrum of \ntrifluoperazine.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nNOTE - In the following procedures, the test and standard \nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\n1 ml of 1 M sodium hydroxide  is equivalent to 0.0921 g of\nC12E12007.\nsubdued light.\nB.Complies with the test for identification of phenothiazines \n(2.3.3), using as reference solution a 0.2 per cent w/v solution \nof trifluoperazine hydrochloride RS in chlorofbrm.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trifluoperazine Hydrochloride Injection\nA.Dissolve 20 mg in 10 ml of water, make the solution alkaline \nto litmus paper  with 5 M sodium hydroxide  and extract with \ntwo quantities,  each of 20 ml, of light petroleum (60° to 80°). \nCombine the extracts, wash with 10 ml of water, shake with 5 g \nof anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and evaporate the filtrate \ncarefully to dryness.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nTrifluoperazine Injection is a sterile solution of Trifluoperazine \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nI 4,4\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.25 per cent, determined on\n1.0 g. \nN \nAssay. To 1.5 g, add 25 ml of 2-propanol, 50 ml of water, \n25.0 ml of 1 M sodium hydroxide and a few glass beads into a \n250-m1 borosilicate-glass flask fitted with a reflux condenser. \nHeat under a reflux condenser for 1 hour and allow to cool. \nTitrate with 1 M hydrochloric acid, using 1 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\nTrifluoperazine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntrifluoperazine. C21 H24 F3N3S.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 1 mg of trifluoperazine per ml.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with trifluoperazine hydrochloride RS treated in \nthe same manner or with the reference spectrum of \ntrifluoperazine.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nNOTE - In the following procedures, the test and standard \nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\n1 ml of 1 M sodium hydroxide  is equivalent to 0.0921 g of\nC12E12007.\nsubdued light.\nB.Complies with the test for identification of phenothiazines \n(2.3.3), using as reference solution a 0.2 per cent w/v solution \nof trifluoperazine hydrochloride RS in chlorofbrm.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."},{"name":"Identification","text":"When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), the final \nsolution obtained in Assay shows an absorption maximum at \nabout 256 nm.\nC.When examined in the range 280 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.01 per cent w/v solution in 0.1 M hydrochloric acid \nmeasured immediately after preparation shows an absorption \nmaximum at about 305 nm. Dilute 10 ml of the solution to"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trifluoperazine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TRIFLUOPERAZINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nmethod D (5 ppm). Prepare the ^standard solution using lead \nstandard solution (T ppm Pb) obtained by diluting, lead \nstandard solution (100-pprmPb); with a mixture of equal \nvolumes of ethanol (95 per cent) and water.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.25 per cent, determined on\n1.0 g.\nAssay . To 1.5 g, add 25 ml of 2-propanol, 50 ml of water, \n25:0 ml of 1 Idsodium hydroxide and a few glass beads into a \n250-ml borosilicate-glass flask fitted with a reflux condenser. \nHeat under ri reflux: condenser for 1 hour and allow to:cool. \nTitrate with 1 M hydrochloric racid, using 1 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 7 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.0921 g of\nC12H20O7.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture. ; -\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride\nC2IH24F3N3S,2HC1 \nMol. Wt. 480.4\nTrifluoperazine hydrochloride is 10-[3-(4-met]iylpiperazin-l- \nyl)propyl] -2-trifluoromethylphenothiazine dihydrochloride.\nTri fluoperazine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C21H24F3N3S, 2HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic; antiemetic.\nDescription . A white to pale yellow, crystallinepowder; slightly \nhygroscopic. . .\nNOTE— Iri the following procedures, the test and standard \nspécimens and thé solutions containing them should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests -without delay and in\nsubdued light. ■' • •"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TRIFLUOPERAZINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nmethod D (5 ppm). Prepare the ^standard solution using lead \nstandard solution (T ppm Pb) obtained by diluting, lead \nstandard solution (100-pprmPb); with a mixture of equal \nvolumes of ethanol (95 per cent) and water.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.25 per cent, determined on\n1.0 g.\nAssay . To 1.5 g, add 25 ml of 2-propanol, 50 ml of water, \n25:0 ml of 1 Idsodium hydroxide and a few glass beads into a \n250-ml borosilicate-glass flask fitted with a reflux condenser. \nHeat under ri reflux: condenser for 1 hour and allow to:cool. \nTitrate with 1 M hydrochloric racid, using 1 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 7 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.0921 g of\nC12H20O7.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture. ; -\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride\nC2IH24F3N3S,2HC1 \nMol. Wt. 480.4\nTrifluoperazine hydrochloride is 10-[3-(4-met]iylpiperazin-l- \nyl)propyl] -2-trifluoromethylphenothiazine dihydrochloride.\nTri fluoperazine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C21H24F3N3S, 2HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic; antiemetic.\nDescription . A white to pale yellow, crystallinepowder; slightly \nhygroscopic. . .\nNOTE— Iri the following procedures, the test and standard \nspécimens and thé solutions containing them should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests -without delay and in\nsubdued light. ■' • •"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Dissolve 20 mg in 10 ml of water, make the solution alkaline \nto litmus paper with 5 M sodium hydroxide and extract with\ntwo quantities, each of 20 ml, of light petroleum (60° to 80°). \nCombine the extracts, wash with lOmlof water, shake with 5 g\nof anhydrous sodium sw/pAate, filter and evaporate thefilt« : \ncarefully to dryness. -■ \n••••-•■. :y\nOn the residue, déterminé by infrared absornp \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with th \nobtained with trifluoperazine hydrochloride IPRSixe^,^' \nthe same manner or with the reference spectrum \ntrifluoperazine. \"■ \n■ \"\nB. Complies with the test for identification of phenothiazines \n(2.3.3), using as reference solution a 0.2,per cent w/v solution \nof trifluoperazine hydrochloride IPRSw\\chloroform.\nC. When examined iri the rangé'280 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7) a \n0.01 per cent w/v solution in 0.7; ^ hydrochloric acid \nmeasured immediately after preparation shows an absorption \nmaximum at about 305 nm. Dilute 10 :ml of the solution to\n100 ml with 0.1 MAyt/rocZtZorte aczi/. When=exainined in the \nrange 230 nm to 280 nm, the resulting solution shows an \nabsorption maximum at about 256 nm; absorbance at about \n256 nm, about 0.65.\nD. Dissolve 5 mg in 2 ml of sulphuric acid and allow to stand \nfor 5 minutes; an orange colour is produced.\nE. A 10 per cent w/v solution gives the reactions of chlorides \n(2-3.1). \n- ,.r."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 1.7 to 2.6, determined in a 10.0 per cent w/v solution.\nRelated substances. Complies with the test for related \nsubstances in phenothiazines (2.3.5), using mobile phase A.\nSulphated ash (2.3:18). Not more than 0.-1 percent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.4 g in 50 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid add 10 ml of mercuric acetate solution. Titrate with \n0.1 M perchloric acid, using crystal violet ¿solution as \nindicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02402 gof \nc21H24F3N3s,2Ha.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trifluoperazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Injection\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride Injection\nTrifluoperazine Injection is a sterile solution of \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections. :\nTrifluoperazine Injection contains riot less than 90:0 pet ce$ \narid not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount\ntrifluoperazine,C2!H24F3N3S; \n;\n3866\nwww.webofpharma.com\nstrength The equivalent of 1 mg of trifluoperazine per ml."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trifluoperazine Injection\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride Injection\nTrifluoperazine Injection is a sterile solution of \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections. :\nTrifluoperazine Injection contains riot less than 90:0 pet ce$ \narid not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount\ntrifluoperazine,C2!H24F3N3S; \n;\n3866\nwww.webofpharma.com\nstrength The equivalent of 1 mg of trifluoperazine per ml."},{"name":"Identification","text":".pgÿripribn.Âclear, colourless solution.. '\n' ’ fflp— thyfollowing procedures, the test and standard\n\\ àhd i^e solutions containing them should be \n^fSrledby carrying out the tests-without delay and in"},{"name":"Identification","text":"^hen examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), the final \nsolution obtained in Assay shows an absorption maximum at\nabout 256 nm\nTestssk Aw\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 tb 5.0.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 172.0 Endotoxin\nUnits per mg of trifluoperazine.\nOther tests. .Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \npreparations (Injections)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Assay To an measured volume of the injection containing \nabout 50 mg of trifluoperazine add 10 ml of 2 Msulphuric acid\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nand extract.with three quantities, each of 25 ml, of carbon \ntetrachloride: Discard the carbon tetrachloride extract after\neach extraction. Add 10 ml of strong ammonia solution and \nextract with five quantities, each of 50 ml, of cyclohexane.\nExtractthecombined cyclohexane extracts with five quantities, \neach of 50 ml, of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid. Dilute the combined\nacid extracts to 500.0 ml and mix. Measure the absorbance of \nthé resulting solution at the maximum at about 256 nni (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C21H24F3N3S taking 743 as the specific \nabsorbance'at 256 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nLabelling, The label states the strength in terms of the \nequivalent amount of trifluoperazine per ml."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trifluoperazine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Tablets\nTrifluoperazine;Hydrochloride Tablets\nWpqperazine Tablets contain not less than 92.5 per cent\nand not more than 107.5 per. cent of trifluoperazine,\nThe tabiets are coated.\nstrengths. The equivalent of 1 mg and 5 mg of\n: ^uoperazinei\n. 7^n ^!e following procedures, the test and standard \nsPe<dntens and the solutiohs containing them should be \nProtected bycarrying out the tests without delay and in \ns^duedlight."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trifluoperazine Tablets\nTrifluoperazine;Hydrochloride Tablets\nWpqperazine Tablets contain not less than 92.5 per cent\nand not more than 107.5 per. cent of trifluoperazine,\nThe tabiets are coated.\nstrengths. The equivalent of 1 mg and 5 mg of\n: ^uoperazinei\n. 7^n ^!e following procedures, the test and standard \nsPe<dntens and the solutiohs containing them should be \nProtected bycarrying out the tests without delay and in \ns^duedlight."},{"name":"TRIFLUOPERAZINE TABLETS","text":"A. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 20 mg \nof trifluoperazine with 30 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid for\n10 minutes, filter, make the filtrate alkaline to litmus paper with \n5 M sodium hydroxide and extract with two quantities, each \nof 20 ml, of lightpetroleum (6(P to 8(f). Combine the extracts, \nwash with 10 ml of water, shake with 5 g of anhydrous sodium \nsulphate, filter and evaporate the filtrate carefully tp dryness.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with trifluoperazine hydrochloride IPRS treated in \nthe same manner or with the reference spectrum of \ntrifluoperazine.\nB. Extract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 5 mg \nof trifluoperazine with 5 ml of acetone, filter and evaporate the \nfiltrate to dryness. Add 2 ml oí sulphuric acid to the residue \nand allow to stand for 5 minutes; an orange colour is produced.\nApparatisNo. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 50 rpm, 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, dilute suitably if necessary with \nthe medium, at the maximum at about 255 nm and at about 278 \nnm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of C2iH24F3N3S. in the.medium \nfrom the difference in absorbance at 255 nm and.at 278 nm, \nobtained from a solution of known concentration of \ntrifluoperazine hydrochloride IPRS in the dissolution \nmedium.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of \nQJI^N.S. \n...\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets. \n■ /\nPlace one tablet in a 100-ml volumetric flask, add 50 ml of a \nmixture of 19 volumes of water and 1 volume ofhydrochloric \nacid, shake until the tablet has completely disintegrated, dilute \nto volume with the same mixture, mix and filter, rejecting the \nfirst few ml ofthe filtrate. Dilute suitably, if necessary with the \nsame solvent mixture and measure the absorbance of the \nresulting solution at the maximum at about 256 nm (2.4.7). \nCalculate the content of C2lH24F3N3S taking 743 as the specific \nabsorbance at 256 nm.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets: Weigha quantity of the \npowder containing1 about £ mg of trifluoperazine, shake for\n15 minutes with 400 ml of a mixture of 19 volumes of water and"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triflupromazine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triflupromazine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triflupromazine Hydrochloride\nFlupromazine Hydrochloride\nD.A 10 per cent w/v solution gives the reactions of chlorides \n(2.3.1)."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triflupromazine Hydrochloride\nFlupromazine Hydrochloride\nD.A 10 per cent w/v solution gives the reactions of chlorides \n(2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 3.5 to 5.2."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Bacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 5.8 Endotoxin Units \nper mg of triflupromazine hydrochloride.\nRelated substances. Complies with the test for Related \nsubstances in phenothiazines (2.3.5), using mobile phase A.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 2 hours.\nAssay. To an measured volume of the injection containing \nabout 20 mg of Triflupromazine Hydrochloride add sufficient \nof a mixture of 19 volumes of water and 1 volume of \nhydrochloric acid to produce 100.0 ml and mix. Dilute 5.0 ml \nof the solution to 100.0 ml with the same mixture and mix. \nMeasure the absorbance of the resulting solution at the \nmaximum at about 256 nm (2.4.7).\nCI8H 19F3N2S, HCI \t\nMol. Wt. 388.9\nAssay. Dissolve 0.8 g in 50 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid add 10 ml of mercuric acetate solution. Titrate with \n0.1 M perchloric acid, using crystal violet solution as \nindicator. Carry out a blank titration.\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride is 10-[3-(dimethylamino)- \npropyl)]-2-trifluoromethylphenothiazine hydrochloride.\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride contains not less than \n97.0 per cent and not more than 103.0 per cent of C I8H 19F3N2S,\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.03889 g of \nC181-119F3N2S, HCI.\nCalculate the content of C I8H 19F3N2S, HCI taking 700 as the \nspecific absorbance at 256 nm.\n HCI, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic; antiemetic.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nDose. As antipsychotic, orally, 30 to 150 mg of triflupromazine \ndaily, in divided doses; by intramuscular injection, 10 mg \nrepeated 4-hourly, if necessary; by intravenous injection, 1 to \n3 mg repeated 4-hourly, if necessary. As antiemetic, 20 to \n30 mg of triflupromazine daily; by intramuscular injection 1 to \n3 mg."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triflupromazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triflupromazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triflupromazine Inj ection -\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride Injection^Flupromazine \nHydrochloride Inj ection; Flupromazine injection\nTriflupromazine Injection is a sterile solution of Triflupromazine \nHydrochloride in Water for Inj ections. \n; -\nTriflupromazine Injection contains not less than 90.0 percent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\ntriflupromazine hydrochloride, Ci8Hi9F3N2S, HCI."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triflupromazine Inj ection -\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride Injection^Flupromazine \nHydrochloride Inj ection; Flupromazine injection\nTriflupromazine Injection is a sterile solution of Triflupromazine \nHydrochloride in Water for Inj ections. \n; -\nTriflupromazine Injection contains not less than 90.0 percent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\ntriflupromazine hydrochloride, Ci8Hi9F3N2S, HCI."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Usual strength. 10 mg per ml.\nDescription. Aclear, almost colourless solution.\nNOTE — In the following procedures, the test andstandard \nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\nsubdued light. . \nwhpiw w\nA. To. am appropriate quantity of the injection add suffici^ \n0.1 M hydrochloric acid to produce a solutioncontain^ \n0.001 per cent w/v solution of Triflupromazine Hydrbchl°rl\n........................................................................................\nexafflinedin the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), the\nsolution shows an absorption maximum at about \nrésultés50\n256nni: \n_\nr a voluihe containing about 100 mg of Triflupromazine \n.¿rf^hlorideadd 5 ml of 8 M nitric acid and mix; a pink to\ncolour develops which qüickly turns dark brown and\n¡hen changes to a clear solution having a yellow tint.\npH(2.4’4).3.5to5.2.\ngacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 5.8 Endotoxin Units\nper mg of triflupromazine hydrochloride."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nAssay. To an measured volume of the injection containing \naboutlOmgbfTriflupromazine Hydrochloride add sufficient \nof a mixture of 19 volumes of water and 1 volume of \nhydrochloric acid to produce 100.0 ml and mix. Dilute 5.0 ml \nof the solution to 100.0 ml with the same mixture and mix, \nMeasure the absorbance of the resulting solution at the \nmaximum at about256 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C!8Hi9F3N2S, HC1 taking 700 as the\nspecific absorbance at 256 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triflupromazine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triflupromazine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triflupromazine Tablets\nTnflupromazine Hydrochloride Tablets; Flupromazine \nHydrochlorideTablets; Fl upromazine Tablets\nTriflupromazine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent\nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntriflupromazine hydrochloride, C18H19F3N2S, HC1.\nUsual strengths. 10 mg; 25 mg.\nIn thè following procedures, the test and standard \nspecimens:and the solutions containing thém should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in \nsubdued light, >"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triflupromazine Tablets\nTnflupromazine Hydrochloride Tablets; Flupromazine \nHydrochlorideTablets; Fl upromazine Tablets\nTriflupromazine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent\nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntriflupromazine hydrochloride, C18H19F3N2S, HC1.\nUsual strengths. 10 mg; 25 mg.\nIn thè following procedures, the test and standard \nspecimens:and the solutions containing thém should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in \nsubdued light, >"},{"name":"identification","text":"Mhake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 20 mg \nljÌfiUPrOmaZÌne Hydrochloride with 30 ml of 7 M\n~ \nM0^cacid for 10 minutes, filter, make the filtrate alkaline\nwith 5 Msodium hydroxide and extract with \nquantities, each of 20 ml, of light petroleum (boiling\nCombine the extracts, wash with 10 ml of \nn * ake with 5 g of anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and \n^Worat^ the filtrate carefully to dryness.\nTRIFLUPROMAZINE TABLETS\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with triflupromazine hydrochloride IPRS, treated \nin the same manner or with the reference spectrum of \ntriflupromazine hydrochloride.\nB. Extract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 5 mg \nof Triflupromazine Hydrochloride with 5 ml of acetone, filter \nand evaporate the filtrate to dryness.\nWhen examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.001 per cent w/v solution in 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid shows \nan absorption maximum at about 256 nm.\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.01Mhydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 100 rpm, 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, dilute suitably if necessary with \nthe medium, at the maximum at about 305 nm (2.4.7). Calculate\nthe content of C18Hl9F3N2S,HCl in the medium from the \nabsorbance obtained from a solution of known concentration \nof triflupromazine hydrochloride IPRS in the dissolution \nmedium\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of \nCl8H19F3N2S.HCl.\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets.\nCrush one tablet and transfer to a 100-ml volumetric flask with \nthe aid of a mixture of 19 volumes of water and 1 volume of \nhydrochloric acid. Shake well, dilute to volume with the same \nmixture and complete the procedure described under the Assay \nbeginning at the words “mix and filter,...”.\nCalculate the content of C j 8H [ 9F3N2S, HC1 in the tablet.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity of the \npowder containing about 50 mg of Triflupromazine\nHydrochloride and shake for 15 minutes with 200 ml of a mixture \nof 19 volumes of water and 1 volume of hydrochloric acid. \nDilute to 500.0 ml with the same solvent mixturé, mix and filter, \nrejecting the first few ml of the filtrate. Dilute 10.0 ml to\n100.0 ml with the same solvent mixture. Measure the absorbance \nof the filtrate at the maximum at about 256 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C!SH19F3N2S, FÍC1 taking 700 as the \nspecific absorbance at 256 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trifluridine-to-dryness-using-a-rotary-evaporator-and-dry-at","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trifluridine to dryness using a rotary evaporator and dry at"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trifluridine to dryness using a rotary evaporator and dry at \n60° under reduced pressure. On the residue, determine by \ninfrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the \nspectrum with that obtained with Ertl. luridine RS treated in the \nsame manner or with the reference spectrum of trifluridine.\nspectrophotometer set at  263 nm, \ninjection volume: 20 p1.\nHN.CF 3\nTime \t\nMobile phase A \t\nMobile phase B \n(in min.) \t\n(per cent v/v) \t\n(per cent v/v) \n0 \t\n95 \t\n5\nO \nN7 \nHO\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\n3 \t\n95 \t\n5\nOH\n10 \t\n85 \t\n15\n15 \t\n85 \t\n15\nC10li11F3N205 Mol.Wt.\n16 \t\n95 \t\n5"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trifluridine to dryness using a rotary evaporator and dry at \n60° under reduced pressure. On the residue, determine by \ninfrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the \nspectrum with that obtained with Ertl. luridine RS treated in the \nsame manner or with the reference spectrum of trifluridine.\nspectrophotometer set at  263 nm, \ninjection volume: 20 p1.\nHN.CF 3\nTime \t\nMobile phase A \t\nMobile phase B \n(in min.) \t\n(per cent v/v) \t\n(per cent v/v) \n0 \t\n95 \t\n5\nO \nN7 \nHO\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\n3 \t\n95 \t\n5\nOH\n10 \t\n85 \t\n15\n15 \t\n85 \t\n15\nC10li11F3N205 Mol.Wt.\n16 \t\n95 \t\n5"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Trifluridine is 5-Trifluoromethyl-2'-deoxyuridine.\n25 \t\n95 \t\n5 \nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plate and\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 6.5.\nTrifluridine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and no \nthan 102.0 per cent of C 10H 1i F3N205, calculated on \nbasis.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\n'1\nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nSolvent mixture. 5 volumes of acetonitrile and 95 volumes of \na 0.05M sodium dihvdrogen orthophosphate, adjusted to pH \n6.0 with ammonia.\nCategory. Pyrimidine nucleoside analogue; antiviral \nviruses).\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \n• solution (a) (0.5 per cent). The sum of areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (1.0 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 times the area \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.05 per cent).\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder.\nTest solution. Dilute the eye drops if necessary  to obtain a \nsolution containing 0.02 per cent w/v of Trifluridine in the \nsolvent mixture."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with  trifluricia \nor with the reference spectrum of trifluridine. \nIS .\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1 volume of the test solution  to\n100 volumes in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 volume of reference solution \n(a) to 10 volumes in the solvent mixture.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution. \nU. '"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Loss on drying (2.4. 1 9). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying under vacuum at 105° for 4 hours. \nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as \ndescribed in the test for Related substances with the following \nmodifications.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such \nas Inertsil ODS 2), \n-\nmobile phase: A. 0.05M sodium dihydrogen ortho-\nphosphate, adjusted to pH 6.0 with ammonia.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 47.0° to + 51.0°, determined \nin 3.0 per cent w/v solution in water.\nReference solution. A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of trifluridine \nRS in the solvent mixture.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nB. acetonitrile. \n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at  263 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\n14, \nSolvent mixture.  5 volumes of acetonitrile and 95 volumes of \na 0.05 M sodium dihvdrogen orthophosphate, previously \nadjusted to pH 6.0 with ammonia.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nTime \t\nMobile phase A \t\nMobile phase B \n(in min.) \t\n(per cent v/v) \t\n(per cent v/v) \n0 \t\n95 \t\n5\nTest solution. Dissolve 20 mg of substance under examination \nin the solvent mixture and dilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent \nmixture.\nCalculate the content of C loH F3N 205. \nStorage. Store protected from light.\n3 \t\n95 \t\n5\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n10 \t\n85 \t\n15\n100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nTrifluridine Eye Drops\n15 \t\n85 \t\n15\nReference solution (b).  Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 10.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\n16 \t\n95 \t\n5 \n25 \t\n95 \t\n5 \nInjegrieferen6e solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \ncohminefficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nTrifluridine Eye Drops  are a sterile solution of Trifluridine in \nPurified Water.\nChromatographic system\n•StainItiS steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \n\"OctadeOlsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such\nTrifluridine Eye Drops contain not less than 9540 per-cent and \nnot more than 105.0  per cent of the stated amount Oftrifluridine, \nCAIIF3N20,\nas Inertsil ODS-2),\nTRIFLURIDINE EYE DROPS"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Inject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.5 per cent). The sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (1.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 \ntimes the area of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.05 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Eye Drops.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as \ndescribed in the test for Related substances with the following \nmodifications.\nReference solution. A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of trifluridine \nRS in the solvent mixture.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C loH li F3N205 in the eye drops.\nStorage. Store protected from light, at a temperature between \n2° to 8°."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trifluridine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trifluridine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TRIFLURIDINE\nTrifluridine\nTriflurothymidene,\nCi0HnF3N2O5 \nMol. Wt 296.2\nTrifluridine is 5-Tri fluor omet h y 1-2 ’-deoxyuridine.\nTrifluridine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CioHiiF3N205, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Pyrimidine nucleoside analogue; antiviral (herpes \nviruses). ' '\nDescription. A white, crystalline ponder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TRIFLURIDINE\nTrifluridine\nTriflurothymidene,\nCi0HnF3N2O5 \nMol. Wt 296.2\nTrifluridine is 5-Tri fluor omet h y 1-2 ’-deoxyuridine.\nTrifluridine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CioHiiF3N205, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Pyrimidine nucleoside analogue; antiviral (herpes \nviruses). ' '\nDescription. A white, crystalline ponder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with trifluridine \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of trifluridine.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nTests. .\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22).+47.0° to + 51.0°, determined \nin 3.0 per cent -w/v solution in water.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n■ '\nSolvent mixture. 5 volumes of acetonitrile and 95; volumes of \na 0.05 M sodium dihydrogen orthophosphate, previously \nadjusted to pH 6.0 with ammonia.\nTest \nDissolve 20 mg of substance under examination\nin the solvent mixture and dilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent \nmixture. ' ■'f''\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution fb) .; Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 10.0 ml with the solvent mixture. : -\nChromatographic system .\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such \nasInertsilODS-2), rr \nm\n....................................... ................ ;................................._Ip2o’>\n- mobile phase: A. 0.05Msodiuirridihydrogen ortfc.\nphosphate, adjusted to pH 6.0 with ammonia .\nB. acetonitrile, \n- a irradient nroeramme using the c<)ndlll0ns given below\n’ \n: A • 7..'. < ... \n” 7 < •\n- flow rate:\n1 ml per minute, \nlotometer set at 263 nm\n- injection1\nrolùme: 20 jil.! '\nspectropl\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/y).\nTime\n0\n95\n5\n3\n95\n...r\n10\n85\n15\n' \n85 \n\\\n16\n95\n25\n95\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plate and \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0. \n: i\nInject reference solution (a), (b). and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.5 limes the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.5 per cent) and the sum of areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (1.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 \ntimes the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.05 per cent).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than l .O per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying under vacuum at 105° for 4 hours.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as \ndescribed under test for Related substances with ttiefollowing\nmodifications. \n7 <zp<'\nReference solution. A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of trifluridine \nIPRS in the solvent mixture. \n7\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent. \n: .\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. -\nCalculate the content of C0H| J;;N2O5\nStorage. Store protected from light. \n; ; ; 7 ; ■ j 7"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trigonella-foenum-graecum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trigonella foenum-graecum"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trigonella foenum-graecum\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A clear, almost colourless to amber yellow liquid,\nfree from sediment, suspended matter and a characteristic mint\nand menthol like odor. \t\n..Z4)\nTest solution.  To 1.0 g of the powdered  substance under \nexamination, add 5.0 ml of the methanol. Heat on a water-bath \nat 65° for 5 minutes, cool and filter. \nReference solution. Dissolve 3 mg of trigonelline \nhydrochloride RS in 1.0 ml of methanol."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trigonella foenum-graecum\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A clear, almost colourless to amber yellow liquid,\nfree from sediment, suspended matter and a characteristic mint\nand menthol like odor. \t\n..Z4)\nTest solution.  To 1.0 g of the powdered  substance under \nexamination, add 5.0 ml of the methanol. Heat on a water-bath \nat 65° for 5 minutes, cool and filter. \nReference solution. Dissolve 3 mg of trigonelline \nhydrochloride RS in 1.0 ml of methanol."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v  solution of the oil under\nexamination in ethanol (95 per cent).\nReference solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of mentha\nApply to the plate 20 j.t1 of each solution as bands 10 mm by \n2 mm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 10 cm. Dry the plate in air \nand examine under ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 366 nm, \nspray with potassium  iodobismuthate solution.  Heat the plate \nat 105° for 10 minutes and examine in day light. The \nchroniatograiNc profile of the test solution is similar to that \nof the'referehet  solution. It also shows in its upper half, a \nbroad light brownish-yellow zone (triglycerides).\narvensis oil RS in ethanol (95 per cent).\nUse chromatographic system described in the Assay.\nThe peaks in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorre.spond Th \n.toe peaks in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference-solution.\n12 \n10 111\nMeth' is dried, ripe seeds of Trigonel la lbenurn-2graecuin L. \nl\n(Fant•  Fabaceae).\n- ----- - - _.,..-ii•-;4-„,-i._..,,„\nB.Flash Point (2.4.44). 118.0° to 122.0°.\n-----7-:-- ---',- 3831 \n,-. \\ --%--------;\no fk"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Swelling Power. Not less than 6.0, determined on the powdered \nsubstance under examination, determined by the following \nmethod.\nTransfer 1 g to a 100 ml stoppered cylinder containing 90 ml of\nwater, shake well for 30 seconds and allow to stand 24 hours, \nshaking gently on three occasions during this period. Add \nsufficient water to produce 100 ml, mix gently for 30 seconds, \navoiding the entrapment of air, allow to stand for 5 hours and \nmeasure the volume of mucilage. Repeat the determination \nthree times. The average of four determinations is not less \nthan 40 ml.\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 5.0 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 12.0 per cent, \ndetermined on 1 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nStorage. Store protected from heat, moisture and against attack \nby insects and rodents."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trihexyphenidyl-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trihexyphenidyl Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trihexyphenidyl Hydrochloride\n,HCI\nC20H3|NO,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 337.9\nBenzhexol Hydrochloride is (AS)-1-cyclohexyl-1-phenyl- ■ \n3-piperidinopropan-l -ol hydrochloride.\nBenzhexol Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C20H31NO,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiparkinsonian.\nDescription. A white or creamy-white, crystalline powder; \nodourless or almost odourless."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trihexyphenidyl Hydrochloride\n,HCI\nC20H3|NO,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 337.9\nBenzhexol Hydrochloride is (AS)-1-cyclohexyl-1-phenyl- ■ \n3-piperidinopropan-l -ol hydrochloride.\nBenzhexol Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C20H31NO,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiparkinsonian.\nDescription. A white or creamy-white, crystalline powder; \nodourless or almost odourless."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with benzhexol \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \nbenzhexol hydrochloride.\nB. Dissolve 0.5 g in 5 ml of warm methanol and make just \nalkaline to litmus paper with 5 M sodium hydroxide', a \nprecipitate is produced, which, after recrystallisation from \nmethanol melts at about 114° (2.4.21).\nC. It gives the reactions of chlorides (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.2 to 6.2, determined in a solution prepared by \ndissolving 1.0 g in 50 ml of carbon dioxide-free water with the \naid of heat, cooling and diluting to 100.0 ml with the same \nsolvent.\nOptical rotation (2.4.22). - 0.10° to + 0.10°, determine in a\n^■0 per cent w/v solution in a mixture of 20 volumes of methanol \nand 80 volumes of dichloromethane.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 20 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml ofthe mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to \n200.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 10.0 ml ofthe solution \nto 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 10 mg oil-phenyl-3-(piperidin-\nl-yl)propan-l-one IPRS (trihexyphenidyl impurity A IPRS) \nin 10.0 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (b) \nto 100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (d). Add 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n1.0 ml of the reference solution (b) and dilute to 100.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 200 volumes of 'water, 0.2\nvolume of tri ethylamine, adjusted to pH 4.0 with \northophosphoric acid and 800 volumes of acetonitrile, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (d). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to trihexyphenidyl and \ntrihexyphenidyl impurity Anot less than 3.0.\nInject reference solution (a), (c) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 3 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof secondary peak cqn'esponding to trihexyphenidyl impurity \nA is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) (0.5 per. \ncent). The area of any other secondary peak is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution (a) (0.1 per cent) and the sum of \nthe areas of all secondary peaks is not more than 5 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak with an \narea less than 0.2 times the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.02 percent).\nSulphated ash (2,3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.7 g in 50 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid previously neutralised using 1-naphtholbenzein solution \nas indicator, warming and cooling, if necessary. Add 15 ml of \nmercuric acetate solution. Titrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimebutine-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimebutine Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"TRIMEBUTINE MALEATE\nIP 2022\n^.raength-10Per 1:6111 w/v-\nidentfflca‘10D\nA EvapQrate a V0'ume °f eye droPs containing 0.1 g of \nTrifluridineto dryness US'ng a rotary evaPorator and drY at\n¿Q^ under reduced pressure. On the residue, determine by \n^¿dabsptpti011 spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the\nneclruni with tha( obtained with trifluridine IPRS treated in \nj^sarne manner or with the reference spectrum of trifluridine.\ng ju the Assay» principal peak in the chromatogram \n(j^a'iiied with'the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nehromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\npH(2.4.24)?4.0to6.5.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n• .\nSolvent mixture. 5 volumes of acetonitrile and 95 volumes of\na 0.05M sodium dihydrogen orthophosphate, adjusted to pH\n6.0 withammonia.\nTest solution. Dilute the eye drops if necessary to obtain a\nsolution containing 0.02 per cent w/v of Trifluridine in the\nsolvent mixture.\nReferehce^blutibnfd). Dilute 1 volume of the test solution to\n100 volumes in,the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 volume of reference solution \n(a) to l 0 volumes in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n,, pctadecylsilane bonded to porous.silica.(5 pm) (Such\nas InertsilODS 2),\n- mobile phase: A. 0.0 5 M sodium dihydrogen ortho­\nphosphate, adjusted to pH 6.0 with ammonia.\n. \nB. acetonitrile,\n~ a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 263 nm,\n” injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime., .. Mobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(mmin.)\n.(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n95\n5\n95\n5\n85\n15\n' \n85 .\n15\n95\n5 ' '\nJWlWLS;\n; \n95\n5\nnject féfèrehci; solution (b). The test is not valid unless the\nWoin efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \nfiling factor is not more than 2.0. \n. ;\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. In.the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is hot more than 0.5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (a) (0.5 per cent) and; the sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (1.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 \ntimes the area of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.05 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Eye Drops.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as \ndescribed under test for Related substances with the following \nmodifications.\nReference solution. A0.02 per cent w/v solution of trifluridine \nIPR S in the solvent mixture.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution/\nCalculate the content of C|0HnF3N2O5 in the eye drops.\nStorage. Store protected from light, at a temperature between \n2°to8°. ' \n■ •\nTrimebutine Maleate\n,COOH\nXOOH\nC26H33NO9 \nMol Wt. 503.5\nTrimebutine MaleateArterolane Maleate is ‘ (27f5')-2- \n(dimethylamino)-2-phenylbutyl 3,4,5-trimethoxybenzoate (Z)- \nbutenedioafe.\nTrimebutine MaleateArterolane Maleate contains not less than \n99.0 per cent and not more than 101.5 per cent of C26H33NO9, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Treatment of irritable bowel syndrome.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"TRIMEBUTINE MALEATE\nIP 2022\n^.raength-10Per 1:6111 w/v-\nidentfflca‘10D\nA EvapQrate a V0'ume °f eye droPs containing 0.1 g of \nTrifluridineto dryness US'ng a rotary evaPorator and drY at\n¿Q^ under reduced pressure. On the residue, determine by \n^¿dabsptpti011 spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the\nneclruni with tha( obtained with trifluridine IPRS treated in \nj^sarne manner or with the reference spectrum of trifluridine.\ng ju the Assay» principal peak in the chromatogram \n(j^a'iiied with'the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nehromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\npH(2.4.24)?4.0to6.5.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n• .\nSolvent mixture. 5 volumes of acetonitrile and 95 volumes of\na 0.05M sodium dihydrogen orthophosphate, adjusted to pH\n6.0 withammonia.\nTest solution. Dilute the eye drops if necessary to obtain a\nsolution containing 0.02 per cent w/v of Trifluridine in the\nsolvent mixture.\nReferehce^blutibnfd). Dilute 1 volume of the test solution to\n100 volumes in,the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 volume of reference solution \n(a) to l 0 volumes in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n,, pctadecylsilane bonded to porous.silica.(5 pm) (Such\nas InertsilODS 2),\n- mobile phase: A. 0.0 5 M sodium dihydrogen ortho­\nphosphate, adjusted to pH 6.0 with ammonia.\n. \nB. acetonitrile,\n~ a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 263 nm,\n” injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime., .. Mobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(mmin.)\n.(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n95\n5\n95\n5\n85\n15\n' \n85 .\n15\n95\n5 ' '\nJWlWLS;\n; \n95\n5\nnject féfèrehci; solution (b). The test is not valid unless the\nWoin efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \nfiling factor is not more than 2.0. \n. ;\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. In.the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is hot more than 0.5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (a) (0.5 per cent) and; the sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (1.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 \ntimes the area of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.05 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Eye Drops.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as \ndescribed under test for Related substances with the following \nmodifications.\nReference solution. A0.02 per cent w/v solution of trifluridine \nIPR S in the solvent mixture.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution/\nCalculate the content of C|0HnF3N2O5 in the eye drops.\nStorage. Store protected from light, at a temperature between \n2°to8°. ' \n■ •\nTrimebutine Maleate\n,COOH\nXOOH\nC26H33NO9 \nMol Wt. 503.5\nTrimebutine MaleateArterolane Maleate is ‘ (27f5')-2- \n(dimethylamino)-2-phenylbutyl 3,4,5-trimethoxybenzoate (Z)- \nbutenedioafe.\nTrimebutine MaleateArterolane Maleate contains not less than \n99.0 per cent and not more than 101.5 per cent of C26H33NO9, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Treatment of irritable bowel syndrome.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"; <\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with trimebutine \nmaleate IPRS or with the reference spectrum of trimebutine \nmaleate.\n3871\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIMEBUTINE - MALEATE"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearanceof solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution in acetone\nis clear (2.4.1) and colourless (2.4.1).\nRelated substances.' Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). : -I -\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 0.24 g of anhydrous sodium \ndihydrdgen phosphate w\\ 1'80 ml of water, adjusted to pH 2.5 \nwith dilute drthophosphoric acid, dilute to 200 ml with water. \nAdd 50 ml of acetonitrile arid mix.\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 10.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution. Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to 100.0 \nml with the solvent mixture. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to 10.0 \nml with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system -■\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. dissolve 3.6 g of anhydrous sodium\ndihydrogen phosphate in 990 ml of water, adjusted to \npH 3.0 with orthophosphoric acid and dilute to 1000.0 \nml with water,\nB. acetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flowrate: 1.5 mlperminute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 215 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n78.\n22\n3 ■\n78 ■\n22 ■\n6.5\n65\n35\n15\n60\n40\n35\n60\n40\n40\n, .78 ■\n22\nÑame\nRelative\nretention time\nMaleic acid\n0.17\nTrimebutine maleate impurity E1\n0.9\nTrimebutine maleate (Retention time\nabout 12 minutes)\n1.0\nTrimebutine maleate impurity D2\n1.3\nTrimebutine maleate impurity C3\n■ ' : 1.4\nl(2Ä5)^2-(methylamino)-2-phenylbutyl 3,4,5-trimethoxybenzoate,- ■\n2(lJ?S)-iq(diméthylamino)methyI]-l-phéiiyIpropyl 3.4,5-trimcihoxy- \nbenzoate, \n.\nhnethyl 3,4,5-trimethoxybenzoate.\nInject the reference solution. The test is riot.valid unless tu \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates nu \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0. \nI > ri;,s;.:\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of a \npeak due to trimebutirie maleate impurity E is not more than 6 \ntimes the area of the principar peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (0.6 per cent), the area of \nany other secondary peak is not more thanthe area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram.obtained with the reference \nsolution (0.1 per cent) and tfcjp sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than. 10 times .the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution (1.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than \n0.5 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (0.05 pier cent) and any \npeak due to maleic acid. \n■ '\nI\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Solvent mixture. 15 volumes ofuw \nand 85 volumes of dioxan.\nDissolve 2 g in20 ml of a solvent mixture. 12 ml of the solution \ncomplies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method D (10 \nppm), using 10 ml oi lead standard solution (! ppm pb)m^ \nsame solvent mixture.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.3 gin 40 ml oí anhydrous acetic acW. Titrate \nwith 0.1 M perchloric acid. Determine;¡the „end point \npotentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.:\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.05035 gof\nC26H33NO9.\nI"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimetazidine-hydrochloride-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimetazidine Hydrochloride Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trimetazidine Hydrochloride Tablets\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride Tablets contain not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of trimetazidine dihydrochloride, C14 1-122N203,2HC1.\nCalculate the content of C141-122N203,2HCI. \nD. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of \nCI4H22\nTrimethobenzamide Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 \nper cent and not more than 100.5 per cent of C21 H29C1N205, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nN203,2HC1.\nUsual strength. 20 mg.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets. \nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). \nSolvent mixture. 50 volumes of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid and \n50 volumes of ethanol (95 per cent). \nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof the powder containing 15 mg of Trimetazidine Hydrochloride \nin about 75 ml of solvent mixture sonicate for 20 minutes and \ndilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture and centrifuge. \nDilute 5.0 ml of this solution to 50.0 ml with 0.1 Mhydrochloric \nacid.\nCategory. Antiemetic"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trimetazidine Hydrochloride Tablets\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride Tablets contain not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of trimetazidine dihydrochloride, C14 1-122N203,2HC1.\nCalculate the content of C141-122N203,2HCI. \nD. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of \nCI4H22\nTrimethobenzamide Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 \nper cent and not more than 100.5 per cent of C21 H29C1N205, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nN203,2HC1.\nUsual strength. 20 mg.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets. \nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). \nSolvent mixture. 50 volumes of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid and \n50 volumes of ethanol (95 per cent). \nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof the powder containing 15 mg of Trimetazidine Hydrochloride \nin about 75 ml of solvent mixture sonicate for 20 minutes and \ndilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture and centrifuge. \nDilute 5.0 ml of this solution to 50.0 ml with 0.1 Mhydrochloric \nacid.\nCategory. Antiemetic"},{"name":"identification","text":"Description. A white to off-white powder.\nA. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution:"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Related substances, the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution.\nB. Solvent mixture. Mix 3 volumes of ethanol (95 per cent) \nand 1 volume of water.\nIMMO\nTriturate a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 10 mg \nof Trimetazidine Hydrochloride with 10 ml of solvent mixture, \nfilter and evaporate the filtrate to a low volume. Add 2 ml of \nwater to residue and shake. To 1 ml of this solution add 1 ml of \np-henzoquinone, boil gently for 2 to 3 minutes and cool. A red"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Reference solution. Dilute 5.0 ml of 0.015 per cent w/v solution \nof trimetazidine dihydrochloride RS in the solvent mixture to \n50.0 ml with 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid. \nChromatographic system\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\ncolour is produced.\nSolvent mixture. 50 volumes of water and 50 volumes of \nacetonitrile.\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nTest s\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 p.m), \n- column temperature: 40°, \n- mobile phase: a mixture of 850 volumes of a buffer\nTest solution. Dissolve about 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in solvent mixture and diluted to 50.0 ml with \nsolvent mixture.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1, \nMedium. 900 ml of water, \nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nsolution prepared by dissolving 6.8 g of potassium \ndihydrogen phosphate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted to \npH 3.0 with orthophosphoric acid and 150 volumes of \nmethanol, \n-\nflow rate: I ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 230 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 10 4\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \ntrimethobenzamide hydrochloride RS in the solvent mixture. \nChromatographic system\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Withdraw a suitable volume of the medium and \nfilter. Dilute 4.0 ml of the filtrate to 25.0 ml with the water,\n-- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \n- mobile phase: A. 0.1 per cent v/v solution of\nfurther dilute 5.0 ml to 10.0 ml with 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.017 per cent w/v \nof trimetazidine dihydrochloride RS in water, further dilute 5 \nml to 50.0 ml with water, further dilute 5.0 ml to 25.0 ml with\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 1.0 per cent.\ntrilluoroacetic acid in water\nB. 0.1 per cent v/v solution of \ntrifluoroacetic acid in acetonitrile, \na linear gradient programme using the conditions given \nbelow, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \ninjection volume: 10 p1.\nwater, further dilute 5.0 ml to 10.0 ml with 0.1 Mhydrochloric \nacid. \nChromatographic system\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CI4H22N 203,2HC1 in the tablets.\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 um), \n-\ncolumn temperature: 40\", \n- mobile phase: a mixture of 850 volumes of a buffer\nexceeding o. \nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not\nTime \n(in min.)\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(per cent v/v)\nsolution prepared by dissolving 6.8 g of potassium"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimetazidine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimetazidine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trimetazidine Hydrochloride\nh3co\nh3co\nMol. Wt 339.3\nC14H22N2O3,2HC1\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride is I-[(2,3,4-trimethoxyphenyl)-\nmethyl]piperazine dihydrochloride.\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 \ncent and not more than 101.5 per cent, calculated on the.dn :\nbasis. ’ \n.... \n....\nCategory. Antiischemic metabolic agent.\n3872\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIMETAZIDINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nIi>2Q2?\npescripii°n‘ A white or almost white, crystalline powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trimetazidine Hydrochloride\nh3co\nh3co\nMol. Wt 339.3\nC14H22N2O3,2HC1\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride is I-[(2,3,4-trimethoxyphenyl)-\nmethyl]piperazine dihydrochloride.\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 \ncent and not more than 101.5 per cent, calculated on the.dn :\nbasis. ’ \n.... \n....\nCategory. Antiischemic metabolic agent.\n3872\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIMETAZIDINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nIi>2Q2?\npescripii°n‘ A white or almost white, crystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"nefcrnane by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nare the. spectrum with that obtained with trimetazidine\nA h drochloride IFRS or with the reference spectrum of \n¿tazidine ^'hydrochloride.\nB. it gives reaction (A) for chlorides (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. A 10 per cent w/v solution is clear\n^2.4 1) and not more intensely coloured than reference solution\n-\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.2 g of the substance under \nexaminationin 50.0 ml of water.\nReference solution. Dilute 2.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with water. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml \nwilhw/er.\nChromatographic: system\n- a stainless steel columnl5 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\n. octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 jim),\n. .r. column temperature: 30°,\n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 35.7 volumes of methanol\nand 64.3 volumes of a 0.29 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium heptanesulphonate, adjusted to pH 3.0 with \northdphdsphoric acid,\nB. methanol,\n~ a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n~ flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 240 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nTime Mobile phase A Mobile phase B \n(in min.)' (per cent v/v) (per cent v/v) \n\"W“ \n95 \n5\n75 \n25\n52 \n95 \n5\nEquilibrate the column for not less than 1 hour using 95\nvo'unies of mobilephase A and 5 volumes of mobile phase B.\niuject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates. The \nrelative retention time with reference to trimetazidine for (2,3,4- \nh^thoxjph^nyl)methanol (trimetazidine impurity D) is about\nA iOr2,3,4-trimethoxybenzaldehyde (trimetazidine impurity \n. • $ about 0,4, for ethyl 4-(2,3,4-trimethoxybenzyl)piperazine-\n■^o^ylate (trimetazidine impurity H) is about 0.6, for 1-\n’ ^'trimethoxybenzyl)piperazine (trimetazidine impurity A)\nand A'-methyltrimetazidine (trimetazidine impurity I) is about \n0.9, for l-(2,4,5-trimethoxybenzyl)piperazine (trimetazidine \nimpurity E) is about 0.95, for l-(2,4,6-trimethoxybenzyl) \npiperazine (trimetazidine impurity F) is about 1.4 and for 1,4- \nbjs(2,3,4-trimethoxybenzyl) piperazine (trimetazidine impurity \nB) is about 1.8.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of \neach peak due to trimetazidine impurity A, B, C, D, E, F, H, I \nmultiplied with correction factor 0.55 for impurity B, 0.37 for \nimpurity C and 0.71 for impurity F is not more than the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution (0.1 per cent), the area of any other \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.1 per cent) and the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks \nis not more than twice the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.2 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 times \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution (0.05 per cent).\nImpurity G. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), \ncoating the plate with silica gel G.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 20 volumes of concentrated \nammonia and 80 volumes of ethanol (95 per cent).\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of methanol.\nReference solution. Dissolve 22.6 mg of piperazine hydrate\n(trimetazidine impurity G) in 100 ml of methanol. Dilute 10 ml \nof the solution to 100 ml with methanol.\nApply to the plate 10 phòf each solution. Allowf the mobile \nphase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate at 105° for 30 minutes and \nspray with iodoplatinate reagent. Any secondary spot \ncorresponding to trimetazidine impurity G is not more intense \nthan the spot in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution (0.1 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). .. \n-\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. .\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 2.5 per centi determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105 ° over diphosphorus \npentoxide at a pressure riot exceeding 15 kPa.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.12 g in 50.0 ml of water,Add 1 ml of nitric \nacid and titrate with 0.1 M silver nitrate, determining the end­\npoint potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 M silver nitrate is-equivalent to 0.01696 g of \nC14H24C12N2O3. \n,\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimetazidine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimetazidine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trimetazidine Tablets\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride Tablets\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride Tablets1 contain not less than \n90.0 per cent and hot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of trimetazidine dihydrochloride, C ^2^263,2HC1.\nUsual strength. 20 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trimetazidine Tablets\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride Tablets\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride Tablets1 contain not less than \n90.0 per cent and hot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of trimetazidine dihydrochloride, C ^2^263,2HC1.\nUsual strength. 20 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"i\nA. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nB. Solvent mixture. Mix 3 volumes of ethanol (95 per cent) \nand 1 volume of water. ;\nTriturate a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 10 mg \nof Trimetazidine Hydrochibride with 10 ml of solvent mixture, \nfilter and evaporate the fillrate to a low volume. Add 2 ml of \nwater to residue and shake. To 1 ml of the solution add 1 ml of \np-benzoquinone, boil gently for 2 to 3 minutes and cool. A red\ncolour is produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),- ■ . - \n.........\nMedium.. 90.0 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2,4.14).\nTest solution. Withdrawn suitable volume of the medium and \nfilter. Dilute 4.0 ml of the filtrate to 25.0 ml with the water, \nfurther dilute. 5,0 ml to .10,0 ml with 0.1Mhydrochloric apid.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.017 per cent w/v\nof trimetazidine dihydrochlpride 1PRS hrwater, further dilute \n5 ml to 50.0.ml with water, further dilute 5.0 ml to 25.0 ml with \nwater, further dilute 5.0 ml to 10,0 ml with 0.1 Mhydrochloric \nacid.\nChromatographic system \n. \n.\n- a stainless steel column .15 cm X;4.6 mm,.packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n• \n• column temperature: 40°,\nj - mobile phase: a mixture of 850 volumes of a buffer\nsolution prepared by dissolving 6.8 g of potassium \ndihydrogen phosphate in 1000 ml of water and adjusted \nto pH 3.0 with ortho phosphoric acid, and 150 volumes \nof methanol,' '. \n.............\n- flow rate: ! ml per minute, .\n- spectrophotometer set at'230 nm,\n- injection volume: 50 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 5000 theoretical plates and\nIP202J\nTRIMETAZIDINE TABLETS\nthe relative standard deviation for replicate injections is rw \nmore than 1.5 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. : M\nCalculate the content of Ci4H22N2O3,2HCl. .; \n; -\nQ. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount T \nc14H22N2O3,2HC1. \ni: ■ \n■■■' \nof\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 50 volumes of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acida^ \n50 volumes of ethanol (95 per cent).\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof the powder containing 15 mg 0 f Trimetazidine Hydrochloride \nin about 75 ml of solvent mixture sonicate for 20 minutés and \ndilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture and centrifuge \nDilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with 0.1M hydrochloric \nacid. \n. ;\nReference solution. Dilute 5.0mf of 0.01,5 per cent w/v solution\nof trimetazidine dihydrochloride IPRS in the solvent mixture \nto 50.0 ml with 0.1 M hydrochloric acid. ;\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 um), \n- column temperature: 40°, ■ - \n:\n- mdbile phase: a mixture of 850 volumes of a buffer\nsolution prepared by dissolving 6.8 g of potassium \ndihydrogén phosphate in 1000 ml ofwater■ 'adjusted to \npH 3.0 with orthophosphoric acid âtid 150 volumes of \nmethanol,' \n■■ \n1 \n'-y\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, ' \n•’ : '\n- spectrophotometer set at 230 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl. ; \n■ \n■i-\nInject the reference solution. The test,is not. valid.unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injeptiohsis not more \nthan 1.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. . ,\nCalculate the content of Cu^^Ch^HCl in the tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimethadione-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimethadione Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trimethadione Capsules\nTroxidone Capsules contain not less than 95.0 per cent and\nUsual strength. 250 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trimethadione Capsules\nTroxidone Capsules contain not less than 95.0 per cent and\nUsual strength. 250 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":".;\nTo a quantity of the contents of the; capsules containing, l-.g of \nTroxidone add 25 ml of ether, set aside in a stoppered flask for \n20 minutes, decant the ether through a filter, and if an insoluble \nresidue remains, digest it with another 10-ml portion of ether \nas before, and filter into the first ether filtrate. Evaporate the \nether extracts to dryness with the aid of a current of air and \ndry the residue at a pressure of 2 kPa for 2 hours. The residue \ncomplies with the following tests.\nTest A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Tests B \nand C may be omitted if test A is carried.out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with troxidone 1PRS \nor with the teference spectrum of troxidone.\nB. To2ml ofa 5.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon dioxide-free \nwater (solution A) add 1 ml of barium hydroxide solution',^ \nwhite precipitate is produced which dissolves on the addition \nof 1 ml of 2 Mhydrochloric acid. ;\nC. Dissolve 0.3 g in a mixture of 5 ml of ethanolic potassium \nhydroxide solution and 5 ml of ethanol. (95. per eentf and \nallow to stand for 10 minutes. Add 0.05 ml ofphenolphthalein \nsolution and carefully add hydrochloric acid until the sfolutibh\nis neutral.; Evaporate to dryness bn a water-bath/ shake the \nresidue with four quantities, each of 5 ml, of ether1, filter the \ncombined ether extracts and.evaporate;the filtrated© dryness. \nThe residue,; after recrystallisatipn from 5,ml of toluene and \ndrying, melts at about 80° (2.4.21). \n.. :■>■>"},{"name":"Tests","text":";\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13). \n' ;\nTest solution. .To a quantity of the contents of the capsules \ncontaining about 1.0 gofTroxidone add25ml ofa 0.5 per cent \nw/v solution of 1-decanol (internal standard) in ethanol"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimethadione","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimethadione"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trimethadione\nH3C\nTest solution. Weigh 0.1 g of the substance under examination \nand dissolve in 10 ml of the solutions prepared by dissolving \n0. 1 25 g of 1-decanol (internal standard) in sufficient ethanol \nto produce 25.0 ml (solution B).\n0 \nH3C\n'C H3\nC6H9NO3 \t\nMol. Wt. 143.1\nReference solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of troxidone RS in sufficient \nsolution B to produce 10.0 ml.\nTroxidone is 3,5,5-trimethyloxazolidine-2,4-dione.\nChromatographic system"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trimethadione\nH3C\nTest solution. Weigh 0.1 g of the substance under examination \nand dissolve in 10 ml of the solutions prepared by dissolving \n0. 1 25 g of 1-decanol (internal standard) in sufficient ethanol \nto produce 25.0 ml (solution B).\n0 \nH3C\n'C H3\nC6H9NO3 \t\nMol. Wt. 143.1\nReference solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of troxidone RS in sufficient \nsolution B to produce 10.0 ml.\nTroxidone is 3,5,5-trimethyloxazolidine-2,4-dione.\nChromatographic system"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Troxidone contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C6H9NO3, calculated on the dried basis.\n- a stainless steel column 0.75 m x 3 mm, packed with\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nporous polymer beads (120 to 150 gm). \n- temperature:\nAssay: Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\ncolumn 210', \ninlet port at 240\" and detector at 270\". .\nDose:for a child, 250 mg to 500 mg daily, in divided doses: for\nTest solution: 'To a quantity of the contents of the capsules \ncontaining about 1.0 g of Troxidone add 25 ml of a 0.5 per cent\nan adult, 1 to 2 g daily, in divided doses.\nw/v solution of 1-decanol (internal standard) in ethanol \n(solution B), shake for 30 minutes, add sufficient of internal \nstandard solution to produce 100.0 ml, mix and centrifuge. \nUse the supernatant liquid.\nReference solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of troxidone RS in sufficient\nsolution B to produce 10.0 ml.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 0.75 m x 3 mm, packed with \nporous polymer beads (120 to 150 lam), \ntemperature: \ncolumn 210°, \ninlet port at 240° and detector at 270°, \n-\nflow rate: 30 ml per minute, using nitrogen as the carrier \ngas.\nInject 1 gl of the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content of cH 9NO3 in the capsules.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimethobenzamide-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimethobenzamide Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trimethobenzamide Hydrochloride\nCfrTHT'lNA \n: -/Molwt.424.9\nTRIMETHOPRIM\nIP2022 ... ............................... ............. ..........................................\n.^tg&eiizariMde Hydrochloride is A-'{[4-(2-dimethylamino- \nTn^ph^riyl]niethy 1}-3,4, S-trimethoxy benzamide\nbydwcWoride* .\n• thobènzamide Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 \n^^nt arid riot more than 100.5 per cent of C21H29C1N2O5, \nJùlàfeMiÜedriedbasis.\nDes£ription. A white to off-while powaei:\n1$ ^e Related substances, the principal peak in the\n¿{y-oriiatbgrarri obtairied with the test solution corresponds to\nthe pnncipàr peak in the chromatogram obtained with the\nreference solution.\nTests:--; • • Y ; '\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(MMTa ? •\nSolvent:mixture. 50 volumes of water and 50 volumes of\nTrimethoprim\nacetonitrile.\nTest solution) Dissolve about 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination;insolvent mixture and diluted to 50.0 ml with\nsolvent mixture.\nReference solution, A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \nirimethobenzamide hydrochloride IPRS in the solvent\nmixture.\nChromatographic system ' \"\n~ a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. 0.1 per cent v/v solution of\n■ ■;T4r$ùôroqcetic acid in water\nB. 0.1 per cent v/v solution of\n- \ntrifluoroacetic acid in acetonitrile,\n- \nalinear gradient programme using the conditions given \nbelow,\n\" ' flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n* \n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- \ninjection volume: 10 pl."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trimethobenzamide Hydrochloride\nCfrTHT'lNA \n: -/Molwt.424.9\nTRIMETHOPRIM\nIP2022 ... ............................... ............. ..........................................\n.^tg&eiizariMde Hydrochloride is A-'{[4-(2-dimethylamino- \nTn^ph^riyl]niethy 1}-3,4, S-trimethoxy benzamide\nbydwcWoride* .\n• thobènzamide Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 \n^^nt arid riot more than 100.5 per cent of C21H29C1N2O5, \nJùlàfeMiÜedriedbasis.\nDes£ription. A white to off-while powaei:\n1$ ^e Related substances, the principal peak in the\n¿{y-oriiatbgrarri obtairied with the test solution corresponds to\nthe pnncipàr peak in the chromatogram obtained with the\nreference solution.\nTests:--; • • Y ; '\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(MMTa ? •\nSolvent:mixture. 50 volumes of water and 50 volumes of\nTrimethoprim\nacetonitrile.\nTest solution) Dissolve about 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination;insolvent mixture and diluted to 50.0 ml with\nsolvent mixture.\nReference solution, A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \nirimethobenzamide hydrochloride IPRS in the solvent\nmixture.\nChromatographic system ' \"\n~ a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. 0.1 per cent v/v solution of\n■ ■;T4r$ùôroqcetic acid in water\nB. 0.1 per cent v/v solution of\n- \ntrifluoroacetic acid in acetonitrile,\n- \nalinear gradient programme using the conditions given \nbelow,\n\" ' flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n* \n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- \ninjection volume: 10 pl."},{"name":"Identification","text":"rime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\nmin.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n80\n20\n30 ■\n' 20\n80\n35 '\n80\n20 ■\n40 . '\n80\n- 20.\n^ect the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\n'.Unin efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and\nfactor is not more than 2.0.\nInject the test solution. In the chromatogram obtained with \ntest solution the per cent area of any secondary peak is not \nmore than 0.2 per cent and the total area per cent of peaks \nother than the principal peak is not more than 1.0 per cent, \ncalculated by area normalisation.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B(20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 4 hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.4 g in 50.0 ml of glacial acetic acid, add\n15 ml of mercuric acetate solution and titrate with 0.1 M \nperchloric acid determining the end point potentiometrically\n(2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.04249 g of \nCACINA\nCI4H18N4O3 \nMol. Wt. 290.3\nTrimethoprim is 5-(3,4,5-trimethoxybenzyl)pyrimidine- \n2,4-diamine.\nTrimethoprim contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C^Hi^Os, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or yellowish white powder.\nTest A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Tests B \nand C may be omitted if test A is carried out,\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with trimethoprim \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of trimethoprim.\nB. Dissolve 25 mg in 25 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and dilute \n2.0 ml to 100 ml with 0.1 Msodium hydroxide.\nWhen examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), the\nresulting solution shows an absorption maximum at about \n287 nm; absorbance at about 287 nm, about 0.49.\nTRIMETHOPRIM\nC. Dissolve about 25 mg in 5 ml of 0.005 M sulphuric acid, \nwith heating if necessary, add 2 ml of a 1.6 per cent wZv solution \nof potassium permanganate mO.I Msodium hydroxide. Heat \nto boiling and to. the hot solution add 0.4 ml of formaldehyde \nsolution. Mix, add 1 ml of 0.5 M sulphuric acid, mix and heat \nto boiling. Cool and filter. Add 2 ml of chloroform to the filtrate \nand shake vigorously. The chloroform layer exhibits a green \nfluorescence when examined under ultraviolet light at 365 nm."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. A 5.0 per cent w/v solution in amixture \nof 10 volumes of chloroform, 9 volumes of methanol and \n2 volumes of water is not more intensely coloured than \nreference solutionBYS7 (2.4.1).\nRelated substances. A. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2-4.14). .\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 25.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n10.0 ml with the mob He phase.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 20.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4 mm, packed with base\ndeactivated octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica \n(5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 30 volumes of methanol and\n70 volumes of a 0.14 per cent w/v solution of sodium \nperchlorate, adjusted to pH 3.6 with orthophosphoric\nacid,\n- flow rate: 1.3 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nTrimethoprim impurity CE\n0.8\n. —■ ■ ■ ■\nT ri methoprim impurity E2\n0.9\n0.53\nTrimethoprim (Retention time\nabout 5 minutes) ■\n1.0\n—\nTrimethoprim impurity A3\n1.5\n__\nTrimethoprim impurity G5\n2.1\n' - ■\nTrimethoprim impurity D4 <\n2.0\nTrimethoprim impurity B6\n•••• 2.3\n0.43\nTrimethoprim impurity J7\n2.7\n0.66\nTrimethoprim impurity F8\n4.0\n—\nTRS,)-(2,4-diäniiriopyrirriidinL5-yl)(3,4,5- trimethoxyphenyl) \nmethanol,\n24-amino-5-(3,4,5-trimethoxybcn'zyl)pyrimidin-2-ol, ; -"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimethoprim-and-sulphamethoxazole-oral-suspension-is-a","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimethoprim and Sulphamethoxazole Oral Suspension is a"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trimethoprim and Sulphamethoxazole Oral Suspension is a \nsuspension of Trimethoprim and Sulphamethoxazole in a \nsuitable flavoured vehicle. It contains 5 parts of \nSulphamethoxazole for 1 part, by weight, of Trimethoprim.\nTrimethoprim impurity C' \n0.8\nacid and mix with 400 ml of\nTrimethoprim impurity E2 \n0.9 \n0.53\n,4\nmethanol, \n{flow\nITT flow rate: 0.8 ml per minute,\nTrimethoprim (Retention time: \nabout 5 minutes) \n1.0\nTrimethoprim and Sulphamethoxazole Oral Suspension \ncontains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than\n- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl. \nName \t\nRelative \t\nCorrection \nretention time \t\nfactor\nTrimethoprim impurity A' \n1.5\n110.0 per cent of the stated amounts of trimethoprim, \nCI4H IsN403, and sulphamethoxazole,\nTrimethoprim impurity D 4 \n2.0 \n-\nTrimethoprim impurity G 5 \n2.1 \n-\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nTrimethoprim impurity B6 \n2.3 \n0.43\nTrimethoprim (Retention time:\nabout 4 minutes)\n1.0 \t\nDose. For children upto 1  year, Trimethoprim, 40 mg and\nTrimethoprim impurity J 7 \n2.7 \n0 .66\n1.8 \t 0 5 \t\nSulphamethoxazole, 200 mg daily, in divided doses. For children \n. \n1 to 5 years of age, Trimethoprim, 80 mg and Sulpha- \n4.9 \t\nmetliOiazole; 400 mg daily, in divided doses. For adults, \n7TrimOhopririi;60 to 480 mg and Sulphamethoxazole, 800 mg \nto Z:4 g daily, in divided doses. \n= \n= \t\n•\nTrimethoprim impurity I-1 1\nTrimethoprim impurity P \n4.0 \n-\nTrimethoprim  impurity 1 2 \n'methyl\n'(RS)77(2.4-datininopyrimidin-5-y1)(3,4.5- \t\ntrimethoxyphe nYI)\n3,4,5-trimethoxybenzoate, \t\n. 11... ,.. :t\nmetha01,..„\nrime