 \nthe spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution I \n(b) and any. other secondary: spot ,is; not more, intense than the I \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). I\nHeavy metals (2.3,13). 1.0 g compì ics with the limit test for. | \nheavy metals, Method B (2Ò ppm). \nI\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Ñót more than Ò. 1 per cent. \n? |\nWater (2.3,43 ). 4,0 to 6,0 per cent, determined on 0,4 g. \nI\nAssay . Dissolve 0.25 g, in a mixture of 5 0 ml of anhydrous | \nglacial acetic acid, 0,5. ml of acetic anhydride and add J 5 ml I\noí mercuric acetate, solution. Titrate with 0.1 Mperchloric | \nacid, using crystal violet solution as indicator. Carry. out 3 I \nblank titration. \n1\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid,is equivalent to 0.01574, g of I \nCiylkN2,HCl. ' \n7 ' . \n’ ’ . ’ \n...^ ■'I\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. \nI\nTablets\nTnproiidine Hydrpchloride Tablets\n. rojitfine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and\nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of triprolidine\n^drochloride>Ci9H22N2JlCI,H?().\nUsual strength. 10 mg.\nIdentification > \n; \n/\nX Extract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing\n10 mg of Triprolidine Hydrochloride with: ether, filter, discard \nthe ethef extract and dry the residue. Extract the residue with \nchloroform, filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness. Add \n01 nil of ether, stir and allowtoevaporate. ...\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with triprolidine hydrochloride IPRS or with , the \nreference spectrum of triprolidine hydrochloride.\nB. In the test.fpr Related substances, the principal spot in the\nchromatogramobtained with.the.test solution corresponds to\nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nApparatus No .f (Basket), . j\nDissolution (2.5.2). \n- ;\nMedium. 500 ml of acetate buffer pH 4.0 prepared by dissolving \n2.45 g of sodium acétate trihydrate and 4.9 g of glacial acetic \nacid in 1000 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm, 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, dilute suitably if necessary with \nthe medium, at the maximum at about 277 nm (2.4.7). Calculate\nthecontentofC|9H22N2.r!Cl.H20imthe medium from the \nabsorbanceobtained from a solution of known concentration \nof triprolidine hydrochloride IPRS in the dissolution medium.\nQ-Not dess than 80 per cent of the stated amount of\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4:17), coating the plate with silica gel I IF254. '\nMobilephase.A mixture of equal volumes of 2-butanone and \n^^methylformamide. . : , -\nsolution. Extract a quantity of powdered tablets containing\nof Triprolidine Hydrochloride with methanol, filter, \nevaporate to dryness and dissolve the residue in 1 ml of \nT’fZ/wwoZ.\nReference solution (a). A 0.02 per cent w/v. solution of\ntriprolidine IPRS in methanol.\nTRIS ODIUM EDETATE1 CONCENTRATE FOR INJECTION\nRéference■ solution (b): A 1.01per cent w/v■ solution of \ntriprolidine^hydrochloride IPRSSwméthdnbhi ■ T ¡ \ns dri\nReference solution (c). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \ntriprolidine: hydrochloride IPRS in methanol. -7 . - -\nApply to thè plate 5 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm. In the chromatogram obtained with the tesf solution \nany spot corresponding to Z-triprolidine is not more intense \nthan the spot in the chromatogram obtained with' referente \nsolution (a) and any other secondary spot is not more,in"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triprolidine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triprolidine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triprolidine Tablets\nTriprolidine Hydrochloride Tablets\nTriprolidine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0per cent ofthe stated amount oftriprolidine\nhydrochloride, CI9HzzNz,HCl,HzO.\nUsual strength. 10 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triprolidine Tablets\nTriprolidine Hydrochloride Tablets\nTriprolidine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0per cent ofthe stated amount oftriprolidine\nhydrochloride, CI9HzzNz,HCl,HzO.\nUsual strength. 10 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Extract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing\n10 mg ofTriprolidine Hydrochloride with ether, filter, discard\nthe ether extract and dry the residue. Extract the residue with\nchloroform, filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness. Add\n0.1 ml ofether, stir and allow to evaporate.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption\nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that\nobtained with triprolidine hydrochloride RS or with the\nreference spectrum oftriprolidine hydrochloride.\nB. In the test for Related substances, the principal spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to\nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography\n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel HF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture ofequal volumes of2-butanone and\ndimethylformamide.\nTest solution. Extract a quantity ofpowdered tablets containing\n10 mg of Triprolidine Hydrochloride with methanol, filter,\nevaporate to dryness and dissolve the residue in 1 ml of\nmethanol.\nReference solution (a). A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of\nZ-triprolidine RS in methanol.\nReference solution (b). A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of\ntriprolidine hydrochloride RS in methanol.\nReference solution (c). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of\ntriprolidine hydrochloride RS in methanol.\nApply to the plate 5 JlI of each solution. After development,\ndry the plate in air and examine in ultraviolet light at 254 nm. In\nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution any spot\ncorresponding to Z-triprolidine is not more intense than the\nspot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a)\nand any other secondary spot is not more intense than the\nspot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution\n(c).\nUniformity ofcontent. (For tablets containing 10mg or less).\nComply with the test stated under Tablets.\nPowder one tablet, weigh accurately a quantityofthe powder\ncontaining 7.5 mgofTriprolidine Hydrochloride and carry out\nthe Assay beginning at the words \"add 15 ml ofwater....\".\nCalculate the content ofC,9H22N2,HCl,H20 in the tablet.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and. powder 20 tablets. Weigh accurately a\nquantity ofthe powder containing about 7.5 mg ofTriprolidine\nHydrochloride, add 15 ml ofwater and 1g ofsodium chloride,\nshake for 2 to 3 minutes and add sufficient 5 M sodium\nhydroxide to make it alkaline. Extract with four quantities,\neach of20 ml, ofether and wash the combined extracts with\ntwo quantities, each of5 ml, ofa mixture ofequal volumes ofa\nsaturated solution ofsodium chloride and water. Extract the\nether solution with 20 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, wash\nthe ether with two quantities, each of 5 ml, ofwater and add\nthe washings to the acid extract. Heat on a water-bath for·\n30 minutes, cool and add sufficient water to produce50.0 ml.\nDilute 10.0 ml to 100.0 nil with 0.1 M hydrochloric acid.\nMeasure the absorbance of the resulting solution at the\nmaximum at about 290 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content ofC19H22N2, HCl, H20 taking 290 as the\nspecific absorbance at 290 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triprolidine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triprolidine"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triprolidine \t\ndrochloride\nMobile phase. A mixture of equal volumes of 2-butanone and \ndimethylfbrmamide.\nApply to the plate 5 p1 of each solution. After development,\nTest solution. Extract a quantity of powdered tablets containing \n10 mg of Triprolidine Hydrochloride with methanol, filter, \nevaporate to dryness and dissolve the residue in 1 ml of \nmethanol.\ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at\n254 nm. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution any\n, HCI , H2O\nspot corresponding to Z-triprolidine is not more intense than\nthe spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b) and any other secondary spot is not more intense than the\nReference solution (a). A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of \nZ-triprolidine RS in methanol.\nC H3\nspot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nC I9H22N2,HC1,H20\nM01. Wt. 332.9\nReference solution (b). A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of \ntriprolidine hydrochloride RS in methanol.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nTriprolidine Hydrochloride is (E)-2-(3-pyrrolidin- 1 -y1-\nMethod B (20 ppm).\nh e vy'\n1-(p-tolypprop-1-enyppyridine hydrochloride monohydrate.\nReference solution (c). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \ntriprolidine hydrochloride RS in methanol.\nnls \nleta  asii (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nSulphated\nTriprolidine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of CI9H22N 2, HCI, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nWater (2.3.43). 4.0 to 6.0 per cent, determined on 0.4 g.\nApply to the plate 5µl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \nany spot corresponding to Z-triprolidine is not more intense \nthan the spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) and any other secondary spot is not more intense \nthan the spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c).\nAssay. Dissolve 0.25 g, in a mixture of 50 ml of anhydrous \nglacial acetic acid, 0.5 ml of acetic anhydride and add 15 ml \nof mercuric acetate solution. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric \nacid, using crystal violet solution as indicator. Carry out a \nblank of o \nktitra. ti m\nion.\nCategory. Histamine H 1-receptor antagonist.\nDose. 10 to 20 mg daily, in divided doses.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; almost odourless.\n1 ml  \nperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.01574 g of"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triprolidine \t\ndrochloride\nMobile phase. A mixture of equal volumes of 2-butanone and \ndimethylfbrmamide.\nApply to the plate 5 p1 of each solution. After development,\nTest solution. Extract a quantity of powdered tablets containing \n10 mg of Triprolidine Hydrochloride with methanol, filter, \nevaporate to dryness and dissolve the residue in 1 ml of \nmethanol.\ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at\n254 nm. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution any\n, HCI , H2O\nspot corresponding to Z-triprolidine is not more intense than\nthe spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b) and any other secondary spot is not more intense than the\nReference solution (a). A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of \nZ-triprolidine RS in methanol.\nC H3\nspot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nC I9H22N2,HC1,H20\nM01. Wt. 332.9\nReference solution (b). A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of \ntriprolidine hydrochloride RS in methanol.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nTriprolidine Hydrochloride is (E)-2-(3-pyrrolidin- 1 -y1-\nMethod B (20 ppm).\nh e vy'\n1-(p-tolypprop-1-enyppyridine hydrochloride monohydrate.\nReference solution (c). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \ntriprolidine hydrochloride RS in methanol.\nnls \nleta  asii (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nSulphated\nTriprolidine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of CI9H22N 2, HCI, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nWater (2.3.43). 4.0 to 6.0 per cent, determined on 0.4 g.\nApply to the plate 5µl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \nany spot corresponding to Z-triprolidine is not more intense \nthan the spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) and any other secondary spot is not more intense \nthan the spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c).\nAssay. Dissolve 0.25 g, in a mixture of 50 ml of anhydrous \nglacial acetic acid, 0.5 ml of acetic anhydride and add 15 ml \nof mercuric acetate solution. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric \nacid, using crystal violet solution as indicator. Carry out a \nblank of o \nktitra. ti m\nion.\nCategory. Histamine H 1-receptor antagonist.\nDose. 10 to 20 mg daily, in divided doses.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; almost odourless.\n1 ml  \nperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.01574 g of"},{"name":"Identification","text":"C1911,2N2,HCI.\nA.Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with triprolidine \nhydrochloride RS or with the reference spectrum of triprolidine \nhydrochloride.\nUniformity of content. (For tablets containing 10 mg or less).\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nComplies with the test stated under Tablets.\nTake one tablet and transfer to a 50-m1 volumetric flask, add \n30 ml of water and sonicate for 15 minutes, make up to volume \nwith water, mix and filter, rejecting the first few ml of filtrate. \nPipette 25 ml of the filtrate and carry out the procedure \nmentioned under Assay begining at the words \"add 1 g of \nsodium chloride\nTriprolidine Tablets\nB. When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.001 per cent w/v solution shows absorption maxima at about \n230 nm and 276 nm; absorbance at about 230 nm, about \n0.50 and at about 276 nm, about 0.25. \t\nquo,"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trisodium-8","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trisodium 8-"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trisodium 8- \t\nAPTS \namino-pyrene-\nSample Preparation\n1,3,6-trisulfonic salt\nNaO3S \tSO3Na\nGlycoprotein samples for monosaccharide analysis should be \nfree of salts, excipients, and other carrier materials (low \nmolecular weight sugars are often used as excipients for \nbiopharmaceuticals). This can be achieved by a number of \nmethods, including the following:\nN-Glyca n Profiling\nReleased glycans can be analyzed or profiled by \nchromatographic, electrophoretic, or MS procedures and, in \ngeneral, by a combination of these. The choice of method can \nbe grouped according to the nature of the glycans and level \nof information required. Analysis of glycans provides \ninformation about the various populations of glycans present \non the.proteinAhigh-mannose, hybrid, complex).\n1.\nDialysis against water or a volatile buffer, using an \nappropriate membrane, and lyophilization;\n2.\nIIPI_C on an appropriate gel-permeation column eluted \nwith watet or a volatile buffer, monitored by UV absorbance \nor refractive index, and followed by lyophilization of the \nsample; or\nMonosaccharide analysis\nProfiling ofglycans by HPLC and/or by electrophoresis and\nDifferent quantitative monosaccharide assa \nfor a number of purposes. In the glycopr\nMS-- Profiling of fluorescent-tag–labelled glycans by HPLC\n-\n3. Sample trapping on a conventional RP-SPE cartridge such \nas a C18 or C8 SPE system, followed by washing away of \nsalts and excipients and elution of the required sample.\nQuantification\nThe common method for quantification of neutral sugars in \nglycoproteins depends on the color generated by heating \nglycans or glycoproteins in the presence of aqueous phenol \nin concentrated sulfuric acid. In many cases, the heat required \nfor this reaction is generated by addition of concentrated \nsulfuric acid to the glycoprotein—phenol mixture in water. Rapid \nand efficient mixing of the solutions is critical for consistent \nresults. Quantitative results are obtained by the simultaneous \nanalysis of standards to generate a standard curve of \nabsorbance against amount of saccharide and/or against a \nreference sample of the product under analysis.\nHydrolysis procedures for polysaccharides and glycoprotein \nglycan chains\nChromatographic methods for the identification and \nquantification of monosaccharide components require \nhydrolysis of the sample before analysis. Appropriate sample \npreparation is required because excipients or process-related \nimpurities may be saccharides, and residual salts may interfere \nwith the hydrolysis or the subsequent chromatographic \nseparation or with fluorophorelabeling. Sialic acid residues \ncan be released either by mild acid hydrolysis or by enzymatic \ntreatment, which leaves other sugar residues attached to the \npeptide backbone. Quantification of the amount of saccharide \npresent is based on addition of an internal standard before or \nafter hydrolysis. The most commonly used standard for sialic \nacid analysis by HPAEC is 3-deoxy-d-glycero-d-galacto-2- \nnonulosonic acid (KDN), and 2-deoxyglucose is widely used \nfor neutral sugars. Both of these sugars are acid labile and \nshould be added after the hydrolysis step. Accurate \nquantification depends both on stoichiometric hydrolysis and \na lack of degradation of the monosaccharide products during \nhydrolysis.\nDetermination of total sialic acids\nSialic acids occur in bacterial polysaccharides and \nglycoproteins generally as N-acetyl and N-glycolyl derivatives \nof neuraminic acid (Neu5Ac and Neu5Gc). The sialic acids \ncan be determined together with other monosaccharides by a \nprocedure that includes acid hydrolysis to liberate constituent \nmonosaccharides, followed by HPLC using an appropriate \nstandard mixture. Alternatively, total sialic acid content can \nbe determined by colorimetric procedures without the need \nfor hydrolysis. One method, commonly referred to as the \nWarren method, is based on the reaction of thiobarbituric acid \nwith the product of periodate oxidation of pottfamfitiKc"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Trisodium 8- \t\nAPTS \namino-pyrene-\nSample Preparation\n1,3,6-trisulfonic salt\nNaO3S \tSO3Na\nGlycoprotein samples for monosaccharide analysis should be \nfree of salts, excipients, and other carrier materials (low \nmolecular weight sugars are often used as excipients for \nbiopharmaceuticals). This can be achieved by a number of \nmethods, including the following:\nN-Glyca n Profiling\nReleased glycans can be analyzed or profiled by \nchromatographic, electrophoretic, or MS procedures and, in \ngeneral, by a combination of these. The choice of method can \nbe grouped according to the nature of the glycans and level \nof information required. Analysis of glycans provides \ninformation about the various populations of glycans present \non the.proteinAhigh-mannose, hybrid, complex).\n1.\nDialysis against water or a volatile buffer, using an \nappropriate membrane, and lyophilization;\n2.\nIIPI_C on an appropriate gel-permeation column eluted \nwith watet or a volatile buffer, monitored by UV absorbance \nor refractive index, and followed by lyophilization of the \nsample; or\nMonosaccharide analysis\nProfiling ofglycans by HPLC and/or by electrophoresis and\nDifferent quantitative monosaccharide assa \nfor a number of purposes. In the glycopr\nMS-- Profiling of fluorescent-tag–labelled glycans by HPLC\n-\n3. Sample trapping on a conventional RP-SPE cartridge such \nas a C18 or C8 SPE system, followed by washing away of \nsalts and excipients and elution of the required sample.\nQuantification\nThe common method for quantification of neutral sugars in \nglycoproteins depends on the color generated by heating \nglycans or glycoproteins in the presence of aqueous phenol \nin concentrated sulfuric acid. In many cases, the heat required \nfor this reaction is generated by addition of concentrated \nsulfuric acid to the glycoprotein—phenol mixture in water. Rapid \nand efficient mixing of the solutions is critical for consistent \nresults. Quantitative results are obtained by the simultaneous \nanalysis of standards to generate a standard curve of \nabsorbance against amount of saccharide and/or against a \nreference sample of the product under analysis.\nHydrolysis procedures for polysaccharides and glycoprotein \nglycan chains\nChromatographic methods for the identification and \nquantification of monosaccharide components require \nhydrolysis of the sample before analysis. Appropriate sample \npreparation is required because excipients or process-related \nimpurities may be saccharides, and residual salts may interfere \nwith the hydrolysis or the subsequent chromatographic \nseparation or with fluorophorelabeling. Sialic acid residues \ncan be released either by mild acid hydrolysis or by enzymatic \ntreatment, which leaves other sugar residues attached to the \npeptide backbone. Quantification of the amount of saccharide \npresent is based on addition of an internal standard before or \nafter hydrolysis. The most commonly used standard for sialic \nacid analysis by HPAEC is 3-deoxy-d-glycero-d-galacto-2- \nnonulosonic acid (KDN), and 2-deoxyglucose is widely used \nfor neutral sugars. Both of these sugars are acid labile and \nshould be added after the hydrolysis step. Accurate \nquantification depends both on stoichiometric hydrolysis and \na lack of degradation of the monosaccharide products during \nhydrolysis.\nDetermination of total sialic acids\nSialic acids occur in bacterial polysaccharides and \nglycoproteins generally as N-acetyl and N-glycolyl derivatives \nof neuraminic acid (Neu5Ac and Neu5Gc). The sialic acids \ncan be determined together with other monosaccharides by a \nprocedure that includes acid hydrolysis to liberate constituent \nmonosaccharides, followed by HPLC using an appropriate \nstandard mixture. Alternatively, total sialic acid content can \nbe determined by colorimetric procedures without the need \nfor hydrolysis. One method, commonly referred to as the \nWarren method, is based on the reaction of thiobarbituric acid \nwith the product of periodate oxidation of pottfamfitiKcid \nreleased in situ from the glycoprotein. Altern*y.4-1y,.the color \ncan be generated by the reaction of resorcinol*Mh neuraminic\n2.2.22. DRUG SUBSTANCES MANUFACTURED BY CELL CULTURE/FERMENTATION \nIP 2018 \n2.2.21. GLYCOPROTEIN AND GLYCAN ANALYSIS \nIP 2018\nHPAEC provides by default. Amperometric detection measures \nthe current, or charge, resulting from the oxidation or reduction \nof analyte molecules at the surface of a working electrode. \nElectrons are transferred from the electroactiveanalyte to the \nelectrode during oxidation reactions and in the opposite \ndirection during reduction reactions. This process allows \nsensitive and highly selective detection of analytes that can \nbe oxidized or reduced, but interfering species that are not \nelectroactive remain undetected. Carbohydrates are easily \noxidized at gold and platinum electrodes at high pH, and the \ncurrent generated is proportional to the carbohydrate \nconcentration.\nacid. For accurate quantification, include a reference standard \nsample is included in each measurement.\nthe glycosylation pattern of a biological drug substance when \nglycosylation occurs, as well as to develop the level of \ninformation required at each stage of production and at batch \nrelease. Then analytical procedures can be derived in a manner \nthat provides information relevant to fulfilling quality \nrequirements. In general, a combination of approaches and \ntechniques is needed, and more detailed glycan structural \nanalysis at early drug development stages is required. \nValidation considerations are central as method development \nand product knowledge progress.\nSelective release of sialic acids — Mild acid hydrolysis or \nenzymatic digestion can be used to selectively release sialic \nacid from glycoprotein glycan chains for quantification by \nchromatographic methods and for quantification of \nunwelcome forms such as Neu5Gc. More aggressive acid \nconditions are required in order to release neutral and amino \nsugars before chromatographi"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trisodium-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trisodium Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trisodium C itrate\n w/v of Sodium Chloride, Potasium Chloride and Calcium \nChloride. If the contents of the container are sterile, the label \nstates (1) Sterile Compound Sodium Chloride  Solution; \n(2) that the solution is not intended for injection.\nHO COONa \n,2H 20 \nNa00C \tCOONa\nSulphates (2.3.17). To 10 ml of solution A add 2 ml of\n7M hydrochloric acid and dilute to 15 ml with distilled water; \nthe resulting solution complies with the limit test for sulphates \n(150 ppm).\nC6H5Na307,2H20\nMol. Wt. 294.1\nto\nTartrates. To a solution of 1 g in 2 ml of water in a test-tube, \nadd 1 ml of a 10 per cent w/v solution of potassium acetate \nand 1 ml of 6 M acetic acid. Scratch the walls of the test-tube \nwith a glass rod; no crystalline precipitate is formed.\nSodium  Citrate is trisodium  2-hydroxypropane-1,2,3- \ntricarboxylate dihydrate.\nSodium Citrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than  101.0 per cent of C6H5Na3O7, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Trisodium C itrate\n w/v of Sodium Chloride, Potasium Chloride and Calcium \nChloride. If the contents of the container are sterile, the label \nstates (1) Sterile Compound Sodium Chloride  Solution; \n(2) that the solution is not intended for injection.\nHO COONa \n,2H 20 \nNa00C \tCOONa\nSulphates (2.3.17). To 10 ml of solution A add 2 ml of\n7M hydrochloric acid and dilute to 15 ml with distilled water; \nthe resulting solution complies with the limit test for sulphates \n(150 ppm).\nC6H5Na307,2H20\nMol. Wt. 294.1\nto\nTartrates. To a solution of 1 g in 2 ml of water in a test-tube, \nadd 1 ml of a 10 per cent w/v solution of potassium acetate \nand 1 ml of 6 M acetic acid. Scratch the walls of the test-tube \nwith a glass rod; no crystalline precipitate is formed.\nSodium  Citrate is trisodium  2-hydroxypropane-1,2,3- \ntricarboxylate dihydrate.\nSodium Citrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than  101.0 per cent of C6H5Na3O7, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trisodium-edetate-concentrate-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trisodium Edetate Concentrate for injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trisodium Edetate Concentrate for\nInjection \n■’\nTri sodium; Edetate Concentrate for Injection is a sterile \nsolution:in Water for Injections; containing 20 per cent w/v \nsolution of trisodium edetate prepared; by the interaction of\n3881\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDisodiumEdetateand Sodium Hydroxide. It should be diluted Tropicamide \nwith a suitable,diluent in-accordance with the manufacturer ’s \ninstructions.\nTrisodium EdetateGoncentfàtè'for Injectioncontains not less ; \nthan 19.0 per cent w/v and not more than 21.0 per cent w/v \nsolution of trisod iumedetate, CioH-sNoNasOs.\nCategory? Used in thè treatment of hypercalcaemia.\nDescription. A colourless solution. '"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trisodium Edetate Concentrate for\nInjection \n■’\nTri sodium; Edetate Concentrate for Injection is a sterile \nsolution:in Water for Injections; containing 20 per cent w/v \nsolution of trisodium edetate prepared; by the interaction of\n3881\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDisodiumEdetateand Sodium Hydroxide. It should be diluted Tropicamide \nwith a suitable,diluent in-accordance with the manufacturer ’s \ninstructions.\nTrisodium EdetateGoncentfàtè'for Injectioncontains not less ; \nthan 19.0 per cent w/v and not more than 21.0 per cent w/v \nsolution of trisod iumedetate, CioH-sNoNasOs.\nCategory? Used in thè treatment of hypercalcaemia.\nDescription. A colourless solution. '"},{"name":"Identification","text":"i -\nA. Dissolve 2 g in 25 ml of water, add 6 ml oi' lead nitrate \nsolution; shake-apd add, 3 ml of potassium iodide solution; \nno yellow precipitate is produced. Make alkaline to redlitmus \npaper with 2 M ammonia and add 5 ml of ammonium oxalate \nsolution; no precipitate is produced.\nB, Dissolve 0.5 g in 10 ml of water, add0.5 ml of a 10 per cent \nw/v solution of calcium chloride/make alkaline to red litmus \npaper with '2 Mammonia and add 3 ml Ofaminonium oxalate \nsolution; no precipitate is produced.\nC. Evaporate to drynèss arid ignite. The residue gives thè \nreactions of sodium salts (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24): 7.0 to 8.0.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3), If necessary, dilute the infusion \n.with water BET. to give, a solution containing 10. mg per ml.\nThe solution contains not more than 1.4 Endotoxin Unit per \nml\nOther tests. Cbrriply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Concentrated Solutions for Injection).\nAssay. Dilute 10.0 ml to 100.0 ml with water and use the solution \nto titrate a mixture of 25.0 ml of 0.05 M magnesium sulphate \nand 10 ml of ammonia buffer pH 10.9 using mordant black 11 \nmixed triturate';as indicator.\n1 ml of 0.05 M magnesium sulpìiàte is equivalent to 0.01791g \nofCJ0H13N2Na3O8.;f\nStorage. Store in \ncontainers.\nLabelling.. The label states (1) the strength in terms of \nanhydrous trisodium edetate contained in a suitable dose­\nvolume; (2) ‘Trisodium Edetate Concentrate for Injection’; \n(3) that the solution must be diluted with neither: Sodium \nChloride Intravenous Infusion or Dextrose Intravenous\nInfusion before administration. \nh.i.-\nTRISODIUM EDETATE CONCENTRATE FOR INJECTION\n$20^\nC17H20N2O2\nMol. Wt. 284.4\nTropicamide is (/i^-A'-ethyl-j-hydroxy^-phenyl-.V-^p^^ \n4-ylmethyI)propionamide\n'Tropicamide contains notless than 99.0 per,cent arid notino^\nthan 101.0 per cent of Cj7I l2oN202,. calculated on the dried \nbasis. \nj - T .jW T'W\nCategory. Mydriatic; cycloplegic.\nDescription. A whitéor almost white, crystalline powder. ;"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2,4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with /rapicamide \nIPRS or with' thè reference spectrum of tropi’eamide?;;' '■: ■\nB. When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2,4,7), a \n0.003 per cent w/v solution in 0.1 M hydrochloric acidshvys \nan absorption maximum only at about 254 nm; absorbance at \nabout 254 nm, about 0.54. \n' 5 h ?\nC. Dissolve 5 mg in 3 ml of a mixture of 9 ml of acetic anhydride,\n1 ml of 6 M acetic aezi/and 0.1 g of citric aóid and heat on a \nwater-bath for 5 to lO minutes; a .reddish yellow colour is \nproduced. \n• ¡'òlfirOnO!\nTests ... \n. \n...... .\nRelated substances. Determine by;liquid,chromatography \n(2.4.14). ' ' . \n■ \n\\ \n... •\nTest soItitioh. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 3 ml of acetonitrile and dilute to 5 0.0 ml with\nwater.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the; test solution to\n100.0 ml with water. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solutionto 10.0ml with\nwater.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 5 mg each of tropicamide\nimpurity A, tropicamide impurity C IP RS and tropicamide \nhermetic ally-sealed, lead-free glass impurity If IPRS in 2 ml of acetonitrile and dilute to 50-0 mi\nwith water. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to 10 iO ml with \nReference solution (cf. Dilute 1.5 ml of reference solution (h)\nto 10.0 nil with water. .   ; \ny t *\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed w\n< eridcapped octadecylsilane bonded to porous sibca\n(3 pm), ■ \n•••\n_colunintemperature: 40°,\n^ tno.bile phase: a mixture of 73 volumes of solution\nPrepared by dissolving 0-135 g of sodium dodecyl\nsUlphate and 3.4 ml of orthophosphoric acid in 950 ml \nof vvutor, adJusted 1° pH 3.0 with sodium hydroxide \n^ëlÜÜoïi'^ià. dilute to 1000 ml with water and 27 volumes\nofqcetowhn7e, \n.......\nflow-rate: 0.8 ml per minute,\n„spectrophotometer set at 210 nm and 254 nm, \n„injection volume: 15 pl.\nCorrection\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor\nTropicamide impurity C1\n0.4\n—\nTropicamide impurity A2\n0.5\n0.8 •\nTropicamide impurity D3\n0.8\ni \n■ .•—\nTropicamide (Retention time:\n10\naijoutinnujuics;\nTropicamide impurity B'1\n•• ■■ 2.3 ••\n0.6\n'troptc'acîd,\n:A'*(pyridin-4-ylmethyl)ethanamine.\nfytiénÿïacëiic acid,\n‘;V-ethyl-2-phenyl-Y-(pyridjn-4-ylrnethyl)propenamide.\nInject reference .solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution at 210 nm between the peaks due to impurities C\nand A and impurities A and D is not less than 2.0."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Inject reference solution (a), (c) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 3 times the retention time of the principal peak.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area \nofanypeakcorre spending to tropicamide impurity B at 254\nnm is notmqre than 3 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.3 per\ncent). The area of any peak corresponding to tropicamide \nimpurity A at 254 nm is not more than 1.5 times the area of the \npnncipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0: i 5 percent) i The Urea of any peak corresponding\nIo tropicamide impurity C at 210 nm is not more than the area \n0^^bff^spdhdihg peak in the chromatograrii obtained with \nreference solution (c) (0.15 per cent). The area of any peak\ncorresponding to tropicamide impurity D at 210 nm is not\nm°re than the area of the corresponding peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) (Oil 5 per"},{"name":"Tests","text":"cenO>the area of any other secondary peak at 254 nm is not \n®orethan the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained withrefcrence solution (a) (0.1 per cent) and the sum \nof areas of all the secondary peaks other than tropicamide\nIID^ritie§ ^ aild D at 254 nm is not more than 5 times the area \nof the principal peak' in the chromatogram obtained ' with \n^ference solution (a) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak at\nwith an area less than 0.5 times the area.of the principal\nTRROPICAMIDE EYE DROPS\npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.05 percent). .\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0:1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 80° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa for 4 hours. •. : \n'\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in 80 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid. Titrate with 0,1Mperchloric, acid, using1-naphthol- \nbenzein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02844 g of\nCpf^oNzCh- \n. \n..... ..\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. ,,"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tromethamine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tromethamine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"; Tromethamine; \nTrometamol; TEAM; Tris (hydroxymethyl) methylamine; 2- \nAmino-2-(hydroxymethyl)-13-propancdiol: C4Hi iNOj f 121.13.\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce. \n.\nWhite crystals or crystalline powder; odour, characteristic;,\nmp, about 170°.\nTris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethane reagent: Weigh \naccurately 1.5g of tris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethane in 100 \nml volumetric flask, dissolve and dilute to,volume with water \nand mix. Transfer 40 ml of this solution to a 200 ml volumetric \nflask and dilute to volume with dimethyl sulphoxide. Use this\nreagent within 4 hours.\nTropic Acid; (2i?S')-3-hydroxy-2-phenylpropanoic acid: \nC9H10O3= 166.2\nTrypsin: Freeze-dried, salt-free beef trypsin containing not \nless than 9000 benzoylarginine ethyl ester units per mg.\nProteolytic enzyme obtained by activation of tyrpsinogen \nextracted from the pancreas of beef (Bos taurus Linn).\nStore at a temperature not exceeding 4°."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"; Tromethamine; \nTrometamol; TEAM; Tris (hydroxymethyl) methylamine; 2- \nAmino-2-(hydroxymethyl)-13-propancdiol: C4Hi iNOj f 121.13.\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce. \n.\nWhite crystals or crystalline powder; odour, characteristic;,\nmp, about 170°.\nTris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethane reagent: Weigh \naccurately 1.5g of tris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethane in 100 \nml volumetric flask, dissolve and dilute to,volume with water \nand mix. Transfer 40 ml of this solution to a 200 ml volumetric \nflask and dilute to volume with dimethyl sulphoxide. Use this\nreagent within 4 hours.\nTropic Acid; (2i?S')-3-hydroxy-2-phenylpropanoic acid: \nC9H10O3= 166.2\nTrypsin: Freeze-dried, salt-free beef trypsin containing not \nless than 9000 benzoylarginine ethyl ester units per mg.\nProteolytic enzyme obtained by activation of tyrpsinogen \nextracted from the pancreas of beef (Bos taurus Linn).\nStore at a temperature not exceeding 4°."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tropic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tropic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tropic acid \n1.3 \n—\nHyoscine impurity G1 \n2.6 \n0.41\n1(17J)2Jf,4S,5Si7s,9r)-9-butyl-9-methyl-7-[(2-phenylprop-2- \nenoyl)oxy]-3-oxa-9-azoniatricyclo[3.3.102,4]iionane (apo-N- \nbutylhyoscine).\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to hyoscine butylbromide \nand tropic acid is not less than 1.5.'\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to hyoscine impurity G is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (1.0 per cent), the area of any peak \ncorresponding to tropic acid is not more than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b) (1.2 per cent), the area of any other secondary \npeak is not more than 0.2 times the area ofthe principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.2 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 2.5 times the area ofthe principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(2.5 per cent). Ignore the peak due to bromide and any peak \nwith an area less than 0.1 times the area ofthe principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.1 percent).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 8.75 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of hyoscine butylbromide.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of injection containing about \n40 mg of Hyoscine Butylbromide in 100.0 ml of 0.001M \nhydrochloric acid.\nReference solution. A 0.04 per cent w/v solution of hyoscine\nbutylbromide IPRS in 0.00IM hydrochloric acid.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (10 pm) (Such as \nLichrosorb C8),\n- mobile phase: a buffer solution prepared by\ndissolving 2.0 g of sodium lauryl sulphate in a mixture \nof 370 volumes of 0.001 Mhydrochloric acid and \n680 volumes of methanol,\n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- injectionvolume:20pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, tailing \nfactor is not more than 2.0 per cent and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C2iH30BrNO4 in the injection.\nStorage. Store at a temperature not exceeding 30° protected \nfrom light and moisture."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Tropic acid \n1.3 \n—\nHyoscine impurity G1 \n2.6 \n0.41\n1(17J)2Jf,4S,5Si7s,9r)-9-butyl-9-methyl-7-[(2-phenylprop-2- \nenoyl)oxy]-3-oxa-9-azoniatricyclo[3.3.102,4]iionane (apo-N- \nbutylhyoscine).\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to hyoscine butylbromide \nand tropic acid is not less than 1.5.'\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to hyoscine impurity G is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (1.0 per cent), the area of any peak \ncorresponding to tropic acid is not more than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b) (1.2 per cent), the area of any other secondary \npeak is not more than 0.2 times the area ofthe principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.2 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 2.5 times the area ofthe principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(2.5 per cent). Ignore the peak due to bromide and any peak \nwith an area less than 0.1 times the area ofthe principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.1 percent).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 8.75 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of hyoscine butylbromide.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of injection containing about \n40 mg of Hyoscine Butylbromide in 100.0 ml of 0.001M \nhydrochloric acid.\nReference solution. A 0.04 per cent w/v solution of hyoscine\nbutylbromide IPRS in 0.00IM hydrochloric acid.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (10 pm) (Such as \nLichrosorb C8),\n- mobile phase: a buffer solution prepared by\ndissolving 2.0 g of sodium lauryl sulphate in a mixture \nof 370 volumes of 0.001 Mhydrochloric acid and \n680 volumes of methanol,\n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- injectionvolume:20pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, tailing \nfactor is not more than 2.0 per cent and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C2iH30BrNO4 in the injection.\nStorage. Store at a temperature not exceeding 30° protected \nfrom light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tropicamide-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tropicamide Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tropicamide Eye Drops\nTropicamide Eye Drops are a sterile solution of Tropicamide \nin Purified Water. They may contain stabilisers, suitable \nantimicrobial agents and .suitable substances to increase the \nviscosity of the solution. \n' ''.\nTropicamide Eye .Drops containnot less than.95.0 per .cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntropicamide, C17H20N2O2. \nV ; ■/\nUsual strengthsi 0:5 per cent w/v; 1.0 per cent Wv.'\nA. Shake a volume containing 20 mg of Tropicamide with\n10 ml of chloroform, dry the chloroform layer over anhydrous \nsodium sulphate, filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness. \nDissolve the residue in minimum quantity of chloroform-, add \ndropwise to finely powdered potassium bromide 1R, mix and \ndry at 60°.\nOn the residue determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with tropicamide 1PRS or with the reference spectrum \nof tropicamide.\nB. When examined inthe range 230 nmto 3£0 nm (2.4.7), the \nfinal solution obtained in the Assay showrs ah absorption \nmaximum at about 254 nm. : \n/ \n/\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 5.8. / J ■\nRelated substances; Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile plidse. A mixture of 95 volumes of chloroform,\n5 volumes, of methanol and 0;5 volume of strong ammonia \nsolution: \n'"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tropicamide Eye Drops\nTropicamide Eye Drops are a sterile solution of Tropicamide \nin Purified Water. They may contain stabilisers, suitable \nantimicrobial agents and .suitable substances to increase the \nviscosity of the solution. \n' ''.\nTropicamide Eye .Drops containnot less than.95.0 per .cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntropicamide, C17H20N2O2. \nV ; ■/\nUsual strengthsi 0:5 per cent w/v; 1.0 per cent Wv.'\nA. Shake a volume containing 20 mg of Tropicamide with\n10 ml of chloroform, dry the chloroform layer over anhydrous \nsodium sulphate, filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness. \nDissolve the residue in minimum quantity of chloroform-, add \ndropwise to finely powdered potassium bromide 1R, mix and \ndry at 60°.\nOn the residue determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with tropicamide 1PRS or with the reference spectrum \nof tropicamide.\nB. When examined inthe range 230 nmto 3£0 nm (2.4.7), the \nfinal solution obtained in the Assay showrs ah absorption \nmaximum at about 254 nm. : \n/ \n/\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 5.8. / J ■\nRelated substances; Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile plidse. A mixture of 95 volumes of chloroform,\n5 volumes, of methanol and 0;5 volume of strong ammonia \nsolution: \n'"},{"name":"TRROPICAMIDE EYE DROPS","text":"7m.vc>/uzzozz.Exrracta volume containing 50 mg of tropicamide \nwith 10 ml of chloroform, filter through sodium sulphate \nanhydrousand dilute to 10,0 ml with chloroform.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1 volume of the eye .drops to \n200 volumes with water,w ...............,....,t'..7,;\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 volume of the eye drops to \n500 volumes with water. . ■ \n... ; \n, . - \n, /\nApply to, the plate 40.pl of each solution. After, development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm. Any secondary, spot in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution is not more intense ttiâri the spot in, the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) and not \nmore than one such spot is more intense than the spot in thé \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Eye Drops.\nAssay. To a volume containing about 30 mg o f Tropicamide \nadd sufficient water to produce 100.0 ml- To 10.0 ml of the \nresulting solution add 2 ml of a 10 per cent w/v solution of \nanhydrous sodium carbonate and extract with four quantities, \neach of 20 ml, of chloroform. Wash the combined chloroform \nextracts with 25 ml of phosphate buffer pH 6.5. Wash the \naqueous layer with' 10 ml of chloroform, combine the \nchloroform extracts and shake with four quantities, each of \n20 ml, of 0.5 M sulphuric acid. Combine the acid extracts; \ndilute to 100,.0 ml with 0.5 M sulphuric acid and measure the \nabsorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at about \n254nm(2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C J7H20N2O2 taking 172 as the specific \nabsorbance at 254 nm.. \n. .... \n-\nStorage. Store in a: refrigerator (8° to 15°). It should not be \nallowed to freeze. : \n- \n■'\nTrospium Ghloride\nGdhoClNCh \n. Mol.Wt. 428.0\n. V-.. \nj ‘.j \nj.: : ; ; :>. \n-, ... ■....... \n."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tropicamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tropicamide"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tropicamide\ncolumn temperature: 40°,\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 73 volumes of solution\nH3C\nprepared by dissolving 0.135 g of sodium dodecyl \nsulphate  and 3.4 ml of orthophosphoric acid in 950 ml \nof water, adjusted to pH 3.0 with sodium hydroxide \nsolution and dilute to 1000 ml with water and 27 volumes \nof acetonitrile, \n-\nflow rate: 0.8 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm and 254 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 15 IA\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 80° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa for 4 hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in 80 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid. Titrate with O./ M perchloric acid,  using 1 -naphthol-\nbenzein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\na \nCI7H20N202 \t\nMol. Wt. 2\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02844 g of \nCI7H2oN202•\nTropicamide is (RS)-N-ethyl-3-hydroxy-2-phenyl-N-( \n4-ylmethyl)propionamide\nName\nRelative \t\nCorrection \nretention time \t\nfactor\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nTropicamide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and  not \nthan 101.0 per cent of C I7H201\\1202, calculated on the \nbasis.\nTropicamide impurity C' \t0.4\n0.8 \tTropicamide Eye Drops\nTropicamide impurity A 2 \t\n0.5\nCategory. Mydriatic; cycloplegic.\nTropicamide impurity D 3 \t\n0.8 \nTropicamide (Retention time: \nabout 11 minutes) \t\n1.0 \nTropicamide impurity B4 \t2.3 \t 0.6\nTropicamide Eye Drops are a sterile solution of Tropicamide \nin Purified Water. They may contain stabilisers, suitable \nantimicrobial agents and suitable substances to increase the \nviscosity of the solution.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nodourless or almost odourless."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tropicamide\ncolumn temperature: 40°,\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 73 volumes of solution\nH3C\nprepared by dissolving 0.135 g of sodium dodecyl \nsulphate  and 3.4 ml of orthophosphoric acid in 950 ml \nof water, adjusted to pH 3.0 with sodium hydroxide \nsolution and dilute to 1000 ml with water and 27 volumes \nof acetonitrile, \n-\nflow rate: 0.8 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm and 254 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 15 IA\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 80° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa for 4 hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in 80 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid. Titrate with O./ M perchloric acid,  using 1 -naphthol-\nbenzein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\na \nCI7H20N202 \t\nMol. Wt. 2\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02844 g of \nCI7H2oN202•\nTropicamide is (RS)-N-ethyl-3-hydroxy-2-phenyl-N-( \n4-ylmethyl)propionamide\nName\nRelative \t\nCorrection \nretention time \t\nfactor\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nTropicamide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and  not \nthan 101.0 per cent of C I7H201\\1202, calculated on the \nbasis.\nTropicamide impurity C' \t0.4\n0.8 \tTropicamide Eye Drops\nTropicamide impurity A 2 \t\n0.5\nCategory. Mydriatic; cycloplegic.\nTropicamide impurity D 3 \t\n0.8 \nTropicamide (Retention time: \nabout 11 minutes) \t\n1.0 \nTropicamide impurity B4 \t2.3 \t 0.6\nTropicamide Eye Drops are a sterile solution of Tropicamide \nin Purified Water. They may contain stabilisers, suitable \nantimicrobial agents and suitable substances to increase the \nviscosity of the solution.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nodourless or almost odourless."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Tropicamide Eye Drops contain not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntropicamide, CI7H20N202.\n'tropic acid,\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tropicamide \nRS or with the reference spectrum of tropicamide.\n2N-(pyridin-4-ylmethyl)ethanamine,\n'phenylacetic acid.\nUsual strengths. 0.5 per cent w/v; 1.0 per cent w/v.\nW-ethyl-2-phenyl-N-(pyridin-4-ylmethyl)propenamide.\nB. When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.003 per cent w/v solution in 0.1 M hydroc:hloric acid shows \nan absorption maximum only at about 254 nm; absorbance at \nabout 254 nm, about 0.54.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution at 210 nm between the peaks due to impurities C \nand A and impurities A and D is not less than 2.0."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Shake a volume containing 20 mg of Tropicamide with\n10 ml of chloroform,  dry the chloroform layer over  anhydrous \nsodium sulphate, filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness.\nInject reference solutions (a), (c) and the test solution. Run \nthe chromatogram 3 times the retention time of the principal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \nthe area of any peak corresponding to tropicamide impurity B \nat 254 nm is not more than 3 times the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.3 per cent). The area of any peak corresponding to \ntropicamide impurity A at 254 nm is not more than 1.5 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (0.15 per cent). The area of any peak \ncorresponding to tropicamide impurity C at 210 nm is not more \nthan the area of the corresponding peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) (0.15 per cent). The area \nof any peak corresponding to tropicamide impurity D at \n210 nm is not more than the area of the corresponding peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) \n(0 . 1 5 per cent). The area of any other secondary peak at\nC. Dissolve 5 mg in 3 ml ofa mixture of9 ml of acetic anhydride,\n1 ml of 6 M acetic acid and 0.1 g of citric acid and heat on a \nwater-bath for 5 to 10 minutes; a reddish yellow colour is \nproduced.\nDissolve the residue in minimum quantity of chloroform,  add \ndropwise to finely powdered potassium bromide IR, mix and \ndry at 60°. \nOn the residue determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with tropicamide RS or with the reference spectrum \nof tropicamide."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 3 ml ofacetonitrile and dilute to 50.0 ml with\nB. When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), the \nfinal solution obtained in the Assay shows an absorption \nmaximum at about 254 nm.\nwater.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to"},{"name":"Tests","text":"100.0 ml with water. Dilute 1.0 ml of this solution to 10.0 nil \nwith water\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 5.8.\nl'Refe;*.rice .solittion (b). Dissolve 5 mg each of tropicamide\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4. 1 7), coating the plate with silica gel GF254. \ncontainers. \t\nimpurity A. tropicamide impurity CRS and tropicamide\nMobile phase. A mixture of 95 volumes of chloroform, \n5 volumes of methanol and 0.5 volume of strong ammonia \nsolution.\nTest solution. Extract a volume containing 50 mg oftropicamide \nwith 10 ml of chloroform, filter through sodium sulphate \nanhydrous and dilute to 10.0 ml with chloroform.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1 volume of the eye drops to \n200 volumes with water.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 volume of the eye drops to\n500 volumes with water.\nApply to the plate 40111 of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm. Any secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution is not more intense than the spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) and not \nmore than one such spot is more intense than the spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Eye Drops.\nAssay. To a volume containing about 30 mg of Tropicamide \nadd sufficient water to produce 100.0 ml. To 10.0 ml of the \nresulting solution add 2 ml of a 10 per cent w/v solution of \nanhydrous sodium carbonate and extract with four quantities, \neach of 20 ml, of chloroform. Wash the combined chlorofbrm \nextracts with 25 ml of phosphate buffer pH 6.5. Wash the \naqueous layer with 10 ml of chloroform, combine the \nchloroform extracts and shake with four quantities, each of \n20 ml, of 0.5 M sulphuric acid. Combine the acid extracts, \ndilute to 100.0 ml with 0.5 M sulphuric acid and measure the \nabsorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at about \n254 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C rE20N,O, taking 172 as the specific \nabsorbance at 254 nm.\nStorage. Store in a refrigerator (8\" to 15'). It should not be \nallowed to freeze."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trospium-chloride-is3-2-iiydroxy-2-2-diphenylacetoxy-spiro","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trospium Chloride is3-(2-IIydroxy-2,2-diphenylacetoxy) spiro"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trospium Chloride is3-(2-IIydroxy-2,2-diphenylacetoxy) spiro \n[bicyclo[3.2.IJoctane-Sjl'-pyTroiidinj-l'-iumchloride. 0C ,\nTROSPIUM CHLORIDE TABLETS\nTrospium Chloride contains not less than 99.0 per ce 'll \nnotmore than 101.0 per cent of Q5H30ClNO3, calculated^ \ndriedbasis.< - - ■ V; < \n: ■■ \n“\"W\n^X^Bi4ìylàcètic‘ acid (benzilic, acid)/ ■ -\nAssay. Dissolve 0.3 g in 50.0 nil of waterTitrate with 0.7 M \nsilver nitrate determining the end-point potentiometr