ound A. Not more than 10 ppm. v\nspray with 0.2 per cent w/v solution.of ninhydrin va & .mixture \nof 95 volumes of butyl alcohol and,5 volumes 2 M acetic acid \nand heat at 100° to 105° for about 15 minutes. Examine the \nplate under day light. Any spot in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution is riot large or mòre intense than the \nspot in the chromatogram; obtained with, reference solution \n(a). The chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b)\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). 7 \" -\nLosson drying (2.4.19): Not more than 0.3 per cent, determined \nonl.Og by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours.\nTest solution. Dissolve 100 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of wafer.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.2 g of the substance under examination, in \na mixture of 3 ml oiformic acid and 50 ml of glacial acetic\nReference solution^ A 0.001 per cent w/v solution of \ntryptophan related compound A IPRS in wafer. Dilute TO ml \nof the solution to 100.0 ml with water.\nQdd and titrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid, determining the\nend-pointpotentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry put a blank titration.\nChromatographic system \n2 l ;:\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\nml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02042 g of shows two clearly separated spots.\ncìiH12N2O2. \nH\n: \nIron (2.3.14). 1.33 g complies with the limit test for iron(30\nborage. Store protected from moisture. \nppm). \n• •{. \n■\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n4126\n4127"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valproate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valproate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Valproate Injection\nValproate Sodium Injection\nValproate Injection is a sterile solution ofsodium valproate in\nWater for Injections with one or more suitable buffering or\nsequestering agents.\nValproate Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount ofvalproic\nacid, CSH I60 2•\nUsual strength. 20 mg per ml."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Valproate Injection\nValproate Sodium Injection\nValproate Injection is a sterile solution ofsodium valproate in\nWater for Injections with one or more suitable buffering or\nsequestering agents.\nValproate Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount ofvalproic\nacid, CSH I60 2•\nUsual strength. 20 mg per ml."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to that in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nB. Gives the reaction ofsodium salts (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24).7.0 to 9.0.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 23.0 Endotoxin\nUnits per ml.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Parenteral\nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nInternal standard solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of\nbiphenyl in dichloromethane.\nTest solution. Dilute a volume ofthe injection containing about\n400 mg ofvalproic acid with 20 ml of5 per cent v/v solution of\nhydrochloric acidand add 50 ml ofinternal standard solution.\nShake for 1 hour, ifemulsion fonus, stir with a glass rod. Take\n5 ml of the organic layer and dilute to 50 ml with\ndichloromethane.\nReference solution (a). A 0.8 per cent w/v solution ofvalproic\nacid RS in internal standard solution.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 5.0 ml ofreference solution (a)\nto 50 m1 with dichloromethane.\nChromatographic system\n- a glass column 1.8 m x 2 mm, packed with 10 per cent\nphase G34 on 80 to 100 mesh support SlA,\ntemperature:\ncolumn. 155°,\ninjection port at 275° and detector at 300°,\n-\na flame ionization detector,\n-\nflow rate. 20 ml per minute using nitrogen as the carrier\ngas."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valproic-acid-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valproic Acid Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"VALPROIC ACID CAPSULES\nValproic Acid Capsules\nValproic Acid Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent \nand1 riot! more than 110.6 per cent of the stated amount of \nvalproic acid, C8H16O2.\nUsual strength. 250 mg. : <\nIdentification, \n;\nA. In theAssay, the retention time ratio of the. principal peak \nto the internal standard peak in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution and reference solution, are not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nB. Disperse a quantity of the content of capsules containing \nabout 250 mg of valproic acid, add 20 ml of 1 M sodium \nhydroxide, shake and allow the, layers to separate. Filter the \naqueous layer, add 4 ml of hydrochloric acid, mix and extract \nwith 40 ml of n-heptane. Filter the n-heptane,layer.through \nglass wool an4;evaporate the .solvent completely on a steam \nbath with the aid of a current of air. Transfer 2 drops of the \nresidue to a test tube containing 0.5 ml, each of, 2 per cent v/ \nv tfpotassiunm iodide solution and 4 per centv/v oipotassium\nipdate solution and mix, a yellow colour is produced."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"VALPROIC ACID CAPSULES\nValproic Acid Capsules\nValproic Acid Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent \nand1 riot! more than 110.6 per cent of the stated amount of \nvalproic acid, C8H16O2.\nUsual strength. 250 mg. : <\nIdentification, \n;\nA. In theAssay, the retention time ratio of the. principal peak \nto the internal standard peak in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution and reference solution, are not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nB. Disperse a quantity of the content of capsules containing \nabout 250 mg of valproic acid, add 20 ml of 1 M sodium \nhydroxide, shake and allow the, layers to separate. Filter the \naqueous layer, add 4 ml of hydrochloric acid, mix and extract \nwith 40 ml of n-heptane. Filter the n-heptane,layer.through \nglass wool an4;evaporate the .solvent completely on a steam \nbath with the aid of a current of air. Transfer 2 drops of the \nresidue to a test tube containing 0.5 ml, each of, 2 per cent v/ \nv tfpotassiunm iodide solution and 4 per centv/v oipotassium\nipdate solution and mix, a yellow colour is produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of a 0.5 per cent w/v solution of sodium lauryl \nsulphate in siinulated intestinal fluid prepared by dissolving\n6.8 g of monobctsic sodiumphosphate in250 ml water, mix and \n77 ml of 0:2 Msoditimhydrbxidd,add 500 ml of water. Adjusted \nto pH 6:8 with (Z 2 Msodium hydroxide or 0.2 hydrochloric \nacid dilute' to 1000ml with water,adjusted to pH 7.5 with'-5 M \nsodium hydroxide, ' \n-\nSpeed arid time. 50 rprii and 60 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by gas chromatography (2.4.13) as described under \nAssay, using the following solutions.\nTest solution. To, 10 ml of the filtrate, add.about 3.p g cA'sodium \nchloride, and mix on a vortex mixer for 5 minutes. Add 1.0 ml of \n6M hydrochloric acid and 5.0 ml of internalstandard solution, \nshake and allow to separate, remove, the n-heptane layer and \nfilter. Discard the aqueous layer.\nReference solution. Weigh a quantity of valproic acid IPRS\narid dilute with the dissolution medium to obtain a solution \nhaving a concentration similar to; that of the test solution. \nTake 10’ml of the solution arid proceed as in test solution \nbeginning with the words “add about 3.0 g......... ”.\nCalculate the content of C8[I;6O2 in the capsule. i-\nQ. Not less than 85 per cent of the stated amount of CgH^C^-\nOther tests. Comply with the tests; stated; under Capsules.\n............. *.......     $2022\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13). \n; .\nTest solution. Take 20 capsules in a container arid add 150 ml \nof dichloromethane, cool in a solid carbon dioxide-acetone \nmixture until the contents have solidified. If necessary \ntransfer the mixture of capsules and dichloromethane to a \nblender jar and blend until all the solidsare reduced to fine \nparticles. Transfer the mixture to a 500-ml flask, add n-heptane \nto volume, mix and allow, the solids to settle. Transfer an \naccurately measured volume of the solution, containing about \n250 mg ofvalproic acid, to a 100-ml flask, dilute with n-Aep/orte \nto volume and mix. Transfer 5.0 ml to a container equipped \nwith a closure. Add 2.0 ml of internal standard solution, close \nthe container and mix. \nr \n. ...:i\nReference solution. A 0.25 per cent w/v solution?-o‘f valproic\nacidIPRS in n-heptane. Transfer 5.0 ml to a container equipped \nwith a closure. Add 2.0 ml of internal standard solution, close \nthe container and mix.\nInternal standard solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of\nbiphenyl in n-heptane.\nChromatographic system\n- a glass column i .8 m x 2 mm, packed with 10 per cent\nphase;G34 ori 80 to 100 mesh support SIA,\n- temperature: 2\ncolumn. 150\", ' ■ \ninjection port and detector. 250°, \n............\n- a flame ionization detector,\n- \nflow rate: 40 ml per minute using nitrogen as the earner\n■ : gas. wx \n<■■ /. xwx-ww\nThe relative retention time with reference to valproic acid for \nbiphenyl is about 2.0. \n.\nInject 2 gl of the reference solution. The test is riot valid unless \nthe resolution .between the peak due to valproic acid and \nbiphenyl is not less than 3.0 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nCalculate the content of C8Hi6O2 in the capsules, ?\nInject 2 gl of the reference solution and the test solution.\nStorage. Store protect from moisture, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30“. \n'■ \n■■■''"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valproic-acid-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valproic Acid Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Valproic Acid Oral Solution\nValproic Acid Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount p/\nvalproic acid, CsHl6O2.\nUsual strengths. 40mgpcrml; 50mgper ml. ' \n2^"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Valproic Acid Oral Solution\nValproic Acid Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount p/\nvalproic acid, CsHl6O2.\nUsual strengths. 40mgpcrml; 50mgper ml. ' \n2^"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. In the Assay, the retention time ratio of the principal peak \nto the internal standard peak in the chromatogram obtained\n3.916\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVALSARIAN\n(P2022 \n..... ................................._............     _\n^jth the test solution and reference solution is not more than \nj.Opercent.\nValsarían\ng Dissolve a volume of solution containing about 250 mg of \nvalproic acid in 40 ml of water and 2.0 ml of hydrochloric \nacid Mix an<* extract w^h 40 ml of n-heptane. Filter the \n^heptane layer through glass wool into a beaker, and \nevaporate the solvent completely on a steam bath with the aid \nofa current of air. Transfer 2 drops of the residue to a test tube \ncontaining 0.5 ml each 2 per cent v/v of'potassium iodide \nsolution and 4 per cent v/v solution of potassium iodate\nSqlutipn and mix; a yellow colour is produced.\nTests'\npH (2.4.24). 7.0 to 8.0.\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Oral Liquids.\nAssay- Ketenni ne by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. Dissolve a volume of solution containing about \n250 ing of valproic acid in 40 ml of water, add 2.0 ml of \nhydrochloric acid and extract with 80 ml of n-heptane until \nthe aqueous layer is clear. Filter the n-heptane layer through \nglasswool with small portions of n-heptane, add the rinsings \nto the flask,dilute to 100.0 ml with n-heptane and mix. Transfer \n5.0 ml to a container equipped with a closure. Add 2.0 ml of the \ninternal standard solution, close the container and mix."},{"name":"Production","text":"Reference solution. A 0.25 per cent w/v solution of valproic \nacidIPRS in n-heptane. Transfer 5.0 ml to a container equipped \nwith a closure. Add 2.0 ml of internal standard solution and \nnix\nInternal standard solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of \nbiphenyl in n-heptane.\nChromatographic system\nglass column 1.8 m x 2 mm, packed with 10 per cent \n;:,phase G34 on 80 to 1010 mesh support S1 A,\n«p-Temperature: •"},{"name":"Identification","text":"; u column. 150°,\ninjection port and detector at 250°,\n; A : A flame ionization detector,\n- flow rate: 40 ml per minute using nitrogen as the carrier \njMgas.\nThe relative retention time with reference to valproic acid for \nbiphenyl is about 2.0."},{"name":"Tests","text":"inject2 pl of the reference solution. The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between valproic acid and biphenyl is not less \n^an 3 0 and the relative standard deviation for replicate \ninjections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\ni|iict.2 pl each of the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C8Hi6O2 in oral solution.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nC24H29N5O3\nMol. Wt. 435.5\nValsarían is V-pentanonyl-7V-[2'-(1//-tetrazol-5-yljbiphenyl\" \n4-ylmethyl]-L-valine.\nValsarían contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 percent of \ncalculated on the anhydrous\nbasis,\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nAs Nitrosamines are classified as probable human carcinogens, \ntheir presence in valsarían should be avoided or limited as\nmuch as possible. For this reason, manufacturers \nof valsarían for human use are expected to perform an \nassessment of the risk ofJV-nitrosamine formation.and \ncontamination during their manufacturing , process; if this \nassessment identifies a potential risk, the manufacturing \nprocess should fie modified to minimise contamination and a \ncontrol strategy implemented to detect and control N- \nnitrosamine impurities in valsarían. The general chapter 5.11."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valproic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valproic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"VALPROIC ACID\njp.2022\n^IjjroicAcid\n■■■\"i Li \n'' 1\n■ <is \n- ' ' \n' ^X\n■ \n■ ■■ H c/x^v,oh'\n. \n- ■ ■ H3° ■- Ji\n- - ■■'. i o ■ ■■ ■ \n;\n; \nMol. Wt 144.2\nValproicacid is 2-propy ¡pentanoic acid.\nVaJproic aeid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \n^ote than 101.0 per cent of CsHi6()2.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\ndescription. A colourless or very slightly yellow liquid, slightly \nW>us. ‘"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"VALPROIC ACID\njp.2022\n^IjjroicAcid\n■■■\"i Li \n'' 1\n■ <is \n- ' ' \n' ^X\n■ \n■ ■■ H c/x^v,oh'\n. \n- ■ ■ H3° ■- Ji\n- - ■■'. i o ■ ■■ ■ \n;\n; \nMol. Wt 144.2\nValproicacid is 2-propy ¡pentanoic acid.\nVaJproic aeid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \n^ote than 101.0 per cent of CsHi6()2.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\ndescription. A colourless or very slightly yellow liquid, slightly \nW>us. ‘"},{"name":"Identification","text":"may be omitted If tests B and C are carried out. Tests B \nand C may be omitted if test A is carried out.\n.0 \n'... ■■■■ ■■ ■■■ ;\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with valproic acid \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of valproic acid.\ngas.\nB. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel G.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 50 volumes of ether and 50 volumes \n^dichloromethane. r \nl\nTest, solution. A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of the substance \nyndpt;examination in methanol..\n^ehence solution. A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of valproic\ndffdlPRSin methanol.\nApply 2 pl of each solution.. Allow the mobile phase to rise\n15 cm. Dry the plate in air and spray with bromocresol green \nsolution. The principal spot in the chromatogram obtained \n\\vith the test solution corresponds to that in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution.\nC. \nTo 1 ml add 3.0 ml of dilute sodium hydroxide solution. Add \n3.0, ml,of water and 1.0 ml of a 10 per cent w/v solution of \ncobalt nitrate. A violet precipitate is formed, filter it. The \nprecipitate dissolves in dichloromethane."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. A20.0 per cent w/v solution of valproic \nacid in 8.5 per cent w/v sodium hydroxide solution is clear \n(2.4.1) and not more intensely colored than reference solution \nYS5(2.4.1).\nRefractive index (2.4.27). 1.422 to 1.425.\nRelated substances. Determine by gas chromatography \n(2.4.13). \n.......... 1\nInternal standard solution. A 0.005 per cent w/v solution of\nbutyric acid in heptane.\nTest solution. A 5.0 per cent w/v solution;of the substance \nunder examination in the internal standard solution. Dilute\n1.0 ml of the solution to 10.0 ml with heptane. •; 717 -J! : '\nReference solution. Dissolve 20 mg of the substance under\nexamination and 20 mg of 2-(l-methylethyl)pentanbic acid \n(valproic acid impurity C) IPRS in 10.0 ml of heptane. Dilute\n1.0 ml of the solution to 10.0 ml with heptane.\nChromatographic system\n- a fused-'silica capillary column 30 m x 0.53 mm, coated\nwith wide-bore mactogdl 20000 2-nitroterephthalate \n(0.5 pm),\n- temperature.\ncolumn 130° from 0-10 minutes and 130° -190° from\n10-30 minutes ’\n- inlet port and detector 220°,\n- a flame ionisation detector,\n- flow rate: 8 ml per minute, using nitrogen as the carrier\nInject 1 pl of the reference solution. The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to valproic acid impurity \nC and valproic acid is not less than 3.0.\nInject 1 pl of the reference solution and the ,test, solution. In \nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area; of \npeak due to any impurity is not more than the area of the peak \ndue to the internal standard (0.1 per cent), and the sum of the \nareas of all the secondary peaks is not more than \n3 times the area of the peak due to the internal standard \n(0.3 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.1 times \nthe area of the peak due to the internal standard (0.01 per \ncent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 2.0 g in 20 ml ethanol (80per \ncent) and use 12 ml of the solution. The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method D \n(20 ppm). Prepare the standard using lead standard solution \n(2 ppm PZ?) obtained by diluting lead standard solution \n(100 ppm Pb) with ethanol (80 per cent).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.1g in 25 ml of ethanol (95per cent). Add \n2 ml of water. Titrate with 0.1, M sodium hydroxide, determining \nthe end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank \ntitration. \n. \n.\n1 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01442 g of \nC8H16O2. , \n;\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\n3915\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valproicacid-very-soluble-in-organic-solvents-slightly-soluble","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"ValproicAcid, Very soluble in organic solvents; slightly soluble"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"ValproicAcid, Very soluble in organic solvents; slightly soluble\nin water. ..\nValsartan. Very soluble in methanol; practically insoluble in\nwaler. - \n. \n- - \n- \n-.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride?Freely soluble in wafer; slightly \nsoluble in ethanol (95 per cent). \n' \n‘\nVanillin. Freely soluble in ethanol\"(95 per cent) and in \nmethanol; soluble in. ether1, slightly soluble: in water. It \ndissolves in dilute solutions of alkalr hydroxides. L\nVasopressin. Soluble in water.\nVecuronium Bromide. Freely soluble in dichioromethane, \nsparingly soluble in -acetonitrile and in ethanol; slightly \nsoluble in wafer.\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in water and in \nmethanol, soluble in anhydrous ethanol, slightly soluble or \npractically insoluble in acetone.\nVerapamil Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in chloroform; \nsoluble in water; sparingly soluble in ethanol (95 per cent); \npractically insoluble in ether.\nVildagiiptin, Freely soluble in water, soluble in ethanol, \ndimethyl formamide andin dimethylsulphoxide.\nVinblastine Sulphate. Freely soluble in water; soluble in \nmethanol; practically insoluble iri ethanol (95 per cent) arid\nin ether. \n'w/' \n■"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"ValproicAcid, Very soluble in organic solvents; slightly soluble\nin water. ..\nValsartan. Very soluble in methanol; practically insoluble in\nwaler. - \n. \n- - \n- \n-.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride?Freely soluble in wafer; slightly \nsoluble in ethanol (95 per cent). \n' \n‘\nVanillin. Freely soluble in ethanol\"(95 per cent) and in \nmethanol; soluble in. ether1, slightly soluble: in water. It \ndissolves in dilute solutions of alkalr hydroxides. L\nVasopressin. Soluble in water.\nVecuronium Bromide. Freely soluble in dichioromethane, \nsparingly soluble in -acetonitrile and in ethanol; slightly \nsoluble in wafer.\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in water and in \nmethanol, soluble in anhydrous ethanol, slightly soluble or \npractically insoluble in acetone.\nVerapamil Hydrochloride. Freely soluble in chloroform; \nsoluble in water; sparingly soluble in ethanol (95 per cent); \npractically insoluble in ether.\nVildagiiptin, Freely soluble in water, soluble in ethanol, \ndimethyl formamide andin dimethylsulphoxide.\nVinblastine Sulphate. Freely soluble in water; soluble in \nmethanol; practically insoluble iri ethanol (95 per cent) arid\nin ether. \n'w/' \n■"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valproicacid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"ValproicAcid"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"VALPROIC ACID\nInject 2111 ofreference solution (b). The test in not valid unless\nthe resolution between the peaks due to valproic acid and\nbiphenyl peaks is not less than 3.0 and the relative standard\ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject 2 III of the test solution and reference solution (a).\nCalculate the content ofCSH I60 2 in the injection.\nStorage. Store at atemperature not exceeding 30° and in single\ndose containers, preferably of Type 1 glass.\nLabelling. The label states the amount of any buffering or\nsequestering agents used.\nValproic Acid\nMol. Wt. 144.2\nValproic acid is 2-propylpentanoic acid.\nValproic acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 percent of CSH I60 2•\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\nDescription. A colourless orvery slightly yellow liquid, slightly\nviscous."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"VALPROIC ACID\nInject 2111 ofreference solution (b). The test in not valid unless\nthe resolution between the peaks due to valproic acid and\nbiphenyl peaks is not less than 3.0 and the relative standard\ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject 2 III of the test solution and reference solution (a).\nCalculate the content ofCSH I60 2 in the injection.\nStorage. Store at atemperature not exceeding 30° and in single\ndose containers, preferably of Type 1 glass.\nLabelling. The label states the amount of any buffering or\nsequestering agents used.\nValproic Acid\nMol. Wt. 144.2\nValproic acid is 2-propylpentanoic acid.\nValproic acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 percent of CSH I60 2•\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\nDescription. A colourless orvery slightly yellow liquid, slightly\nviscous."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may be omittediftests Band C are carried out. Tests B\nand C may be omitted iftest A is carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with valproic acid\nRS or with the reference spectrum ofva1proic acid.\nB. Detenuine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel G.\nMobilephase. Amixture of50 volumes ofether and 50 volumes\nof dichloromethane.\nTest solution. A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of the substance\nunder examination in methanol.\nReference solution. A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of valproic\nacid RS in methanol.\nApply 2 III ofeach solution. Allow the mobile phase to rise 15\ncm. Dry the plate in air and spray with bromocresol green\nsolution. The principal spot in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution corresponds to that in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution.\nVALPROIC ACID\nC. To 1ml add 3.0 m1 ofdilute sodium hydroxidesolution. Add\n3.0 ml of water and 1.0 m1 of a 10 per cent w/v solution of\ncobalt nitrate. A violet precipitate is formed, filter it. The\nprecipitate dissolves in di~hloromethane."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance ofsolution. A20.0 per cent w/v solution in dilute\nsodium hydroxide is clear (2.4.1) and not more intensely\ncoloured than reference solution YS5 (2.4.1).\nRefractive index (2.4.27). 1.422 to 1.425.\nRelated substances. Determine by gas chromatography\n(2.4.13).\nInternal standard solution. A 0.005 per cent w/v solution of\nbutyric acid in heptane.\nTest solution. A 5.0 per cent w/v solution of the substance\nunder examination in the internal standard solution. Dilute 1.0\nml ofthis solution to 10 ml with heptane.\nReference solution. Dissolve 20 mg of the substance under\nexamination and 20 mg of 2-(l-methylethyl)pentanoic acid\nRS (valproic acid impurity C RS) in 10 ml ofheptane. Dilute\n1.0 ml ofthis solution to 10 ml with heptane.\nChromatographic system\n-\na fused-silica capillary column 30 m x 0.53 mm, coated\nwith wide-bore rnacrogol20000 2-nitroterephthalate (0.5\n11m),\n-\ntemperature.\ncolumn 130° from 0-10 minutes and 130° - 190° from 10-\n30 minutes\n-\ninlet port and detector 220°,\n-\na flame ionisation detector,\n-\nflow rate. 8 ml per minute ofthe carrier gas (helium).\nInject I III ofthe reference solution. The test is not valid unless\nthe resolution between the peaks due to valproic acid impurity\nC and valproic acid is not less than 3.0.\nInject I III of the test solution and the reference solution. In\nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of\npeak due to any impurity, for each impurity is not more than\n0.1 times the area ofthe peak due to the internal standard (0.1\nper cent). The sum of areas of all the secondary peaks is not\nmore than 0.3 times the area of the peak due to the internal\nstandard (0.3 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than\n0.01 times the area of the peak due to the internal standard\n(0.01 percent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 2.0 g in 20 ml ethanol (80per\nc::enJL!!'DJ1.J!~e.J2.ml of.Jhes.olutioD...J'he.f.e.s.uhings.olution\ncomplies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method D (20\nppm). Prepare the standard using lead standard solution (2\nppm Pb) obtained by diluting lead standard solution (100\nppm Pb) with ethanol (80 per cent).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about O.lg and dissolve in 25 m! of\nethanol (95 per cent). Add 2 ml of water. Titrate with 0.1 M\nsodium\nhydroxide,\ndetermining\nthe\nend-point\npotentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of0.1 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01442 g of\nCgHI60 2•\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valsartan-and-hydrochlorothiazide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valsartan and Hydrochlorothiazide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Valsartan and Hydrochlorothiazide \nTablets\nValsaban and Hydrochlorothiazide Tablets contain not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated amount of valsartan, C24H29N5O3 and \nhydrochlorothiazide, C7HsCIN3O4S2.\nUsual strength. Valsartan, 80 mg and Hydrochlorothiazide,\n12.5 mg; Valsartan 160 mg, and Hydrochlorothiazide, 12.5 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Valsartan and Hydrochlorothiazide \nTablets\nValsaban and Hydrochlorothiazide Tablets contain not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated amount of valsartan, C24H29N5O3 and \nhydrochlorothiazide, C7HsCIN3O4S2.\nUsual strength. Valsartan, 80 mg and Hydrochlorothiazide,\n12.5 mg; Valsartan 160 mg, and Hydrochlorothiazide, 12.5 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nVALSARTAN AND HYDROCHLOROTHIAZIDE TABLETS\n__ _............................................ .......... ............................\nMobile phase, A mixture of 80 volumes of ethyl acetate, 20 \nvolumes of dehydrated alcohol, and 10 volumes of a 25 per \ncent w/v solution of ammonium hydroxide.\nTest solution. 'Dispersé a quantity of powdered tablets \ncontaining about 20 mg of valsartan in 100.0 ml of acetone, \nsonicate for 15 minutes and filter. \n=\nReference solution. A solution equivalent to test\nconcentrations of valsartan IFRS and hydrochlorothiazide \nIPRS in acetone. \n■ :\nApply to the plate 2 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 7 cm. Dry the plate in a current of warm air and\nexamine under ultraviolet light at 254 nm. The principal spot in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds \nto .that in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution.\nB. In the Assay, the retention timé of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 1000 ml of phosphate buffer pH 6.8, \nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtered solution, suitably diluted with \nthe medium if necessary, at the maximum at about 250 nm for \nvalsartan and 272 nm for hydrochlorothiazide (2.4.7). Calculate \nthe content of C24H29N5O3 and CTIgÓIN3Ó4S2 in the medium \nfrom the absorbance obtained from a solution of known \nconcentration of valsartan IPRS and hydrochlorothiazide \nIPRS in the same medium.\nCalculate the content of valsartan dissolved by the formula:\n________ (AT2x Al percent H^yZ^Tl x Al per cent H¡2 5()0\n(yil percent K30ml x Al percent ) - (Al percent x /ÍI per cent H^„1 \n’\nCalculate the content of hydrochlorothiazide dissolved by \nthe formula:\n____________(XTl x AlpercentV2¡línJ - (AT2 x Al per centV^) \n* gQ\n(Al percent ffmmix Al per cent \n(ai percent H2S[lm) x Al per cent Vmml )\nwhere, ATI - absorbance of the test solution at\n272 nm,\nAT2 = absorbance of the test solution at\n250 nm,\nAI per cent V272 nm = absoiptivity (1 per cent, 0.2 cm,272 nm)\nof valsartan in medium,\nAI per cent V2So nm = absorptivity (1 per cent, 0.2 cm, 250 nm)\nof valsartan in medium,\n3919\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVALSARIAN AND HYDROCHLOROTHIAZIDE TABLETS\nA1 per, cent H272nm = absorptivity (1 per cent, 0.2 cm, 272 nm)\nof hydrochlorothiazide in medium,\nAl per centH¡so™ = absorptivity(1 percent, 0.2 cm,250nm)\n.. of hydrochlorothiazide in medium,\nQ. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of C24H29N5O3 \nandC7HgCIN3O4S2. ■w'fo.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay using the following \nsolutions. \n,\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of acetonitrile aaA water.\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of powdered tablets \ncontaining about 62.5 mg of Hydrochlorothiazide with 5;0 ml \nofwa/erand 100 ml of solvent mixture,/sonicatefor 15 minutes \nand shake for 30 minutes. Dilute to 250 ml with the solvent \nmixture, centrifuge and dilute 25.0 ml of the supernatant to \n200.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.003 per cent \nw/v of benzothiadiazine impurity A (4-amino-6-chloro-1,3- \nbenzenedisulphonamide) IPRS, 0.006 per cent w/v of \nhydrochlorothiazide IPRS, 0.008 per cent w/v of valsarían \nIPRS and 0.02 per cent w/v of valsarían impurity B (N-butyryl- \nN-{[2 ‘-(lH~tetrazole-5-yl)biphenyl-4-yl]methyl}-L~valine)\nIPRSin the solvent mixture.\nReference solution '(&). Dilute 5.0 mi of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture. \n;\nto 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture. ;\nReference solution (c). Dilute 10 ml of reference solution (b)\nInject reference solutions (b) and (c). The test is not valid \nunless in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b), the resolution between valsarían impurity B and valsarían \nand . between benzothiadiazine impurity A and \nhydrochlorothiazide is not less than 1.4. The relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 10 per cent \nin the chrorhatogram obtained with reference solution (c).\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and (c) and the test solution. \nThe area of the peak due to benzothiadiazine impurity A is not \nmore than 1.0 per cent; the area of any secondary peak other \nthan valsarían impurity A is not more than 0.2 per cent; and \nthe sum of areas of all the secondary peaks other than \nvalsarían impurity Ais not more than 1.3 per cent. (Valsarían \nimpurity A is the enantiomer of valsarían and coelutes with \nvalsarían in this test.)\nUniformity of content. Complies with the tests stated under \nTablets.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as described \ncinder Assay using the following solution as the test solution.\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of acetonitrile and water.\nTest solution. Disperse 1 tablet with 5.0 ml of water and about\n100 ml of the solvent mixture, sonicate for 15 minutes and\ndilute to 200.0 ml with the solvent mixture. Centrifuge a portion \nof the solution and dilute a volume of the clear- supernatant \nwith the solvent mixture to obtain a 0.02 per centw/y solution \nofvalsartan, . , \n: \n.\nCalculate the content of C24HÍ9N5O3 and C7H8CIN3O4S2 in the \ntablet. '■ \n'■ -■ ■\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets! '\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of acetonitrile and water\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing about 62.5 mg of Hydrochlorothiazide \nwith5.0 ml of water andl 00 ml of solvent mixture, sonicate for\n15 minutes and shake for 30 minutes. Dilute to 250 ml with the \nsolvent mixture, centrifuge and dilute 25.0 ml of the \nsupernatant to 200.0 ml with the solvent mixture. Dilute a \nsuitable"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valsartan-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valsartan Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Valsartan Tablets\nValsartan Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not\nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of valsartan,\nCZ4Hz9Ns03.\n.\nUsual strengths. 40 mg; 80 mg; 160 mg; 320 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Valsartan Tablets\nValsartan Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not\nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of valsartan,\nCZ4Hz9Ns03.\n.\nUsual strengths. 40 mg; 80 mg; 160 mg; 320 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Shake a quantity ofthe powdered tablets containing 0.1 g\nofValsartan with 40 ml ofmethanol, filter and evaporate the\nfiltrate to dryness. On the residue, determine by infrared\nabsorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum\nwith that obtained from valsartan RS or with the reference\nspectrum of valsartan.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No.1,\nMedium. 900 ml ofphosphate bufferpH 6.8,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume ofthe medium and filter. Measure\nthe absorbance of the filtered solution, suitably diluted with\nthe medium if necessary, at the maximum at about 250 nm\n(2.4.7). Calculate the content of CZ4Hz9Ns03 in the medium\nfrom the absorbance obtained from a solution of known\nconcentration of valsartan RS prepared by dissolving in\nminimum amount ofmethanol and diluted with the dissolution\nmedium.\nD. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of\nCz~z9Ns03'\nRelated Substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets\ncontaining 25 mg ofValsartan with 15 Jill of mobile phase and\ndilute to 25.0 ml with the mobile phase, filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valsartan","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valsartan"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"VALSARTAN\nReference solution (a). A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of\nvalsartan IPRS in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (&). Diluted ;0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a siamlcssstcel column! 2.5 cm x 3.0 mm, packed with\noctadccylsHanc bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such \nas Nucleosil),:\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of water, 50\nvolumes of ’acetonitrile and 0.1 volume of glacial acetic\nacid,\n- flowrate: 0.4 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 225 nm,\n■ injection volume: 10 pl. ■\nInject reference solution (a). The test is riot valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nInject reference, solution (b) and the test‘solution. Run the \nchromatogram three times the retention time of the principal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \nthe area of any secondary peak is not more than 0.2 times the \narea of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b) (0.2 per cent) and the sum , of areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than , 0.5 times the area of the \npeakin the chromatogram obtained;with reference solution\n(b) (0.5 per cent). \n< \n;\nHeavy metals (2.3.13); T .0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). \n'-■■■■' ■\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined on 1 g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). - ■ ■■■\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 50.0 ml of the mobile phase. Diluted .0 ml of the \nsolution to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.005 percent w/v solution of valsartan \nIPRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system . \n, \n? .\n- a stainless steel column 12.5 cm x 3.0 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such \nas Purospher 18e),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of water, 50\nvolumes of acetonitrile and 0.1 volume of glacial acetic \nacid,\n- flow rate: 0.4 ml per minute, \n--Spectrophotometer set at 273nm, ■ \n- injection volume: 10 pl. \n: ; \\\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInj ect the reference solution and the test solution.\n....................... ................................._ IP'2022;';Wf\nCalculate the content of C24H29N5O3;-' - :\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture at xV \ntemperature not exceeding 30°. \n...:>.••/■■ Cy\nValsarían Tablets, ...\"\nValsartan Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of valsarían, \nC24H29N5O3. \n- J\nUsual strengths. 40 mg; 80 mg; 160 mg; 320 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"VALSARTAN\nReference solution (a). A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of\nvalsartan IPRS in the 