otometer set at 273nm, ■ \n- injection volume: 10 pl. \n: ; \\\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInj ect the reference solution and the test solution.\n....................... ................................._ IP'2022;';Wf\nCalculate the content of C24H29N5O3;-' - :\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture at xV \ntemperature not exceeding 30°. \n...:>.••/■■ Cy\nValsarían Tablets, ...\"\nValsartan Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of valsarían, \nC24H29N5O3. \n- J\nUsual strengths. 40 mg; 80 mg; 160 mg; 320 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 0.1 g \nof Valsartan with 40 ml of methanol, filter and evaporate the \nfiltrate to dryness. On the residue, determine by infrared \nabsorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum \nwith that, obtained from valsartan IPRS or with the reference \nspectrum of valsartan.\nB. In the Assay, the principal; peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nTests ......\nDissolution (2.5.2). \n, \n;\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle), \n? / / ’\nMedium. 900 ml ofphosphate bufferpH 6.8, \n:\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtered solution, suitably diluted with \nthe medium if necessary, at the maximum at about 250 nm \n(2.4.7). Calculate the content of C24H29N5O3 in the medium \nfrom the absorbance obtained from a solution of known \nconcentration of valsartan IPRS prepared by dissolving in \nminimum amount of methanol and diluted with the dissolution \nmedium. \n••\n' ■■\" ■\"\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). •• \nf' .\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 25 mg of Valsartan with 15 ml of mobile phase and \ndilute to 25.0 nil with the mobile phase, filter. ¡\nReference solution (p). ff 0,1 per .cent w/v solution of\nvalsartan IPRS in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system as described under Assay.\n3918\nwww.webofpharma.com\nf -gct reference solution (a).. The test is not valid unless the \nj^jng^actor is not more than 2.0. \n■ ■\n^¿¿freference solution (b) arid the test solution. In the\ncjyomatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \n^ondary peak is not more than 0.5 times the area of the\nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsojution.(b) (0-5 per cent) and the.sum of areas of all.the\nsecondaryPea^s is .not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n^ q .0 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssaÿ- Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nWeigh arid powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing 50 mg of Valsartan in 25 ml of the\n■^otiiife phase and dilute to 100.0 ml with the rriobile phase and\nDilute 5.0 mldfthe solution to 50.0 ml with the mobile\nphase.\nReference solution. A 0.005 per cent w/v solution of valsartan \nfPfiyin the mobile phase."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Chromatographic system\n¿:ij(ajniess steei column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n\"g \"’octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\nmobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of water, 50 \n,^n. v;ojumes pp acetonitrile and 0.1 volume of glacial acetic \nf' acid,\n:/J \" flow rate: ! ml per minute,\n’ ’spectrophotometer set at 273 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing'factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate irijectioris is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. ,\nCalculate the content ofC24H29N5O3 in the tablets. .\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vancomycin-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vancomycin Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vancomycin Capsules\nVancomycin Hydrochloride Capsules\nVancomycin Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nvancomycin, C^HysCLNgChi. '■; ,\nUsual strength. 125 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vancomycin Capsules\nVancomycin Hydrochloride Capsules\nVancomycin Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nvancomycin, C^HysCLNgChi. '■; ,\nUsual strength. 125 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by paper chromatography (2.4:15), using a suitable \nsheet of chromatographic filter paper. \n. ; ;\nMobile phase. A mixture of 6 volumes of butyl alcohol, 4 \nvolumes of water and 3 volumes of pyridine.\nTest solution. Disperse the content of capsules containing \nabout 100 mg of vancomycin in 100.0 ml of water. \n;\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of vancomycirt \nhydrochloride IPRS water. \n‘\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. Develop by \ndescending chromatography for about 7 hours. Af^tef \ndevelopment,, dry the paper and place it on an inoculated ag^r. \nsurface of sufficient area to accommodate the paper and \nprepared for vancomycin assay, described; in microbiological \nassay of antibiotics (2.2.10) except to use Medium B. Remove \nthe paper from the. agar surface after 30 minutes and incubate \nthe agar medium at 37° for 18 hours; clear zones of inhibitipri \nare produced at corresponding positions on the two \nchromatograms. '\nDissolution (2.5.2). \n............... : 4\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket), \n' ■\nTests .... \n... \n:.a\n3922\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vancomycin-hydrochloride-for-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution is a dry powder \nconsisting of Vancomycin Hydrochloride with or without \nexcipients. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe contents of the sealed container, comply with the \nrequirements, stated under Powders and Granules for Oral \nSolutions and Oral Suspensions stated under Oral Liquids, \nand with the following requirements.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution contains not less \nthan 88.0 per cent of Vancomycin B."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution is a dry powder \nconsisting of Vancomycin Hydrochloride with or without \nexcipients. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe contents of the sealed container, comply with the \nrequirements, stated under Powders and Granules for Oral \nSolutions and Oral Suspensions stated under Oral Liquids, \nand with the following requirements.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution contains not less \nthan 88.0 per cent of Vancomycin B."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A, In the test: for Vancomycin B, the retention time, of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the test\nsolution corresponds to that in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution. \n; u. •\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1).\nTests -\nAppearance of solution. A 10.0 per cent w/v solution is clear \n(2.4.1) and the absorbance at 450 nm (2.4.7) is not more than\n0.1. \n- >\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 4.5, determined on a 5.0 per cent w/v solution \nof Vancomycin Hydrochloride.\nVancomycin B. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTE—Use the solutions within 4 hours of preparation. .\nTest solution (a). Dissolve a quantity of the powder in mobile \nphase A to produce a solution containing 2,000 IU of \nvancomycin per ml.\nTest solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of test solution (aj to 25.0 ml\nwith mobile phase A.\nTest solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml of test solution (b) to 40.0 ml \nwith mobile phase A.\nReference solution. A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of vancomycin\nhydrochloride IPRS in water. Heat at 65° for 24 hours and\nallow to cool.- ' \n;\n■\nGhromatographic system \n■ ■\nf . _ ; a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \nJ^^^tcappedoctadecylsilarie bonded to porous silica"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vancomycin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vancomycin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vancomycin Hydrochloride"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vancomycin Hydrochloride"},{"name":"Tests","text":"C^H^Q1;N9O24jHC1 \nMol.Wt. 1485.7\nVancomycin Hydrochloride is a mixtüre of related \nglycopeptides, . consisting, .principally of the \nmonohydrochloride of (35,6Ä,7R,22R,235,265,30a5,36Ä, \n38aÄ)-3-(2-amino-2-oxoethyl)-44-[[2-O-(3-amino-2,3,6- \ntridedxy-3-C“methyl“^-L-/yxo-hexopyranosyl)-/?-D- \n¿ihi:ppy^anosyl]oxy]-i0,r9-dichioro-7,22,28)30,32- \npentahydroxy-6-[[(277)-4-rncthyl-2-(methylamino)pentanoyI[ \namino^2,5,24,38,39-pcntaoxo-2,3,4,5,6,7,23,24,25,26,36,37,38, \n38ä'tetradecahydro-227/-8.11:18,2 l-diethe.no-23,36- \n(iniinprnethano)-13,16:31,35-dimetheno-\\H, 13H-[ 1,6,9] \noxadia2;acycIohexadecinol[4,5-/M] [ 10,2,16]benzoxadiaza- \ncyclotetracösme-26-carboxylic acid (vancomycin B).\nIt is produced by certain strains of Amycolatopsis orientalis \n°r obtained by any other means.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride contains not less than 1050 IU\nper mg of C66H75C12N9O24, HC1, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis and not less than 93.0 per cent of vancomycin B.\nVANCOMYCIN HYDROCHLORIDE\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white hygroscopic powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. In the test for Vancomycin B’ the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution (a).corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. A10.0 per cent w/v solution in woier \nis clear (2.4.1), absorbance of the solution at 450 nm (2.4.7) is \nnot more than 0.1.\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 4.5, determined in a 5.0 per cent w/v solution \nin cqrbondioxide-jree water.\nVancomycin B. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTE- - Use freshly prepared solutions.\nTest solution (a). Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in mobile phase A and dilute to 5.0 ml with mobile \nphase A.\nTest solution (b). Dilute 2.0 ml of test solution (a) to 50.0 ml \nwith mobile phase A,\nTest solution (c/Dilute 0.5 ml of test solution (b) to 20.0 ml \nwith mobile phase A.\nReference solution. A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of vancomycin \nhydrochloride IPRS in water. Heat at 65° for 24 hours, allow \nto cool.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 1 volume of tetrahydro-\nfuranj volumes of acetonitrile and 92 volumes of buffer\nsolution prepared by diluting 1 ml of triethylamine to \n500 ml with water, adjusted to pH 3.2 with ortho­\nphosphoric acid,\nB. a mixture of 1 volume of tetrahydro­\nfuran, 29 volumes of acetonitrile and 70 volumes of\nbuffer solution, \n1\n- a gradient programme using conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\n-* injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\n. Mobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n100\n0\n13\n100\n0\n22\n0 .\n100\n. 26\n0\n100\n28\n100\n0\n3921\nwww.webofpharma.com\nInject the reference solution and test solution (a),, (b) and (c).' \nThe test is not valid unless .the resolution between the two \nprincipal peaks in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution is not less than 5.0, signal-to-hoise ratio for \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with test \nsolution (c) is not less than 5.0 and the tailing factor for the \npeak due to vancomycin iii the chromatogram obtained with \nthe test solution (b) is not more than 1.6.\nCalculate the percentage content of vancomycin B \nhydrochloride using the following expression:\n. . ^xlOO .\n( 4 Y '\nA, + “\n125 )\nwhere Ab = area of the peak due to vancomycin B in the\nchromatogram obtained with test solution (b);\nAt ~ sum of the areas of the peaks.due to impurities\nin the chromatogram obtained with test \n/ solution (a).:\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Vancomycin B with following \nmodifications.\nInject test solutions (a), (b) and (c). Iii the' chromatogram \nobtained with test solution (a), the area of any secondary \npeak is not more than 4.0 per cent and the sum of areas of all \nthe secondary peaks is,not more than 7.0 per cent. Ignore any \npeak with an area, less than the area of the principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with test solution (c) (0.1 per cent).\nfollowing expression: y :;.\nCalculate the percentage content of each, impurity using the\ni~|xl0Ô\n' ' 125 J\n■ A + Y2-\n125J\n. Ai - area of the peak due to an impurity in the\nchromatogram obtained with test solution (a);\n■ Ab ~ .area, of the peak due to vancomycin B in the\nchromatogram obtained with test solution (b); \nAt = sum of the areas of the peaks due to impurities\nin the chromatogram obtained with test \n- ■ solution (a). >■\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 0.67 g complies with thé limit test for \nheavy metals, MethodB(30 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 1.0 per cent.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.25 Endotoxin \nUnit per mg;\nVANCOMYCIN HYDROCHLORIDE\n{p 2022\nVancomycin Hydrochloride intended fohiuse in thfT \nmanufacture of parenteral preparations without a further \nappropriate sterilization procedure complies with the \nfollowing additional requirement. . \n\"'J :\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with thetestfor sterility.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 5.0 per cent, determined on\nAssay. Determine by the microbiological assay of antibiotics \nMethod A or Method B (2.2.10) using vancomycin \nhydrochloride IPRS.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture, at a \ntemperature between 2° to 8°.If the substancejs sterile; store ■ \nin a sterile, air tight, tamper proof container. \n; ■;\nIf it is intended for use in the manufacture of parenteral \npreparations, the container should be sterile, tamper-proof \nand sealed so as to exclude micro-organisms.: \n7"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vancomycin-intravenous-infusion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vancomycin Intravenous Infusion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"VANCOMYCIN INTRAVENOUS INFUSION\n......................-..................... -...........................................\n^gium. 900 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nCarry out the method as described under Assay.\nA; Not Jess than 85 per cent of the stated amount of"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"VANCOMYCIN INTRAVENOUS INFUSION\n......................-..................... -...........................................\n^gium. 900 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nCarry out the method as described under Assay.\nA; Not Jess than 85 per cent of the stated amount of"},{"name":"Identification","text":"■\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 8.0 per cent.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Determine by the microbiological assay of antibiotics, \nMethod A(2.2.10) on a solution prepared in the following\nmanner.\npj^apf less .than 5 capsules in a glass blender jar and blend"},{"name":"Tests","text":"at high speed for 3 to 5 minutes with a sufficient volume of \nBuffer No. 3 to yield a stock solution having a convenient \nconcentration of vancomycin. Dilute a measured volume of\nsolution with Buffer No. 3 to obtain'a test dilution \na concentration assumed to be equal to the median \ndose level of the standard.\nSfoHige. Store protected from moisture.\nVancomycin Intravenous Infusion\nVancomycin Intravenous Infusion is a sterile solution of \nVancomycin Hydrochloride in Water for Injections. It is \nprepared by dissolving Vancomycin.Hydrochloride for \nIntravenous Infusion in Water for injections and then diluting\nwith the requisite volume of a suitable diluent in accordance \nwith the manufacturer’s instructions.\nVancomycin Intravenous Infusion contains not less than 90.0 \nper,cent and not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount \nof vancomycin, C/.0H -<Cl2N9O24.\nThe intravenous infusion complies with the tests stated under \nParenteral Preparations.,\nStorage. , Vancomycin Intravenous Infusion should be used \nimmediately after preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer when prepared and stored \nstrictly in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions.\nUsual strengths. 500 mg; 1 g.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Intravenous \nInfusion\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Intravenous Infusion is a sterile \nWerial consisting of Vancomycin Hydrochloride with or. \nwithout excipients. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Intravenous Infusion contains \nnot less than 8 8.0 per cent of Vancomycin B.\nA. In the test for vancomycin B, the retention time of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution corresponds to that in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution.\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. A 10.0 per cent w/v solution of \nVancomycin Hydrochloride is clear (2.4.1) and the absorbance, \nofthe solution at 450 nm (2.4.7) is not more than 0.1.\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 4.5, determined on a 5.0 per cent w/v solution \nof Vancomycin Hydrochloride.\nVancomycin B. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTE—Use the solutions within 4 hours of preparation.\nTest solution (a). Dissolve a quantity of the substance under \nexamination in sufficient mobile phase Ato produce a solution \ncontaining 2,000 IU of vancomycin per ml. .\nTest solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of test solution (a) to 25.0 ml \nwith mobile phase A.\nTest solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml of test solution (b) to 40.0 ml. \nwith mobile phase A. '\nReference solution. A 0.05, per cent w/v solution of \nvancomycin hydrochloride IP RS in water. Heat at -65° for \n24 hours and allow to cool.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nendcapped octadecylsilane bonded tb porous silica \n(5 pm) (Such as Hypersil ODS),\n- \n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 1 volumes of teirahydro- \nfuran, ! volumes of acetonitrile and 92 volumes of\nbuffer solution prepared by diluting 1 ml of triethylamine \nto 500 ml,with water, adjusted to pH 3.2 with \northophosphoric acid,\nB. a mixture of 1 volume of tetrahydro- \nfiiran, 29 volumes of acetonitrile and 70 volumes of\nbuffer solution,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\n. , - spectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\n3923\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVANCOMYCIN INTRAVENOUS INFUSION\nTime ■\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(percent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n100\n••.••-.•V. • \n0 •••\n13\n. \n100\n;y ■ \n0\n21\n100 .\n25\n100\n0\n35\n100\n0\nInject the reference solution, test solution (b)and (c), The test \nis not valid unless inJhechromatogram obtained with test \nsolution (c), signal-to- noise ratio of the principal peak is not \nless than 5; the tailing factor of the vancomycin peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution (b) is not more than\n1.6 and the resolution between the two principar peaks in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution is not less \nthan 5.0.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), using test solutions (a), (b) and (c) as described under \nVancomycin B. . \n: \n< \n;\nChromatographic system as described under Vancomycin B.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with test solution (a) calculate \nthe content of each impurity.\nThe content of any impurity is not more than 4.0 per cent and \nthe sum of the contents of any such impurities is not more \nthan 12.0 per cent. Ignore any peak with an area less than that \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with test \nsolution (c) (0.1 per cent).\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 5.0 percent, determined on 0.5 g.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Dissolve the contents of the \nsealed container in tris-chloride buffer pH 7.4 prepared using \n■water BET to give a solution containing 9000 Ünits of \nvancomycin per ml. Carry out the test on the resulting solution; \nthe maximum allowable endotoxin concentration of the solution \nis 2.5 Units of endotoxins per ml. Carry out the test using the \nmaximum valid dilution of the prepared solution calculated \nfrom the declared sensitivity of the lysate used in the test.\nAssay. Determine the weight of the contents of ten containers \nas described under test fpr? Uniformity of weight. under \nParenteral Preparations (Powders for Injection).;.\nDetermine on the mixed contents of ten containers by \nmicrobiological assay of antibiotics, Method A or Method B \n(2.2.10). \n.'\nLabelling. The label states (1) the total number of Units of \nvancomycin in the container and (2) the number of Units of \nvancomycin per mg."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vancomycin-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vancomycin Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vancomycin Oral Solution\nVancomycin Oral Solution is a solution of Vancomycin \nHydrochloride in a suitable flavoured vehicle. It is prepared \nby dissolving Vancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution in \nthe requisite amount of a suitable diluent.\nThe oral solution complies with the tests stated -undei'' \n \nLiquids.\n•"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vancomycin Oral Solution\nVancomycin Oral Solution is a solution of Vancomycin \nHydrochloride in a suitable flavoured vehicle. It is prepared \nby dissolving Vancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution in \nthe requisite amount of a suitable diluent.\nThe oral solution complies with the tests stated -undei'' \n \nLiquids.\n•"},{"name":"VANCOMYCIN INTRAVENOUS INFUSION","text":"IP 2018 \n200"},{"name":"VANILLIN","text":"Storage. Vancomycin Oral Solution should be used\n_ mobile phase: A. a mixture of 4 ml of triethylamine\nVanillin\n imediately after preparation but, in any case, within the p\neriod\ndiluted with 1996 ml of water, adjusted to pH 3.2 with \northophosphoric acid (solution A), 10 volumes of \ntetrahydrofuran and 70 volumes of acetonitrile to 920 \nvolumes of solution A,\n recomended by the manufacturer when prepared and stor\ned \nstrictly in accordance with the manufacturer's instructions.\nCHO\nUsual strength. 50 mg per ml.\nB. a mixture of 10 volumes of tetrahydro-\nfilran, 290 volumes of acetonitrile  and 700 volumes of"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vancomycin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vancomycin"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vancomycin \t\nBacillus subtilis \t\n6633 \nStaphylococcus aureus \t\n6538\n1. American Type Culture Collection, 21301 Park Lawn Drive, \nRockville, MD20852, USA.\nPreparation of inoculum. Prepare the micro, \nfor the inoculum for the assay as given i\n2.2.10. MICROBIOLOGICAL ASSAY OF ANTIBIOTICS \n2.2.10. MICROBIOLOGICAL ASSAY OF ANTIBIOTICS \nIP 2018 \t\nIP 2018\nTable 5 — Preparation of inoculum\nsuspensions are prepared by these methods, growth \ncharacteristics are sufficiently uniform so that the inoculum \ncan be adequately determined by the trials given below.\nSuggested \t\nSuggested inoculum composition \ndilution Medium \t\nAmount \t\nAntibiotics \nfactor \t (ml per 100 ml) \t\nassayed\nTest organism \nIncubation conditions \nMedium/ Temp. Time \nMethod of \t\n(°) \nPreparation\nDetermination of inoculum\nFor Method A. After the suspension is prepared as given under \nTable 5, add different volumes of it to each of several different \nflasks containing 100 ml of the medium specified in Table 5 \n(the volume of suspension suggested in Table 5 may be used \nas a guide). Using these inocula, prepare inoculated plates as \ndescribed for the specific antibiotic assay. While conducting \ncylinder-plate assays, double-layer plates may be prepared \nby pouring a seed layer (inoculated with the desired micro \norganism) over a solidified uninoculated base layer. For each \nPetri dish, 21 ml of base layer and 4 ml of the seed layer may be \ngenerally suitable. Fill each cylinder with the median \nconcentration of the antibiotic (Table 3) and then incubate the \nplates. After incubation, examine and measure the zones of \ninhibition. The volume of suspension that produces the \noptimum zones of inhibition with respect to both clarity and \ndiameter determines the inoculum to be used for the assay.\nF \t\nAs required \t\nOxytetracycline\nBacillus cereus var. mycoides(11778)A' /2 \n32-35 \n5 days"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Vancomycin \t\nBacillus subtilis \t\n6633 \nStaphylococcus aureus \t\n6538\n1. American Type Culture Collection, 21301 Park Lawn Drive, \nRockville, MD20852, USA.\nPreparation of inoculum. Prepare the micro, \nfor the inoculum for the assay as given i\n2.2.10. MICROBIOLOGICAL ASSAY OF ANTIBIOTICS \n2.2.10. MICROBIOLOGICAL ASSAY OF ANTIBIOTICS \nIP 2018 \t\nIP 2018\nTable 5 — Preparation of inoculum\nsuspensions are prepared by these methods, growth \ncharacteristics are sufficiently uniform so that the inoculum \ncan be adequately determined by the trials given below.\nSuggested \t\nSuggested inoculum composition \ndilution Medium \t\nAmount \t\nAntibiotics \nfactor \t (ml per 100 ml) \t\nassayed\nTest organism \nIncubation conditions \nMedium/ Temp. Time \nMethod of \t\n(°) \nPreparation\nDetermination of inoculum\nFor Method A. After the suspension is prepared as given under \nTable 5, add different volumes of it to each of several different \nflasks containing 100 ml of the medium specified in Table 5 \n(the volume of suspension suggested in Table 5 may be used \nas a guide). Using these inocula, prepare inoculated plates as \ndescribed for the specific antibiotic assay. While conducting \ncylinder-plate assays, double-layer plates may be prepared \nby pouring a seed layer (inoculated with the desired micro \norganism) over a solidified uninoculated base layer. For each \nPetri dish, 21 ml of base layer and 4 ml of the seed layer may be \ngenerally suitable. Fill each cylinder with the median \nconcentration of the antibiotic (Table 3) and then incubate the \nplates. After incubation, examine and measure the zones of \ninhibition. The volume of suspension that produces the \noptimum zones of inhibition with respect to both clarity and \ndiameter determines the inoculum to be used for the assay.\nF \t\nAs required \t\nOxytetracycline\nBacillus cereus var. mycoides(11778)A' /2 \n32-35 \n5 days"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vanillin-glacial-acetic-acid-reagent-equal-volumes-of-5-per","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vanillin glacial acetic acid reagent: Equal volumes of 5 per"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vanillin glacial acetic acid reagent: Equal volumes of 5 per\ncent w/v solution of Vanillin in glacial acetic acid and 10 per \ncent w/v solution of sulphuric acid in water. Use freshly \nprepared reagent.\nVanillin Solution; Phosphoric: Dissolve 1g of'vanillin in \n25 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and .add 25 ml of water, and\n35 ml of orthophosphoric acid.\nVanillin Sulphuric Acid: Mix 98 volumes of a 1.0 per cent w/ \nv solution of vanillin in ethanol (95 per cent) and 2 volumes \nof sulphuric acid. \n'\nVanillin Sulphuric Acid Solution: A 5.0 per cent w/v solution \nof vanillin sulphuric acid.. . : .\nNOTE—Vanillin sulphuric acid solution ' should be freshly \nprepared.\nVeratricAcid; 3,4-DimethoxybenzoicAcid; C9li|0O4- 182.17\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nmp, about 180°.\n2-Vinylpyridine: C7H7N = 105.1\nYellow liquid, miscible in water.\ndll ’ a^out 0.97; \n, about 1.549.\n4-Vinylpyridine; 4-Ethenylpyridine: C7H7N =105.1\nClear, deep yellowish-brown liquid; mp, 58° to 61°.\nWash Solution pH 2.5: To 500, ml of a 1 percent v/v solution of \nnitric, acid add strong ammonia solution until pH of the \nsolution is 2.5. To the resulting solution add 10 ml of buffer \nsollutionpH2.5 and mix.\nWater: Purified water of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nWater, Ammonia-free: To 100 ml of water add 0.1 ml of \nsulphuric acid and distil discarding the. first: 10 ml and\ncollecting the following 50 mE . \n,\nComplies with the following test.\nTo 50 ml, add 2 ini of alkaline potassium mercuri-iodide\nsolution\", no colour is produced.\nWater, Carbon Dioxide-free: Water which has been boiled \nvigorously for a few minutes and protected from the\natmosphere during cooling and storage.\n1132\nwww.webofpharma.com\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS\nIP2022\nWater, Distilled: Purified Water of the Indian Pharmacopoeia \nthat has been prepared by distillation.\nWater for Injection: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nWater for Injection, Sterile: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia. .\nWater, Nitrate free: To 100 ml of water add about 5 mg each \naipotassium permanganate and barium hydroxide and distil \ndiscarding the first 10 ml and collecting the following 50 ml.\nWater Piirified: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia\nWool Fat: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia\nXanthydrol; 9-Hydroxyxanthene; Xanthen-9-ol; C13H!0O2 =\n198.22\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite to pale yellow powder; mp, about 123p; contains not\nless than 90.0 per cent of C j3Hi0O2.\nXanthydrol is also available.as a solution in methanol \ncontaining 9.0 to 11.0;per cent w/v C]3Hj0O2\nassay—In a250 ml flask disslove 0.3 gin 3 nA of methanol or\nuse 3 ml of solution. Add 50 ml of glacial acetic acid and add, \ndropwise with shaking, 25 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution of\nurea. Allow to stand for 12 hours, collect the precipitate on a \nsintered glass filter (16 mm), wash with 20 ml of ethanol \n(95per cent), dry the precipitate at 105° and weigh.\n1 gof the precipitate is equivalent to 0.9429 g oftheCi3HI0O2.\nStore protected from light. If a methanolic solution is used, \nstore in small, sealed ampoules and filter before use, if\nnecessary.\nXanthydrol Reagent: Dissolve about 0.125 g ofxanthydrolin\n100 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic acid. Add 1 ml of \nhydrochloric acid immediately before use.\nXylene; Mixture of o-, m- and p-isomers; C6H4(CH3)2 = 106.17\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless, clear, flammable liquid; bp, about 140°; wt. per ml,\nabout 0.855 g.\nw-Xylene; 1,3-Dimethylbenzene: C8Hi0 = 106.2\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nd^, about 0.884; n™, about 1.497; bp, about 139°; mp, about\n-47°.\nXylose: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nZinc: Zn= 65.38\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce;.\nSilver-white cylinders, granules, pellets dr filings with a blue\nsheen; contains not less than 99.5 per cent of Zn. .\nComplies with the following test.\narsenic — To 5 g add 15 ml of hydrochloric acid and 25 ml of \nwater. Add 0.1 ml of stannous chloride solution AsT and 5 ml \nof 0.1 Mpotassium iodide^ Carry out the limit test for arsenic \n(2.3.10); n"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Vanillin glacial acetic acid reagent: Equal volumes of 5 per\ncent w/v solution of Vanillin in glacial acetic acid and 10 per \ncent w/v solution of sulphuric acid in water. Use freshly \nprepared reagent.\nVanillin Solution; Phosphoric: Dissolve 1g of'vanillin in \n25 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and .add 25 ml of water, and\n35 ml of orthophosphoric acid.\nVanillin Sulphuric Acid: Mix 98 volumes of a 1.0 per cent w/ \nv solution of vanillin in ethanol (95 per cent) and 2 volumes \nof sulphuric acid. \n'\nVanillin Sulphuric Acid Solution: A 5.0 per cent w/v solution \nof vanillin sulphuric acid.. . : .\nNOTE—Vanillin sulphuric acid solution ' should be freshly \nprepared.\nVeratricAcid; 3,4-DimethoxybenzoicAcid; C9li|0O4- 182.17\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nmp, about 180°.\n2-Vinylpyridine: C7H7N = 105.1\nYellow liquid, miscible in water.\ndll ’ a^out 0.97; \n, about 1.549.\n4-Vinylpyridine; 4-Ethenylpyridine: C7H7N =105.1\nClear, deep yellowish-brown liquid; mp, 58° to 61°.\nWash Solution pH 2.5: To 500, ml of a 1 percent v/v solution of \nnitric, acid add strong ammonia solution until pH of the \nsolution is 2.5. To the resulting solution add 10 ml of buffer \nsollutionpH2.5 and mix.\nWater: Purified water of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nWater, Ammonia-free: To 100 ml of water add 0.1 ml of \nsulphuric acid and distil discarding the. first: 10 ml and\ncollecting the following 50 mE . \n,\nComplies with the following test.\nTo 50 ml, add 2 ini of alkaline potassium mercuri-iodide\nsolution\", no colour is produced.\nWater, Carbon Dioxide-free: Water which has been boiled \nvigorously for a few minutes and protected from the\natmosphere during cooling and storage.\n1132\nwww.webofpharma.com\n4.2. GENERAL REAGENTS\nIP2022\nWater, Distilled: Purified Water of the Indian Pharmacopoeia \nthat has been prepared by distillation.\nWater for Injection: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nWater for Injection, Sterile: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia. .\nWater, Nitrate free: To 100 ml of water add about 5 mg each \naipotassium permanganate and barium hydroxide and distil \ndiscarding the first 10 ml and collecting the following 50 ml.\nWater Piirified: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia\nWool Fat: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia\nXanthydrol; 9-Hydroxyxanthene; Xanthen-9-ol; C13H!0O2 =\n198.22\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite to pale yellow powder; mp, about 123p; contains not\nless than 90.0 per cent of C j3Hi0O2.\nXanthydrol is also available.as a solution in methanol \ncontaining 9.0 to 11.0;per cent w/v C]3Hj0O2\nassay—In a250 ml flask disslove 0.3 gin 3 nA of methanol or\nuse 3 ml of solution. Add 50 ml of glacial acetic acid and add, \ndropwise with shaking, 25 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution of\nurea. Allow to stand for 12 hours, collect the precipitate on a \nsintered glass filter (16 mm), wash with 20 ml of ethanol \n(95per cent), dry the precipitate at 105° and weigh.\n1 gof the precipitate is equivalent to 0.9429 g oftheCi3HI0O2.\nStore protected from light. If a methanolic solution is used, \nstore in small, sealed ampoules and filter before use, if\nnecessary.\nXanthydrol Reagent: Dissolve about 0.125 g ofxanthydrolin\n100 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic acid. Add 1 ml of \nhydrochloric acid immediately before use.\nXylene; Mixture of o-, m- and p-isomers; C6H4(CH3)2 = 106.17\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nColourless, clear, flammable liquid; bp, about 140°; wt. per ml,\nabout 0.855 g.\nw-Xylene; 1,3-Dimethylbenzene: C8Hi0 = 106.2\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nd^, about 0.884; n™, about 1.497; bp, about 139°; mp, about\n-47°.\nXylose: Of the Indian Pharmacopoeia.\nZinc: Zn= 65.38\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce;.\nSilver-white cylinders, granules, pellets dr filings with a blue\nsheen; contains not less than 99.5 per cent of Zn. .\nComplies with the following test.\narsenic — To 5 g add 15 ml of hydrochloric acid and 25 ml of \nwater. Add 0.1 ml of stannous chloride solution AsT and 5 ml \nof 0.1 Mpotassium iodide^ Carry out the limit test for arsenic \n(2.3.10); no stain is produced on the mercuric chloride paper.\nactivity — Repeat the test for arsenic using the same \nreagents and adding 1 ml of arsenic standard solution\n(1 ppm As); a distinct stain appears on the mercuric chloride \npaper. \n2\nZincAcetate:(C2H302)2Zn,2H2O=219.5\nGeneral reagent grade of commerce.\nmp, about 237°.\nZinc Acetate, 0.25 M: Mix 600 ml of water with 150 ml of \nglacial acetic acid and 54.9 g of zinc acetate, stir to dissolve.\nWhile stirring, add 150 ml of ammonium hydroxide, cool to \nroom temperature, and adjust to pH of 6.4 with ammonium \nhydroxide and dilute to 1000 ml with water."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vanillin-solution-phosphoric-dissolve-1-g-of-vanillin-in-25","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vanillin Solution, Phosphoric: Dissolve 1 g of vanillin in 25"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vanillin Solution, Phosphoric: Dissolve 1 g of vanillin in 25\nARSENIC - To 5 g add 15 ml of hydrochloric acid and 25 ml of \nwater. Add 0.1 ml ofstannous chloride solution AsT  and 5 ml \nof 0.1 Mpotassium iodide. Carry out the limit test for arsenic \n(2.3.10); no stain is produced on the mercuric chloride paper.\nml of ethanol (95per cent) and add 25 ml of water and 35 ml of\northophosphoric acid.\nVanillin Sulphuric Acid: Mix 98 volumes of a 1.0 per cent w/ \nv solution of vanillin in ethanol (95 per cent) and 2 volumes \nof sulphuric acid.\n1 g of the precipitate is equivalent to 0.9429 g of the C 13H 1002. \nStore protected from light. If a methanolic solution is used, \nstore in small, sealed ampoules and filter before use, if \nnecessary.\nACTIVITY - Repeat the test for ARSENIC using the same \nreagents and adding 1 ml of arsenic standard solution \n(1 ppm As); a distinct stain appears on the  mercuric chloride \npaper.\nVanillin Sulphuric Acid Solution: A 5.0 per cent w v solution \nof vanillin in sulphuric acid.\nNOTE-Vanillin sulphuric acid solution should be freshly \nprepared.\nXanthydrol Reagent: Dissolve about 0.125 g ofxanthydrol in\nZinc AsT: Granulated zinc which complies with the following \nadditional test. To 10 g add 15 ml ofstannous chloride solution \nAsT and 5 ml of 0.1 Mpotassium iodide. Use the apparatus\n100 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic acid. Add 1 ml of \nhydrochloric acid immediately before use.\nVeratric Acid; 3,4-Dimethoxybenzoic Acid; C9H1004= 182.17\nXylene; Mixture of o-, m- and p-isomers; C 6H4(CH3)2 = 106.17\nand method described in Appendix 2.3.10, but continue the \naction for 1 hour; no visible stain is produced on mercuric \nchlort& Taper: Repeat the test with the addition of 0.1 ml of \narse* standard solution (10 ppm); a faint but distinct stain\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nmp, about:1809 :-\nWash-SolutiOrpH 23: To 500 ml of a 1 per cent v/v solution of\nColourless, clear, flammable liquid; bp, about' \nabout 0.855 g.\nnitric acid add strong ammonia solution until pH of the\nis produceds\nZN\\ \nff. 4-4ft\nZinc Bis(diphenyldithiocarbamate): C6H 12N2S2n = 305.8\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite odourless powder, mp, about 250°."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Vanillin Solution, Phosphoric: Dissolve 1 g of vanillin in 25\nARSENIC - To 5 g add 15 ml of hydrochloric acid and 25 ml of \nwater. Add 0.1 ml ofstannous chloride solution AsT  and 5 ml \nof 0.1 Mpotassium iodide. Carry out the limit test for arsenic \n(2.3.10); no stain is produced on the mercuric chloride paper.\nml of ethanol (95per cent) and add 25 ml of water and 35 ml of\northophosphoric acid.\nVanillin Sulphuric Acid: Mix 98 volumes of a 1.0 per cent w/ \nv solution of vanillin in ethanol (95 per cent) and 2 volumes \nof sulphuric acid.\n1 g of the precipitate is equivalent to 0.9429 g of the C 13H 1002. \nStore protected from light. If a methanolic solution is used, \nstore in small, sealed ampoules and filter before use, if \nnecessary.\nACTIVITY - Repeat the test for ARSENIC using the same \nreagents and adding 1 ml of arsenic standard solution \n(1 ppm As); a distinct stain appears on the  mercuric chloride \npaper.\nVanillin Sulphuric Acid Solution: A 5.0 per cent w v solution \nof vanillin in sulphuric acid.\nNOTE-Vanillin sulphuric acid solution should be freshly \nprepared.\nXanthydrol Reagent: Dissolve about 0.125 g ofxanthydrol in\nZinc AsT: Granulated zinc which complies with the following \nadditional test. To 10 g add 15 ml ofstannous chloride solution \nAsT and 5 ml of 0.1 Mpotassium iodide. Use the apparatus\n100 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic acid. Add 1 ml of \nhydrochloric acid immediately before use.\nVeratric Acid; 3,4-Dimethoxybenzoic Acid; C9H1004= 182.17\nXylene; Mixture of o-, m- and p-isomers; C 6H4(CH3)2 = 106.17\nand method described in Appendix 2.3.10, but continue the \naction for 1 hour; no visible stain is produced on mercuric \nchlort& Taper: Repeat the test with the addition of 0.1 ml of \narse* standard solution (10 ppm); a faint but distinct stain\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nAnalytical reagent grade of commerce.\nmp, about:1809 :-\nWash-SolutiOrpH 23: To 500 ml of a 1 per cent v/v solution of\nColourless, clear, flammable liquid; bp, about' \nabout 0.855 g.\nnitric acid add strong ammonia solution until pH of the\nis produceds\nZN\\ \nff. 4-4ft\nZinc Bis(diphenyldithiocarbamate): C6H 12N2S2n = 305.8\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nWhite odourless powder, mp, about 250°."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vanillin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vanillin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vanillin\nf (5 pm) (Such as Hypersil ODS), ' \n'• \n'\nA. mobile phase: A. a mixture of 4 rril oftriethylamine\ndiluted with 1996 ml of water, adjusted to pH 3.2 with \northophosphoric' acid (solution A), 10 volumes of\n' tetrahydrofuran and 70 volumes of acetonitrile to. 920\niVgre volumes of solution A,\n.: \nB. a mixture of 10 volumes of tetrdhydro- \no s-rfaran, 290 volumes of acetonitrile and 700 volumes of\n¿^ resolution A, : \n■■'■■■ -\nkçÿra giadientprogramme using the conditions given below,\n_ flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\ny- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nTime.\nMobile phase A . .\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/y) ,\n(per cent v/v)\n'100\n0"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vanillin\nf (5 pm) (Such as Hypersil ODS), ' \n'• \n'\nA. mobile phase: A. a mixture of 4 rril oftriethylamine\ndiluted with 1996 ml of water, adjusted to pH 3.2 with \northophosphoric' acid (solution A), 10 volumes of\n' tetrahydrofuran and 70 volumes of acetonitrile to. 920\niVgre volumes of solution A,\n.: \nB. a mixture of 10 volumes of tetrdhydro- \no s-rfaran, 290 volumes of acetonitrile and 700 volumes of\n¿^ resolution A, : \n■■'■■■ -\nkçÿra giadientprogramme using the conditions given below,\n_ flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\ny- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nTime.\nMobile phase A . .\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/y) ,\n(per cent v/v)\n'100\n0"},{"name":"Identification","text":"13 :.\n.100\n. 0 ■\n¿i 21 . .\n. 0\n100 f\n. 25\n100\no\n.:.35 - -\n100\n0\nInject each solution. The test is not valid unless in the \ncfirohiatogram obtained with test solution (c) signal-to- noise \nratio¡’of the principal'peak is not less than 5.0. The tailing \nfactor of thé principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith test solution (b) is not more than 1.6; and the resolution \nbetween the two principal peaks in thé chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution is not less than 5.0.\nRelated substances. Determine by Hquid chromatography \n(2.4,14), using test solution (a), (b) and (c) as described under \nVancomycin B."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Use chromatographic system as described under Vancomycin\nInject the reference solution and test solution (c). The area of \nany secondary peak is not more than 4.0 per cent and the sum \nof the areas of all the secondary peaks is not more than\n12.0per cent. Ignore any peak with an area less than the area \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution (c) (O’. 1 per cent).\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 5.6 per cent, determined on \n0-5g.\nAssay; Determine on the mixed contents of ten containers by \nthemicrobiological assay of antibiotics, Method A of Method \nB(2.2JO). \n'\nLabelling. The label states (1) the total number of Units of\nvancomycin in the container and (2) the number of Units of \nvancomycin per mg.\n'VANILLIN\nCHO\ny^OCHj\n' \nOH\nC8HsO3 \n■ ' \nMol. Wt. 152.2\nVanillin is 4-hydroxy-3-methoxybenzaldehyde.\nVanillin contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more than\n101.0 per cent of C8H8O3, calculated On the dried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (flavouring agent).\nDescription: A white or slightly yellow powder or crystalline \nneedles.\nTest Amay he omitted if tests B, G and Dare carried out. Tests \nB, C and D may be omitted if test A is carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4,6); \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with vanillin IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of vanillin.\nB. In the test for Related substances, the principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution (b) corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b). Examine the chromatograms in daylight after spraying.\nC. To 5 ml of a saturated solution add 0.2 ml offerric chloride \nsolution', a blue colour is produced. Heat to 80°; the solution \nbecomes brown and a white precipitate is produced on cooling.\nD. Melting range (2.4.21 ). 81° to 84°.\nAppearance of solution. A 5.0 per cent w/v solution in ethanol \n(95per cent) is clear (2.4.1) and not more intensely coloured \nthàn reference solution BS6 (2.4.1 ).\nRelated substances: Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gelGF254;\nMobile phase. A mixture of 98.5 volumes of dichloro methane,\n1 volume of methanol and 0.5 volume of anhydrous acetic \nacid. ' \n' \n■■■'-'■ \n■ /\nTest solution (a). Dissolve 0.2 g of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of methanol.\nTest solution (b). Dissolve 0.2 g of the substance under\nexamination in 100.0 ml of methanol.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 10 mg Of the substance under \nexamination in 100.0 ml oAmethanol.\n3925\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/varicella-vaccine-live","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Varicella Vaccine, Live"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Varicella Vaccine, Live\nVaricella Vaccine (Live) is a freeze-dried preparation of a suitable \n.attenuated strain of Herpesvirus varicellae."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Varicella Vaccine, Live\nVaricella Vaccine (Live) is a freeze-dried preparation of a suitable \n.attenuated strain of Herpesvirus varicellae."},{"name":"Production","text":"General provisions\n.The production of vaccine is based on a virus séed-lot system\nand a cell-bank system. The production method shall have \nbeen shown to yield consistently live varicella vaccines of\nadequate immunogenicity arid safety in man. The virus in the \nfinal Vaccine shall not have been passaged in cell cultures \n■beyond the 38th passage from the original isolated virus.\nThe production method is validated to demonstrate that the \nproduct, if tested, would comply with the test for safety and \nefficacy. \n• ''\nSubstrate for virus propagation"},{"name":"Identification","text":"The virus is propagated inhuman diploid cells (2.7.2).\nseed lot\nThe strain of varicella virus shall be identified as being suitable\nby historical records which shall include information on the \norigin of the strain and its subsequent manipulation. The virus \nshall at no time have been passaged in continuous cell lines. \nSeed lots are prepared in the same kind of cells as those used \nfor the production of the final vaccine. To avoid the\nVARICELLA VACCINE, LIVE\nunnecessary, use. of monkeys in the test for neurovirulence, \nvirus seed lots are prepared in large quantities and stored at \ntemperatures below -20°, if freeze-dried, or below -6()c, if not \nfreeze-dried.\nOnly a virus seed lot that complies with the following \nrequirements may be used for virus propagation.\nThe master and working seed lots are identified as varicella \nvirus by serum; neutralisation in cell culture, using specific \nantibodies.\nVirus concentration. The virus concentration of the master \nand working seed lots is determined as prescribed under Assay \nto monitor consistency of production.\nExtraneous agents (2.7.3). Complies with the requirements for \nseed lots for live virus vaccines; a sample of 50 ml is taken for \nthe test in cell cultures.\nNeuroviruience (2.7.5). Complies with the test for \nneurovirulence of live virus vaccines.\nMycoplasma (2.7.4).Complies with the test for absence of \nmycoplasma.\nPROPAGATION AND HARVEST\nAll processing of the cell bank and subsequent cell cultures is \ndone under aseptic conditions in an area where no other cells \nare handled. Approved animal (but not human) serum may be \nused in the media. Serum and trypsin used in the preparation \nof cell suspensions and media are shown to be free from \nextraneous agents. The cell culture medium may contain a pH \nindicator such as phenol red and approved antibiotics at the \nlowest effective concentration. It is preferable to have a \nsubstrate free from antibiotics during production. 5.0 per cent, \nbut not less than 50.0 ml, of the cell Cultures employed for \nvaccine production is set aside as uninfected cell cultures \n(control cells). The infected cells constituting a single harvest \nare washed, released from the support surface and pooled. \nThe cell suspension is disrupted by sonication. ;\nOnly a virus harvest that complies with the following \nrequirements may be used in the preparation of the filial bulk \nvaccine. \n• \n• •\nThe virus harvest contains virus that is identifiedas varicella \nvirus by serum neutralisation in cell culture, using specific \nantibodies.\nVirus concentration. The concentration of infective virus in \nvirus harvests is determined as prescribed under assay to \nmonitor consistency of production and to determine the \ndilution to be used for the final bulk vaccine.; J : >\nExtraneous agents (2.7.3). Use 50 ml for the test in cell cultures.\n4491\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVARICELLA VACCINE, LIVE\nIP 202'2\nControl cells. The control cells of the production cell culture \nfrom which the single harvest is derived comply with a test for \nidentity and with the requirements for extraneous agents \n(2.7.3).\nMycoplasma (2.7.4).Complies with the test for absence of \nmycoplasma.\nFIN AL BULK VACC INE\nVirus harvests that comply with the above tests are pooled \nand clarified to remove cells. A suitable stabiliser maybe added \nand the pooled harvests diluted as appropriate.\nOnly a final bulk vaccine that complies with the following \nrequirements may be used in the preparation of the final lot.\nBacterial and fungal contamination. Carry out test for sterility \n(2.2.11) using 10 ml of bulk for each sterility medium.\nFINAL LOT\nThe final bulk vaccine is distributed aseptically into sterile, \ntamper-proof containers and freeze-dried,to a moisture content \nshown to be favourable to the stability of the vaccine. The \ncontainers are then closed so as to prevent contamination \nand the introduction of moisture.\nOnly a final lot that is satisfactory with respect to each of the \nrequirements given below under Identification, Tests and \nAssay màÿ be released for use. Provided that the test for \nbovine serum albumin has been carried out with satisfactory \nresults on the final bulk vaccine, it may be omitted on the final \nlot."},{"name":"Identification","text":"When the vaccine reconstituted as stated on the label is.mixed \nwith specific Herpesvirus varicellae antibodies, it is no longer \nable to infect susceptible cell cultures."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Bacterial and fungal contamination. Complies with the test\nfor sterility (2.2.11).\nBovine serum albumin. Not more than 0.5 pg per human dose, \ndetermined by a suitable immunochemical method (2.2.14).\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 3.0 per cent, determined by the \nsemi-micro determination of water."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Mycoplasma (2.7.4).Complies with the test for absence of \nmycoplasma."},{"name":"Assay","text":"Titrate for infective virus, using at least ten cell cultures for \neach fourfold dilution or by a technique of equal precision. \nUse a suitable virus reference preparation to validate each \nassay. The virus concentration is not less than the minimum \nstated on the label.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the strain of virus used for thé \npreparation of the vaccine; (2) the type and origin of the cells \nused for the preparation of the vaccine; (3) that contact with \ndisinfectants is to be avoided; (4) the minimum: virus \nconcentration; (5) that the vaccine is not to be administered \nto pregnant women; (6) the time within which the vaccine \nmust be used after reconstitution."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/varicellavaccine-live","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"VaricellaVaccine, Live"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Varicella Vaccine, Live\nVaricellaVaccine (Live) is a freeze-dried preparationofa suitable\nattenuated strain of Helpesvirus varicellae."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Varicella Vaccine, Live\nVaricellaVaccine (Live) is a freeze-dried preparationofa suitable\nattenuated strain of Helpesvirus varicellae."},{"name":"Production","text":"General provisions\nThe production ofvaccine is based on a virus seed-lot system\nand a cell-bank system. The production method shall have\nbeen shown to yield consistently live varicella vaccines of\nadequate immunogenicity and safety in man. The virus in the\nfinal vaccine shall not have been passaged in cell cultures\nbeyond the 38th passage from the original isolated virus.\nThe production method is validated to demonstrate that the\nproduct, if tested, would comply with the test for safety and\nefficacy.\nSubstrate for virus propagation\nThe virus is propagated in human diploid .cells (2.7.2).\nSEED LOT\nThe strain ofvaricella virus shall be identified as being suitable\nby historical records which shall include information on the\norigin ofthe strain and its subsequent manipulation. The virus\nshall at no time have been passaged in continuous cell lines.\nSeed lots are prepared in the same kind ofcells as those used\nfor the production of the final vaccine. To avoid the\nunnecessary use of monkeys in the test for neurovirulence,\nvirus seed lots are prepared in large quantities and stored at\ntemperatures below -20°, iffreeze-dried, or below -60°, ifnot\nfreeze-dried.\nOnly a virus seed lot that complies with the following\nrequirements may be used for virus propagation."},{"name":"Identification","text":"The master and working seed lots are identified as varicella\nvirus by serum neutralisation in cell culture, using specific\nantibodies.\nVirus concentration. The virus concentration of the master\nand working seed lots is detelmined as prescribed underAssay\nto monitor consistency of production.\nExtraneous agents (2.7.3). Complies with the requirements for\nseed lots for live virus vaccines; a sample of50 ml is taken for\nthe test in cell cultures.\nNeurovirulence (2.7.5). Complies with the test for\nneurovirulence of live virus vaccines.\nPROPAGATION AND HARVEST\nAll processing ofthe cell bank and subsequent cell cultures is\ndone under aseptic conditions in an area where no other cells\nare handled. Approved animal (but not human) serum may be\nused in the media. Serum and trypsin used in the preparation\nof cell suspensions and media are shown to be free from\nextraneous agents. The cell culture medium may contain a pH\nindicator such as phenol red and approved antibiotics at the\nlowest effective concentration. It is preferable to have a\nsubstrate free from antibiotics during production. 5.0 per cent,\nbut not less than 50.0 ml, of the cell cultures employed for\nvaccine production is set aside as uninfected cell cultures\n(control cells). The infected cells constituting a single harvest\nare washed, released from the support surface and pooled.\nThe cell suspension is disrupted by sonication.\nOnly a virus harvest that complies with the following\nrequirements may be used in the preparation ofthe final bulk\nvaccine."},{"name":"Identification","text":"The virus harvest contains virus that is identified as varicella\nvirus by serum neutralisation in cell culture, using specific\nantibodies.\nVirus concentration. The concentration ofinfective virus in\nvirus harvests is determined as prescribed under assay to\nVARICELLAVACCINE, LIVE\nmonitor consistency of production and to determine the\ndilution to be used for the final bulle vaccine.\nExtraneous agents (2.7.3). Use 50 ml for the testincell cultures.\nControl cells. The control cells ofthe production cell culture\nfrom which the single harvest is derived comply with a test for\nidentity and with the requirements for extraneous agents\n(2.7.3).\nFINAL BULK VACCINE\nVirus harvests that comply with the above tests are pooled\nand clarified to remove cells. A suitable stabiliser may be added\nand the pooled harvests diluted as appropriate.\nOnly a final bulle vaccine that complies with the following\nrequirements may be used in the preparation ofthe final lot.\nSterility (2.2.11). Cany out test for sterility using 10 ml ofbulle\nfor each sterility medium.\nFINAL LOT\nThe final bulle vaccine is distributed aseptically into sterile,\ntamper-proofcontainers and freeze-dried to a moisture content\nshown to be favourable to the stability of the vaccine. The\ncontainers are then closed so as to prevent contamination\nand the introduction of moisture.\nOnly a final lOt that is satisfactory with respect to each ofthe\nrequirements given below under Identification, Tests and\nAssay may be released for use. Provided that the test for\nbovine serum albumin has been carried out with satisfactory\nresults on the final bulle vaccine, it may be omitted on the final\nlot."},{"name":"Identification","text":"When the vaccine reconstituted as stated on the label is mixed\nwith specific Herpesvinls varicellae antibodies, it is no longer\nable to infect susceptible cell cuIhIres."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Sterility (2.2.11) Complies with the test for sterility.\nAbnormal toxicity (2.2.1). Complies with the test for abnormal\ntoxicity.\nBovine serum albumin. Notmore than 0.5 J.Lg perhuman dose,\ndetelmined by a suitable immunochemical method (2.2.14).\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 3.0 per cent, detelmined by the\nsemi-micro determination ofwater.\n."},{"name":"Assay","text":"Tih'ate for infective virus, using at least ten cell culhu'es for\neach fourfold dilution or by a technique of equal precision.\nUse a suitable virus reference preparation to validate each\nassay. The virus concentration is not less than the minimum\nstated on the label."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vasaka-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vasaka Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Mobile phase. A mixture of 8 volumes of ethyl acetate, \n2 volumes of methanol and 1 volume of strong ammonia.\nTest solution. Dissolve 1.0 g of extract under examination with \n25 ml of methanol arid filter.\nReference solution. Afò.X ¡per cent w/v solution of vasicine \nhydrochloride IPRS in methanol.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution as bands 10 mm \nby 2 mm. Allow the mobile phase tò risei8 cm.Dry the plate in \nair and examine under ültfàvi.o.let light at 254 nm and \n366 nm. Spray the platew'ith/yrage/zr/ó'y/freagent. The \nchromatographic profile ofthe test solution is similar to that \nof the reference solution. ••^4 ¡yr \\ •. ' y. • ■..\nTests \"'Vg \n■\nLoss of drying (2.4,19). Not more than 6.0 per cent, determined \non 2.0 g when dried at 105 ° for 3 hours, i\nTotal ash (2.3.19), Not more than 2.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with thé limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nMicrobial contamination (2:2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination test.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve about 1.5 g of the extract containing \n20 mg of vasicine in 100 ml of methanoVoy gently heating, and\nfilter. \ny . <:■' ■■■;> ■\nReference solution. A 0.025 per cent w/y solution of yasicine \nhydrochloride IPRS in methanol. . \n......\nChromatographic system ’ \n,\n- a stainless steel column 250 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\nsilica bonded with cyanopropyl groups (5 gm),\n- mobile phase'. A mixture of 92 volumes of buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 1.3 6 g ófpotassium dihydfbgen \northo phosphate in 500 ml of water and 2 ml of \northophosphoric acid, dilute to; 1000 ■ ml with water,\n. 5 volumes of acetonitrile and. 3 volumes of tetra-\n. \n- ; flow rate: 1,0 mlper minute, . ;.. r , ; ;\nhydrofuran, \n■ 'w \ntl.w..’\n- \n- spectrophotometer set at 300 nm,,,. - L ;; •;,\n- injection volume: 20 pl. . \n... \n;;;;\nInject the reference solution. The test is riot valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent. - \n5/- \n•\nInject the refemce solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of vasicine.\nStorage. Store protected from heat and moisture."},"sections":[{"name":"VASAKA EXTRACT","text":"Mobile phase. A mixture of 8 volumes of ethyl acetate, \n2 volumes of methanol and 1 volume of strong ammonia.\nTest solution. Dissolve 1.0 g of extract under examination with \n25 ml of methanol arid filter.\nReference solution. Afò.X ¡per cent w/v solution of vasicine \nhydrochloride IPRS in methanol.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution as bands 10 mm \nby 2 mm. Allow the mobile phase tò risei8 cm.Dry the plate in \nair and examine under ültfàvi.o.let light at 254 nm and \n366 nm. Spray the platew'ith/yrage/zr/ó'y/freagent. The \nchromatographic profile ofthe test solution is similar to that \nof the reference solution. ••^4 ¡yr \\ •. ' y. • ■..\nTests \"'Vg \n■\nLoss of drying (2.4,19). Not more than 6.0 per cent, determined \non 2.0 g when dried at 105 ° for 3 hours, i\nTotal ash (2.3.19), Not more than 2.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with thé limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nMicrobial contamination (2:2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination test.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve about 1.5 g of the extract containing \n20 mg of vasicine in 100 ml of methanoVoy gently heating, and\nfilter. \ny . <:■' ■■■;> ■\nReference solution. A 0.025 per cent w/y solution of yasicine \nhydrochloride IPRS in methanol. . \n......\nChromatographic system ’ \n,\n- a stainless steel column 250 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\nsilica bonded with cyanopropyl groups (5 gm),\n- mobile phase'. A mixture of 92 volumes of buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 1.3 6 g ófpotassium dihydfbgen \northo phosphate in 500 ml of water and 2 ml of \northophosphoric acid, dilute to; 1000 ■ ml with water,\n. 5 volumes of acetonitrile and. 3 volumes of tetra-\n. \n- ; flow rate: 1,0 mlper minute, . ;.. r , ; ;\nhydrofuran, \n■ 'w \ntl.w..’\n- \n- spectrophotometer set at 300 nm,,,. - L ;; •;,\n- injection volume: 20 pl. . \n... \n;;;;\nInject the reference solution. The test is riot valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent. - \n5/- \n•\nInject the refemce solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of vasicine.\nStorage. Store protected from heat and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vasaka","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vasaka"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vasaka\nAdulasa; Adhatoda vasica\nIF*2022:\nVasaka consists of the dried mature leaves of Adhatoda vasica \nNees.(Fam. Acanthaceae). • \n.;\nVasaka contains not less than 0.6 per cent w/w of vasicine \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n’\nCategory. Expectorant, bronchodilator, kustha.\nDescription.Taste, bitter."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vasaka\nAdulasa; Adhatoda vasica\nIF*2022:\nVasaka consists of the dried mature leaves of Adhatoda vasica \nNees.(Fam. Acanthaceae). • \n.;\nVasaka contains not less than 0.6 per cent w/w of vasicine \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n’\nCategory. Expectorant, bronchodilator, kustha.\nDescription.Taste, bitter."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Macroscopic — Leaf pieces membranous, brittle, greyish- \nbrown, a few pieces green coloured. Floral bracts leaf-like\nB. Microscopic — Stomata diacytic,. more on the lower \nepidermis; glandular and non-glandrilar hair on both surfaces \nof leaf; elongated cystoliths present in palisade cells; . ■ / ■;\nC. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2,4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254. \n“\nMobile phase. A mixture of 8 volumes of ethyl acetate, \n2 volumes of methanol and 0.2 volume of strong ammonia \nsolution: \n■' ■ \n■ ■■ \n,\nTest solution. Reflux 1 g of coarsely powderedsubstance under \nexamination with 50 ml methanol for 15 minutes, cool and \nfilter. Reflux the residue further with 2 x 50 ml of methanol, \ncool and filter. Combine all the, filtrates arid.concentrate tq\nlomi.\nReference solution. Reflux 0.5 g of vaSaka lPBRS with 50 ml \nmethanol for 4’5 minutes, cool and filter Reflux the residue \nfurther with 2 x 50 ml of methanol, cool and filter. Combine all \nthe filtrates and concentrate to 5 ml. A \nA\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution as bands 10 mm by \n2 mm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. bry the plate in air \nand examine under ultraviolet light at 254 rim and 366 nm, \nspray with Dragendorff ’s reagent. Heat the plate at400° for \n5-10 minutes and examine in day light The chromatographic \nprofile of the test solution is similar to that of the reference \nsolution. \n<• rh v, \n_\nTests \":: ' ’ \n:\nForeign organic matter (2.6.1). Not more than 2.0 per cent\nEthanol-soluble extractive (2.6.2). Not less than 3.0 per cent\nWater-soluble extractive (2.6.3), Not less than22percentby \nMethodi. \n’ \n•.<>./.\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 21.0 per cent, \n...,,g ,\nAcid-insoluble ash (2.3.19). Not more than 2,0. per cent y\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit fost for\nheavy metals. Method B (20 ppm). -\nLoss on drying (2.4.19), Not more than l2;0per cent,\ndetermined on 5 g by drying in an oven at 105°. : :\nJp2022\n^fict&bial contamination (2.2; 9). Complies with the microbial \ncoi^rnipation iests-\n^ay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14):\nReflux about 2 g of the coarsely powdered \n'^^ace'under examination'with 50 ml of methanol'bn a\nhath for 15 minutes, cool and filter. Reflux the residue\nwith methanol till the last extract turns colorless, cool \n^filter. Combine all the filtrates and concentrate to 100,0 ml.\n¿ference solution. Dissolve 25 mg of vasicine hydrochloride\n50 ml of methanol. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to 50.0 \n¿jwitjiwcvAuno/.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with \npctadecyIsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\nL mobile phase: a mixture of 3 volumes of the solution \n:^’^prepared' by dissolving 1 g of sodium hexane-\n’ sulphonate in 1000 ml of water, 1 volume of acetonitrile \nf&f and 20 volumes of glacial acetic acid,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 300 nm,\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nInject ilie reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\nrelative standard deviation for the replicate'injections is nbt \nindrethan 2.0 per cent. \n■-\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of vasicine. \n: > r,\nStorage. Store proiected from heat, moisture and against attack\nby insects and rodents. \n~\nYasaka Extract\nVasaka extract is obtained by extracting Vasàka (Adhatoda \nvasica Nées,- Fam. Acanthaceae) dried matured leaves with \neftapol or any other suitable solvent. The powdered extract \n^y çpntain suitable excipients.\nVasaka extract contains not less than 90:0 per cent w/w and\nnot more than 120.0 percent w/w of the stated amount of\n. \n.... \n• \n..I......-;\nCategory. Expectorant, bronchodilator, kustha.\nUsual strengths. 1 per cent w/w; 1.5 per cent w/w.\nDescription. Light green to greenish brown powder; \n^racteristic odour and bitter taste. ,"},{"name":"identification","text":"A-Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17),coaiing\nplate with silica gel GF254. ; \n■ ■"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vasopressin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vasopressin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"VASOPRESSIN INJECTION\ntest solution; In the chromatogram obtained with \nlatest solution, the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"VASOPRESSIN INJECTION\ntest solution; In the chromatogram obtained with \nlatest solution, the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks"},{"name":"Identification","text":"• jiotinore than 5 per cent of the area of the principal peak. \n^crQbjal contamination (2.2.9). Total viable count not more\n^fl^Ojofu per g. For products of animal origin, 1 g is free\n^^Escherichia coll and 10 g is free from Salmonella and"},{"name":"Tests","text":"(2.3.43). Not more than 8.0 percent.\n^ty.Dctermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nDissolve about 10 mg of the substance under \n¿¿a^riation in 0.25 per cent v/v solution of glacial acetic\nand dilute to 25.0 ml with the glacial acetic acid.\nReference solution (a): Dissolve the entire contents of a vial \ndivasopressin IPRS in a known volume of 0.25 per cent v/v \nsolution of glacial acetic acid.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.0025 per cent\nw/v, each of, lypressin IPRS and vasopressin IPRS in 0.25 per \ncentv/v acetic acid.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n/ - mobile phase: a mixture of 87 volumes of buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 6.6 g of dibasic ammonium \nphosphate in about 950 ml of water, adjusted to pH 3.6\n= v with \nacid, dilute to 1000 ml with water\nand 13 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n¡spectrophotometer set at 220 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nEquilibrate the column at least for 1 hour.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to vasopressin and the \nlypressin is not less than 1.1 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent \nfor vasopressin.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C46H65Ni5O12S2..\nStorage?! Store protected from moisture, preferably of type I \nglass and at a temperature between 2° to 8°.\nVasopressin Injection\nVasopressin Injection is sterile solution of vasopressin in \nsuitable diluent.\nEach mJ of Vasopressin Injection contains an activity of not \nless than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated on the label in Vasopressin Units. It may contain a \nsuitable preservative.\nUsual strength. 20 Units per ml.\nIn the Assay, the principalpeak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 4.5.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 17.0 Endotoxin Units \nper Unit of Vasopressin.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 5.0 g ofchlorobutqnol in 5.0 ml of \nglacial acetic acid, add 1.1 g of sodium acetate, 5.0 g of\nethanol (95 per cent), and dilute to 1000 ml with water.\nTest solution. Dilute 2.0 ml (20 Units per ml) of the injection to \n25.0 ml with 0.25 per cent w/v of glacial acetic acid.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve the contents of one vial of \nvasopressin IPRS in a known volume of the solvent mixture to \nobtain the same concentration of test solution.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.0025 per cent \nw/v, each of, lypressin IPRSand vasopressin IPRS in 0.25 per \ncent v/v glacial acetic acid.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n' octadecylsilanê bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column temeperature: 40°,\n- \nmobile phase : a mixture of 87 volumes of buffer solution \nprepared by dissolving 6.6 g of dibasic ammonium \nphosphate in about 950 ml of water, adjusted to pH 3.0\nwith orthophosphoric acid, dilute to 1000 ml with water \nand 13 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate : 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometersetat220nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between vasopressin and lypressin is not less than\n1.1 and relative standard deviation for replicate injections is \nnot more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of \nin the injection.\nStorage. Store in single-dose or multiple-dose containers, \npreferably of type I glass. Do not freeze.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the number of Units of the \nVasopressin Units per ml; (2) indicate its origin (animal or \nsynthetic).\n3927\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vasopressin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vasopressin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"VASOPRESSIN\nReference solution (ft). AO.2 per cent w/v solution of vanillin \nIPRS in methanol.\nUse an unsaturated tank and allow the mobile phase to rise\n10 cm. Apply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. After \ndevelopment, dry the plate in cold air and examine under \nultraviolet light at 254 nm. Any secondary spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution (a) is not more \nintense than the spot in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a). Spray with dinitrophenylhydrazine- \naceto-hydrochloric solution and examine in daylight. Any \nsecondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with test \nsolution (a) is not more intense than,the spot in the \nchromatpgramobtained with-reference solution(a).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent. ;\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying over phosphorus pentoxide for 4 hours.\nAssay. Weigh 0.12 g, dissolve in 20 ml of ethanol (95 per \ncent), add 60 ml of carbon dioxide-free waler. Titrate with \n0.1 M sodium hydroxide^ determining the end-point potentio­\nmetrically (2.4.25). Carry but a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0:1 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01521 g of \nCgH8O3. ......... • \n■ \n- \n■ \n■■ ■ ■\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nVasopressin\nCys—Tyr—Phe—;Gin—Asn—'Cys—Pro—Arg—Gly—NH2\n1_________________________J\nArginine Vasopressin\nVasopressin, 8-1 .-arginine\nC46H65N15O12S2 \n; Mol. WL 1084.2\nVasopressin, is a polypeptide hormone haying the properties \nof causing the contraction of vascular and other smooth \nmuscles and of antidiuresis. It is prepared by chemical \nsynthesis or by animal origin.\nVasopressin contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C46H65Ni5O12S2, calculated on anhydrous \nand acetic acid-free basis.\n1 mg of anhydrous Vasopressin is equivalent to 410 \nVasopressin Units.\nCategory. Antidiuretic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.'"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"VASOPRESSIN\nReference solution (ft). AO.2 per cent w/v solution of vanillin \nIPRS in methanol.\nUse an unsaturated tank and allow the mobile phase to rise\n10 cm. Apply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. After \ndevelopment, dry the plate in cold air and examine under \nultraviolet light at 254 nm. Any secondary spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution (a) is not more \nintense than the spot in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a). Spray with dinitrophenylhydrazine- \naceto-hydrochloric solution and examine in daylight. Any \nsecondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with test \nsolution (a) is not more intense than,the spot in the \nchromatpgramobtained with-reference solution(a).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent. ;\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying over phosphorus pentoxide for 4 hours.\nAssay. Weigh 0.12 g, dissolve in 20 ml of ethanol (95 per \ncent), add 60 ml of carbon dioxide-free waler. Titrate with \n0.1 M sodium hydroxide^ determining the end-point potentio­\nmetrically (2.4.25). Carry but a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0:1 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01521 g of \nCgH8O3. ......... • \n■ \n- \n■ \n■■ ■ ■\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nVasopressin\nCys—Tyr—Phe—;Gin—Asn—'Cys—Pro—Arg—Gly—NH2\n1_________________________J\nArginine Vasopressin\nVasopressin, 8-1 .-arginine\nC46H65N15O12S2 \n; Mol. WL 1084.2\nVasopressin, is a polypeptide hormone haying the properties \nof causing the contraction of vascular and other smooth \nmuscles and of antidiuresis. It is prepared by chemical \nsynthesis or by animal origin.\nVasopressin contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C46H65Ni5O12S2, calculated on anhydrous \nand acetic acid-free basis.\n1 mg of anhydrous Vasopressin is equivalent to 410 \nVasopressin Units.\nCategory. Antidiuretic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.'"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogm \nwith the test solution corresponds to the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (\nin the\nTests . '\nAcetic acid. Note more than 15:0 percent.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 95 volumes of a 0.07 per cent v/v \northophosphoric acid in water, adjusted to pH 3.0\non of \nRper\ncent w/v solution of sodium hydroxide and 5 ? \nmethanol.\nes of\nTest solution. Prepare the solution as described under\nsolution. The amount of material used can bë||||pte£{\ndepending on the amount of acetic acid expected.’*0®:\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution \nacetic acid IPRS in the solvent mixture (The concentration \ncan be adjusted depending on the amount of acetate or acetic \nacid expected to be present in the substance under examination).\nChromatographic system \n’•\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), .\n- mobile phase: A. a 0.Q7 per cent, v/v solution of\northophosphoric acid in water, adjusted to pH 3.0 with \n42 per cent w/v solution of sodium hydroxide, ,\nB. methanol, , \n.. ...\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute, \n.•^4.\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm, \n\"f\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase À\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)'\n0\n95\n5 \n'\n5\n95\n5\n10\n50\n50\n20\n50\n50\n22\n95\n. \n5.\nThe retention time of acetic acid peak is 3 to 4 minutes.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the? \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not mote \nthan 5 per cent. \n?<\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of acetic acid.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography/\n(2.4.14), as described under Assay with the following \nmodifications. \n3^-.\n&"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vecuronium-bromide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vecuronium Bromide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vecuronium Bromide Injection\nVecuronium Bromide Injection is a sterile material consisting \nof Vecuronium Bromide with or without auxiliary agents. It is \nsupplied in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the\nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nEjections, immediately before use. -"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vecuronium Bromide Injection\nVecuronium Bromide Injection is a sterile material consisting \nof Vecuronium Bromide with or without auxiliary agents. It is \nsupplied in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the\nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nEjections, immediately before use. -"},{"name":"VECURONIUM BROMIDE INJECTION","text":"The constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections). ■ v\nVecuronium Bromide Injection contains¡ hot less thari 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nvecuronium bromide, Cffi ffiBrNgCh.\nThe contents of the sealed Container .comply 'with the \nrequirements slatted under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powdersfor Injection) and with the following requirements.\nDescription. A white to off white lyophilized mass.\nIn the Related substances, the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nSolution A. Reconstitute the content of a vial with Water for \nInjections.\npH (2.4,24)..3.5 to 4.5, determined in solution A.\nLight-absorption (2,4-7). Not more than 0.1 at 420. nm, \ndetermined on solution A.\nRelated substances. Determine by .liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14)......    ;...........\nSolvent mixture A. Dilute 8.5 ml of concentrated'hydrochloric \nacid to 100 ml with water. Dilute 10 ml of the solution to 1QOO \nml with acetonitrile.\nSolvent mixture. ¿^Dissolve.! g of citric acid monohydrate \nand 730 mg of disodium hydrogen phosphate in water and \ndilute to 100 ml with water.\nTest solution. Reconstitute 10.vials with Water for Injections \nand dilute the solution containing 20 mg of vecuronium \nbromide to 5.0,0 ml with solvent mixture A, sonicate for 30 to 40 \nminutes, filter. Dilute 10.0. ml of the solution to 25,0; ml. with \nsolvent mixture A.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 20 mg of vecuronium \nbromide IPRS in 5.0 ml of solvent mixture B and dilute to 50.0 \nml withkblvent mixture A, soiiicate for 20 minutes, filter. Dilute\n10.0 ml of the solution to 25.0 ml with solvent mixture A.\nReference solution (b). Transfer 25 rcgoivecuronium bromide \nIPRS and 10 mg ofpotassium carbonate (previously dried at\n105° for 30 minutes) in 25-ml volumetric flask and make up the \nvolume with methanol. Shake vigorously, decant the solution \nand inject immediately.\nChromatographic system\n- - a stainless steel column 25 cm.x 4.6 mm, packed with\nporous silica (5 pm) (Such as Hypersil silica),\n- column temperature: 40°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 60 volumes of a solution\nprepared by dissolving 8.0 g of sodium perchlorate in\n3929\nwww.webofpharma.com\n' 6 ml of water an diluteto 1000 ml with acetonitrile and\n; \n40 volumes of a solution prepared by dissolving 1.60 mg\nof ammonium chloride in 100 ml. of 25 per cent w/v\n- ammonium hydroxide solution and dilute 8 ;ml: of the\nsolution to 1000 ml with methanol,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,,,\n- spectrophotometer set at 215 nm,\n- injection volume: 100 ill.\nName \n. Relative.\n. , .. .... \n. ...retention.time.\nVecuronium bromide (Retention time:\nabout 5 minutes) \n1.0\nVecuronium bromide diol impurity - = 1.65\nVecuronium bromide mono impurity \n• 1;15 ,\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless thè \nresolution between the peaks due to vecuronium bromide and \nvecuronium bromide mono impurity is not less than 1.5.\nInject the test solution. The area of any peaks due, to \nvecuronium bromide mono impurity and diol impurity is not \nmore than 1.0 per cent. The sum of the areas of all thè secondary \npeaks is not more than 2.0 per cent, calculated bÿ area \nnormalization method.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 3.0 per cent, determined on \n0.1g.\nBacterial Endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 50 Endotoxin Units\nper mg of Vecuronium Bromide.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as \ndescribed under Related substances with the following \nmodifications/ \n: '\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to vecuronium bromide and \nvecuronium bromide mono impurity is not less than 1.5, the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 3500 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C^^Br^CÙ in the injection."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vecuronium-bromide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vecuronium Bromide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"VECURONIUM BROMIDE\nVecuronium Bromide\nC34H57BrN2O4 \n' MoI.Wt.638\nVecuronium Bromide is l-(3a,17p-Diacetoxy-2p-piperidino- \n5a-androstan-16 (3 -yl) -1 -methylpiperidiniurh bromide.\nVecuronium Bromide contains pot less than 99.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C34H57BrN2O4, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis. \n-r---■■'■■■ ■■>■\nCategory. Non depolarizing neuromuscular blocker.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystals or crystalline \npowder. ... \n. \n...... rf.... ....."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"VECURONIUM BROMIDE\nVecuronium Bromide\nC34H57BrN2O4 \n' MoI.Wt.638\nVecuronium Bromide is l-(3a,17p-Diacetoxy-2p-piperidino- \n5a-androstan-16 (3 -yl) -1 -methylpiperidiniurh bromide.\nVecuronium Bromide contains pot less than 99.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C34H57BrN2O4, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis. \n-r---■■'■■■ ■■>■\nCategory. Non depolarizing neuromuscular blocker.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystals or crystalline \npowder. ... \n. \n...... rf.... ....."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Complies with the test for Specific optical rotation.\nB. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6), \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with vecuronium \nbromide IPRS or with the reference spectrum of vecuronium \nbromide.\nC. It gives reaction (A), of bromides? (2.3.1).. :"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution; A1.0 per cent w/v solution in 0.5 per \ncent v/v of hydrochloric acid is clear (2.4.1) and not more \nintensely coloured than reference solution BYS7 (2.4.1).\nSpecific optical rotation (2,4.22). + 30,5° to + 35.0°, determined \non 1.0 per cent w/v solution in 0.05 Mhydrochloric acid.\nImpurity B. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), \ncoating the plate with ¿vY/c^gc/6-. \n- o -\nMobile phase. Dissolve 1 g of sodium bromide in 5 ml of \nwater. Add 85 ml of 2- propanol, then 10 ml of acetonitrile.\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination in dichloromethane 'and dilute to 5.0ml with \ndichloromethane. \n<■ .-J.- j-m- j ; i. v\nReference solution (a). A solution'containing 0.11 per cent \nw/v, each of. the substance ¿under examination and vecuronium \nbromide, impurity B IPRS (pancuronium bromide.IPRSfvn \ndichloromethane. \ni ■■ ■; .\nReference solution: (b): A 0.005 Percent^w/vj7olution \nvecuronium bromide impurity B IPRS m dicflordmethan\nfe.\n9iìióbìi|\nApply to the plate 1 pl of each solution, \nphase to rise 15 cm. Dry the plate in air for||( \nspray with a 0.25 percent w/v solution of iod \nof equal volumes of methanol and; dichloh \nspot due to vecuronium bromide impurity B is n6 \nthan the spot in the chromatogram obtained\næwWy\nB^ntensfl'\neferencef\nsolution (b) (0.25 per cent). The chromatogranS \nreference solution (a)J shows 2 clearly separat51\n.edwithg \nfs'/' \"\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid, c\n(2.4.14). \n’ : : \nr/.v. m'- ',.7 • 7 \"4\ngraphy\nNOTE Use freshly prepared solutions. . ,\nTest solution. Dissolve 40 mg of the subs \nexamination in a 0.02 per cent w/v solution of \nacid vo methanol and dilute to 20,0 ml with\nunder \nchloric\nReference solution (a). Dilute 5.0 ml of the tcsttsbluiibn to\n100.0 ml with a 0.02 per cent w/v solution \nacid in methanol.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 5.0 nil of reference ¿oliition (a) \nto 100.0 ml with a 0.02 per.cent w/v solution of hydrochloric \nacid in mëthanol \n'\nReference solution (ç). Dilute 10.0 ml of reference, solution (b)\nto 50,0 ml with a 0.02 per cent w/v solution of hydrochloric \nacid in methanol. - \n, ■\nChromatographic system \n. ;\n- a stainless steel column 25 cni x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm);' <\n- column temperature; 40°, ■ \n. .\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of a 1.8 per cent\nw/v soiution of tétraméthylammonium ’ hydroxide, \nadjusted to pH 6.5 with orthophosphoric acid, \n250 volumes of methanol and'700 volumes of \nacetonitrile,\n- flow rate : 2 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm, \n: ; /\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n. ..y;\nName -\nRelative \nretention time\n■ Correction\n, . factor S\nVecuronium impurity C1\n0.8\n1.4\nVecuronium impurity D2\n°-9\n----EU\nVecuronium (Retention time: \nabout 5 minutes) ■'\n■ L0 7\nVecuronium impurity E3\n1.2\n—\nVecuronium impurity A4\n■ 1.3 \n7'\n■ 0.6\n!l-[17p-(acetÿloxy)-3à-hydfoxy-2p-(piperidiri-l'-yl)-5d-androstàfr \nÏ6p-yl]-l-methylpiperidinium, \nV;\n3928\nwww.webofpharma.com\n^dihy4roxy-2p-.(piperidin-l-yl)-5a-androstanr16pryl]71- \nieridjpiuin,;.;.. ; \n\"\nggtyloxy)-!7p-hydroxy-2p-(piperidin-l-yl)-5a-androstan-\nSmethylpip'eridinium, \n''\n^(piperidin-l-yl)-5d -androstane-3a, 170 -diyl diacetate.\nTerence solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nefficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and\n^¿¿t’refercnce solution (b), (c) and the test solution. Run \n^chromatogram 2.5 times the retention time of the principal \ni/'f^^l^hzthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \n£ ^Jafeuofanypeak corresponding to vecuronium bromide"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A, C, D, E is not more than the area of the principal \n^Mnftbe chromatogram obtained with reference solution\n(b) (0 25 per cent), the area of any other secondary peak is not\n■ moreffian twice the area of the principal peak in the\n^m^ogram obtained with reference solution (c) (0.1 per \n^t)^and the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks is not \nn^$ithah.2.8 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchiioniatograrn obtained with reference solution (b) (0.7 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.2 timds the area \nof the -principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.05 per cent)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Sajphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\n^atery(2,3.43). Not more than 4.0 per,cent, determined on\n: - -.I- \n■■-■■■/■ g;\nAssay. Dissolve 0.45 g in 50 ml of glacial acetic 'acid. Titrate\nper chloric acid, determining the end-point \npotentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration,\n4 ml of 0.7-M-perchloric acid is equivalent to .0,0638 g of \nCjJfoBrNiC.r- ■ \n;\nVecuronium Bromide intended for use in the manufacture of \nppreyiteral preparations, without a further appropriate \nprocedure for the removal of bacterial endotoxins complies\nwith the following additional requirement.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 10.0 Endotoxin \nUnks per mg of vecuronium bromide.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vegetable-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vegetable Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vegetable Oil\nX\nX\nX\nExtract of Sample in\nVegetableOil\n- . or subcutaneous implantation). ; \n' \n......\nclassification does not apply to plastics that are intended for \nuse as containers for oral or topical products, or that may be \nused as ah integral part of a drug'formulation. Table 1 does \nnot apply to natural, elastomers, which are to be tested in \nSodium Chloride Injection and Vegetable Oils only.\nThe Systemic injection Test and the intracutaneous Test are \ndesigned, to determine .the systemic and local, respectively, \nb iol ogical re sponses of animals to plastics and other po lymers \nby the single-dose injection of specific extracts prepared from\na sample. The Implantation Test is designed to evaluate the \nreaction of living tissue to the plastic and other polymers by\nthe implantation of the Sample, itself into animal tissue. The \nproper preparation and placement of the specimens under \naseptic conditions are important in the conduct of the \nImplantation Test. • - l. . f'\n2.2.24. TEST FOR BIOLOGICAL REACTIVITY, IN VIVO\nIP 2022\nTable 1 - Classification of Plastics\nI 11\"\n111\nIV\nV\nVI\nTest Material\nAnimal\n. Dose\nProcedure2\nMouse\n50 ml/kg\nA(IV)\n0.2 ml/animalat \neach of 10 or 6 sites\nB(IC)\nRabbit or\nGuinea Pig\nMouse\n50ml/kg\n, A(IP) .\nRabbit or ■ \nGuinea Pig\nB(IC)\n0.2 ml/animal at \neach of 10 or 6 sites\n•. 10g/kg\nA(ff) \nB(IC)\nMouse\": \nRabbit or \nGuinea Pig\n0.2 ml/animalat \neach of 10 or 6 sites\nA(EP) ...\nBflQ\nlOg/kg\nMouse \nRabbit or \nGuinea Pig\n0.2 ml/animal at \neach of 10 or 6 sites\n. A(IP) ■\nMouse \nRabbit or \nGuinea Pig\n50m1/kg\nB(IC)\n0.2 ml/animal at \neach of 10 or 6 sites\n■ 50 ml/kg \n0.2 ml/animal at ! \neach of 10 or 6 sites ■\nA (IP )\nMouse \nRabbit or \nGuinea Pig\nB. IC)\n■ X\nX -\nImplant strips of Sample\nRabbit\n- 4 strips/animal\nC\nX ■\nImplant Sample\nRat\n■ 2 samples/animal\nc\nTests required for each'class,, are indicated by “x5* in appropriate columns!\n2 Legend: A (IP) - Systemic Injection Test' (intraperitoneal); B (IC)- Intracutaneous Test (Intracutaneous); C -Implantation Test (intramuscular\nThese tests are designed for application to plastics and Other \npolymers in the condition in which they are used. Ifthe material \nis to be exposed to any cleansing or sterilization process prior \ntp its. end-use, then, the tests are to be conducted on a sample \nprepared from a specimen, preconditioned by the same \nprocessing. Factors such as liiaterial composition, processing \nand cleaning procedures, contacting media, inks, adhesives, \nabsorption, adsorption and permeability ofpreseryatiyes, and \nconditions of storage mayalso affect the, suitability of a material \nfor a specific use. Evaluation of such factors should be made \nby appropriate additional specific tests to determine the \nsuitability of ?..material for its intended use,. ■ ;.\nApparatus. The apparatus for the tests includes the following:\nAutoclave. Use an autoclave capable of maintaining a \ntemperature of 121 L2-. equipped with a thermometer,: a\npressure gauge, a vent cock, a rack adequate to accommodate \nthe test containers above the water level, and a water cooling' \nsystem that will allow for cooling of the test containers to \nabout 20°j but not below, immediately following the heating \ncycle.\nOven. Use an oven, preferably a forced-circulation model, that \nwill maintain operating temperatures of 50° or 70 ± 2°.\nExtraction Containers. Use containers such as ampoules or \nscrew-capped culture test tubes, of Type I glass. If used, \nculture test-tubes are closed with screw caps having suitable \nelastomeric liners. The exposed surface ofthe elastomeric liner \nis completely protected with an inert solid disc 0.05- 0.075 mm \nin thickness. A suitable disc may be fabricated from a polytef \nresin.\nPreparation of apparatus. Clean all glassware thoroughly with \nchromic acid mixture, or if necessary, with hot nitric acid, \nfollowed by prolonged rinsing with water. Clean the cutting \ndevices by an appropriate method (e.g., successive cleaning \nwith acetone and methylene chloride) prior t"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Vegetable Oil\nX\nX\nX\nExtract of Sample in\nVegetableOil\n- . or subcutaneous implantation). ; \n' \n......\nclassification does not apply to plastics that are intended for \nuse as containers for oral or topical products, or that may be \nused as ah integral part of a drug'formulation. Table 1 does \nnot apply to natural, elastomers, which are to be tested in \nSodium Chloride Injection and Vegetable Oils only.\nThe Systemic injection Test and the intracutaneous Test are \ndesigned, to determine .the systemic and local, respectively, \nb iol ogical re sponses of animals to plastics and other po lymers \nby the single-dose injection of specific extracts prepared from\na sample. The Implantation Test is designed to evaluate the \nreaction of living tissue to the plastic and other polymers by\nthe implantation of the Sample, itself into animal tissue. The \nproper preparation and placement of the specimens under \naseptic conditions are important in the conduct of the \nImplantation Test. • - l. . f'\n2.2.24. TEST FOR BIOLOGICAL REACTIVITY, IN VIVO\nIP 2022\nTable 1 - Classification of Plastics\nI 11\"\n111\nIV\nV\nVI\nTest Material\nAnimal\n. Dose\nProcedure2\nMouse\n50 ml/kg\nA(IV)\n0.2 ml/animalat \neach of 10 or 6 sites\nB(IC)\nRabbit or\nGuinea Pig\nMouse\n50ml/kg\n, A(IP) .\nRabbit or ■ \nGuinea Pig\nB(IC)\n0.2 ml/animal at \neach of 10 or 6 sites\n•. 10g/kg\nA(ff) \nB(IC)\nMouse\": \nRabbit or \nGuinea Pig\n0.2 ml/animalat \neach of 10 or 6 sites\nA(EP) ...\nBflQ\nlOg/kg\nMouse \nRabbit or \nGuinea Pig\n0.2 ml/animal at \neach of 10 or 6 sites\n. A(IP) ■\nMouse \nRabbit or \nGuinea Pig\n50m1/kg\nB(IC)\n0.2 ml/animal at \neach of 10 or 6 sites\n■ 50 ml/kg \n0.2 ml/animal at ! \neach of 10 or 6 sites ■\nA (IP )\nMouse \nRabbit or \nGuinea Pig\nB. IC)\n■ X\nX -\nImplant strips of Sample\nRabbit\n- 4 strips/animal\nC\nX ■\nImplant Sample\nRat\n■ 2 samples/animal\nc\nTests required for each'class,, are indicated by “x5* in appropriate columns!\n2 Legend: A (IP) - Systemic Injection Test' (intraperitoneal); B (IC)- Intracutaneous Test (Intracutaneous); C -Implantation Test (intramuscular\nThese tests are designed for application to plastics and Other \npolymers in the condition in which they are used. Ifthe material \nis to be exposed to any cleansing or sterilization process prior \ntp its. end-use, then, the tests are to be conducted on a sample \nprepared from a specimen, preconditioned by the same \nprocessing. Factors such as liiaterial composition, processing \nand cleaning procedures, contacting media, inks, adhesives, \nabsorption, adsorption and permeability ofpreseryatiyes, and \nconditions of storage mayalso affect the, suitability of a material \nfor a specific use. Evaluation of such factors should be made \nby appropriate additional specific tests to determine the \nsuitability of ?..material for its intended use,. ■ ;.\nApparatus. The apparatus for the tests includes the following:\nAutoclave. Use an autoclave capable of maintaining a \ntemperature of 121 L2-. equipped with a thermometer,: a\npressure gauge, a vent cock, a rack adequate to accommodate \nthe test containers above the water level, and a water cooling' \nsystem that will allow for cooling of the test containers to \nabout 20°j but not below, immediately following the heating \ncycle.\nOven. Use an oven, preferably a forced-circulation model, that \nwill maintain operating temperatures of 50° or 70 ± 2°.\nExtraction Containers. Use containers such as ampoules or \nscrew-capped culture test tubes, of Type I glass. If used, \nculture test-tubes are closed with screw caps having suitable \nelastomeric liners. The exposed surface ofthe elastomeric liner \nis completely protected with an inert solid disc 0.05- 0.075 mm \nin thickness. A suitable disc may be fabricated from a polytef \nresin.\nPreparation of apparatus. Clean all glassware thoroughly with \nchromic acid mixture, or if necessary, with hot nitric acid, \nfollowed by prolonged rinsing with water. Clean the cutting \ndevices by an appropriate method (e.g., successive cleaning \nwith acetone and methylene chloride) prior to use in \nsubdividing a specimen.\nClean all other equipment by thorough scrubbing with a \nsuitable detergent and prolonged rinsing with water. Render \ncontainers and equipment used for extraction, and in transfer \nand administration of test material, sterile and dry by a suitable \nprocess.\nNOTE—If ethylene oxide is used as the sterilizing agent,\nallow adequate time for complete degassing.\nExtracting media\n(a) Sodium Chloride Injection. A 0.9 per cent w/v solution\nof sodium chloride in water for injections.\n(b) A 5 per cent v/v solution of ethanol in sodium chloride\ninjection.\n(c) Polyethylene Glycol 400.\n(d) Vegetable Oil. Use freshly, refined sesame oil or\ncottonseed oil or other suitable vegetable oils that meets \nthe following additional requirement. Using three properly \nprepared animals, and injectthe oil 0.2 ml intracutaneoiisly\nat each of 10 sites per animal, and examine the animals at \n24, 48, and 72 hours following injection. Rate the \nobservations at each site on the numerical scale indicated \nin Table 2. For the 3 rabbits or guinea pigs (30 or 18\n■ injection sites), at any observation time, the average\nresponse for erythema is not. greater than 0,5 and for \nedema is hot greater than 1.0, and no site shows a tissue\n' ' reaction larger than 10 mm iti overall diameter. The residue'\nof oil at the injection site should not be misinterpreted as \nn edema. Edematous tissue blanches when gentle pressure\nis applied. ... \n.\n(e) Drug Product Vehicle (where applicable). \n, \n.\n2.2.24. TEST FOR BIOLOGICAL REACTIVITY, IN EIKO\nIP 2022\nWater for Injection.\nTable 2-Evaluation of Skin Reactions’\nErythema and Eschar Formation \nScore\n: No erythema ■ \n0\nVery slight erythema (barely perceptible) \n1\nWell-defined Erythema \n2\nModerate to severe Erythema \n3\nSevere erythema (beet-redness) to slight eschar \nformation (injuries in depth) \n4\n’ Edema Formation2 \nScore.\nNo edema \n0\nVery slight edema (barely perceptible) \n1\nSlight edema (edges of area well defined by\ndefinite raising) \n2\nM"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/venlafaxine-hydrochloride-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride Tablets\nVenlafaxine Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nvenlafaxine, C./H^/NOi-\nUsual strengths. 25 mg; 37.5 mg; 75 mg. . ■\n3933\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVENLAFAXINE TABLETS"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride Tablets\nVenlafaxine Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nvenlafaxine, C./H^/NOi-\nUsual strengths. 25 mg; 37.5 mg; 75 mg. . ■\n3933\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVENLAFAXINE TABLETS"},{"name":"Identification","text":";\nSolvent mixture. 3,0 volumes of cyclohexane and 70 volumes \noî dichlpromethane.\nDissolve a quantity of the powdered tablets containing about \n350 mg of venlafaxine in a 100 ml of the solvent mixture with \naid of ultrasound for 30 minutes, filter the extract through \nanhydrous sodium sulphate and evaporate the. filtrate to \ndryness. Wash the residue with the solvent mixture, filter and \ndry the residue. On the residue, determine by infrared \nabsorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum \nwith that obtained with venlafaxine hydrochloride IPRS \ntreated in the same manner or with the reference spectrum of \nvenlafaxine hydrochloride."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),.\nMedium. 900 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the mediüm and filter. Reject \nfirst few ml of the filtrate and dilute a suitable volume of the \nfiltrate with dissolution medium to obtain a 0.0025 per cent \nw/v solution of venlafaxine. Measure the absorbance of the \nfiltrate, at the màximum at about274 nni(2.4.7). Calculate the \ncontent of C17H27NO2 in the medium from the absorbance \nobtained from a solution having a known concentration of \nvenlafaxine hydrochloride IPRS similar to the concentration \nof the test solution. \n;\n1 mg of C17H27NO2,HClis equivalent to 0.884 mg of Ci7H27NO2.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of Ç17H27NÔ2.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of powdered tablets \ncontaining 200 mg of venlafaxine in 80 ml of a 2.4 per cent v/v \nsolution of orthophosphoric acid with the aid of ultrasound \nfor 30 minutes, cool and dilute to 100.0 ml with water and \ncentrifuge: \n.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n500.0 ml with mobile phase A.\nReference solution (b). A 0.2 per cent w/v solution of \nvenlafaxine impurity IPRS (containing impurity D and F) in \nmobile phase A. \n\" cd ■' Nf” : 4\nReference solution (c). Dilute 25.0 ml of reference solutionna)\nto 100.0 ml with mobile phase A.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x. 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), (Such \nas Partisil ODS 3), \n: \n:\n_________ ._____________________________\n■ \nmobile phase: A. a mixture of 1 volume of Methylarniné\n20 volumes of acetonitrile and 80 volumes ofwater \nadjusted to pH 3.5 with orthophosphoric acid < \n’\n: B : a mixture of1 volume of triethylamine \n50 volumes of acetonitrile and 50 volumes of water \nadjusted to pH 3.5 with orthophosphoric acid, ?\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below\n■ ■ flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 226 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n<\nTime \nMobile phase A \nMobile phase B\n(in min.) (per cent v/v) (per cent v/v)\n0 \nloo \n0\n20 .............. 100 \n0 ; ;\n30 \n0 \n: ; \n100\n45 \n0 \nTOO\n48 \n100 \n0\n60 \n100 \n' \n0\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to venlafaxine and \nvenlafaxine impurity D is not less than 1.5.\nInject reference solutions (a), (c) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak due to venlafaxine impurity D ((l-[(l;iW)-l-(4- \nmethoxyphenyl)-2-(methylamino) ethyl] cyclohexanol) or \nvenlafaxine impurity F ((2&S)-2-(cyclohex4-enyl)-2-(4- \nmethoxyphenyl)NV,A-dimethylethanamine) is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent of each), the area of \nany other secondary peak is not more than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.2 per cent), the sum of areas of all the secondaiy \npeaks is not more than 2.5 times the area of the principalpeak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a),(0,5 \nper cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with r eference \nsolution (c) (0.05 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets, .\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). . r :i\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a \nquantity of the powder containing 50 mg of Venlafaxine in \n250.0 ml of a 0.2 per cent v/v solution of orthophosphoric \nacid with the aid of ultrasound for 30 minutes, cool, mix and \ncentrifuge. Dilute 4.0 ml of the solution to 10.0 ml with the \nmobile phase. \nf\nReference solution (a). A 0.009 per cent w/v solution of\nvenlafaxine hydrochloride IPRS in the mobile phase. ; ; j \nReference solution (b). Â 0:01 per cent w/v solution of\nvenlafaxine impurity IPRS va. the mobile phase; ; ‘ ; '\n3934\n!\nwww.webofpharma.com\nff.2Q22\nChromatographic system\n_a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilanc bonded to porous silica (5 pm), (Such as \nZorbaxC8),\n'. .i. mobile phase: a mixture of 25 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 75 volumes of a 1 per cent v/v solution of \ntriethylamine previously adjusted to pH 3.0 with \northophosphoric acid, ,\n\" - flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 226 nm,\n. ' l injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to impurity D and venlafaxine\nis hot less than 1.0.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Calculate the content of C|7H27NO2 in the tablets.\ni mg of C]7H27NO2, HC1 is equivalent to 0.884 mg of C17H27NO2.\nLabelling. The label states the strength in terms of the \nequivalent amount of venlafaxine."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/venlafaxine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride\nCI7H28C1NO2 \n< Mol. Wt. 313.9\nVECURONIUM BROMIDE INJECTION\nIp202i\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride is l-[(17?iST)-2-(dimethylamino)-uir \nmethoxyphenyl)elhyl|cyclohexanol hydrochloride.\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 percent\nand, not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci7ll2SClNO2, calculate \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant. \nU’/y.'\nDescription. A white or almost white powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11)."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride\nCI7H28C1NO2 \n< Mol. Wt. 313.9\nVECURONIUM BROMIDE INJECTION\nIp202i\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride is l-[(17?iST)-2-(dimethylamino)-uir \nmethoxyphenyl)elhyl|cyclohexanol hydrochloride.\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 percent\nand, not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci7ll2SClNO2, calculate \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant. \nU’/y.'\nDescription. A white or almost white powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11)."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6)\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with.venlqfaxine \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \nvenlafaxine hydrochloride. \n: - : < ;\nNOTE —If the spectra obtained in the solid state show \ndifferences, dissolve the substance under examination and \nreference substance in 2-propanol, evaporate to dryness and \nrecord new spectra using the residues.\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1). \n, r ,\nTests ' \nr '\nAcidity or alkalinity. Dissolve in 0.2 g in IQ, nfr of curhqw \ndioxide free water. Add 0.05 ml of methyl red solution and 0.1 \nml of 0.01M hydrochloric acid. The solution is pink. Not \nmore than 0.2 ml of 0.01Msodium hydroxide is required to \nchange the colour of the indicator to yellow.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 25:0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference soliition(a): Dilute l;0 ml of the test’solution to\n10.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml ofthe solutionto \n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. \n- . 2 \n? -\nReference' so lutiori(b). Dissolve the contents of a vial of\nvenlafaxine for system suitability IPRS (containing impurities \nD and F) in 1.0 ml of the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n■ \n■ mobile phase: a mixture of 149 volumes: of a buffer \nsolution prepared by dissolving 1.7 g of ammonium \ndihydrogen orthophosphate in 149 ml of water, adjusted \nto pH 4.4 with brthophosphoric acid and 51 volumes of \nacetonitrile, \n' \n- .\n- flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute, \n■ \n-\n• - spectrophotometer set at 225 nm, : v ; / - ■ \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\n3930\nwww.webofpharma.com\n~ : Relarive"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/venlafaxine-prolonged-release-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Venlafaxine Prolonged-release Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"VENLAFAXINE PROLONGED-RELEASE CAPSULES\n________________________________________\ns : \n■ >. retentiontime\nWn|afnxinc impurity D \n0.9\nVenlafaxine (Retention time is about 9 minutes) \n1.0\nVenlafaxine impuntyf2_____________________ ' 3-4\nt!^f^j^$J-l-(4-riiethoxypheny[)-2-(rnethyIatnino)ethyl] cyclohexanol,\n^j^-2!-(cycl6hex-l-enyl)-2-(4-niethoxyphenyl)-M^dimethyl- \nethanamine.\nRun the .chromatogram 10 times the retention time of \nvenlafaxine.\nInJeW^crknce solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolutionubetween the peaks due to venlafaxine and \nvenlafaxine impurity D is not less than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of the \npeak due to venlafaxine impurity F is not more than the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.1 per cent), the area of any other secondary \npeak is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per \ncent), the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks is not more \nthan twice the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent). Ignore any \npeak with an area less than 0.5 times of the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0105 per cent)."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"VENLAFAXINE PROLONGED-RELEASE CAPSULES\n________________________________________\ns : \n■ >. retentiontime\nWn|afnxinc impurity D \n0.9\nVenlafaxine (Retention time is about 9 minutes) \n1.0\nVenlafaxine impuntyf2_____________________ ' 3-4\nt!^f^j^$J-l-(4-riiethoxypheny[)-2-(rnethyIatnino)ethyl] cyclohexanol,\n^j^-2!-(cycl6hex-l-enyl)-2-(4-niethoxyphenyl)-M^dimethyl- \nethanamine.\nRun the .chromatogram 10 times the retention time of \nvenlafaxine.\nInJeW^crknce solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolutionubetween the peaks due to venlafaxine and \nvenlafaxine impurity D is not less than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of the \npeak due to venlafaxine impurity F is not more than the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.1 per cent), the area of any other secondary \npeak is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per \ncent), the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks is not more \nthan twice the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent). Ignore any \npeak with an area less than 0.5 times of the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0105 per cent)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Sulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1,0 g by drying in a vacuum at 80° for 3 hours.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.25g dissolve in a mixture of\n5 mlof 0.0 J M hydrochloric acid and 50 ml of ethanol (95per \ncent). Titrate with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide. Determining the \nend-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Read the volume added \nbetween the 2 points of inflexion.\n1 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.03139 g of \nC17H28C1NO2.\nVenlafaxine Prolonged-release \nCapsules\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride Prolonged-release Capsules\nVenlafaxine Prolonged-release Capsules manufactured by \ndifferent manufacturers, whilst complying with the \nrequirements of the monograph, are not interchangeable, as \nIhe dissolution profile of the products of different \nmanufacturers may not be same.\nVenlafaxine Prolonged-release Capsules contain not.less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of venlafaxine, C17H27NO2.\nUsual strengths. 37.5 mg; 75 mg; 150 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Solvent mixture. 30 volumes of cyclohexane and 70 volumes \nof dichloromethane.\nDissolve a quantity of the powdered tablets containing about \n350 mg of venlafaxine in a 100 ml of the solvent mixture for 30 \nminutes, filter the extract through anhydrous sodium sulphate \nand evaporate the filtrate to dryness., Wash the residue with \nthe solvent mixture, filter and dry the residue. On the residue, \ndetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with venlafaxine \nhydrochloride IPRS treated in the same manner or with the \nreference spectrum of venlafaxine hydrochloride.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of the powdered capsules \ncontaining 200 mg of venlafaxine in 80 ml of a 2.4 per cent v/v \nsolution of orthophosphoric acid with: the aid of ultrasound \nfor 30 minutes, cool and dilute to 100.0 ml with water and \ncentrifuge.\nReference solution (a). Dilute L0 ml of test solution to\n500.0 ml with mobile phase A.\nReference solution (b). A 0.2 per cent w/v solution of \nvenlafaxine impurity IPRS (containing impurity D and F) in \nmobile phase A.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 25.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with mobile phase A.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), (Such \nas Partisil CDS 3),\n- \nmobile phase: A. a mixture of 1 volume of triethylamine, \n20 volumes of acetonitrile and 80 volumes of water, \nadjusted to pH 3.5 with drthophosphoric acid,\nB: a mixture of 1 volume of triethylamine, \n50 volumes of acetonitrile and 50 volumes of water, \nadjusted to pH 3.5 with orthophosphoric acid,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given\nbelow,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 226 nm,\n- \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\n3931\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTime\nMobile; phase Ay î ? -Mobile phase B ; '\nnmin.) : (per cent v/v)\n(percentv/v)\n0\n10Ó\n20\n? : 100; r •=\n30\n0\n100\n45\n0\n. 1(j¿)\n. 48\n; 100 ;\n???>??? ,???0?? ,V.\n60\n100\nInject reference -solution (b), Thetest ismorivalid unless; the \nresolutionbetweenthé; peaks due to .venlafaxine and \nvenlafaxine impurity Dis not lessthan. 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a), (c) and the test solution: In the \nchrdmatogfam obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak due to venlafaxine impurity D (1 -[(l/tS)-1 -(4- \nmethoxyphenyl)“2-(methylamino) ethyl] cyclohcxanol) or \nvenlafaxine impurity F ((27?S)-2-(cyclohex-1 -enyl)-2 -(4- \nmethoxyphenyl)-.V,Mdimethylethanaminc) is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent), the area of any b'iher \nsecondary peak is not more than the.arça of the principalpeak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.2 \nper cent), the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks is not \nmore than 2.5 times of the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtamcd with reference solution (a) (0.5 per \ncent), ignore any peak with an area less than the area of the \nprincipal peak i n the! chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (c) (0.05 percent).\nOther fests. Comply; with the; tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (214.14).\nTest'solution. 'Shake a- quantity of thé mixed contents of 20 \ncapsules containing’475: mg of Venlafaxine in 100.0 ml of \nmethanol, ultrasound for 30 minutes and filter. Dilute 5.0 ml of \nthe solution to 100.0 ini with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). A 0.01 pier cent w/v of venlafaxine\nhydrochloride IPRS in the mobile phase.\nReference solution fb). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \nvenlafaxine impurity IPRS (containing impurity D) in the \nmobile phase. \n/. , \n\" L\nChromatographic system A \n.\n- a stainless ¡steel ¡column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), (Such \nv. as ZorbaxRx 018), : ; . :\n.mobile phase: a mixture of 20 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 80 volumes of a 1 per cent v/v?solution\n: ? \noTtriethylamine previously adjusted to pH 3.0 with\northophosphoric acid, \n6.\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 226 nm, . . . .\n- injectionvolume: lOpl, , '.?\" \n?..-?.??? ?\n■■■■íygg\nIP 2022\nVENLAFAXINE PROLONGED-RELEASE CAPSULES\nInject, reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolutionbetween the peaks due to impurity D and venlafaxine \nis not less than 1.0. \n.. ,.. L -...\nInj ect reference solution (a), and the test solution.^ ? .\nCalculate the content of Ci7H27NO2 in the CapsuJ.es.;, 7 ; :\n1 mg of Ci7H27NO2, HC1 is equivalent io 0.884 mg ofC17H27no,\nLabelling. The label states the strength ip- terms of the \nequivalent amount of venlafaxine. \n... i; ; : :"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/venlafaxine-prolonged-release-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Venlafaxine Prol