size filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4,14).\nTest solution. Use the filtrate, dilute if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium.\nReference solution. Dissolve a quantity of zonisamide 1PRS \ntominimum quantity of acetonitrile and dilute with dissolution \nmedium to obtain a solution having a known concentration \nsimilar to the test solution.\nChromatographic system\n- - a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 p’m),\n~ mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of acetonitrile,\n10 volumes of methanol and 140 volumes of a buffer \nsolution prepared by diluting 52 ml of 40 per cent \ntetrabutylammonium hydroxide solution in 948 ml of \nwater, adjusted to pH 7.5 with orthophosphoric acid,"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zolpidem-tartrate-prolonged-release-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zolpidem Tartrate Prolonged-release Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"ZOLPIDEM TARTRATE PROLONGED-RELEASE TABLETS\n1 miof 0. IM perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.03824 g of \nh./•> / -mm :\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture, at ;a \ntemperature not exceeding 30°.\nZolpidem Tartrate Prolonged-release\nZolpidem Tartrate Prolonged-release Tablets manufactured \nby different manufacturers, whilst complying wjth, the \nrequirements of the monograph, are.notinterchangeable,as \nthe dissolution profile of the products of different \nmanufacturers may not be the same.\nZolpidem Tartrate Prolonged release Tablets contain not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and hot more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated amount of zolpidem tartrate, C42H48N6O8.\nUsual strengths. 6.25 mg, 12.5 mg"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"ZOLPIDEM TARTRATE PROLONGED-RELEASE TABLETS\n1 miof 0. IM perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.03824 g of \nh./•> / -mm :\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture, at ;a \ntemperature not exceeding 30°.\nZolpidem Tartrate Prolonged-release\nZolpidem Tartrate Prolonged-release Tablets manufactured \nby different manufacturers, whilst complying wjth, the \nrequirements of the monograph, are.notinterchangeable,as \nthe dissolution profile of the products of different \nmanufacturers may not be the same.\nZolpidem Tartrate Prolonged release Tablets contain not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and hot more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated amount of zolpidem tartrate, C42H48N6O8.\nUsual strengths. 6.25 mg, 12.5 mg"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution (b) corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b)i"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2). Complies with the test stated under \nTablets. \ne \n•\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \ntablets.\nDetermine by liquid, chromatography (2.4.14), using the \nchromatographic conditions as described, under Assay.\nTest solution. Transfer one tablet to 25-ml; volumetric flash \nadd about 20.0 ml of a mixture of ethanol and 0.01 M \nhydrochloric acid (4:2) and disperse withtthe aid of ultrasound \nfor20 minutes. Cool and dilute to volume with the same diluent \nand filter, rej ecting the first few ml of the filtrate. Dilute 10.0 ml \nof the filtrate to 25.0 ml; with mobile phase.\ninject reference solution (b) and test solution.\nCalculate the content of C42H4SN6OS in the tablet. ■\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid ¿chromatography \n(2.4.14). . .... \n' \n(.. \n.\nUsing the buffer solution, mobile phase, reference solution \n(a), test solution (b) and chromatographic system as described \nunder Assay. \n'J.\nReference solutipn(b)y Dissolve<a suitable quantity of \nzolpidem related compound A IPRSiaVae reference solution\n(a) to obtain a solution containing 0.0001 per cent*, \nof zolpidem related compound <4. Dilute 1.0 ml of \nsolution to 5;0 ml with mobile phase.\nName. \n? . \nRelative .\nretention time\nkneift\nlactof;.\nO-Aft\nZolpidem acid1 \n0.3 \n:\n1.0 ■Jr\nZolpidem related compound A2 \n0.9\nZolpidem : \n\" \n1.0\nAny unspecified dégradation \nproduct \n”\n- ! Vàÿhj\nTotal impurities \n—\n'2-(6-Methyl-2-p-tolyliinidazo[l,2-a]pyridin-3-yI)acetic acid, T\nW,7V-Dimethyl-2-(7-methyI-2-/>-tolyIimidazo[l,2-a]pyridin-fy^ \nacetamide. \n■■■ ; \n■ \n. 'r\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peak due to zolpidem related compound \nA and zolpidem is not less than1.5, the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 percent \nand tailing factor is not more than 3.0.\nInject test solution (b). The area ofpeak due to zolpidem acid \nis not more than 0.2 per cent, the area of peak due to \nzolpidem related compound A is not more than 0.2 per ceht, \nthe area of peak due to any unspecified degradation product \nis not more than 0.2 per cent, and the sum of all the peaks is \nnot more than 0.5 per cent, calculated by area normalization \nmethod.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets;\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). ;\nTest solution (a). Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a \nquantity of powder containing 50 mg of Zolpidem Tartrate in \n70 ml of a mixture of ethanol nvdO.Ol M hydrochloric acid \n(5:2) stir for 1 hour arid dilute to 100.0 ml with ethanol. Shake \nwell and filter.\nTest solution (b). Dilute test solution (a), suitably with niobile \nphase, to obtam a solution containing 0,01 per cent w/v \nof Zolpidem Tartrate.\nReference solution (a). A 0.05 per cent w/v of zolpidem\ntartrate IPRSva. a mixture of ethanoland 0.01M hydrochloric \nacid(4:l). :•: ■ \n¿¡ftp\nReference solution (b). A 0.01 per cent wlv ofzolpidem \ntartrate 1PRS from the reference solution (a) in mobile phase.\nChromatographic system \n’•\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),,,, \n- column, temperature 40°, \ni\n- \n: mobile phase: a mixture of 11 volumes of a buifcrsolution \n; y prepared by dissolving^ .3 ml of orthophosphoricacid\n4004\nwww.webofpharma.com\n.^3i$lipOp ml of water, adjusted to pH 5.5 with triethylamine,\n^volumes of acetronitrile and 5 volumes of methanol, \n_ flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n„ spectrophotometer set at 240 nm, \n_ injection volume: 15 pl.\n^ectthdreference solution (b). The test is not valid unless \n^relative standard deviation for replicate injections is not \n^e^ian 2.0 and the tailing factor is not more than 3.0.\n[njectthereference solution (b) and the test solution (b).\nCalculate thc content of C42H4SN6O8 in the tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture, at a"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zolpidem-tartrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zolpidem Tartrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zolpidem Tartrate\n(Ci9H2iNjÔ)2/C4H6O6 \nMol. Wt. 765.0\nZolpidem Tartrate is bis[X^-dimethyl-2-[6-methyl-2-(4- \nmethÿlphényl)imidazo[r,2-a]pyridine-3-yl]acetaniidej tartrate.\nZolpidem Tartrate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and hot \nmore than 101.0 per cent of (C|9l-I2;N3O)2, C4H6C\\ calculated \non the anhydrous basis. ’ * \n'\nCategory.Sedatives.\nDescription ^ white or almost white crystalline powder.\nt • / ,<•’ \nt' - : ' ; \n. \n• •>: \n-"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zolpidem Tartrate\n(Ci9H2iNjÔ)2/C4H6O6 \nMol. Wt. 765.0\nZolpidem Tartrate is bis[X^-dimethyl-2-[6-methyl-2-(4- \nmethÿlphényl)imidazo[r,2-a]pyridine-3-yl]acetaniidej tartrate.\nZolpidem Tartrate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and hot \nmore than 101.0 per cent of (C|9l-I2;N3O)2, C4H6C\\ calculated \non the anhydrous basis. ’ * \n'\nCategory.Sedatives.\nDescription ^ white or almost white crystalline powder.\nt • / ,<•’ \nt' - : ' ; \n. \n• •>: \n-"},{"name":"identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with zolpidem \n^rtraty IPRS or with the. reference spectrum of zolpidem \ntertrate. \n■\n$• A10 per cent w/v solution in methanol gives reaction (c) of \n^ates (2.3.1). \n' \n''' ?'\nTests ...■=<■ \n. \n■ ■■ ■ .\nAppearance of solution . Dissolve 1.0 g of the substance under \nlamination in 4 00 ml of a 0.5 per cent w/v solution of tartaric\nZOLPIDEM TARTRATE\nacid..The.solution is clear (2.4.1), and not more intensely \ncoloured than reference solution YS6 or B YS6 (2.4.1).\nRelated: substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 50.0 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution (af AÛ.01 per .cent w/v ^solution of N,N- \ndimethyl-2-[7-methyl-2-(4-methylphenyl) imidazo[l,2-a] \npyridin~3~yI acetamide IPRS (zolpidem impurity A IPRS) in\nReference solution (b).A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of \nzolpidem tartrate IPRS in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml of the reference solution\n(b) to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (A), To 2.0 ml of reference solution (b),\nadd 10.0 ml of reference solution (a).\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (4 pm),.\n- mobile phase: 18 volumes of acetonitrile, 23 volumes\n: .oT methanol and 59 volumes of 0.56 per cent w/v.\nsolution of orthophosphoric acid, adjusted to pH 5.5 \nwith triethylamine, . . ...\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (d). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to. zolpidem impurity A and \nzolpidem tartrate is not less than 2.0.\nInject reference solution (c) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtainedwith\"the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak’is not more than 0.2 times the area of the peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) \n(0.2 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than the area pf the peak in the. chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) (TO per cent). Ignore any \npeak with a relative retention time of 0.16,to the. zolpidem peak, \ncorresponding to tartaric acid and any-secondary peak \nwith an area less, than 0.05 times the area of the peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) \n(0.05 per cent). ■'\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent. • b - '■ ■■■'\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 3.0 per cent, determined on \n0.5g. \n-s-iuP-s TfPPs Avk sP ib -\nAssay. Dissolve Ô.3 g in a mixture of 20 ml of anhydrous \nacetic acid and 20 ml of acetic anhydride. Titrate with 0JM \nperchloric acid, determining; the end point potentiometrically\n(2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration \nv\n4003\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zone-electrophoresis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zone Electrophoresis"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zone Electrophoresis\nThis method requires the use of small samples only. -.\nIn zone electrophoresis, the sample is introduced as a narrow \nzone or spot in a column, slab or film of buffer. Migration of \nthe components as narrow zones permits their .complete \nseparation. Remixing of the separated zones by thermal \nconvection is prevented by stabilising the electrolyte in. a \nporous matrix such as powdered solid, or a fibrous material\nsuch as paper, foil or gel such as agar, starch or polyacrylamide,\nThe rate of migration depends on four main factors viz. the \nmobility of the charged particle, the electrq-endosmotic flow, \nthe evaporation How. and the strength of the field. It is\nnecessary to operate under clearly defined experimental \nconditions, and to use, wherever .possible, Reference \nSubstances. \n. .\nApparatus\nThe apparatus consists essentially of the following:\n(1) An appropriate power source supplying a direct current \nand provided with means for indicating and controlling either \nthe output voltage or the current consumption as appropriate; \nthe output may be stabilised suitably.\n(2) An electrophoresis chamber which is usually rectangular \nand made of glass or rigid plastic, with two separate troughs, \nthe anodic and the cathodic, containing the electrolyte \nsolution. In one compartment of each trough is immersed an \nelectrode; these are connected by means of an appropriately \nisolated circuit to the corresponding terminal of the power \nsupply to form the anode and the cathode. The level of the \nliquid in the two troughs is kept equal to prevent siphoning. \nContact between the inner and outer compartments of each \ndouble trough is made either by means of “bridges” of \nelectrophoresis paper (suitable filter paper that has been \nwashed chromatographically for 16 hours with amixture of20 \nvolumes of acetone and 10 volumes of water, dried and cut \ninto strips of appropriate size) or by perforating the central \nportion with several holes or by any other means. The \nelectrophoresis chamber is fitted with an airtight lid which \nmaintains a moisture-saturated atmosphere during operation \nand reduces evaporation of the solvent A safety device may \nbe used to cut off the power when the lid is removed. If the \nelectrical power measured across the strip exceeds 10 W, it is \nadvisable to cool the support.\n(3) A support-carrying device suitable for the type of \nelectrophoresis to be carried out.\n(a) For strip electrophoresis — The supporting strip,\npreviously wetted with the same conducting solution and\ndipping at each end into that compartment of each trough \nthat does not contain the electrode, is appropriately \ntightened and fixed on to a suitable-carrier designed to \nprevent diffusion of the conducting electrolyte, such aS \na horizontal frame, inverted-V standbr a uniform surface\n. .with contact points at suitable intervals; -\nk\nlgllll\n1\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\n2.4.12. ELECTROPHORESIS\n(b) For gel electrophoresis — The device consists\nessentially of a glass plate over the whole surface ;of \nwhich isdeposited a firmly adhering layer of gel of uniform \nthickness. The connection between'the gel and the \nconducting solution is effected in various ways according\n■ to the type of apparatus used. Precautions are to be taken\nto avoid condensation of moisture or drying of the solid\nlayer.\n(4) A measuring device or means of detection.\nMethod\nIntroduce the electrolyte solution into the electrode \ncompartments. Place the support suitably impregnated with \nelectrolyte solution in the chamber under the conditions \nprescribed for the type of apparatus used. Locate the starting \nline and apply the sample. Apply the electric current for the \nprescribed time. Switch off the current and remove the support \nfrom the chamber, dry and visualise.\nCAUTION— Voltages used in electrophoresis can deliver a \nlethal shock. The equipment, with the exception of the power \nsupply, should be enclosed in either a grounded metal case \nor a case of insulating mat"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Zone Electrophoresis\nThis method requires the use of small samples only. -.\nIn zone electrophoresis, the sample is introduced as a narrow \nzone or spot in a column, slab or film of buffer. Migration of \nthe components as narrow zones permits their .complete \nseparation. Remixing of the separated zones by thermal \nconvection is prevented by stabilising the electrolyte in. a \nporous matrix such as powdered solid, or a fibrous material\nsuch as paper, foil or gel such as agar, starch or polyacrylamide,\nThe rate of migration depends on four main factors viz. the \nmobility of the charged particle, the electrq-endosmotic flow, \nthe evaporation How. and the strength of the field. It is\nnecessary to operate under clearly defined experimental \nconditions, and to use, wherever .possible, Reference \nSubstances. \n. .\nApparatus\nThe apparatus consists essentially of the following:\n(1) An appropriate power source supplying a direct current \nand provided with means for indicating and controlling either \nthe output voltage or the current consumption as appropriate; \nthe output may be stabilised suitably.\n(2) An electrophoresis chamber which is usually rectangular \nand made of glass or rigid plastic, with two separate troughs, \nthe anodic and the cathodic, containing the electrolyte \nsolution. In one compartment of each trough is immersed an \nelectrode; these are connected by means of an appropriately \nisolated circuit to the corresponding terminal of the power \nsupply to form the anode and the cathode. The level of the \nliquid in the two troughs is kept equal to prevent siphoning. \nContact between the inner and outer compartments of each \ndouble trough is made either by means of “bridges” of \nelectrophoresis paper (suitable filter paper that has been \nwashed chromatographically for 16 hours with amixture of20 \nvolumes of acetone and 10 volumes of water, dried and cut \ninto strips of appropriate size) or by perforating the central \nportion with several holes or by any other means. The \nelectrophoresis chamber is fitted with an airtight lid which \nmaintains a moisture-saturated atmosphere during operation \nand reduces evaporation of the solvent A safety device may \nbe used to cut off the power when the lid is removed. If the \nelectrical power measured across the strip exceeds 10 W, it is \nadvisable to cool the support.\n(3) A support-carrying device suitable for the type of \nelectrophoresis to be carried out.\n(a) For strip electrophoresis — The supporting strip,\npreviously wetted with the same conducting solution and\ndipping at each end into that compartment of each trough \nthat does not contain the electrode, is appropriately \ntightened and fixed on to a suitable-carrier designed to \nprevent diffusion of the conducting electrolyte, such aS \na horizontal frame, inverted-V standbr a uniform surface\n. .with contact points at suitable intervals; -\nk\nlgllll\n1\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\n2.4.12. ELECTROPHORESIS\n(b) For gel electrophoresis — The device consists\nessentially of a glass plate over the whole surface ;of \nwhich isdeposited a firmly adhering layer of gel of uniform \nthickness. The connection between'the gel and the \nconducting solution is effected in various ways according\n■ to the type of apparatus used. Precautions are to be taken\nto avoid condensation of moisture or drying of the solid\nlayer.\n(4) A measuring device or means of detection.\nMethod\nIntroduce the electrolyte solution into the electrode \ncompartments. Place the support suitably impregnated with \nelectrolyte solution in the chamber under the conditions \nprescribed for the type of apparatus used. Locate the starting \nline and apply the sample. Apply the electric current for the \nprescribed time. Switch off the current and remove the support \nfrom the chamber, dry and visualise.\nCAUTION— Voltages used in electrophoresis can deliver a \nlethal shock. The equipment, with the exception of the power \nsupply, should be enclosed in either a grounded metal case \nor a case of insulating material. High-voltage cables should \nbe such that the central conductor is completely shielded \nand the base of the apparatus should be grounded metal or \ncontain a grounded metal rim so constructed that any \nleakage of electrolyte will produce a short-cutting of the \npower supply before the electrolyte can flow beyond the \nprotective enclosure.\nMethod"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zonisamide-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zonisamide Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 238 nm,\n, - injection volume: 10 pl.\nInj ect the reference solution. The test is mot valid unless the \ntailing .factor is not more than 1.5 and the relative standard\ndeviation of replicate inj ections is not more than 3.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CgHgN2O3S.\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nCgHgN2O3S. - ■ \n< \n;\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). : \n! T \\ \n’’\nTest, solution. Weigh a quantity of mixed contents of 20 \ncapsules containing about 0.4 g of Zonisamide add 20 ml of \nwaterpmà.disperse with the aid of ultrasound for 30 minutes \nand dilute to 100.0 ml with, the mobile phase. Mix well and \nfilter. Further dilute 8.0 ml of resulting solution to 100.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase.\nReference solution faf Dissolve a quantity of zonisamide \n1PRS in minimum quantity of acetonitrile and dilute with \nmobile phase to obtain a solution having concentration of \n0.032 percentw/vofzonisamide. ; ; \n> .-\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100,0 ml with the mobile phase and further dilute 1.0 ml of \nthe solution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase. ;; } -, ■ ;: s ;\nReference solution (c). Dissolve ’a oïzomsamide\nimpurity CIPRS in the 10.0 ml of methanol and dilute to 50.0 \nml with the mobile phase. \n•: ;\nReference solution (d). Equal volumes of reference solution\n(a) and reference solution (c).\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),..\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of acetonitrile,\n10 . volumes of methanol and 140 volumes of a buffer \nsolution prepared by , diluting 5,2 ml of 40 per cent \ntetrabutylammonium hydroxide solution.in 948 ml of \nwater, adjusted to pH 7.5 with orthophosphoric acid,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 238 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nName\nr Relative ,\nretention time"},"sections":[{"name":"ZONISAMIDE CAPSULES","text":"- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 238 nm,\n, - injection volume: 10 pl.\nInj ect the reference solution. The test is mot valid unless the \ntailing .factor is not more than 1.5 and the relative standard\ndeviation of replicate inj ections is not more than 3.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CgHgN2O3S.\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nCgHgN2O3S. - ■ \n< \n;\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). : \n! T \\ \n’’\nTest, solution. Weigh a quantity of mixed contents of 20 \ncapsules containing about 0.4 g of Zonisamide add 20 ml of \nwaterpmà.disperse with the aid of ultrasound for 30 minutes \nand dilute to 100.0 ml with, the mobile phase. Mix well and \nfilter. Further dilute 8.0 ml of resulting solution to 100.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase.\nReference solution faf Dissolve a quantity of zonisamide \n1PRS in minimum quantity of acetonitrile and dilute with \nmobile phase to obtain a solution having concentration of \n0.032 percentw/vofzonisamide. ; ; \n> .-\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100,0 ml with the mobile phase and further dilute 1.0 ml of \nthe solution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase. ;; } -, ■ ;: s ;\nReference solution (c). Dissolve ’a oïzomsamide\nimpurity CIPRS in the 10.0 ml of methanol and dilute to 50.0 \nml with the mobile phase. \n•: ;\nReference solution (d). Equal volumes of reference solution\n(a) and reference solution (c).\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),..\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of acetonitrile,\n10 . volumes of methanol and 140 volumes of a buffer \nsolution prepared by , diluting 5,2 ml of 40 per cent \ntetrabutylammonium hydroxide solution.in 948 ml of \nwater, adjusted to pH 7.5 with orthophosphoric acid,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 238 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nName\nr Relative ,\nretention time"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zonisamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zonisamide"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zonisamide\n,N H2"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zonisamide\n,N H2"},{"name":"Tests","text":"\\ \nN 0/ 10\nDissolution (2.5.2).\n:tr.ettir\nC81-0203S \t\nMol. Wt. 212.2\nInject reference solution (d). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to zolpidem impurity A and \nzolpidem tartrate is not less than 2.0.\nApparatus No.1, \nMedium. 900 ml of 0.01 M hydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 30 minutes.\nZonisamide is 1-(1,2-benzoxazol-3-yOmethanesulfonamide.\nZonisamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C 8H8N203S, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nInject reference solution (c) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c) (0.5 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than twice the area of the peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) \n(2.° Per cent). Ignore any peak with a relative retention time of \n0.16 to the zolpidem peak, corresponding to tartaric acid and \nany secondary peak with an area less than 0.05 times the-area \nsoofluthtieon (c) o.05  \npeakinthpeer ce\nchrono.\nmatogram obtained' With reference\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nDescription. A white to off-white powder.\nTest solution. Dilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Reference solution.  Dissolve 0.01 g of zolpidem tartrate RS\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained from zonisamide RS \nor with the reference spectrum of zonisamide.\nin sufficient methanol to produce 100 ml. Dilute the resulting \nsolution with the dissolution medium to obtain a solution \ncontaining 0.0005 per cent w/v of zolpidem tartrate.\nB. In the As*, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with die test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchrOmatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nUse tine chromatographic system described under test for\n„ \t\n.\n•\nZONISAMIDE"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 100.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.0001 per cent w/v\nof zonisamide RS and 0.00015 per cent w/v of zonisamide \nimpurity A RS (1,2-benzisoxazole-3-methane sulphonic  acid \nRS) in the mobile phase.\nUse the chromatographic system as described under Assay.\nThe relative retention time with reference to zonisamide for \nzonisamide impurity A is 0.51.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to zonisamide and \nzonisamide impurity A is not less than 10.0.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram twice the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof any peak corresponding to zonisamide impurity A is not \nmore than the area of the corresponding peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (0.15 per \ncent), the area of any other secondary peak is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (0.1 per cent) and the sum of the areas \nof all the secondary peaks is not more than 3 times the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (0.3 per cent).\nHeavy metals. (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test of \nheavy metals, Method B (10 ppm).\nSulphated ash. (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.8 per cent, determined on \n0.5 g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 100.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution ofzonisamide \nRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 10 volumes of  acetonitrile,\n10 volumes of methanol and 80 volumes of a 0.136 per \ncent w/v solution of monobasic pota \nadjusted to pH 3.0 with orthophosph \nflow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n.f=\n11)201 8"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zopiclone-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zopiclone Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"ZOPICLONE TABLETS\nAssay. Dissolve 0.3 g in a mixture of 10 ml of anhydrous acetic \nacid and 40 ml of acetic anhydride. Titrate with 0.7 M \nperchloric acid, determining the end-point potentiometrically\n(2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.03888 g of \nCl7Hl7ClN6O3.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nZopiclone Tablets\nZopiclone Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of zopiclone, Ci7Hi7ClN6O3.\nNOTE — Carry out all of thëfollowingprocedures protected \nfromlight.\nUsual strengths. 3.75 mg; 7.5 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"ZOPICLONE TABLETS\nAssay. Dissolve 0.3 g in a mixture of 10 ml of anhydrous acetic \nacid and 40 ml of acetic anhydride. Titrate with 0.7 M \nperchloric acid, determining the end-point potentiometrically\n(2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.03888 g of \nCl7Hl7ClN6O3.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nZopiclone Tablets\nZopiclone Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of zopiclone, Ci7Hi7ClN6O3.\nNOTE — Carry out all of thëfollowingprocedures protected \nfromlight.\nUsual strengths. 3.75 mg; 7.5 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 37.5 mg \nofZopiclone with 30 ml of acetone arid dilute to 50 ml with \nacetone arid filter. Evaporate the fíltrate to dryness on a water­\nbath arid dry the residue in an oven at 60° for 2 hours. On the \nresidue, determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry \n(2.4.6). Compáre the spectrum with that obtained with \nzopiclone IPRS or with the reference spectrum of zopiclone."},{"name":"Tests","text":". \n... \nr r ; ■\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle), \n;\nMedium, 5.00 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid.\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of thé medium and filter. Dilute a \nsuitable volume of the filtrate, if necessary with dissolution \nmedium. Measure the absorbance of the resulting solution at \nthe máximum at about 305 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of \nC17H17ClN6O3in the medium from the absorbance obtained \nfrom a solution of known concentration of zopiclone IPRS.\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of\nc17h17cho3. \n• \n: ;\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \nJ \"\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 37.5 mg ofZopiclone with 50 ml of the mobile phase \nwith the aid of ultrasound for 20 minutes arid dilute to 100:0 ml \nwith the mobile phase. \n: .\nReference solution (af; Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. ;\n4009\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZOPICLONE -TABLETS\nReference solution (bfAsola tion containing 0.0001 percent \nw/v, each of, zopiclone IPRS,zopiclone oxide IPRS and 2- \namtno-5-chloropyridine !PRS in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml of réference solution (a) \nto 10-0 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 3 7.5 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 62.5 volumes of a solution containing 0.5 per cent \nw/v solution of sodium dodecylsulphate and 0.0) per \ncent w/v solution of sodium dihydrogen ortho­\nphosphate, adjusted to pH 4.0 with 'orthophosphoric\nacid, \n; '\n1 - .flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- - spectrophotometer set at 303 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 gl.\nThe retention times for 2-amino-5-chloropyridine, zopiclone \noxide and zopiclone are about 7 minutes, about 20 minutes \nand about 26 minutes respectively.\nInject reference solution (b) and (c)...The>testi$ not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to zopiclone and \nzopiclone,oxide is not less than 3.0 in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) and in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c), the. signal-to-noi se ratio \nofthe principal peak is not less than 10.\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of the \npeak due to 2-amino-5-chloropyridine is not more than,twice \nthe area of the peak due to 2-amino-5-chloropyridine in \nreference solution (b) (0.5 per cent):'The area of any other \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.3 times thé area of thé \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.3 per cent) and the sum of areas of all the \nsecondary;peaks is,not more than 1.5 limes the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) ( 1.5. per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less \nthan0.1 times the area of the principal peak in reference solution \n(a) (0.1 per cent). \n. .. .. ..\nUniformity .of content. Complies with the; test stated under \nTablets as described under Assay using the followingsplution \nas the test solution.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). \n-\nTest solution. Disperse. 1 tablet with the mobile phase to obtain \na solution containing 0.0075 per cent w/v of Zopiclone.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). - 4 :\nTest solution; Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nofthe powder containing 37.5 mg of Zopiclone with.60 mt of\nwww.webofpharma.com\nwww.webofpharma.com\nthe mobile phase with thè aid of ultrasoundfor 20 minutesand \ndilute to 500.0 ml with thè mobile phase; filter. \n:\nReference solution. A 0.0075 per. cent ,w/v solution of \nzopiclone IPRS in the mobile phase. \n4\nChromatographic system \n.-i ; \n;\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), :\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 1 volume of acetonitrile^ i\nI volume of a solution containing 0.5 per cent w/v of \nsodium dodecyl sulphate and 0.1 per centw/v of sodium \ndihydrogen orthophosphate, \n; \n. . . ..\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 303 nm,\ninjection volume: 10 pl. '' \n: \n'\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is hot more than 1.4 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate inj ections is riot more than 210 per cent\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C^H^ClNeCb in the tablets. •\nStorage. Store protected from light. \nA .;:; \n\\:"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zopiclone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zopiclone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zopiclone\n2 l-benzisoxazol-3-yl-Af-methylniethanesulphon.arni.de, - '\n3l,2-benzisoxazole-3-methâne'sulphoriicacid.\nInject reference solution (d). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to zonisamide and \nzonisamide impurity C is not less than 2.0 and the tailing factor \nis not more than 1.5. \n- \n: \n■\nInject reference solution (b) \"and the test solution. Ruh the \nchromatogram 6 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area \nof the peak due to methyl zonisamide and zonisamide impurity \nA is not more than 2 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 per \ncent). The area of any other secondary peak is not more than \n2 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 per cent) and the sum \nof areas of all the secondary peaks is not more than 5 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak with an \narea less than that of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.1 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14)..\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the mixed contents of \n20 capsules coiitainihg 100 mg of Zonisamide, and transfer to \na 100.0 ml volumetric flask. Add about 80.0 ml of methanol and \ndisperse with theaid of ultrasoundfor about 15 minutes, cool \nand dilute to volume with methanol. Mix well and filter. Dilute\n1.0 ml of the filtrate to 10.0 ml with mobile phase.\nReference solution. Diluterlr.0 ml of 0.1 percent w/v solution \nofzonisamide IP RS in methanol to 10.0 ml with mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm),\n- : mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of acetonitrile,\n40 volumes of methanol and 160 volumes Of 0.1 per \ncent v/v solution of trifluoroacetic acid,\n- flow rate: I ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 237 nm,\n-■ injection volume: 1 Ogi.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless tailing \nfactor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard déviation \nof replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 1.5 times the retention time of the principal \npeak.\nCalculate the content of C8H8N2O3S in the capsules.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°.\nZONISAMIDE CAPSULES\n..................................    _ \nIP 2022\nZopiclone is (JRS)-6-(5-chloro-2-pyridyl)-6,7-dihydro-7-oxo- \n5if-pyrrolo[3,4-h]pyrazin-5-yl 4-methyipiperâzine-\n1-carboxylate.\nZopiclone contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of C|7Hi7ClN6O3.\nCategory. Non-benzodiazepine hypnotic.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellowish powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zopiclone\n2 l-benzisoxazol-3-yl-Af-methylniethanesulphon.arni.de, - '\n3l,2-benzisoxazole-3-methâne'sulphoriicacid.\nInject reference solution (d). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to zonisamide and \nzonisamide impurity C is not less than 2.0 and the tailing factor \nis not more than 1.5. \n- \n: \n■\nInject reference solution (b) \"and the test solution. Ruh the \nchromatogram 6 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area \nof the peak due to methyl zonisamide and zonisamide impurity \nA is not more than 2 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 per \ncent). The area of any other secondary peak is not more than \n2 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 per cent) and the sum \nof areas of all the secondary peaks is not more than 5 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak with an \narea less than that of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.1 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14)..\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the mixed contents of \n20 capsules coiitainihg 100 mg of Zonisamide, and transfer to \na 100.0 ml volumetric flask. Add about 80.0 ml of methanol and \ndisperse with theaid of ultrasoundfor about 15 minutes, cool \nand dilute to volume with methanol. Mix well and filter. Dilute\n1.0 ml of the filtrate to 10.0 ml with mobile phase.\nReference solution. Diluterlr.0 ml of 0.1 percent w/v solution \nofzonisamide IP RS in methanol to 10.0 ml with mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm),\n- : mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of acetonitrile,\n40 volumes of methanol and 160 volumes Of 0.1 per \ncent v/v solution of trifluoroacetic acid,\n- flow rate: I ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 237 nm,\n-■ injection volume: 1 Ogi.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless tailing \nfactor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard déviation \nof replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 1.5 times the retention time of the principal \npeak.\nCalculate the content of C8H8N2O3S in the capsules.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°.\nZONISAMIDE CAPSULES\n..................................    _ \nIP 2022\nZopiclone is (JRS)-6-(5-chloro-2-pyridyl)-6,7-dihydro-7-oxo- \n5if-pyrrolo[3,4-h]pyrazin-5-yl 4-methyipiperâzine-\n1-carboxylate.\nZopiclone contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of C|7Hi7ClN6O3.\nCategory. Non-benzodiazepine hypnotic.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellowish powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Tests A and C may be omitted if test B is carried outifestB \nmay be omitted if tests A and C are carried out.\nA. When examined in the range 220 nm to 350 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.001 per cent w/v solution in 0.35 per cent solution of \nhydrochloric acidshow^ absorption maxima at about 303 nm \nand specific absorbance at the absorption maximum is 340 to \n380. \nJ:\nB. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with zopiclone IPRS, \nor with the reference spectrum of zopiclone.\nC Determine by thin layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plsite with silicdgel GF254. JL 1 \"\nMobile phase. A mixture of 2 volumes of triethylamine, \n50 volumes of acetone and 50 volumes of ethyl acetate.\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in dichloromethane and dilute to 10.0 ml with \ndichloromethane.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of zopiclone \nIPRS in dichloromethane. \n\\\nApply to the plate 10 gl of each solution. Allow thc mobile \nphase to rise 15 cm. Dry the plate in air and examine under \nultraviolet light at 254 nm. The principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"■\nSolution A: A5.0 per cent w/v solution in dimethylformamide.\n^oneafance of solution. Solution A is not more opalescent \n^opalescence standard OS2 (2.4.1).\nOpricalrotation (2.4.22). - 0.05° to + 0.05°, determined in\nWJOmlof solution A, diluted to 50.0 ml with dimethylformamide.\ndialed substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\ns°lutions immediately before use.\npyspsolution. Dissolve 40 mg of the substance under \ng^tnihation in the mobile phase arid dilute to 10.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution (af Dilute 3.0 ml of the test solution to\n:¡00.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to\n10^0 ml with the mobile phase.\nR^rence solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n400,0. ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to \n100 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (c). A 0.04 per cent w/v solution of \nzopiclone impurity A IPRS (zopiclone oxide IPRS) in the \nmobile phase. To 10.0 ml of the. solution, add 1.0 ml of the test \nsolution and dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\nA-/ a stainless steel colqmp.25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed, with \nf octadecylsilanc bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n.....; mobile phase: a mixture of 38 volumes of acetonitrile\n, and 62 volumes of a solution containing 0,81 per cent\nw/v solution oi sodium laurylsulphate and 0.16 per cent\n. .. w/v solution of sodium dihydrogen phosphate,\nadjusted to pH 3.5 with a 10 per cent v/v solution of \northophosphoric acid, -\n. . - flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 303 nm,\n. - injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to zopiclone impurity A and \nzopiclone is hot less than 3.0. \n:\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 1.5 times the retention time of the principal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \ndie area of any secondary peak is not more than the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.3 per cent) and not more than 2 such peaks \nhave ap area more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.1 per \ncent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zuclopenthixol-acetate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Acetate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"ZUCLOPENTHIXOL ACETATE INJECTION\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nsilica (5 pm) (Such as Spherisorb S 5 W),\nmobile phase: a mixture of 0,03 volume of 13.5 M\n, ■_ ammonia, 45 volumes of dichloromethane, 45 volumes\nof heptane and 50 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- inj ection volume: 15 pl.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the two principal peaks is not less than \n¿¡s7m 7A-A ■■■7 7... ; i 7 ' ■ 7; 7\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding, to trans-clopenthixol acetate \n(zuclopenthixol acetate impurity A) is not more than the area \nof the principal peak in the; chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (1.0 per cent). :\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). \n- r : \n:\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0,1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4,19). Not more than 0.4 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying at 60° at a pressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa \nfor3hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.2 g in 50 ml of anhydrous acetic acid, \ndetermining the end point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry \nout a blank titration.\n.1 .ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02215 g of \nQ^CINAS.\nStorage. Store protected from light, at a temperature not \nexceeding-20°.\nZuclopenthixol Acetate Injection\nZuclopenthixol Acetate Injection is a sterile solution of \nZuclopenthixol Acetate in a suitable vegetable oil.\nZuclopenthixol Acetate. Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of C24H27CIN2O2S."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"ZUCLOPENTHIXOL ACETATE INJECTION\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nsilica (5 pm) (Such as Spherisorb S 5 W),\nmobile phase: a mixture of 0,03 volume of 13.5 M\n, ■_ ammonia, 45 volumes of dichloromethane, 45 volumes\nof heptane and 50 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- inj ection volume: 15 pl.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the two principal peaks is not less than \n¿¡s7m 7A-A ■■■7 7... ; i 7 ' ■ 7; 7\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding, to trans-clopenthixol acetate \n(zuclopenthixol acetate impurity A) is not more than the area \nof the principal peak in the; chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (1.0 per cent). :\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). \n- r : \n:\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0,1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4,19). Not more than 0.4 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying at 60° at a pressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa \nfor3hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.2 g in 50 ml of anhydrous acetic acid, \ndetermining the end point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry \nout a blank titration.\n.1 .ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02215 g of \nQ^CINAS.\nStorage. Store protected from light, at a temperature not \nexceeding-20°.\nZuclopenthixol Acetate Injection\nZuclopenthixol Acetate Injection is a sterile solution of \nZuclopenthixol Acetate in a suitable vegetable oil.\nZuclopenthixol Acetate. Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of C24H27CIN2O2S."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel F254.\nNOTE —Protect the solutions from light.\nMobile phase.- A mixture of 3 volumes of diethylamine and \n90 volumes cd cyclohexane.\nZUCLOPENTHIXOL: ACETATE INJECTION\nReference solution (a). A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of \nzuclopenthixol acetate dihydrochloride IPRS in ethanol \n(95 per cent): ' \n’’’ \n• 1 \n■'\nReference solution (b)„A solution containing 0,5 per cent \nw/v, each of, zuclopenthixol acetate dihydrochloride IPRS \nand zuclopenthixol decanoate dihydrochloride IPRS in \nethanol (95 per cent). \n: \n. e\nApply to the plate 5 p.1 of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 15 cm. Dry the plate in air, spray with a 1.0 per \ncent w/v solution of sodium molybdate in sulphuric acid \nheat at 110° for 20 minutes and> examine in daylight. The \nprincipal spot in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution corresponds to that, in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) shows two \nclearly separated spots. . \n,\ni \nìdiij Ï i\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nB\nTests .\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel.F254.\nNOTE— Protect the solutions from light.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 10 volumes of diethylamine, \n40 volumes of dichloromethane and 50 volumes of \ncyclohexane.\nTest solution:Dissolve a quantity of the injection containing \n0.1 g of Zuclopenthixol Acetate in dichloromethahe and dilute \nto 50.0 ml with dichloromethane.\nReference solution (a). A 0.001 per cent w/v solution of \n2-chlorathioxanthone IPRS in dichloromethane.\nReference solution (b). A 0.002 per cent w/v solution of \nzuclopenthixol hydrochloride IPRS in dichloromethane \n(containing a few drops of diethylamine). .■ ... , . ...\nReference solution (c). A 0.0004 per, cent w/v solution of \nzticlppenthixol hydro chloride IPRS in dichloromethane \n(containing a few drops of diethylamine).\nReference solution (d). A 0.0002 per cent w/v solution of \nzuclopenthixol hydrochloride IPRS in dichloromethane \n(containing a few drops of diethylamine). / T - 1 ;\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 15 cm. Dry the plate in air, spray with a mixture of \nequal volumes of sulphuric acid and ethanol, heat, at 110°. for \n5 minutes and examine under ultraviolet light at 365 nm. In the"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zuclopenthixol-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Acetate\nC24H27C1N,O2S \n:i’’: \n;Ä^.443.0\nZuclopenthixol Acetate is (Z)-2-{4-[3-(2-chlorothioxanthene- \n9-y!idenc)propyl]pipcrazin- 1-yl}ethyl acetate.'J;‘*T ’\nZuclopenthixol Acetate contains not less than, 98.0 per rent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C24H27CIN2Ö2S, calculated \non the dried basis. • \nf 5\nCategory.Neuroleptic. \n... ......\nDescription: Ayellowish, viscous oil. ' ■ • - \n'j'"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zuclopenthixol Acetate\nC24H27C1N,O2S \n:i’’: \n;Ä^.443.0\nZuclopenthixol Acetate is (Z)-2-{4-[3-(2-chlorothioxanthene- \n9-y!idenc)propyl]pipcrazin- 1-yl}ethyl acetate.'J;‘*T ’\nZuclopenthixol Acetate contains not less than, 98.0 per rent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C24H27CIN2Ö2S, calculated \non the dried basis. • \nf 5\nCategory.Neuroleptic. \n... ......\nDescription: Ayellowish, viscous oil. ' ■ • - \n'j'"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. V/hen examined in the range 210 nm to 350:nm.(^4.7)>a \n0.0015 per cent w/v solution in ethanol (95per cent) exhibit5 \ntwo maxima at 230 nm and 268 nm. The absorbancesat the\nmaxima are about 1.18 and 0.51 respectively^\nB. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6)- \nCompare the:spectrurii with that obtained with zuclopenthixol\n'^^^/Pffiÿtftwith the reference spectrum of zuclopenthixol\n\"'7 /\neh siibsfances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n^coatingtheplate with5z/zcage/f254.\ng\nmixture of 10 volumes of diethylamine, \n^wblumesof'-dichloromethane and 50 volumes of \ncyclohexane.\nfei^d/uh‘bn. A 0.25 per cent w/v solution1 of the substance \nunder examination in dichloromethqne.\n^^^.spjulion- (a); A 0.00025.per cent w/v solution of the\nsubs^nch underexamination in dichloromethane.\nReference solution (b). A 0.000125 per cent w/v solution of \nthe substance under examination in dichloromethane.\nReference solution (c). A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \n2-chloróthioxanthone IPRS in dichloromethane.\nR^efrnçe. solution.(d),. A 0.000625 per cent w/v. solution of \nzucjppenthixol hydrochloride IPRS in dichloromethane \n^Q^xlhd^hft^^o^6idieihylamirie)i\nApply to the plate 4 pl of each solution. After removal of the \nplate, dry the plate in air, spray with a mixture of equal volumes \nof sulphuric acid and ethanol, heat at 110° for 5 minutes and \nexamine under ultraviolet light at 365 nm immediately. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, any spot \ncorresponding to 2-chlorothioxanthone (zuclopenthixol \nacetate impurity B) is not more intense than the spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) (0.2 per \ncent) and any spot corresponding to zuclopenthixol \n(zuclopenthixol acetate impurity C) is not inore intense than \nthe spot in thé chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(d) (0.25 per cent).v\\ny other secondary spot is not more \nintense than the spot in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (0.1 per cent) and;not more than three \nsuch spots are more intense than the spot in the chromatogram \n°^riined with reference solution (b) (0.05 per cent). .\nTrahs-Isomer. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nhOTE-- protectedfrom light-. \n;\n7est solution. A 0.04 per cent w/v of the substance under\nexamination in dichloromethane.\nReference ,solution .(a). A 0.00046 per cent w/v solution of \nfrans-clopenthixol acetate dihydrochloride IPRS in \ndichloromethane. \n■\nReferencesolution (b); A solution containing 0.02 per cent\nof the substance under examination and 0.023 per cent \nw^v of trans-clopenthixol acetate dihydrochloride iPRS m. \ndichloromethane. \n. :."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zuclopenthixol-decanoate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Decanoate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Decanoate Injection\nZùclopenthixoi Decanoate Injection is a sterile solution of \nZuclopenthixol Decanoate in a suitable vegetable oil.\nZuclopenthixol Decanoate Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount ofzuclopenthixol decanoate,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zuclopenthixol Decanoate Injection\nZùclopenthixoi Decanoate Injection is a sterile solution of \nZuclopenthixol Decanoate in a suitable vegetable oil.\nZuclopenthixol Decanoate Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount ofzuclopenthixol decanoate,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GE 254. Heat the plate at 110° for \n30 minutes before use. ;\nNOTE --Protect the solutionsfrom light.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 3 volumes of diethylamine and \n90 volumesofcyclohexane. \n_\nTest, solution: Dilute a volume of the injection with ethanol \n(95 per cent) to.obtaima solution containing 0.5 ,per cent w/v, \nof Zuclopenthixol Decanoate. \n;;\nww.webofpharma.com\nReference solution (a). A 0.5 per cent w/v soIutiop||Bi- \nzuclopenthixol decanoate dihydrochloride IPRS jn\n(95 per cent). . 'h: \n; - - ■ ■/\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.5 per\nw/v, each of. zuclopenthixol decanoate dihydrochloi-iReip^ \nand zuclopenthixol acetatedihydrochloride IPRS in etha^i \n(95 per cent). . \n. ... \n..\nApply to the plate 5,;jil of each solution. After developn^. \ndry the plate in air, spray with 1 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium molybdate in sulphuric acid, heat at 110° fop \n20 minutes and examine in day light. The principal spot inthe \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to X \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a).The test is not valid unless the chromatogram obtained |\nwith reference solution (b) shows two clearly separated spots; |\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the |\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4,14).\nNOTE — Protect the solutions from light. , ; \n. ;\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of the injection, containing \n5Q0 mg of Zuclopenthixol Decanoate to, 100,0 ml with \nacetonitrile.\nReference solution (a). A 0.015 per cent w/y solution of \nzuclopenthixol hydrochloride IPRS vo. acetonitrile. ,\nReference solution (b). A 0.0025 per cent w/v solution of 2« \nchlorothioxanthone IPRS in acetonitrile. ■ \n'■\nReference solution (c). A0.005 per cent w/v solution of trans-\nclopenthixol decanoate IPRS in: acetonitrile.w y ;\nReference solution (d). A solution containing 0.015 per cent \nw/v of zuclopenthixol hydrochloride IPRS, 0.0025 per cent \nw/v of 2-chlorothioxanthone IPRS and 0.005 per cent y/y of \ntxans-clopenthixol decanoate IPRS in the test solution.\nChromatographic system \ni ; -\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\ndctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column temperature: 40°, \n3 -\nmobile phase: a mixture of 26 volumes of abuffer solution \nprepared by dissolving 8.9 g of dipctyl sodium \nsulfosuccinate in water, stirring for about. 6-? hours,\nand dilute to 1000 ml with water, 74 volumes of^dnid \n(95 per cent) and 0.1 volume of orthophosphoricacid,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n''NW.■■■'*;'\\\n- spectrophotometer set at 270 nm,\n- \n• injection volume: 20 ul.\n: 1\n? * t reference solution (d). The test is not valid unless the\nî §onÈ^ain sh°WS c,early seParated peaks.\n■preference solution (a), (b), (c) and the test solution. In\n■ \n°bta’ned with the test solution, the area of\nv-nëâk corresponding to zuclopenthixol is not more than\n■ principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with\nper cen$’ the area of any peak \n¿responding to 2-chlorothioxanthone is not more than the\n■ area oTprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with\niefèreh^oihtion (b) (0.5 per cent) and the area of any peak\ncorresponding to trans-clopenthixol decanoate is not more\n? (¿a thearea of principal peak in the chromatogram obtained\nvwthrefêfehce Solution (c) (1.0 per cent).\nQtfcei* tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral\nSéfflity (2-2. 11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nAssay.Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), using \nchromatographic system as described under Related \n¿uèstancès.\nZUCLOPENTHIXOL DECANOATE INJECTION\n..............................................................................\nNOTE — Protect the solutions from light.\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of the injection containing \n500 mg of Zuclopenthixol Decanoate to 100.0 ml with \nacetonitrile.\nReference solution. A 0.57 per cent w/v solution of \nzuclopenthixol decanoate dihydrochloride 1PRS in \nacetonitrile.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CsjH^CINjOsS in the injection.\n1 mg of Zuclopenthixol Decanoate dihydrochloride is \nequivalent to 0.8852 mg of Zuclopenthixol Decanoate.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nOII-SOLUBLEVITAMINS CAPSULES\n#2022 \n~ . ........ . ....... _.......... _..... _ _........ _........\nVitamins Capsules ‘\nI\n§(jluble Vitamins Capsules contain two or more of the \nllf^b'ii-sbluble vitamins: Vitamin A, Vitamin D as \ng^g^aiciferol (Vitamin D2) or Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3),\nPhytonadione (Vitamin KJ, and Beta Carotene, \ncontain not less than 90.0 per cent and not more\nper cent of the labeled amounts of vitamin A \nvATj^b) as retinol or esters of retinol in the form of retinyl \nt \nor retinyl palmitate (C36H60O2); vitamin D as\nciMralbferol (C27II44O) or ergocalciferol (C^BUO); vitamin\nalpha tocopherol (C29I I50O2), alpha tocopheryl acetate \n(GnSsiOj)» or alpha tocopheryl acid succinate (C33H54O5); \nphytonadione (CsJ UO2); and beta carotene (C40H56).\nQil-Soluble Vitarnins Capsules contain no other vitamins or \nany;minerals. They may contain other labeled added \nsubstances that are generally recognized as safe, in amounts \nthatare unobjectionable.\nTests..\ncontamination (2.2.9). The total aerobic viable count \nis hot more than 3000 cfuper g, the total combined molds and \nyeasts count is not more than 300 cfo per g. 1 g is free from \nEscherichia coli. 10 g is free from salmonella.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules."},{"name":"Assay","text":"Vitamin A. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTE Where the use of a vitamin A ester (retinyl acetate\nor retinyl palmitate) is specified in the following procedure, \nuse the chemical form present in the formulation. Vitamin A \nIPRSis retinyl acetate. It is to be used where Vitamin A IPRS \nis specified. Use low-actinic glassware throughout this \nprocedure.\nTest solution (a). Weigh 20 capsules. Open the capsules \nwithout the loss of shell material and transfer the contents to \na 100-ml beaker, equivalent to 5 capsules, to a container having \na polytef-lined screw-cap. (NOTE — For hard gelatin \nCapsules, remove, as completely as possible, the contents of \nNLT 20 Capsules by . cutting open the Capsule shells,\ntransferring the shells and their contents to a suitable \ncontainer, and triturating to a homogeneous mass. Transfer \na portion of the mass, equivalent to 5 Capsules, to a container \nhaving a polytef-lined screw cap.) Add 10 ml of dimethyl \nsulphoxide and 15 ml of n-hexane, and shake for 45 minutes\non a wrist-action shaker in a water-bath maintained at\n(NOTE — Set up the wrist-action shaker to ensure that \n{he contents of the container are mixed vigorously and \nthoroughly). Centrifuge at 3000 rpm for 10 minutes, and \ntransfer the hexane layer by means of a pipet to a 100-ml \nvolumetric flask. Add 15 ml of n-hexane to the dimethyl\nsulphoxide layer, shake thoroughly for 5 minutes, and transfer \nthe n- hexane layer by means of a pipet to the 1 00-ml volumetric \nflask. Repeat this extraction with 3 additional 15-ml portions \nof n-hexane. Dilute the extracts in the volumetric flask with n- \nhexane to volume. \nw k . , \\\nTest solution (b). Dilute a suitable volume of test solution (a) \nwith n-hexane to obtain a solution having concentration 0;0015 \nper centw/v of Vitamin A as Retinol. \ni\nReference solution (a). A 0.0015 per centw/v solution oFretinyl \nacetate from vitamin A1PRS in n-hexane.\nReference solution (b).A 0.0015 per cent w/v solution of retinyl \npalmitate IPRS in n-hexane. \nf\nReference solution (c). A mixture of equal volumes of reference\nsolution (a) and reference solution (b) with n-hexane io obtain \na solution having concentration 0.00075 per cent w/v each of \nretinyl acetate and retinyl palmitate.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\naminopropylsilane bonded to porous silica (3pm),\n- mobile phase: n-hexane,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 325nm, \n- injection volume: 40 pl.\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to alL/ra^.v-retinyl acetate \nand al\\-trans-xetinyl palmitate is not less than 10.0 and the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 3.0 per cent in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nSOiution(c). , -;;\nInject reference solution (a) and test solution (b).\nNOTE — Measure the peak area of all-trans-retinyl acetate \nfrom the Standard solution and the peak area of all-trans-\nretinyl acetate or all-trans-retj.nyl palmitate from the Test \nsolution (b).\nCalculate the content of vitamin A, as retinol (C20H30O) in the \ncapsules.\nNOTE — The molar responses ofretinyl ¡acetate arid retinyl \npalmitate are equivalent.\n1 mg of retinyl acetate C22H32O2 is equivalent to 0.872 mg of \nvitamin A, as retinol C2&H30O.\nNOTE — One Vitamin A IPRS Unit, y 0.3 pg of all-trans- \nretinol(vitamin At alcohol) or 0.344 yg of qll-trans-retinyl \nacetate (vitamin A acetate) or 0 55 pg of all-trans-retinyl \npalmitate (vitamin Apalmiiate), and I pg of retinol (3.3\nVitamin A IPRS Units) =1 retinol equivalent (RE).\nVitamin D. Determine by liquid chromatography (2,4.14).\nNOTE— Where vitamin D (cholecalciferol or ergocalciferol)\nis specified in the following procedure, use the chemical"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zuclopenthixol-decanoate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Decanoate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"ZUCLOPENTHIXOL DECANOATE\n........................—.............................. \n-W46?- \n' chromatogram obtained with the test solution,\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of the injection with ethanol \n(95per cent) to obtain a solution containing 0.5 per cent w/v \nofZuclopenthixol Acetate. •.\nReference solution (b).A 0.00025 percentw/vsolutionof \nzuclopenthixol impurity B in acetomtrile^yA\nReference solution (c). Dissolve the contents pf 'a'viai o¥\nDissolve 20 mg ofzuclopenthixol acetate\ncorresponding to 2-chlorothioxanthone is not more \nthan the spot in the chromatogram obtained with refti \nsolution (a) (0.5 per cent). Any spot correspond^ \nzuclopenthixol is not more intense